  <doc-data|<doc-date|Draft of November 12, 2026>|<doc-title|The construction
  of four-dimensional quantum Yang--Mills theory>>

  <abstract-data|<\abstract>
    For every closed subgroup <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|n<rsub|G>|)>> with
    simple nonabelian Lie algebra<nbsp><math|<g>> and every positive block
    penalty, the coefficient of the soft constraint that ties each coarse
    link to a predictor computed from the fine transports, we construct
    gauge-invariant local fields from four-dimensional Wilson measures. Exact
    scale integration and tuning to a common endpoint coupling give the full
    ultraviolet limit at fixed volume. Reflection positivity and chessboard
    bounds give volume-uniform control; compactness gives a limit along a
    refinement of any prescribed strictly increasing volume sequence. The
    limit decomposes into phases, each the Euclidean continuation of a
    faithful Wightman theory with unique vacuum. The fields carry a conserved
    stress tensor, an operator product expansion with spectator-independent
    coefficients and phase-uniform remainders, and distributional extensions
    through collisions. In a selected phase, the centered stress trace has a
    two-point function <math|<tracetwopoint>> with
    <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)><space|0.17em><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>\<to\>3*dim<rsub|<R>><g>/\<pi\><rsup|4>>
    as <math|r*\<downarrow\>*0>, and on no interval
    <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> does <math|<tracetwopoint>> agree with a
    finite Wick profile, a finite sum of products of free mass-shell Laplace
    kernels. No mass gap, uniqueness of the volume limit or phase, or
    independence from the block penalty is proved.

    This paper was written entirely by AI agents from OpenAI's Lean code for
    the construction of four-dimensional quantum Yang--Mills theory (commit
    <with|font-family|tt|3456e5e>). The Lean proof is kernel-checked, with
    only the standard axioms. The agents compared every numbered statement
    here with the Lean declarations used by the main theorem; the
    correspondence table is published separately. The prose arguments were
    written and reviewed by AI agents. They are not machine-checked and have
    not been refereed by human mathematicians
    (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:intro-source>).
  </abstract>>

