  <part|The theorem and the architecture of its proof>

  <section|The result><label|sec:introduction><label|sec:result>

  How can a lattice model give a quantum field theory in continuous
  space-time? We answer this question for four-dimensional Yang\UMills theory
  with a compact gauge group whose Lie algebra is simple and nonabelian,
  starting from Wilson's lattice gauge theory<nbsp><cite|Wilson1974>. On a
  finite periodic lattice the variables are group elements attached to the
  links, and their law is an ordinary probability measure. Let
  <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|n<rsub|G>|)>> be a closed matrix group,
  <math|s\<geqslant\>1> an integer and <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|s>=<around|(|<Z>/s<Z>|)><rsup|4>>.
  A configuration assigns <math|U*<around|(|y,a|)>\<in\>G> to the positive
  link from the site<nbsp><math|y> in the coordinate direction<nbsp><math|a>,
  and <math|U*<around|(|y,a|)><rsup|-1>> to the reversed link. For
  <math|\<beta\>\<in\><R>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:intro-wilson>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|U<rsub|y;a*b>>|<cell|=U*<around|(|y,a|)>*U*<around|(|y+e<rsub|a>,b|)>*U*<around|(|y+e<rsub|b>,a|)><rsup|-1>*U*<around|(|y,b|)><rsup|-1>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|s><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|s>><big|sum><rsub|a\<less\>b><around*|(|1-n<rsub|G><rsup|-1><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><space|0.17em>U<rsub|y;a*b>|)>,<space|2em>d*\<mu\><rsub|s,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsub|s><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|y,a>d*h*<around|(|U*<around|(|y,a|)>|)><space|0.17em>,>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  where <math|d*h> is probability Haar measure and <math|Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>>>
  normalizes the measure. The plaquette holonomy <math|U<rsub|y;a*b>> is the
  transport around an elementary square, and <math|\<beta\>> penalizes its
  distance from the identity. The problem is to choose the inverse coupling
  and the local observables as functions of the lattice spacing so that their
  correlations converge as the spacing tends to zero and the volume grows,
  and to identify the limit.

  <paragraph|Main result.> Let the real Lie algebra <math|<g>>
  of<nbsp><math|G> be simple and nonabelian; <math|G> need not be connected.
  Fix a block penalty <math|<blockpenalty>\<gtr\>0>: the coefficient of the
  soft constraint that ties each coarse link to a predictor computed from the
  fine transports in the block-spin kernel
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>). We construct one family of Wilson
  measures on tori of physical side <math|L<rsub|p>*\<uparrow\>*\<infty\>>
  with spacings <math|a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*\<downarrow\>*0>, together
  with continuous gauge-invariant local insertions on fixed finite stencils,
  all chosen before any sequence of volumes is prescribed. Its bare inverse
  couplings satisfy the one-loop running law

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:intro-running><frac|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>|log
    <around|(|1/a<rsub|N>|)>>\<longrightarrow\><slope>=<frac|11<casimir>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*<space|2em><around|(|N\<to\>\<infty\>|)>,<space|1em><text|uniformly
    in >p<space|0.17em>,<label|eq:I12-bare-slope>
  </equation>

  where <math|<casimir>> is the adjoint Casimir in the trace normalization of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01> (for <math|S*U<around|(|n|)>>,
  <math|<slope>=11*n<rsup|2>/<around|(|12*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>;
  Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:SUn>). The family has the following properties;
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B> in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:main-theorem>
  states them precisely.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(i)>At each fixed volume, correlations of the insertions, smeared
    against smooth tests supported away from coincident points and inside one
    period cell, converge along the full sequence of spacings.

    <item*|(ii)>Every prescribed strictly increasing sequence
    <math|<math-ss|p>> of volume indices has a strictly increasing refinement
    <math|<math-ss|q>> such that, with <math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>>
    and the lattice correlations <math|<latsmear><rsub|N,p>> of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D03>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:intro-ordered-limit>lim<rsub|k\<to\>\<infty\>><around*|(|lim<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>><latsmear><rsub|N,P<around|(|k|)>><around|(|v;f|)>|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x
    </equation>

    for one table <math|S> of Euclidean correlation functions, symmetric
    under permutations, translations and signed permutations of the axes,
    reflection positive, smooth away from collisions and polynomially bounded
    near them (properties E1\UE7 of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:euclidean-properties>).
    One refinement serves all insertions and all tests.

    <item*|(iii)><math|S=<big|int><rsub|<phases>>S<rsup|\<xi\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>
    is an average of phases over a compact metric space with a fully
    supported probability measure, and each <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> is the
    Euclidean continuation of a faithful Wightman theory with unique vacuum.

    <item*|(iv)>The fields carry a conserved symmetric stress tensor,
    normalized by its action on the other fields. In a selected phase, the
    two-point function <math|<tracetwopoint>> of the centered trace of this
    tensor satisfies

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:intro-trace-law>lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0>
      r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)><space|0.17em><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>=<frac|3*dim<rsub|<R>><g>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>\<gtr\>0<space|0.17em>,
    </equation>

    and on no interval <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> does
    <math|<tracetwopoint>> agree with a finite Wick profile, a finite sum of
    finite products of free mass-shell Laplace kernels
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D17>).

    <item*|(v)>Nearby fields have an operator product expansion whose
    coefficients depend neither on the other fields nor on the phase, with
    remainders bounded uniformly in the phase. The correlations of each phase
    extend across coincident points by an explicit subtraction of Taylor
    jets.
  </enumerate>

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B> holds for every
  <math|<blockpenalty>\<gtr\>0>. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:correspondence>
  states the existence theorem in the exact form of the formal statement
  (Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:A>), which asks only for some positive
  logarithmic slope, and deduces it from Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>.

  <paragraph|What is not claimed.> The Clay
  problem<nbsp><cite-detail|JaffeWitten2006|Ÿ4> asks for existence and a
  positive mass gap. The construction gives Wightman theories with unique
  vacuum, one in each phase; no mass gap is proved or used, and uniqueness of
  the vacuum gives no lower bound on the rest of the energy spectrum. The
  infinite-volume limit, the phase space and the selected phase are not shown
  to be unique, and independence from<nbsp><math|<blockpenalty>> is not
  proved. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:main-theorem> lists the limitations
  next to Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>.

  <subsection|Source of the result and of this text><label|sec:intro-source>

  The mathematical result is due to OpenAI's Lean construction at
  commit<nbsp><with|font-family|tt|3456e5e> of the repository
  <hlink|<with|font-family|tt|yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028><nbsp><cite|OpenAI2026YM>.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B> records that construction;
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:correspondence> states its top-level existence
  theorem and deduces it. Three kinds of checking are involved, and they
  should be kept apart.

  <\itemize>
    <item>The Lean kernel checks the formal proof. Its <hlink|kernel-check
    record|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/verification/joint-check.log>
    reports no unproved placeholders in the proved theorem and only
    propositional extensionality, classical choice and quotient soundness as
    axioms.

    <item>AI agents compared every numbered statement of this text with the
    Lean declarations used by the main theorem: hypotheses, definitions,
    quantifier order, normalizations and conclusions, with usage determined
    from the compiled dependencies. The correspondence table is published
    separately; the source repository supplies a <hlink|declaration-usage
    table|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/verification/declaration-usage.tsv>.

    <item>The prose arguments were written and reviewed by AI agents. They
    are not machine-checked and have not been refereed by human
    mathematicians.
  </itemize>

  A correct formal declaration does not by itself show that an English
  statement has kept its hypotheses and quantifiers, or that a prose proof
  proves that statement. This text was written entirely by AI agents under
  human direction, with separate writing and checking of statements and
  arguments, by the method described in <hlink|<em|How the manuscript is
  written>|https://github.com/scottnarmstrong/yang-mills-informalization/blob/main/manuscript/PROCESS.md>.
  The work is carried out in collaboration with Martin Hairer's
  <hlink|<em|humanise-yang-mills>|https://github.com/hairer/humanise-yang-mills>
  project.

  <subsection|Earlier work and the method><label|sec:intro-earlier>

  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:earlier-work> lists, for the main mechanisms of
  the proof, the closest earlier rigorous result and its use here. The
  comparison concerns how these operations are combined; it assigns none of
  them a new origin, and we make no priority claim for renormalization-group
  principles or physical predictions.

  <assign|arraystretch|<macro|1.12>>

  <big-table|<label|tab:earlier-work> <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-width|0.25par>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-width|0.42par>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-width|0.28par>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-hyphen|t>|<table|<row|<cell|Mechanism>|<cell|Earlier
  result>|<cell|Use here>>|<row|<cell|Nonlinear block averages, gauge fixing,
