  Fröhlich--Israel--Lieb--Simon<nbsp><cite-detail|FrohlichIsraelLiebSimon1978|Ÿ4,
  Theorems<nbsp>4.1--4.2>, lattice version for local
  functions<nbsp><cite-detail|Seiler1982|Theorem<nbsp>2.2>>|<cell|Volume-uniform
  bounds; reflection Hilbert spaces>>|<row|<cell|Analytic continuation of
  Euclidean correlations>|<cell|Osterwalder--Schrader<nbsp><cite|OsterwalderSchrader1973>,
  with the growth and boundary-value issues addressed
  in<nbsp><cite|OsterwalderSchrader1975>>|<cell|Reconstruction in every
  phase, from slab and distribution estimates; reflection positivity alone
  does not give it>>|<row|<cell|One-loop running>|<cell|Gross--Wilczek,
  Politzer<nbsp><cite|GrossWilczek1973|Politzer1973>; background-field
  coefficient<nbsp><cite-detail|Abbott1982|Ÿ3.3>>|<cell|The
  slope<nbsp><math|<slope>>, on the exact block map>>|<row|<cell|Operator
  products, trace anomaly, Taylor subtraction>|<cell|Wilson<nbsp><cite-detail|Wilson1969|ŸII>;
  all-order perturbative OPE in Euclidean Yang--Mills theory with
  gauge-invariant closure and associativity<nbsp><cite|FrobHolland2016>;
  Collins--Duncan--Joglekar<nbsp><cite-detail|CollinsDuncanJoglekar1977|(3.19)--(3.21)>;
  Brunetti--Fredenhagen<nbsp><cite-detail|BrunettiFredenhagen2000|Ÿ5.2>>|<cell|A
  nonperturbative OPE at each finite truncation, uniform in the phase; the
  trace law; contact extensions>>>>>|Mechanisms, earlier results and their
  use here.>

  The renormalization group is organized as in the method of Bauerschmidt,
  Brydges and Slade<nbsp><cite|BauerschmidtBrydgesSlade2019>: an explicitly
  defined map, a finite-dimensional flow of couplings, norms in which the
  remaining coordinate contracts, and a global trajectory with a tuned
  initial parameter. What requires proof here is the compatibility of the
  one-loop slope with the exact gauge block map, the coupling reset and the
  tuning to a common endpoint, followed by continuum identifications on one
  family: control of composite fields, restoration of rotations through
  stress-tensor fluxes, the trace law through all radii and product bounds
  uniform over phases.

  <subsection|Reading guide><label|sec:reading-guide>

  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:definitions> defines the objects needed to
  state Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>, which is
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:main-theorem>.
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:strategy> explains the route of the proof and
  the mechanisms that make it work, with a dependency map
  (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:dependency>).
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:continuum-results> state the principal results,
  each with the idea of its proof, and Section<nbsp><reference|sec:deduction>
  deduces Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>. Part<nbsp>II gives the proofs.
  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:principal-results> lists the principal results;
  their tags appear in statement titles. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>
  records the order in which constants are chosen. A first reading can skip
  the coordinates of the effective densities
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:density>), the marked decomposition
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:marked>) and
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. The index of notation follows the
  appendices.

  <big-table|<label|tab:principal-results>
