  <subsection|Notation and conventions><label|sec:conventions>

  Throughout, <math|<N>=<around|{|0,1,\<ldots\>|}>>, coordinate indices run
  from <math|1> to <math|4>, <math|e<rsub|a>> is the <math|a>th coordinate
  vector, and time is the fourth coordinate. For the relativistic statements
  write

  <\equation*>
    <minksquare><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|x<rsup|4>|)><rsup|2>-<around|\||<wide|x|\<vect\>>|\|><rsup|2>,<space|2em><forwardcone>=<around|{|\<b-up-p\>:<around|\||<wide|\<b-up-p\>|\<vect\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>|}><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Inner products are antilinear in the first variable, and spacetime
  integrals use Lebesgue measure. Correlations of arity zero are included;
  empty sums are zero and empty products are one. The group
  <math|<hypercubic>> consists of the signed permutation matrices, and
  <math|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|refl>>> denotes reflection in the
  <math|a>th coordinate.

  <section|Definitions><label|sec:definitions>

  This section defines the objects needed to state the main theorem: the
  gauge group and the Wilson measure, families of lattice theories and the
  convergence of their separated correlations, the local fields, the
  Euclidean properties of the limit, its field spaces, Wightman theories, and
  the quantities of the trace and product statements. The
  renormalization-group objects that construct the family are defined in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>, where they are used.

  <subsection|The gauge group and the Wilson measure><label|sec:wilson>

  The matrix normalization fixes both the Wilson energy and the coefficient
  of the logarithmic coupling flow.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Gauge normalization><label|def:D01> Fix
    <math|n<rsub|G>\<geqslant\>1> and a closed subgroup
    <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|n<rsub|G>|)>> with simple nonabelian real Lie
    algebra

    <\equation*>
      <g>=<around|{|X\<in\>M<rsub|n<rsub|G>><around|(|<C>|)>:exp
      <around|(|t*X|)>\<in\>G<text| for every
      >t\<in\><R>|}>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|X,Y|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>=-n<rsub|G><rsup|-1><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><around|(|X*Y|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For an orthonormal basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>><rsup|<g>>|)>>,
    put

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<dimG>>|<cell|=dim<rsub|<R>><g>,>|<cell|<casimir>>|<cell|=-<dimG><rsup|-1><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><math-up|tr><rsub|<g>><around*|(|<math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|\<alpha\>><rsup|<g>>|)><rsup|2>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|b<rsub|0>>|<cell|=<frac|11<casimir>|48*\<pi\><rsup|2>>,>|<cell|<slope>>|<cell|=2*b<rsub|0><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>
  </definition>

  Here <math|<math-up|ad><around|(|X|)>*Y=<around|[|X,Y|]>> and
  <math|<math-up|tr><rsub|<g>>> is the real operator trace
  on<nbsp><math|<g>>. Simplicity means <math|<around|[|<g>,<g>|]>\<ne\>0> and
  no proper nonzero real ideal; connectedness of<nbsp><math|G> is not
  assumed. The adjoint maps are skew-adjoint for this metric, so
  nonabelianity gives <math|<casimir>\<gtr\>0> and <math|<slope>\<gtr\>0>.
  For <math|u\<in\>G> the plaquette energy and the normalized chord are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-price-chord><wilsonterm><around|(|u|)>=1-n<rsub|G><rsup|-1><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><space|0.17em>u,<space|2em><chord><around|(|u|)>=n<rsub|G><rsup|-1/2>*<around|\<\|\|\>|u-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>,<space|2em><chord><around|(|u|)><rsup|2>=2<wilsonterm><around|(|u|)><space|0.17em>;
  </equation>

  both are invariant under conjugation and inversion, and the chord measures
  the distance of a plaquette holonomy from the identity.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|The normalization for <math|S*U<around|(|n|)>>><label|rem:SUn> For
    <math|G=S*U<around|(|n|)>> in its defining representation,
    <math|<dimG>=n<rsup|2>-1>, <math|<casimir>=2*n<rsup|2>> and
    <math|<slope>=11*n<rsup|2>/<around|(|12*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>. With the
    conventional Wilson coupling <math|\<beta\>=2*n/g<rsup|2>>, the running
    law<nbsp><eqref|eq:intro-running> reads
    <math|g<rsup|-2><around|(|a|)>\<sim\><around|(|11*n/24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>*log
    <around|(|1/a|)>>, the leading behaviour of the one-loop law
    <math|\<mu\>*<space|0.17em>d*g/d*\<mu\>=-<around|(|11*n/48*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>*g<rsup|3>>
    with <math|\<mu\>=1/a>.
  </remark>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Wilson measure><label|def:D02> For an integer
    <math|s\<geqslant\>1> and <math|\<beta\>\<in\><R>>,
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|s,\<beta\>>> is the probability
    measure<nbsp><eqref|eq:intro-wilson> on the configurations of
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|s>=<around|(|<Z>/s<Z>|)><rsup|4>>, with plaquette
    holonomies <math|U<rsub|y;a*b>>, Wilson energy
    <math|E<rsub|s><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|y><big|sum><rsub|a\<less\>b><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|y;a*b>|)>>
    and normalizing constant

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>>=<big|int>e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsub|s><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|y,a>d*h*<around|(|U*<around|(|y,a|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  The integrand of <math|Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>>> is continuous and strictly
  positive on the compact configuration space, so
  <math|0\<less\>Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>. A site gauge
  transformation <math|\<gamma\><rsub|<math-up|g>>:\<Lambda\><rsub|s>\<to\>G>
  acts by <math|U<rsup|\<gamma\><rsub|<math-up|g>>>*<around|(|y,a|)>=\<gamma\><rsub|<math-up|g>><around|(|y|)>*U*<around|(|y,a|)>*\<gamma\><rsub|<math-up|g>>*<around|(|y+e<rsub|a>|)><rsup|-1>>;
  it changes each plaquette holonomy by conjugation at its base point, so the
  energy is gauge invariant, and invariance of Haar measure under left and
  right multiplication makes the measure gauge invariant. The measure is also
  invariant under the lattice isometries <math|x\<mapsto\>h*x+b>,
  <math|h\<in\><hypercubic>>, which transport links and invert reversed ones
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-equations>).

  <subsection|Wilson families and their correlations><label|sec:continuum><label|sec:families>

  A Wilson family, indexed by <math|N,p\<in\><N>>, consists of strictly
  increasing positive integer sequences <math|<around|(|D<rsub|N>|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|L<rsub|p>|)>>, real couplings <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>, a
  set <math|\<cal-T\>> of insertion labels, and complex insertions
  <math|I<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v,y;U|)>>, functions of the configurations on
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>> for <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\>> and
  sites<nbsp><math|y>. The index <math|N> controls the spacing and <math|p>
  the physical volume:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-physical-placement>a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<mu\><rsub|N,p>=\<mu\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>,\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>,<space|2em><wide|y|^>=a<rsub|N><around*|(|r<rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>><around|(|y<rsup|a>|)>|)><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><space|0.17em>,
  </equation>

  where <math|r<rsub|s><around|(|j|)>> is the integer representative of
  <math|j\<in\><Z>/s<Z>> in <math|<around|(|-s/2,s/2|]>>. The lattice has
  <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>> sites per side at physical
  distance<nbsp><math|a<rsub|N>>, so its physical side
  is<nbsp><math|L<rsub|p>>; <math|<wide|y|^>> places a site in the centered
  period cell.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Lattice moments><label|def:D03> For
    <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>>, sites
    <math|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>><rsup|n>> and a test
    <math|f:<around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>\<to\><C>>, define

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<latmoment><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>>|<cell|=<big|int><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>I<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v<rsub|i>,y<rsub|i>;U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p><around|(|U|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<latsmear><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;f|)>>|<cell|=a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>><rsup|n>>f<around|(|<wide|y|^>|)><latmoment><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>
  </definition>

  The moment is the expectation of a product of insertions; the smeared
  correlation is its Riemann sum against a test, the factor
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>> being the lattice volume element. The main
  theorem concerns a family with the following regularity.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Q1. Regular Wilson family><label|def:Q1> A Wilson family is
    regular if (a) each insertion <math|U\<mapsto\>I<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v,y;U|)>>
    is continuous and gauge invariant; (b) for each <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\>>
    there is a finite <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|v>\<subset\><Z><rsup|4>>,
    independent of <math|N,p,y>, such that
    <math|I<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v,y;U|)>> depends only on the links with both
    endpoints in <math|y+\<Sigma\><rsub|v>> modulo
    <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>; (c) for some <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>/log <around|(|1/a<rsub|N>|)>\<to\>b<rsub|\<ast\>>>
    as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>.
  </definition>

  The physical diameter of the stencil <math|y+\<Sigma\><rsub|v>> tends to
  zero with <math|a<rsub|N>>, so the insertions are local. Condition<nbsp>(c)
  is a logarithmic growth law for the bare inverse coupling, uniform in the
  volume.

  Correlations are tested on separated configurations. For positions
  in<nbsp><math|<around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>> put

  <\equation*>
    <Conf|n>=<around|{|x:x<rsub|i>\<ne\>x<rsub|j><text| for
    >i\<ne\>j|}>,<space|2em><sepinf><around|(|x|)>=min<rsub|i\<ne\>j><around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|\<infty\>><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|<sepinf><around|(|x|)>=+\<infty\>> for <math|n\<leqslant\>1>. A
  <em|separated test> is a smooth function
  on<nbsp><math|<around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>> with compact closed support
  in<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>; it need not be a product of one-point tests. It is
  <em|<math|p>-admissible>, or supported inside volume<nbsp><math|p>, if its
  support lies in<nbsp><math|<around|(|<around|(|-L<rsub|p>/2,L<rsub|p>/2|)><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>.
  The following definition keeps the order of the two limits explicit.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Ordered convergence><label|def:D09> A Wilson family converges to
    kernels<nbsp><math|K> along<nbsp><math|P:<N>\<to\><N>> if, for every
    <math|n>, <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>> and separated
    test<nbsp><math|f>, the test is eventually
    <math|P<around|(|k|)>>-admissible and there are numbers
    <math|C<rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|UV>>*<around|(|v;f|)>> with

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<latsmear><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;f|)>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\>C<rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|UV>>*<around|(|v;f|)>*<space|1em><around|(|N\<to\>\<infty\>|)>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|at
      every admissible >p,>>|<row|<cell|C<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>><rsup|<math-up|UV>>*<around|(|v;f|)>>|<cell|\<longrightarrow\><big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*K<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x*<space|1em><around|(|k\<to\>\<infty\>|)><space|0.17em>.>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </align*>
  </definition>

  The inner limit uses the full sequence of spacings at every volume
  containing the support of the test; only the outer limit runs
  along<nbsp><math|P>. At volumes not containing the support,
  <math|C<rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|UV>>> is unconstrained. In
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B> the same<nbsp><math|P> serves every arity,
  list of labels and test (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:two-limits>).

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-1.svg|376.18144pt|78.456219pt||><label|fig:two-limits>
  >|The two limits of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09>. At every fixed
  volume the full sequence of spacings is used (vertical arrows); the volume
  limit then runs along the retained indices <math|P<around|(|k|)>>
  (circled), chosen once for all fields and tests.>

  <subsection|Local fields><label|sec:local-fields>

  Local fields are labelled by invariant polynomials in the Taylor
  coefficients of a connection. The labels use radial, or Fock\USchwinger,
  gauge, in which the radial component of the connection vanishes. In this
  gauge a smooth connection satisfies <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>t*<space|0.17em>x<rsup|b>*F<rsub|b*a>*<around|(|t*x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t>,
  so its homogeneous part of degree <math|k+1> is determined by the
  <math|k>th Taylor coefficients of the curvature at the origin.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Polynomial labels><label|def:D07> Let <math|\<cal-J\>> consist of
    formal <math|<g>>-valued arrays <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>\<cal-A\><rsub|k,a><around|(|x|)>>,
    with <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|k,a>> homogeneous of degree <math|k+1> and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>x<rsup|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|k,a><around|(|x|)>=0>. A
    coordinate of <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|k>> has bidegree
    <math|<around|(|k+2,1|)>>: engineering weight <math|k+2> and factor count
    one. Put

    <\equation*>
      V<rsub|d>=<C><around|[|\<cal-J\>|]><rsup|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>><rsub|<math-up|weight>\<leqslant\>d><space|0.17em>,
    </equation*>

    with fixed bihomogeneous bases <math|b<rsub|d,i>> of bidegree
    <math|<around|(|w<rsub|i>,k<rsub|i>|)>>, the supremum coefficient norm,
    conjugation and the induced <math|<hypercubic>> action.
  </definition>

  Curvature has weight two and each derivative adds one; for instance the
  curvature square <math|<curvaturesquare>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b,\<alpha\>>F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*b>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*b>>
  has a label of bidegree <math|<around|(|4,2|)>>. Three gradings play
  different roles below:

  <\itemize>
    <item>the engineering weight <math|w> fixes the power of the spacing in
    the Taylor normalization<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-section-Taylor>;

    <item>the factor count <math|k> fixes the scaling by the small-field
    threshold, through the operator <math|<factorcount>b<rsub|d,i>=k<rsub|i>*b<rsub|d,i>>
    and <math|t<rsup|<factorcount>>*b<rsub|d,i>=t<rsup|k<rsub|i>>*b<rsub|d,i>>;

    <item>the degree <math|d> of the space <math|V<rsub|d>> fixes the factor
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>> in the transfer of insertions under
    blocking and the physical prefactor <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>>.
  </itemize>

  A polynomial of weight at most <math|d\<leqslant\>e> lies in
  <math|V<rsub|d>> and in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|e>>; its two insertions differ,
  since the normalization below depends on the degree.

  Lattice holonomies are compared with smooth connection jets. For
  <math|<math-ss|j>=<around|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|k>|)><rsub|k>\<in\>\<cal-J\>>,
  put <math|\<cal-A\><rsup|\<leqslant\>d>=<big|sum><rsub|k+2\<leqslant\>d>\<cal-A\><rsub|k>>.
  The test holonomies at spacing <math|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>> are, on a
  positive link <math|<around|(|y,a|)>> with <math|y\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-test-holonomy>U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)><around|(|y,a|)>=u<around|(|1|)>,<space|2em>u<rprime|'><around|(|t|)>=u<around|(|t|)>*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*\<cal-A\><rsub|a><rsup|\<leqslant\>d>*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*<around|(|y+t*e<rsub|a>|)>|)>,<space|2em>u<around|(|0|)>=1<rsub|G><space|0.17em>,
  </equation>

  and the jet dilation is <math|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>=<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|k+2>*\<cal-A\><rsub|k>|)><rsub|k>>,
  so that <math|U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>=U*<around|(|1,<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>>.
  For a function<nbsp><math|O> of the links near the origin, its <em|Taylor
  map> <math|<taylormap><rsub|d><space|0.17em>O> is the part of weight at
  most<nbsp><math|d> of the Taylor series at <math|<math-ss|j>=0> of
  <math|<math-ss|j>\<mapsto\>O<around|(|U*<around|(|1,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1> constructs a <em|local section>
  <math|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|y;U|)>>, linear in
  <math|\<b-up-c\>\<in\>V<rsub|d>> and polynomial in the real and imaginary
  parts of the link entries on a fixed finite stencil
  <math|y+\<Sigma\><rsub|d>>; it is gauge invariant, commutes with complex
  conjugation, is <math|<hypercubic>> covariant, sends <math|\<b-1\>> to one,
  and satisfies, for <math|0\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>d>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-section-Taylor><around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|q>><localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|0;U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>|\|><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=0>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|q>>*\<b-up-c\><around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>|\|><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=0><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Equivalently, <math|<taylormap><rsub|d><localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>=\<b-up-c\>>:
  the local section is a right inverse of the Taylor map, which explains its
  name. A homogeneous label of weight <math|w> specifies the coefficient of
  <math|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|w>>.

  An <em|insertion label> is a pair <math|v=<around|(|d,\<b-up-c\>|)>> with
  <math|d\<in\><N>> and <math|\<b-up-c\>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>; the set of
  insertion labels is <math|\<cal-T\>=<big|sqcup><rsub|d>V<rsub|d>>. The
  insertions of the construction are normalized at a fixed physical scale.
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization> fixes, for each <math|d>, a
  reference level <math|<reflevel|d>>, the small-field threshold
  <math|t<rsub|N>> at the cutoff<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-scales>, and, along the
  trajectories of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>, an invertible linear map
  <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> of <math|V<rsub|d>>, the terminal map: it
  sends a coefficient vector given at the cutoff to the coefficient vector of
  the blocked insertion at level <math|<reflevel|d>>
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:marked>). For <math|N\<less\><reflevel|d>> it
  is a fixed invertible map.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Renormalized insertions><label|def:D08> For every <math|N,p>,
    <math|<around|(|d,\<b-up-c\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-T\>> and site<nbsp><math|y>,
    put

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|<around|(|d,\<b-up-c\>|)>,y;U|)>=O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|\<b-up-c\>,y;U|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d><localsection|d><around*|[|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|y;U|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The unit coefficient is <math|\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>=a<rsub|<reflevel|d>><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>.
  </definition>

  The inverse terminal map makes the transferred coefficient equal to the
  prescribed <math|\<b-up-c\>> at the reference level, so <math|\<b-up-c\>>
  is specified at the fixed physical scale <math|a<rsub|<reflevel|d>>>, in
  that level's lattice units; <math|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>>> measures
  each factor in units of the small-field threshold, and
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>> converts to physical units. Because blocking can
  mix labels of different weight within <math|V<rsub|d>>, the normalization
  is an invertible linear map of <math|V<rsub|d>> rather than a factor for
  each label. As a first check, the insertion of <math|\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>> is
  the constant one for <math|N\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>: the terminal map
  sends <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>> to
  <math|a<rsub|<reflevel|d>><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>), <math|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>>*\<b-1\>=\<b-1\>>,
  and the section sends <math|\<b-1\>> to one. Part<nbsp>II follows the
  curvature square through these maps (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-example>).

  <subsection|Euclidean properties><label|sec:euclidean-properties>

  A <em|table>, or family of Schwinger functions, assigns to each degree list
  <math|<around|(|d<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>> and <math|x\<in\><Conf|n>>
  a complex multilinear function <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x|)>> of
  <math|c<rsub|i>\<in\>V<rsub|d<rsub|i>>>. For
  <math|v<rsub|i>=<around|(|d<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>|)>> write
  <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>>, and for the word
  <math|w=<around|(|v<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>>
  write <math|S<around|(|w|)>>; the product of words concatenates their
  lists. Conjugation and <math|<hypercubic>> act on labels as in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>, and <math|h\<in\><hypercubic>> acts on
  positions linearly.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|E1--E3. Euclidean symmetries><label|def:E1-E3> For every <math|n>,
    degree list, <math|c> and <math|x\<in\><Conf|n>>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(E1)><with|the-label|E1|<label|it:E1>> <math|S<rsub|0>=1>,
      <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c<rsub|\<pi\>>;x<rsub|\<pi\>>|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x|)>>
      for every permutation <math|\<pi\>> of the slots, and
      <math|S<rsub|n+1>*<around|(|\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>,c;x<rsub|0>,x|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x|)>>;

      <item*|(E2)><with|the-label|E2|<label|it:E2>>
      <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|h*c;h*x|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x|)>> for
      <math|h\<in\><hypercubic>>, and <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|<wide|c|\<bar\>>;x|)>=<wide|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x|)>|\<bar\>>>;

      <item*|(E3)><with|the-label|E3|<label|it:E3>>
      <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x+z|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x|)>> for
      <math|z\<in\><R><rsup|4>>, with <math|x+z=<around|(|x<rsub|i>+z|)><rsub|i>>.
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  A word is positive-side for the axis<nbsp><math|a> when all its positions
  have positive <math|a>th coordinate. For a word <math|w>, let
  <math|\<theta\><rsub|a>*w=<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|refl>><around|(|w<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>>,
  where <math|w<rsup|\<ast\>>> reverses the word and applies conjugation to
  its labels, and <math|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|refl>>> reflects
  positions and acts on labels through<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|E4--E5. Reflection positivity><label|def:E4-E5>

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(E4)><with|the-label|E4|<label|it:E4>> For every
      axis<nbsp><math|a>, positive-side separated words<nbsp><math|w<rsub|i>>
      and <math|z<rsub|i>\<in\><C>>, <math|<big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|z|\<bar\>><rsub|i>*z<rsub|j>*S*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|a><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>w<rsub|j>|)>\<geqslant\>0>.

      <item*|(E5)><with|the-label|E5|<label|it:E5>> The same positivity holds
      for finite sums of words smeared against joint smooth compactly
      supported tests on positive-side separated configurations, and every
      mixed pairing is absolutely integrable.
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  For a fixed label list, <math|D<rsub|x><rsup|k>> is the <math|k>th Fréchet
  derivative in the positions, with the product Euclidean norm. The last
  condition controls the approach to collisions uniformly over translations
  and over configurations of large diameter.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|E6--E7. Euclidean regularity><label|def:E6-E7>

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(E6)><with|the-label|E6|<label|it:E6>> Each
      <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;\<cdot\>|)>> is smooth
      on<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>.

      <item*|(E7)><with|the-label|E7|<label|it:E7>> For all <math|n,d,c,k>
      there are <math|C*<around|(|n,d,c,k|)>\<geqslant\>0> and an integer
      <math|A*<around|(|n,d,c,k|)>\<geqslant\>0> such that
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsub|x><rsup|k>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|-A>>
      whenever <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1> and
      <math|<sepinf><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>r>.
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  <subsection|Field spaces and phases><label|sec:field-spaces>

  A field is described in three successive ways. An insertion label
  <math|<around|(|d,c|)>> specifies a lattice insertion. Its class
  <math|<around|[|c|]>> identifies labels with the same separated
  correlations: two labels represent the same field when every spectator sees
  the same answer. The response <math|\<iota\><rsub|d><around|[|c|]>> records
  those correlations against every spectator list and places fields of all
  degrees in one space. Write <math|v<around|(|x|)>=<around|(|v<rsub|1><around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,v<rsub|n><around|(|x<rsub|n>|)>|)>>
  for a spectator word.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Observable fields><label|def:D10> For a table<nbsp><math|S> put

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>>|<cell|=<around*|{|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>:S<rsub|n+1>*<around|(|c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>=0<text|
      for every >n,v<text| and ><around|(|x<rsub|0>,x|)>\<in\><Conf|n+1>|}>,>>|<row|<cell|F<rsub|d>>|<cell|=V<rsub|d>/<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>,<space|2em><wide|<around|[|c|]>|\<bar\>>=<around|[|<wide|c|\<bar\>>|]>,<space|2em>h<around|[|c|]>=<around|[|h*c|]>*<space|1em><around|(|h\<in\><hypercubic>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<iota\><rsub|d><around|[|c|]><around|(|v;x<rsub|0>,x|)>>|<cell|=S<rsub|n+1>*<around|(|c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-L\>=<math-up|span><big|cup><rsub|d>\<iota\><rsub|d>*F<rsub|d><space|0.17em>,>>>>
    </align*>

    with conjugation-invariant quotient norms on the finite-dimensional
    spaces<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>>.
  </definition>

  By<nbsp><reference|it:E2>, <math|<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>> is invariant
  under conjugation and<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, so both actions on
  <math|F<rsub|d>> are well defined. By<nbsp><reference|it:E1>, correlations
  do not depend on representatives in any slot: for
  <math|\<b-A\>=<around|(|<around|[|c<rsub|1>|]>,\<ldots\>,<around|[|c<rsub|n>|]>|)>>
  put <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|c;x|)>>. The
  unit field is <math|<unitfield|d>=<around|[|\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>|]>>. Write
  <math|\<bbb-F\>=<big|sqcup><rsub|d>F<rsub|d>>, <math|deg
  <around|(|d,A|)>=d> and <math|\<iota\>*<around|(|d,A|)>=\<iota\><rsub|d>*A>;
  the elements of <math|\<cal-L\>> are the field labels.

  <paragraph|Phases.> The main theorem writes the limiting table as an
  average <math|S=<big|int><rsub|<phases>>S<rsup|\<xi\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>
  of tables <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> on the same field spaces, over a compact
  metric space <math|<phases>> with a fully supported probability
  measure<nbsp><math|\<nu\>>; the points<nbsp><math|\<xi\>> are the phases. A
  phase is not a set of configurations: Section<nbsp><reference|sec:phases>
  obtains the phases as the characters of a commutative algebra of operators.

  <subsection|Wightman theories><label|sec:wightman-defs>

  Write <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|n>=\<cal-S\><around|(|<R><rsup|4*n>;<C>|)>> for
  the Schwartz space and <math|\<cal-S\>=\<cal-S\><rsub|1>>. Let
  <math|\<cal-P\>=S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>\<ltimes\><R><rsup|4>> be the
  proper orthochronous Poincaré group, where
  <math|S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>> consists of the linear maps of
  determinant one that preserve <math|<minksquare>> and <math|<forwardcone>>;
  write <math|\<gamma\>=<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<gamma\>>,z<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>>
  and <math|<around|(|\<gamma\>\<cdot\>f|)><around|(|x|)>=f*<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<gamma\>><rsup|-1>*<around|(|x-z<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>|)>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Relativistic datum><label|def:D14a><label|def:D14b> A relativistic
    datum consists of a complex Hilbert space <math|\<cal-H\>>, a complex
    vector space <math|\<cal-L\>> of field labels, a linear subspace
    <math|\<cal-D\>\<subset\>\<cal-H\>>, a vacuum
    <math|\<Omega\>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>, and fields
    <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>:\<cal-D\>\<to\>\<cal-D\>>, linear
    operators that are complex bilinear in
    <math|<around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>\<in\>\<cal-L\>\<times\>\<cal-S\>>; an
    antilinear map <math|\<ell\>\<mapsto\>\<ell\><rsup|\<ast\>>>; strongly
    continuous unitary translations <math|<math-ss|T><around|(|z|)>>,
    <math|z\<in\><R><rsup|4>>; a Borel projection-valued measure
    <math|<math-ss|E>> on<nbsp><math|<R><rsup|4>> with finite diagonal
    measures <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<psi\>><around|(|B|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,<math-ss|E><around|(|B|)>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>;
    a strongly continuous unitary representation <math|<math-ss|U>>
    of<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>> with <math|<math-ss|U><around|(|1,z|)>=<math-ss|T><around|(|z|)>>;
    and linear bijections <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|lab>><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>>
    of<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\>>.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Wightman theory><label|def:wightman> A relativistic datum is a
    Wightman theory if:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(W0)><with|the-label|W0|<label|def:W0>>
      <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,<math-ss|T><around|(|z|)>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*\<b-up-p\>\<cdot\>z>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|\<psi\>><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>>
      for all <math|\<psi\>,z>, with the Euclidean pairing;
      <math|<math-ss|E><around|(|<R><rsup|4>\<setminus\><forwardcone>|)>=0>;
      and every vector fixed by all translations lies in <math|<C>\<Omega\>>.

      <item*|(W1)><with|the-label|W1|<label|def:W1>>
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>>=1>; <math|\<cal-D\>> is
      dense and spanned by the vectors <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|1>,f<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|k>,f<rsub|k>|)>*\<Omega\>>,
      <math|k\<geqslant\>0>; each <math|f\<mapsto\>\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>*\<psi\>>
      is continuous from <math|\<cal-S\>> to<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\>>; and
      <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>*\<psi\>,\<chi\>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|\<ast\>>,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>*\<chi\>|\<rangle\>>>
      for <math|\<psi\>,\<chi\>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>.

      <item*|(W2)><with|the-label|W2|<label|def:W2>>
      <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|lab>>> is a representation;
      <math|<math-ss|U><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>*\<cal-D\>\<subset\>\<cal-D\>>,
      <math|<math-ss|U><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\>> and
      <math|<math-ss|U><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>*\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>*\<psi\>=\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|lab>><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>*\<ell\>,\<gamma\>\<cdot\>f|)><math-ss|U><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>*\<psi\>>
      for <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-D\>>.

      <item*|(W3)><with|the-label|W3|<label|def:W3>>
      <math|<around|[|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\><rprime|'>,h|)>|]>*\<psi\>=0>
      for <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-D\>> whenever
      <math|<minksquare><around|(|x-y|)>\<less\>0> for all
      <math|x\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f>,
      <math|y\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>h>.
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Faithful labels><label|def:D14c> The labels are faithful if, for
    every nonzero <math|\<ell\>\<in\>\<cal-L\>>, some <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>>
    has <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>\<ne\>0>.
  </definition>

  Reconstruction uses one function in two limits: at imaginary times it
  equals the Euclidean correlation, and approaching real times from the
  ordered tube gives the Wightman distributions. We first specify the
  continuation, then its boundary value.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Ordered-time continuation><label|def:D15> For a
    phase<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, put <math|\<cal-Z\><rsub|n>=<around|{|z\<in\><C><rsup|n>:<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z<rsub|1>\<less\>\<cdots\>\<less\><math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z<rsub|n>|}>>.
    A continuation <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|\<b-A\>;z,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>>,
    for <math|\<b-A\>\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>>, is holomorphic in
    <math|z\<in\>\<cal-Z\><rsub|n>> at each fixed real spatial configuration
    and satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|\<b-A\>;<around|(|i*t<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>=S<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;<around|(|<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|i>,t<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>|)>*<space|2em><around|(|t<rsub|1>\<less\>\<cdots\>\<less\>t<rsub|n>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Boundary condition><label|def:D14f> For a phase <math|\<xi\>>, a
    relativistic datum <math|<around|(|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<xi\>>,\<cal-L\>,\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>,\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>,\<ldots\>|)>>,
    the maps <math|\<iota\><rsub|d>> and a continuation
    <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>>, require, for every <math|n>,
    <math|\<b-A\>\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>>, real
    <math|h<rsub|1>\<less\>\<cdots\>\<less\>h<rsub|n>> and
    <math|f<rsub|i>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, that the integrand below be integrable
    for every <math|r\<gtr\>0> and

    <\multline*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0>
      <big|int><math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|\<b-A\>;<around|(|x<rsub|i><rsup|4>+i*r*h<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>,<around|(|<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|i>|)><rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i>f<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>>|<row|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><around|(|A<rsub|1>|)>,f<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><around|(|A<rsub|n>|)>,f<rsub|n>|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </multline*>
  </definition>

  A phase <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> is the <em|Euclidean continuation> of a
  Wightman theory when continuations satisfying
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D15> and<nbsp><reference|def:D14f> exist
  for that theory and the response maps of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>.

  <subsection|The trace and product statements><label|sec:short-distance-defs>

  For a phase <math|\<xi\>> and a field <math|\<Theta\>\<in\>F<rsub|4>>, the
  time-axis two-point function is

  <\equation*>
    <tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>=S<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;-r*e<rsub|4>/2,r*e<rsub|4>/2|)>*<space|2em><around|(|r\<gtr\>0|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The trace law is compared with free fields through the following class. Fix
  <math|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>\<geqslant\>0>, integers
  <math|j<rsub|h>,k<rsub|E>\<geqslant\>0> and a continuous real
  <math|h<rsub|<math-up|mom>>> on <math|<R><rsup|3>> with
  <math|h<rsub|<math-up|mom>>*<around|(|t*\<b-up-k\>|)>=t<rsup|j<rsub|h>>*h<rsub|<math-up|mom>><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>>
  for <math|t\<gtr\>0>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Finite Wick profiles><label|def:D17> Put
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>=<sqrt|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>><rsup|2>+<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|><rsup|2>>>
    and, for <math|r\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>,k<rsub|E>,h<rsub|<math-up|mom>>><around|(|r|)>=<big|int><rsub|<R><rsup|3>><frac|\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)><rsup|k<rsub|E>>*h<rsub|<math-up|mom>><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>|2*\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>>*e<rsup|-r*\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-k\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    A finite Wick profile is a function <math|W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>><around|(|r|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|N>c<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|j=1><rsup|n<rsub|i>>K<rsub|m<rsub|i*j>,k<rsub|i*j>,h<rsub|i*j>><around|(|r|)>>,
    <math|r\<gtr\>0>, with <math|c<rsub|i>\<in\><C>> and finitely many terms
    and factors of this kind; empty products are one.
  </definition>

  The momentum integrals converge absolutely for <math|r\<gtr\>0>. For a free
  scalar field of mass <math|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>> the time-axis two-point
  function is <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-3>*K<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>,0,1><around|(|r|)>>;
  derivatives give momentum factors, and Wick contractions give finite sums
  of products at the same radius.

  The product expansion replaces a cluster of nearby fields by fields at one
  point, inside correlations with distant spectators. For finite index sets
  <math|I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>,I<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>, write
  <math|S<rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>,x;\<b-B\>,y|)>> for the
  correlation of the concatenated lists, in a fixed enumeration of
  <math|I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>\<sqcup\>I<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>, where
  <math|\<zeta\>\<in\><phases>\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>> indexes a
  phase or the mixture, <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>=S>. A pair
  <math|<around|(|x,o|)>>, with <math|x\<in\><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)><rsup|I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>>
  and <math|o\<in\><R><rsup|4>>, is an <math|<around|(|r,M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>,\<eta\>|)>>-cluster
  if <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-o|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>*r> and
  <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i<rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<eta\>*r>
  for distinct slots; spectators <math|y\<in\><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)><rsup|I<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>>
  are <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>>-clear if
  <math|<around|\||o-y<rsub|j>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>>
  and <math|<around|\||y<rsub|j>-y<rsub|j<rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>>
  for distinct slots. The expansion uses finite channel sets
  <math|<channels|j>>, real fields <math|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>\<in\>F<rsub|j>>
  forming bases of conjugation-invariant complements of <math|F<rsub|j-1>> in
  <math|F<rsub|j>> (<math|F<rsub|-1>=0>), and coefficient functions
  <math|c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x,o|)>>, all fixed once before
  spectators, states and truncations; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-products>
  describes how they are read off. Put <math|<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>=<big|sum><rsub|i>deg
  A<rsub|i>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Product remainder><label|def:D18> For <math|D\<in\><N>>,
    <math|\<b-A\>\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>> and
    <math|\<b-B\>\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|I<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>>, put

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<zeta\>>=S<rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>,x;\<b-B\>,y|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|D><big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>>c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x,o|)>*S<rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>><around|(|o|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|y|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  If <math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>*r\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>>, the
  concatenated positions are separated, <math|<around|\||x<rsub|i>-y<rsub|j>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>-M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>*r\<gtr\>0>,
  so the remainder is defined in the small-radius range used below. A
  <em|contact extension> of <math|S<rsub|n><rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;\<cdot\>|)>>
  is a distribution on <math|<R><rsup|4*n>> whose value on every separated
  test<nbsp><math|f> is <math|<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|n><rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>;
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT> constructs one by an explicit subtraction.

  <section|The main theorem><label|sec:main-theorem><label|sec:main-theorems>

  The main theorem concerns one Wilson family<nbsp><math|\<cal-W\>>, fixed
  before any sequence of volumes is prescribed: the renormalized insertions
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08> on the bare couplings of the
  multiscale theorem (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>), for a fixed block
  penalty. These couplings are tuned, volume by volume, so that the running
  coupling reaches one common endpoint coupling<nbsp><math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>
  at the unit scale (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trajectories>).
  Clause<nbsp>(ii) also packages the objects of clauses<nbsp>(iii)\U(v) as
  hierarchy data satisfying the conditions Q2\UQ6 of the formal statement
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:correspondence>); there <math|\<nu\>> is the
  measure of the phase decomposition (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH>),
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> the selected phase
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:phases>) and <math|\<Theta\>> the centered
  stress trace (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D16>), and
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:deduction> assembles the hierarchy data from
  these objects.

  <assign|maintheorem-nr|1>

  <\maintheorem>
    <dueto|Main theorem><label|thm:B> For every gauge group<nbsp><math|G> of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01> and<nbsp><math|<blockpenalty>\<gtr\>0>,
    the renormalized insertions (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>) on the
    penalty-<math|<blockpenalty>> trajectories of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT> form a regular Wilson
    family<nbsp><math|\<cal-W\>> with <math|a<rsub|N>*\<downarrow\>*0>,
    <math|L<rsub|p>*\<uparrow\>*\<infty\>> and <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>/log
    <around|(|1/a<rsub|N>|)>\<to\><slope>> uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>.
    Moreover:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)><with|the-label|i|<label|it:B1>> For every<nbsp><math|p>,
      <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>> and <math|p>-admissible
      separated<nbsp><math|f>, <math|<latsmear><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;f|)>\<to\><big|int>f*M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>>
      as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>.

      <item*|(ii)><with|the-label|ii|<label|it:B2>> For every strictly
      increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>> there are a strictly
      increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>>, a table<nbsp><math|S> satisfying
      E1--E7 and hierarchy data over<nbsp><math|\<cal-W\>> satisfying Q2--Q6
      with <math|\<cal-W\>\<to\>S> and <math|\<cal-W\>\<to\>S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>\<circ\>\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>>>
      along<nbsp><math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>>. In (iii)--(v),
      <math|S> is this table, <math|F<rsub|d>=V<rsub|d>/<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>>,
      and <math|\<nu\>>, <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>, <math|\<Theta\>> are
      those of these hierarchy data.

      <item*|(iii)><with|the-label|iii|<label|it:B3>>
      <math|S=<big|int><rsub|<phases>>S<rsup|\<xi\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>
      with <math|\<nu\>> a fully supported probability measure on a compact
      metric space; each<nbsp><math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> is the Euclidean
      continuation of a faithful Wightman theory with unique vacuum.

      <item*|(iv)><with|the-label|iv|<label|it:B4>> A conserved stress tensor
      is normalized by its shell fluxes (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>);
      in the selected phase<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> its centered
      trace<nbsp><math|\<Theta\>> has

      <\equation*>
        r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>\<to\>3<dimG>/\<pi\><rsup|4>,
      </equation*>

      and <math|<tracetwopoint>> agrees with no finite Wick profile on any
      interval<nbsp><math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>>.

      <item*|(v)><with|the-label|v|<label|it:B5>> The
      fields<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>> have an operator product expansion with
      spectator-independent coefficients and

      <\equation*>
        <around|\||\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<zeta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|D+1-<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>-\<varepsilon\>>,
      </equation*>

      with the quantifiers and uniformity of
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP>, and contact extensions from the same
      prescription.
    </enumerate>
  </maintheorem>

  The kernel <math|M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>> of clause<nbsp>(i) is the
  fixed-volume limit of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>, the convergence
  <math|\<cal-W\>\<to\>S> in clause<nbsp>(ii) is that of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09>, and
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>>*<around|(|d,\<b-up-c\>|)>=<around|(|d,<around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>|)>>
  sends an insertion label to its field. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:deduction>
  proves the theorem from the principal results of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:continuum-results>.

  <paragraph|Order of choices.> The gauge group, its realization and the
  block penalty are fixed first. The lattice family, with its couplings and
  insertions, is then fixed before the volume
  sequence<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>>. The refinement<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>>,
  the table<nbsp><math|S>, the field spaces, the phases, the selected phase,
  the relativistic theories, the product coefficients and the trace field may
  depend on<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>>. One refinement serves every arity, every
  list of insertion labels and every separated test. The lattice limit in
  clause<nbsp>(ii) is the mixture<nbsp><math|S>; the relativistic theories of
  clause<nbsp>(iii) belong to the phases, and the trace law of
  clause<nbsp>(iv) is stated in the selected phase.

  <paragraph|What the theorem does not assert.> It does not prove a mass gap,
  that is, a positive lower bound on the nonzero energies; uniqueness of the
  vacuum means only that the zero-energy space is one-dimensional. It does
  not assert that the infinite-volume limit, the phase space or the selected
  phase is unique, nor that different block penalties give the same theory,
  and it does not take the spacing and volume limits simultaneously. It does
  not show that widely separated groups of observations become independent in
  the mixed state, or that any one phase is itself a limit of lattice states.
  The lattice correlations converge against separated tests; the contact
  extensions are constructed afterwards from the separated correlations, are
  not limits of unsubtracted lattice correlations at coincident points, and
  are not asserted to be unique, positive or to satisfy contact Ward
  identities. The product expansion is an estimate at each finite truncation
  degree, not the convergence of a series as that degree tends to infinity.
  The trace law is asserted in the selected phase only, and it excludes the
  finite Wick profiles of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D17>; a generalized
  free field with the spectral measure of<nbsp><math|\<Theta\>> has the same
  two-point function.

  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:correspondence> states
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:A>, the existence statement in the exact form
  of the formal statement, with its conditions Q0\UQ6, and deduces it from
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B> with any <math|<blockpenalty>\<gtr\>0>.

  <section|Strategy of the proof><label|sec:strategy><label|sec:intro-outline>

  The proof fixes one lattice family, takes its two limits and identifies the
  resulting fields; every estimate applies to this same family.
  Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:dependency> shows the order in which the
  principal results use one another. This section explains the mechanisms
  that make the main steps work; Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>\U<reference|sec:continuum-results>
  state the results with the ideas of their proofs.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-2.svg|397.443118pt|274.13383pt||><label|fig:dependency>
  >|Dependency map. An arrow means that the later result uses the earlier
  one; the trace law and the products branch off the main route. The
  coordinate reflection spaces and slab bounds use only the volume limit and
  the field calculus; they enter the rotation argument before continuous
  rotations are available, so the order is not circular. The renormalization
  group runs only from the cutoff to the unit scale, and no mass gap is
  used.>

  <subsection|Why the ultraviolet induction closes><label|sec:closes>

  Blocking integrates the fine links against a probability kernel and returns
  an exact density for the coarse links (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>);
  it lowers the level from <math|m+1> to <math|m> and multiplies the spacing
  by the odd block ratio <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>>
  (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:scales>). The density is written as a curvature
  action with the running coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|m>>, a vacuum scalar,
  regular activities on small-field supports and forced activities covering
  every large-field site (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:density>). The equality
  is exact; the estimates control the coordinates in which it is written.
  Five points make the induction close.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-3.svg|327.112747pt|52.012055pt||><label|fig:scales>
  >|Levels and the direction of integration. Level <math|m> has spacing
  <math|a<rsub|m>=D<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>; blocking runs from the fine level
  <math|m+1> to the coarse level <math|m>, so
  <math|a<rsub|m+1>\<less\>a<rsub|m>>, and the running coupling decreases by
  about <math|<slope>log \<ell\><rsub|m>>, so
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|m+1>\<gtr\>\<beta\><rsub|m>> to leading order. The
  ratios grow: <math|log \<ell\><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> and <math|log
  \<ell\><rsub|m>/log D<rsub|m>\<to\>0>. The endpoint coupling
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> is prescribed at level zero, the reference
  unit of length, which is not a measured mass scale.>

  <paragraph|One coupling to tune.> In the local radial-gauge expansion of a
  gauge-invariant local term (Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>\U<reference|sec:partII-D>),
  the contributions of engineering weight at most four are a constant, terms
  linear in the curvature and its derivatives, and a quadratic curvature
  term. The constant goes into the vacuum scalar. The linear terms vanish,
  since an <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant linear function on the
  simple algebra<nbsp><math|<g>> is zero; in particular there is no mass
  term. Simplicity of<nbsp><math|<g>> and the hypercubic and reflection
  symmetries leave one invariant quadratic curvature term, whose coefficient
  the construction moves into the coupling. The regular activities that
  remain vanish at the flat configuration and, on supports that do not wrap
  around the torus, have zero Hessian along affine directions
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>). This subtraction plays the role of
  the localization operator of Bauerschmidt, Brydges and
  Slade<nbsp><cite-detail|BauerschmidtBrydgesSlade2019|Ÿ5.2>.

  <paragraph|The scales.> Write, at one level, <math|b=<refbeta><rsub|m>> for
  the reference coupling, <math|M=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  b|)><rsup|2>|)>=<around|(|log b|)><rsup|log log b>>, which grows faster
  than every power of <math|log b> and slower than every power
  of<nbsp><math|b>, the small-field threshold <math|t=b<rsup|-1/2>*M> and the
  regular allowance <math|\<varepsilon\>=b<rsup|-11/10>>
  (equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-scales>). Plaquette fluctuations at
  coupling<nbsp><math|b> have size <math|b<rsup|-1/2>>, so <math|t> is that
  size enlarged by<nbsp><math|M>, and <math|t\<to\>0>. The comparisons that
  fix these choices are, for <math|\<beta\>\<asymp\>b>:

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|5|5|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Comparison>|<cell|Consequence>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<beta\>*t<rsup|5>\<asymp\>b<rsup|-3/2>*M<rsup|5>=o<around|(|\<varepsilon\>|)>>>|<cell|the
    Wilson energy matched through fourth order leaves a regular error inside
    the allowance>>|<row|<cell|<math|t<rsup|3>/\<varepsilon\>=b<rsup|-2/5>*M<rsup|3>\<to\>0>>|<cell|a
    cubic term with bounded coefficient at the threshold fits inside the
    allowance>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<varepsilon\>/t<rsup|2>=b<rsup|-1/10>*M<rsup|-2>\<to\>0>>|<cell|a
    quadratic term at the threshold does not: residual quadratic coefficients
    go into the coupling>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>\<asymp\>M<rsup|2>>>|<cell|a
    plaquette above the threshold costs a factor of order
    <math|e<rsup|-c*M<rsup|2>>>, which is <math|o<around|(|b<rsup|-k>|)>> for
    every fixed<nbsp><math|k>>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The forced allowance <math|e<rsup|-M<rsup|1/5>>> is
  <math|o<around|(|b<rsup|-k>|)>> for every fixed<nbsp><math|k> but much
  larger than <math|e<rsup|-c*M<rsup|2>>>; this leaves room for the
  combinatorial losses of the forced terms.

  <paragraph|The cancellations.> Two cancellations make the regular bound
  contract. First, an incoming regular activity, written in the coarse field,
  loses its constant, linear and affine quadratic terms by flat
  normalization, colour invariance and the affine Hessian condition; the
  remaining terms of its principal part carry at least
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-5>> (on large supports after conversion of the
  support weight), against <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|4>> fine anchors above
  a coarse anchor, a net factor <math|V<rsub|j>/\<ell\><rsub|m>> with
  <math|V<rsub|j>> fixed before the ratio, while the comparison remainder has
  relative size <math|b<rprime|'><rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>, where
  <math|b<rprime|'>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>> is the reference coupling of the
  fine level (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited>). Second, the new terms
  produced by the integration are connected local terms whose bound from the
  connected expansion is only <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|b<rprime|'>|)>*b<rprime|'><rsup|-7/16>>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>), where <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>> and
  <math|P<rsub|j>> denotes functions of subpower growth fixed with the stage;
  this is far from the allowance <math|<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-11/10>>. Most
  of these terms are already small enough: the connected expansion still
  converges when every elementary factor is weighted by
  <math|b<rprime|'><rsup|3/8>>, so terms with three or more factors, or with
  a large-field overlap, are at most <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|b<rprime|'>|)>*b<rprime|'><rsup|-19/16>\<leqslant\>b<rprime|'><rsup|-23/20>>,
  and long paths, large supports and products with an incoming activity also
  stay below <math|b<rprime|'><rsup|-23/20>>. For the rest, the improvement
  comes from evaluating totals rather than separate terms.
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D> selects a finite family of low-order
  connected terms (at most two connected products of at most two factors,
  short paths, no incoming activity), invariant under translations
  and<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>; the unselected low-order terms have weighted
  norm at most <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>>; every complete read
  support of these terms, relative to a site receiving a share, lies within
  distance<nbsp><math|R> of that site. Let <math|F<rsub|y>> be the sum of the
  shares at the site<nbsp><math|y>, a connected term being shared equally
  among the sites of its support and a path term assigned to its anchor. For
  a radius <math|w\<gtr\>4*R> fixed in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>,
  with <math|2*<around|(|w+5|)>> less than the side of<nbsp><math|\<Lambda\>>
  by the period requirements of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>, let
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> be the torus of side <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>>,
  <math|i=0,\<ldots\>,4>, let <math|V<rsub|i,x>> be the configuration on
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> obtained from the configuration
  near<nbsp><math|x> by repeated reflection, let
  <math|F<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|y>> be the same local formula
  on<nbsp><math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> with its flat value subtracted, and put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:five-torus>\<lambda\><rsub|i>=<frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>|2<rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>4!>,<space|2em>G<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>F<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|y><around|(|V<rsub|i,x>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Five-torus localization><label|lem:five-torus> For the selected
    low-order family, the function <math|G<rsub|x>> depends only on the links
    within distance <math|w+5> of<nbsp><math|x>, and, in the local derivative
    seminorm of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|b<rprime|'>|)>*<around*|(|b<rprime|'><rsup|-23/20>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    On configurations without large-field sites,
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|y>F<rsub|y>=c\|\<Lambda\>\|+<big|sum><rsub|x>G<rsub|x>>
    for a constant<nbsp><math|c>.
  </lemma>

  This is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>, which also controls the
  correction near large-field sites. Two facts give it. On each auxiliary
  torus the total of the selected family is, up to the omitted terms, a
  normalized local integral, in which the flat value, the classical cubic and
  quartic terms and the one-loop quadratic term are subtracted exactly and
  Gaussian parity removes a lone cubic term; what remains has at least three
  factors of size <math|b<rprime|'><rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>> or a further
  Taylor gain. To recover a local function from totals, classify the sites of
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> by their distances from the reflection faces,
  evaluating the summand at<nbsp><math|y> on the configuration
  <math|V<rsub|i,x-<wide|y|~>>> reflected about <math|x-<wide|y|~>>, with
  <math|<wide|y|~>> the integer representative of<nbsp><math|y>.
  Site<nbsp><math|y> of that cube then sees the configuration
  near<nbsp><math|x>, so each class contributes a fixed local value, with a
  multiplicity that is a polynomial in<nbsp><math|i> of degree at most four;
  degree four occurs only for the interior class, with leading
  coefficient<nbsp><math|2<rsup|4>>. Since
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>i<rsup|k>>
  is <math|0> for <math|k\<less\>4> and <math|4>! for <math|k=4>, the weights
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>> cancel every boundary class and keep one copy of
  the interior value <math|F<rsub|x>-F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>>; summing over
  <math|x>, which turns <math|x-<wide|y|~>> back into<nbsp><math|x>, gives
  the identity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:five-torus>. As
  <math|23/20\<gtr\>11/10>, the localized terms fit the allowance with room
  to spare.

  <paragraph|Large fields.> The estimates keep the configurations with large
  fields; they never replace the measure by its small-field restriction. A
  forced activity vanishes unless its support contains a large-field site,
  and the large-field lower bound on the quadratic energy
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>) gives a factor
  <math|e<rsup|-c*\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-c*M<rsup|2>/2>> for
  each element of a selection (large fine plaquettes, mismatched coarse edges
  and flagged coarse sites), which pays for the polynomial and combinatorial
  losses of the forced terms.

  <paragraph|Growing block ratios.> The drift error of one step is bounded by
  a constant <math|\<omega\><rsup|<math-up|RG>><rsub|j>> depending only on
  the stage <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>> (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>). With a
  fixed block ratio<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>, the accumulated drift bound
  <math|N*\<omega\><rsup|<math-up|RG>>> would be of the same order as the
  main term <math|<slope>log D<rsub|N>=N<slope>log \<ell\>>. The construction
  instead lets the ratios grow, <math|log \<ell\><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> and
  <math|log \<ell\><rsub|m>/log D<rsub|m>\<to\>0>, and chooses them with
  <math|\<omega\><rsup|<math-up|RG>><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>j<rsup|-2>*log
  \<ell\><rsub|m>>; the summed errors are then <math|o<around|(|log
  D<rsub|m>|)>> and the exact one-loop slope survives
  (equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-drift-sum>). Each level carries a stage
  <math|J<rsub|m>>, with <math|4\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>+1>
  and <math|J<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>, which fixes the derivative order, the
  other estimate constants and the ratio <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>=\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>.
  A stage may persist through many levels: entry into the next stage is
  delayed until the coupling and the period meet its requirements. Every
  fixed derivative order is therefore eventually available, while each step
  uses finitely many constants. All estimate constants of the induction are
  uniform in the volume; some are fixed before the block ratios and hence
  uniform in the ratio, the others may depend on a stage whose ratio is
  already fixed (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>).

  <subsection|Tuning, uniqueness and convergence><label|sec:tuning-convergence>

  Three different tasks concern the cutoff, and they are not interchangeable.

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Task>|<cell|Mechanism>|<cell|What
    it does not supply>>|<row|<cell|Reach the endpoint coupling
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> at level zero>|<cell|Continuous shooting
    and bracketing (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>)>|<cell|Uniqueness of
    the shooting parameter>>|<row|<cell|Identify the limits at a fixed
    level>|<cell|Weighted contraction toward the common endpoint
    (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:fixed-level>)>|<cell|Convergence of the
    vacuum scalars>>|<row|<cell|Remove the cutoff at fixed
    volume>|<cell|Fixed-level convergence and a uniform stopping error
    (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>)>|<cell|An infinite-volume limit
    without refinement>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  Uniqueness of the shooting parameter is not needed: trajectories for
  different cutoffs are compared only through their common endpoint, and two
  fixed-level limits with that endpoint coincide by the contraction.
  Remark<nbsp><reference|prop:endpoint-tuning> records the uniqueness and
  analytic dependence that the recursion also gives; the proofs do not use
  it.

  The ultraviolet limit is a Cauchy argument through a common level. For
  insertions at points of <math|a<rsub|m><Z><rsup|4>>, which lies in every
  finer lattice, let <math|M<rsub|N>> be the lattice moment and
  <math|C<rsub|N,m>> the normalized level-<math|m> integral of the unmarked
  density and the transferred one-insertion functions, with the components
  joining two or more insertions omitted. For cutoffs beyond a threshold
  depending on<nbsp><math|m>,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||M<rsub|N>-M<rsub|N<rprime|'>>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><wide*|<around|\||M<rsub|N>-C<rsub|N,m>|\|>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|<text|stopping
    error>>+<wide*|<around|\||C<rsub|N,m>-C<rsub|N<rprime|'>,m>|\|>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|<text|fixed
    level>>+<wide*|<around|\||C<rsub|N<rprime|'>,m>-M<rsub|N<rprime|'>>|\|>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|<text|stopping
    error>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\>4*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>+<around|\||C<rsub|N,m>-C<rsub|N<rprime|'>,m>|\|>,>>>>
  </align*>

  where <math|F<rsub|0>> does not depend on <math|m> or on the cutoffs
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-stopping>). The reason is separation. At fine
  levels the sites of distinct insertions are at least
  <math|B<rsub|m><rsup|2>> apart, so every omitted component, which joins two
  or more insertions, has covering number at least <math|B<rsub|m>/64>.
  Multiplying all these components by a complex<nbsp><math|z> with
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>> therefore costs only
  two units of the support exponent, and the resulting normalized integral
  <math|F<rsub|m><around|(|z|)>>, carried back to a base level fixed
  before<nbsp><math|m>, is bounded there by<nbsp><math|F<rsub|0>>. Since
  <math|F<rsub|m><around|(|1|)>=M<rsub|N>> and
  <math|F<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>=C<rsub|N,m>>, Cauchy's estimate gives
  <math|<around|\||M<rsub|N>-C<rsub|N,m>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>>.
  Choose <math|m> to make the stopping errors small, hold it fixed, and let
  <math|N,N<rprime|'>\<to\>\<infty\>>. The middle term tends to zero: the
  unmarked coordinates converge at each fixed level
  (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:fixed-level>); the vacuum scalar cancels
  between the correlation and its partition function, whose scalar-free part
  stays bounded below; and the marked coefficients converge because one step
  halves the distance <math|d<rsub|m>*<around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>> between two
  marked graphs at level<nbsp><math|m>, so that

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|m>*<around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|1-<around|(|h-m|)>>+<big|sum><rsub|i=m><rsup|h-1>2<rsup|-<around|(|i-m|)>>*\<delta\><rsub|i>*<around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>
  </equation*>

  for every <math|h\<gtr\>m>, where each fixed-step discrepancy
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>*<around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>> tends to zero
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level>). First choose <math|h> large,
  then the cutoffs. The bounds of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> extend
  the convergence from <math|<big|cup><rsub|m>a<rsub|m><Z><rsup|4>> to all
  separated positions and to derivatives.

  <subsection|Rotations><label|sec:strategy-rotations>

  Continuous rotational covariance is a conclusion of the construction; the
  hypercubic lattice symmetry does not supply it. The ultraviolet comparison
  identifies each <math|F<rsub|d>> with the Maxwell space through scaling
  frames (Propositions<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>
  and<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>). In degree four, divergence maps the centered
  real symmetric tensorial families onto the corresponding vectors of degree
  five with a one-dimensional kernel (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:conserved-line>),
  which supplies a conserved tensor<nbsp><math|J>. Its fluxes through small
  shells around one insertion define charge maps on the field spaces
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.3>).
  Conservation turns the sum of the angular charges of all insertions into
  the integral of<nbsp><math|J> against the derivative of a vector field that
  equals the rotation field <math|y\<mapsto\>K*y> on a large anisotropic box;
  the sum does not depend on the size of the box. For configurations on a
  coordinate line this integral tends to zero by the coordinate reflection
  spaces of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14>, without a mass gap. The
  mechanism is a sign conflict (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H-coordinate-signs>,
  <reference|lem:H-G3> and<nbsp><reference|lem:G-collinear-flux>). Reflecting
  the integration variable in each axis replaces the spectator word by its
  projection that is odd under the reflections in the two axes of the
  rotation plane and even under the others. Because the word lies on a
  coordinate line, the transverse reflections fix its positions, so this
  projection is again a combination of words on the line. Such a combination
  has a positive spectral representation in each coordinate time axis, with
  the sign of its parity in that axis; one correlation represented with
  opposite signs bounds the low-energy spectral mass, and conservation with
  reflection positivity bounds that of the diagonal components
  of<nbsp><math|J>. Every term of the integral is then bounded by a negative
  power of the box size. Finite Ward tests identify the translation charges
  with derivatives; analyticity then extends the identity to all separated
  configurations; finite tests remove the defects in the rotation algebra,
  and the charges integrate to continuous representations
  of<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>).

  <subsection|Phases and reconstruction><label|sec:strategy-reconstruction>

  Reconstruction starts from separated Euclidean correlations and
  quantitative slab bounds; it does not require a positive extension across
  collision diagonals. Three objects should be kept apart.

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Object>|<cell|Role>>|<row|<cell|<math|S<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>>
    on separated configurations>|<cell|Euclidean correlations, reflection
    positive on positive-time words>>|<row|<cell|Ordered-time continuations
    and boundary values>|<cell|The Wightman functions and the relativistic
    fields>>|<row|<cell|Contact extensions
    <math|\<frak-D\><rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|\<b-A\>>>>|<cell|A specified extension
    through collisions, with controlled local order>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The infrared control available is a uniform low-energy estimate rather than
  a spectral gap. For a fixed finite combination <math|A> of compactly
  supported smeared field words, uniformly in the phase<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>><around|(|<around|{|0\<less\>\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>u|}>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1|)>,>>|<row|<cell|0\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>-P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>|)>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M<rprime|'><rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|s\<geqslant\>1|)>,>>>>
  </align*>

  with <math|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>> the projection onto
  <math|<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.3>). Vacuum
  uniqueness first holds for almost every phase; continuity of the
  vacuum-subtracted heat pairings in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>> and full support
  of<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> then extend it to every phase. The polynomial bound
  <math|M<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>> is not a positive lower bound on the spectrum.

  <subsection|The trace><label|sec:strategy-trace>

  The free Maxwell stress tensor is traceless, so a qualitative Maxwell limit
  cannot determine the first nonzero trace: it requires the next coefficient,
  with a rate. Heuristically, at scale<nbsp><math|r> the rescaled trace
  <math|r<rsup|4>*\<Theta\>> is close, in the comparison maps, to
  <math|<around|(|4*d<rsub|2>/\<beta\><around|(|r|)>|)><curvaturesquare>>
  with <math|d<rsub|2>=-b<rsub|0>/2> and <math|\<beta\><around|(|r|)>\<approx\><slope>log
  <around|(|1/r|)>=2*b<rsub|0>*log <around|(|1/r|)>>, so that

  <\equation*>
    r<rsup|4>*\<Theta\>\<approx\>-<frac|<curvaturesquare>|log
    <around|(|1/r|)>>,<space|2em><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>\<approx\><frac|<maxwellcorr><rsub|2><around|(|<curvaturesquare>,<curvaturesquare>;-<tfrac|r|2>e<rsub|4>,<tfrac|r|2>e<rsub|4>|)>|log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>>=<frac|3<dimG>|\<pi\><rsup|4>>*<space|0.17em>r<rsup|-8>*log<rsup|-2><around|(|1/r|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  by homogeneity of degree <math|-8>. The amplitude does not depend on
  <math|b<rsub|0>>: the trace coefficient and the running coupling both carry
  <math|b<rsub|0>>, and only their ratio enters.

  The coefficient <math|d<rsub|2>> is computed, not assumed, in five steps
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-I>). (1)<nbsp>The centered real
  symmetric <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial free tensors of degree four are
  spanned by the Maxwell stress, the trace direction
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|a*b><curvaturesquare>> and one further tensor, and a
  linear functional reads the trace of such a tensor from its divergence.
  (2)<nbsp>A lattice stress current built from plaquette curvatures has
  divergence equal to a variation of the action up to a small defect, and
  block transfer keeps its trace a lattice difference. (3)<nbsp>This lattice
  Ward identity has the regulated coefficient <math|-12<slope>>, the
  derivative <math|-\<mu\>*<space|0.17em>d/d*\<mu\>> of a regulated one-loop
  determinant whose growth <math|12<slope>log \<mu\>> comes from the
  computation that gives the running slope (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator>). Along suitable radii the trace
  functional is carried to the continuum frames with error
  <math|o<around|(|E<rsup|-1>|)>> for a slowly growing scale<nbsp><math|E>;
  the rate is needed because the cancelling coefficients of step<nbsp>(4) may
  grow like a smaller scale<nbsp><math|R>, with <math|R/E\<to\>0>
  (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I-transfer>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:I-stability>). (4)<nbsp>Cancelling the divergence
  of the transferred current, multiplied by<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>, with
  multiples of the other two tensors gives a conserved tensor. By
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:conserved-line> it is a multiple
  of<nbsp><math|T>, the shell normalization forces the multiple to tend to
  one, and its coefficient along <math|\<delta\><rsub|a*b><curvaturesquare>>
  tends to <math|d<rsub|2>=-<slope>/4>; the trace <math|4<curvaturesquare>>
  of that direction gives <math|4*d<rsub|2>/\<beta\>>
  (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:I-coefficient>). (5)<nbsp>The positive
  spectral representation makes <math|<tracetwopoint>> nonincreasing, which
  turns this comparison along subsequences of radii into a limit through all
  radii (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-products>). The product expansion,
  the last branch of Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:dependency>, is explained
  with its statement in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-products>.

  <section|The renormalization group><label|sec:renormalization>

  This section defines the block map, the coordinates of the effective
  densities and their norms, and states the inductive step, the multiscale
  theorem and the two ultraviolet results built on them. Fix
  <math|<blockpenalty>\<gtr\>0>. Throughout we use the common choices of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-A>\U<reference|sec:partII-E>, made once
  for the group realization and the penalty in the order of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>; constants may depend on these
  choices, and further dependence is stated where it occurs.

  <subsection|Scales and the block map><label|sec:block-map>

  With odd ratios <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>3>, set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-levels>D<rsub|0>=1,<space|2em>D<rsub|m+1>=\<ell\><rsub|m>*D<rsub|m>,<space|2em>a<rsub|m>=D<rsub|m><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>s<rsub|m>=log
    D<rsub|m><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Level<nbsp><math|m> has spacing<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>>, and blocking passes
  from level<nbsp><math|m+1> to level<nbsp><math|m>; the level-<math|m> torus
  has side <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>> in lattice units and <math|L<rsub|p>>
  in physical units. The cutoff sequence of the family is <math|D<rsub|N>>.
  Each level carries the stage <math|J<rsub|m>> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:closes>. The endpoint coupling
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<gtr\>1> is chosen large (item<nbsp>7 of
  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>), and

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<refbeta><rsub|m>>|<cell|=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<slope>s<rsub|m>,>|<cell|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>>|<cell|=exp
    <around*|(|<around|(|log log <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|)>,>|<cell|t<rsub|m>>|<cell|=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2><cutoffsize><rsub|m>,<eq-number><label|eq:I12-scales>>>|<row|<cell|B<rsub|m>>|<cell|=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>,>|<cell|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>|<cell|=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-11/10>,>|<cell|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>>|<cell|=e<rsup|-<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|1/5>><space|0.17em>.<eq-number><label|eq:I12-scales-b>>>>>
  </align>

  The reference coupling <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>> follows the leading running
  slope; <math|t<rsub|m>> separates small from large plaquette fields;
  <math|B<rsub|m>> is the radius of the support weights; and
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>,
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>> are the allowances, the
  bounds kept by the induction for the regular and forced activity sums
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:closes> explains these sizes). They depend on
  the reference coupling, so they stay fixed as the actual coupling varies in
  the band <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m>=<around|[|<refbeta><rsub|m>/2,2<refbeta><rsub|m>|]>>.
  The physical periods <math|L<rsub|p>> are strictly increasing multiples of
  four, every positive integer divides <math|L<rsub|p>> for infinitely
  many<nbsp><math|p>, and <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>>.
  At stage<nbsp><math|j>, <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|j>> is the derivative order,
  <math|\<varpi\><rsub|j>> the spatial power, <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>> the
  support exponent and <math|\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>>\<geqslant\>0>
  the coupling drift bound (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>).

  At a step <math|m+1\<to\>m>, the fine links <math|U> and coarse links
  <math|V> live on <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m+1>>> and
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>>; put <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>,
  <math|\<ell\>=\<ell\><rsub|m>> and <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>=<around|{|-R,\<ldots\>,R|}><rsup|4>>.
  Fine transports between two coarse endpoints are compared after expressing
  them in the same frames. A frame is a gauge transformation
  <math|h<rsub|y,z>\<in\>G> at each fine site <math|\<ell\>*y+z> of the patch
  of radius <math|<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\>> around <math|\<ell\>*y>, built
  from parallel transports in the patch (Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D20a>\U<reference|def:D21>).
  For a coarse edge <math|e=<around|(|y,a|)>> the framed straight connector
  is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-framed-connector>Y<rsub|e,z>=h<rsub|y,z><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|r=0><rsup|\<ell\>-1>U*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+z+r*e<rsub|a>,a|)>|)>*h<rsub|y+e<rsub|a>,z><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>,
  </equation>

  the fine transport from <math|\<ell\>*y+z> to
  <math|\<ell\>*<around|(|y+e<rsub|a>|)>+z> in the frames at its ends. With
  <math|<math-up|Unif>> the uniform probability on a box and <math|\<ast\>j>
  the <math|j>-fold convolution, the averaging weights and the predictor are

  <\gather>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>=<around*|(|<math-up|Unif><around|(|\<bbb-B\><rsub|<around|(|\<ell\>-1|)>/2>|)>|)><rsup|\<ast\>j><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em><wide|Y|\<bar\>><rsub|e>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>*Y<rsub|e,z><space|0.17em>,>>|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>=<math-up|argmin><rsub|u\<in\>G><big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>><wilsonterm><around|(|Y<rsub|e,z>*u<rsup|-1>|)>=<math-up|argmin><rsub|u\<in\>G><around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|Y|\<bar\>><rsub|e>-u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>><space|0.17em>.<eq-number><label|eq:I12-predictor>>>>>
  </gather>

  The second form holds because <math|<wilsonterm><around|(|Y*u<rsup|-1>|)>=<around|(|2*n<rsub|G>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|\<\|\|\>|Y-u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2>>
  for unitary <math|Y,u>: the predictor is the point of<nbsp><math|G> nearest
  to the weighted average of the connectors, an average of group variables
  taken through the ambient matrices. On a good patch the connectors lie near
  one another, and this branch, on which the minimizer is unique, is used
  when the minimum lies below a fixed cutoff. Where the frame test or the
  predictor test fails, the kernel chooses at random, an axial frame from a
  finite list or a single connector <math|Y<rsub|e,z>> with probability
  <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>><rsub|z>>; these branches complete the kernel on
  the remaining configurations (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-B> gives
  the decision rules, summarized in Table<nbsp><reference|tab:B-branches> and
  Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:B-predictor>). All discrete choices form a tag
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>>, drawn with probabilities

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-tag-mass>\<lambda\><rsup|<math-up|tag>>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>|)>\<geqslant\>0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>>\<lambda\><rsup|<math-up|tag>>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>|)>=1<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Given <math|U> and the tag, the coarse links are independent, each
  concentrated within about <math|<around|(|<blockpenalty>\<beta\>|)><rsup|-1/2>>
  of its predictor, with the normalized density
  <math|q<rsub|\<gamma\>><around|(|u|)>=e<rsup|-\<gamma\><wilsonterm><around|(|u|)>>/<big|int><rsub|G>e<rsup|-\<gamma\><wilsonterm>>*<space|0.17em>d*h>;
  the block penalty plays the role of the smearing parameter of soft
  block-spin transformations.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Exact block map><label|def:D04> For fine links <math|U> and coarse
    links <math|V>, define

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>>\<lambda\><rsup|<math-up|tag>>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|e<math-up|coarse>>q<rsub|<blockpenalty>\<beta\>>*<around*|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>|)><rsup|-1>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\>>f|)><around|(|V|)>>|<cell|=<big|int><blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>*f<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U<space|0.17em>,>>>>
    </align*>

    with probability Haar measures on both link sets.
  </definition>

  For fixed <math|U> and tag, right invariance of Haar measure makes each
  coarse factor integrate to one; summing the tag masses gives
  <math|<big|int><blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*V=1>.
  Hence, for integrable<nbsp><math|f>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-partition-preservation><big|int><around|(|<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\>>f|)><around|(|V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*V=<big|int>f<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U<space|0.17em>:
  </equation>

  the map integrates one scale exactly and keeps the partition integral.
  Branches, measurability and symmetries of the kernel are proved in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-B>.

  <subsection|The density and its norms><label|sec:density>

  The density produced by the integrations records four things: the leading
  curvature action with its running coupling, the field-independent scalar
  accumulated so far, small local corrections on small-field supports, and
  the contributions of large-field regions. At level<nbsp><math|m> write
  <math|\<Lambda\>=\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>>. The flagged sites
  <math|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>>, or large-field sites, are the sites
  incident to a plaquette whose chord exceeds<nbsp><math|t<rsub|m>>; a
  support is healthy, or small-field in the sense of Baªaban, if it contains
  none. A regular activity <math|E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> is a Borel
  real gauge-invariant function, local on its support <math|X<rsub|i>>,
  anchored at <math|o<rsub|i>\<in\>X<rsub|i>> and vanishing at the flat
  configuration; it is used only on healthy supports. A forced activity
  <math|K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>>, one of the large-field polymer
  activities, is local on its support <math|Y<rsub|i>> and carries a nonempty
  inventory <math|I<rsub|i>\<subset\>Y<rsub|i>> of flagged sites; it vanishes
  off its eligibility event

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-forced-eligibility>\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|elig>>=<around|{|U:Y<rsub|i>\<cap\><flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=I<rsub|i>|}>\<cap\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|extra>>,<space|2em>K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>><around|(|U|)>=0*<space|1em><around|(|U\<nin\>\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|elig>>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The first condition fixes exactly which sites of <math|Y<rsub|i>> are
  flagged. The extra event is a fixed Borel gauge-invariant test supplied
  with the activity, which reads only links with both endpoints in
  <math|Y<rsub|i>> and the flags in<nbsp><math|Y<rsub|i>>; it distinguishes
  local alternatives with the same support and inventory. The activity
  families are countable.

  The path action carries the kinetic coefficient and the quadratic
  corrections generated by integration. At level<nbsp><math|m> abbreviate the
  clipping constants and relaxation length of item<nbsp>4 of
  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>,

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>,<space|2em>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>=c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,J<rsub|m>>,<space|2em>0\<less\>16*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>,1|)>,<space|2em>R<rsup|<math-up|rel>>=R<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|rel>><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<gamma\><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> is half a uniform lower bound for
  the free effective plaquette forms of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>.
  Distances are periodic maximum-coordinate distances in lattice units: on a
  torus of side<nbsp><math|n>, <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>=min<rsub|z\<in\>n<Z><rsup|4>><around|\||<wide|x|~>-<wide|y|~>+z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>>
  for lifts <math|<wide|x|~>,<wide|y|~>\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>. Set

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|clip><rsub|t><around|(|r|)>>|<cell|=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*r<rsup|2>/2,>|<cell|r\<leqslant\>t,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t*r,>|<cell|r\<gtr\>t,>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|\<chi\><rsup|<math-up|rel>><rsub|m,x><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=\<b-1\>*<space|-0.17em><around*|{|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|y;a*b>|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|m>*e<rsup|<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>/R<rsup|<math-up|rel>>><text|
    for every >y,a\<less\>b|}><space|0.17em>.<eq-number><label|eq:I12-clipping>>>>>
  </align>

  For each plaquette orientation <math|\<frak-p\>=<around|(|a,b|)>>, let
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>*<around|(|U,x|)>> be a curvature
  <math|<math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>-U<rsub|\<square\>><rsup|-1>|)>/2>,
  with the orthogonal projection for the metric of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, transported along a specified finite
  path to<nbsp><math|x>, and put <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|\<frak-p\>><rsup|<math-up|path>><around|(|U,x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>*\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>*<around|(|U,x|)>>,
  a smeared lattice curvature at<nbsp><math|x>. The action splits the
  effective kinetic form into a clipped part, quadratic below
  <math|t<rsub|m>> and linear beyond, and the remaining positive form,
  written as a sum of squares with one row per
  <math|<around|(|x,\<frak-p\>|)>>; a row is switched off once some plaquette
  exceeds its distance-relaxed threshold, that is, when the <em|relaxed
  threshold test> <math|\<chi\><rsup|<math-up|rel>><rsub|m,x>>
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-clipping> vanishes. With
  <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|q|)>><around|(|U,x|)>>,
  <math|q=2,3,4>, the fixed real invariant <math|q>-linear expressions in
  transported curvatures, each multiplied by the health indicator of its
  footprint (the union of its slot footprints and the anchor;
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D22>),

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|<pathaction>|)><rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>>|<cell|=\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|x,\<frak-p\>><around*|[|<math-up|clip><rsub|t<rsub|m>><around|(|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|x;\<frak-p\>>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<chi\><rsup|<math-up|rel>><rsub|m,x><around|(|U|)><around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|\<frak-p\>><rsup|<math-up|path>><around|(|U,x|)>|\|><rsup|2>|]>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>+\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|x,i><around|(|\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|3|)>>+\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>|)>*<around|(|U,x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|x,i>\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|2|)>><around|(|U,x|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:I12-path-action>>>|<row|<cell|<pathaction><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>>|<cell|=<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|h\<in\><hypercubic>><around|(|<pathaction>|)><rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><space|0.17em>,>>>>
  </align>

  where <math|h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U> transports links by the lattice
  isometry<nbsp><math|h>. The path coefficients and multilinear expressions
  are independent of<nbsp><math|p> (Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D22>\U<reference|def:D23>,
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>); the action is affine
  in<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>, and its last sum does not depend
  on<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>. The clip drops at <math|r=t> from
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*t<rsup|2>/2> to
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t<rsup|2>>. Accordingly the density coordinates
  are Borel functions; derivatives are taken only on healthy configurations
  with the masks held fixed, so coupling derivatives never differentiate the
  flag boundary. The positive linear branch beyond the threshold is what the
  local large-field energy bound (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>) uses.

  For these fixed path actions and thresholds, a <em|representation> at
  level<nbsp><math|m> is the datum

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-representation>\<rho\>=<around*|(|\<beta\>,v<rsub|<math-up|vac>>,<around|(|X<rsub|i>,o<rsub|i>,E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|)><rsub|i>,<around|(|Y<rsub|i>,I<rsub|i>,\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|extra>>,K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsub|i>|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation>

  with vacuum scalar <math|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>>> and running coupling
  <math|\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>=\<beta\>>. Let
  <math|\<frak-F\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>> be the set of finite
  <math|\<frak-a\>\<subset\><N>> such that the supports <math|Y<rsub|i>>,
  <math|i\<in\>\<frak-a\>>, are pairwise disjoint and
  <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<frak-a\>>I<rsub|i>=<flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>>:
  the exact covers of the flagged sites.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Effective density><label|def:D06> For a
    representation<nbsp><math|\<rho\>>, put

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-V\><rsub|\<rho\>><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=<pathaction><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>><around|(|U|)>-v<rsub|<math-up|vac>><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>,>>|<row|<cell|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=e<rsup|-\<cal-V\><rsub|\<rho\>><around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<frak-a\>\<in\>\<frak-F\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>><big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<frak-a\>>K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>
  </definition>

  If there are no flagged sites, <math|\<frak-F\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=<around|{|\<varnothing\>|}>>
  and the forced sum is one. Otherwise every flagged site lies in exactly one
  selected support, but the sum runs over all exact covers: one support may
  carry two flags in one term while two disjoint supports carry one flag each
  in another (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:covers>). The local regularity of
  the activities and the restrictions on forced terms are
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D25>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-4.svg|354.728867pt|76.84802pt||><label|fig:covers>
  >|Exact covers of two flagged sites (dots). (a) One support carries both
  flags; the dashed line marks the larger region its eligibility test
  inspects. (b) Two disjoint supports carry one flag each. Both selections
  occur in the sum of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>, which does not
  choose a canonical cover. (c) Overlapping supports are not a cover.>

  For a link <math|e> let <math|e<rsup|->> be its initial site. The left
  derivative <math|D<rsup|L><rsub|<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>>>> differentiates
  the increments <math|U<rsub|e>\<mapsto\>e<rsup|t*<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|e>>*U<rsub|e>>,
  with <math|<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|e>\<in\><g>> normed by
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Local derivative seminorm><label|def:D05a> For a regular activity
    anchored at <math|o<rsub|i>>, set

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>=sup<rsub|0\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>
      sup<rsub|U,<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|q>><around*|\||D<rsup|L><rsub|<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*D<rsup|L><rsub|<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|q>>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>><around|(|U|)>|\|><space|0.17em>,
    </equation*>

    over configurations healthy on <math|X<rsub|i>> and directions with
    <math|<around|\||<around|(|<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|k>|)><rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|m>*<around*|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|e<rsup|->,o<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>>,
    <math|1\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>q>.
  </definition>

  The seminorm measures ordered local derivatives in units of the permitted
  field size. For a support<nbsp><math|X>, let
  <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>> be the least number of
  radius-<math|B<rsub|m>> lattice balls with connected union
  covering<nbsp><math|X>; the exponential weight below penalizes supports
  that need many such balls.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Weighted activity sums><label|def:D05b> For nonnegative majorants
    <math|e<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|maj>>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>>
    and <math|k<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|maj>>\<geqslant\>sup<rsub|U><around|\||K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>><around|(|U|)>|\|>>,
    define

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|m>=sup<rsub|x> <big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=x>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*e<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|maj>>,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>=sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>Y<rsub|i>>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|maj>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  The induction keeps the bounds

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-activity-caps>\<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  These numerical bounds do not by themselves define the classes on which the
  step acts. The Wilson start is the continuous curve

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-Wilson-initialization>\<beta\>\<longmapsto\>\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>,<space|2em>\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>|)>=\<beta\>,<space|2em><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>=e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>>*<space|1em><around|(|\<beta\>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|N>|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation>

  with vacuum scalar zero (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>). The <em|admissible
  classes> consist of everything reachable from it. Let
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>=<around|{|\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>:\<beta\>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|N>|}>>
  and <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,N,p>=\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>>. For
  <math|m\<less\>N>, a representation at level<nbsp><math|m> belongs to
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>> if it is obtained from some
  <math|\<rho\><rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m+1,p>> with coupling
  in<nbsp><math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>> by one block step of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>: its path is
  <math|<pathaction><rsub|N,m>>, its activities are produced by local
  subtraction, path replacement and fusion, its coupling is that
  of<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rprime|'>> plus the one-loop coefficient
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> and a
  correction bounded by the first inequality of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-retained>,
  and its density is the block integral of the density
  of<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rprime|'>> (the precise conditions precede
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2>). Membership is a property of a single
  representation; in particular the classes contain the outputs of the step
  construction on continuous curves and their restrictions to closed
  subintervals. Let <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>\<subset\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>\<times\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m+1,p>>
  be the set of the corresponding output\Uinput pairs. Every member satisfies
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D25>, the path bounds
  of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>
  and<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3> at the
  Wilson start, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:D2> at
  later levels). A curve <math|u\<mapsto\>\<rho\><rsub|u>> in
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>> is continuous
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25a>) if its supports, anchors,
  inventories and extra tests do not depend on<nbsp><math|u>, its coupling
  and vacuum scalar are continuous, and, as <math|u\<to\>u<rsub|0>>, the
  differences of regular derivatives on healthy configurations and of forced
  values in supremum norm admit majorants whose weighted sums tend to zero.

  <subsection|The inductive step><label|sec:inductive-step>

  Exact integration removes one scale. Extracting its marginal
  curvature-square term into the coupling leaves a bounded drift error and
  returns a density in the next admissible class.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|RG. One step><label|prop:RG> For every <math|N,p>,
    <math|m\<less\>N>, reals <math|u<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|1>> and
    continuous <math|\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m+1,p>>
    with <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>=\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>|)>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>
    for all <math|u\<in\><around|[|u<rsub|0>,u<rsub|1>|]>>, there is a
    continuous <math|\<rho\><rsub|u>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>> with
    <math|<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|u>,\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>|)>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>>
    and, for every <math|u\<in\><around|[|u<rsub|0>,u<rsub|1>|]>> and coarse
    configuration <math|V>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>|)><around|(|V|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|u>|)>>|<cell|=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>-<slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>+e<rsub|u>,<space|2em><around|\||e<rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|<math-up|RG>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>
  </theorem>

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-B>\U<reference|sec:partII-D>;
  the proof follows Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2>).

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|Step 1>The density identity is exact because the output
    coordinates rewrite the block-integrated density on every configuration
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5>); by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-partition-preservation>
    the partition integral is kept. Approximation enters only the coupling
    drift and the activity estimates.

    <item*|Step 2>On small fields, gauge fixing in a rooted block-tree gauge
    and the stationary background turn the local integral into a Gaussian
    integral with its determinant and Haar-coordinate factors; the
    large-field lower bound (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>) pays for the
    forced terms.

    <item*|Step 3>Classical fourth-order matching fixes the local terms, and
    the background curvature-square coefficient of the one-loop action,
    assembled from the inherited quadratic loop term, the fluctuation
    determinant and the Haar Jacobian, gives the change <math|-<slope>log
    \<ell\><rsub|m>> up to a stage-bounded error
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>).

    <item*|Step 4>The local expansion, the contraction of inherited terms and
    the five-torus localization (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:five-torus>) give
    the regular bound; forced fusion gives the forced bound; the scalar
    coupling reset absorbs the residual kinetic coefficient
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>).
  </enumerate>

  The bound <math|\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>>> depends only on the
  stage, not on <math|N,p>, the interval or the curve. Since
  <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>-<slope>log \<ell\><rsub|m>>, a
  step changes <math|\<beta\>-<refbeta>> by <math|e<rsub|u>> only.

  <subsection|Multiscale trajectories><label|sec:trajectories>

  A trajectory is a tuple <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>>,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>, with
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,N>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>> and
  <math|<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>,\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m+1>|)>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>>
  for <math|m\<less\>N>; write <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>=\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>|)>>.
  The bare coupling is selected by shooting. Put
  <math|\<frak-T\><rsub|m>=<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<tfrac|<slope>|2>s<rsub|m>,<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+2<slope>s<rsub|m>|]>\<subset\>\<frak-B\><rsub|m>>,
  so that <math|\<frak-T\><rsub|0>=<around|{|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|}>>.
  Start with the Wilson curve on <math|\<frak-T\><rsub|N>>. Suppose a closed
  parameter interval has level-<math|<around|(|m+1|)>> couplings in
  <math|\<frak-T\><rsub|m+1>>, taking its end values at its endpoints. Since
  <math|s<rsub|m+1>=s<rsub|m>+log \<ell\><rsub|m>> and
  <math|<around|\||e<rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsup|<math-up|RG>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<leqslant\><tfrac|<slope>|4>log
  \<ell\><rsub|m>> (item<nbsp>5 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>),
  the output couplings at the two endpoints satisfy

  <\equation*>
    \<beta\>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<tfrac|<slope>|2>s<rsub|m>-<tfrac|<slope>|4>log
    \<ell\><rsub|m>\<less\>min \<frak-T\><rsub|m>,<space|2em>\<beta\>\<geqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+2<slope>s<rsub|m>+<tfrac|3<slope>|4>log
    \<ell\><rsub|m>\<gtr\>max \<frak-T\><rsub|m><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The intermediate value theorem gives a closed subinterval whose couplings
  lie in <math|\<frak-T\><rsub|m>> and take its end values at its endpoints.
  After <math|N> steps the level-zero coupling is
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>. The couplings are chosen this way for all
  <math|N,p> before any sequence of volume indices is prescribed.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|MT. Multiscale trajectory><label|prop:MT> There are bare couplings
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> and trajectories
    <math|<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>|)><rsub|m=0><rsup|N>>, for all
    <math|N,p\<in\><N>>, with <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,N>=\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>>
    and <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,N>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>, satisfying

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,0>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|m>*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>=<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m+1>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<less\>N|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    For every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, some
    <math|m<rsub|0><around|(|\<varepsilon\>|)>> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<slope>s<rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>*s<rsub|m>*<space|2em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0>,p\<in\><N>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  The proof is the shooting argument above
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.1>,
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>\U<reference|lem:E1.2>). Fix these
  trajectories for all later constructions. The drift bound of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG> holds on every adjacent pair, and all
  their integrals equal <math|Z<rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>,\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>>
  by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-partition-preservation> and the Wilson start. Summing
  the drift from the fixed endpoint gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I12-drift-sum><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<refbeta><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|m-1>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>><rsup|<math-up|RG>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Item<nbsp>5 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices> bounds the sum
  by <math|<around|(|<slope>/4|)>*s<rsub|m>>, which also keeps the couplings
  in <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m>>. Its stronger bound
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>><rsup|<math-up|RG>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>*log
  \<ell\><rsub|i>> makes the sum <math|o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>: split it at
  a fixed level and bound the tail by <math|sup<rsub|i\<geqslant\>i<rsub|0>>
  J<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>> times<nbsp><math|s<rsub|m>>. Since
  <math|J<rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>> and <math|s<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>, the
  finite initial sum and <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> disappear after
  division by<nbsp><math|s<rsub|m>>, uniformly in <math|N\<geqslant\>m>
  and<nbsp><math|p>. As <math|\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>>\<geqslant\>1>,
  the same bound forces <math|log \<ell\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>J<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>\<to\>\<infty\>>:
  the block ratios must grow, and the level choices also give <math|log
  \<ell\><rsub|m>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0>. The step construction of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG> is fixed once on whole continuous curves
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2>); the trajectories it produces from
  <math|\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>>, and their restrictions,
  form the <em|specified recursion>, to which the trajectories of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT> belong. Different cutoffs are compared
  through the following limit.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Fixed-level convergence><label|prop:fixed-level> For fixed
    <math|p> and <math|m>, as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> along the full
    sequence, the couplings <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>> of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT> converge, and the activity coordinates
    of <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>> converge in the weighted spaces
    <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>> of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-common-norm>. No
    convergence of the vacuum scalars is asserted.
  </proposition>

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D-cutoff-continuity>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fixed-level>). The argument uses geometric
  truncation, not compactness of a ball in an infinite-dimensional space. The
  scalar coordinates lie in fixed compact intervals, so a diagonal argument
  gives subsequential limits. At a fixed higher level <math|k>, replace the
  activity coordinate by zero and run the same maps down to
  level<nbsp><math|m>: the error is at most
  <math|<around|(|8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|k-m>> with
  <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>=1/128>, uniformly in the cutoff, while the truncated
  coordinates converge by the cutoff compatibility of finitely many step maps
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-cutoff-continuity>). Letting
  <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>> gives the activity limits. Two limits have the same
  endpoint <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>, and their distance
  <math|\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>> satisfies
  <math|\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>>,
  so they coincide.

  <subsection|Local fields under blocking><label|sec:marked>

  Blocking a density that carries one insertion returns a density of the same
  form: the unmarked density times a local insertion with new coefficients,
  plus regular remainders with vanishing Taylor coefficients through
  order<nbsp><math|d> and forced remainders attached to large fields. The
  coefficients evolve linearly, and the insertions of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08> are normalized by prescribing them at
  the reference level. Choose the reference level <math|<reflevel|d>> with
  <math|J<rsub|<reflevel|d>>\<geqslant\>d+3> and
  <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d+1>,
  <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d> for
  <math|m\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>). Fix
  <math|N\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>; starting from a fine-cutoff coefficient
  vector <math|\<b-up-v\>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, the marked density at the origin
  is initialized and blocked by

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|H<rsup|<math-up|ins>><rsub|N,p,N;d>*<around|(|\<b-up-v\>;U|)>>|<cell|=<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,N><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em><localsection|d><around|[|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>>*\<b-up-v\>|]><around|(|0;U|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:I12-marked-evolution>>>|<row|<cell|H<rsup|<math-up|ins>><rsub|N,p,m;d><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>>|<cell|=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d><blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>>H<rsup|<math-up|ins>><rsub|N,p,m+1;d><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|<reflevel|d>\<leqslant\>m\<less\>N|)><space|0.17em>.<eq-number>>>>>
  </align>

  For fixed <math|N,p,d>, the transfer construction chooses coefficient
  vectors <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>> and regular and
  forced remainders <math|R<rsub|m,i><rsup|<math-up|reg>><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>>,
  <math|Q<rsub|m,i><rsup|<math-up|for>><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>>, all linear
  in<nbsp><math|\<b-up-v\>>. Write <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|m>=x<rsub|m,0>*\<b-1\>+\<b-up-x\><rsub|m><rsup|\<circ\>>>
  and <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>=\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>>. The regular remainders have
  supports <math|Z<rsub|m,i>> containing the origin. Their sum and the forced
  remainder are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>*<around|(|\<b-up-v\>;U|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|Z<rsub|m,i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*R<rsub|m,i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>*<around|(|\<b-up-v\>;U|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>*<around|(|\<b-up-v\>;U|)>>|<cell|=e<rsup|-\<cal-V\><rsub|\<rho\><rsub|m>><around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<frak-a\>\<in\>\<frak-F\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>\<frak-a\>>Q<rsub|m,i><rsup|<math-up|for>>*<around|(|\<b-up-v\>;U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>\<frak-a\>\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|for>><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>,<eq-number><label|eq:I12-marked-remainders>>>>>
  </align>

  with all unmarked quantities taken from<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|m>>. The
  marked density has the exact decomposition

  <\multline>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|H<rsup|<math-up|ins>><rsub|N,p,m;d>*<around|(|\<b-up-v\>;U|)>=<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>*<around*|[|x<rsub|m,0><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>+\<b-1\><rsub|\<Sigma\><rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>><localsection|d><around|[|t<rsub|m><rsup|-<factorcount>>*\<b-up-x\><rsub|m><rsup|\<circ\>><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>|]><around|(|0;U|)>|]>>>|<row|<cell|+<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>*\<cal-R\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>*<around|(|\<b-up-v\>;U|)>+\<cal-R\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>*<around|(|\<b-up-v\>;U|)><space|0.17em>,<eq-number><label|eq:I12-marked-representation>>>>>
  </multline>

  with the stencil <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> placed at the origin of the
  level-<math|m> torus. If <math|10<math-up|dist><around|(|x,0|)>\<less\>L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>
  for every <math|x\<in\>Z<rsub|m,i>>, the regular remainder
  <math|R<rsub|m,i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> has zero radial-jet Taylor
  coefficients through order<nbsp><math|d>; the forced remainders vanish
  outside their unmarked eligibility events
  (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>\U<reference|lem:E2.2a>). The fixed
  decomposition selected there gives the terminal map of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, invertible by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>:

  <\equation*>
    \<b-up-x\><rsub|N><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>=\<b-up-v\>,<space|2em>\<b-up-x\><rsub|<reflevel|d>><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>\<b-up-v\><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The factor <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>=<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|d>>
  uses the degree of the whole space <math|V<rsub|d>>, and the terminal map
  accounts for the mixing between its weights. The unit identity
  <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>|)>=a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>,
  proved with the transfer symmetries (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>),
  explains why the coefficient of the constant insertion is
  <math|a<rsub|<reflevel|d>><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>, although <math|V<rsub|d>> also
  contains polynomials of lower weight. With
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>, these insertions specify one Wilson
  family; each insertion is continuous, gauge invariant and local on its
  fixed stencil, and Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT> at <math|m=N> gives the
  volume-uniform slope <math|<slope>\<gtr\>0>.

  <subsection|Uniform bounds and the fixed-volume limit><label|sec:uv-limit>

  For a finite list <math|\<b-l\>> of pairs <math|<around|(|i,a|)>>, with
  <math|i> a slot and <math|a> an axis, the normalized differences and
  derivatives are

  <\equation*>
    \<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>=<big|prod><rsub|<around|(|i,a|)>\<in\>\<b-l\>>a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|i,a>-1|)>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>=<big|prod><rsub|<around|(|i,a|)>\<in\>\<b-l\>>\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|\<tau\><rsub|i,a>> shifts slot<nbsp><math|i> forward by one
  lattice link. The torus separation of a lattice tuple is
  <math|min<rsub|i\<ne\>j> min<rsub|z\<in\>L<rsub|p><Z><rsup|4>><around|\||<wide|y|^><rsub|i>-<wide|y|^><rsub|j>+z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>>,
  with value <math|+\<infty\>> for <math|n\<leqslant\>1>, and

  <\equation*>
    <Gen|n>=<around|{|x:x<rsub|i><rsup|a>\<ne\>x<rsub|j><rsup|a><text| for
    >i\<ne\>j,a=1,\<ldots\>,4|}>,<space|2em><NoHit|L|n>=<around|{|x:x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|j>\<nin\>L<Z><rsup|4><text|
    for >i\<ne\>j|}><space|0.17em>;
  </equation*>

  the last set excludes collisions on a torus of physical side<nbsp><math|L>.
  The first result bounds lattice correlations and their differences
  independently of the volume.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|UB. Uniform difference bounds><label|prop:UB> For every
    <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>>, finite difference
    list<nbsp><math|\<b-l\>> and <math|r\<gtr\>0>, there are
    <math|B*<around|(|v,\<b-l\>,r|)>\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|N<rsub|0>*<around|(|v,\<b-l\>,r|)>> such that, for every
    <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>, every<nbsp><math|p> and every lattice
    tuple of torus separation at least<nbsp><math|r>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>><latmoment><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2>). Choose an
  intermediate level whose physical blocks fit below the separation, so the
  insertions lie in distinct blocks. By the
  decomposition<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-representation>, at that level each
  insertion is a bounded local function plus small remainders, with bounds
  independent of <math|N> and<nbsp><math|p>; differences of insertions are
  matched in a higher degree, their equation directions are removed, and the
  remaining coefficients are tested against a finite free comparison matrix,
  whose injectivity (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests>) rests on the
  free positivity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>. The chessboard estimate
  then bounds the correlation by a product of reflected moments,
  schematically

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|i>O<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i><around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|g\<in\>\<cal-G\>>g*O<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>><rsup|1/<around|\||\<cal-G\>|\|>>
  </equation*>

  for <math|O<rsub|i>> localized in distinct blocks and <math|\<cal-G\>>
  generated by the reflections in the block boundaries; each factor is
  independent of the volume. The reflected-moment constant is chosen before
  the intermediate level, and only the cutoff threshold depends on it.

  The second result removes the spacing at each fixed volume along the full
  sequence; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:tuning-convergence> displays the
  Cauchy argument.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|UV. Fixed-volume limit><label|prop:UV><label|thm:UV> For every
    <math|p,n> and <math|v\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsup|n>>, there is
    <math|M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>|)>>
    such that, for every finite<nbsp><math|\<b-l\>> and every
    <math|p>-admissible separated test<nbsp><math|f>,

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>> a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>><rsup|n>>f<around|(|<wide|y|^>|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>><latmoment><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1>\U<reference|lem:E3.2>).
  The Cauchy argument of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:tuning-convergence>
  gives convergence on <math|<big|cup><rsub|m>a<rsub|m><Z><rsup|4>>: the
  transferred correlations at a fixed level converge, and the stopping error
  <math|2*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>>, with <math|F<rsub|0>> independent
  of<nbsp><math|m>, compares them with the original ones.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> extends convergence to all separated
  positions and identifies every derivative, also at moving points crossing
  the boundary of the chosen period cell. For <math|\<b-l\>=\<varnothing\>>
  the left side is <math|lim<rsub|N><latsmear><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;f|)>>, so
  the inner limits of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09> exist at every
  admissible volume; admissibility places the support of<nbsp><math|f> inside
  <math|<NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>>. The limiting kernel is periodic in each
  position and smooth away from periodic collisions, including where
  positions share coordinates. The thresholds may depend on<nbsp><math|p>.

  <section|The continuum limit, stress, phases, trace and
  products><label|sec:continuum-results>

  The lattice family of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization> is now
  fixed; its couplings and insertions were chosen before any sequence of
  volume indices. This section takes the volume limit and identifies the
  continuum fields: their calculus, the stress tensor and the rotations, the
  phases and their reconstruction, the trace law and the products. Constants
  may depend on the group, its realization, the penalty and the construction
  choices of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>; further dependence is
  stated.

  <subsection|The volume limit and the field
  calculus><label|sec:volume-limit>

  One volume refinement serves every field and all later constructions.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|IV. Infinite-volume limit><label|prop:IV><label|thm:IV> For every
    strictly increasing <math|<math-ss|p>:<N>\<to\><N>>, there are a strictly
    increasing <math|<math-ss|q>:<N>\<to\><N>> and a multilinear
    table<nbsp><math|S> such that the Wilson family converges
    to<nbsp><math|S> along <math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>> in the
    sense of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09>. The table satisfies E1\UE7.
  </theorem>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Dependence on the volume sequence><label|rem:volume-choice> The
    limiting table<nbsp><math|S> may depend on the prescribed
    sequence<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>> and the
    refinement<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>>, hence on the retained volume
    indices<nbsp><math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>>.
  </remark>

  Fix<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>>, <math|<math-ss|q>>, <math|P> and<nbsp><math|S>
  from now on.

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>\U<reference|lem:F3>).

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|Step 1>Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> bounds the
    correlations and all their lattice differences uniformly in the volume; a
    diagonal extraction along<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>> gives local smooth
    limits on<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>> (E6). At each admissible volume the
    fixed-volume kernels are those of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>, so
    the full inner limit is kept; only the outer limit uses a subsequence.

    <item*|Step 2>The chessboard seminorm, the cost of repeating one
    observation throughout the box, gives<nbsp><reference|it:E7> at order
    zero, and the corrected derivative maps below give it at positive orders;
    lattice reflection positivity passes to the limit,
    giving<nbsp><reference|it:E5> and then<nbsp><reference|it:E4>.

    <item*|Step 3>A further refinement fixes finitely many coefficient maps
    in each degree. This auxiliary subsequence concerns finite-dimensional
    coefficients only; the limits of the correlations are the full
    ultraviolet limits already established.
  </enumerate>

  The coefficient maps are needed because the normalization
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>> depends on the
  degree. The same <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>\<subset\>V<rsub|e>> defines
  different lattice fields as <math|<around|(|d,c|)>> and as
  <math|<around|(|e,c|)>>, and a lattice difference of a degree-<math|d>
  insertion is a degree-<math|<around|(|d+1|)>> object with yet another
  normalization; the naive inclusion <math|V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|e>> does not
  preserve correlations. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> constructs linear
  <em|corrected coefficient maps> relating these normalizations, and they
  induce the inclusions and derivatives of the next proposition. Its
  dimension statement uses the free comparison space.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Free Maxwell comparison><label|def:D11> For <math|<dimG>>
    independent colours, take

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\>*A<rsub|a><rsup|\<alpha\>><around|(|x|)>*A<rsub|b><rsup|\<beta\>><around|(|y|)>=\<delta\><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>*\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*\<Gamma\>*<around|(|x-y|)>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>>,<space|2em>F<rsub|a*b>=\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*A<rsub|a><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|<maxwellspace|d>> consist of the complex
    <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant Wick polynomials in curvature
    jets of weight at most<nbsp><math|d>, modulo antisymmetry, the linear
    Bianchi identities and <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|a*b>=0>.
    Their separated Wick correlations are<nbsp><math|<maxwellcorr>>.
  </definition>

  The constant one is the Maxwell unit, coefficient conjugation gives the
  real structure, and inclusions keep the same polynomial at a larger weight
  bound. Free differentiation is <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>(:P:)=:\<partial\><rsub|a>*P>:,
  and the rotation action <math|R<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|h|)>(:P:)=:h*P>:
  is determined by <math|h*F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>h<rsub|i*a>*h<rsub|j*b>*F<rsub|i*j><rsup|\<alpha\>>>
  and <math|h*\<partial\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|i*a>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*h>
  for <math|h\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>>; these operations preserve the defining
  relations.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|FC. Field calculus><label|prop:FC> For the table<nbsp><math|S> of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV>, there are complex linear maps
    <math|\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|e>> for
    <math|d\<leqslant\>e> and <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>>
    for every <math|d,a> such that, for every <math|A\<in\>F<rsub|d>>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<iota\><rsub|e>\<circ\>\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>=\<iota\><rsub|d>,<space|2em>\<iota\><rsub|d+1>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*A|)>=\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|a>>*\<iota\><rsub|d><around|(|A|)>,>>|<row|<cell|dim<rsub|<C>>
      F<rsub|d>=dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </gather*>
  </proposition>

  The proof is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. The corrected coefficient maps
  supply representatives of inclusions and derivatives; the equation and rank
  comparisons of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>\U<reference|lem:E2.10>
  bound <math|dim F<rsub|d>> from above, and the free tests of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests>, with the free positivity of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, bound it from below. Each
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|d>> is injective by
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, so the inclusions are unique and
  injective, <math|\<jmath\><rsub|e,f>*\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>=\<jmath\><rsub|d,f>>,
  <math|\<jmath\><rsub|d,d>=<math-up|id>>, and by<nbsp><reference|it:E1> they
  carry <math|<unitfield|d>> to<nbsp><math|<unitfield|e>>. Their images are
  nested, so <math|\<cal-L\>=<big|cup><rsub|d>\<iota\><rsub|d>*F<rsub|d>>.
  Permutation symmetry gives, for <math|\<b-A\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i>F<rsub|d<rsub|i>>>,
  <math|x\<in\><Conf|n>>, slot<nbsp><math|i> and axis<nbsp><math|a>,

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|A<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,A<rsub|n>;x|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  and<nbsp><reference|it:E2> with injectivity of the responses makes
  inclusions and derivatives commute with conjugation, inclusions commute
  with<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, and <math|h*\<partial\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|b>h<rsub|b*a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*h>
  for <math|h\<in\><hypercubic>>.

  The table also defines reflection Hilbert spaces along the coordinate axes.
  They and their slab bounds use only the volume limit and the field calculus
  (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F3>, <reference|lem:F4> and
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>), and they enter the rotation argument
  of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:stress> before continuous rotations are
  available.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Coordinate reflection spaces><label|def:D14> For an
    axis<nbsp><math|a>, let <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> be
    the completion of the complex span of words at distinct points in
    <math|<around|{|x<rsup|a>\<gtr\>0|}>> modulo the null space of
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|w,v|\<rangle\>><rsub|a>=S*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|a><around|(|w|)>*<space|0.17em>v|)>>,
    and put <math|e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|OS>>><around|[|v|]>=<around|[|\<tau\><rsub|t*e<rsub|a>>*v|]>>
    for <math|t\<geqslant\>0>. Let <math|\<Omega\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
    be the class of the empty word and <math|P<rsup|0><rsub|a>> the
    projection onto <math|ker H<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>.
  </definition>

  By<nbsp><reference|it:E4> the form is positive semidefinite;
  with<nbsp><reference|it:E7>, the translations
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|t*e<rsub|a>>>, <math|t\<geqslant\>0>, preserve its null
  space and define a strongly continuous self-adjoint contraction semigroup,
  whose generator is <math|H<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|OS>>\<geqslant\>0> (proof
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>). Inserting a word between positive-time
  vectors is bounded after a positive time translation
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>); these slab bounds are the coordinate
  estimates used before rotations.

  <subsection|Stress and rotations><label|sec:stress>

  The fields are compared with Maxwell fields at small distances, through
  bases that may vary with the radius to accommodate mixing, and a conserved
  tensor is then normalized by its shell fluxes. The volume refinement
  and<nbsp><math|S> remain fixed; the subsequences below concern comparison
  radii only.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Scaling frames><label|def:D12> A scaling sequence consists of
    <math|r<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>, <math|r<rsub|k>\<to\>0>, and complex linear
    isomorphisms <math|<scaleframe|k|d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>. It
    is stable if, for every <math|D\<in\><N>>, these maps eventually preserve
    conjugation, <math|<hypercubic>>, units and inclusions exactly in all
    degrees <math|d\<leqslant\>e\<leqslant\>D>. Scaling dimensions are
    included in the maps: a comparison insertion is <math|<scaleframe|k|d>u>
    at<nbsp><math|r<rsub|k>*x>, with no further
    factor<nbsp><math|r<rsub|k><rsup|d>>.
  </definition>

  After rescaling positions the interacting correlations approach their
  Maxwell counterparts; one sequence serves every fixed list of fields.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|MC. Maxwell comparison><label|prop:MC> There is one stable scaling
    sequence such that, for every arity<nbsp><math|n>, degree list
    <math|<around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>> and <math|u<rsub|i>\<in\><maxwellspace|d<rsub|i>>>,

    <\equation*>
      S<rsub|n>*<around|(|<scaleframe|k|d<rsub|i>>u<rsub|i>;r<rsub|k><space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em>|)>\<longrightarrow\><maxwellcorr><rsub|n><around|(|u;\<cdummy\>|)><space|1em><text|in
      >L<rsup|1><rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1>). The lattice
  comparison stops near the requested physical scale and compares the
  correlations with the free Gaussian ones. Finitely many separating
  correlations make the comparison maps injective, the upper bound
  <math|dim<rsub|<C>> F<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> makes them isomorphisms, and
  finite-dimensional corrections impose the eventual equalities. Local
  <math|L<rsup|1>> convergence is equivalent, by duality and smooth
  approximation on slightly larger separated compact sets, to convergence
  uniform over smooth tests with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>1>
  and fixed compact support. This weaker form is used to construct and
  normalize the conserved current, before the stress tensor exists.

  To read translation and angular charge, integrate a conserved symmetric
  tensor through a shell surrounding one insertion. We normalize the stress
  tensor so that its translation flux acts as
  <math|+\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|b>>> on the enclosed insertion; with
  the minus sign in the flux below, this gives the negative of the usual
  Euclidean canonical Maxwell tensor (displayed after
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>). Let <math|A\<in\>F<rsub|d>>, let
  <math|\<b-B\>> be a list of<nbsp><math|n> spectator fields
  at<nbsp><math|x>, and take <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x|)>\<in\><Conf|n+1>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Shell fluxes><label|def:D13> For
    <math|J<rsub|a*b>=J<rsub|b*a>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> with
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>=0> and
    <math|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>,
    choose a real shell cutoff <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>>
    equal to one near<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>> and supported away from the
    spectators. Define

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-P\><rsub|b><rsup|J>>|<cell|=-<big|sum><rsub|a><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>,A<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-Y\><rsub|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>><rsup|J>>|<cell|=-<big|sum><rsub|a,b><big|int>\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|y|)>*<around|(|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>|)><rsub|b>*S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>,A<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y<space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>
  </definition>

  The integrals avoid every insertion point, and they do not depend on the
  shell cutoff: two choices differ by a function vanishing near every
  insertion, integration by parts with Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>
  removes the divergence term, and the extra angular term is
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a,b><around|(|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>|)><rsub|b*a>*J<rsub|a*b>=0>
  since <math|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>> is skew and <math|J> symmetric.
  Conservation makes the flux independent of the radius of the shell; it does
  not by itself control a flux through a distant surface. A field is real
  when fixed by conjugation; a family <math|J<rsub|a*b>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> is
  <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial when <math|h*J<rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>h<rsub|i*a>*h<rsub|j*b>*J<rsub|i*j>>
  for <math|h\<in\><hypercubic>>, conserved when
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>=0>
  in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|5>> for every<nbsp><math|b>, and centered when every
  component has zero one-point function.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Conserved line><label|prop:conserved-line> The centered real
    symmetric <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial conserved families
    <math|<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>|)>> in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|4>> form a real
    vector space of dimension one.
  </proposition>

  This is the first assertion of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>; its proof
  shows that divergence maps the centered real symmetric tensorial families
  onto the corresponding vectors in degree five, with one-dimensional kernel.
  Along a refinement of the sequence of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>,
  positive multiples of the rescaled nonzero member approach the free stress
  line in the degree-four frames. For a nonzero member<nbsp><math|J>,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.3> constructs complex linear charge maps
  <math|<math-ss|p><rsub|b>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>> and
  <math|<math-ss|Y><rsub|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>,
  the latter real linear in<nbsp><math|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>>, with
  <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|b><rsup|J>=S*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|p><rsub|b>A|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>>
  and <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>><rsup|J>=S*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|Y><rsub|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>>A|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>>
  for every degree, field, spectator list, separated configuration and shell
  cutoff.

  These charges restore the rotations. Conservation of<nbsp><math|J> turns
  the sum of the angular charges of the insertions, each combining
  <math|<math-ss|Y><rsub|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>>> at its own point with the
  translation charge weighted by the rotated position, into the integral
  of<nbsp><math|J> against the derivative of a vector field equal to
  <math|y\<mapsto\>K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>*y> on a large anisotropic box. For
  configurations on a coordinate line, the coordinate reflection spaces of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14> show that this integral tends to zero
  as the box grows (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-collinear-flux>;
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:strategy-rotations> explains the mechanism), so
  the angular sum vanishes there. Finite Ward tests then identify
  <math|<math-ss|p><rsub|b>> with <math|c<rsub|\<star\>>*\<partial\><rsub|b>>,
  and analyticity extends the identity, infinitesimal rotation invariance, to
  all separated configurations. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1> thereby
  constructs continuous complex linear representations <math|R<rsub|d>>
  of<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>>, extending<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, fixing
  units and commuting with conjugation and inclusions; for a real
  <math|c<rsub|\<star\>>\<ne\>0> common to every degree,

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*t>*R<rsub|d><around|(|e<rsup|t*K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>>|)>|\|><rsub|t=0>,<space|2em><math-ss|p><rsub|b>=c<rsub|\<star\>>*\<partial\><rsub|b>,<space|2em><math-ss|Y><rsub|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>>=c<rsub|\<star\>><math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Integrating gives full covariance (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>):
  <math|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|R<rsub|d<rsub|i>><around|(|h|)>*A<rsub|i>;h*x|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>>
  for every <math|\<b-A\>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i>F<rsub|d<rsub|i>>>,
  <math|x\<in\><Conf|n>> and <math|h\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>>. A family is
  <math|O<around|(|4|)>>-tensorial if <math|R<rsub|4><around|(|h|)>*J<rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>h<rsub|i*a>*h<rsub|j*b>*J<rsub|i*j>>
  for every <math|h\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>>. The common
  constant<nbsp><math|c<rsub|\<star\>>> fixes the normalization of the stress
  tensor by its action on fields.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|ST. Stress tensor><label|prop:ST><label|thm:ST> There is a real
    symmetric <math|O<around|(|4|)>>-tensorial family
    <math|T<rsub|a*b>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> with
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*T<rsub|a*b>=0> such that, for
    every degree, field, spectator list, separated configuration and shell
    cutoff of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>, every<nbsp><math|b> and
    every <math|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-P\><rsub|b><rsup|T>=\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|b>>*S*<around|(|A<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>><rsup|T>=S*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>|)>*A|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  The proof is in Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>\U<reference|lem:G3.2>:
  divide <math|J> by<nbsp><math|c<rsub|\<star\>>> and average its tensor
  transform over<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>> with probability Haar measure.
  Covariance and the transformation laws of derivatives and generators give
  the same shell Ward identities for each transformed current, and
  integration preserves them, as well as symmetry, reality, centering and
  conservation. Fix this tensor; it is centered, since <math|J> is and
  <math|R<rsub|4>> preserves zero one-point values. The free stress
  <math|<maxwellstress>> is normalized by the same shell Ward identities,
  with <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> and the free generators
  <math|<math-ss|L><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G.1>):

  <\equation*>
    <maxwellstress><rsub|a*b>=-<big|sum><rsub|c,\<alpha\>>:F<rsub|a*c><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|b*c><rsup|\<alpha\>>:+<frac|\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|4>*<big|sum><rsub|i,j,\<alpha\>>:F<rsub|i*j><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|i*j><rsup|\<alpha\>>:<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The last comparison includes stress insertions. For a free list
  <math|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|n>>, <math|u<rsub|i>\<in\><maxwellspace|d<rsub|i>>>,
  a stress-slot choice is a subset <math|I\<subset\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>
  with component pairs <math|<around|(|a<rsub|i>,b<rsub|i>|)>> for
  <math|i\<in\>I>; put <math|A<rsub|i><rsup|0>=<maxwellstress><rsub|a<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>>>
  for <math|i\<in\>I> and <math|A<rsub|i><rsup|0>=u<rsub|i>> otherwise. The
  shell Ward identities fix the stress normalization, so its comparison
  insertion needs only the engineering factor<nbsp><math|r<rsub|k><rsup|4>>.
  Below, <math|D<rsub|x><rsup|j>> differentiates the whole function
  <math|x\<mapsto\>S<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-A\><rsub|k>;r<rsub|k>*x|)>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|SC. Differentiated short-distance comparison><label|prop:SC> Along
    the refinement of the scaling sequence of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC> chosen in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>, for every fixed free list and
    stress-slot choice, every derivative order<nbsp><math|j> and compact
    <math|\<cal-K\>\<subset\><Conf|n>>, with
    <math|A<rsub|k,i>=r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*T<rsub|a<rsub|i>*b<rsub|i>>>
    on<nbsp><math|I> for the fixed tensor of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>
    and <math|A<rsub|k,i>=<scaleframe|k|d<rsub|i>>u<rsub|i>> elsewhere,

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|x><rsup|j>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-A\><rsub|k>;r<rsub|k>*x|)>\<longrightarrow\>D<rsub|x><rsup|j><maxwellcorr><rsub|n><around|(|\<b-A\><rsup|0>;x|)><space|1em><text|uniformly
      on >\<cal-K\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  Fix this refined sequence <math|<around|(|r<rsub|k>,<scaleframe|k|d>|)>>
  from now on. The proof follows Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.3>; with
  <math|I=\<varnothing\>> and <math|j=0> it gives
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC> in uniform form, but the earlier
  comparison is needed first, to construct the tensor. Along this sequence,
  for fixed <math|d,a,K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>>, the transported infinitesimal
  actions converge in operator norm: <math|r<rsub|k><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d+1>|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a><scaleframe|k|d>\<to\>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
  and <math|<around|(|<scaleframe|k|d>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>|)><scaleframe|k|d>\<to\><math-ss|L><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K<rsub|<math-up|rot>>|)>>.

  <subsection|Phases and reconstruction><label|sec:phases>

  Compression to zero energy separates the mixed correlations into phases. In
  the reflection space of the time axis, a long empty time interval removes
  the positive-energy components; the word insertions that survive commute on
  the zero-energy space, and a character of the algebra they generate assigns
  compatible scalar values to all of them. The phases are these characters.
  We now use the fourth coordinate as time.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Compression><label|def:compression> For
    <math|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>\<gtr\>0> and a word <math|w> with times in
    <math|<around|(|0,h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>|)>>, let
    <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>><around|(|w|)>> be the
    bounded extension to <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>) of the slab insertion
    <math|A<rsub|w>*v=w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>*e<rsub|4>>*v>
    on positive-time words<nbsp><math|v>, and put
    <math|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>=P<rsup|0><rsub|4><math-ss|B><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>><around|(|w|)>\|<rsub|ker
    H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>.
  </definition>

  The slab insertion places <math|w> in the slab
  <math|<around|(|0,h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>|)>> below the translated state. A
  separated word is translated into a slab to define its compression;
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2> proves independence of width and placement
  and that the compressed operators generate a separable commutative unital
  C<rsup|<math|\<ast\>>>-algebra

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-A\><rsub|0>=C<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>:w<text|
    separated>|)>
  </equation*>

  with cyclic and separating vector <math|\<Omega\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>.
  Its vacuum state <math|a\<mapsto\><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>,a*\<Omega\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>|\<rangle\>>>
  satisfies <math|S<around|(|w|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>,M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>|\<rangle\>>>
  by translation invariance; the measure below represents this state on the
  character space, and the separating property gives full support. Call a
  character <math|\<xi\>> a phase and <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> its table.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|PH. Phase decomposition><label|prop:PH> The algebra
    <math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> has a compact metric character space
    <math|<phases>> with a full-support probability measure <math|\<nu\>>
    such that, for every separated word <math|w>,

    <\equation*>
      S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<xi\><around|(|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>|)>,<space|2em>S<around|(|w|)>=<big|int><rsub|<phases>>S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Each phase satisfies E1\UE7 and has exactly the null spaces
    <math|<nullspace|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>=<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>>.
    The identities of FC, <math|O<around|(|4|)>> covariance and ST hold with
    <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> in place of <math|S>, for the same maps and tensor
    <math|T>. For fixed <math|w>, <math|\<xi\>\<mapsto\>S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>>
    is continuous.
  </theorem>

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>,
  <reference|lem:H1.2> and<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>). The matrix element
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|<around|[|u|]>,M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|2>|)><around|[|v|]>|\<rangle\>>>
  is a limit of correlations of four words placed far apart in time; the long
  time intervals project onto <math|ker H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>. The
  same four words can instead be spread along a second positive coordinate
  direction, so that the two middle words occur in the opposite order. By
  <math|O<around|(|4|)>> covariance (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>) both
  arrangements have the same limit, so <math|M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|2>|)>=M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|2>|)>*M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>>:
  rotation covariance is an input to the phase decomposition. Reflection
  positivity holds in each phase because the compressed reflected Gram
  matrices are positive operators and characters are positive; that no field
  becomes null in a phase uses the free tests at short distances.
  Commutativity alone gives neither.

  For each phase apply Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D14>
  and<nbsp><reference|def:compression> to <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>>, obtaining
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>,
  <math|H<rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> and
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>. Fix
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> in the full-measure set with one-dimensional
  time-axis reflection ground space supplied by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>; Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC> extends
  ground-space uniqueness to every phase. Two further notions enter the
  reconstruction and the trace law.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Bounded weak commutant><label|def:D14d> A bounded operator
    <math|B> belongs to the weak commutant if, for every smeared field
    <math|\<Phi\>> and all <math|u,v\<in\>\<cal-D\>>, one has
    <math|u\<in\><math-up|Dom><space|0.17em>\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>> (automatic
    by<nbsp><reference|def:W1>) and <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*u,B*v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|u,B*\<Phi\>*v|\<rangle\>>>,
    where <math|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>> is the Hilbert-space adjoint.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Hermitian scalar labels><label|def:D14e> A label is Hermitian if
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|\<ast\>>=\<ell\>>, and Lorentz scalar if
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|lab>>*<around|(|\<Lambda\>,0|)>*\<ell\>=\<ell\>>
    for every <math|\<Lambda\>\<in\>S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>>.
  </definition>

  For the theory at a phase <math|\<xi\>> write
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>>,
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>>,
  <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|\<xi\>>>, and define its energy by
  <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|t*e<rsub|4>|)>=e<rsup|i*t*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>>.
  Its reflection comparison map is the linear isometry
  <math|<math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>:\<cal-H\><rsub|\<xi\>>\<to\>\<cal-H\><rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
  with <math|<math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>=\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
  that sends each product of smeared fields applied to the vacuum to the norm
  limit of its heat-regularized positive-time reflection vectors as the
  ordered heights tend to zero (proofs of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.2>).

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|RC. Reconstruction><label|prop:RC> For every
    <math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>, there are a faithful Wightman theory
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:wightman>) on <math|\<cal-L\>> and
    functions <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>> satisfying
    Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D15> and<nbsp><reference|def:D14f> with
    the response maps <math|\<iota\><rsub|d>> of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>. Its OS comparison map
    <math|<math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> is unitary,
    intertwines spatial translations and satisfies
    <math|<math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>=e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>><math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
    for <math|s\<geqslant\>0>, and

    <\equation*>
      ker H<rsub|\<xi\>>=<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<space|2em>ker
      H<rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>=<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Every bounded operator in the weak commutant of its fields is scalar.
  </theorem>

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>\U<reference|lem:H2.4>).
  Reflection positivity supplies the Hilbert spaces and the nonnegative
  energy; the slab bounds supply the growth estimates behind the boundary
  values; the restored rotations, continued to boosts, supply Lorentz
  covariance and, with the continuation, locality. The uniform low-energy
  estimate of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:strategy-reconstruction>,
  continuity of the vacuum-subtracted heat pairings and full support
  of<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> extend vacuum uniqueness from almost every phase to
  every phase, so the choice of<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> stays valid.
  The same lemmas give joint Schwartz distributions, phase averages and
  stress continuation.

  The label maps are common to all phases. The adjoint is induced by time
  reflection followed by conjugation, <math|<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d>*A|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=\<iota\><rsub|d>*<around|(|R<rsub|d><around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|refl>>|)>*<wide|A|\<bar\>>|)>>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-fields>). The Lorentz label action is the
  continuation of<nbsp><math|R<rsub|d>><nbsp><cite|FrohlichOsterwalderSeiler1983>:
  spatial rotations keep their Euclidean action, and for <math|i=1,2,3> the
  boost along <math|e<rsub|i>> has generator
  <math|-i<math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K<rsub|4*i>|)>>, where
  <math|K<rsub|4*i>=e<rsub|4>*e<rsub|i><rsup|<math-ss|T>>-e<rsub|i>*e<rsub|4><rsup|<math-ss|T>>>
  generates rotation in the <math|<around|(|e<rsub|4>,e<rsub|i>|)>> plane. In
  particular, a real <math|O<around|(|4|)>>-invariant field gives a Hermitian
  Lorentz scalar label. Retain the theory at <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> and
  suppress its subscript in <math|\<cal-H\>,\<cal-D\>,\<Omega\>,\<Phi\>> and
  the other relativistic data.

  <subsection|Trace and products><label|sec:trace-products>

  Several ultraviolet statements of different strength are proved, and they
  should not be confused.

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|5|5|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Statement>|<cell|What
    it asserts>>|<row|<cell|Running (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>)>|<cell|A
    volume-uniform leading logarithmic slope of the bare coupling of the
    tuned family>>|<row|<cell|Maxwell comparison
    (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>)>|<cell|Convergence along a
    sequence of radii, through scale-dependent frames that include field
    renormalization and mixing; not a perturbative expansion of each
    composite correlation>>|<row|<cell|Stress comparison
    (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>)>|<cell|The same, with derivatives
    and with the normalized stress insertions>>|<row|<cell|Trace law
    (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>)>|<cell|A specific asymptotic through
    all positive radii, in the selected phase>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The trace is centered in the phase already selected.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Centered trace><label|def:D16> For the tensor <math|T> fixed after
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST> and <math|r\<gtr\>0>, put

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<tau\>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4>T<rsub|a*a>,>|<cell|e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>>|<cell|=S<rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|\<tau\>|)>,>|<cell|\<Theta\>>|<cell|=\<tau\>-e<rsub|<math-up|tr>><unitfield|4>,>>|<row|<cell|<tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>>|<cell|=S<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;-r*e<rsub|4>/2,r*e<rsub|4>/2|)>,>|<cell|<traceamplitude>>|<cell|=<frac|3<dimG>|\<pi\><rsup|4>><space|0.17em>.>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>
    </align*>
  </definition>

  The one-point value is position independent, and centering removes the
  vacuum contribution in the selected phase. Adding a real multiple of
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|a*b><unitfield|4>> to <math|T> preserves its shell
  Ward identities, the limit in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC> and
  <math|\<Theta\>>. The fixed stress tensor is centered in the mixture; the
  selected-phase centering subtracts its own one-point value.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|TR. Trace law><label|prop:TR> The selected field <math|\<Theta\>>
    is nonzero, real and <math|O<around|(|4|)>> invariant; its label
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>> is Hermitian and Lorentz scalar
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14e>). For every
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>,f|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    As <math|r*\<downarrow\>*0> through all positive radii,

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>=<traceamplitude>\<gtr\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  The amplitude comes from the free scalar curvature square, with
  <math|\<alpha\>=1,\<ldots\>,<dimG>> indexing an orthonormal colour basis:

  <\equation*>
    <curvaturesquare>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b,\<alpha\>>:F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>:,<space|2em><maxwellcorr><rsub|2><around|(|<curvaturesquare>,<curvaturesquare>;-e<rsub|4>/2,e<rsub|4>/2|)>=<frac|3<dimG>|\<pi\><rsup|4>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  At running coupling <math|\<beta\>> the scalar trace coefficient is
  <math|4*d<rsub|2>/\<beta\>>, with <math|d<rsub|2>=-b<rsub|0>/2>, computed
  from a lattice Ward identity and the conserved line
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:strategy-trace>,
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:I-coefficient>); since
  <math|<slope>=2*b<rsub|0>>, its leading value at radius <math|r> is
  <math|4*d<rsub|2>/<around|(|<slope>log <around|(|1/r|)>|)>=-1/log
  <around|(|1/r|)>>, and squaring it with the free correlation
  gives<nbsp><math|<traceamplitude>> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:strategy-trace>).
  The comparison needs fourth-order information beyond
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>, with the rate that the slowly growing
  coefficients require; its estimates are uniform in the phase, although the
  theorem is stated at<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> only.

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-I>;
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.1>, <reference|lem:I1.2>,
  <reference|lem:I-radii> and<nbsp><reference|lem:I-transfer>).

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|Step 1>Begin with any sequence of positive radii tending to zero.

    <item*|Step 2>Adjustable stopping levels give the comparison along a
    subsequence, at generic configurations, not at the time-axis pair
    defining<nbsp><math|<tracetwopoint>>.

    <item*|Step 3>The positive spectral representation below makes
    <math|<tracetwopoint>> real and nonincreasing; the monotone function is
    <math|<tracetwopoint>> itself, not <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>>.

    <item*|Step 4>By <math|O<around|(|4|)>> invariance, generic separations
    slightly below and slightly above the target radius bound
    <math|<tracetwopoint>> there from both sides.

    <item*|Step 5>Every starting sequence thus has a subsequence with the
    same normalized limit, which gives the limit through all radii.
  </enumerate>

  The same proof identifies centering on the word domain,
  <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>,f|)>=\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<tau\>,f|)>-e<rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<big|int>f|)><math-up|Id><rsub|\<cal-H\>>>,
  and constructs the unique positive tempered spectral measure
  <math|<tracespectrum>>, supported in <math|<forwardcone>>, with

  <\equation*>
    d*\<mu\><rsub|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>,f|)>*\<Omega\>>=<around|\||<fourierplus|f>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum>,<space|2em><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-r*\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|r\<gtr\>0|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<fourierplus|f><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*\<b-up-p\>\<cdot\>x>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>.
  Its Lorentz invariance and high-energy limits are
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> and<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.3>. Finite Wick
  profiles cannot reproduce the factor <math|r<rsup|-8>*log<rsup|-2><around|(|1/r|)>>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|NF. Finite-profile exclusion><label|prop:NF> For every finite Wick
    profile <math|W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>>> and every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,
    there is <math|0\<less\>r\<less\>\<delta\>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>\<ne\>W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>><around|(|r|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </proposition>

  The reason is the form of the expansions (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.1>).
  Each mass-shell kernel has an expansion to every finite order in integer
  powers of <math|r> and nonnegative powers of <math|log r>, so a finite
  profile is, to bounded error, a finite sum of monomials
  <math|r<rsup|a><around|(|log r|)><rsup|k>> with <math|a\<in\><Z>>,
  <math|k\<in\><N>>. Multiplication by <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>>
  makes the terms with <math|a\<gtr\>-8> vanish and the leading remaining
  nonzero term diverge, so a finite limit must be zero; the positive limit of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR> excludes equality on every interval
  <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.2> treats
  polynomial mass-shell comparisons, and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.3>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.4> give Fock countertests with their own
  comparison classes.

  The phase quantifiers differ from statement to statement, and the following
  table records them.

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|6|6|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Statement>|<cell|Scope>>|<row|<cell|Slab,
    comparison and low-energy estimates>|<cell|Uniform in the
    phase>>|<row|<cell|Vacuum uniqueness in the proof of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH>>|<cell|Almost every phase (an
    intermediate step)>>|<row|<cell|Reconstruction,
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>>|<cell|Every phase>>|<row|<cell|Trace
    law, Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>>|<cell|The selected phase
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>; uniform estimates in its proof do not change
    this>>|<row|<cell|Product expansion, Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP>>|<cell|Every
    phase and the mixture; degree cutoff and spectators fixed before the
    threshold, states and positions after it>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The product expansion is a decoder, stated for the tables
  <math|S<rsup|\<zeta\>>>, <math|\<zeta\>\<in\><phases>\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>>,
  on the common quotient fields. For each degree<nbsp><math|j>, finitely many
  probe words placed away from the origin, with biorthogonal coordinate
  functionals, distinguish the fields of<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>. Applied to a
  cluster of fields at positions<nbsp><math|x> around a centre<nbsp><math|o>,
  the probes read off the field with the same answers, averaged over
  <math|O<around|(|4|)>> and conjugation; lower degrees are removed before
  the next one is read, and the coordinates in the basis
  <math|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>> are the coefficients
  <math|c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x,o|)>>
  (Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D31>\U<reference|def:D33>). For two
  fields, <math|A<around|(|x|)>*B<around|(|0|)>\<sim\>C<rsup|\<b-1\>><rsub|A*B><around|(|x|)>*\<b-1\>+<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>C<rsup|\<lambda\>><rsub|A*B><around|(|x|)>*O<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|0|)>+\<cdots\>>
  is tested inside every correlation with distant spectators, and the error
  after degree <math|D> is at most <math|r<rsup|D+1-d<rsub|A>-d<rsub|B>-\<varepsilon\>>>:
  for small <math|D> it may still be singular, and what improves with
  <math|D> is its order. The probes, the bases and the coefficients are fixed
  once from the mixed table, before spectators, state indices and
  truncations; spectator and state independence hold by construction, and the
  content of the theorem is the size of the remainder of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>. The coefficients depend on this
  prescription, the table and the probes; they are not a canonical expansion,
  and the theorem concerns this one coefficient system.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|OP. Operator product expansion><label|prop:OP> For every finite
    list <math|\<b-A\>>, <math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<eta\>,\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|j\<in\><N>> and
    <math|\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>>, some <math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> gives,
    for <math|0\<less\>r\<less\>r<rsub|0>> and every
    <math|<around|(|r,M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>,\<eta\>|)>>-cluster,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x,o|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|j-<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>-\<varepsilon\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For every pair of finite lists <math|\<b-A\>,\<b-B\>>,
    <math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<eta\>,\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>,\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|D\<in\><N>>, some <math|r<rsub|1>\<gtr\>0> gives, for
    <math|0\<less\>r\<less\>r<rsub|1>>, such clusters,
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>>-clear spectators and all state
    indices<nbsp><math|\<zeta\>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<zeta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|D+1-<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>-\<varepsilon\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  <em|Idea of the proof> (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1>\U<reference|lem:J1.2>).
  The discarded part is bounded in the compression algebra; evaluation at a
  character or at the vacuum state does not increase the bound, so it holds
  in every phase and in the mixture. The bound enters through moments
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-roots>): lattice cluster replacement makes a
  group of insertions minus its matched field, one insertion at the centre,
  small in chessboard seminorm; the chessboard estimate applied to <math|2*n>
  widely translated, alternately conjugated copies bounds the <math|n>th
  vacuum moment of the compressed remainder<nbsp><math|U>, so that
  <math|<big|int><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|><rsup|2*n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsup|2*n>>
  for every<nbsp><math|n>, and continuity in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>> with full
  support of<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> gives <math|sup<rsub|\<xi\>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B>.
  Lattice estimates at three scales, the resolution of the insertions, the
  fusion scale and the exterior cell, give the degree count
  <math|D+1-<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>> up to factors
  <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|log <around|(|1/r|)>|)>>>, which
  <math|r<rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>> absorbs for small<nbsp><math|r>. The two
  thresholds have the separate dependencies of the statement. The estimate
  holds at each finite truncation<nbsp><math|D>; no series in<nbsp><math|D>
  is claimed to converge. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.4>\U<reference|lem:J1.5>
  and Remark<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.3> give nested and integrated estimates.

  Contact subtractions use the same original fields and correlations, with
  separate projections that extract fields through finite correlation probes;
  their clearance is a radius keeping the collapsed cluster separated from
  these probes, independent of the spectator
  clearance<nbsp><math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>>. A collision forest is a
  family of subsets of slots, any two disjoint or nested. Since
  <math|S<rsub|n><rsup|\<zeta\>>> satisfies<nbsp><reference|it:E7>, some
  contact extension exists by the standard extension theorem for kernels of
  polynomial growth at the diagonals; the content of the next theorem is that
  the explicit subtraction of normal Taylor jets along collision forests
  produces one, with the local order bounds of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.3>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|CT. Contact extension><label|prop:CT> For every state index
    <math|\<zeta\>>, arity <math|n> and list
    <math|\<b-A\>\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>>, the contact construction of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-J.2>, fixed on the underlying
    prescription of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1>, gives a distribution
    <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>>> on <math|<R><rsup|4*n>>
    such that, for every separated test <math|f>,

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|n><rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </theorem>

  The proof is in Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.1>\U<reference|lem:J2.3>. For
  two insertions, write the test in the centroid and one relative coordinate;
  for each contact degree <math|w> below the critical order<nbsp><math|L> of
  the pair, subtract the degree-<math|w> contact projection of the pair at
  the centroid, tested against the Taylor jet of order <math|L-1-w> of the
  test in the relative coordinate, and the subtracted expression gains in
  each shell; three or more insertions bring partial collisions and nested or
  disjoint forests. A separated test extended by zero has vanishing normal
  jets on every collision diagonal, so only the correlation integral remains.
  For a general test, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> controls the
  contact terms and the signed residuals after the child subtractions, and
  the shell induction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.2> gives a continuous
  functional; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.1> supplies covariant contact
  projections with real bases. The extension is a later construction from the
  separated correlations, not a limit of unsubtracted lattice correlations at
  coincident points; neither uniqueness, positivity nor contact Ward
  identities are asserted.

  <section|Deduction of the main theorem><label|sec:deduction>

  The principal results of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:continuum-results> concern one lattice family and
  one volume refinement; the deduction assembles them in the order of their
  choices.

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>>
    <\enumerate>
      <item*|1><with|the-label|1|<label|step:deduction-choice>>
      Fix<nbsp><math|G> and<nbsp><math|<blockpenalty>\<gtr\>0>, and make the
      common choices in the order of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>.
      The Wilson start<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-Wilson-initialization> begins the
      induction, Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG> advances it, and
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT> selects trajectories reaching the
      common endpoint. These choices precede any volume sequence.

      <item*|2><with|the-label|2|<label|step:deduction-family>> Fix the local
      sections, reference levels and terminal maps on these trajectories;
      Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08> defines every insertion, also below
      its reference level. The local sections are polynomials in the links of
      their fixed stencils, hence continuous and local, and the terminal maps
      change only their coefficients, which preserves gauge invariance.
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT> at <math|m=N> gives the volume-uniform
      running law with <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=<slope>\<gtr\>0>. Thus
      <math|\<cal-W\>> is regular (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:Q1>).

      <item*|3><with|the-label|3|<label|step:deduction-uv>>
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV> gives the full fixed-volume limit for
      every field and difference list, so every admissible separated test has
      a full inner limit before any volume extraction. This proves<nbsp>(i).

      <item*|4><with|the-label|4|<label|step:deduction-volume>> Given a
      strictly increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>>,
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV> chooses<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>>
      and<nbsp><math|S> with ordered convergence along
      <math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>> and properties E1\UE7; the same
      extraction fixes the corrected coefficient maps. This proves the table
      part of<nbsp>(ii).

      <item*|5><with|the-label|5|<label|step:deduction-stress>> Define
      <math|F<rsub|d>=V<rsub|d>/<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>>
      and<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\>> by Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>.
      Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> gives the inclusions and
      derivatives, with injective response maps whose images
      cover<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\>>, and Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST> gives
      the conserved tensor, normalized by its shell fluxes, on these fields.

      <item*|6><with|the-label|6|<label|step:deduction-phase>>
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH> gives
      <math|<phases>,\<nu\>,S<rsup|\<xi\>>> on these quotient spaces.
      Choose<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> in the full-measure set with
      one-dimensional time-axis reflection ground space
      (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>). Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>
      reconstructs every phase with a unique vacuum, which proves<nbsp>(iii);
      retain the theory and continuation at<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>.

      <item*|7><with|the-label|7|<label|step:deduction-hierarchy>> Set
      <math|\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>>*<around|(|d,\<b-up-c\>|)>=<around|(|d,<around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>|)>>;
      representatives make it onto. The unit response is nonzero since its
      one-point function is one, so faithfulness in
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC> gives a nonzero acting field and, with
      Step<nbsp><reference|step:deduction-stress>, the label clauses of Q3.
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH> gives its phase clauses, and the
      reconstruction gives Q2 and Q4. Assign arbitrary values to the quotient
      mixture and phase correlations on the collision diagonals; this
      supplies the function domains of Q0 and has no distributional content.
      Separated tests vanish there, so ordered convergence is unchanged, and
      representative independence gives <math|S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>>*v;x|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>>
      on<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>. This identifies the lattice limit
      <math|\<cal-W\>\<to\>S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>\<circ\>\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>>>
      of<nbsp>(ii).

      <item*|8><with|the-label|8|<label|step:deduction-trace>> Center the
      trace of the fixed tensor in the selected phase by
      Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D16>. Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>
      gives vacuum centering, the positive amplitude and the full-radius law,
      and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:NF> excludes every finite Wick
      profile; these give Q6 and<nbsp>(iv).

      <item*|9><with|the-label|9|<label|step:deduction-products>> Choose the
      underlying prescription of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1> once, with
      its original fields and correlation table.
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP> gives Q5 and its error bounds.
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT> and
      Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.1>\U<reference|lem:J2.3> use the same
      prescription, with separate contact projections, separating functionals
      and clearance, to construct real bases of the contact strata and the
      contact extensions; the contact proof uses the lattice-derived
      estimates of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated> and the
      signed transfer of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer>. This
      proves<nbsp>(v) and completes<nbsp>(ii).
    </enumerate>
  </render-proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Further results><label|rem:further-results> Further conclusions
    about these same objects are proved in Part<nbsp>II.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2> gives convergence at moving points, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.3> the uniform low-energy bounds. For the
    field<nbsp><math|\<Theta\>> of Step<nbsp><reference|step:deduction-trace>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.1> gives the operator identity of centering,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> the unique spectral measure and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.3> its high-energy limits.
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.2>, <reference|lem:I2.3>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.4> give further polynomial and Fock
    countertests in the selected phase, and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.4>\U<reference|lem:J1.5> and
    Remark<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.3> nested and integrated product estimates
    for the same prescription.
  </remark>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Endpoint tuning><label|prop:endpoint-tuning> The specified
    recursion also varies the endpoint analytically. There is a width
    <math|w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>\<gtr\>0>, independent of<nbsp><math|N,p>,
    such that, for every<nbsp><math|N,p> and <math|s\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>,
    exactly one bare coupling<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|s|)>>
    starts a trajectory of the specified recursion reaching
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>*s> at level zero. It
    satisfies <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|0|)>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> and
    has a holomorphic extension<nbsp><math|b<rsub|N,p>> to
    <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>1> with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I12-endpoint-tuning>b<rsub|N,p><around|(|s|)>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|s|)>*<space|2em><around|(|-1\<less\>s\<less\>1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    This is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.3>; the proofs above do not use it.
  </remark>

  <section|Correspondence with the formal
  statement><label|sec:correspondence>

  This section states the existence theorem in the exact form of the formal
  statement and deduces it from Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>. The formal
  statement constrains abstract hierarchy data by the conditions Q0\UQ6, of
  which Q1 is Definition<nbsp><reference|def:Q1>. They do not impose every
  property established above: the correlations need not be multilinear, the
  logarithmic slope <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>> is only required to be positive,
  and the degree-four field<nbsp><math|\<Theta\>> is not identified as a
  stress trace. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:formal-conventions> records the
  conventions in which the formal statement differs from the main text.

  <subsection|Hierarchy data and the conditions
  Q0\UQ6><label|sec:hierarchy-data>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Q0. Hierarchy data><label|def:Q0> Hierarchy data over a Wilson
    family<nbsp><math|\<cal-W\>> consist of:

    <\itemize>
      <item>complex Hilbert and vector spaces<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\>,\<cal-L\>>,
      and a relativistic datum with the group of
      Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:formal-poincare>;

      <item>finite-dimensional complex normed spaces<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>>,
      linear maps<nbsp><math|\<iota\><rsub|d>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>\<cal-L\>>, and
      <math|\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>>:\<cal-T\>\<to\>\<bbb-F\>>, where
      <math|\<bbb-F\>=<big|sqcup><rsub|d>F<rsub|d>>;

      <item>a compact metric space<nbsp><math|<phases>>, a Borel
      measure<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> and <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\><phases>>;

      <item>functions <math|S<rsub|n><rsup|\<zeta\>>:\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>\<times\><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>\<to\><C>>
      for <math|\<zeta\>\<in\><phases>\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>>,
      and <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n>:\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>\<times\><C><rsup|n>\<times\><around|(|<R><rsup|3>|)><rsup|n>\<to\><C>>;

      <item>finite sets<nbsp><math|<channels|j>>,
      fields<nbsp><math|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>\<in\>F<rsub|j>>, and
      coefficients <math|c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>:\<bbb-F\><rsup|I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>\<times\><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)><rsup|I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>\<times\><R><rsup|4>\<to\><C>>
      for every finite index set<nbsp><math|I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>;

      <item>an element<nbsp><math|\<Theta\>\<in\>F<rsub|4>>.
    </itemize>
  </definition>

  The spaces, maps and functions of Q0 are arbitrary on the displayed
  domains; put <math|deg <around|(|d,A|)>=d> and
  <math|\<iota\>*<around|(|d,A|)>=\<iota\><rsub|d>*A>. The conditions below
  constrain the correlations at separated positions. In Q2\UQ6 all cited
  definitions and formulas are read with these hierarchy data and with the
  conventions of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:formal-conventions>; in
  definitions indexed by a phase, read <math|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>>,
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>> and
  <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>> as the
  <math|\<Omega\>>, <math|\<Phi\>> and <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n>> of these
  data.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Q2. Wightman axioms><label|def:Q2> The relativistic datum of Q0 is
    a Wightman theory in the sense of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:wightman>.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Q3. Labels and phases><label|def:Q3> (a) Every
    nonzero<nbsp><math|\<ell\>\<in\>\<cal-L\>> has
    <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\>,f|)>\<ne\>0> for
    some<nbsp><math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>>. (b) Each<nbsp><math|\<iota\><rsub|d>>
    is injective, their images cover<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\>>, and
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>>> is onto. (c)
    <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><around|(|A|)>,f|)>\<ne\>0> for
    some<nbsp><math|A\<in\>\<bbb-F\>> and <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>>. (d) The
    measure<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> is a probability measure of full support, and
    for every <math|n>, <math|\<b-A\>\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>> and
    <math|x\<in\><Conf|n>>, <math|\<xi\>\<mapsto\>S<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>>
    is continuous and <math|S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>=<big|int><rsub|<phases>>S<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>.
    (e) Each<nbsp><math|S<rsub|n><rsup|\<zeta\>>> is simultaneously
    permutation symmetric and smooth on<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Q4. Selected continuation><label|def:Q4> The
    functions<nbsp><math|<math-ss|F><rsub|n>> satisfy the ordered-time
    continuation condition of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D15> and the
    boundary condition of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14f>
    at<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>, read as in
    Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:integral-zero>, using the relativistic datum
    and the maps<nbsp><math|\<iota\><rsub|d>> of the hierarchy data.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Q5. Products><label|def:Q5> For the channels, fields, coefficients
    and tables<nbsp><math|S<rsup|\<zeta\>>> of Q0,
    form<nbsp><math|\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<zeta\>>> by
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>. These coefficients and remainders
    satisfy the two inequalities of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP>: the
    coefficient threshold depends on <math|I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>,\<b-A\>,M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>,\<eta\>,\<varepsilon\>,j,\<lambda\>>;
    the remainder threshold depends on <math|I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>,I<rsub|<math-up|sp>>,\<b-A\>,\<b-B\>,M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>,\<eta\>,\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>,\<varepsilon\>,D>.
    Each threshold is uniform in positions and state indices.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Q6. Trace test><label|def:Q6> For the
    chosen<nbsp><math|\<Theta\>\<in\>F<rsub|4>>, with <math|<tracetwopoint>>
    its time-axis two-point function in the
    phase<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>
    (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:short-distance-defs>) and
    <math|<traceamplitude>=3<dimG>/\<pi\><rsup|4>>, require
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>,f|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=0>
    for every <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>>,

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|r*\<downarrow\>*0> r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>=<traceamplitude>\<gtr\>0<space|1em><text|in
      ><C><space|0.17em>,
    </equation*>

    and the finite-profile exclusion of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:NF>.
  </definition>

  For the lattice limit, <math|S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>\<circ\>\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>>>
  denotes the kernels <math|K<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>=S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|mix>>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>><around|(|v<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>><around|(|v<rsub|n>|)>;x|)>>.

  <assign|maintheorem-nr|0>

  <\maintheorem>
    <dueto|Existence><label|thm:A> For every gauge group of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, there is a regular (Q1) Wilson
    family<nbsp><math|\<cal-W\>> such that, for every strictly
    increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>:<N>\<to\><N>>, there are a strictly
    increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>> and hierarchy data
    over<nbsp><math|\<cal-W\>> (Q0) satisfying Q2--Q6, with

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-W\>\<longrightarrow\>S<rsup|<math-up|mix>>\<circ\>\<pi\><rsub|<math-up|quo>><space|1em><text|along
      >P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q><space|1em><text|in the sense of
      Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </maintheorem>

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:A> fixes the lattice theory before the volume
  sequence. Q2 gives relativistic fields; Q3 and Q4 express the limiting
  correlations as a phase average and continue the selected phase to those
  fields; Q5 approximates local products uniformly in the state and in
  separated spectators; Q6 fixes the vacuum centering and the short-distance
  law of the distinguished degree-four field.

  <\render-proof|Deduction of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:A>>
    <\enumerate>
      <item*|10><with|the-label|10|<label|step:deduction-conclusion>>
      Steps<nbsp><reference|step:deduction-choice>\U<reference|step:deduction-products>
      of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:deduction> give
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B> and hierarchy data satisfying Q2\UQ6 on
      the same family and volume refinement. Fix
      any<nbsp><math|<blockpenalty>\<gtr\>0>, for
      instance<nbsp><math|<blockpenalty>=1>. The regular family
      precedes<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>>; its refinement and hierarchy data
      follow it, with the ordered convergence of
      Step<nbsp><reference|step:deduction-hierarchy>. This proves
      Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:A>.
    </enumerate>
  </render-proof>

  As in the formal statement, Q0\UQ3 do not require
  <math|S<rsub|n><rsup|\<zeta\>>> to be multilinear in the fields; in the
  construction they are, since <math|S> and every <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>>> are
  tables (Theorems<nbsp><reference|prop:IV> and<nbsp><reference|prop:PH>).
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:A> leaves <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>
  unspecified; Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT> supplies
  <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=<slope>=2*b<rsub|0>=11<casimir>/<around|(|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>>.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Nontriviality><label|rem:nontriviality> Q3(c) requires a nonzero
    smeared field; given Q3(a)\U(b) this is equivalent to
    <math|\<cal-L\>\<ne\>0>, and in the construction it is met by the unit
    field (Step<nbsp><reference|step:deduction-hierarchy>). Nontriviality
    comes from the trace: <math|\<Theta\>\<ne\>0>
    (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>), so faithfulness gives
    <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>,f|)>\<ne\>0> for
    some<nbsp><math|f>, while its vacuum expectation vanishes; hence it is
    not a multiple of the identity. Q6 gives the positive limit
    <math|3<dimG>/\<pi\><rsup|4>> of <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>>,
    and on no interval <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> does
    <math|<tracetwopoint>> agree with a finite Wick profile. Thus every
    Gaussian or finite free-field realization whose two-point profile
    for<nbsp><math|\<Theta\>> belongs to the class of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D17> is excluded.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Formal conventions><label|sec:formal-conventions>

  The formal statement differs from the main text in the following
  conventions; none of them changes the content of the results.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Abstract Poincaré group><label|rem:formal-poincare> In the formal
    statement a relativistic datum carries a set <math|\<cal-P\><rprime|'>>
    with a unit, a product and a topology, and a homeomorphism
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|P>>:\<cal-P\><rprime|'>\<to\>S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>\<ltimes\><R><rsup|4>>
    respecting the identity and the products (which makes
    <math|\<cal-P\><rprime|'>> a topological group), a strongly continuous
    unitary representation <math|<math-ss|U>> of <math|\<cal-P\><rprime|'>>
    with <math|<math-ss|U><around|(|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|P>><rsup|-1>*<around|(|1,z|)>|)>=<math-ss|T><around|(|z|)>>,
    label maps <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|lab>>>
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\><rprime|'>>, and the action on tests through
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|P>>>. Transport along
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|P>>> turns such a datum into one for the
    ordinary group of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14a>, and conversely,
    and it preserves W0\UW3. Definition<nbsp><reference|def:wightman> is
    therefore equivalent to the formal one.
  </remark>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Nonintegrable integrands><label|rem:integral-zero> In the boundary
    condition of Q4 the formal statement does not require integrability:
    there a nonintegrable function has integral zero. For the continuations
    constructed in Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC> the integrands are
    integrable for every <math|r\<gtr\>0> (by the
    bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-poly> and rapid decay of the tests), so this
    convention is never used for them, and
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14f> requires integrability.
  </remark>

  The formal development also states the volume limit as local uniform
  convergence, on <math|<Gen|n>>, of the fixed-volume kernels
  <math|M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|P<around|(|k|)>,v>> restricted to the
  periodic generic domains <math|<around|{|x:x<rsub|i><rsup|a>-x<rsub|j><rsup|a>\<nin\>L<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>><Z><around|(|i\<ne\>j,a=1,\<ldots\>,4|)>|}>>.
  This form follows from<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>: a compact subset
  of<nbsp><math|<Gen|n>> has bounded diameter and positive minimal coordinate
  separation, so it eventually lies in the periodic generic domain, and
  <math|<Gen|n>\<subset\><Conf|n>>.

  <subsection|An averaged countertest><label|sec:averaged-countertest>

  The averaged Fock countertest of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-I>
  belongs to this correspondence: it compares the trace field with free
  comparison vectors modified by arbitrary maps that preserve physical inner
  products and fix <math|U*\<cal-H\>>. Its integrands are constant, so the
  convention of Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:integral-zero> is not needed for
  them.

  Let <math|n\<geqslant\>0> and let <math|U> be a comparison
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D30>), with <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|n>> as in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D29>. For a probability space
  <math|<around|(|Z,\<lambda\>|)>>, let each
  <math|P<rsub|z>:\<cal-F\><rsub|n>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|n>> preserve physical
  inner products and fix <math|U*\<cal-H\>> pointwise, without assuming
  linearity. The maps <math|P<rsub|z>> model transformations of the free
  comparison vectors that fix every embedded interacting state in
  <math|U*\<cal-H\>>. Because physical inner products are preserved, neither
  matrix element in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.3> changes, so the averaged
  inequality states that averaging these transformations cannot restore the
  relative-locality identity.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Localized averaged countertest><label|lem:I2.4> For every such
    <math|n,U,<around|(|Z,\<lambda\>|)>,<around|(|P<rsub|z>|)>> and
    <math|r\<gtr\>0>, there are open <math|A,B>, with
    <math|<wide|A|\<bar\>>\<subset\>B*<around|(|0,r|)>> and
    <math|<minksquare><around|(|x-y|)>\<less\>0> on
    <math|<wide|A|\<bar\>>\<times\><wide|B|\<bar\>>>, and tests <math|f,h> as
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.3>, supported respectively in <math|A,B>,
    such that

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|Z><around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|z>*\<cal-E\>*<around|(|-h|)>,\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)>|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><around|(|z|)>\<ne\><big|int><rsub|Z><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>,P<rsub|z>*\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><around|(|z|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For physical <math|v> and <math|w\<in\>U*\<cal-H\>>
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D30>),
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|z>*v,w|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|P<rsub|z>*v,P<rsub|z>*w|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|v,w|\<rangle\>>>.
    Thus averaging preserves both integrands.

    Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.3> in <math|B*<around|(|0,r/2|)>>.
    Compactness and strict spacelike separation give support neighbourhoods
    <math|A,B> with spacelike separated closures and
    <math|<wide|A|\<bar\>>\<subset\>B*<around|(|0,r|)>>.
  </proof>

  <section|Constants and order of choice><label|sec:constants>

  The choices come in four groups. First fix the gauge group, the block
  penalty, the local sections and, for each stage, the constants that do not
  involve the block ratio. Next choose the block ratios, and then delay the
  entry into each stage until the coupling and the period satisfy its
  remaining inequalities. These choices determine the lattice family, with
  its couplings and insertions, before any volume sequence is prescribed;
  only afterwards are the volume refinement and the continuum objects chosen.
  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices> records the individual
  requirements in this order. At each stage there are finitely many
  inequalities; a later coupling threshold or entry level may be increased
  without changing the earlier data.

  } } } } }

  <big-table|<label|tab:constant-choices>
  <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-width|0.035par>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-width|0.22par>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-width|0.71par>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-hyphen|t>|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|Choice>|<cell|Dependence,
  requirements and purpose>>|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|Gauge normalization;
  <math|<blockpenalty>\<gtr\>0>>|<cell|Fix<nbsp><math|G>, its realization,
  probability Haar measure and the metric,
  then<nbsp><math|<dimG>,<casimir>,b<rsub|0>,<slope>> as in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>; <math|<slope>\<gtr\>0>. All later
  choices use these data.>>|<row|<cell|2>|<cell|Frame and predictor cutoffs;
  axial factor>|<cell|Depend on<nbsp><math|G>. The cutoffs select the
  unique-minimizer branches of the block kernel and are small enough for
  these branches to be valid on every patch (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>).
  On a radius-<math|R> patch, the total link chord in an eligible axial frame
  is at most <math|a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*<around|(|2*R+1|)>> times the total
  plaquette chord, with <math|a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>\<geqslant\>16>.>>|<row|<cell|3>|<cell|Bases,
  norms, local sections, stencils>|<cell|Depend on<nbsp><math|G,d>; Taylor
  normalization, reality and<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>> covariance
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>, Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D07>--<reference|def:D08>).>>|<row|<cell|4>|<cell|Stage
  constants>|<cell|Depend on items 1--3 and<nbsp><math|j>. Derivative
  orders<nbsp><math|\<frak-q\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>6>, spatial
  powers<nbsp><math|\<varpi\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>2>, support
  exponents<nbsp><math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>=100>; the path weight
  <math|w<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>\<gtr\>0> and the bound
  <math|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>\<gtr\>0> on the weighted row, cubic
  and quartic sums of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>. Fix the whole
  nondecreasing sequence<nbsp><math|K<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> bounding the
  leading coefficient maps and their inverses, including the finite symmetry
  averages (before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>); then nondecreasing
  column bounds <math|<colbound|j>\<geqslant\>64*K<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>>,
  including derivative factorials and basis-recombination constants
  (item<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns> there), which bound the marked
  coefficient transfer (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>).>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|With
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> half a uniform lower bound for the free
  effective plaquette forms of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, for the top
  form and both incoming stages, choose clipping coefficients
  <math|0\<less\>16*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,j>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>\<leqslant\>min
  <around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|j>,1|)>>, so that the remaining free form has the
  positive row factor of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>; the threshold
  of the relaxed threshold test grows through
  <math|e<rsup|<math-up|dist>/R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>> with
  <math|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*w<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>\<geqslant\>12>.
  With <math|d<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0> the ratio-independent one-loop bound of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>, fix the drift bound
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>>\<geqslant\>d<rsub|j>+1>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>). Orders, powers and support exponents are
  nondecreasing along levels, and orders and powers tend to infinity. All
  costs that may not depend on the ratio are now
  fixed.>>|<row|<cell|5>|<cell|Odd stage ratios
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>>>|<cell|Depend on items 1--4;
  nondecreasing, <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>\<geqslant\>3> and
  above every ratio threshold, including<nbsp><math|u<rsub|j>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>. At <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>> and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>=\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>>,
  <math|<with|math-display|true|<frac|\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>>|log
  \<ell\><rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>min <around|(|<slope>/4,j<rsup|-2>|)>,<frac|log
  <around|(|1+A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>+<around|(|w<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)><rsup|-1>+<colbound|j>+R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>+\<sigma\><rsub|j>|)>|log
  \<ell\><rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>j<rsup|-2>,>>which control the drift and the
  logarithmic stage costs relative to<nbsp><math|log \<ell\><rsub|m>>
  (Theorems<nbsp><reference|prop:RG> and<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>). Also the
  graph and matching requirements<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-graph-reserve>--<eqref|eq:E-matching-choices>,
  the local marked error requirement of item<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph>
  before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>, the ratio
  inequalities<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-stage-room> and the source comparison
  constants through<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>; among them
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|j,k>*\<zeta\><rsub|j>>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>) and <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pb>>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1/256>
  (proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-cutoff-continuity>).>>|<row|<cell|6>|<cell|Exponents
  and coupling entry bounds>|<cell|Depend on items 1--5. The positive
  antitone exponents<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|j>> control the quadratic loop
  bound of item<nbsp>9. The bounds<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ent>>>
  are lower bounds on the reference coupling whenever stage<nbsp><math|j> is
  used. They meet the finite maximum of the remaining stage requirements,
  including those of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G.2>,
  <reference|sec:partII-I.1> and<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-J>, the
  comparison entry bounds<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-Gaussian-entry> for each arity
  admitted at that stage, and the insertion-initialization bounds of
  item<nbsp><reference|it:E-birth>. They are chosen so that, at every level
  from stage entry on, <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>log
  <refbeta><rsub|m>> (item<nbsp><reference|it:E-ratio-log> before
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>) and <math|max
  <around|(|\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|J<rsub|m>>,\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|J<rsub|m>-1>|)>\<leqslant\><cutoffsize><rsub|m>>,
  with both adjacent stage ratios at stage transitions and the finite
  coefficient-error thresholds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-transfer> (used
  in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-I.1>
  and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-loss>).>>|<row|<cell|7>|<cell|<math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>,
  entry levels <math|m<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ent>>>, minimum periods
  <math|n<rsub|j><rsup|min>>>|<cell|Depend on items 1--6. The
  levels<nbsp><math|m<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ent>>> delay the first use of
  stage<nbsp><math|j>; <math|n<rsub|j><rsup|min>> are its minimum lattice
  periods. Choose <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<gtr\>1> large enough for the
  initial coupling requirements, including
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|224>>, so that
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1/128>
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-coordinate>, and <math|t<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1>
  at every level. Entry levels and periods satisfy the remaining level and
  geometric requirements, including the scale
  restrictions<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> and the comparison
  intervals<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window>. For each incoming stage
  <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>, fix integers <math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>,h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>\<geqslant\>1>,
  depending only on the stage data, and require at every step and
  every<nbsp><math|p> <math|<with|math-display|true|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>><space|0.17em>,>>which
  postponing stage entry achieves. The marked construction uses
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-marked-costs>, with weight
  requirements<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-weights> and entry
  requirements<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-entrance>; also the support-enlargement and
  occupancy requirements in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-lattice>
  and the regular marked weight inequalities before
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-marked-costs>.>>|<row|<cell|8>|<cell|<math|J<rsub|m>,\<ell\><rsub|m>,D<rsub|m>>,
  <math|a<rsub|m>,L<rsub|p>>, <math|<reflevel|d>>>|<cell|Depend on items
  1--7. <math|D<rsub|0>=1>, <math|D<rsub|m+1>=\<ell\><rsub|m>*D<rsub|m>>,
  <math|a<rsub|m>=D<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>, <math|s<rsub|m>=log D<rsub|m>>,
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>=\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>,
  <math|4\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>+1>,
  <math|J<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|0\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m><rsup|-1>>,
  <math|log \<ell\><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|log
  \<ell\><rsub|m>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0>. Strictly increasing <math|L<rsub|p>>
  with <math|4\<mid\>L<rsub|p>> and every positive integer dividing
  infinitely many<nbsp><math|L<rsub|p>>; <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>>
  and <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m+1>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|<around|(|J<rsub|m+1>+3|)>*\<ell\><rsub|m>+1|)>>.
  <math|J<rsub|<reflevel|d>>\<geqslant\>d+3>, with
  <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d+1> and
  <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d> for
  <math|m\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>.>>|<row|<cell|9>|<cell|Derived level
  scales>|<cell|Determined by earlier choices:
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-scales>, <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m>=<around|[|<refbeta><rsub|m>/2,2<refbeta><rsub|m>|]>>
  and <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>=2<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>>,
  which bounds the weighted quadratic loop sum of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>.>>|<row|<cell|10>|<cell|Actions, classes,
  recursion, trajectories, couplings; width<nbsp><math|w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>>>|<cell|Depend
  on items 1--9: the path actions, the admissible classes, the specified
  recursion and its trajectories for every finite<nbsp><math|N,p>, with the
  common endpoint (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>).
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:fixed-level> gives the fixed-level
  convergence; Remark<nbsp><reference|prop:endpoint-tuning> records the
  analytic dependence on the endpoint, not used
  later.>>|<row|<cell|11>|<cell|Marked transfers, terminal maps,
  insertions>|<cell|Depend on items 1--10; reference normalization
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>). Fixed before any volume
  sequence.>>|<row|<cell|12>|<cell|<math|<math-ss|p>>, then
  <math|<math-ss|q>,P,S,F<rsub|d>,\<partial\><rsub|a>,\<cal-L\>>>|<cell|An
  arbitrary strictly increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>> is prescribed first.
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV> and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>
  give <math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>> and the continuum fields;
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV> keeps the full inner
  limit.>>|<row|<cell|13>|<cell|Comparison radii, frames, <math|J>, charges,
  <math|c<rsub|\<star\>>,R<rsub|d>,T>>|<cell|Depend on item 12 and the
  lattice family: Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>, then
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST> and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>.
  Only comparison radii are refined.>>|<row|<cell|14>|<cell|Slab operators,
  <math|<phases>,\<nu\>,S<rsup|\<xi\>>>, then<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>>|<cell|Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH>;
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> is chosen in the full-measure set with
  one-dimensional time-axis reflection ground space
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>), before
  reconstruction.>>|<row|<cell|15>|<cell|Theories and continuations in every
  phase; <math|\<Theta\>,<tracespectrum>>>|<cell|Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>;
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D16> centers the trace in the already
  selected phase; Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2>.>>|<row|<cell|16>|<cell|Product
  prescription; contact projections, distributions, bases,
  scales>|<cell|Depend on items 12--14 and the lattice family.
  Theorems<nbsp><reference|prop:OP> and<nbsp><reference|prop:CT> use the same
  original fields and correlation table; the contact projections, separating
  functionals and clearance are chosen separately from those of the product
  coefficients.>>|<row|<cell|17>|<cell|Estimate constants and
  thresholds>|<cell|Depend only on the data preceding them in each statement,
  uniformly in positions, volumes and states wherever asserted.>>>>>|Choices
  in the order of dependence. Stage choices precede entry levels at each
  fixed<nbsp><math|j\<geqslant\>4>.>

  <section*|Index of notation>

  <with|font-series|bold|Two dictionaries.> Geometric objects: a <em|site> is
  a point of <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|s>>; a <em|plaquette> is an elementary
  square, with holonomy <math|U<rsub|y;a*b>>; a <em|path> is a finite chain
  of links along which curvatures are transported; the <em|stencil>
  <math|y+\<Sigma\><rsub|v>> is the finite site set an insertion depends on;
  the <em|support> <math|X<rsub|i>> or <math|Y<rsub|i>> is the set on which
  an activity is local; the <em|complete read support> consists of all links
  and flags a local term depends on, including those inspected by its tests;
  a <em|cover> is an exact cover <math|\<frak-a\>\<in\>\<frak-F\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>>
  of the flagged sites by disjoint forced supports; the <em|inventory>
  <math|I<rsub|i>> is the set of flagged sites in a forced support; a
  <em|cluster> is a connected graph of polymers in a logarithmic expansion,
  or a group of nearby insertions in the product expansion. Field objects: a
  <em|label> is <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>> and an <em|insertion label> is
  <math|<around|(|d,c|)>\<in\>\<cal-T\>>; the <em|coefficient vector>
  <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>> is the label of a blocked
  insertion; the <em|lattice insertion> is
  <math|I<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v,y;U|)>>; the <em|quotient field> is
  <math|<around|[|c|]>\<in\>F<rsub|d>>; its <em|response> is
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|d><around|[|c|]>>, its correlations against all
  spectators; a <em|field label> is <math|\<ell\>\<in\>\<cal-L\>>.

  <with|font-series|bold|Conventions and gauge group>

  <math|e<rsub|a>> coordinate vectors, time <math|a=4>;
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:conventions>.

  <math|<minksquare>>, <math|<forwardcone>> Minkowski square, closed forward
  cone; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:conventions>.

  <math|<hypercubic>>, <math|h> signed permutation matrices;
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:conventions>.

  <math|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|refl>>> reflection in
  coordinate<nbsp><math|a>; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:conventions>.

  <math|G>, <math|<g>>, <math|n<rsub|G>> gauge group, Lie algebra;
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>.

  <math|<dimG>>, <math|<casimir>>, <math|b<rsub|0>>, <math|<slope>>
  dimension, adjoint Casimir, one-loop coefficient, slope;
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>.

  <math|<wilsonterm>>, <math|<chord>> plaquette energy, normalized chord;
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-price-chord>.

  <vspace*|1fn><with|font-series|bold|Lattice theory and families>

  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|s>> periodic lattice;
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:intro-wilson>.

  <math|U<rsub|y;a*b>>, <math|E<rsub|s><rsup|<math-up|W>>>,
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|s,\<beta\>>>, <math|Z<rsub|s,\<beta\>>> plaquette
  holonomy, Wilson energy, measure, normalization;
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D02>.

  <math|\<cal-W\>>, <math|D<rsub|N>>, <math|L<rsub|p>>,
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>> Wilson family, cutoffs, periods, bare couplings;
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:families>.

  <math|a<rsub|N>>, <math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>>, <math|<wide|y|^>> spacing,
  measure, physical placement; equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-physical-placement>.

  <math|<latmoment><rsub|N,p>>, <math|<latsmear><rsub|N,p>> lattice moment,
  smeared correlation; Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D03>.

  <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|v>>, <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>> stencil, slope of a
  regular family; Definition<nbsp><reference|def:Q1>.

  <math|<Conf|n>>, <math|<sepinf>> distinct configurations, minimum
  separation; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:families>.

  <math|C<rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|UV>>> fixed-volume smeared limit;
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09>.

  <vspace*|1fn><with|font-series|bold|Local fields>

  <math|\<cal-J\>>, <math|V<rsub|d>>, <math|b<rsub|d,i>> radial jets,
  polynomial labels, bases; Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>.

  <math|<factorcount>>, <math|t<rsup|<factorcount>>> factor-count operator
  and scaling; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:local-fields>.

  <math|U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>>,
  <math|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>>> test holonomies, jet
  dilation; equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-test-holonomy>.

  <math|<taylormap><rsub|d>> Taylor map; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:local-fields>.

  <math|<localsection|d>>, <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> local section, its
  stencil; equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-section-Taylor>.

  <math|\<cal-T\>> insertion labels; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:local-fields>.

  <math|I<rsub|N,p>>, <math|\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>> renormalized insertion, unit
  coefficient; Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>.

  <math|<reflevel|d>>, <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> reference level,
  terminal map; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:marked>.

  <math|H<rsup|<math-up|ins>><rsub|N,p,m;d>>, <math|\<b-up-v\>> marked
  density, fine-cutoff coefficient vector;
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-evolution>.

  <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|m>>, <math|R<rsup|<math-up|reg>><rsub|m,i>>,
  <math|Q<rsup|<math-up|for>><rsub|m,i>> coefficient vectors and remainders;
  equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-remainders>\U<eqref|eq:I12-marked-representation>.

  <vspace*|1fn><with|font-series|bold|Scales and blocking>

  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>>, <math|D<rsub|m>>, <math|a<rsub|m>>,
  <math|s<rsub|m>> block ratio, levels; equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-levels>.

  <math|J<rsub|m>>, <math|\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|j>> stage, stage
  ratio; Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:closes>
  and<nbsp><reference|sec:block-map>.

  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> endpoint coupling;
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:block-map>.

  <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>>, <math|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>>, <math|t<rsub|m>>
  reference coupling, field multiplier, small-field threshold;
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-scales>.

  <math|B<rsub|m>>, <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>,
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>> support radius, allowances
  (bounds kept by the induction); equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-scales-b>.

  <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m>>, <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|j>>,
  <math|\<varpi\><rsub|j>>, <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>>,
  <math|\<omega\><rsup|<math-up|RG>><rsub|j>> coupling band and stage
  constants; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:block-map>.

  <math|<blockpenalty>>, <math|q<rsub|\<gamma\>>> block penalty, coarse-link
  density; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:block-map>.

  <math|Y<rsub|e,z>>, <math|Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>>,
  <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>><rsub|z>> framed connector, predictor, weights;
  equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-framed-connector>\U<eqref|eq:I12-predictor>.

  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|<math-up|tag>>> tag masses;
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-tag-mass>.

  <math|<blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>>>, <math|<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\>>>
  block kernel, exact block map; Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>.

  <vspace*|1fn><with|font-series|bold|Densities and classes>

  <math|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>> flagged (large-field) sites;
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:density>.

  <math|E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>, <math|K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>>,
  <math|I<rsub|i>> regular and forced activities, inventory;
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-forced-eligibility>.

  <math|<math-up|clip><rsub|t>>, <math|\<chi\><rsup|<math-up|rel>><rsub|m,x>>
  clip, relaxed threshold test; equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-clipping>.

  <math|<pathaction><rsub|N,m>>, <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|\<frak-p\>>>
  path action, smeared curvature; equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-path-action>.

  <math|\<rho\>>, <math|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>>> representation, vacuum scalar;
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-representation>.

  <math|\<frak-F\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>> exact covers of the flags;
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:density>.

  <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|\<rho\>>>, <math|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\>>
  action, effective density; Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>.

  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>>, <math|D<rsup|L>>
  local derivative seminorm, left derivative;
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>.

  <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>>>, <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|m>>,
  <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|m>> ball-cover count, weighted sums;
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05b>.

  <math|\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>> Wilson start;
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-Wilson-initialization>.

  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>>, <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>>,
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>> admissible classes, initial classes, adjacent
  pairs; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:density>.

  <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>>, <math|\<frak-T\><rsub|m>> trajectory
  couplings, shooting intervals; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trajectories>.

  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>>, <math|G<rsub|x>> five-torus weights, localized
  term; equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:five-torus>.

  <vspace*|1fn><with|font-series|bold|Limits and fields>

  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>>, <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>>
  differences, derivatives; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:uv-limit>.

  <math|<Gen|n>>, <math|<NoHit|L|n>> generic and periodically separated
  configurations; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:uv-limit>.

