  <part|Proofs>

  <section|Local polynomial fields><label|sec:partII-A>

  The polynomial labels of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07> prescribe
  continuum Taylor coefficients, whereas the Wilson measure integrates
  functions of group-valued links. Local sections connect the two:
  polynomials in the real and imaginary parts of finitely many link entries
  whose expansion on smooth test configurations reproduces a prescribed
  label. They initialize the renormalized insertions of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, hence the Wilson family of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>.

  To build them we recover the connection jet from finitely many transported
  plaquette curvatures, whose linearizations are unit-square circulations
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-sections>); each curvature factor costs one
  power of the small-field threshold (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-bounds>).
  For the equation projections of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>,
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-equations> constructs auxiliary sections
  whose regraded equation labels <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d><around|(|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>|)>>
  have exactly vanishing moments against distant observables.
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-example> follows the curvature square through
  these constructions.

  The lemmas of this section use only Part<nbsp>I; the idea paragraph and
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-example> also cite later results for
  illustration. The gauge metric and the Wilson measure of
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D01>\U<reference|def:D02> fix the
  measurements and the one-link integration by parts,
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07> supplies the labels, and the derivative
  seminorm of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a> measures the bounds. The
  sections are chosen once for each degree, and all constants of this section
  are fixed before the lattice volume and the stage block ratios, in the
  order of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>.

  <subsection|Labels, test holonomies and the Taylor map><label|sec:A-labels>

  Write <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j>> for the real space of the components
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|j>> in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>, the
  homogeneous radial potentials of degree<nbsp><math|j+1>. Every label
  in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>> is a polynomial on the finite-dimensional space

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>=<big|oplus><rsub|j+2\<leqslant\>d>\<cal-J\><rsub|j><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Radial gauge and Euler's identity give <math|<around|(|j+2|)>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,a><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b>x<rsup|b>*F<rsup|<around|(|j|)>><rsub|b*a><around|(|x|)>>,
  with <math|F<rsup|<around|(|j|)>>> the degree-<math|j> part of the
  curvature; so a coordinate of<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|j>> has
  weight<nbsp><math|j+2>, two for the curvature and one for each derivative,
  and only finitely many monomials have weight at most<nbsp><math|d>. Adjoint
  action and conjugation preserve both gradings. Real and imaginary parts
  span each invariant component over<nbsp><math|<C>>, giving bihomogeneous
  bases as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>. Conjugation in this real
  basis is coefficientwise and agrees with conjugation of polynomial values
  on real jets. Write <math|k<rsub|i>> for the number of jet-coordinate
  factors in<nbsp><math|b<rsub|d,i>>. We identify a coefficient vector with
  its polynomial:

  <\equation*>
    \<b-up-c\><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*b<rsub|d,i><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|d,0>=\<b-1\><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  We evaluate lattice observables on the test
  holonomies<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-test-holonomy>, on the
  lattice<nbsp><math|<Z><rsup|4>> covering the torus. With
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsup|\<leqslant\>d>=<big|sum><rsub|j+2\<leqslant\>d>\<cal-A\><rsub|j>>,
  they are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:A-holonomy>U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)><around|(|x,a|)>=u<around|(|1|)>,<space|2em>u<rprime|'><around|(|s|)>=u<around|(|s|)>*<space|0.17em>h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*\<cal-A\><rsub|a><rsup|\<leqslant\>d>*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*<around|(|x+s*e<rsub|a>|)>|)>,<space|2em>u<around|(|0|)>=1<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  This linear equation with continuous <math|<g>>-valued coefficients has a
  unique global solution<nbsp><cite-detail|Teschl2012|Theorem<nbsp>3.9>. It
  lies in the closed group<nbsp><math|G>, being a uniform limit of products
  of exponentials of elements of<nbsp><math|<g>> by Gronwall's
  inequality<nbsp><cite-detail|Teschl2012|Lemma<nbsp>2.7>, and its Volterra
  series shows that it depends smoothly on
  <math|<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>>. Homogeneity and
  uniqueness give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:A-dilation>U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>=U*<around|(|1,<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>,<space|2em><math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>=<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|j+2>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j>|)><rsub|j>,<space|2em>U*<around|(|0,<math-ss|j>|)>=1<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The Taylor map of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:local-fields> reads off the
  continuum polynomial seen by a lattice observable. Recall that, for a
  smooth function<nbsp><math|O> of finitely many cover links,
  <math|<taylormap><rsub|d><space|0.17em>O> is the Taylor polynomial at zero
  of <math|<math-ss|j>\<mapsto\>O<around|(|U*<around|(|1,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>>
  on<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>>, with the monomials of weight
  above<nbsp><math|d> discarded. By<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-dilation>, a monomial of
  weight<nbsp><math|w> contributes <math|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|w>> to
  <math|O<around|(|U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>>.
  Hence the derivatives of <math|O<around|(|U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>>
  at <math|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=0> through order<nbsp><math|d> are those of
  <math|\<b-up-c\><around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>>
  for every jet, where <math|\<b-up-c\>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, exactly when
  <math|<taylormap><rsub|d><space|0.17em>O=\<b-up-c\>>. A local section,
  constructed below, is a right inverse of the Taylor map, linear in the
  label: this is the sense of the word <em|section>.

  For a cover plaquette<nbsp><math|\<square\>> based at<nbsp><math|z> and a
  path<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>> from<nbsp><math|0> to<nbsp><math|z>, write
  <math|U<rsub|\<gamma\>>> for the ordered product of successive oriented
  links, read from the initial point to the endpoint. Define the transported
  curvature (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:A-transported>) by

  <\equation*>
    \<sigma\><rsub|\<gamma\>,\<square\>><around|(|U|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>*F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>=<math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><frac|U<rsub|\<square\>>-U<rsub|\<square\>><rsup|-1>|2><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The projection is orthogonal for the normalized Frobenius metric of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>. These measurements transform by the
  adjoint action at<nbsp><math|0>. They are real polynomial functions of the
  real and imaginary parts of link entries, since
  <math|U<rsub|e><rsup|-1>=U<rsub|e><rsup|\<ast\>>>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-5.svg|106.6692pt|57.220822pt||><label|fig:A-transported>
  >|A transported curvature <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<gamma\>,\<square\>>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>*F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>>,
  drawn in one coordinate plane: the curvature of the
  plaquette<nbsp><math|\<square\>> based at<nbsp><math|z> is carried to the
  origin along the lattice path<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>>.>

  <subsection|Taylor-normalized sections><label|sec:A-sections>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Local sections><label|def:D19> For a finite site stencil
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>\<subset\><Z><rsup|4>>, a local section has the
    form

    <\equation*>
      <localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>,\<mu\>>p<rsub|\<nu\>*\<mu\>><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|\<b-up-c\>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|e,\<alpha\>,\<delta\>>U<rsub|e>*<around|(|\<alpha\>,\<delta\>|)><rsup|\<nu\><rsub|e*\<alpha\>*\<delta\>>><wide|U<rsub|e>*<around|(|\<alpha\>,\<delta\>|)>|\<bar\>><rsup|\<mu\><rsub|e*\<alpha\>*\<delta\>>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The product uses positive links with both endpoints
    in<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and matrix indices
    <math|1\<leqslant\>\<alpha\>,\<delta\>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|G>>. The sum is
    finite over nonnegative multi-indices; each coefficient is complex linear
    in<nbsp><math|\<b-up-c\>>. The construction below chooses such a map to
    represent each continuum label.
  </definition>

  For a torus of side<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>> (the
  side<nbsp><math|s> of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D02>), write
  <math|<around|[|x|]>> for reduction of <math|x\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>
  modulo<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>>. Placement at a torus
  site<nbsp><math|y> means evaluation on the periodic extension
  <math|U<rsup|<around|[|y|]>>*<around|(|x,a|)>=U*<around|(|y+<around|[|x|]>,a|)>>;
  denote it by <math|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|y;U|)>>.
  On the cover, <math|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|0;U|)>=<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>>.
  For <math|h\<in\><hypercubic>>, define the configuration and jet actions by

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><around|(|x,a|)>>|<cell|=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|U*<around|(|h*x,b|)>,>|<cell|h*e<rsub|a>=e<rsub|b>,>>|<row|<cell|U*<around|(|h*x-e<rsub|b>,b|)><rsup|-1>,>|<cell|h*e<rsub|a>=-e<rsub|b>,>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|h<math-ss|j>|)><rsub|j,a><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|b>h<rsub|a*b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,b>*<around|(|h<rsup|-1>*z|)>,<space|2em><around|(|h*\<b-up-c\>|)><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>=\<b-up-c\><around|(|h<rsup|-1><math-ss|j>|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align*>

  This action preserves homogeneity and radial gauge. Reality and covariance
  mean

  <\equation*>
    <localsection|d><around|[|<wide|\<b-up-c\>|\<bar\>>|]><around|(|U|)>=<wide|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>|\<bar\>>,<space|2em><localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>*<around|(|h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>=<localsection|d><around|[|h*\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>*<space|1em><around|(|h\<in\><hypercubic>|)>,
  </equation*>

  with conjugation as fixed above.

  The following result supplies the sections specified in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:local-fields>; the unit normalization is exact
  at every configuration.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Taylor-normalized sections><label|lem:A1> For every<nbsp><math|d>,
    a box stencil <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>=<around|[|-\<varrho\><rsub|d>,\<varrho\><rsub|d>|]><rsup|4>\<cap\><Z><rsup|4>>
    and sections can be chosen gauge invariant, real and <math|<hypercubic>>
    covariant, with <math|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-1\>|]>=1>, so that,
    for every <math|\<b-up-c\>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, radial
    jet<nbsp><math|<math-ss|j>> and <math|0\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>d>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-Taylor><around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|q>><localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>*<around|(|U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>|\|><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=0>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|q>>*\<b-up-c\><around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>|\|><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=0><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  Equivalently, <math|<taylormap><rsub|d><localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>=\<b-up-c\>>
  for every <math|\<b-up-c\>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>. The identity imposes no
  equality at positive spacing, and no compatibility between
  <math|<localsection|d>> and <math|<localsection|d+1>> is required. We fix
  the sections constructed in the following proof.

  <\proof>
    There are three steps: finitely many curvature measurements determine the
    truncated jet; an equivariant polynomial inverse of these measurements,
    composed with the label, gives a section with the right Taylor map;
    averaging over<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>> gives covariance and a common
    stencil. If <math|d\<less\>2>, the labels are constant and their constant
    sections suffice. Fix <math|d\<geqslant\>2>.

    <em|Step 1: Curvatures determine the truncated jet.> The measurements
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<gamma\>,\<square\>>*<around|(|U*<around|(|1,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>>
    are smooth in<nbsp><math|<math-ss|j>>, and all links equal<nbsp><math|1>
    at <math|<math-ss|j>=0>. The first variation of a link solution is the
    edge integral of the potential; that of a plaquette measurement is the
    circulation of the potential around the plaquette. Transport
    along<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>> contributes nothing at first order, because
    <math|F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|1|)>=0>. By the fundamental theorem of
    calculus on each edge, the circulation around the unit square
    at<nbsp><math|x> in the plane of the axes <math|a\<less\>b> is

    <\equation*>
      N<rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><space|-0.17em><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|a>|)>*<around|(|x+\<xi\>*e<rsub|a>+\<eta\>*e<rsub|b>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<xi\>*<space|0.17em>d*\<eta\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Suppose all these linearized measurements vanish at
    <math|\<cal-A\>=<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<cal-A\><rsub|j>>.
    Then the polynomial<nbsp><math|N<rsub|a*b>> vanishes
    on<nbsp><math|<Z><rsup|4>>, hence identically: a polynomial vanishing
    on<nbsp><math|<Z><rsup|4>> is zero, by induction on the number of
    variables. The averaging shifts only lower the degree, so the homogeneous
    part of<nbsp><math|N<rsub|a*b>> of degree<nbsp><math|j<rsub|\<ast\>>> is
    <math|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,a>|)><around|(|x|)>>.
    It vanishes, and antisymmetry covers the other ordered pairs.
    Differentiating the radial condition and using Euler's identity gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|j<rsub|\<ast\>>+2|)>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>z<rsup|a>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,a>|)><around|(|z|)>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Remove this component and descend in<nbsp><math|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>: the
    common kernel is zero. Adding a measurement whenever the kernel is
    nonzero decreases its dimension, so finitely many measurements
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<sigma\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>>
    give a joint map

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|S>:\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>\<longrightarrow\><g><rsup|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>,<space|2em><math-ss|S><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>=<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|l>*<around|(|U*<around|(|1,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>|)><rsub|l=1><rsup|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>
    </equation*>

    with injective derivative<nbsp><math|D<math-ss|S><around|(|0|)>>.

    <em|Step 2: A polynomial inverse to the required order.> By uniqueness of
    the link equation, a constant gauge transformation <math|v\<in\>G> takes
    the test links of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|j>> to those
    of<nbsp><math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|v|)><math-ss|j>>,
    so<nbsp><math|<math-ss|S>> is adjoint equivariant. The range of
    <math|D<math-ss|S><around|(|0|)>> is invariant; composing the inverse of
    <math|D<math-ss|S><around|(|0|)>> on it with orthogonal projection onto
    it gives an equivariant linear map <math|C:<g><rsup|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>\<to\>\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>>
    with <math|C*D<math-ss|S><around|(|0|)>=I>. By the inverse function
    theorem<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1976|Theorem<nbsp>9.24>,
    <math|C<math-ss|S>> has a local inverse<nbsp><math|\<varphi\>> near zero,
    smooth by differentiating <math|D*\<varphi\>=<around|(|D<around|(|C<math-ss|S>|)>\<circ\>\<varphi\>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    The adjoint actions are isometric, so <math|\<varphi\>> can be taken on
    invariant balls, where injectivity of<nbsp><math|C<math-ss|S>> makes it
    equivariant. Put <math|f=\<varphi\>\<circ\>C>. Near zero,
    <math|f<math-ss|S><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>=<math-ss|j>>,
    <math|f<around|(|0|)>=0> and <math|f*<around|(|<math-up|Ad><around|(|v|)>*z|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|v|)>*f<around|(|z|)>>
    for <math|v\<in\>G>. Consequently the Taylor polynomial
    <math|T<rsub|d>*f<around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=1><rsup|d>D<rsup|k>*f<around|(|0|)><around|[|z,\<ldots\>,z|]>/k!>
    is adjoint equivariant and has no constant term. Define

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=b<rsub|d,i>*<around|(|T<rsub|d>*f<around|(|z|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<Psi\><rsub|\<b-up-c\>><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*Q<rsub|i>*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|1><around|(|U|)>,\<ldots\>,\<sigma\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>><around|(|U|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The polynomials<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|i>> are invariant and real on real
    arguments, and <math|Q<rsub|0>=1>. If <math|b<rsub|d,i>> has factor
    count<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i>>, every monomial of<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|i>> has
    at least<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i>> factors. Taylor's formula gives
    <math|T<rsub|d>*f<around|(|<math-ss|S><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>|)>=<math-ss|j>+O<around|(|<around|\||<math-ss|j>|\|><rsup|d+1>|)>>
    near zero, hence

    <\equation*>
      \<Psi\><rsub|\<b-up-c\>>*<around|(|U*<around|(|1,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>=\<b-up-c\><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>+O<rsub|\<b-up-c\>><around|(|<around|\||<math-ss|j>|\|><rsup|d+1>|)>,<space|2em><taylormap><rsub|d><space|0.17em>\<Psi\><rsub|\<b-up-c\>>=\<b-up-c\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The polynomial<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|i>> itself need not have degree at
    most<nbsp><math|d> in the curvature measurements.

    <em|Step 3: Covariance and a common stencil.> Average the section by

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-average><localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|h\<in\><hypercubic>>\<Psi\><rsub|h<rsup|-1>*\<b-up-c\>>*<around|(|h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Changing the summation variable to <math|h<rprime|'>*h> proves
    <math|<localsection|d><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>*<around|(|h<rprime|'><rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>=<localsection|d><around|[|h<rprime|'>*\<b-up-c\>|]><around|(|U|)>>.
    Simultaneous adjoint covariance of the measurements gives gauge
    invariance. The real basis and the signed-permutation action give
    reality; the constant column remains one. The action preserves factor
    count, so averaging retains the lower bound on the number of curvature
    factors.

    For a positive image direction, the pulled-back link equation has the
    coefficient of<nbsp><math|h<rsup|-1><math-ss|j>>, namely
    <math|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*<big|sum><rsub|b>h<rsub|b*a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|b><rsup|\<leqslant\>d>*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>*h*<around|(|x+s*e<rsub|a>|)>|)>>.
    Reversing the parameter and inverting the transport covers a negative
    image direction. Uniqueness gives <math|h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>=U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,h<rsup|-1><math-ss|j>|)>>.
    Each term of<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-average> therefore has Taylor
    map<nbsp><math|\<b-up-c\>>. A signed image of a transported curvature is
    again a transported curvature, up to sign: reversal changes
    <math|F<rsub|\<square\>>> to <math|-F<rsub|\<square\>>>, and a change of
    base point gives conjugation by the intervening boundary transport.
    Enclose the finitely many resulting paths and plaquettes in a box
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>=<around|[|-\<varrho\><rsub|d>,\<varrho\><rsub|d>|]><rsup|4>\<cap\><Z><rsup|4>>.
    This stencil contains zero and is signed-permutation invariant. Expanding
    the finite polynomial in matrix entries gives
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D19>. This
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-Taylor>, which
    is<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-section-Taylor>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Small-field bounds><label|sec:A-bounds>

  We bound normalized initial insertions using the ordered left derivatives
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>. At a placement<nbsp><math|y>, set
  <math|Y=y+<around|[|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|]>>; being healthy on<nbsp><math|Y>
  means that every plaquette meeting<nbsp><math|Y> has chord at
  most<nbsp><math|t>. A direction is permitted when

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>t*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|e<rsup|->,y|)>|)><rsup|\<varpi\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<around|[|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|]>> is the torus image of the
  stencil, and <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>=min<rsub|z\<in\>n<rsub|<math-up|tor>><Z><rsup|4>><around|\||<wide|x|~>-<wide|y|~>+z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>>
  is the periodic maximum-coordinate distance, for any lifts
  <math|<wide|x|~>,<wide|y|~>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Section bounds><label|lem:A-bounds> Each basis column is a
    polynomial in transported curvatures with at least<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i>>
    factors per monomial. For all <math|d,k,\<varpi\>\<in\><N>> there are
    finite constants <math|C<rsub|d,k,\<varpi\>>> and <math|C<rsub|d>> such
    that, on every torus, for every placement<nbsp><math|y>,
    <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>1>, configuration healthy on<nbsp><math|Y>,
    and permitted direction list of length <math|q\<leqslant\>k>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-section-bound><around*|\||D<rsup|L><rsub|<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*D<rsup|L><rsub|<wide|\<cal-A\>|\<dot\>><rsub|q>><localsection|d><around|[|b<rsub|d,i>|]><around|(|y;U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d,k,\<varpi\>>*t<rsup|k<rsub|i>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For arbitrary configurations,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-global-bound>sup<rsub|i,y,U><around|\||<localsection|d><around|[|b<rsub|d,i>|]><around|(|y;U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  Each curvature factor is at most of the size<nbsp><math|t> of the
  plaquettes it reads, so a column with <math|k<rsub|i>> factors is of
  order<nbsp><math|t<rsup|k<rsub|i>>> on healthy configurations. The proof
  uses the curvature polynomials constructed in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>.

  <\proof>
    First note that <math|<around|\||F<rsub|\<square\>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><chord><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>>:
    orthogonal projection is contractive and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U<rsub|\<square\>>-U<rsub|\<square\>><rsup|\<ast\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\<\|\|\>|U<rsub|\<square\>>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>>.
    Adjoint transport is isometric, so an undifferentiated curvature
    measurement is bounded by<nbsp><math|t>. An order-<math|q> left
    derivative of a fixed path product is a finite sum of products of unitary
    matrices and exactly<nbsp><math|q> direction matrices. On the fixed
    stencil their norms are at most a constant times<nbsp><math|t>, since
    periodic distance is bounded by cover distance. Thus each differentiated
    measurement is <math|O<around|(|t<rsup|q>|)>>, and hence
    <math|O<around|(|t|)>> when <math|q\<geqslant\>1>. This argument also
    applies when cover links coincide after placement: one first
    differentiates the cover expression and then assigns the same direction
    to coincident links.

    Apply the product rule to the curvature polynomials
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-average>. Every differentiated monomial still has at
    least<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i>> factors, each bounded by a constant
    times<nbsp><math|t>. There are finitely many terms; since
    <math|t\<leqslant\>1>, their sum gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-section-bound>,
    including order zero. Each entry of a unitary matrix has absolute value
    at most one, so the finite matrix-entry polynomial
    gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-global-bound>. The formula also gives smoothness,
    measurability and locality. The constants depend on the sections,
    <math|G,d,k,\<varpi\>> as applicable, and finite maxima make them
    nondecreasing in the integer parameters. They are fixed before the stage
    block ratios in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>.
  </proof>

  By linearity, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-bounds> bounds permitted
  derivatives of <math|<localsection|d><around|[|t<rsup|-<factorcount>>*\<b-up-c\>|]>>
  by <math|C<rsub|d,k,\<varpi\>>*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>>
  on the healthy set: the factor <math|t<rsup|-k<rsub|i>>> cancels
  <math|t<rsup|k<rsub|i>>>. Taking <math|t=t<rsub|N>>,
  <math|k=\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>> and
  <math|\<varpi\>=\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>> bounds the initial marked
  density of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-evolution> on the healthy set, in the
  sense of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>. On unhealthy configurations
  the global bound gives <math|C<rsub|d>*<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>*t<rsup|-k<rsub|i>>>;
  the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1> places this contribution in a
  merged forced remainder of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-representation>.

  <subsection|Auxiliary equation sections><label|sec:A-equations>

  Exact lattice equation identities require different sections, still
  polynomial and local with the same small-field bounds.
  On<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j>> define the Maxwell subspace and the
  equation labels by

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\<nobracket\>|\<cal-J\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|Max>>=<around*|{|\<cal-A\><rsub|j>:<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j,a>|)>=0<text|for
    every >b|}>,<space|2em>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>=<around*|{|p\<in\>V<rsub|d>:p|\|><rsub|<big|prod><rsub|j>\<cal-J\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>=0|}><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here restriction uses only <math|j+2\<leqslant\>d>. Substitution into
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|j>\<cal-J\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> preserves
  bidegree, so <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> is the direct sum of its
  bihomogeneous parts. Order bidegrees<nbsp><math|<around|(|w,k|)>> by the
  lexicographic order of<nbsp><math|<around|(|k,w|)>>. A triangular regrading
  (a unipotent change of labels) is a complex-linear map
  on<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>> whose difference from the identity sends each
  bihomogeneous polynomial into strictly later bidegrees. There are finitely
  many bidegrees, so this difference is nilpotent and the map is invertible;
  the map and its inverse preserve the filtration by minimum factor count.

  <paragraph|Idea: free equations in an interacting theory.> Haar integration
  by parts in one link, a lattice Schwinger\UDyson identity, shows that for
  <math|\<beta\>\<ne\>0> the force<nbsp><math|\<cal-E\><rsub|e>>
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-link-equation>, the derivative of the Wilson energy in
  the link<nbsp><math|e>, has zero moments when multiplied by a function
  independent of<nbsp><math|U<rsub|e>> and by observables of distant links.
  Its linearization is the divergence of the linearized curvature, and the
  Maxwell equations enter only through this linearization. An equation label
  is a sum of products with one factor vanishing on Maxwell jets. Replacing
  that factor by forces and the others by curvature measurements gives an
  observable with exactly vanishing exterior moments, whose Taylor map is the
  label plus later terms, produced by the higher-weight linear terms of the
  lattice force and by nonlinear corrections. The labels with exact lattice
  equations therefore form<nbsp><math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d><around|(|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>|)>>,
  not<nbsp><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>: this is the lattice form of the
  vanishing of equation-of-motion operators at separated points. For example,
  put <math|F<rsub|a*b>=\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|0,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|0,a>>
  and <math|<around|(|\<partial\>\<cdot\>F|)><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|c>\<partial\><rsub|c>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|c>*\<cal-A\><rsub|1,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|1,c>|)>>,
  both constant. The label <math|q<rsub|<math-up|eq>>=<big|sum><rsub|b><around|\<langle\>|<around|(|\<partial\>\<cdot\>F|)><rsub|b>,<around|(|\<partial\>\<cdot\>F|)><rsub|b>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>>
  lies in<nbsp><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|6>> and has bidegree
  <math|<around|(|6,2|)>>. The only later bidegree of weight at most six
  is<nbsp><math|<around|(|6,3|)>>, so <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|6>q<rsub|<math-up|eq>>-q<rsub|<math-up|eq>>>
  is a cubic invariant polynomial in the<nbsp><math|F<rsub|a*b>>; nothing
  below requires it to vanish. The equation count is shared with the free
  theory, the separated correlations are not:
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-equation-count>
  give <math|dim <nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>=dim \<cal-I\><rsub|d>>, while in
  the selected phase <math|<tracetwopoint>> is asymptotic to
  <math|<traceamplitude>r<rsup|-8>*log<rsup|-2><around|(|1/r|)>>
  (Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>), and on no interval
  <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> does <math|<tracetwopoint>> agree with a
  finite Wick profile (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:NF>).

  <paragraph|Lattice symmetries.> We record the symmetries of the Wilson
  measure deferred from Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D02>. A site gauge
  transformation acts on plaquette holonomies by conjugation, and an affine
  isometry <math|h<rsub|<math-up|iso>><around|(|x|)>=h*x+b> with
  <math|h\<in\><hypercubic>> by conjugation, possibly after inversion; both
  preserve<nbsp><math|<wilsonterm>>. Product probability Haar measure is
  invariant under fixed left and right multiplication, inversion and
  permutation<nbsp><cite-detail|Folland2015|Corollary<nbsp>2.28 and
  Proposition<nbsp>2.31>. Hence, for every integer side
  <math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>1>, real<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>, site
  gauge transformation<nbsp><math|\<gamma\><rsub|<math-up|g>>> and bounded
  measurable<nbsp><math|F>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:A-Wilson-invariance><big|int>F<around|(|U<rsup|\<gamma\><rsub|<math-up|g>>>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=<big|int>F*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|iso>><rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=<big|int>F<around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  For a torus site<nbsp><math|y>, put <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|r><around|(|y|)>=<around|{|x:<math-up|dist><around|(|x,y|)>\<leqslant\>r|}>>.
  An exterior spectator outside<nbsp><math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|r><around|(|y|)>>
  is a bounded measurable function of the positive links having both
  endpoints outside<nbsp><math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|r><around|(|y|)>>; it need not
  be gauge invariant. The following identity supplies the auxiliary equation
  observables used in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Auxiliary equation sections><label|lem:A-R4> For
    every<nbsp><math|d> there are a local
    section<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>>> as in
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D19>, an invertible triangular
    regrading<nbsp><math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>> of<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>>, and
    <math|r<rsub|d>,M<rsub|d>\<in\><N>> with the following properties.

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)>The section has its own box stencil, is gauge invariant,
      real and <math|<hypercubic>> covariant, satisfies
      <math|V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|\<b-1\>|]>=1>
      and<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-Taylor>, and satisfies
      <eqref|eq:A-section-bound>\U<eqref|eq:A-global-bound> with its own
      constants.

      <item*|(ii)>The map<nbsp><math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>>
      fixes<nbsp><math|\<b-1\>> and commutes with conjugation
      and<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>.

      <item*|(iii)>For every <math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>M<rsub|d>>,
      <math|\<beta\>\<ne\>0>, placement<nbsp><math|y>,
      <math|p\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> and exterior
      spectator<nbsp><math|F<rsub|<math-up|ext>>>
      outside<nbsp><math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|r<rsub|d>><around|(|y|)>>,

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:A-aux-zero><big|int>V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>p|]><around|(|y;U|)>*F<rsub|<math-up|ext>><around|(|U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><around|(|U|)>=0<space|0.17em>.
      </equation>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  The exact-equation subspace <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d><around|(|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>|)>>
  is compared with the selected primary family in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>. All choices in the following proof
  depend only on<nbsp><math|G,d> and the label basis.

  <\proof>
    There are three steps: one-link integration by parts gives zero moments;
    forces realize every linear functional vanishing on Maxwell jets;
    products of forces and curvature measurements, averaged, represent
    equation labels up to a triangular regrading, which is then inverted.

    <em|Step 1: One-link equations.> Extend the curvature
    <math|F<rsub|\<square\>>> of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-labels> to
    <math|F<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|U;x|)>> for ordered axes by antisymmetry,
    with <math|F<rsub|a*a>=0>. For a positive link <math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>>
    set

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|e><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b\<ne\>a><around*|(|F<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|U;x|)>-<math-up|Ad><around|(|U*<around|(|x-e<rsub|b>,b|)><rsup|-1>|)>*F<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|U;x-e<rsub|b>|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    On a torus of side at least two, direct differentiation gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:A-link-equation>D<rsup|L><rsub|e,A>*E<rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U|)>=<around|\<langle\>|A,\<cal-E\><rsub|e><around|(|U|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Indeed, the forward plaquette contributes
    <math|-n<rsub|G><rsup|-1><math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><around|(|A*U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|A,F<rsub|\<square\>>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>>.
    The backward plaquette, rebased at<nbsp><math|x>, contributes the
    negative of the transported curvature in the display. These are the two
    incidences for each transverse axis; antisymmetry gives the signs for the
    opposite ordering.

    If a continuous multiplier<nbsp><math|g> is independent
    of<nbsp><math|U<rsub|e>>, and a bounded
    measurable<nbsp><math|F<rsub|<math-up|ext>>> is also independent
    of<nbsp><math|U<rsub|e>>, left invariance of Haar measure gives

    <\equation*>
      0=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*\<epsilon\>>*<big|int>g<around|(|U|)>*F<rsub|<math-up|ext>><around|(|U|)>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|U<rsup|e,A,\<epsilon\>>|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*U|\|><rsub|\<epsilon\>=0>=-\<beta\>*Z<rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>>*<big|int><around|\<langle\>|A,\<cal-E\><rsub|e>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>*g*F<rsub|<math-up|ext>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>,\<beta\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|U<rsup|e,A,\<epsilon\>><rsub|e>=e<rsup|\<epsilon\>*A>*U<rsub|e>>
    and all other links are fixed. Compactness bounds the derivative of the
    smooth density uniformly, justifying differentiation even for
    measurable<nbsp><math|F<rsub|<math-up|ext>>>.

    Choose a path<nbsp><math|\<gamma\><rsub|e>> from<nbsp><math|0>
    to<nbsp><math|e<rsup|->> avoiding<nbsp><math|e>. If<nbsp><math|g> also
    avoids<nbsp><math|e>, then

    <\equation*>
      \<Upsilon\><rsub|e,\<xi\>><around|(|U|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<xi\>,<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|e>>|)>*\<cal-E\><rsub|e><around|(|U|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>*g<around|(|U|)>
    </equation*>

    has zero moment for every <math|\<xi\>\<in\><g>> against every exterior
    spectator. To see this, expand <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|\<gamma\><rsub|e>><rsup|-1>|)>*\<xi\>>
    in a fixed orthonormal basis of<nbsp><math|<g>> and apply the preceding
    identity to each coefficient times<nbsp><math|g*F<rsub|<math-up|ext>>>.
    These multipliers are independent of<nbsp><math|U<rsub|e>>. There is one
    radius and one minimum torus side for any finite collection of such
    expressions: enlarge the radius to contain both endpoints of every varied
    link, and the side until distinct links in their finite cover supports
    remain distinct after wrapping. The same choices work at every placement.

    <em|Step 2: Enough measurements with avoidance.> Any path can avoid one
    specified link: replace a traversal of that link by three sides of a
    square in a transverse direction. For each plaquette orientation only
    finitely many base points give a plaquette containing that link. Removing
    these points does not affect Step<nbsp>1 of the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>: if a polynomial vanishes at all but
    finitely many points of<nbsp><math|<Z><rsup|4>>, its restriction to each
    coordinate line through an integer point has infinitely many zeros, so it
    vanishes on<nbsp><math|<Z><rsup|4>> and hence identically. Thus curvature
    measurements whose paths and plaquettes avoid<nbsp><math|e> still have
    injective joint linearization. Finite-dimensional duality therefore
    realizes every linear functional on<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>>
    by finitely many components.

    For every linear functional<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j>> vanishing
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>, a finite
    combination of force linearizations equals
    <math|<math-ss|j>\<mapsto\>\<ell\><around|(|\<cal-A\><rsub|j>|)>> on
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>j+2>=<big|oplus><rsub|i\<leqslant\>j>\<cal-J\><rsub|i>>.
    To see this, extend the circulations<nbsp><math|N<rsub|a*b>> of
    Step<nbsp>1 of the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>
    antisymmetrically. The force linearization
    at<nbsp><math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>> is

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|a>*<around|(|<math-ss|j>;x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b><around*|(|N<rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>-N<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|x-e<rsub|b>|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Suppose that every <math|L<rsub|a>*<around|(|<math-ss|j>;x|)>>,
    <math|x\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>, vanishes for a jet
    <math|\<cal-A\>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<leqslant\>j<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>.
    Each <math|L<rsub|a>*<around|(|<math-ss|j>;\<cdot\>|)>> is a polynomial,
    hence zero. In <math|N<rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>-N<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|x-e<rsub|b>|)>>
    a component<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> contributes a polynomial of
    degree at most<nbsp><math|i-1>, and <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
    contributes <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,a>|)><around|(|x|)>>
    plus terms of lower degree. The part of
    degree<nbsp><math|j<rsub|\<ast\>>-1> therefore gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|b>\<partial\><rsub|b>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>,a>|)>=0>,
    that is, <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<in\>\<cal-J\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>.
    A functional of this top component annihilating<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
    therefore annihilates the common kernel of the force measurements.
    Finite-dimensional duality puts it in their linear span. Transport paths
    do not enter the linearization and can be chosen to avoid their varied
    links. On<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|\<leqslant\>d>> the same combination
    can have additional linear terms only at engineering weights
    above<nbsp><math|j<rsub|\<ast\>>+2>.

    <em|Step 3: Polynomial lifts and the triangular map.> Choose linear
    projections <math|\<pi\><rsub|j>:\<cal-J\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<cal-J\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
    and put <math|\<pi\>=<big|prod><rsub|j>\<pi\><rsub|j>>. A polynomial
    belongs to<nbsp><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> precisely when
    <math|p\<circ\>\<pi\>=0>. For a product of linear coordinates,

    <\equation*>
      <big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|k<rsub|<math-up|tot>>>\<ell\><rsub|i>-<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|k<rsub|<math-up|tot>>><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>\<circ\>\<pi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k<rsub|<math-up|tot>>><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|l\<less\>i><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|l>\<circ\>\<pi\>|)>|]>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>-\<ell\><rsub|i>\<circ\>\<pi\>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|l\<gtr\>i>\<ell\><rsub|l><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently every bihomogeneous equation label is a finite sum of
    products with one linear factor vanishing on
    some<nbsp><math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>, with the same
    engineering weight and factor count. The individual factors need not be
    invariant. We use the Taylor map<nbsp><math|<taylormap><rsub|d>> of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-labels>; every coordinate has weight at
    least two. Call a lattice observable<nbsp><math|O> a lift of a
    bihomogeneous polynomial<nbsp><math|q> if
    <math|<taylormap><rsub|d><space|0.17em>O-q> consists of monomials later
    than<nbsp><math|q> in the <math|<around|(|k,w|)>> order.

    Lift the distinguished factor by a finite combination of transported
    forces from Step<nbsp>2. For each summand, lift the other factors by
    curvature measurements avoiding its varied link, matching their full
    linear parts through engineering weight<nbsp><math|d>. The resulting
    expression has the zero-moment property of Step<nbsp>1 and at
    least<nbsp><math|k<rsub|<math-up|tot>>> curvature factors. Indeed, every
    force is a difference of two transported curvatures per transverse axis.
    The expression is a lift of the prescribed product: the additional linear
    terms of the force have greater engineering weight, and every nonlinear
    correction increases factor count. The square-measurement lifts have
    their prescribed full linear parts, so introduce only the latter kind of
    correction. Taylor polynomials exist by the smooth dependence of the test
    holonomies on the jet.

    Average over constant gauge transformations. All factors transform by
    adjoint matrices at the origin, so the orbit lies in the
    finite-dimensional span of finitely many fixed-degree colour-coordinate
    monomials on the same measurements; each orbit function keeps zero
    moments, with colour vectors rotated by
    <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|v<rsup|-1>|)>>. The Haar average is therefore
    a gauge-invariant finite combination of orbit functions; it keeps zero
    moments, minimum factor count and the lift property, and its Taylor map
    is invariant.

    In each bidegree extend a basis of<nbsp><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> to a
    basis of the invariant label space in that bidegree, lifting the
    complementary vectors by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>.
    Lift<nbsp><math|\<b-1\>> to<nbsp><math|1>, since <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>
    contains no nonzero constant. Summing gives a linear
    lift<nbsp><math|p\<mapsto\>\<cal-Y\><rsub|p>>. Replace this lift by the
    two successive averages

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-Y\><rprime|'><rsub|p><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=<frac|1|<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|h\<in\><hypercubic>>\<cal-Y\><rsub|h<rsup|-1>*p>*<around|(|h<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-Y\><rsup|<math-up|sym>><rsub|p>>|<cell|=<frac|1|2>*<around*|(|\<cal-Y\><rprime|'><rsub|p>+<wide|\<cal-Y\><rprime|'><rsub|<wide|p|\<bar\>>>|\<bar\>>|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The Taylor map commutes with both operations. The real Maxwell equations
    and the chain rule make <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>, and
    hence<nbsp><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>, stable under signed permutations,
    adjoint transformations and conjugation. The zero-moment property
    survives the signed average by<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-Wilson-invariance>,
    changing the spectator by the inverse isometry; the exterior set is
    preserved. Conjugation uses<nbsp><math|<wide|F<rsub|<math-up|ext>>|\<bar\>>>.
    A reversed varied link uses a right derivative of the Wilson energy,
    whose Haar identity is identical. Thus the averaged equation
    representatives remain finite sums of one-link action derivatives with
    link-independent multipliers and avoiding transport paths.

    Let <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>p=<taylormap><rsub|d><space|0.17em>\<cal-Y\><rsup|<math-up|sym>><rsub|p>>.
    Then <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>-I> strictly
    raises<nbsp><math|<around|(|k,w|)>>. There are finitely many bidegrees of
    weight at most<nbsp><math|d>, so <math|<around|(|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>-I|)><rsup|n<rsub|0>>=0>
    for some integer<nbsp><math|n<rsub|0>>, and

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|R><rsub|d><rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|l=0><rsup|n<rsub|0>-1><around|(|-1|)><rsup|l>*<around|(|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>-I|)><rsup|l>,<space|2em>V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|\<b-up-c\>|]>=\<cal-Y\><rsup|<math-up|sym>><rsub|<math-ss|R><rsub|d><rsup|-1>\<b-up-c\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This gives invertibility and Taylor normalization; the averages give the
    symmetry and unit identities. The inverse preserves minimum factor count,
    so Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-bounds> applies to these sections with
    their own constants. A signed-invariant box enclosing all paths and
    plaquettes gives a common stencil. Take <math|r<rsub|d>,M<rsub|d>> as
    maxima for an ideal basis; linearity gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-aux-zero>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Example: the curvature square><label|sec:A-example>

  We follow one familiar observable through the construction.
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E> and<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>
  construct the terminal map and the quotient used at its end.

  <paragraph|The label.> With <math|F<rsub|a*b>> as in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-equations>, radial gauge gives
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0,a><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|b>x<rsup|b>*F<rsub|b*a>>;
  since <math|\<cal-A\><around|(|0|)>=0>, <math|F<rsub|a*b>> is the curvature
  of the jet at the origin. The curvature square is the label

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|F<rsup|2>><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*b>,F<rsub|a*b>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b,\<alpha\>><around|(|F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>|)><rsup|2><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  with coordinates<nbsp><math|F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*b>> in an orthonormal
  basis of<nbsp><math|<g>>: the Yang\UMills density at the origin. It is
  invariant, real and <math|<hypercubic>> invariant, of bidegree
  <math|<around|(|4,2|)>>, and lies in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>> for every
  <math|d\<geqslant\>4>.

  <paragraph|Two lattice readings.> For <math|a\<less\>b> and
  <math|s,s<rprime|'>=\<pm\>1>, let <math|\<square\><rsub|a*b><rsup|s*s<rprime|'>>>
  be the plaquette with corner<nbsp><math|0> spanned
  by<nbsp><math|s*e<rsub|a>> and<nbsp><math|s<rprime|'>*e<rsub|b>>. The
  clover

  <\equation*>
    P<rsup|<math-up|cl>><around|(|U|)>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|a\<less\>b><big|sum><rsub|s,s<rprime|'>=\<pm\>1><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\><rsub|a*b><rsup|s*s<rprime|'>>>|)>
  </equation*>

  satisfies <math|<taylormap><rsub|4><space|0.17em>P<rsup|<math-up|cl>>=c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>>.
  Indeed, on test holonomies each of these plaquettes has
  <math|U<rsub|\<square\>>=e<rsup|X>> with
  <math|X=\<pm\>h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|2>*F<rsub|a*b>+O<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|3>|)>\<in\><g>>,
  and <math|<wilsonterm><around|(|e<rsup|X>|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|X,X|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>+O<around|(|<around|\||X|\|><rsup|3>|)>>
  because <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em><tr><space|0.17em>X=0>; hence
  <math|P<rsup|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|U*<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>,<math-ss|j>|)>|)>=h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|4>*c<rsub|F<rsup|2>><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>+O<around|(|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|5>|)>>.
  This familiar leading-order reading is gauge invariant, real and
  <math|<hypercubic>> invariant, and at most<nbsp><math|3*t<rsup|2>> when its
  plaquettes have chord at most<nbsp><math|t>: the
  size<nbsp><math|t<rsup|k<rsub|i>>>, <math|k<rsub|i>=2>, of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-bounds>. The column
  <math|<localsection|4><around|[|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|]>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1> is built differently, as the
  <math|<hypercubic>> average of <math|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>*<around|(|T<rsub|4>*f*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<sigma\><rsub|M<rsub|<math-up|meas>>>|)>|)>>:
  it recovers the truncated jet from curvature measurements and evaluates the
  label on it. The two differ by an observable with vanishing Taylor map; the
  terminal map below refers to the section of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>.

  <paragraph|The insertion.> Take <math|d=4>. A nonconstant monomial of
  weight at most four is a coordinate of<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0>>,
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|1>> or<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|2>>, or a product of
  two coordinates of<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0>>, and invariant linear
  terms vanish because <math|<g>> is simple. As the adjoint representation is
  absolutely irreducible, every label in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|4>> is
  <math|m<rsub|0>*\<b-1\>+<big|sum><rsub|P,Q>m<rsub|P*Q>*<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|P>,F<rsub|Q>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>>>,
  summed over coordinate planes, with <math|m> symmetric. A reflection of one
  axis changes the sign of<nbsp><math|F<rsub|P>> exactly for the planes
  containing that axis. Hence <math|<hypercubic>> invariance
  forces<nbsp><math|m> to be diagonal and, by permutations, constant: the
  real <math|<hypercubic>> invariant labels in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|4>> are the
  real combinations of<nbsp><math|\<b-1\>> and<nbsp><math|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>>.
  For <math|N\<geqslant\><reflevel|4>>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>
  makes <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,4>> commute with conjugation
  and<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, and gives
  <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,4>\<b-1\>=<around|(|a<rsub|<reflevel|4>>/a<rsub|N>|)><rsup|4>*\<b-1\>>.
  The fine-cutoff coefficient vector and, by
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, the insertion are therefore

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,4><rsup|-1>c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>>|<cell|=\<lambda\><rsub|N,p>*c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>+\<mu\><rsub|N,p>*\<b-1\><space|0.17em>,>>|<row|<cell|O<rsup|<around|(|4|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>,y;U|)>>|<cell|=a<rsub|N><rsup|-4>*<around*|(|\<lambda\><rsub|N,p>*t<rsub|N><rsup|-2><localsection|4><around|[|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|]><around|(|y;U|)>+\<mu\><rsub|N,p>|)><space|0.17em>,>>>>
  </align*>

  with real <math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>> and real
  <math|\<lambda\><rsub|N,p>\<ne\>0>, since <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,4>>
  preserves<nbsp><math|<C>\<b-1\>>. The renormalization of the curvature
  square is a multiplicative constant together with an additive one.

  Mixing enters through the transfer: the
  representation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-representation> of the blocked
  marked density keeps a unit coordinate<nbsp><math|x<rsub|m,0>>, through
  which integration can turn part of a curvature insertion into a constant.
  The inverse terminal map makes the transported coefficient at the reference
  level exactly<nbsp><math|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>>, with unit coordinate zero,
  which specifies the label at the physical
  scale<nbsp><math|a<rsub|<reflevel|4>>>. A single factor
  (<math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>=0>) would require the transfer to preserve the line
  through<nbsp><math|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>>, and nothing in the construction
  forces this. In degree six, <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,6><rsup|-1>c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>>
  may involve every real <math|<hypercubic>> invariant label of weight at
  most six, including any that are not rotation invariant.

  The degree tag acts separately from weight and factor count: it
  fixes<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>>, <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>> and
  the terminal map. Thus <math|<around|(|4,c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|)>> and
  <math|<around|(|6,c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|)>> are different insertion labels; the
  inclusion <math|\<jmath\><rsub|4,6>> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> maps the class of the first to the
  class in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|6>> with the same correlations, which need not
  be the class of the second.

  <paragraph|The field.> Degree four has no invisible labels. Labels
  in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|4>> depend only on<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0>>, and
  <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|Max>>=\<cal-J\><rsub|0>> because the
  curvature of<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0>> is constant; so
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|4>=0>, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-equation-count> with
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> gives <math|dim F<rsub|4>=dim
  V<rsub|4>>. Thus <math|<nullspace|4><around|(|S|)>=0>, the quotient map
  <math|V<rsub|4>\<to\>F<rsub|4>> is bijective, and
  <math|<around|[|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|]>\<ne\>0>. In each
  phase<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC> gives the
  smeared field <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4><around|[|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|]>,f|)>>.
  Its free counterpart is the Maxwell curvature
  square<nbsp><math|<curvaturesquare>> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-products>, whose two-point function at
  separation<nbsp><math|r> is <math|3<dimG>\<pi\><rsup|-4>*r<rsup|-8>>;
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-I> compares the stress trace with it.

  <section|Gauges and block integration><label|sec:partII-B>

  The block map of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04> averages fine parallel
  transports in local frames. A fixed axial frame would distinguish an
  ordering of the axes. We instead minimize a symmetric average of transport
  energies when the field is sufficiently small, and average over
  curvature-controlled axial frames elsewhere.
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-kernel> shows that these choices give a
  measurable, covariant probability kernel; consequently the block
  integration in Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG> preserves the partition
  function.

  The remaining subsections prepare the small-field integration used in
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C> and<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>.
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-free> solves the free block problem: the
  free action at every level, the free interpolation<nbsp><math|W<rsub|0>>
  from coarse to fine fields, and an exact square completion.
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-stationary> constructs the stationary fine
  field about a coarse field and integrates the Gaussian fluctuations about
  it; the determinant, the Haar Jacobian and the inherited loop action form
  the one-loop action<nbsp><math|<math-ss|T>>.
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-inverse> supplies what
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C> needs to extract the curvature-square
  coefficient of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|T>>: inverse bounds uniform in the block
  ratio, vertex bounds whose input-dependent part carries a
  factor<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>>, and a Ward identity showing that the
  predictor contact term (the chain-rule term containing the second
  derivative of the predictor) does not change that coefficient.
  Sections<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-energy>
  and<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-sector> give the local large-field energy
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>) and the simultaneous differentiated local
  integral (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>) used in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>.

  Three uniformities require care.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(i)>The frame and predictor cutoffs must work on patches with
    arbitrarily many sites. Centred frame minimizers satisfy a contraction
    whose constant does not depend on the patch
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>).

    <item*|(ii)>The free kernels must stay controlled after arbitrarily many
    blockings. We separate the principal Fourier pole before inverting
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>).

    <item*|(iii)>The determinant comparison must not see the
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> sites of a fine cell. The difference of the two
    compared operators either factors through coarse spaces or contains the
    regulator <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2><around|(|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|)><rsup|3>>,
    and <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>> is trace class uniformly
    in<nbsp><math|\<ell\>> (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:C6>).
  </enumerate>

  <paragraph|Induction scheme.> Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-B>\U<reference|sec:partII-D>
  prove one descending induction on<nbsp><math|m>, started at <math|m=N> by
  the Wilson representation of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>. The hypothesis
  of the step <math|m+1\<to\>m> has two parts: the path bounds and
  cancellations of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2> at
  level <math|m+1>, for the input path action of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>; and an input curve satisfying
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D25a>
  and<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps>. The stationary integration of this
  section and the determinant comparison of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C> first construct the level-<math|m>
  path arrays; the joint proof after Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> shows that
  they satisfy Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2> at
  level<nbsp><math|m>. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> construct the output activities.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> then fixes the output coupling; its proof
  uses Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>, <reference|lem:D1>, <reference|lem:D5>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:D6>, none of which uses
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2> closes the
  step. Curl division (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C3>) and gauge completion
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C5>) do not use the induction.

  Throughout, use the group and metric of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, the Wilson
  term<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-price-chord>, and the level
  choices<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-levels>. At a step <math|m+1\<to\>m>, put
  <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>, <math|\<ell\>=\<ell\><rsub|m>> and
  <math|R=<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\>>. Unless stated otherwise, periods satisfy
  the minimum-period requirement in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>.
  All finite operators use counting inner products and are periodizations of
  the kernels described below. An adjoint at complex momentum means the
  analytic continuation of the real-momentum adjoint.

  <subsection|The block kernel><label|subsec:B-kernel>

  Before averaging transports, we express them in common endpoint frames.
  When the resulting connectors lie close to one another in<nbsp><math|G>,
  their weighted average lies close to<nbsp><math|G>, and its nearest point
  of<nbsp><math|G> serves as a group-valued average. The other branches
  complete the kernel on the remaining configurations.
  Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:B-predictor> shows the two cases, and
  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:B-branches> lists the tests.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-6.svg|375.376468pt|90.588682pt||><label|fig:B-predictor>
  >|The predictor of a coarse link <math|e=<around|(|y,a|)>>. (a) Fine
  connectors between two blocks, each expressed in the frames at its two
  endpoints. (b) When the connectors lie close together in<nbsp><math|G>,
  their weighted average <math|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsup|<math-up|av>><rsub|z>*Y<rsub|e,z>>
  is a matrix just off<nbsp><math|G>; the predictor is its nearest point
  of<nbsp><math|G>, and the coarse link<nbsp><math|V<rsub|e>> fluctuates
  about it with weight <math|e<rsup|-<blockpenalty>\<beta\><wilsonterm><around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>>>.
  (c) When the connectors are spread out, a single connector
  <math|Y<rsub|e,z>> is chosen with probability
  <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>><rsub|z>> and <math|V<rsub|e>> fluctuates about
  it. This branch is part of the definition of the kernel on every
  configuration where the minimum test fails.>

  For <math|o\<in\>S<rsub|4>>, let <math|T<rsub|U>*<around|(|y\<to\>z;o|)>>
  be the ordered product along the path that changes the four coordinates in
  the order<nbsp><math|o>, using inverse links for negative steps.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Axial frames><label|def:D20a> The frame tagged by
    <math|<around|(|z,o|)>>, its normalization and its link coordinates are

    <\equation*>
      g<rsub|y><rsup|z,o>=T<rsub|U>*<around|(|z\<to\>y;o|)>,<space|2em>h<rsub|y><rsup|z,o>=<around|(|g<rsub|0><rsup|z,o>|)><rsup|-1>*g<rsub|y><rsup|z,o>,<space|2em>U<rsub|<around|(|y,a|)>><rsup|z,o>=g<rsub|y><rsup|z,o>*U*<around|(|y,a|)><around|(|g<rsub|y+e<rsub|a>><rsup|z,o>|)><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    These frames provide the finite alternatives for the symmetric gauge
    choice.
  </definition>

  In the patch <math|\<ell\>*y+\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>> we abbreviate the frame
  <math|h<rsub|y,r>> of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-framed-connector> at
  offset<nbsp><math|r\<in\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>> to<nbsp><math|h<rsub|r>>. Here
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>\<gtr\>0> is the frame cutoff and
  <math|a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>\<geqslant\>16> the axial factor of item<nbsp>2
  of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>; patch sums count internal
  positive links and plaquettes. The frame test below compares transports
  around loops through the patch centre: under a gauge transformation such a
  loop transport is conjugated, so its chord is gauge invariant.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Symmetric frame choice><label|def:D20b> For
    <math|x,y\<in\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>> and <math|o,o<rsub|0>\<in\>S<rsub|4>>
    let <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|x*y;o><rsup|o<rsub|0>>=g<rsub|x><rsup|0,o<rsub|0>>*T<rsub|U>*<around|(|x\<to\>y;o|)><around|(|g<rsub|y><rsup|0,o<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|-1>>,
    the transport from <math|0> to<nbsp><math|x> in
    order<nbsp><math|o<rsub|0>>, then to<nbsp><math|y> in
    order<nbsp><math|o>, then back to<nbsp><math|0> along the reversed route
    of order<nbsp><math|o<rsub|0>>. Tags lie in
    <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>\<times\>S<rsub|4>>.

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(a)><em|Minimizing branch.> If
      <math|<chord><around|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|x*y;o><rsup|o<rsub|0>>|)>\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>>
      for all <math|x,y,o,o<rsub|0>>, the frame
      <math|<around|(|h<rsub|r>|)><rsub|r\<in\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>> is the
      minimizer with <math|h<rsub|0>=1> of

      <\equation*>
        <frac|1|24<around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>|\|><rsup|2>>*<big|sum><rsub|r,r<rprime|'>\<in\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>><big|sum><rsub|o\<in\>S<rsub|4>><wilsonterm><around|(|h<rsub|r>*T<rsub|U>*<around|(|r\<to\>r<rprime|'>;o|)>*h<rsub|r<rprime|'>><rsup|-1>|)>,
      </equation*>

      and every tag has mass <math|<around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>\<times\>S<rsub|4>|\|><rsup|-1>>.

      <item*|(b)><em|Axial branch.> Otherwise a tag <math|<around|(|z,o|)>>
      is <em|eligible> if

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|patch>><chord><around|(|U<rsub|e><rsup|z,o>|)>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*<around|(|2*R+1|)>*<big|sum><rsub|\<square\><math-up|patch>><chord><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)><space|0.17em>;
      </equation*>

      the frame is <math|h<rsup|z,o>>, and eligible tags have equal mass.
    </enumerate>
  </definition>

  On the minimizing branch the frame does not depend on its tag. For a coarse
  edge<nbsp><math|e> use the framed connector<nbsp><math|Y<rsub|e,z>>
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-framed-connector> and the averaging
  weights<nbsp><math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>>> of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-predictor>;
  the weight is supported in<nbsp><math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|j*<around|(|\<ell\>-1|)>/2>\<subset\>\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Coarse predictors><label|def:D21> If the minimum
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-predictor> is below<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>>,
    then <math|Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>> is the minimizer there, and
    predictor tags have uniform mass on<nbsp><math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>.
    Otherwise a tag<nbsp><math|z> selects
    <math|Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>=Y<rsub|e,z>>, with
    mass<nbsp><math|w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>>. The product of frame and
    predictor masses is <math|\<lambda\><rsup|<math-up|tag>>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>|)>>.
    These choices define every summand of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>.
  </definition>

  <big-table|<with|par-mode|center|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|3|3|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|4|4|1|1|cell-col-span|4>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Choice>|<cell|Test>|<cell|If
  the test holds>|<cell|Otherwise>>|<row|<cell|Frame>|<cell|<math|<chord><around|(|\<cal-T\><rsup|o<rsub|0>><rsub|x*y;o>|)>\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>>
  for all loops through the patch centre>|<cell|the unique minimizing frame
  with <math|h<rsub|0>=1>; all tags equally likely>|<cell|an eligible axial
  frame <math|h<rsup|z,o>>; eligible tags equally
  likely>>|<row|<cell|Predictor>|<cell|minimum
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-predictor> below<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>>>|<cell|the
  nearest point of<nbsp><math|G> to <math|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>>; tags uniform
  on<nbsp><math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>>|<cell|a connector <math|Y<rsub|e,z>>,
  chosen with mass <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>><rsub|z>>>>|<row|<cell|Result:
  for every fine configuration<nbsp><math|U>, a probability law on coarse
  links, Borel in<nbsp><math|U> and covariant (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>
  and the paragraph after its proof). Analytic dependence on<nbsp><math|U> is
  proved only on the minimizing branches near the flat field
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>).>|<cell|>|<cell|>|<cell|>>>>><label|tab:B-branches>
  >|The two branch decisions of the block kernel.>

  The branch choices require constants independent of the patch radius. Their
  normalization then follows from finite probability sums and Haar
  invariance.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Branches and normalization><label|lem:B1> There are
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>,\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>\<gtr\>0>,
    depending only on <math|G\<subset\>U<around|(|n<rsub|G>|)>>, such that
    for every patch <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>>,
    <math|R=<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\>>, and every
    <math|a<rsub|<math-up|ax>>\<geqslant\>16>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)>on the minimizing branch of
      Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D20b>, the frame with <math|h<rsub|0>=1>
      is unique;

      <item*|(ii)>on the minimizing branch of
      Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D21>, the predictor is unique;

      <item*|(iii)>for every <math|o\<in\>S<rsub|4>> some tag
      <math|<around|(|z,o|)>> is eligible;

      <item*|(iv)>the masses and the kernel are normalized:

      <\equation*>
        <big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>>\<lambda\><rsup|<math-up|tag>>*<around|(|U,\<alpha\><rsub|<math-up|tag>>|)>=1,<space|2em><big|int><blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>*<space|0.17em>d*V=1<space|0.17em>.
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  The proof treats the predictor, the frame and the axial alternatives in
  turn, then the two normalizations.

  <\proof>
    <em|Predictor branch.> Work in the real Euclidean space of complex
    matrices with Frobenius norm. The tubular projection lemma
    of<nbsp><cite-detail|Foote1984|p.<nbsp>153, Lemma>, applied to the
    compact smooth embedded submanifold <math|G>, gives a neighbourhood with
    unique smooth nearest-point projection<nbsp><math|\<pi\>>. At the
    identity its derivative is the orthogonal
    projection<nbsp><math|P<rsub|<g>>> onto<nbsp><math|<g>>. Consequently,
    for any <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, shrinking this neighbourhood gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:B-projection><around|\<\|\|\>|\<pi\><around|(|A|)>-\<pi\><around|(|B|)>-P<rsub|<g>>*<around|(|A-B|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*<around|\<\|\|\>|A-B|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>
    </equation>

    when <math|A,B> lie in a sufficiently small ball about<nbsp><math|1>.
    Integrate <math|D*\<pi\>-P<rsub|<g>>> along their joining segment.

    Put <math|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>*Y<rsub|e,z>>.
    Expansion of the square gives

    <\equation*>
      2*n<rsub|G>*<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>><wilsonterm><around|(|Y<rsub|e,z>*u<rsup|-1>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>\<\|\|\>Y<rsub|e,z>-<wide|Y|\<bar\>>\<\|\|\><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>-u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Thus the minimizers are precisely the nearest group points
    to<nbsp><math|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>>. If the minimum is
    below<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>>, then
    <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|<wide|Y|\<bar\>>,G|)>\<less\><sqrt|2*n<rsub|G>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|pred>>>>.
    Choose this smaller than a tubular radius. This choice uses neither the
    weights nor their number.

    <em|Frame branch: a contraction uniform in<nbsp><math|R>.> First make an
    origin-based axial change of gauge. Its route matrices, denoted
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|r*r<rprime|'>;o>>, satisfy
    <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|r*r;o>=1> and <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|r<rprime|'>*r;o<rprime|'>>=\<cal-T\><rsub|r*r<rprime|'>;o><rsup|-1>>
    for the reversed order<nbsp><math|o<rprime|'>>. On the minimizing branch
    choose <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>> so that
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\><rsub|r*r<rprime|'>;o>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>d>,
    where <math|d\<leqslant\>1/4> will be fixed below. Write
    <math|<math-up|av>> for the uniform averages over the indicated indices.
    A global minimizer<nbsp><math|h> exists by compactness. Varying its
    coordinate<nbsp><math|h<rsub|r>> and pairing the two orientations shows
    that <math|h<rsub|r>> is a nearest group point to

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|r><around|(|h|)>=<math-up|av><rsub|r<rprime|'>,o><space|0.17em>h<rsub|r<rprime|'>>*\<cal-T\><rsub|r*r<rprime|'>;o><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The self term is harmless: replacing it by its value at a proposed
    competitor adds a nonnegative square. Comparison with the constant frame
    and the triangle inequality give

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|av><rsub|r,r<rprime|'>><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|r>-h<rsub|r<rprime|'>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>4*d<rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Left-multiply<nbsp><math|h> so that <math|1> is a nearest point to its
    centroid <math|<wide|h|\<bar\>>=<math-up|av><rsub|r><space|0.17em>h<rsub|r>>.
    Jensen's inequality then gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|h|\<bar\>>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>2*d>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|r><around|(|h|)>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>3*d>.

    Center two minimizers<nbsp><math|h,k> in this way, and put
    <math|u=max<rsub|r><around|\<\|\|\>|h<rsub|r>-k<rsub|r>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>>.
    Their centroids are normal to<nbsp><math|G> at<nbsp><math|1>, so
    <math|P<rsub|<g>>*<around|(|<wide|h|\<bar\>>-<wide|k|\<bar\>>|)>=0>.
    Moreover,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|r><around|(|h|)>-F<rsub|r><around|(|k|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>u,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|r><around|(|h|)>-F<rsub|r><around|(|k|)>-<around|(|<wide|h|\<bar\>>-<wide|k|\<bar\>>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|F>>\<leqslant\>d*u<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-projection> with <math|\<epsilon\>=1/4>, choosing
    <math|3*d> inside its ball. At a coordinate attaining<nbsp><math|u> it
    yields <math|u\<leqslant\><around|(|d+1/4|)>*u\<leqslant\>u/2>. Hence the
    centered minimizers coincide. All minimizers differ by a common left
    multiple, which <math|h<rsub|0>=1> fixes uniquely. The contraction factor
    <math|d+1/4> comes from averages only, so neither<nbsp><math|d>
    nor<nbsp><math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|fr>>> depends on the number of
    sites.

    <em|Axial branch.> For a rectangular slice<nbsp><math|X> let
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|link>>*<around|(|X,U|)>> and
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X,U|)>> be the sums of
    link and plaquette chords internal to<nbsp><math|X>. We show, by
    induction on the number<nbsp><math|r> of varying axes of<nbsp><math|X>,
    that some axial frame satisfies <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|link>>*<around|(|X,U<rsup|<math-up|ax>>|)>\<leqslant\>4*r*<around|(|2*R+1|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X,U|)>>.
    Choose a layer<nbsp><math|X<rsub|0>> perpendicular to the last axis whose
    plaquette sum is at most the slice average, so that
    <math|<around|(|2*R+1|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X<rsub|0>,U|)>\<leqslant\>\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X,U|)>>,
    and apply the induction hypothesis there. Extend its frame along the last
    axis, making those links equal to<nbsp><math|1>. A link in another layer
    differs from its transported link in<nbsp><math|X<rsub|0>> by a product
    of intervening plaquettes. Each plaquette lies between at most <math|2*R>
    such pairs. The chord triangle inequality and conjugation invariance,
    summed over the links, give

    <\equation*>
      \<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|link>>*<around|(|X,U<rsup|<math-up|ax>>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*R+1|)>*<around|{|\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|link>>*<around|(|X<rsub|0>,U<rsup|<math-up|ax>>|)>+\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X,U|)>|}>\<leqslant\>4*r*<around|(|2*R+1|)>*\<Sigma\><rsub|<math-up|plaq>>*<around|(|X,U|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The second inequality uses the induction bound for<nbsp><math|X<rsub|0>>
    and the choice of the layer. At <math|r=4> this proves eligibility, for
    each axis ordering, with factor<nbsp><math|16>.

    <em|Normalization.> Each frame mass therefore sums to one; each predictor
    mass sums to one since <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>>\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>=1>. Successively
    summing the finite tag choices proves the first identity. For fixed tags,
    every coarse-link factor in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>
    integrates to one by right invariance of Haar measure. Their product and
    the tag sum prove the second identity.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Measurability and covariance of the block kernel.> The branch
  tests are Borel; unique compact minimizers depend continuously on their
  data. Axial eligibility is a closed inequality. Thus the finite tag sum is
  Borel, and it is bounded for fixed<nbsp><math|\<beta\>> since
  <math|0\<leqslant\><wilsonterm>\<leqslant\>2> and the Haar denominator is
  positive. Gauge transformations act by conjugation on the rooted transports
  and transform both ends of a connector; lattice isometries permute the
  roots, orders and offsets, including inversion of reversed transports. The
  objective functions, eligibility inequalities and tag masses are preserved.
  Uniqueness on the minimizing branches and reindexing on the other branches
  therefore give

  <\equation*>
    <blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U<rsup|\<gamma\>>,V<rsup|\<gamma\>\<circ\>\<iota\>>|)>=<blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>,<space|2em><blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|s<rsub|\<ell\>*b>*U,s<rsub|b>*V|)>=<blockkernel|m><rsub|\<beta\>><around|(|U,V|)>,<space|2em>\<iota\><around|(|y|)>=\<ell\>*y<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|s<rsub|b>> is the pullback by <math|x\<mapsto\>O*x+b>, with
  <math|O\<in\>H<rsub|4>>; <math|s<rsub|\<ell\>*b>> uses the same <math|O> on
  fine sites. Fubini's theorem and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>
  give<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-partition-preservation>. Thus the block kernel is
  measurable, covariant and normalized.

  <subsection|Free quadratic forms><label|subsec:B-free>

  Write <math|d<rsub|r>> for the forward lattice exterior derivative on
  <math|r>-forms. Thus <math|<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>=a<rsub|b>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>-a<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>-a<rsub|a>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|b>|)>+a<rsub|a><around|(|x|)>>.
  A history at level <math|m> is the finite sequence
  <math|<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>,J<rsub|i+1>|)><rsub|m\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>>,
  read from fine to coarse. Its stages decrease by at most one. Let
  <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>> be the derivative of the predictor at the flat
  field, and let

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-M\><rsub|m>*a<around|(|e|)>=<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>*<big|sum><rsub|r=0><rsup|\<ell\>-1>a*<around|(|\<ell\>*e<rsup|->+z+r*e<rsub|<math-up|dir><around|(|e|)>>,<math-up|dir><around|(|e|)>|)>
  </equation*>

  be straight-link averaging.

  The free problem is Gaussian. Let a free fine field<nbsp><math|a> have
  diagonal covariance <math|<math-up|diag><around|(|c<rsub|a>|)>>; at the top
  level <math|c<rsub|a>=\<lambda\><rsup|-1>>, the Feynman-gauge covariance of
  the Wilson Gaussian. Its blocked image <math|v=\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*a+<blockpenalty><rsup|-1/2>\<xi\>>,
  with independent standard noise<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, again has a diagonal
  covariance. The frame changes <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|m>> only by a gradient,
  so this recursion commutes with gauge reduction. Under blocking
  by<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>, a coarse momentum<nbsp><math|p> receives
  contributions from the fine momenta <math|\<theta\><rsub|z>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/\<ell\>>,
  <math|z\<in\><around|(|<Z>/\<ell\><Z>|)><rsup|4>>, its <em|aliases>. Near
  <math|p=0> the alias <math|z=0> carries the massless pole; the other
  aliases are regular. Aliasing the straight-link filter and adding
  <math|<blockpenalty><rsup|-1>> gives the
  recursion<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-cov-recursion> below. To write it, set

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|q<rsub|a><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<math-up|e><rsup|i*\<theta\><rsub|a>>-1,<space|1em>\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>q<rsub|a><around|(|\<theta\>|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-\<theta\>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|r<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|t|)>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|s=0><rsup|\<ell\>-1><math-up|e><rsup|i*s*t>,<space|1em><wide|w|^><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a>\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||s|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<ell\>-1|)>/2><math-up|e><rsup|i*s*\<theta\><rsub|a>>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Here <math|<wide|w|^><rsub|\<ell\>>> is the symbol of the uniform weight on
  <math|\<bbb-B\><rsub|<around|(|\<ell\>-1|)>/2>>, so
  <math|<wide|w|^><rsup|<math-up|av>>=<wide|w|^><rsub|\<ell\>><rsup|<space|0.17em>j>>.
  Starting from <math|c<rsub|a>=\<lambda\><rsup|-1>>, update these auxiliary
  diagonal covariances at each step <math|i+1\<to\>i> of the history, with
  <math|\<ell\>=\<ell\><rsub|i>> and <math|j=J<rsub|i+1>>, by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-cov-recursion>c<rprime|'><rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=<blockpenalty><rsup|-1>+\<ell\><rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|z<bmod>\<ell\>><wide|w|^><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)><rsup|2*j>*r<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)><rsub|a>|)>*r<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|-<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)><rsub|a>|)>*c<rsub|a><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>,<space|2em>\<theta\><rsub|z>=<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/\<ell\>.
  </equation>

  Gauge reduction keeps only the precision of the curl: minimizing
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>c<rsub|a><rsup|-1>*<around|\||a<rsub|a>+q<rsub|a>*\<phi\>|\|><rsup|2>>
  over gradients<nbsp><math|\<phi\>> gives
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,<math-ss|H><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>*a|\<rangle\>>>
  with the diagonal two-form metric below. For <math|p\<ne\>0>, define this
  unmodified metric and the chosen metric by

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsup|<space|0.17em>0><rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|p|)>=<frac|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|(|a,b|)>=<around|(|c,d|)>>|c<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*c<rsub|b><around|(|p|)>*<big|sum><rsub|h>q<rsub|h><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|h>*<around|(|-p|)>/c<rsub|h><around|(|p|)>>,<space|2em><wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsup|<space|0.17em>0><around|(|0|)>=I,>>|<row|<cell|<math-ss|H><rsup|-1>=<around|(|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>+\<zeta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><around*|(|d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2> shows that the value at <math|p=0> is the
  analytic extension of the formula. Since <math|d<rsub|2>*d<rsub|1>=0>, the
  modification satisfies <math|<math-ss|H>d<rsub|1>=<math-ss|H><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>>:
  the free action on curls is unchanged. It changes <math|<math-ss|H>> only
  off the range of<nbsp><math|d<rsub|1>>, where it makes the adjacent
  defect<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-free-defect> positive, as the square completion of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1> requires. Fix <math|\<zeta\><rsub|k>=max
  <around|(|\<zeta\><rsup|0><rsub|k,k>,\<zeta\><rsup|0><rsub|k+1,k>|)>>,
  where the positive thresholds for the two incoming stage pairs are chosen
  in the proof below. These constants are fixed before
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|k>> and the clipping coefficients. The ratio threshold
  also depends on the incoming <math|\<zeta\>>. The free action is

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|S><rsup|<math-up|free>><rsub|N,m><around|(|a|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>d<rsub|1>*a|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|K><rsub|N,m>=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>d<rsub|1>.
  </equation*>

  The smooth interpolation <math|W<rsub|0>> minimizes the fine free action
  plus <math|<blockpenalty><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a-v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>,
  with the remaining gradient fixed to match the predictor; the tree
  representative is obtained afterwards.

  Let <math|T> be the free two-form transfer (the lift of the straight-link
  block average): a periodized analytic alias kernel from coarse to fine
  two-forms which on curls satisfies

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|1>*\<cal-M\><rsub|m>,<space|2em>T<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>=<math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>*W<rsub|0>.
  </equation*>

  Its extension to all forms is given by the same alias formula, with a
  straight geometric-sum factor for each of the two indices. The two
  conditions are compatible: <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>-\<cal-M\><rsub|m>> takes
  values in gradients, <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|c>d<rsub|0>=0> and
  <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|f>W<rsub|0>=\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|K><rsub|c>>,
  so

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*a,<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>*v|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|a,\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|K><rsub|c>v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|a,<math-ss|K><rsub|f>W<rsub|0>*v|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,<math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>*W<rsub|0>*v|\<rangle\>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2> shows that the adjacent defect factors
  through<nbsp><math|d<rsub|2>> on both sides,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-free-defect><math-ss|H><rsub|c><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>-T<rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|-1>T-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1>d<rsub|1>*d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><math-ss|H><rsub|c>=d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-W\><rsub|3>*d<rsub|2>,
  </equation>

  with the three-form kernel <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|3>> given in its proof. The
  factorization rests on one-leg exterior division. For a
  translation-invariant kernel<nbsp><math|f> with
  coefficients<nbsp><math|f<rsub|x>> and a direction<nbsp><math|a>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-exterior-division><frac|f<around|(|p|)>-f<around|(|p\|<rsub|p<rsub|a>=0>|)>|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>>=<big|sum><rsub|x>f<rsub|x><math-up|e><rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x><space|0.17em><frac|1-<math-up|e><rsup|-i*p<rsub|a>*x<rsub|a>>|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>>,
  </equation>

  and each quotient on the right is a finite geometric sum with
  <math|<around|\||x<rsub|a>|\|>> terms. Applied in a leg in which the kernel
  is closed, this division writes it through<nbsp><math|d<rsub|2>>.

  Write <math|k=J<rsub|m>>, and let <math|C<rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|j,k>> be
  the stage-pair transfer bound obtained below. Include the transfer
  inequality in the next lemma among the ratio requirements of item<nbsp>5 of
  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>. At <math|m=N> the free form is
  the identity on curls. This free integration fixes the normalization of the
  kinetic coefficient in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>. The
  coefficient arrays considered below are those of
  <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>>, <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>>, together with <math|W<rsub|0>> and <math|T> at
  the step <math|m+1\<to\>m>; we write <math|W<rsub|0;N,m>> and
  <math|T<rsub|N,m>> when the cutoff matters. For a translation-invariant
  kernel<nbsp><math|K> between two-forms, links or sites put

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c>=max
    <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>e<rsup|c<around|\||z|\|>><around|\||K<rsub|\<cdot\>\<cdot\>><around|(|z|)>|\|>,
  </equation*>

  the maximum running over the finite component and residue indices. The
  constants <math|\<gamma\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> below are uniform over
  histories, and <math|c<rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> depends only on<nbsp><math|k>;
  strip and coefficient bounds are uniform in <math|N,m,p>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Free quadratic forms><label|lem:B2> For histories satisfying
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|j,k>*\<zeta\><rsub|j>>
    and <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,k>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|k>>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)><math|W<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|K><rsub|N,m+1>W<rsub|0>+<blockpenalty><around|(|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>-I|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>-I|)>=<math-ss|K><rsub|N,m>>;

      <item*|(ii)><math|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsub|N,m><around|(|0|)>=I> and
      <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>\<geqslant\>2*\<gamma\><rsub|k>*I>;

      <item*|(iii)><math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,k>*I=\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<rho\><rsub|N,m>>;

      <item*|(iv)>the adjacent defect<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-free-defect> has
      <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|3>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|k>*I>;

      <item*|(v)>the coefficient arrays and their limits have uniform
      analytic strip bounds and exponential coefficient decay, with some rate
      <math|c\<gtr\>0>; at fixed<nbsp><math|m>, for every
      <math|0\<less\>c<rprime|'>\<less\>c>, as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>,

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:B-free-limit>

        <\aligned>
          <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>-<math-ss|H><rsub|\<infty\>,m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m><rsup|-1>-<math-ss|H><rsub|\<infty\>,m><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>-\<rho\><rsub|\<infty\>,m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|2em>+<around|\<\|\|\>|W<rsub|0;N,m>-W<rsub|0;\<infty\>,m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|N,m>-T<rsub|\<infty\>,m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>>\<longrightarrow\>0.>>>>
        </aligned>
      </equation>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  The proof separates the principal Fourier pole to obtain analytic,
  uniformly positive metrics, makes the defect positive off the curl range,
  and then proves the energy identity and the cutoff limit; the history
  bounds come first, so that inversion at zero momentum is justified.

  <\proof>
    <em|Step 1: the covariance recursion and the two-form metric.> The
    straight-link multiplier is <math|\<ell\>*<space|0.17em><wide|w|^><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsup|j>*r<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|a>|)>>,
    and <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*d<rsub|0>*\<phi\>=d<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<phi\>\<circ\>\<iota\>|)>>,
    so gauge reduction commutes with the block step. Taking aliases of
    <math|<math-up|Cov><around|(|v|)>=\<cal-M\><rsub|m><math-up|diag><around|(|c<rsub|a>|)>*\<cal-M\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>+<blockpenalty><rsup|-1>I>
    gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-cov-recursion>;
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>=\<ell\><rsup|-4>*\<ell\><rsup|2>> combines
    counting adjoints with the two straight connectors. For
    <math|w<rsub|a>=1/c<rsub|a>>, minimization of the diagonal precision over
    gradients gives

    <\equation*>
      min<rsub|\<phi\>> <big|sum><rsub|a>w<rsub|a>*<around|\||a<rsub|a>+q<rsub|a>*\<phi\>|\|><rsup|2>=<frac|<big|sum><rsub|a\<less\>b>w<rsub|a>*w<rsub|b>*<around|\||q<rsub|a>*a<rsub|b>-q<rsub|b>*a<rsub|a>|\|><rsup|2>|<big|sum><rsub|d>w<rsub|d><around|\||q<rsub|d>|\|><rsup|2>>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed the minimizer is <math|\<phi\>=-<big|sum><rsub|a>w<rsub|a>*<wide|q|\<bar\>><rsub|a>*a<rsub|a>/<big|sum><rsub|a>w<rsub|a><around|\||q<rsub|a>|\|><rsup|2>>;
    expanding its square proves the identity. This is precisely the two-form
    metric defined above.

    <em|Step 2: the cumulative filter.> For total history
    dilation<nbsp><math|M> at stage<nbsp><math|j>, the cumulative averaging
    filter is

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<wide|w|^><rsub|M><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsup|j>*<big|prod><rsub|<text|stage
      increases at dilation >M<rprime|'>><wide|w|^><rsub|M<rprime|'>><around|(|\<theta\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Successive dilations<nbsp><math|M<rprime|'>> have ratio at
    least<nbsp><math|3>. On the enlarged coarse cube
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>5*\<pi\>/4>,
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>p<rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<eta\>>,
    the geometric-sum formula gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:B-filter><around|\||f*<around|(|<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/M|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*<big|prod><rsub|a><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|-j>,<space|2em><around|\||f*<around|(|p/M|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0<space|1em><around|(|p<text|
      real>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For the extra product, use <math|<around|\||<wide|w|^><rsub|M<rprime|'>>*<around|(|p/M|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><math-up|e><rsup|4*\<eta\>*M<rprime|'>/M>>
    and the corresponding real lower bound
    <math|<math-up|e><rsup|-C*M<rprime|'>/M>>. The sum
    of<nbsp><math|M<rprime|'>/M> is bounded by a geometric series. The
    identities <math|<wide|w|^><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>*<wide|w|^><rsub|\<ell\><rprime|'>>*<around|(|\<ell\>*\<theta\>|)>=<wide|w|^><rsub|\<ell\>*\<ell\><rprime|'>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>>
    and <math|r<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|t|)>*r<rsub|\<ell\><rprime|'>>*<around|(|\<ell\>*t|)>=r<rsub|\<ell\>*\<ell\><rprime|'>><around|(|t|)>>
    follow by writing each integer in its quotient and residue classes. They
    give the cumulative filter. The basic
    <math|<wide|w|^><rsub|M><rsup|<space|0.17em>j>> factor supplies the
    displayed alias decay. It also bounds
    <math|r<rsub|M>*<around|(|<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)><rsub|a>/M|)>> by
    <math|C/<around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)>>, with a positive
    principal lower bound. Thus every nonprincipal alias is damped by the
    <math|j>-th power of the filter, uniformly in the number of blockings.

    <em|Step 3: the principal pole.> Separate the principal alias in the
    iterated<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-cov-recursion>. With
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>=M<rsup|2>*\<lambda\>*<around|(|p/M|)>>
    this reads

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=<frac|A<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>>+c<rsup|<math-up|np>><rsub|a><around|(|p|)>,<space|2em>A<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>=f*<around|(|p/M|)>*f*<around|(|-p/M|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|p<rsub|a>/M|)>*r<rsub|M>*<around|(|-p<rsub|a>/M|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The nonprincipal part<nbsp><math|c<rsup|<math-up|np>><rsub|a>> contains
    nonprincipal aliases, bounded by<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter> and
    <math|<around|\||M<rsup|2>*\<lambda\>*<around|(|<around|(|p+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/M|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>c<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>
    for <math|z\<ne\>0> on a smaller strip. Each earlier noise contributes
    <math|C<rsub|j><around|(|M<rprime|'>|)><rsup|-2>> by the squared filter
    bound. Summing these geometric contributions makes
    <math|c<rsup|<math-up|np>><rsub|a>> analytic and uniformly bounded; it is
    nonnegative on real momenta.

    Put <math|B<rsub|a>=A<rsub|a>+\<lambda\><rsub|M>*c<rsup|<math-up|np>><rsub|a>>
    and <math|F=f*<around|(|p/M|)>*f*<around|(|-p/M|)>>, so
    <math|c<rsub|a>=B<rsub|a>/\<lambda\><rsub|M>>. The identity for a
    straight geometric sum implies

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p|)>*F=M<rsup|2>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|p/M|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p/M|)>*A<rsub|a><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    It removes the apparent singularity in

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-h\><around|(|p|)>=F<rsup|-1>-<big|sum><rsub|a><frac|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p|)>*c<rsup|<math-up|np>><rsub|a>|A<rsub|a>*B<rsub|a>>,<space|2em>\<lambda\><rsub|M>*\<frak-h\>=<big|sum><rsub|a><frac|q<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-p|)>|B<rsub|a>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    On the real enlarged cube <math|B<rsub|a>> is bounded above and below by
    positive constants. Since <math|\<lambda\><around|(|p|)>> and
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>> are uniformly comparable there,
    the last identity bounds<nbsp><math|\<frak-h\>> above and below,
    including <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|0|)>=1>. Thin the strip using the
    uniform analytic bounds. The unmodified plaquette metric is then

    <\equation*>
      <wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsup|<space|0.17em>0><rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|p|)>=<frac|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|(|a,b|)>=<around|(|c,d|)>>|\<frak-h\><around|(|p|)>*B<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>*B<rsub|b><around|(|p|)>>*<space|2em><around|(|a\<less\>b,c\<less\>d|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Substitution of <math|w<rsub|a>=\<lambda\><rsub|M>/B<rsub|a>> in the
    minimization of Step<nbsp>1 gives exactly
    <math|1/<around|(|\<frak-h\>*B<rsub|a>*B<rsub|b>|)>>. Thus this formula
    extends the defined metric analytically to zero. It and its inverse are
    uniformly positive on real momenta, with
    <math|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsup|<space|0.17em>0><around|(|0|)>=I>.

    <em|Step 4: the inverse modification and the defect.> The inverse
    modification defining <math|<math-ss|H>> has fixed stage coefficient
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|j>>. This positive addition has uniform analytic
    bounds. Since <math|d<rsub|2>*d<rsub|1>=0>, it gives
    <math|<math-ss|H>d<rsub|1>=<math-ss|H><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>>; it also leaves
    <math|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><around|(|0|)>=I>.

    The defect annihilates curls, for both the unmodified and the modified
    metrics: by the two conditions on<nbsp><math|T> and
    <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|-1><math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|0>d<rsub|1>=d<rsub|1>>,
    its value on <math|d<rsub|1>*v> is

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|H><rsub|c>d<rsub|1>*<around|(|v-\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>*v-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1><math-ss|K><rsub|c>v|)>=0,
    </equation*>

    since <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>=I-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1><math-ss|K><rsub|c>>
    and <math|d<rsub|1>*d<rsub|0>=0>. For the unmodified metrics, the defect
    is closed in both legs, so one-leg division<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-exterior-division>
    in each leg writes it as <math|d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|0>*d<rsub|2>>.
    The two divisions lose two powers of path length; the exponential bounds
    leave a smaller strip. Symmetrize the resulting three-form
    kernel<nbsp><math|F<rsub|0>>. If <math|S<rsub|0>> is the corresponding
    lift of <math|d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>*T<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>>,
    Woodbury inversion gives <math|d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|H><rsub|c>=D<rsub|c>*d<rsub|2>>.
    Substituting the two inverse modifications into the defect, the outgoing
    addition is <math|\<zeta\><rsub|k>*D<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*D<rsub|c>> and
    the incoming subtraction is <math|-\<zeta\><rsub|j>*D<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*S<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*S<rsub|0>*D<rsub|c>>.
    Hence

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-W\><rsub|3>=D<rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|F<rsub|0>+\<zeta\><rsub|k>*I-\<zeta\><rsub|j>*S<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*S<rsub|0>|)>*D<rsub|c>,<space|2em>D<rsub|c>=<around|(|I+\<zeta\><rsub|k>*d<rsub|2><around|(|<math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|0>|)><rsup|-1>*d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    The pole-free alias estimates give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|S<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|j,k>/\<ell\><rsup|2>>
    on real momenta: the lift has per-residue order <math|\<ell\><rsup|-3>>,
    whose square is summed over <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> residues. Choose
    <math|\<zeta\><rsup|0><rsub|j,k>=C+2> and then
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|j>*C<rsup|<math-up|tr>><rsub|j,k>>
    for the stage pair <math|<around|(|j,k|)>>. The parenthesis is at least
    <math|I>. Both <math|D<rsub|c>> and its inverse have uniform analytic
    bounds at the fixed stages. Consequently
    <math|c*I\<leqslant\>\<cal-W\><rsub|3>\<leqslant\>C*I> on real momenta,
    uniformly in the history. This proves<nbsp>(iv); it is the input for the
    square terms in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>.

    <em|Step 5: coercivity and the row factor.> Choose
    <math|2*\<gamma\><rsub|k>> below the lower bounds for the top and the two
    incoming possibilities at stage<nbsp><math|k>. Then
    <math|<math-ss|H>-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,k>*I\<geqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|k>*I>.
    Its positive square root<nbsp><math|\<rho\>> is analytic on a smaller
    common strip: expand the square root about a scalar exceeding the common
    upper bound. The resulting convergent matrix power series agrees with the
    positive root on the real torus. Contour displacement in Fourier
    inversion gives exponential coefficient decay and the same bound for all
    periodizations. This proves<nbsp>(ii) and<nbsp>(iii).

    <em|Step 6: the normal equations and the energy identity.> The normal
    equations for<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-cov-recursion>, with the gradient chosen
    to match the rooted predictor, give

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|K><rsub|f>W<rsub|0>=\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|K><rsub|c>,<space|2em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>=I-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1><math-ss|K><rsub|c><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    At nonzero momentum write <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|f>=<math-up|diag><around|(|c<rsub|a>|)>>
    and <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|c>=\<cal-M\><rsub|m><math-ss|C><rsub|f>\<cal-M\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>+<blockpenalty><rsup|-1>I>.
    Gaussian conditioning gives <math|W<rsub|0>=<math-ss|C><rsub|f>\<cal-M\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|C><rsub|c><rsup|-1>>
    modulo gradients and <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>=I-<blockpenalty><rsup|-1><math-ss|C><rsub|c><rsup|-1>>.
    Reduce these identities over gradients and select the gradient matching
    <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>>. This gives the two normal equations. The
    pole-free formulas above extend them to zero; the constant fine potential
    is the coarse potential divided by<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>. Expanding the
    square gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|a,<math-ss|K><rsub|f>a|\<rangle\>>+<blockpenalty><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a-v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|v,<math-ss|K><rsub|c>v|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|a-W<rsub|0>*v,<around|(|<math-ss|K><rsub|f>+<blockpenalty>\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|a-W<rsub|0>*v|)>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Set <math|a=W<rsub|0>*v> and polarize. This is<nbsp>(i). The empty
    history gives <math|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>=I> and hence the Wilson form on
    curls, also after the inverse modification.

    <em|Step 7: the cutoff limit.> Choose <math|c\<gtr\>0> below the common
    decay rate of the forms, inverses, row factors and the kernels
    <math|W<rsub|0>,T>. For fixed <math|m> let <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>, so
    the cumulative dilation <math|M\<to\>\<infty\>>. At each nonzero real
    momentum <math|p>, <math|\<lambda\><rsub|M><around|(|p|)>\<to\><around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>>;
    <math|A<rsub|a><around|(|p|)>> converges as a product of filter factors
    whose deviations from one are summable; the nonprincipal aliases converge
    by dominated convergence with<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter>; and the noise
    contributions form a convergent series since <math|M> at least doubles at
    each step. Hence the symbols of <math|<math-ss|H><rsup|0>>,
    <math|<math-ss|H><rsup|\<pm\>1>>, <math|W<rsub|0>> and <math|T> converge
    at every such <math|p>, and so does that of <math|\<rho\>> by continuity
    of the positive square root. The uniform strip bounds make the
    coefficient arrays precompact under diagonal extraction; an array with
    these bounds is determined by its symbol at nonzero real momenta, so all
    subsequential limits coincide, and dominated convergence gives
    convergence at every smaller width. This
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-free-limit>. Absolute summation also gives
    convergence of every fixed periodization.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The stationary background and Gaussian
  coordinates><label|subsec:B-stationary>

  In each block <math|\<ell\>*y+\<bbb-B\><rsub|<around|(|\<ell\>-1|)>/2>> fix
  an axial spanning tree rooted at <math|\<ell\>*y>, and set its tree links
  to<nbsp><math|1>. (Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-energy> allows every
  rooted block-tree gauge.) The remaining logarithmic link coordinates form
  the axial field<nbsp><math|\<psi\>>. For a fine edge
  <math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>> put

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|V<rsub|<around|(|y,a|)>>,>|<cell|y=<math-up|parent><around|(|x|)>,<math-up|parent><around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>=y+e<rsub|a>,>>|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|<math-up|parent><around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>=<math-up|parent><around|(|x|)>,>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|U*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)><rsub|e>=exp
    <around|(|<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*\<psi\>|)><rsub|e>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>exp
    v|)><rsub|e>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Here <math|<math-up|dir><around|(|e|)>=a> for <math|e=<around|(|x,a|)>>,
  <math|<math-up|parent>> takes the centred block containing a site,
  <math|<math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>=<math-up|parent><around|(|x|)>>, and
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>> extends by zero on tree edges. The
  block-constant lift <math|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V> is one on every
  tree edge. The fine leading action is the hypercubic average of

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|f><around|(|U|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>*<big|sum><rsub|\<square\>><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|x,\<frak-p\>><around|\||\<cal-L\><rsub|\<frak-p\>><rsup|<math-up|path>><around|(|U,x|)>|\|><rsup|2>+<big|sum><rsub|x,i><around|(|\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|3|)>>+\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|4|)>>|)>*<around|(|U,x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The transported curvatures and coefficients are those of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>, with masks and clipping removed. The
  action excludes the inherited quadratic loop action and the common inverse
  coupling. Let <math|S*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)>> be this action at
  <math|U*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)>> plus

  <\equation*>
    <blockpenalty><big|sum><rsub|e<math-up|coarse>><wilsonterm><around*|(|<around|(|exp
    v|)><rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>>*<around|(|U*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)>|)><rsup|-1>|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  again without the common inverse coupling, and put
  <math|<math-ss|Q>=D<rsub|\<psi\>><rsup|2>*S*<around|(|0,0|)>>. The path
  bounds make these unclipped sums analytic near zero.

  <paragraph|Block norms.> A fine field that is smooth on the block scale has
  energy of order <math|\<mu\><rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>,
  with <math|\<mu\>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>>, so ordinary <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>>
  norms would lose a factor of order<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|2>> in inverse
  bounds. The block norm removes this loss by weighting each fine Fourier
  alias by <math|<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>+<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>>.
  Put <math|\<mu\>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>>, <math|\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<around|(|\<xi\>+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/\<ell\>>,
  with <math|x,z\<in\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,\<ell\>-1|}><rsup|4>>, and

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<omega\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<around*|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>+<big|sum><rsub|a><around|\||q<rsub|a><around|(|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|)>|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|1/2>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-U\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsub|x*z>=\<ell\><rsup|-2><math-up|e><rsup|i*\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<cdot\>x>,<space|1em>T<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=\<cal-U\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><math-up|diag><around|(|\<omega\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsup|-1>|)>*\<cal-U\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|0|)><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  A fine Bloch field at coarse momentum<nbsp><math|\<xi\>> obeys
  <math|u*<around|(|x+\<ell\>*n|)>=<math-up|e><rsup|i*\<xi\>\<cdot\>n><space|0.17em>u<around|(|x|)>>,
  and <math|<wide|u|^>=\<cal-U\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsup|-1>*u>
  are its alias coordinates on the fixed cell. For real<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>
  the block norm and its dual are

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>><rsup|2>=<big|sum><rsub|z>\<omega\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsup|2><around|\||<wide|u|^><rsub|z>|\|><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>,\<ast\>><rsup|2>=<big|sum><rsub|z>\<omega\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsup|-2><around|\||<wide|f|^><rsub|z>|\|><rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  the fine <math|H<rsup|1>> norm with mass<nbsp><math|\<mu\>> and its dual,
  in Bloch coordinates; coarse coordinates carry the Euclidean norm. Since
  <math|\<cal-U\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>> is unitary for
  real<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsup|-1>*u|\<\|\|\>>>;
  the last factor of<nbsp><math|T<rsub|\<ell\>>> fixes the reference cell. On
  the strip <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|\<eta\>>=<around|{|\<xi\>\<in\><C><rsup|4>:<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>\<xi\><rsub|a>|\|>\<less\>\<eta\>|}>>
  we use this formula as the definition: <math|T<rsub|\<ell\>>> on fine links
  or sites, the identity on coarse coordinates, direct sums on products, and
  <math|T<rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|-\<xi\>|)><rsup|-T>> on dual fields. Axial
  variables inherit the norm of their slice embedding. For kernels set

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|K|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\>><rsup|<math-up|bl>>=sup
    <around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|<math-up|out>><around|(|\<xi\><rsub|<math-up|out>>|)><rsup|-1>*K*<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|<math-up|out>>,\<xi\><rsub|<math-up|in>>|)>*T<rsub|<math-up|in>><around|(|\<xi\><rsub|<math-up|in>>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|op>><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  the supremum over momenta whose indicated shifts all lie
  in<nbsp><math|\<cal-S\><rsub|\<eta\>>>. Use tensor products for several
  inputs; field insertions are normalized coarse directions. The weights use
  <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<theta\><rsub|z>> and need not be
  analytic. Write <math|W<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*v=<around|(|v,D*\<psi\><around|(|0|)>*v|)>>
  for the axial tangent, and retain <math|W<rsub|0>> for the smooth free
  interpolation before axial gauge. They are distinct coordinate
  representatives.

  The next lemma supplies the stationary configuration and identifies its
  first derivative with the free solution. Let
  <math|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>> denote any product of <math|r> forward
  fine differences. Fix an integer <math|r<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0> with
  <math|r<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>2> or <math|j\<geqslant\>r<rsub|0>+3>, and let
  <math|x> be any centred fine residue. The constants
  <math|c<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0> and <math|C<rsub|j,r>> depend on the stage and
  derivative order, but not on the ratio, history or period.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stationary interpolation><label|lem:B3>

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)>Near zero there is an analytic stationary branch with

      <\equation*>
        D<rsub|\<psi\>>*S*<around|(|v,\<psi\><around|(|v|)>|)>=0,<space|1em>\<psi\><around|(|0|)>=0,<space|1em><math-ss|Q>\<geqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>>*I<space|0.17em>,
      </equation*>

      where <math|\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>>\<gtr\>0> does not depend on the
      period.

      <item*|(ii)><math|D*\<psi\><around|(|0|)>> is the axial representative
      of the free interpolation: <math|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>v+\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*D*\<psi\><around|(|0|)>*v>
      is the tree representative of<nbsp><math|W<rsub|0>*v>.

      <item*|(iii)>The averaging symbol equals one at zero and vanishes to
      order at least <math|j> at every nonzero alias.

      <item*|(iv)>For <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>r<rsub|0>> and every
      bounded coarse field<nbsp><math|v>,

      <\gather*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+x,y<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>.>>>>
      </gather*>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  The proof uses a local axial estimate for coercivity, a finite-dimensional
  implicit equation for the branch, and the alias decay of the averaging
  symbol for the kernel bounds.

  <\proof>
    <em|The minimizing choices are analytic.> At the flat field the frame
    objective has quadratic part <math|<math-up|av><rsub|r,r<rprime|'>><around|\||h<rsub|r>-h<rsub|r<rprime|'>>|\|><rsup|2>/2>.
    On <math|h<rsub|0>=0> this is positive definite. The predictor objective
    has Hessian<nbsp><math|I> in its group variable. Their chart equations
    are real analytic. Their local complexifications and the analytic
    implicit function theorem make both minimizing choices real analytic near
    zero. Apply<nbsp><cite-detail|Lebl2026|Theorem<nbsp>1.3.8> to the
    complexified chart equations, which vanish at zero and have the displayed
    invertible Hessians. The neighbourhoods may depend on the period.

    For a fine infinitesimal field<nbsp><math|a>, let
    <math|I<rsub|a>*<around|(|x\<to\>x<rprime|'>;o|)>> be its signed sum
    along the route. Differentiating the frame equation gives

    <\equation*>
      \<tau\><rsub|y><around|(|r|)>=-<math-up|av><rsub|r<rprime|'>,o><space|0.17em>I<rsub|a>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+r\<to\>\<ell\>*y+r<rprime|'>;o|)>,<space|1em>h<rsub|y,r><rprime|'>=\<tau\><rsub|y><around|(|r|)>-\<tau\><rsub|y><around|(|0|)>,<space|1em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a=\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*a+d<rsub|0>*\<cal-F\><rsub|m>*a<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|m>*a<around|(|y|)>=\<tau\><rsub|y><around|(|0|)>-<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r><rsup|<math-up|av>>*\<tau\><rsub|y><around|(|r|)>>.
    In particular <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*d<rsub|0>*\<phi\>=d<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<phi\>\<circ\>\<iota\>|)>>.

    <em|Coercivity on the axial slice.> Telescoping the boundary of each
    coordinate rectangle expresses a free link as a block-constant
    coarse-link value plus curls in its enlarged block. Averaging the
    straight connectors replaces the coarse-link value
    by<nbsp><math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a>; the frame differences are route sums
    of the same curls. For example, interchanging two positive steps gives

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|a><around|(|x|)>+a<rsub|b>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>-a<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>-a<rsub|a>*<around|(|x+e<rsub|b>|)>=<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    Reverse steps change the sign and translate this identity. Repeated
    interchanges compare the tree route, a straight connector and its
    averaged translates. Thus, at each free edge<nbsp><math|e>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||a<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<square\>\<in\>\<ell\><space|0.17em><math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>+\<bbb-B\><rsub|<around|(|j+7|)>*\<ell\>>><around|\||<around|(|d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|\<square\>>|\|>+<around|\||<around|(|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a|)><rsub|<math-up|parent><around|(|e|)>,<math-up|dir><around|(|e|)>>|\|><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This is an identity followed by a triangle inequality: tree-edge
    coordinates are zero, and every interchange of two successive route steps
    contributes exactly one signed curl. Cauchy\USchwarz and the bounded
    number of enlarged blocks containing a given plaquette give constants
    <math|A<rsub|j,\<ell\>>,B<rsub|j,\<ell\>>\<less\>\<infty\>>, independent
    of the period, with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|1>*a*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|2><rsup|2>+B<rsub|j,\<ell\>>|\<\|\|\>>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The row construction of Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:C-matching>, at
    the finer input level, identifies the leading Hessian with
    <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|f>=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|H><rsub|f>d<rsub|1>>;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> fixes its normalization at zero momentum.
    Cubic and quartic curvature atoms have zero quadratic derivative.
    Consequently, for the slice embedding<nbsp><math|\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,<math-ss|Q>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*\<psi\>,<math-ss|H><rsub|f>d<rsub|1>*\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>+<blockpenalty><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><space|0.17em>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>>=min
    <around|(|2*\<gamma\><rsub|j>,<blockpenalty>|)>/<around|(|1+A<rsub|j,\<ell\>>+B<rsub|j,\<ell\>>|)>>
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>(ii)).

    <em|The branch and its tangent.> The map
    <math|\<cal-E\><around|(|v,\<psi\>|)>=D<rsub|\<psi\>>*S*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)>>
    is analytic, <math|\<cal-E\><around|(|0,0|)>=0>, and
    <math|D<rsub|\<psi\>>*\<cal-E\><around|(|0,0|)>=<math-ss|Q>>. The same
    implicit function theorem gives <math|\<psi\><around|(|v|)>>.
    Differentiation yields

    <\equation*>
      D*\<psi\><around|(|0|)>=-<math-ss|Q><rsup|-1>D<rsub|v>*D<rsub|\<psi\>>*S*<around|(|0,0|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    To identify this tangent, note that at the flat field the quadratic part
    of <math|S*<around|(|v,\<cdot\>|)>> in the fine field
    <math|a=<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>v+\<iota\><rsub|<math-up|ax>>*\<psi\>>
    is one half of <math|<around|\<langle\>|a,<math-ss|K><rsub|f>a|\<rangle\>>+<blockpenalty><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*a-v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
    By the energy identity (Step<nbsp>6 of the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>), <math|W<rsub|0>*v> minimizes it over all
    fine fields; a gradient <math|d<rsub|0>*\<phi\>> with <math|\<phi\>>
    vanishing at the block roots changes neither term, and exactly one such
    modification of<nbsp><math|W<rsub|0>*v> has zero tree links. By
    coercivity it is the unique minimizer over the slice, which
    proves<nbsp>(ii). The implicit neighbourhood may depend on the volume;
    the coercivity constant does not.

    <em|Kernel bounds.> Finally <math|<wide|w|^><rsup|<math-up|av>><around|(|\<theta\>|)>=<wide|w|^><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|\<theta\>|)><rsup|j>>.
    At a nonzero alias <math|2*\<pi\>*z/\<ell\>>, at least one centred
    geometric sum has a simple zero, so the product has a zero of order at
    least<nbsp><math|j\<geqslant\>4>. At zero it equals one. For a word of
    <math|r> fine differences the alias multiplier is a product of <math|r>
    factors <math|q<rsub|a<rsub|h>><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>>, each
    bounded by <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)>>.
    This includes a difference crossing a fine cell boundary: its Bloch phase
    shifts the coarse base and gives exactly the same multiplier. After
    separating the principal pole as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, the
    interpolation alias has the bound <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|-j>>
    on a common smaller strip. The centred-residue exponential is bounded
    there. Consequently the sum of absolute alias terms after <math|r>
    differences is at most

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r>*<big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>><around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|r>*<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|4><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|-j>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'><rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r>*<space|2em><around|(|r\<leqslant\>j-2|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|>|)><rsup|r>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|r>*<big|prod><rsub|a><around|(|1+<around|\||z<rsub|a>|\|>|)><rsup|r>>
    and each remaining exponent is at least two. The pole-free principal term
    has the same bound. This proves the required symbol estimate through
    order two for <math|j\<geqslant\>4>, and through order <math|r<rsub|0>>
    for <math|j\<geqslant\>r<rsub|0>+3>. There are finitely many difference
    words at a fixed stage; take a common smaller strip for them. Shifting
    the four coarse Fourier contours towards the sign of the displacement
    gives the first bound in<nbsp>(iv). It includes all link components and
    is uniform in the centred fine residue<nbsp><math|x>. Summing over
    <math|y<rprime|'>> proves the second bound. Periodization preserves the
    row bound by the triangle inequality.

    The same kernel bound permits polynomially growing inputs. For
    <math|0\<leqslant\>c<rprime|'>\<less\>c<rsub|j>> and
    <math|h\<geqslant\>0> put

    <\equation*>
      w<rsub|c<rprime|'>,h><around|(|y|)>=e<rsup|c<rprime|'><around|\||y|\|>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||y|\|>|)><rsup|h>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>,h>=sup<rsub|y>
      <frac|<around|\||v<around|(|y|)>|\|>|w<rsub|c<rprime|'>,h><around|(|y|)>>.
    </equation*>

    The triangle inequality gives <math|w<rsub|c<rprime|'>,h><around|(|y<rprime|'>|)>/w<rsub|c<rprime|'>,h><around|(|y|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|c<rprime|'>*<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>|)><rsup|h>>.
    Thus absolute kernel summation yields

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:B-weighted-interp>sup<rsub|y,x>
      <frac|<around|\||\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>*v*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+x|)>|\|>|w<rsub|c<rprime|'>,h><around|(|y|)>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r,c<rprime|'>,h>*\<ell\><rsup|-1-r><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|c<rprime|'>,h>.
    </equation>

    The sum converges since <math|c<rprime|'>\<less\>c<rsub|j>>. In
    particular<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-weighted-interp> applies to polynomial jets
    of any fixed degree and to a cubic remainder when <math|j\<geqslant\>6>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Gaussian coordinates.> Write
  <math|U<rsub|v>=U*<around|(|v,\<psi\><around|(|v|)>|)>> and
  <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>=D<rsub|\<psi\>><rsup|2>*S*<around|(|v,\<psi\><around|(|v|)>|)>>.
  Let <math|s> change axial logarithmic increments to left increments
  at<nbsp><math|U<rsub|v>>, and let <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>> be the
  product Haar density in these coordinates divided by its value at zero. For
  <math|x=\<ell\>*y+r> in its centred block, set
  <math|\<Psi\>*<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)><around|(|x|)>=h<rsub|<math-up|fr>><around|(|y|)>*h<rsub|y,r><around|(|U|)>>,
  using the patch frame of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D20b>. This extends
  a coarse frame<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>> over each fine block. The
  joint variable is <math|<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>>, with gauge
  action <math|<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>\<mapsto\><around|(|U<rsup|\<gamma\>>,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>*<around|(|\<gamma\>\<circ\>\<iota\>|)><rsup|-1>|)>>.
  Let <math|\<cal-R\>> insert fine site gauge parameters, and let
  <math|\<cal-I\>> embed free-edge increments, with zero coarse-frame
  component. Thus <math|<around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)>:<g><rsup|<math-up|fine
  sites>>\<oplus\><g><rsup|<math-up|free edges>>\<to\><g><rsup|<math-up|fine
  edges>>\<oplus\><g><rsup|<math-up|coarse sites>>> is square: the number of
  tree edges is the number of fine sites minus the number of block roots.
  Write <math|<math-ss|J><around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>> for the joint
  left Hessian of the leading action plus block penalty, with the coarse
  field fixed. We evaluate it along the stationary background; its value at
  the flat field has fine kinetic part <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|f>>. Put

  <\equation*>
    X=D<rsub|w=0>*<around|[|-log <around|(|\<Psi\>*<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)><rsup|-1>*\<Psi\><around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|w><around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>|)>|)>|]>,<space|2em>\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1>=<big|sum><rsub|x,i>\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|2|)>><around|(|<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>,x|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The last sum is the inherited quadratic loop action of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>. The <em|one-loop action> is

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
    det <math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>-log J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|v|)>.
  </equation*>

  It is the negative logarithm of the field-dependent Gaussian factor.

  The determinant and coordinate density must be compared in the same
  counting coordinates. Let <math|n> be the number of real axial coordinates.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Gaussian coordinates><label|lem:B4> Near <math|v=0> the Hessian
    <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>> is positive,

    <\equation*>
      J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>=<around|\||det
      D*s|\|>\<gtr\>0,<space|2em>J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|0|)>=1,<space|2em>X*\<cal-R\>=I,<space|2em>det
      <around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)><rsup|2>=1,
    </equation*>

    and, for every <math|\<beta\>\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|v|)><space|0.17em><math-up|e><rsup|-\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>><big|int><math-up|e><rsup|-<frac|\<beta\>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|w,<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>*w|\<rangle\>>><space|0.17em>d*w=<around*|(|<frac|2*\<pi\>|\<beta\>>|)><rsup|n/2><math-up|e><rsup|-<math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Thus the stationary Gaussian integral, including the inherited loop
    action and the Haar density, has field-dependent factor
    <math|<math-up|e><rsup|-<math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>>>.
  </lemma>

  The proof computes the Haar density in axial coordinates at the stationary
  point. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C5> then rewrites the determinant in
  gauge-completed form; <eqref|eq:B-one-loop-box> below collects the
  resulting normalization.

  <\proof>
    Tree gauge is an exact Haar change of variables. In tree order, each tree
    link is a Haar translate of its child gauge variable, whose integral is
    one. For fixed <math|V>, gauge transformations fixing every block root
    leave the kernel of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04> invariant.
    Integration of these gauge variables reduces its integral to product Haar
    measure on free links; root transformations are not integrated here. In
    exponential coordinates write this measure as
    <math|J<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|n<rsub|e>>*<big|prod><rsub|e>J<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|e>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<psi\>>,
    with <math|J<around|(|0|)>=1> and <math|n<rsub|e>> the number of free
    links.

    At a fixed stationary axial point<nbsp><math|y=\<psi\><around|(|v|)>> the
    coordinate change on each free link is

    <\equation*>
      s<rsub|y><around|(|\<psi\>|)><rsub|e>=log <around|(|exp
      \<psi\><rsub|e>*exp <around|(|-y<rsub|e>|)>|)>,<space|2em>s<rsub|y><around|(|y|)>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Right invariance of Haar measure gives
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|e>J<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|e>|)>=<around|\||det
    D*s<rsub|y><around|(|\<psi\>|)>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|e>J<around|(|s<rsub|y><around|(|\<psi\>|)><rsub|e>|)>>.
    Evaluate at<nbsp><math|\<psi\>=y> to obtain
    <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|v|)>=<around|\||det
    D*s<rsub|y><around|(|y|)>|\|>>. Since <math|D*s<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=I>,
    its value at zero is one, and it stays positive nearby. Stationarity
    removes the second-derivative term in the Hessian chain rule, giving

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:B-hessian-chain><math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>=<around|(|D*s|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>\<cal-I\>|)>*D*s
    </equation>

    for the joint left Hessian<nbsp><math|<math-ss|J>>.

    Gauge covariance of<nbsp><math|\<Psi\>> gives <math|X*\<cal-R\>=I>. In
    tree-height order, each tree-edge row
    of<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)>> has its child gauge
    coordinate<nbsp><math|\<pm\>I> and earlier gauge coordinates; each
    coarse-frame row has its root gauge coordinate <math|-I>, and every
    free-edge slice column has its identity entry. Thus <math|det
    <around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)><rsup|2>=1>. Positivity of
    <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|0|)>> from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>
    persists near zero.

    The Gaussian integral of the positive form
    <math|<frac|\<beta\>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|w,<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>*w|\<rangle\>>>
    equals <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>/\<beta\>|)><rsup|n/2><around|(|det
    <math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>>. Multiplying by
    <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|v|)><math-up|e><rsup|-\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>>>
    gives the displayed identity by the definition
    of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|T>>. The factors
    <math|J<rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|n<rsub|e>>> and
    <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>/\<beta\>|)><rsup|n/2>> do not depend
    on<nbsp><math|v>; they enter the vacuum scalar.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Inverse, vertex and Ward estimates><label|subsec:B-inverse>

  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C> extracts the curvature-square
  coefficient of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|T>> by comparing it with an auxiliary
  fine determinant. This subsection supplies three inputs for that
  comparison: inverse bounds uniform in the block ratio
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>), vertex bounds
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>), and a Ward identity for the predictor
  contact term (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B6>).

  <paragraph|The regulator.> For a fine site field put

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|d<rsub|U>*\<phi\>|)><rsub|e>=\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|->|)>-<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|e>|)>*\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|+>|)>,<space|1em>\<Delta\><rsub|U>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>,<space|1em>P<rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>,<space|1em>\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|9><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|\<mu\>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>>. The regulator
  <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>> acts as a mass at the block scale:
  by<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-mass-floor> below, <math|\<Delta\><rsub|0>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|-10>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|0>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>,
  so below momentum<nbsp><math|\<mu\>> it is a mass of
  order<nbsp><math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>, while above<nbsp><math|\<mu\>> it decays
  like <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<mu\>/<around|\||k|\|>|)><rsup|18>>.
  The ninth power is chosen because <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>> is trace
  class at each coarse momentum, uniformly in<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>(i)). After two field derivatives of
  <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2><around|(|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|)><rsup|3>>
  one undifferentiated factor <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>> remains, and its
  trace norm keeps the <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> fine aliases out of the
  determinant comparison.

  <paragraph|The predictor contact term.> Let <math|p<around|(|w|)>> be the
  logarithm of the framed predictor, in left coordinates of the joint
  fine-link and frame variables<nbsp><math|w>, and
  <math|<math-up|Pen><around|(|p|)>=<blockpenalty><big|sum><rsub|e><wilsonterm><around*|(|<around|(|exp
  v|)><rsub|e><around|(|exp p<rsub|e>|)><rsup|-1>|)>>. The second-derivative
  chain rule gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-contact>D<rsub|w><rsup|2>*<around|(|<math-up|Pen>\<circ\>p|)>=H<rsup|\<ast\>>*B*H+<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>d<rsup|\<lambda\>>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>>,<space|2em>H=D<rsub|w>*p,B=D<rsub|p><rsup|2><math-up|Pen>,d=D<rsub|p><math-up|Pen>,C<rsub|\<lambda\>>=D<rsup|2><rsub|w>*p<rsub|\<lambda\>>.
  </equation>

  The index <math|\<lambda\>> comprises a coarse link and a colour index. The
  last sum is the <em|predictor contact term>; this use of the word is
  unrelated to the contact extensions of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT>.
  Along the smooth interpolated field, <math|d<around|(|0|)>=0> and
  <math|D*d<around|(|0|)>=-<math-ss|K><rsub|c>>, by the normal equation
  <math|<blockpenalty><around|(|I-\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>|)>=<math-ss|K><rsub|c>>
  (Step<nbsp>6 of the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>).

  <paragraph|The compared operators.> The gauge-completed Hessian, the ghost
  operator and the auxiliary fine operator are

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>=<math-ss|J>+F<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|\<mu\>>,<space|1em>F<rsub|\<mu\>>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X,<space|2em><math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|A><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>=<math-ss|A>+d<rsub|U>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>+<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|P<rsub|f>> projects to fine links,
  <math|<math-ss|A><around|(|U|)>> is the fine leading Hessian and
  <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>> applies <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>> to
  each link component. Frame covariance gives
  <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-R\>=<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>>. Write
  <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>> for
  <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>> with the contact term
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-contact> removed; at the flat field
  <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>=<math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>>,
  since <math|d<around|(|0|)>=0>.

  These operators give the one-loop action a single normalization. The second
  line below follows from<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-hessian-chain> and
  <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>=<around|\||det D*s|\|>>, the third from
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C5>. For every <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0> and <math|v>
  in a neighbourhood of zero,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-one-loop-box>

    <boxed|<\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>>|<cell|=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
      det <math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>-log J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|v|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
      det <around|(|\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J><around|(|v|)>*\<cal-I\>|)>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
      det <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)>-log det
      <math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)>.>>>>
    </aligned>>
  </equation>

  The factor <math|<tfrac|1|2>> comes from the Gaussian integral; the Haar
  Jacobian converts the axial Hessian to left coordinates: <math|det
  <math-ss|Q>=J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><rsup|2>*det
  <around|(|\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>\<cal-I\>|)>>; the ghost
  determinant enters with power one because the gauge block of the completion
  contributes <math|<around|(|det <math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|2>> to
  <math|det <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>>. The left side does not depend
  on<nbsp><math|\<mu\>>. Field-independent factors, such as
  <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>/\<beta\>|)><rsup|n/2>> and the Haar
  normalizations, form the vacuum scalar; they cancel from every normalized
  expectation, so the convergence of correlations does not require them to
  converge. Only the dependence of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|T>> on<nbsp><math|v>
  matters for correlations: Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C> extracts
  the coupling coefficient from its quadratic kernel, and its higher Taylor
  terms enter the activities of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>.

  <paragraph|Insertion bounds.> For weighted bounds use the nearest
  projection<nbsp><math|\<pi\>> from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1> and
  centred coarse frames: <math|c<rsub|y><around|(|U|)>=\<pi\><around|(|<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r><rsup|<math-up|av>>*h<rsub|y,r><around|(|U|)>|)>>,
  <math|<wide|h|~><rsub|<math-up|fr>>=h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>*c<around|(|U|)>>.
  Pull back the joint quadratic forms by this change. Its tangent is
  triangular with identity diagonal, so it preserves determinants.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C6> compares <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>>
  with <math|<math-ss|A><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>>; derivatives are
  at zero in free interpolated coarse directions. Let <math|A<rsub|\<mu\>>>
  denote either of these two operators.

  The uninserted inverse and the geometric insertions are controlled by one
  constant<nbsp><math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>>, chosen before the
  nonlinear path bounds; the derivatives of the full Hessian also contain the
  input path coefficients. For <math|r=1,2> let <math|M<rsub|r>> be the
  supremum of <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>> from
  the primal block space to its dual, in normalized smooth coarse directions,
  on any common smaller strip for these inserted kernels. Define

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|0><around|(|C|)>=C,<space|2em>Q<rsub|1><around|(|C|)>=C<rsup|2>*M<rsub|1>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|2><around|(|C|)>=2*C<rsup|3>*M<rsub|1><rsup|2>+C<rsup|2>*M<rsub|2>.
  </equation*>

  The norms <math|M<rsub|1>,M<rsub|2>> retain the actual cubic and quartic
  path contributions. The proof of the next lemma also shows, on a smaller
  common strip,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-Mr>sup<rsub|\<ell\>> M<rsub|r>\<leqslant\>\<cal-P\><rsub|j,r><around|(|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)>,<space|2em>r=1,2,
  </equation>

  where <math|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>> is the stage path bound of
  item<nbsp>4 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>; the fixed
  polynomials also depend on the interpolation, free metric, frame and
  penalty bounds.

  These estimates control the determinant comparison without a factor equal
  to the number of fine sites.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Scaled inverse insertions><label|lem:B7> There are
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|inv>>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>\<less\>\<infty\>>, fixed before the
    ratio and path bounds, such that for <math|A<rsub|\<mu\>>\<in\><around|{|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>,<math-ss|A><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>|}>>,
    <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>2> and every odd <math|\<ell\>> allowed
    by item<nbsp>5 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)><math|sup<rsub|\<xi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|\<eta\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|inv>>>><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><rsup|3>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>>
      and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r>*P<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<eta\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|inv>>><rsup|<math-up|bl>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>>;

      <item*|(ii)><math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D<rsup|r><around|(|A<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|dual>\<to\><math-up|primal>>\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|r><around|(|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>|)>>
      and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\><rsup|2>*D<rsup|r><around|(|A<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|<math-up|bl>>\<leqslant\>Q<rsub|r><around|(|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>|)>>;

      <item*|(iii)>on the common smaller strip, block-penalty and frame
      insertions have norms at most <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  We first prove real coercivity in block units, then extend it to a strip
  and differentiate the inverse. Constants depend on the free bounds of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, the stage and<nbsp><math|<blockpenalty>>.

  <\proof>
    <em|Step 1: the mass floor.> At zero background, <math|\<Delta\><rsub|0>>
    has symbol<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\>|)>>. With
    <math|x=\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\>|)>\<geqslant\>0>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:B-mass-floor>2<rsup|-10>*<around|(|x+\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>\<leqslant\>x+\<mu\><rsup|2><around*|(|<frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|x+\<mu\><rsup|2>>|)><rsup|9>\<leqslant\>x+\<mu\><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For <math|x\<geqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|2>> the first term supplies the lower
    bound; for <math|x\<less\>\<mu\><rsup|2>> the parenthesis is at
    least<nbsp><math|1/2>. On links, the exterior identity
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|1>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\>|)><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2> give the same lower bound, with a fixed
    positive factor, for the auxiliary fine operator. Changing its quadratic
    form by<nbsp><math|T<rsub|\<ell\>>> therefore gives a positive lower
    bound independent of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>.

    <em|Step 2: coercivity modulo gauge.> In centred coordinates fine gauge
    insertion has coarse component <math|-<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r><rsup|<math-up|av>>*\<phi\>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+r|)>>:
    differentiate <math|h<rsub|y,r><around|(|U<rsup|\<gamma\>>|)>=\<gamma\>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y|)>*h<rsub|y,r><around|(|U|)>*\<gamma\>*<around|(|\<ell\>*y+r|)><rsup|-1>>
    and the centroid. The uncompleted joint form is

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*u,<math-ss|H><rsub|f>d<rsub|1>*u|\<rangle\>>+<blockpenalty><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*u-d<rsub|0>*\<vartheta\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Modulo a joint gauge transformation choose <math|\<vartheta\>=0> and make
    every nonprincipal fine alias transverse. This is possible by dividing
    its longitudinal part by<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>>;
    the remaining principal gauge parameter fixes<nbsp><math|\<vartheta\>>,
    since the principal averaging multiplier is bounded below
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter>. Write <math|u<rsub|z>> for the resulting
    normalized alias coordinates and <math|H<rsub|\<gtr\>>=<big|sum><rsub|z\<ne\>0>\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|2><around|\||u<rsub|z>|\|><rsup|2>>.
    The fine curvature energy bounds <math|c*H<rsub|\<gtr\>>>. The averaging
    multiplier on the principal alias is at least <math|c<rsub|j>*\<mu\>> in
    unitary cell coordinates; the weighted norm of all other alias columns is
    at most<nbsp><math|C<rsub|j>>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|c<rsub|j>*\<mu\><around|\||u<rsub|0>|\|>|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2\<\|\|\>\<cal-M\><rsub|m>*u\<\|\|\><rsup|2>+2*C<rsub|j><rsup|2>*H<rsub|\<gtr\>>,<space|2em>\<omega\><rsub|0>\<leqslant\>C*\<mu\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Combining these inequalities shows that the uncompleted form
    at<nbsp><math|w> is at least <math|c<math-up|dist><rsub|<math-up|bl>><around|(|w,<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>\<cal-R\>|)><rsup|2>>,
    with <math|c> independent of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>.

    <em|Step 3: gauge completion.> Let <math|\<cal-R\>*\<phi\>> be joint
    gauge insertion. Frame covariance gives
    <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-R\>=\<Delta\><rsub|0>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>>
    at zero background. By<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-mass-floor>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-R\>*\<phi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|0>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>*\<phi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For the second bound one must also estimate
    <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X> on arbitrary fine increments. In the
    centred frame, <math|X> is a sum of normalized averages of routes of
    length at most <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\>>. Its row and column sums are at
    most <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\>>, so

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|X*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<mu\><rsup|-2><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>.
    </equation*>

    The coarse-frame part satisfies the same inequality since its embedding
    has <math|\<ell\><rsup|2>> as counting-norm factor and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|2>>.
    Also <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>>.
    For the first bound a fine gauge component contributes
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|z>*<around|\||q<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z>|)>*\<phi\><rsub|z>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|2><around|\||\<phi\><rsub|z>|\|>>,
    and its averaged coarse component is bounded by the summable alias filter
    times<nbsp><math|\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|2><around|\||\<phi\><rsub|z>|\|>>.
    For <math|w=w<rsub|\<perp\>>+\<cal-R\>*\<phi\>>, the completed energy
    bounds <math|c\<\|\|\>w<rsub|\<perp\>>\<\|\|\><rsub|<math-up|bl>><rsup|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>>.
    The last two inequalities give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|w<rsub|\<perp\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>+C<around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*w*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|2>+C|\<\|\|\>>*w<rsub|\<perp\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Squaring proves real coercivity of the completed form with a constant
    independent of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>.

    <em|Step 4: the complex strip.> The uninserted quadratic symbols and the
    geometric frame and penalty kernels have bounded weighted norms on a
    fixed coarse strip. To verify this, each fine derivative is bounded
    by<nbsp><math|C*\<omega\><rsub|z>>, a free interpolated field
    by<nbsp><math|C*\<mu\>>, and normalized frame or predictor averages by
    the summable filter<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter>. At the flat field these
    operators depend only on the free quadratic action, the interpolation,
    the frame and the penalty. Their defining inserted kernels use the smooth
    interpolation; its fine differences of order <math|s\<leqslant\>2> are
    <math|O<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-1-s>|)>> by the alias
    estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-filter>. For complex momenta, freeze the
    weights at a real momentum: if the coarse displacement has norm at most
    one, the geometric-sum formula and <math|<around|\||q<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|)>-q<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\><rprime|'>|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<mu\>*<around|\||\<xi\>-\<xi\><rprime|'>|\|>>
    make the two weights comparable by a fixed factor. Cauchy's
    estimate<nbsp><cite-detail|Lebl2026|Proposition<nbsp>1.2.2> on a smaller
    disc bounds the change of the conjugated symbol by<nbsp><math|C*\<eta\>>.
    Choose<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|inv>>> so this is less
    than half the real lower bound. A Neumann series gives the weighted
    inverse on that strip.

    <em|Step 5: insertions.> For inserted directions <math|v,w>, write
    <math|A<rsub|v>=D*A<rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|v|]>> and
    <math|<math-ss|R>=A<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>>. Direct differentiation gives

    <\equation*>
      D<math-ss|R><around|[|v|]>=-<math-ss|R>A<rsub|v><math-ss|R>,<space|2em>D<rsup|2><math-ss|R><around|[|v,w|]>=<math-ss|R>A<rsub|v><math-ss|R>A<rsub|w><math-ss|R>+<math-ss|R>A<rsub|w><math-ss|R>A<rsub|v><math-ss|R>-<math-ss|R>D<rsup|2>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|[|v,w|]><math-ss|R><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The geometric insertions have norm at most <math|C>: each smooth field is
    <math|O<around|(|\<mu\>|)>>, each derivative on it is
    <math|O<around|(|\<mu\><rsup|2>|)>>, and
    <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|z>>. With two arbitrary fine legs,
    Cauchy\USchwarz accounts for their two factors
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|-1>>; a difference on either leg supplies
    its corresponding <math|\<omega\><rsub|z>>. This bounds the block
    penalty, frame and <math|\<Delta\><rsub|U>> insertions independently of
    the nonlinear path coefficients. For the path contribution, expand each
    transported curvature through total degree four. Its third derivative has
    one curl and its fourth has zero or more differences after two smooth
    insertions. In either case two arbitrary fine legs contribute at most
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|-2>>, while the two smooth insertions in the fourth
    derivative contribute <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>. For the third derivative a
    smooth insertion and the remaining curl give the same bound. Summation by
    parts moves a difference off a smooth leg when necessary; its path-length
    loss is summable against the exponential path bound. Each coefficient is
    a product of at most two path-row coefficients, or one atom coefficient.
    The row and atom sums therefore give a fixed polynomial
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|j,r><around|(|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)>>,
    uniformly in <math|\<ell\>>. Adding the geometric insertions
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-Mr>. The inverse identities therefore give
    dual-to-primal norms <math|C<rsup|2>*M<rsub|1>> and
    <math|2*C<rsup|3>*M<rsub|1><rsup|2>+C<rsup|2>*M<rsub|2>>. The inclusion
    of a primal source in the dual space has norm at most
    <math|C*\<mu\><rsup|-2>>; include its fixed factor in
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>>. Multiplication by
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> gives precisely <math|Q<rsub|1>,Q<rsub|2>>.
    Applying the same identities to <math|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I>,
    with the geometric insertion bounds, gives the stated bounds for
    <math|D<rsup|r>*P<rsub|\<mu\>>>. No derivative of a weight is taken.

    <em|Step 6: the trace bound.> In centred alias representatives,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<frac|\<mu\><rsup|2>|\<lambda\><around|(|\<theta\><rsub|z><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For real<nbsp><math|\<xi\>> this follows from
    <math|<around|\||q<around|(|k|)>|\|>\<geqslant\>2<around|\||k|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>/\<pi\>>
    on the fine momentum cube and <math|<around|\||\<xi\>+2*\<pi\>*z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>\<geqslant\>\<pi\><around|\||z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>>
    for centred aliases and <math|\<xi\>\<in\><around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>>.
    The complex denominator differs from its real value by a small multiple
    of<nbsp><math|\<omega\><rsub|z><rsup|2>>, and the Bloch Fourier matrices
    have bounded norms. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<dimG><big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>><around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-3>\<less\>\<infty\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    In <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|9>=<around|(|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|)><rsup|3>>,
    two insertions leave one undifferentiated cube, whose trace norm bounds
    the regulator products; the remaining factors have bounded operator
    norms.
  </proof>

  The inverse is thus bounded in block units with a constant that sees
  neither the ratio nor the nonlinear path coefficients; these enter only
  through the insertion norms <math|M<rsub|1>,M<rsub|2>>.

  <paragraph|Vertices.> For a translation-invariant Taylor kernel, momentum
  order at least<nbsp><math|s> means that every coefficient of total momentum
  degree less than<nbsp><math|s> vanishes at zero. Let <math|V<rsub|r>> be
  the degree-<math|r> Taylor kernel of the fine leading action minus Wilson,
  for <math|r=2,3,4>. We use:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(a)>smooth free directions <math|\<cal-W\>*v=<around|(|v,W<rsub|0>*v,0|)>>
    in the coarse-link, fine-link and centred coarse-frame variables;

    <item*|(b)>the joint action

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-S\><around|(|v,u,\<vartheta\>|)>=\<cal-A\><rsub|f><around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|u>|)>+<blockpenalty><big|sum><rsub|e><wilsonterm><around*|(|<around|(|exp
      v|)><rsub|e><around*|[|Q<rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|u>|)><rsup|h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>>|]><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>=exp
      \<vartheta\>*<space|0.17em>c<around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|u>|)><rsup|-1>,
    </equation*>

    where the superscript is the coarse gauge action and <math|c> is the
    centring map above, and its third and fourth derivatives
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|3>,\<cal-V\><rsub|4>> at zero;

    <item*|(c)>the vertex bounds <math|C<rsub|3>,C<rsub|4>>: the suprema on a
    smaller strip <math|\<cal-S\><rsub|\<eta\>>> of the absolute scalar
    Fourier entries of <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|3>,\<cal-V\><rsub|4>> on
    momentum-conserving tuples whose legs are all <math|\<cal-W\>*v<rsub|i>>
    with <math|<around|\||v<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, or normalized coarse
    legs and one fine leg of block norm at most one; these include the
    predictor and frame derivatives;

    <item*|(d)><math|S<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|v|)>=S*<around|(|v,\<psi\><around|(|v|)>|)>>,
    and, for two mixed cubic functionals annihilating joint gauge directions,

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|h>=\<cal-V\><rsub|3>*<around|[|\<cal-W\>*v<rsub|h,1>,\<cal-W\>*v<rsub|h,2>,\<cdot\>|]>,<space|2em>h=1,2,
    </equation*>

    with axial restrictions <math|b<rsub|1,<with|font-family|rm|a*x>>,b<rsub|2,<with|font-family|rm|a*x>>>.
  </enumerate>

  These pairings express the stationary fourth derivative in smooth
  coordinates. The constants <math|a<rsub|3>,c<rsub|4>> below depend only on
  preceding stage data; <math|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>> may
  also depend on the input path bound. Put <math|E<rsub|<math-up|ins>>=max
  <around|(|1,C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>|)>> with the constant of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>.

  The extra momentum order of the difference makes the classical matching
  bounds independent of the input amplitude after
  enlarging<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Vertex bounds><label|lem:B5>

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)>The kernels <math|V<rsub|2>,V<rsub|3>,V<rsub|4>> have
      momentum orders at least <math|3,2,1>.

      <item*|(ii)>There is <math|u<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>max
      <around|(|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>|)>>, depending on the
      input path data, such that for <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|j>>

      <\equation*>
        C<rsub|3>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|3>+\<ell\><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|3><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|3>+1,<space|2em>C<rsub|4>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|4>+\<ell\><rsup|-1>*c<rsub|4><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|4>+1<space|0.17em>.
      </equation*>

      <item*|(iii)>In normalized coarse directions,
      <math|<around|\||D<rsup|4>*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|4>+3*C<rsub|3><rsup|2>*E<rsub|<math-up|ins>>>.

      <item*|(iv)>Gauge-annihilating mixed functionals satisfy
      <math|b<rsub|1><math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>b<rsub|2>=b<rsub|1,<with|font-family|rm|a*x>><math-ss|Q><rsup|-1>b<rsub|2,<with|font-family|rm|a*x>>>,
      with <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>> at the flat field.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  We first prove the low-momentum cancellations, using the input arrays of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>, then count powers under
  the interpolation of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>, and finally pair the
  cubic vertices through the completed inverse.

  <\proof>
    <em|Momentum orders.> The quadratic difference is

    <\equation*>
      <wide|V|^><rsub|2><around|(|p|)>=<tfrac|1|2><wide|d|^><rsub|1>*<around|(|-p|)><rsup|T>*<around|(|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsub|f><around|(|p|)>-I|)>*<wide|d|^><rsub|1><around|(|p|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its middle factor vanishes at zero and its two curls each supply one
    momentum, proving order three.

    To include the nonlinear transport terms, expand a connection with
    connection components of weight one and coordinate derivatives of weight
    one. A plaquette curvature starts at weight two, with leading term
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|b>-\<partial\><rsub|b>*A<rsub|a>+<around|[|A<rsub|a>,A<rsub|b>|]>>.
    Changing its transport path changes this term only at higher weight. The
    row part at weight four is therefore its curvature square with
    coefficient<nbsp><math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*\<delta\><rsub|\<gamma\>*\<delta\>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>s<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<gamma\>>*s<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<delta\>>=\<delta\><rsub|\<gamma\>*\<delta\>>>
    by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> at the finer input level;
    <math|s<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<gamma\>>> are the path-row coefficients of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D22>. Cubic and quartic curvature atoms
    start at weights six and eight. The Wilson action has the same
    weight-four term. All contributions of weight at most four consequently
    cancel in their difference. Polynomial connection jets vary
    independently. Expand their finite parallel transports through weight
    four and sum using the exponential path bound. A degree-<math|r> field
    term of weight at least five has at least <math|5-r> coordinate
    derivatives, equivalently that many momentum powers. This proves the
    three claimed orders, including cubic and quartic terms arising inside a
    quadratic curvature row.

    <em|Power counting.> An analytic coefficient <math|f> vanishing at zero
    factors as

    <\equation*>
      f<around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|a>p<rsub|a>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1>\<partial\><rsub|a>*f*<around|(|t*p|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Apply this to the vanishing Taylor coefficients, successively, on a
    smaller strip. Equivalently in position space, write
    <math|<math-up|e><rsup|i*p\<cdot\>x>-1> as a sum of coordinate
    differences; the losses are fixed powers of the true path length. The
    exponential path norm bounds their sums. Wilson's degrees <math|r=2,3,4>
    have orders <math|4-r>; the differences have orders <math|5-r>.

    Use the fine-difference bound for <math|W<rsub|0>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>. For one arbitrary fine leg use
    Cauchy\USchwarz over the block. If it carries <math|s=0> or <math|1>
    differences, its sum of absolute values over the block is at most
    <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|3-s><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>>:
    there are <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> sites,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>\<mu\><rsup|-1><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>>,
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\><rsub|f>*u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|bl>>>.
    Pair this with the other bounded legs. Summation by parts places at most
    one difference on the arbitrary leg. This gives the same bound in the
    dual weighted norm for a mixed vertex.

    There are <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> sites per block. A term with <math|r>
    fields and <math|s> fine differences thus has scale
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|4-r-s>>. Wilson gives scale<nbsp><math|1> at its
    leading orders, and the differences give<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>>.
    The penalty has a fixed bound: normalized route and offset averages have
    mass one, straight sums cancel the factor<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>> in
    a smooth input, and implicit differentiation of the centred frame and
    predictor equations bounds their derivatives through order four. In
    centroid-normalized frame variables the linear equation is identity minus
    the constant projection; every remaining term is a normalized average, so
    these bounds have no factor counting patch sites. These contributions
    define <math|a<rsub|3>,c<rsub|4>>; the path-dependent sums define
    <math|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>>. Choose
    <math|u<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>max <around|(|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>|)>>,
    so that <math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>*max <around|(|a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,c<rsub|4><rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>1>
    for <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|j>>. This gives<nbsp>(ii),
    independently of the input path bound.

    <em|The exchange term.> In the joint coordinates, the exact nonlinear
    tree gauge change and the stationary chain rule give

    <\equation*>
      D<rsup|4>*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>>=\<cal-V\><rsub|4><around|[|\<cal-W\><rsup|4>|]>-<big|sum><rsub|<text|three
      pairings>>\<cal-V\><rsub|3>*<around|[|\<cal-W\>,\<cal-W\>,\<cdot\><around|]|<space|0.17em><math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>\<cal-V\><rsub|3>|[>\<cdot\>,\<cal-W\>,\<cal-W\>|]>.
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>(ii) identifies the axial tangent with the
    tree representative of <math|W<rsub|0>>: their difference is an
    infinitesimal joint gauge direction, including its coarse-frame
    component. A joint gauge transformation of the axial stationary curve
    remains stationary and can be chosen with tangent <math|\<cal-W\>*v>.
    Differentiating this curve gives the displayed formula
    for<nbsp><math|D<rsup|4>*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>>>. Each paired functional
    annihilates the infinitesimal gauge directions by differentiated
    stationarity and gauge invariance. It may therefore be paired through the
    completed inverse of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>. If
    <math|b*\<cal-R\>=0>, the shift <math|x\<mapsto\>x-\<cal-R\>*<around|(|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-R\>|)><rsup|-1>*F<rsub|\<mu\>>*x>
    sets <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*x> to zero and preserves both the uncompleted
    energy and <math|b<around|(|x|)>>. Thus its pairing through the completed
    inverse equals its pairing through the slice inverse, which
    proves<nbsp>(iv). Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7> gives the
    constant<nbsp><math|E<rsub|<math-up|ins>>> independently
    of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>> and the input path bound. Duality then bounds each
    pairing by <math|C<rsub|3><rsup|2>*E<rsub|<math-up|ins>>>. The three
    pairings in a symmetric fourth derivative have identical bounds. This
    proves<nbsp>(iii).
  </proof>

  <paragraph|The stationary Ward identity.> On the smooth interpolated field
  write <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)>> for
  the contact-removed completed Hessian, and let <math|d<around|(|v|)>> and
  <math|C<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>> be the factors
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-contact> there. Thus the full Hessian differs from
  <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)>> by
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>d<rsup|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>>.
  To keep the derivatives of the contact insertion as well as those of the
  inverse, introduce an independent coarse link source <math|u> and put

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|R><around|(|v,u|)>=<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>u<rsup|\<lambda\>>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|F><around|(|v,u|)>=<tr><math-ss|R><around|(|v,u|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|F><rsub|2><around|(|v,u|)>=<tr><math-ss|R><around|(|v,u|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation*>

  The traces sum colour and cell coordinates. Their translation-invariant
  kernels, with internal momentum integrated using
  <math|d*\<theta\>/<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|4>>, are

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|b><rsub|i*\<lambda\>>=\<partial\><rsub|v<rsub|i>>*\<partial\><rsub|u<rsub|\<lambda\>>><math-ss|F><around|(|0,0|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|c><rsub|\<lambda\>*\<rho\>>=\<partial\><rsub|u<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*\<partial\><rsub|u<rsub|\<rho\>>><math-ss|F><rsub|2><around|(|0,0|)>.
  </equation*>

  In particular <math|<math-ss|c><rsub|\<lambda\>*\<rho\>>> includes the two
  product-rule terms. Put <math|<math-ss|a><rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=-<math-ss|K><rsub|c><around|(|\<xi\>|)>*e<rsub|i>>.
  The contact difference <math|Y<rsup|<math-up|con>>> is the Hessian kernel
  of one half of the full minus contact-removed logarithmic determinant,
  evaluated on the stationary branch.

  The coefficient to be preserved is read off by

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*g<around|(|t|)>=<tfrac|1|2>g<rprime|''><around|(|0|)>,<space|2em>\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y|)>=2<math-up|Re><around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*<space|0.17em>Y<rsub|<around|(|2,b|)>,<around|(|2,b|)>>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|1>|)>,
  </equation*>

  for a quadratic Taylor kernel<nbsp><math|Y>, including the factor
  <math|1/2>!, and a colour index<nbsp><math|b>. The Wilson kinetic form
  <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,d<rsub|1>*a|\<rangle\>>>
  has entry <math|<frac|1|2><around|\||q<rsub|1><around|(|t|)>|\|><rsup|2>=<frac|1|2>*t<rsup|2>+O<around|(|t<rsup|4>|)>>,
  hence <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>=1>; thus <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y|)>>
  is the multiple of the curvature square that <math|Y> carries along
  transverse plane waves of small momentum.
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C> uses <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>> to
  define the one-loop coefficient. The cancellation below is exactly the one
  needed in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C6>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stationary Ward estimates><label|lem:B6> The first penalty
    derivative has momentum order two; <math|<math-ss|b>> vanishes at zero
    and <math|<math-ss|c>> is analytic there. For a fixed
    colour<nbsp><math|b> and the transverse link index
    <math|i=<around|(|2,b|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      Y<rsup|<math-up|con>><rsub|i*i>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|1>|)>=O<around|(|t<rsup|3>|)>,<space|2em>\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsup|<math-up|con>>|)>=0.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  The proof derives the trace contraction from the determinant expansion and
  then applies the stationary gauge identity, through the tangent
  identification of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>, to the mixed kernel.

  <\proof>
    The free normal equation gives <math|<blockpenalty><around|(|I-\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>*W<rsub|0>|)>=<math-ss|K><rsub|c>>.
    Hence the first penalty derivative is
    <math|<math-ss|a>=-<math-ss|K><rsub|c>>, of order two because
    <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|c>=d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|H><rsub|c>d<rsub|1>>.

    The determinant difference along the stationary curve is

    <\equation*>
      <tfrac|1|2>log det <around|(|I+<math-ss|R><around|(|v,d<around|(|v|)>|)>|)>=<tfrac|1|2><math-ss|F><around|(|v,d<around|(|v|)>|)>-<tfrac|1|4><math-ss|F><rsub|2><around|(|v,d<around|(|v|)>|)>+O<around|(|<around|\||v|\|><rsup|3>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The first derivatives of each invariant scalar determinant vanish at the
    flat field: each is an adjoint-invariant linear functional on <math|<g>>,
    hence zero because <math|<g>=<around|[|<g>,<g>|]>>. In particular
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|u><math-ss|F><around|(|0,0|)>=0>, so the
    chain-rule term <math|\<partial\><rsub|u><math-ss|F><around|(|0,0|)>*<space|0.17em>D<rsup|2>*d<around|(|0|)><around|[|v,w|]>>
    vanishes and the Hessian along the stationary curve equals that along its
    tangent. Since <math|<math-ss|F>> is linear in <math|u>, its pure
    <math|v> and pure <math|u> Hessians vanish at <math|<around|(|0,0|)>>.
    The mixed derivative retains both contributions

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|v<rsub|i>><tr><around|(|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>=<tr><around*|(|-<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|v<rsub|i>><math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>|)><math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>+<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|-1>\<partial\><rsub|v<rsub|i>>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The two mixed chain-rule terms of <math|<math-ss|F>> and the pure
    <math|u> Hessian of <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|2>> now give

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Y<rsup|<math-up|con>><rsub|i*i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|{|W<rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>+W<rsub|i>*<around|(|-\<xi\>|)>-<tfrac|1|2>V<rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|}>,>>|<row|<cell|W<rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>><math-ss|a><rsub|i><rsup|\<lambda\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><math-ss|b><rsub|i*\<lambda\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>,<space|2em>V<rsub|i><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>,\<rho\>><math-ss|a><rsub|i><rsup|\<lambda\>><around|(|-\<xi\>|)><math-ss|c><rsub|\<lambda\>*\<rho\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)><math-ss|a><rsub|i><rsup|\<rho\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    These identities hold first for finite momentum sums.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7> bounds their traces on a common strip, so
    period limits give the indicated integrals and their derivatives.

    Write <math|y<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>=<tr><around|(|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)><rsup|-1>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|v|)>|)>>.
    The stationary gauge identity is <math|D*y<around|(|0|)>*d<rsub|0>*\<phi\>=0>:
    gauge covariance of the stationary field gives
    <math|y<around|(|<around|(|exp 0|)><rsup|\<gamma\>>|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|\<gamma\>|)>*y<around|(|0|)>=0>,
    and differentiation gives this identity since <math|y<around|(|0|)>=0>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> identifies the stationary tangent with the
    smooth one, so it applies to the kernels just defined. In Fourier
    coordinates it gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a>q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-\<xi\>|)><math-ss|b><rsub|<around|(|a,b<rprime|'>|)>,\<lambda\>><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=0
    </equation*>

    for each colour <math|b<rprime|'>> and source index <math|\<lambda\>>.
    Restrict to <math|\<xi\>=t*e<rsub|a>>. Only
    <math|q<rsub|a>*<around|(|-t*e<rsub|a>|)>> remains, hence the
    corresponding component is zero for <math|t\<ne\>0>. Continuity gives
    <math|<math-ss|b><around|(|0|)>=0> for every component. Analyticity
    implies <math|<math-ss|b>=O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>|)>> and
    <math|<math-ss|c>=O<around|(|1|)>>. On the tested ray
    <math|W<rsub|i>=O<around|(|t<rsup|3>|)>> and
    <math|V<rsub|i>=O<around|(|t<rsup|4>|)>>. The displayed diagonal identity
    therefore has zero second Taylor coefficient, proving the assertion.
  </proof>

  Removing the predictor contact term therefore changes the tested entry of
  the quadratic kernel only at order<nbsp><math|t<rsup|3>>, and leaves the
  curvature-square coefficient unchanged.

  <subsection|The local large-field energy><label|subsec:B-energy>

  The Gaussian integral is performed only on part of each field
  configuration. Its complement must retain a positive energy per selected
  large-field test. This energy is the local sum of completed squares and
  signed error terms, on a consistent selection of tests. The estimate is
  deterministic and keeps every configuration: a large field is not removed
  from the measure but charged the energy of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>,
  which Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> turns into a suppression factor.

  Fix one hypercubic orientation. In Sections<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-energy>
  and<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-sector> write

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|<math-up|f>>=t<rsub|m+1>,<space|2em>t<rsub|<math-up|c>>=t<rsub|m>,<space|2em>B=B<rsub|m>,<space|2em>R<rsub|\<ast\>>=<around|\<lfloor\>|B/100000|\<rfloor\>>,<space|2em>t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1><rsup|-49/100>
  </equation*>

  for the fine and coarse thresholds, the support radius, the path cutoff and
  the threshold of very large tests. Similarly
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>> and
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|c>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>>,
  with the same convention for<nbsp><math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>>. Recall
  <math|<refbeta><rsub|m+1>=<refbeta><rsub|m>+<slope>log \<ell\>>. Increase
  the coupling threshold so that <math|B\<geqslant\>j+4>. In these two
  subsections the estimates hold on every coarse torus of period
  <math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B+1|)>>, with fine
  period <math|\<ell\>*n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>>. These periods also satisfy
  <math|\<ell\>*n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\>+1|)>>,
  so the patch-size requirement used in Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>\U<reference|lem:B4>
  holds. Use the same periodized kernels and local sector conditions. By the
  formula for <math|t<rsub|m>> in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>
  and <math|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>=exp <around|(|<around|(|log log
  <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|)>> in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>,

  <\equation*>
    t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/t<rsub|<math-up|c>>\<to\>1,<space|2em>B<rsup|n>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>\<to\>0*<space|2em><around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>,\<ell\><text|
    fixed>|)>
  </equation*>

  for every fixed<nbsp><math|n>. All limits below are taken in this way.

  <paragraph|Square completion.> The input and output row maps on two-forms
  are <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|f>=<around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>>*I,\<rho\><rsub|f>|)><rsup|T>>
  and <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|c>=<around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|c>>>*I,\<rho\><rsub|c>|)><rsup|T>>.
  They satisfy <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\><rsub|f>=<math-ss|H><rsub|f>>
  and <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\><rsub|c>=<math-ss|H><rsub|c>>.
  The free square-completion maps are

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|M<rsub|f>=\<cal-B\><rsub|f><math-ss|H><rsub|f><rsup|-1>T*\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|n>=<blockpenalty><rsup|-1/2>d<rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>,>>|<row|<cell|N=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I-\<cal-B\><rsub|c><math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-W\><rsub|3><rsup|1/2>*d<rsub|2><math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>>>>>>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  They satisfy <math|M<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|f>+M<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|n>+N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N=I>.
  Indeed <math|P<rsub|c>=\<cal-B\><rsub|c><math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>>
  is the orthogonal projection onto the range
  of<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\><rsub|c>>, so the first block of<nbsp><math|N>
  contributes <math|I-P<rsub|c>>, while multiplying<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-free-defect>
  by <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|c><math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>> on the left and by
  its adjoint on the right gives

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-B\><rsub|c><math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>d<rsub|2><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-W\><rsub|3>*d<rsub|2><math-ss|H><rsub|c><rsup|-1>\<cal-B\><rsub|c><rsup|\<ast\>>=P<rsub|c>-M<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|f>-M<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|n>.
  </equation*>

  Thus a coarse row vector splits isometrically into a part transferred from
  the fine rows (<math|M<rsub|f>>), a part carried by the penalty
  (<math|M<rsub|n>>), and a remainder (<math|N>) seen only off the range
  of<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\><rsub|c>> and by the three-form defect. For an
  infinitesimal pair of fine and coarse fields, let
  <math|X<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|lin>>,X<rsub|c><rsup|<math-up|lin>>> be their
  curls followed by <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|f>,\<cal-B\><rsub|c>>, and let
  <math|Z<rsup|<math-up|lin>>> be <math|<sqrt|<blockpenalty>>> times their
  linear predictor discrepancy. The free interpolation identities give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-flat-rows>M<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|lin>>+M<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z<rsup|<math-up|lin>>=X<rsub|c><rsup|<math-up|lin>>.
  </equation>

  These identities apply to the untruncated flat kernels. We retain them when
  estimating the errors below.

  For the actual rows, transport every kernel coefficient along the fixed
  coordinate route joining its endpoints, using the block-constant lift of
  the coarse configuration<nbsp><math|V>. Retain a coefficient only when that
  route, measured in fine units, has length at most <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
  For a fine-to-coarse coefficient also retain its residue and all the
  endpoints of that route. Denote the resulting maps, respectively truncating
  <math|M<rsub|f>,M<rsub|n>,N>, by <math|\<Phi\>,\<Phi\><rsub|n>,\<cal-N\>>.
  Write <math|L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>> and <math|L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
  for the path rows of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23> truncated by their
  true lengths in fine units. At a plaquette <math|p> put

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|X<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>>*F<rsub|p><around|(|U|)>,L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|U,p|)>|)>,<space|1em>X<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|c>>>*F<rsub|p><around|(|V|)>,L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|V,p|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=<around*|(|min
    <around|(|1,t<rsub|<math-up|c>>/<chord><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>|)><sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|c>>>*F<rsub|p><around|(|V|)>,\<chi\><rsub|p><around|(|V|)>*L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|V,p|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|Z<around|(|e|)>=<sqrt|<blockpenalty>>*<space|0.17em>F*<around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>Q=Q<rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|U|)>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  Here <math|F<around|(|g|)>=P<rsub|<g>>*<around|(|g-g<rsup|-1>|)>/2>, the
  ratio in <math|v<rsub|c>> is one at zero chord, and <math|\<chi\><rsub|p>>
  is the relaxed threshold test <math|\<chi\><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-clipping> at the plaquette base. At each plaquette
  <math|X<rsub|f>>, <math|X<rsub|c>> and<nbsp><math|v<rsub|c>> have two
  components, direct and path; norms and inner products are taken over both.
  The capped coarse coordinates <math|v<rsub|c>> are bounded by
  <math|C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>> everywhere. Put

  <\equation*>
    K<rsup|<math-up|loc>><rsub|f><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|clip><rsub|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>><around|(|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<chi\><rsub|p><around|(|U|)>*<around|\||L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|U,p|)>|\|><rsup|2>,<space|1em>K<rsup|<math-up|loc>><rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=<math-up|clip><rsub|t<rsub|<math-up|c>>><around|(|<chord><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<chi\><rsub|p><around|(|V|)>*<around|\||L<rsub|c,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|V,p|)>|\|><rsup|2>,
  </equation*>

  the clips and tests using the input and output coefficients, respectively.
  We split the local kinetic-energy difference into six terms. The fine and
  mismatch terms <math|E<rsub|f>,E<rsub|n>> supply the energy of selected
  large-field tests. The coarse term <math|E<rsub|c>> compares the retained
  coarse energy with its clipped version, and <math|E<rsub|N>> is
  nonnegative. The terms <math|E<rsub|L>> and<nbsp><math|E<rsub|D>> measure
  the defects <math|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>+\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z-X<rsub|c>>
  and <math|I-\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>-\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rsub|n>-\<cal-N\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-N\>>,
  which vanish in the flat untruncated identities. The six local energy terms
  are

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|E<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=K<rsup|<math-up|loc>><rsub|f><around|(|p|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>\|<rsup|2>-<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,X<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|n><around|(|e|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|<blockpenalty>|2><chord><around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>+<tfrac|1|2>\|\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c><around|(|e|)>\|<rsup|2>-<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c><around|(|e|)>,Z<around|(|e|)>|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,X<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>|\<rangle\>>-<tfrac|1|2>\|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>\|<rsup|2>-K<rsup|<math-up|loc>><rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|N><around|(|q|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><around|\||\<cal-N\>*v<rsub|c><around|(|q|)>|\|><rsup|2>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|L><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f><around|(|p|)>+\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z<around|(|p|)>-X<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>|\<rangle\>>,>>|<row|<cell|E<rsub|D><around|(|p|)>>|<cell|=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>,<around|(|I-\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>-\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rsub|n>-\<cal-N\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-N\>|)>*v<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>|\<rangle\>>.>>>>
  </align*>

  The index <math|q> runs over the two-form complement and three-form
  components of<nbsp><math|N>. Summing over every anchor, the cross terms of
  <math|E<rsub|f>,E<rsub|n>,E<rsub|c>> cancel against<nbsp><math|E<rsub|L>>,
  and the squares <math|<frac|1|2>\|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>*<around|\||<rsup|2>+<frac|1|2>|\|>*\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>*<around|\||<rsup|2>+<frac|1|2>|\|>*\<cal-N\>*v<rsub|c>\|<rsup|2>-<frac|1|2><around|\||v<rsub|c>|\|><rsup|2>>
  cancel against<nbsp><math|E<rsub|D>>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-global-rows><big|sum>E<rsub|f>+<big|sum>E<rsub|n>+<big|sum>E<rsub|c>+<big|sum>E<rsub|N>+<big|sum><around|(|E<rsub|L>+E<rsub|D>|)>=<big|sum>K<rsup|<math-up|loc>><rsub|f>+<tfrac|<blockpenalty>|2><big|sum><rsub|e><chord><around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)><rsup|2>-<big|sum>K<rsup|<math-up|loc>><rsub|c>.
  </equation>

  The adjoints in this identity are those of the truncated transported
  matrices, not the flat matrices. The right side is the truncated clipped
  kinetic energy difference; the higher atoms and omitted tails are separate
  factors in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>.

  <paragraph|Selections.> Fix a mismatch multiplier <math|W\<gtr\>0>, chosen
  after <math|\<ell\>> below, and write <math|n<rsub|e>=<chord><around|(|V<rsub|e>*Q<rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>>.
  The fine field is taken in the tree slice of a rooted block tree.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Selections and allocation sectors><label|def:B-selection> A
    <em|selection> <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|f>,A<rsub|n>,A<rsub|c>|)>>
    consists of fine plaquettes, coarse mismatch edges and coarse sites;
    <math|<around|\||A|\|>=<around|\||A<rsub|f>|\|>+<around|\||A<rsub|n>|\|>+<around|\||A<rsub|c>|\|>>.
    Its location consists of every projected plaquette corner, both endpoints
    of each edge, and its sites; <math|A<rsup|<around|[|r|]>>> is the
    radius-<math|r> coarse neighbourhood of the location. The selection is
    <em|consistent> with <math|<around|(|U,V|)>> if every test meeting
    <math|A<rsup|<around|[|5*B|]>>> obeys

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)><math|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<geqslant\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/4>
      for <math|p\<in\>A<rsub|f>>, and <math|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/2>
      for <math|p\<nin\>A<rsub|f>>;

      <item*|(ii)><math|n<rsub|e>\<geqslant\>W*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>> for
      <math|e\<in\>A<rsub|n>>, and <math|n<rsub|e>\<leqslant\>2*W*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>
      for <math|e\<nin\>A<rsub|n>>;

      <item*|(iii)>in <math|A<rsup|<around|[|5*B|]>>>, <math|A<rsub|c>> is
      the set of coarse sites touched by an output plaquette with
      <math|<chord><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\<gtr\>t<rsub|<math-up|c>>>, and
      every chosen frame tag has positive mass.
    </enumerate>

    An <em|allocation sector> fixes these selected tests, their assignment to
    connected components of their neighbourhoods, a block tree, and the frame
    and predictor tags. Its <em|local energy><nbsp><math|\<cal-E\><rsub|A>>
    sums the six local energy terms over anchors in
    <math|A<rsup|<around|[|2*B|]>>>, fine anchors being projected to coarse
    sites.
  </definition>

  Let <math|D> select the fine or mismatch tests exceeding
  <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>, and let <math|<math-up|Aff>> be the set of
  output anchors within <math|4*B/5> of<nbsp><math|D>, the <em|affected>
  anchors. Put <math|r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>=<chord><around|(|U<rsub|p>|)>>
  for fine plaquettes and <math|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>=<chord><around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>>
  for coarse ones. All sums below are restricted to the local anchors
  defining<nbsp><math|\<cal-E\><rsub|A>>.

  The positive fine and mismatch terms absorb the losses of the signed terms.
  The order of choices in the next lemma is <math|\<ell\>>, then <math|W>,
  then <math|\<kappa\>> and the coupling threshold.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local large-field energy><label|lem:B-D1> Fix the stage path
    bounds, free interpolation and penalty. There is <math|\<ell\><rsub|0>>
    such that for every odd <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|0>> there
    is <math|W<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|\<ell\>|)>> such that for every
    <math|W\<geqslant\>W<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|\<ell\>|)>> there are
    <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0> and a coupling threshold with the following
    property. For <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>> above the threshold and every
    coarse period <math|n<rsub|<math-up|tor>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B+1|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|A><around|(|U,V|)>\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>
    </equation*>

    for every rooted block-tree gauge, allocation sector and consistent
    selection <math|A>. The constant <math|\<kappa\>> and the threshold are
    uniform in the period and the sector.
  </lemma>

  The proof gives <math|\<kappa\>=a/2> with

  <\equation*>
    a=min <around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|f>>/8,<space|0.17em>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>/128,<space|0.17em><blockpenalty>/32,<space|0.17em>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|c>>/64|)>,
  </equation*>

  so <math|\<kappa\>> does not in fact depend on <math|\<ell\>>
  or<nbsp><math|W>; only the coupling threshold does.
  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:B-energy> lists which positive energy pays for
  which loss. We first bound the fine, mismatch and coarse terms, then the
  signed terms near very large tests and in the remaining small-field region,
  and finally absorb the losses by choosing <math|\<ell\>>,
  then<nbsp><math|W>, then the coupling threshold.

  <big-table|<with|par-mode|center|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|8|8|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Event
  charged>|<cell|Energy cost>|<cell|Loss that the cost
  pays>>|<row|<cell|selected fine plaquette,
  <math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/4\<leqslant\>r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>>|<cell|<math|\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>/128>>|<cell|its
  share <math|a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>> of the
  bound>>|<row|<cell|selected fine plaquette,
  <math|r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>\<gtr\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>>|<cell|<math|\<geqslant\><frac|1|2>*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|f>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>>,
  after the dropped path rows>|<cell|signed-term loss
  <math|C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>>
  (<math|\<ell\>> large)>>|<row|<cell|selected mismatch,
  <math|n<rsub|e>\<geqslant\>W*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>>|<cell|<math|\<geqslant\><frac|<blockpenalty>|8>*n<rsub|e><rsup|2>>>|<cell|signed-term
  loss <math|C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*n<rsub|e>> (<math|W>
  large)>>|<row|<cell|test above <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> (in
  <math|D>)>|<cell|<math|\<geqslant\>a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>>|<cell|affected-region
  losses <math|<around|(|4*a*K+Z<rsub|<math-up|aff>>*B<rsup|4>|)>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>>
  per test (coupling large)>>|<row|<cell|coarse
  flag>|<cell|<math|\<geqslant\>4*a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>> per incident
  output plaquette with <math|t<rsub|<math-up|c>>\<less\>r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>>>|<cell|its
  share <math|a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>>; at most four flags per
  plaquette>>|<row|<cell|output plaquette,
  <math|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<gtr\>\<delta\>>>|<cell|none;
  <math|E<rsub|c>\<geqslant\>-c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|c>>*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>*r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>>>|<cell|lies
  in <math|<math-up|Aff>>; cancelled by the positive signed term
  there>>|<row|<cell|output plaquette, <math|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|<math-up|c>>>;
  signed terms off <math|<math-up|Aff>>>|<cell|none>|<cell|losses
  <math|C*t<rsub|<math-up|c>><rsup|4>> per local anchor and
  <math|<frac|a|4>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>> per selected test (coupling
  large)>>>>><label|tab:B-energy> >|Gains and losses in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>. Every selected test is charged energy; no
  configuration is excluded. The losses are absorbed in the order listed:
  <math|\<ell\>>, then<nbsp><math|W>, then the coupling threshold.>

  <\proof>
    The path bounds of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>
    give the row and column sums of the transported maps, and the free
    kernels of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2> give the flat identities; both
    are taken at the input and output levels already fixed in the descending
    construction.

    <em|Step 1: fine, mismatch and coarse terms.> Exponential row bounds,
    together with unitary parallel transport, give row and column sums for
    <math|\<Phi\>> bounded by <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|-2>> and
    <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|2>> respectively. Both sums for
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|n>,\<cal-N\>> are at most <math|C>. Truncation
    restricts every coarse displacement to <math|B/50>. The capped
    coordinates give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>.

    Write <math|R<rsub|f>=<big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|f>:r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>\<gtr\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>>
    and <math|R<rsub|n>=<big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|n>>n<rsub|e>>. For the direct
    component, <math|<around|\||F<around|(|g|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><chord><around|(|g|)>>
    gives

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|f><rsup|<math-up|dir>><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\><tfrac|1|2><around|(|<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>>*r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>-<around|\||\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>*<around|(|p,0|)>|\|>|)><rsup|2>*<space|1em><around|(|r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Choose <math|\<ell\>> so <math|<around|\||\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ell\>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>,
    with <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<leqslant\><sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>>/8>.
    Each selected <math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/4\<leqslant\>r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>
    contributes <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>/128>.
    For <math|r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>\<gtr\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>, the
    linear clip and <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ell\>>*<sqrt|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|f>>/4>
    leave <math|3*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|f>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>/4>.

    The path component is a square when its relaxed threshold test holds.
    When the test fails the term has a cross term of either sign, which must
    be summed. If a path row based at <math|p> is dropped, some plaquette
    <math|q> obeys <math|r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|q>\<gtr\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*exp
    <around|(|<math-up|dist><around|(|p,q|)>/R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>|)>>.
    For a fixed <math|q>, the number of possible <math|p> is at most
    <math|C<rsub|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>*<around|(|r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|q>/t<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>>:
    its radius is <math|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*log
    <around|(|r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|q>/t<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>>, and
    <math|<around|(|1+R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*log
    y|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>*y> for
    <math|y\<geqslant\>1>. Since <math|r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|q>\<leqslant\>2>,
    each witness is within <math|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*log
    <around|(|2/t<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>\<leqslant\>B/50> for
    large<nbsp><math|<refbeta><rsub|m>>. It therefore lies in the
    neighbourhood where consistency applies and belongs to <math|A<rsub|f>>.
    Split a path row into endpoints with chord at most
    <math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>> and those above <math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>.
    The row bound controls the first part by <math|C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>
    times the number of dropped rows; the column bound controls the second by
    <math|C*R<rsub|f>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|<text|dropped >p><around|\||L<rsub|f,R<rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|U,p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|C<rsub|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>+1|)>*R<rsub|f>.
    </equation*>

    Multiplication by <math|<around|\||\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|\<ell\>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>
    loses at most <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|f>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*R<rsub|f>/4>
    after increasing <math|\<ell\>>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum>E<rsub|f>\<geqslant\><tfrac|1|2>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|f>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*R<rsub|f>+<tfrac|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|f>>|128>t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>*<around|\||A<rsub|f>\<setminus\><around|{|r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|p>\<gtr\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>|}>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    For a selected mismatch <math|n<rsub|e>\<geqslant\>W*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>,
    <math|<around|\||\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>.
    Choose <math|W> so this is at most <math|<sqrt|<blockpenalty>>*<space|0.17em>n<rsub|e>/2>.
    The same square inequality gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum>E<rsub|n>\<geqslant\><tfrac|<blockpenalty>|8><big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|n>>n<rsub|e><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|E<rsub|c>> the path component cancels exactly, including its
    relaxed threshold test. Put <math|\<alpha\><rsub|p>=min
    <around|(|1,t<rsub|<math-up|c>>/r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>|)>>. The
    direct component is

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|c>>*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|p>-\<alpha\><rsub|p><rsup|2>/2|)>\|F<around|(|V<rsub|p>|)>\|<rsup|2>-<math-up|clip><rsub|t<rsub|<math-up|c>>><around|(|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The nearest-point Taylor expansion gives
    <math|<around|\||F<around|(|g|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\><chord><around|(|g|)><rsup|2>-C<chord><around|(|g|)><rsup|4>>
    for small chord: the deficit is the squared normal displacement of
    <math|g-1>, whose normal part is <math|O<around|(|<chord><around|(|g|)><rsup|2>|)>>.
    Fix <math|\<delta\>> small enough that also
    <math|<around|\||F<around|(|g|)>|\|><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>3<chord><around|(|g|)><rsup|2>/4>
    below <math|\<delta\>>. Since <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|c>>\<gtr\>16*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|c>>>,
    the three ranges yield

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|c><around|(|p|)>\<geqslant\><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|-C*t<rsub|<math-up|c>><rsup|4>,>|<cell|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|<math-up|c>>,>>|<row|<cell|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,<math-up|c>>*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>*r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>/4,>|<cell|t<rsub|<math-up|c>>\<less\>r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>,>>|<row|<cell|-c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|c>>*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>*r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>,>|<cell|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<gtr\>\<delta\>.>>>>>
    </equation*>

    The last range uses only nonnegativity of the first term. Fix the
    constant<nbsp><math|a> above and set <math|\<epsilon\>=a/4>.

    <em|Step 2: signed terms near very large tests.> We first compare large
    output plaquettes with fine data. Let
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>=<big|sum><rsub|q>m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)><chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>>
    be the fine plaquette sum in the lifted macro-patch centred at
    <math|\<ell\>*y>, with <math|m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)>> its actual
    multiplicity there. The macro radius is <math|<around|(|j+7|)>*\<ell\>>,
    so <math|<big|sum><rsub|y>m<rsub|y><around|(|q|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j>>
    independently of <math|\<ell\>> and the period. On its axial frame,
    denote a straight connector by <math|Y<rsub|z>> and its two root
    adjustments by <math|p<rsub|0>,p<rsub|1>>. The averaging weight satisfies
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>*w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>>\<leqslant\>1>.
    Telescoping each length-<math|\<ell\>> connector and the two end routes,
    then summing their crossings, gives

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|z>w<rsub|z><rsup|<math-up|av>><chord><around|(|p<rsub|0><rsup|-1>*Y<rsub|z>*p<rsub|1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>.
    </equation*>

    For the straight segment, a fine edge occurs in at most <math|\<ell\>>
    translated segments, and the eligible axial link sum is at most
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>>. The two
    end-frame sums obey the same <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>>
    bound: write their coordinate routes, count each link at most
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\>> times after the normalized transverse average,
    and use the nearest-point comparison for the minimizing frame. The axial
    alternatives use their defining link-sum bound instead. The predictor
    branch comparison bounds its adjusted chord by
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|pred>>> times this weighted chord sum, on either
    branch. Around the four sides of an output plaquette the root adjustments
    cancel. Comparing its four links with <math|Q> gives

    <\equation*>
      r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|e\<in\>\<partial\>*p>n<rsub|e>+C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*\<cal-P\><rsub|y><around|(|U|)>.
    </equation*>

    A coarse plaquette thus sees fine curvature only with weight
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>>, and each fine plaquette enters boundedly many
    such comparisons. In an eligible axial frame a fine link differs from its
    replicated coarse link by at most <math|C> times the macro-patch
    plaquette sum, plus the mismatch on a boundary edge. Compare the four
    sides of a coarse plaquette, average the straight paths, and use the
    predictor equation. Each fine plaquette occurs with total coefficient at
    most <math|C*\<ell\><rsup|-2>>; mismatch chords enter linearly. Split
    each fine chord into its part at most <math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>> and its
    selected excess, and each mismatch into its part at most
    <math|2*W*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>> and its selected part. The resulting bound
    has the form

    <\equation*>
      r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>+\<Delta\><rsub|p>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|p>\<Delta\><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C*<around*|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*<big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|f>:r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|q>\<gtr\>t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>r<rsup|<math-up|f>><rsub|q>+<big|sum><rsub|A<rsub|n>>n<rsub|e>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The excess constant depends on the fixed stage, predictor and penalty
    bounds and is fixed before <math|\<ell\>,W>; only the bounded-field term
    may depend on them. This follows by summing the actual route
    multiplicities before enlarging a macro-patch. Applying the same bound to
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>> and
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z>, with the column estimates above,
    bounds their sums on<nbsp><math|<math-up|Aff>> by <math|C> times that
    excess sum plus <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><around|\||<math-up|Aff>|\|>>.
    Since <math|<around|\||v<rsub|c>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>,
    these estimates give

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|<math-up|Aff>><around|(|E<rsub|L>+E<rsub|D>|)>\<geqslant\>-C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>-C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||<math-up|Aff>|\|>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|E<rsub|D>>, expand its four operator terms. The bounds on
    <math|v<rsub|c>,\<Phi\>*v<rsub|c>,\<Phi\><rsub|n>*v<rsub|c>,\<cal-N\>*v<rsub|c>>,
    together with both row and column sums of the three maps, bound each term
    by <math|C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>> per anchor. The constants also
    depend on the predictor and penalty bounds.

    Every output plaquette with <math|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<gtr\>\<delta\>>
    lies in <math|<math-up|Aff>> for large<nbsp><math|<refbeta><rsub|m>>:
    outside <math|<math-up|Aff>> the macro-patch comparison has no fine or
    mismatch test above <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>, so
    <math|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>\<less\>\<delta\>>.
    On <math|<math-up|Aff>> the same comparison and
    <math|t<rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>2*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>> give

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|c>>*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>*<big|sum><rsub|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<gtr\>\<delta\>>r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>\|<math-up|Aff>\|+C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Add this inequality to the previous lower bound. There is a geometric
    constant <math|K>, fixed before the coupling threshold, with
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|Aff>|\|>\<leqslant\>K*B<rsup|4><around|\||D|\|>>
    and at most <math|K*B<rsup|4><around|\||A|\|>> local output anchors.
    Increasing the constants defines <math|Z<rsub|<math-up|aff>>=C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*K>
    and gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|<math-up|Aff>><around|(|E<rsub|L>+E<rsub|D>|)>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,<math-up|c>>*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>*<big|sum><rsub|r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>\<gtr\>\<delta\>>r<rsup|<math-up|c>><rsub|p>-C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>-Z<rsub|<math-up|aff>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>*B<rsup|4><around|\||D|\|>.
    </equation*>

    The positive term cancels the third coarse-plaquette range of
    Step<nbsp>1; the losses are paid by the fine excess
    (through<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>), the mismatches (through<nbsp><math|W>) and
    the tests in<nbsp><math|D>.

    <em|Step 3: signed terms in the small-field region.> For an anchor
    outside <math|<math-up|Aff>>, no fine chord or mismatch above
    <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> occurs in the surrounding macro-patches. The
    preceding plaquette comparison gives coarse plaquette chords at most
    <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> on this region. Choose an
    axial gauge in a radius-<math|O<around|(|B|)>> coarse cube.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1> bounds its link chords by <math|C*B> times
    the sum of its <math|O<around|(|B<rsup|4>|)>> plaquette chords, hence by
    <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*B<rsup|5>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>. Its
    block-constant extension acts on the fine field. Comparing a fine link
    with its replicated coarse link uses the local block-tree route; its fine
    plaquette sum is <math|O<rsub|j,\<ell\>><around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>|)>>
    and its boundary mismatch is at most <math|t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>>. Both
    fields are consequently small in this common gauge, with the convenient
    bound

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>=C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,W>*B<rsup|10>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>
    </equation*>

    through distance <math|2*B/5>. In this region compare each transported
    kernel with its flat uncut kernel. A path of length <math|n> has
    transport error at most <math|C*n*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>>, by
    telescoping its product. Let <math|\<tau\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> be a common
    exponential decay rate of the fixed-stage kernels. Their tails are at
    most <math|C*e<rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|0>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>. Each curvature or
    predictor differs from its linearization by
    <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>><rsup|2>>. With
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>=<around|(|1+R<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>+<math-up|e><rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|0>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>>,
    subtract<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-flat-rows> and
    <math|M<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|f>+M<rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*M<rsub|n>+N<rsup|\<ast\>>*N=I>
    before applying the triangle inequality. This gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*X<rsub|f>+\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*Z-X<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|I-\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\>-\<Phi\><rsub|n><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Phi\><rsub|n>-\<cal-N\><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<cal-N\>|)>*v<rsub|c>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>.
    </equation*>

    The assertion is local: extend the small logarithms by zero off the
    neighbourhood; the cut rows do not change there. Their range fits inside
    <math|2*B/5> for sufficiently large<nbsp><math|<refbeta><rsub|m>>. Thus
    each signed term is bounded below by <math|-C*<around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>+t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|loc>>>.
    Since <math|B<rsup|n>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>><rsup|2>/t<rsub|<math-up|f>>\<to\>0>
    and <math|B<rsup|n>*e<rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|0>*R<rsub|\<ast\>>>/t<rsub|<math-up|f>>\<to\>0>
    for every fixed <math|n>, their total is at least
    <math|-\<epsilon\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>. Away
    from very large tests, the transported identities thus hold up to errors
    that are small compared with<nbsp><math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>> per
    selected test.

    <em|Step 4: absorption of the losses.> The fine and mismatch bounds leave
    <math|a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>> per selected fine or mismatch test,
    <math|a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>> per test in <math|D>,
    and still retain the losses <math|C*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>*R<rsub|f>+R<rsub|n>|)>>
    from the signed terms. First increase <math|\<ell\>> to handle the fine
    loss, then <math|W> to handle the mismatch loss. Coarse flags can be
    assigned to incident output plaquettes with chord above
    <math|t<rsub|<math-up|c>>>, with at most four flags per plaquette.
    Plaquettes above <math|\<delta\>> number at most <math|K<around|\||D|\|>>
    by the macro-patch comparison; all local output anchors number at most
    <math|K*B<rsup|4><around|\||A|\|>>. Using
    <math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>\<leqslant\>2*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>>, the middle
    range of Step<nbsp>1 leaves <math|4*a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>> per
    remaining such plaquette and so covers every other coarse flag.

    The large output plaquette loss cancels against the positive term of
    Step<nbsp>2; <math|E<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>0>. The residual losses are at
    most <math|<around|(|4*a*K+Z<rsub|<math-up|aff>>*B<rsup|4>|)>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>\|D*<around|\||+C*t<rsub|<math-up|c>><rsup|4>*K*B<rsup|4>|\|>*A\|+a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>/4>.
    Increase the coupling threshold, with <math|\<ell\>,W,a> fixed, until

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|4*a*K+Z<rsub|<math-up|aff>>*B<rsup|4>|)>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*t<rsub|<math-up|hi>>,<space|2em>C*t<rsub|<math-up|c>><rsup|4>*K*B<rsup|4>\<leqslant\>a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>/4.
    </equation*>

    The limits stated at the beginning of this subsection justify both
    choices. At least <math|a*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>/2>
    remains. Take <math|\<kappa\>=a/2>. Every multiplicity used a
    fixed-radius local count or a row or column sum; none counts the period.
    This proves the asserted uniformity and the stated order of choices.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Sector Gaussian integration><label|subsec:B-sector>

  We now specify the Gaussian law for simultaneous local integration. It
  differs from the law of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B4>, whose
  covariance<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)><rsup|-1>> defines the
  one-loop action. The sector law is the reference law under which products
  of local factors are estimated, and its covariance is built from the
  linearized retained square terms of an allocation sector.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> uses no identity between the two
  covariances: the difference between the sector integrand and the reference
  law is carried exactly by the determinant and Gaussian-density correction
  factors listed before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>.

  Fix the allocation, its actual block trees, the selected tests, all tags,
  and the frozen fine links<nbsp><math|Y>. Active coordinates are the
  remaining free-edge logarithms in the complement of the selected
  radius-<math|10*B> neighbourhoods. Linearize the retained fine and mismatch
  square terms at the block-constant lift of the coarse field, applying their
  actual cutoffs and stencils. Their stacked affine map is
  <math|a\<mapsto\><math-ss|D>a+b<rsub|Y>>, so their quadratic energy is

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|Y><around|(|a|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\||<math-ss|D>a+b<rsub|Y>|\|><rsup|2>,<space|2em><math-ss|A><rsub|Y>=<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|D>,<space|2em>f<rsub|Y>=<math-ss|D><rsup|\<ast\>>b<rsub|Y>.
  </equation*>

  The stack includes the hypercubic average with its square-root
  normalization. A stencil for a term anchored at<nbsp><math|z> contains
  every fine edge with projected distance at most
  <math|R<rsub|\<ast\>>+5+r<rsub|<math-up|pred>>> from<nbsp><math|z>;
  <math|r<rsub|<math-up|pred>>> is the maximum coarse distance read by a
  patch predictor. Stencils are thus fixed in advance and do not depend on
  the configuration.

  For an edge-coordinate <math|i> write <math|E<rsub|i>> for the two
  endpoints projected to the coarse lattice. The change of sector and the
  differentiated local integral use, respectively, the symmetric windows

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|sec>>>|<cell|=<around|{|i<rprime|'>:max<rsub|u\<in\>E<rsub|i>,v\<in\>E<rsub|i<rprime|'>>><math-up|dist><around|(|u,v|)>\<leqslant\>B/20|}>,>>|<row|<cell|\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>|<cell|=<around|{|i<rprime|'>:min<rsub|u\<in\>E<rsub|i>,v\<in\>E<rsub|i<rprime|'>>><math-up|dist><around|(|u,v|)>\<leqslant\>B|}>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Each window is restricted to the coordinates of its sector; unused
  coordinates may be completed by identity modes. For either prescription put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:B-window>P<rsub|i<rprime|'>*i>=\<b-1\><rsub|i<rprime|'>\<in\>\<Omega\><rsub|i>><around|(|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>\|<rsub|\<Omega\><rsub|i>><rsup|-1>|)><rsub|i<rprime|'>*i>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|P+P<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>,<space|2em>m<rsub|Y>=-P<rsup|\<ast\>>*f<rsub|Y>.
  </equation>

  Each column of <math|P> inverts the precision only inside its window.
  Symmetrization gives the covariance<nbsp><math|\<Gamma\>>, and
  <math|m<rsub|Y>> approximates the full Gaussian mean. The compressed
  columns make the covariance finite range:
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i*i<rprime|'>>=0> unless
  <math|i<rprime|'>\<in\>\<Omega\><rsub|i>> or
  <math|i\<in\>\<Omega\><rsub|i<rprime|'>>>. Factors whose dependence sets
  contain their windows and are disjoint are therefore exactly independent
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>(v)), and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows> shows that the cost relative to
  <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><rsup|-1>> is <math|O<around|(|e<rsup|-c*B>|)>>.
  Both windows contain a radius-<math|B/60> ball for large<nbsp><math|B>,
  since the two projected endpoints are at distance at most one. The
  following argument applies to both prescriptions, with their own retained
  affine terms and mean. The Schur norm of a kernel is the maximum of its
  absolute row and column sums.

  Fix a coarse root <math|o> for the derivative directions. The coarse flag
  tests remain the correct ones at the base field. Derivatives hold the
  selected index sets, <math|Y> and the tags fixed, and are taken in coarse
  directions of size at most <math|t<rsub|<math-up|c>>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,e|)>|)><rsup|q>>.
  Put <math|\<upsilon\><rsub|i>=<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,i|)>|)><rsup|q>>;
  each derivative moves one such factor to the coordinate<nbsp><math|i>.
  Assume <math|0\<less\>\<beta\>\<leqslant\>2<refbeta><rsub|m+1>>, a weaker
  hypothesis than <math|\<beta\>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>> that suffices
  here, and put <math|g=\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>. All bounds below hold for
  sufficiently large<nbsp><math|<refbeta><rsub|m>>.

  For any principal coordinate set <math|\<Omega\>>, write
  <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>>=<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><around|[|\<Omega\>,\<Omega\>|]>>
  and <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<Omega\>>=\<Gamma\>*<around|[|\<Omega\>,\<Omega\>|]>>;
  their inverses are extended by zero outside <math|\<Omega\>>. They control
  the Gaussian marginals and the compressed columns
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-window>. In the next statement, <math|K> ranges over
  <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>>, <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><rsup|-1>>,
  <math|\<Gamma\>>, <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>> and
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>>, and <math|<math-ss|E>> over
  <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>\<Gamma\>-I> and
  <math|\<Gamma\>-<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><rsup|-1>>. At derivative order zero,
  exponential entry bounds give exponential Schur bounds by summing over
  coordinate locations.

  The following estimate justifies differentiating the common Gaussian law
  and replacing its window covariance by the full inverse.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Window covariance bounds><label|lem:B-windows> For each fixed
    derivative order <math|k> and direction power <math|q>, there are
    <math|c\<gtr\>0> and <math|C<rsub|k>\<less\>\<infty\>>, depending only on
    the fixed stage and ratio data and <math|k,q>, such that, uniformly in
    the period, sector and <math|\<Omega\>>, for
    <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>k>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>\<geqslant\>c*I,<space|2em>\<Gamma\>\<geqslant\>c*I,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||D<rsup|r>*K<rsub|i*i<rprime|'>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k><math-up|e><rsup|-c<math-up|dist><around|(|i,i<rprime|'>|)>><space|0.17em>\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>,<space|2em><around|\||D<rsup|r><math-ss|E><rsub|i*i<rprime|'>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k><math-up|e><rsup|-c*B-c<math-up|dist><around|(|i,i<rprime|'>|)>><space|0.17em>\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||D<rsup|r>*m<rsub|Y><around|(|i|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>,<space|2em><around|\||D<rsup|r>*<around|[|g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>m<rsub|Y>+f<rsub|Y>|)>|]><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k><math-up|e><rsup|-c*B><space|0.17em>\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>.>>>>
    </gather*>
  </lemma>

  Coercivity comes from the axial estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>,
  decay from a Combes\UThomas argument, and the window errors from the fact
  that a compressed column solves <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>x=e<rsub|i>>
  exactly inside its window.

  <\proof>
    <em|Coercivity.> The linearized square terms and an eligible predictor
    control a fine link by those term values in a fixed macro-patch plus
    <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|x>> times the surrounding fine increments, where
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|x>=C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>> tends to
    zero. Summing squares, using the bounded number of macro-patches meeting
    an edge, gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|D>a*<around|\<\|\|\>|<rsub|2><rsup|2>+C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*\<delta\><rsub|x><rsup|2>|\<\|\|\>>*a|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>.
    </equation*>

    For large<nbsp><math|<refbeta><rsub|m>> the last coefficient is at most
    <math|1/2>, proving <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>\<geqslant\>c*I>. All-frozen
    sectors have zero-dimensional active space and the assertion is vacuous
    there. Adding identity coordinates for inactive links preserves this
    lower bound.

    <em|Decay.> Exponential path coefficients bound the weighted row and
    column sums of <math|<math-ss|D>>. Products give the same Schur bound for
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>>. By the Combes\UThomas argument, for
    sufficiently small fixed <math|\<theta\>\<gtr\>0> multiply the
    coordinates by <math|<math-up|e><rsup|\<theta\><math-up|dist><around|(|i,\<cdot\>|)>>>
    on the left and by its inverse on the right. The resulting matrix differs
    from <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>> by at most <math|C*\<theta\>> in operator
    norm: <math|<around|\||<math-up|e><rsup|\<theta\>*d>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>*d<math-up|e><rsup|\<theta\>*d>>
    is summable against the exponential coefficient bound. Choose
    <math|C*\<theta\>\<less\>c/2>. The inverse Neumann series now gives
    exponential row and column bounds for <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><rsup|-1>>
    and every principal compression, with constants independent of the number
    of coordinates.

    <em|Windows.> A compressed inverse column solves
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>P*e<rsub|i>=e<rsub|i>> inside its window.
    Outside that window, its residual is a sum of products whose combined
    length crosses the boundary. Every window contains a radius-<math|B/60>
    ball and there are only <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>> coordinates per coarse
    site. The exponential Schur bounds therefore give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P-<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|Schur>>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-c*B>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\>-<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|Schur>>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-c*B>.
    </equation*>

    The entrywise column and inverse bounds retain exponential decay in
    <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|i,i<rprime|'>|)>>, while the preceding
    difference has the factor <math|<math-up|e><rsup|-c*B>>. Taking the
    geometric mean of these bounds and decreasing <math|c> gives both factors
    together. For sufficiently large <math|B>, the uniform upper bound on
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>> and <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><rsup|-1>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|-1>*I>
    give <math|\<Gamma\>\<geqslant\>c<rprime|'>*I> for a fixed
    <math|c<rprime|'>\<gtr\>0>. Its row and column sums are bounded by a
    fixed constant. The same estimate gives
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>\<Gamma\>-I=O<around|(|<math-up|e><rsup|-c*B>|)>>.
    Apply the exponential conjugation argument to <math|\<Gamma\>> and its
    principal compressions; their common lower bound gives the stated bounds
    for <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>>.

    <em|Derivatives.> Differentiate <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>>
    by <math|D<around|(|<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>|)>=-<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1><around|(|D<math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>>|)><math-ss|A><rsub|\<Omega\>><rsup|-1>>
    and iterate through order <math|k>. The index sets <math|\<Omega\>> stay
    fixed. Each derivative of a path coefficient is bounded by a fixed power
    of the path length and by the direction weight;
    <math|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,i<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|o,i|)>|)>*<around|(|1+<math-up|dist><around|(|i,i<rprime|'>|)>|)>>
    moves it to<nbsp><math|\<upsilon\><rsub|i>>, so <math|r> derivatives give
    <math|\<upsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|r>>. A smaller exponential rate sums all
    remaining powers. In every differentiated residual, at least one chain
    still crosses the window boundary. This proves the displayed derivative
    bounds for <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>> and
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>\<Gamma\>-I>. For the inverse difference, use

    <\equation*>
      \<Gamma\>-<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><rsup|-1>=<math-ss|A><rsub|Y><rsup|-1><around|(|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>\<Gamma\>-I|)>.
    </equation*>

    Every differentiated product retains the exponentially small residual
    factor. The same inverse differentiation formula, applied to
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>>, <math|\<Gamma\>> and their principal
    compressions, proves the remaining bounds for <math|K>. The spatial
    weights are moved to the first index as above; reducing the decay rate
    sums the intervening polynomial factors.

    <em|Mean.> The affine source is formed from the same retained terms and
    from a bounded smooth extension of the frozen logarithms. Its derivatives
    are <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>> with the same spatial
    weights. Multiplication by <math|P<rsup|\<ast\>>> proves the mean bound.
    Subtracting the exact inverse solution gives
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>m<rsub|Y>+f<rsub|Y>=<around|(|I-<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>P<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*f<rsub|Y>>,
    whose derivatives are <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>*e<rsup|-c*B>|)>>.
    Finally <math|g<rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>C<refbeta><rsub|m+1><rsup|1/2>> and
    <math|B=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>; this fixed power is absorbed by
    decreasing <math|c>. The estimates refer to the actual
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>,\<Gamma\>,m<rsub|Y>>
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-window> throughout.
  </proof>

  The windowed law thus costs only <math|e<rsup|-c*B>> relative to the full
  Gaussian, and this bound persists through every fixed number of
  derivatives.

  <paragraph|The differentiated local integral.> We state the local estimate
  for the finite products used in the expansion. In a fixed allocation
  sector, join selected large-field tests when their fixed enlarged
  neighbourhoods intersect; the connected components <math|A<rsub|\<eta\>>>
  are the <em|large-field components>. Write <math|A<around|(|M|)>> for the
  union of the fine-plaquette, mismatch and coarse-site selections belonging
  to the components chosen in a product<nbsp><math|M>. Fix the support
  enlargement <math|h<rsub|<math-up|loc>>> once for the local factors.
  Compatibility means that the radius-<math|h<rsub|<math-up|loc>>*B>
  neighbourhoods of the distinct chosen components are disjoint.

  A factor has a complete read support <math|S<rsub|i>> containing the
  projected endpoints of every link it uses, every path and relaxed threshold
  test in its formula, every tag and frozen link, and the entire window
  <math|\<Omega\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> for each Gaussian coordinate
  <math|a> it reads. Thus a change outside <math|S<rsub|i>> leaves its
  formula and its Gaussian marginal unchanged. A finite product <math|M> is a
  <em|polymer> when the intersection graph of its supports is connected.
  Choose <math|o> in their union. Its size <math|s<rsub|M>> is the sum of the
  numbers of radius-<math|B> blocks covering these supports, including
  retained path lengths in the same units.

  For an incoming forced activity retain its support <math|Y<rsub|i>> and
  inventory <math|I<rsub|i>> from Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>.
  The admitted products satisfy

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Y<rsub|i>\<cap\>Y<rsub|i<rprime|'>>=\<varnothing\>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<ne\>i<rprime|'>|)>,<space|2em>I<rsub|i>\<subset\><big|cup><rsub|p\<in\>A<rsub|f><around|(|M|)>><math-up|corners><around|(|p|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>\<cap\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>M>S<rsub|i>=\<varnothing\>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  They also satisfy the component compatibility just specified. These
  conditions retain exactly the incidence and separation used in local
  integration.

  Some incoming forced activities are only measurable, so the differentiated
  integral cannot be estimated by differentiating every factor in its link
  arguments. We instead change Gaussian coordinates as the coarse field
  varies, so that each physical fine link read by such an activity stays
  fixed. Derivatives then act on the smooth factors, the Jacobian and the
  Gaussian density. The nested chart cutoffs below ensure that this change is
  valid wherever a differentiated factor is nonzero.

  <paragraph|Factors and their bounds.> The retained square corrections are
  the exponential differences between the exact fine or mismatch terms and
  their affine squares <math|<around|\||<math-ss|D>a+b<rsub|Y>|\|><rsup|2>/2>.
  The other smooth factors are incoming regular activities, chart densities,
  and determinant and Gaussian-density corrections. Incoming forced
  activities are bounded by their measurable supremum envelopes.

  For the chart cutoffs choose a smooth <math|\<chi\>:<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>\<to\><around|[|0,1|]>>
  equal to one on <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> and zero on
  <math|<around|[|2,\<infty\>|)>>. On an active logarithmic coordinate put
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|a<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|(|u<rsub|e>|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|<around|\||u<rsub|e>|\|>/<around|(|a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>|)>>.
  The fixed mean bound is <math|<around|\||m<rsub|Y><around|(|e|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>,
  and choose <math|a<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>c<rsub|\<ast\>>+1>. Fix a logarithm
  radius <math|r<rsub|log>\<gtr\>0> and take <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>> large
  enough that <math|4*a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>\<less\>r<rsub|log>/2>.
  The smaller chart is <math|<around|\||u<rsub|e>|\|>\<less\>a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>;
  the broader cutoff equals one for <math|<around|\||u<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>.
  A protected link is an active link accompanied by the broader cutoff
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|2*a<rsub|\<ast\>>>> whenever an incoming measurable
  factor reads it. Nonactive free links are the frozen links <math|Y>; their
  physical values are held fixed. The nesting
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|a<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<ne\>0\<Rightarrow\>\<chi\><rsub|2*a<rsub|\<ast\>>>=1>
  is what allows the physical-link change below on all derivative supports.

  Cutoff remainders are the departures from one of the fine-plaquette and
  mismatch cutoffs, the frame and predictor tag likelihoods, the inventory
  tests, or the active chart cutoffs. The addresses are fine plaquettes for
  fine cutoffs, coarse edges for mismatches and predictor tags, coarse sites
  for frame tags, fine sites for inventory tests, and fine edges for chart
  cutoffs. A site's location is its coarse projection; an edge or plaquette
  is located at its projected initial vertex. Near a large-field component
  the corresponding departure is included in its component factor. Away from
  those components, each remaining cutoff remainder<nbsp><math|w> and all its
  derivatives are supported on

  <\equation*>
    E<rsub|w>=<around|{|z:\<exists\>i,<math-up|dist><around|(|i,<math-up|loc><around|(|w|)>|)>\<leqslant\>2*B,<space|1em><around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<xi\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/<around|(|2*g|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  Indeed, if all these coordinates are below that threshold, then
  <math|<around|\||m<rsub|Y><around|(|e|)>+g*z<rsub|e>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|c<rsub|\<ast\>>+\<xi\>/2|)>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>\<less\>a<rsub|\<ast\>>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>
  after reducing <math|\<xi\>>, so both chart cutoffs equal one. The local
  predictor bound then makes the fine chord less than
  <math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/4> and the mismatch less than
  <math|W*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>>, and both tag likelihoods and the inventory
  test equal one. The strict margin persists on derivative supports. Choose
  the fixed <math|\<xi\>\<gtr\>0> small enough for these finitely many local
  implications. Let <math|\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>> be the set of distinct
  selected cutoff-remainder addresses and
  <math|E<rsub|\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>=<big|cap><rsub|w\<in\>\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>E<rsub|w>>
  their simultaneous event. Their amplitudes are bounded by the fixed
  stage-and-ratio constant

  <\equation*>
    M<rsub|w>=2+<around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>\<times\>S<rsub|4>|\|>+<around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|R>|\|>.
  </equation*>

  This includes the likelihood relative to uniform tags. Fix any
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|w>\<gtr\>0> before the coupling threshold, and put
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1><rsup|-\<tau\><rsub|w>>>.
  For a factor<nbsp><math|i> that is not a cutoff remainder, <math|a<rsub|i>>
  is the product of its absolute coefficient bound (for a square correction,
  its deterministic envelope), its selected-test or path suppression, and the
  Gaussian exponential factor <math|exp <around|(|C*\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|)>> of
  Step<nbsp>4 of the proof below. The weights are

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|i>=a<rsub|i>*<space|1em><around|(|i\<nin\>\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>|)>,<space|2em>f<rsub|w>=M<rsub|w>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|w>*<space|1em><around|(|w\<in\>\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Each distinct address contributes once.

  <paragraph|The reference law and the transported integrand.> Assume
  <math|\<beta\>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>. Use the local prescription
  <math|\<Omega\><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> in<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-window> throughout
  this local integral. Conditional on the fixed sector and tags the reference
  law is

  <\equation*>
    d*\<nu\>=d*H<rsub|F><around|(|Y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|z|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|H<rsub|F>> is product Haar measure on the frozen tree-slice
  links and <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<Gamma\>>> is the centred Gaussian.
  Reference tags are independent uniform tags; their actual likelihoods are
  included among the factors. All estimates below are uniform in those tags.
  A finite set of Gaussian reads <math|I> has marginal covariance
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|I>=\<Gamma\>*<around|[|I,I|]>>. For a finite selection
  write its product as

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|M>*<around|(|V,Y,z|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>M\<setminus\>\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>F<rsub|i>*<around|(|V,Y,z|)>*<big|prod><rsub|w\<in\>\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>W<rsub|w>*<around|(|V,Y,z|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|F<rsub|i>> are the factors other than cutoff remainders and
  <math|W<rsub|w>> the cutoff remainders. The transported product uses one
  change of Gaussian coordinates on their common set of reads.

  To differentiate while holding physical fine links fixed, fix a base coarse
  field <math|V<rsub|0>> and write <math|u=m<rsub|Y><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>+g*z>.
  For a nearby <math|V>, set

  <\equation*>
    u<rsub|V><around|(|e|)>=log <around*|(|exp
    <around|(|u<around|(|e|)>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V<rsub|0>|)><rsub|e><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e><rsup|-1>|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|V>=g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|u<rsub|V>-m<rsub|Y><around|(|V|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Use a fixed smooth extension of this formula outside
  <math|<around|\||u<around|(|e|)>|\|>\<less\>r<rsub|log>/2>, equal to the
  identity plus a bounded correction there. Its derivative is invertible for
  <math|V> sufficiently near <math|V<rsub|0>>, uniformly over the chart
  argument; the extension is used only with the stated protected cutoff. On
  active protected links,

  <\equation*>
    exp <around|(|u<rsub|V><around|(|e|)>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V|)><rsub|e>=exp
    <around|(|u<around|(|e|)>|)><around|(|<math-up|rep><space|0.17em>V<rsub|0>|)><rsub|e>.
  </equation*>

  Every incoming measurable activity evaluated on those links is therefore
  constant under this differentiation. It is bounded by its supremum majorant
  and no field derivative is applied to it.

  The density of the changed marginal, relative to the base one on the union
  <math|I> of all reads, is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|I>*<around|(|V,z|)>=<around|\||det
    D<rsub|z>*z<rsub|V,I>|\|><space|0.17em><frac|<around|(|det
    \<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>*exp
    <around|[|-<tfrac|1|2>z<rsub|V,I><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V|)><rsup|-1>*z<rsub|V,I>|]>|<around|(|det
    \<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)>|)><rsup|-1/2>*exp
    <around|[|-<tfrac|1|2>z<rsub|I><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><around|(|V<rsub|0>|)><rsup|-1>*z<rsub|I>|]>>.
  </equation*>

  Define the transported integrand by

  <\equation*>
    <wide|F|~><rsub|M>*<around|(|V,Y,z|)>=\<cal-R\><rsub|I>*<around|(|V,z|)>*F<rsub|M>*<around|(|V,Y,z<rsub|V>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The next lemma bounds its ordered derivatives at <math|V<rsub|0>> under the
  same base law, including products containing incoming measurable forced
  activities. It is the second local input to
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D>. Its bounds may depend on the fixed
  mismatch multiplier<nbsp><math|W> and energy constant<nbsp><math|\<kappa\>>
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>.

  For an admitted polymer put <math|A=A<around|(|M|)>>. For a large-field
  component with selection <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|f>,A<rsub|n>,A<rsub|c>|)>>
  and <math|A<rsub|c>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>, let <math|F<rsub|A>> contain its
  full local sector factor and every read meeting it. Adjoin every
  large-field component met by those reads. In this value estimate only,
  replace the healthy-support condition within this component by the
  consistency condition of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:B-selection>.
  Retain the incoming-support disjointness and inventory incidence. The other
  factors attached to <math|F<rsub|A>> have the bounds <math|f<rsub|i>>
  above; their product excludes the component's selected-test suppression and
  cutoff remainders already included in that component. Fix
  <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>\<less\>\<kappa\>> before the
  coupling threshold. Let <math|s<rsub|A>> count the radius-<math|B> blocks
  of its complete read support, including retained path lengths in those
  units. The component itself is estimated in value; derivatives fall only on
  attached smooth factors and the common Gaussian density. Fix the orders
  <math|k,q> and take odd <math|\<ell\>> above the stage-data threshold. A
  function has <em|upper rate><nbsp><math|r> if it is at most
  <math|C<rsub|\<epsilon\>><refbeta><rsub|m+1><rsup|r+\<epsilon\>>> for every
  <math|\<epsilon\>\<gtr\>0> once <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>> is large. Write
  <math|P=P<rsub|j,\<ell\>,k,q>>, of upper rate zero, and
  <math|d=d<rsub|j,\<ell\>,k,q>>; the large-field component bound may also
  depend on <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>>. Directions and sector
  parameters have the restrictions of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Simultaneous local integration><label|lem:B-D2>

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)>Admitted polymers <math|M> have integrable majorants
      <math|G<rsub|M>> for their ordered derivatives through order <math|k>,
      with

      <\equation*>
        <big|int>G<rsub|M>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>P<around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>M>f<rsub|i>.
      </equation*>

      <item*|(ii)>If <math|A<rsub|c>=\<varnothing\>>, the selected-test
      envelopes satisfy, through order <math|k>,

      <\equation*>
        <big|prod><rsub|i<math-up|selected
        test>>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>.
      </equation*>

      <item*|(iii)>A large-field component with
      <math|A<rsub|c>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>> has a value majorant
      <math|G<rsub|A>> with

      <\equation*>
        <big|int>G<rsub|A>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>P<around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|A>|)><rsup|d>*e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>*<big|prod><rsub|i<math-up|attached>>f<rsub|i>.
      </equation*>

      <item*|(iv)>Simultaneous cutoff remainders at <math|r> distinct
      addresses have probability at most <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w><rsup|2*r>>.

      <item*|(v)>Disjoint complete read supports factorize, including Haar
      and tag coordinates.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  The window bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows> control the
  Gaussian scores and exponentials, the energy of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1> supplies the suppression, and finite range
  gives the factorization.

  <\proof>
    <em|Step 1: the affine Gaussian substitution.> For any chart-supported
    <math|F<rsub|Y>>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|int><math-up|e><rsup|-\<beta\>*Q<rsub|Y><around|(|a|)>><space|0.17em>F<rsub|Y><around|(|a|)>*<space|0.17em>d*a=>|<cell|<around|(|2*\<pi\>*g<rsup|2>|)><rsup|n/2><around|(|det
      \<Gamma\>|)><rsup|1/2><math-up|e><rsup|-\<beta\>*Q<rsub|Y><around|(|m<rsub|Y>|)>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<times\><big|int>F<rsub|Y>*<around|(|m<rsub|Y>+g*z|)>*exp
      <around*|{|<around|\<langle\>|\<eta\><rsub|Y>,z|\<rangle\>>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|z,<around|(|\<Gamma\><rsup|-1>-<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>|)>*z|\<rangle\>>|}>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|\<Gamma\>><around|(|z|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<eta\><rsub|Y>=>|<cell|-g<rsup|-1>*<around|(|<math-ss|A><rsub|Y>m<rsub|Y>+f<rsub|Y>|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Here <math|n> counts active real coordinates; the Haar density and chart
    cutoffs are included in <math|F<rsub|Y>>. Expansion of
    <math|Q<rsub|Y>*<around|(|m<rsub|Y>+g*z|)>> proves the identity,
    including its determinant and residual linear term. The identity holds
    for either window prescription. The local expansion in
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D> uses
    <math|\<Omega\><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>.

    <em|Step 2: scores.> For the common change of coordinates defined above,
    the first logarithmic derivative of the Gaussian density is

    <\equation*>
      -<tfrac|1|2><tr><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<dot\>><rsub|I>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>z<rsub|V><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<wide|\<Gamma\>|\<dot\>><rsub|I>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*z<rsub|V>-z<rsub|V><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\><rsub|I><rsup|-1>*<wide|z|\<dot\>><rsub|V>.
    </equation*>

    Add the logarithmic Jacobian derivative. Repeated differentiation bounds
    every score through order <math|k> by a polynomial in the absolute values
    of the marginal Gaussian coordinates, with coefficients controlled by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows> and the fixed smooth chart
    extension. The number of placements is a fixed polynomial in
    <math|1+s<rsub|M>>, since <math|k> is fixed and
    <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*B<rsup|4>*s<rsub|M>>.
    Connectivity and the chosen root bound all distances from <math|o> by
    <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*B*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)>>, so the spatial
    direction weights give another fixed polynomial.

    The normalized coarse directions have size
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|c>>|)>> with their stated spatial
    weights. On a fixed chart, derivatives of its multiplication, logarithm
    and bounded extension are bounded. The mean derivatives are
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>>, while
    <math|t<rsub|<math-up|c>>/t<rsub|<math-up|f>>> stays bounded. In
    <math|z<rsub|V>> the additional normalization is <math|g<rsup|-1>>. Thus
    the score coefficients are bounded by a fixed power of
    <math|g<rsup|-1>*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>>, <math|B> and <math|1+s<rsub|M>>.
    Since

    <\equation*>
      g<rsup|-1>*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>\<leqslant\>C*exp <around|{|<around|(|log
      log <refbeta><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>|}>,
    </equation*>

    all fixed powers have upper rate zero. The small inverse and stationarity
    residuals in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows> have the same property
    after this normalization.

    <em|Step 3: square corrections.> The chart-supported square corrections
    have deterministic envelopes: on their support all logarithms are
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>|)>>, the affine remainder is
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>|)>>, and
    <math|<around|\||<tfrac|1|2>|\|>*r+e*<around|\||<rsup|2>-<tfrac|1|2>|\|>*r<around|\||<rsup|2>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||r|\|>\|e\|+<around|\||e|\|><rsup|2>/2>
    gives an energy correction <math|O<around|(|\<beta\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|3>|)>>.
    The exponentially summable path coefficients bound the same quantities
    with fixed path-length powers after differentiation. These bounds enter
    <math|a<rsub|i>>.

    <em|Step 4: unbounded exponentials.> The unbounded exponentials come from
    the residual Gaussian density and its local covariance comparisons. Write
    their upper exponents as <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>\<cdot\>z+z<rsup|T>*K<rsub|i>*z/2>,
    with <math|K<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>0>. At the base field the Gaussian
    likelihood is one; its derivatives are the polynomial scores already
    estimated. Its exponent tends to zero with <math|V-V<rsub|0>>, so on a
    sufficiently small neighbourhood its weighted row sums are at most
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|B>=C<rsub|j,k>*B<rsup|d><math-up|e><rsup|-c*B>>. The
    residual Gaussian density has this bound by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>. The resolvent identity for each
    compressed inverse gives <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|B>> for its local
    quadratic corrections. Each factor's coefficients decay exponentially
    from its own address, and each address has bounded multiplicity. Thus the
    sum at a coordinate is bounded by <math|C*\<delta\><rsub|B>*<big|sum><rsub|y><math-up|e><rsup|-c<math-up|dist><around|(|y,a|)>>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*\<delta\><rsub|B>>,
    uniformly in the period and number of factors. Here <math|y> ranges over
    address locations and <math|a> is the coordinate location. In the linear
    terms, weighted Cauchy\USchwarz at each coordinate gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<eta\><rsub|i>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>,<space|2em><around*|\||\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*<big|sum><rsub|i>K<rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<delta\><rsub|B>.
    </equation*>

    To specify the linear bound, let <math|w<rsub|i*a>> be the sum of the
    absolute path weights contributing to coordinate <math|a> of
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|i>>; bounded endpoint multiplicity gives
    <math|sup<rsub|a> <big|sum><rsub|i>w<rsub|i*a>\<leqslant\>C>. Put

    <\equation*>
      \<Lambda\><rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|a><frac|<around|\||\<eta\><rsub|i><around|(|a|)>|\|><rsup|2>|w<rsub|i*a>>+<tr><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*K<rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|)>,
    </equation*>

    with zero summand if both numerator and denominator vanish. This quantity
    assigns the common linear exponential bound to the individual factors. To
    see the first bound, use <math|<around|\||<big|sum><rsub|r>x<rsub|r>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|(|<big|sum><rsub|r>w<rsub|r>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|r><around|\||x<rsub|r>|\|><rsup|2>/w<rsub|r>>
    with the absolute path weights <math|w<rsub|r>>; their sums are the
    preceding bounded row sums. The Schur bound for <math|\<Gamma\>>
    completes the estimate. In particular, this bounds
    <math|<around|\||\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*\<eta\><rsub|0>|\|><rsup|2>> with
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|0>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<eta\><rsub|i>>, including all
    terms <math|\<eta\><rsub|i><rsup|T>*\<Gamma\>*\<eta\><rsub|i<rprime|'>>>
    with <math|i\<ne\>i<rprime|'>>. This is the factor <math|exp
    <around|(|C*\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|)>> in <math|a<rsub|i>>. Put
    <math|K=<big|sum><rsub|i>K<rsub|i>>. Since
    <math|0\<leqslant\>K<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K>, each
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*K<rsub|i>*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|\<\|\|\>>>
    is at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>*K*\<Gamma\><rsup|1/2>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1/8>
    for large <math|B>, also after the fixed enlargement needed for two
    moments. Diagonalization and square completion, with <math|-log
    <around|(|1-u|)>\<leqslant\>2*u> for <math|0\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>1/2>,
    give

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><rsub|\<Gamma\>><math-up|e><rsup|2*\<eta\><rsub|0>\<cdot\>z+z<rsup|T>*K*z>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|(|C*<big|sum><rsub|i>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Under this tilt the covariance is at most <math|2*\<Gamma\>> and its mean
    is <math|<around|(|I-2*\<Gamma\>*K|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|2*\<Gamma\>*\<eta\><rsub|0>|)>>.
    Polynomial moments are bounded by the preceding linear estimate;
    increasing the fixed constant in <math|exp
    <around|(|C*\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|)>> includes their mean contributions.
    Thus the differentiated factors and likelihood have, after removing the
    cutoff-remainder amplitudes, one <math|L<rsup|2>> majorant bounded by
    <math|P<rsub|j,\<ell\>,k,q><around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d>*<big|prod>a<rsub|i>>.

    <em|Step 5: cutoff remainders.> We next bound the simultaneous event
    defined above, including all derivative supports. Let <math|p<rsub|0>>
    bound the number of Gaussian coordinates per coarse site and
    <math|m<rsub|0>> the number of distinct cutoff-remainder addresses per
    site. These are fixed once the stage and ratio are fixed. For <math|r>
    distinct addresses, choose one violating coordinate for each. There are
    at most <math|<around|(|p<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|>|)><rsup|r>>
    choices. A single coordinate can serve at most
    <math|m<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|>> addresses. The Schur
    bound gives covariance operator norm at most <math|M<rsub|0>>. For a set
    <math|J> of chosen distinct coordinates, Gaussian exponential moments and
    summation over signs give

    <\equation*>
      Pr <around|{|<around|\||z<rsub|i>|\|>\<geqslant\>T*<around|(|i\<in\>J|)>|}>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|<around|\||J|\|>>*exp
      <around|{|<around|(|M<rsub|0>/2-T|)><around|\||J|\|>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Take <math|T=\<xi\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/<around|(|2*g|)>> and choose
    <math|H\<gtr\>0> with <math|M<rsub|0>/2+H*m<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|>\<leqslant\>T>.
    The multiplicity bound gives

    <\equation*>
      Pr <around|(|E<rsub|\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|(|2*p<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|><math-up|e><rsup|-H>|)><rsup|r>.
    </equation*>

    The factor <math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/g> grows faster than every fixed
    power of <math|B> and <math|log <refbeta><rsub|m+1>>. Consequently
    <math|H> can exceed <math|2*\<tau\><rsub|w>*log <refbeta><rsub|m+1>+log
    <around|(|2*p<rsub|0><around|\||\<bbb-B\><rsub|2*B>|\|>|)>>,
    proving<nbsp>(iv). A cutoff remainder is thus rare because its violating
    coordinate lies <math|t<rsub|<math-up|f>>/g\<gg\>1> standard deviations
    out.

    <em|Step 6: the common majorant.> Take the finite sum of the absolute
    differentiated smooth expressions and scores, multiplied by the fixed
    incoming measurable envelopes, the cutoff-remainder amplitudes
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|w>M<rsub|w>>, and the simultaneous indicator. The
    product rule bounds every derivative through order <math|k> by this same
    nonnegative function<nbsp><math|G<rsub|M>>. Its integral is bounded by
    its polynomial <math|L<rsup|2>> bound times <math|Pr
    <around|(|E<rsub|\<cal-J\><around|(|M|)>>|)><rsup|1/2>>. This retains
    precisely <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w><rsup|r>> and proves<nbsp>(i).

    <em|Step 7: selected-test suppression.> Distribute the local energy of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1> among the selected-test factors before
    differentiating. When <math|A<rsub|c>=\<varnothing\>> the admitted
    support is healthy. The physical-link change holds the fine links and
    selected test indices fixed; differentiated test cutoffs retain the
    selected-test inequalities on their supports. The exponential energy is
    retained in each differentiated envelope. Annular affine errors have size
    <math|E<around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)><around|\||A|\|>> with <math|E> of
    upper rate <math|-3/2>, and the remaining per-test logarithmic
    corrections have a fixed-power bound <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>,k,q>*<around|(|1+log
    <refbeta><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|d<rsub|0>><around|\||A|\|>>. Derivatives of
    the smooth factors through order <math|k> multiply this bound by fixed
    powers of <math|B>, <math|g<rsup|-1>*t<rsub|<math-up|c>>> and the number
    of selected tests. Their logarithms per test are
    <math|o<around|(|\<beta\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>|)>>, since
    <math|\<beta\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>> is comparable to
    <math|<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>, which exceeds every
    fixed power of <math|log <refbeta><rsub|m+1>>. Also
    <math|E<around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)>/t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>\<to\>0>.
    Increase the coupling threshold after fixing
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>\<less\>\<kappa\>>; absorption into
    <math|<around|(|\<kappa\>-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>|)>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>
    gives<nbsp>(ii) through order <math|k>. The support factor
    <math|<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d>> remains outside this per-test
    estimate. The determinant and covariance corrections use the exponential
    bounds above. Integrating the independent frozen Haar variables and
    summing the finite tag probabilities preserve the bound.

    <em|Step 8: large-field components.> Retain the full sector masks of the
    component and all frozen links read by it. The lower bound in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1> gives
    <math|\<kappa\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>> in its local
    energy. The enlarged neighbourhood used by the component adds annular
    affine remainders bounded by <math|E<around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)><around|\||A|\|>>,
    where <math|E> has upper rate <math|-3/2>: the term error is
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|3>|)>>, and the number of
    positions per test is a fixed power of <math|B>. The chart-density, tag,
    local determinant and normalized frozen-coordinate factors contribute at
    most <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<around|(|1+log
    <refbeta><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|d<rsub|0>><around|\||A|\|>> to the upper
    exponent, for a fixed <math|d<rsub|0>>. Indeed, their read counts are at
    most <math|C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*B<rsup|4><around|\||A|\|>>; the logarithm of
    each inverse Gaussian normalization is <math|O<around|(|1+log
    <refbeta><rsub|m+1>|)>>, the chart logarithms are bounded on the
    protected chart, and the local determinant terms have the bounds of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>. The retained value envelope is thus
    at most

    <\equation*>
      exp <space|-0.17em><around*|[|-\<kappa\>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>\|A\|+<around|{|\<beta\>*E<around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)>+C<rsub|j,\<ell\>>*<around|(|1+log
      <refbeta><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|d<rsub|0>>|}><around|\||A|\|>|]>.
    </equation*>

    As in Step<nbsp>7, both corrections are
    <math|o<around|(|\<beta\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2>|)>> per test.
    Choose <math|\<kappa\><rprime|''><rsub|B>> strictly between
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>> and <math|\<kappa\>> and increase the
    coupling threshold. This bounds the component envelope by
    <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|''><rsub|B>*\<beta\>*t<rsub|<math-up|f>><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>>.
    Use this component envelope and the incoming measurable supremum
    envelopes. The attached factors and Gaussian scores have exactly the
    <math|L<rsup|2>> bound proved above. Cauchy\USchwarz with their
    simultaneous event retains every attached <math|f<rsub|i>>. Polynomial
    factors in the number of component reads are bounded by a fixed
    polynomial in <math|1+s<rsub|A>> times a fixed power of <math|B>, the
    latter entering <math|P>. Any remaining fixed per-test logarithmic loss
    is absorbed by <math|\<kappa\><rprime|''><rsub|B>-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>>.
    Frozen Haar integration has mass one. This proves<nbsp>(iii), without
    differentiating its flag masks or taking a supremum of a Gaussian
    coordinate.

    <em|Step 9: factorization.> The Gaussian marginal on <math|I> has
    covariance <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|I>>. Every complete read support contains
    <math|\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> for each coordinate <math|i>
    it reads. The windows are symmetric. Hence if <math|i,i<rprime|'>> belong
    to disjoint complete read supports, <math|i<rprime|'>\<nin\>\<Omega\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>
    and <math|i\<nin\>\<Omega\><rsub|i<rprime|'>><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>, so
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i*i<rprime|'>>=0> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-window>. The
    Gaussian characteristic function therefore factorizes over these
    supports. The frozen Haar and reference tag coordinates are disjoint as
    well, proving<nbsp>(v).
  </proof>

  <section|Path actions and the one-loop coupling><label|sec:partII-C>

  After each integration the effective action keeps its curvature terms
  through fourth order in a path action. Its quadratic part is normalized so
  that <math|\<beta\>> is the coefficient of the squared Wilson curvature at
  zero momentum at every level. The quadratic terms independent of
  <math|\<beta\>>, the loop atoms, have vanishing zeroth and first
  displacement moments, so they carry no curvature-square term. This section
  constructs these arrays and computes the one-loop change of the coupling:
  the blocked determinant lowers the curvature-square coefficient by
  <math|<slope>log \<ell\><rsub|m>>, up to an error bounded independently of
  the block ratio (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>).

  The path bounds enter Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>, and the coefficient
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> enters Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>.
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-B> supplies the inputs. The free forms
  and their row factor (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>) fix the quadratic
  part; the stationary interpolation and the vertex bounds
  (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> and<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>) give the
  classical cubic and quartic terms; the Gaussian coordinates, the inverse
  insertions and the Ward estimates (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B4>,
  <reference|lem:B7> and<nbsp><reference|lem:B6>) control the determinant.
  The scalar reset of the coupling uses the activities of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-D> and is proved there, in
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:C-reset>.

  Throughout, the group and metric are those of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, and the scales are those of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>. At a step <math|m+1\<to\>m>
  put <math|\<ell\>=\<ell\><rsub|m>> and <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>. The path
  arrays (row coefficients and atoms of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D22>)
  depend on <math|N,m> and the intervening block ratios, and are chosen on
  <math|<Z><rsup|4>> before periodic evaluation. They are independent of the
  volume index<nbsp><math|p> and of the running coupling. Constants declared
  uniform in<nbsp><math|\<ell\>> are chosen before<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>.

  The arrays are constructed by descending induction on<nbsp><math|m>. Write
  <math|<around|(|I<rsub|m>|)>> for the path bounds and cancellations of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2> at level<nbsp><math|m>,
  the path part of the induction scheme of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-B>. The top-level Wilson matching in the
  proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4> starts the induction. Under
  <math|<around|(|I<rsub|m+1>|)>>, Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>,
  <reference|lem:C6> and<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> treat the step
  <math|m+1\<to\>m>; the joint proof after Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>
  constructs the output arrays and proves<nbsp><math|<around|(|I<rsub|m>|)>>.
  Curl division and the gauge determinant identity are independent of this
  induction.

  <subsection|Curvature coordinates and the path
  action><label|subsec:C-coordinates>

  A path transports curvature to an anchor, where invariant colour
  contractions can be taken. Write <math|U<rsub|\<gamma\>>> for the ordered
  link product along<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>>,
  <math|\<frak-p\>=<around|(|a,b|)>>, <math|a\<less\>b>, for an orientation,
  and <math|U<rsub|x;\<frak-p\>>> for a plaquette holonomy. The footprint of
  a slot is the set of vertices visited by its path and plaquette boundary.
  The footprint of an atom is the union of its slot footprints and the
  origin, translated to its anchor. Recall that a set is healthy if it avoids
  <math|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>);
  an atom is masked by the health of its footprint.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Curvature slots><label|def:D22> A slot
    <math|s=<around|(|\<gamma\>,\<frak-p\>|)>>, with
    <math|\<gamma\>:0\<to\>z<rsub|s>>, has length
    <math|<around|\||\<gamma\>|\|>+4> and value

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-F\><rsub|s>*<around|(|U,x|)>=<math-up|Ad><around|(|U<rsub|x+\<gamma\>>|)><math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><frac|U<rsub|x+z<rsub|s>;\<frak-p\>>-U<rsub|x+z<rsub|s>;\<frak-p\>><rsup|-1>|2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Transport is from <math|x+z<rsub|s>> to<nbsp><math|x>. Set
    <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|\<frak-p\>><rsup|<math-up|path>>=<big|sum><rsub|i>r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>*\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>>.
    For <math|k=2,3,4>, the atoms <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>>
    are real invariant <math|k>-linear expressions in slots, masked by health
    on their footprints. Atom length sums slot lengths;
    <math|<around|\||\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>|\|>> sums
    absolute colour coefficients. These arrays retain local terms through
    fourth order.
  </definition>

  Here <math|r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>\<in\><R>> and
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>> denotes the slot assigned to
  row<nbsp><math|\<frak-p\>> and index<nbsp><math|i\<in\><N>>. In the
  orthonormal colour basis <math|<around|(|e<rsub|b><rsup|<g>>|)><rsub|b>> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, an atom has the form

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|U,x|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i>+x<space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*<big|sum><rsub|b<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|k>>c<rsub|i>*<around|(|b<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,b<rsub|k>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|\<nu\>=1><rsup|k><around|\<langle\>|e<rsub|b<rsub|\<nu\>>><rsup|<g>>,\<cal-F\><rsub|s<rsub|i,\<nu\>>>*<around|(|U,x|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The coefficients are invariant under the simultaneous adjoint action on all
  slots, and <math|X<rsub|i>> is the footprint just defined.

  For each stage<nbsp><math|k>, take <math|\<gamma\><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0> from
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, so the free plaquette forms for the top and
  both incoming stages are at least <math|2*\<gamma\><rsub|k>*I>. Write
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>> and
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>=c<rsub|<math-up|lin>,J<rsub|m>>>. Whenever
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>, the form
  <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*I> below is at least
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*I> and has the positive row factor used
  below.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Path action><label|def:D23> At level <math|m> the path action
    <math|<pathaction><rsub|N,m>> is<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-path-action>, with
    the clip and relaxed threshold test of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-clipping>, the
    rows and atoms of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D22>, and clipping
    constants <math|0\<less\>16*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>,1|)>>. Write

    <\equation*>
      <pathaction><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<beta\>,U|)>=\<beta\><math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m>> is the hypercubic
    average of the clipped plaquette terms, the row squares and the cubic and
    quartic atoms, and <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|m>> that of the quadratic atoms.
  </definition>

  The kinetic part carries the coupling; the loop part collects the quadratic
  corrections that do not scale with<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>. The clip drops at
  <math|u=t> from <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*t<rsup|2>/2> to
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t<rsup|2>>, and its linear branch gives
  <math|<math-up|clip><rsub|t><around|(|u|)>=c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t*u\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t<rsup|2>>
  for <math|u\<gtr\>t>. This positive linear large-field branch is the lower
  bound used in the local large-field energy estimate,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>, and through it in the forced bounds of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. Because of the jump the path action
  is only Borel in the links. The derivatives used later are taken on healthy
  configurations, where every clip of the support is on its quadratic branch
  and every mask is fixed, and coupling derivatives hold the physical links
  and the masks fixed (Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-analytic>). No
  derivative of a flag boundary or of the clip is ever taken.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Path bounds><label|lem:C1> For every <math|N> and
    <math|m\<leqslant\>N> the chosen arrays are independent of <math|p>
    and<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>. There are widths
    <math|0\<less\>w<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|k>\<less\>w<rprime|'><rsub|k>>
    and amplitudes <math|A<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>, depending
    only on the stage<nbsp><math|k>, such that, uniformly in<nbsp><math|N>,
    with <math|w=w<rprime|'><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>|\|>*e<rsup|w<math-up|length><around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\||\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>|\|>*e<rsup|w<math-up|length><around|(|\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>,>|<cell|k=2,>>|<row|<cell|A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>,>|<cell|k=3,4,>>>>>>>>>
    </align*>

    where <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>=2<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>>
    depends on the level. A fortiori these bounds hold at width
    <math|w<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>.
  </lemma>

  The joint proof of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> follows Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>. To state
  the cancellations, put

  <\equation*>
    s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>>=<big|sum><rsub|i:<space|0.17em><math-up|orientation><around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>|)>=\<frak-q\>>r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For ordered orientations <math|\<frak-q\>,\<frak-r\>> let
  <math|I<rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>> be the set of quadratic atoms whose
  two slots have orientations <math|\<frak-q\>,\<frak-r\>>, with colour
  coefficients <math|c<rsub|i>*<around|(|b<rsub|1>,b<rsub|2>|)>> and slot
  offsets <math|z<rsub|i,1>,z<rsub|i,2>>.

  The cancellations have a Fourier meaning. To quadratic order in the links,
  an atom in <math|I<rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>> pairs the curvatures at two
  points displaced by <math|z<rsub|i,2>-z<rsub|i,1>>, so the loop atoms
  contribute the plaquette-kernel symbol <math|<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>>c<rsub|i>*<around|(|b<rsub|1>,b<rsub|2>|)>*e<rsup|i*\<xi\>\<cdot\><around|(|z<rsub|i,2>-z<rsub|i,1>|)>>>
  between two curvatures. A zero total coefficient removes its constant term
  and a zero first displacement moment removes its linear term, so the symbol
  is <math|O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>|)>>. Each curvature is a
  curl, of order <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>> in the link variables. Hence
  the loop atoms have a link kernel of order four and contain no
  curvature-square term: the marginal curvature square is carried by the
  coupling alone. Under blocking, the cell volume <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>>, the
  two interpolated fields <math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>> and the rescaled momentum
  <math|\<xi\>/\<ell\>> turn an order-four link kernel into
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>/\<ell\>|\|><rsup|4>|)>=\<ell\><rsup|-2>*O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|4>|)>>,
  an inverse-block-square gain (proof of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>).

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Kinetic and quadratic cancellations><label|lem:C2> For all
    orientations <math|\<frak-q\>,\<frak-r\>> and colours
    <math|b<rsub|1>,b<rsub|2>>,

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*\<delta\><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<frak-p\>>s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>>*s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-r\>>=\<delta\><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>>c<rsub|i>*<around|(|b<rsub|1>,b<rsub|2>|)>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>>c<rsub|i>*<around|(|b<rsub|1>,b<rsub|2>|)>*<around|(|z<rsub|i,2>-z<rsub|i,1>|)>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    All these moment series converge absolutely.
  </lemma>

  Thus the kinetic part has the Wilson normalization
  <math|<wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsub|N,m><around|(|0|)>=I> below, and the loop
  atoms do not change the curvature-square coefficient.

  <subsection|Fourth-order matching><label|subsec:C-matching>

  Near the flat field all relaxed threshold tests equal one. By
  <math|<chord><rsup|2>=2<wilsonterm>> from<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-price-chord>,
  the leading analytic action is obtained from
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23> by replacing the clipped term by
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>><wilsonterm>> and omitting the indicators and
  loop atoms. Denote this action, without its factor<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>, by
  <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|N,m>>. Its prescribed quadratic part is

  <\equation*>
    <frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>d<rsub|1>*a|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*I+\<rho\><rsub|N,m><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<rho\><rsub|N,m>,<space|2em><wide|<math-ss|H>|^><rsub|N,m><around|(|0|)>=I<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|d<rsub|1>> is the lattice curl. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>
  gives the exact adjacent free relation and the row factor
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>>, with <math|<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>-c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*I\<geqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*I>.
  The rows are read off from <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>>. Write
  <math|<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>*F|)><rsub|\<frak-p\>><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<frak-q\>><big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>\<rho\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>><around|(|z|)>*F<rsub|\<frak-q\>>*<around|(|x+z|)>>.
  For each <math|\<frak-p\>,\<frak-q\>,z> with
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>><around|(|z|)>\<ne\>0> take one
  slot <math|<around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|z>,\<frak-q\>|)>>, with
  <math|\<gamma\><rsub|z>> a fixed coordinate path from <math|0> to <math|z>,
  and row coefficient <math|\<rho\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>><around|(|z|)>>.
  At the flat field <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|\<frak-p\>><around|(|U,x|)>>
  linearizes to <math|<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>*d<rsub|1>*a|)><rsub|\<frak-p\>><around|(|x|)>>,
  so the direct and row terms have quadratic part
  <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|d<rsub|1>*a,<math-ss|H><rsub|N,m>d<rsub|1>*a|\<rangle\>>>;
  also <math|s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>>=<wide|\<rho\>|^><rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>><around|(|0|)>>,
  and the exponential decay of <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>> gives the row bound
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>. The cell volume contributes
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> and each curvature contributes
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>>; hence <math|\<ell\><rsup|4><around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2>|)><rsup|2>=1>,
  preserving the coefficient<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>.

  We need a division that expresses a gauge-invariant Taylor difference in
  curvatures. For a momentum<nbsp><math|\<xi\>> put
  <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=e<rsup|i*\<xi\><rsub|a>>-1>; its
  exterior action <math|d<rsub|1><around|(|\<xi\>|)>*a=q<around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<wedge\>a>
  is the curl symbol. Momentum order<nbsp><math|s> means vanishing of all
  Taylor coefficients of total momentum degree less than<nbsp><math|s>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Curl division><label|lem:C3> Let <math|k\<geqslant\>2>,
    <math|\<sigma\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|A\<geqslant\>0>, and let <math|f> be a
    periodic analytic kernel in independent momenta
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<xi\><rsub|k-1>>
    (<math|\<xi\><rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>\<xi\><rsub|\<nu\>>>),
    valued in <math|<big|otimes><rsub|\<nu\>=0><rsup|k-1>\<Lambda\><rsup|1><C><rsup|4>>,
    bounded coordinatewise by <math|A> on
    <math|<around|\||<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>\<xi\><rsub|\<nu\>><rsup|a>|\|>\<less\>\<sigma\>>,
    and transverse in every leg: <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<nu\>>*f=0>,
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|\<nu\>>=\<iota\><rsub|q<around|(|\<xi\><rsub|\<nu\>>|)>>>.
    If <math|k=2> assume also momentum order <math|s+2>. Then there are
    <math|0\<less\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>\<sigma\>> and <math|K\<geqslant\>0>,
    depending only on <math|k,\<sigma\>>, and a periodic analytic <math|g>
    valued in <math|<big|otimes><rsub|\<nu\>>\<Lambda\><rsup|2><C><rsup|4>>,
    of order <math|s> if <math|k=2>, with

    <\equation*>
      f=\<delta\><rsub|0>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|k-1>*g,<space|2em>sup<rsub|\<eta\><text|-strip>><around|\||g|\|>\<leqslant\>K*A<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The Fourier coefficients of <math|g> decay exponentially.
  </lemma>

  In words: a gauge-invariant multilinear kernel factors through the curls of
  its legs.

  <\proof>
    Dividing one leg by its curl is straightforward when its momentum is
    independent of the others. The difficulty is the leg with momentum
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|0>>: a primitive in that leg may fail to remain
    transverse in another leg. We first construct the one-leg homotopy, then
    correct this transversality defect. Each correction reduces the number of
    independent momenta by one and raises the exterior degree in the
    dependent leg by two, and the iteration terminates when that degree
    exceeds four. The two-leg case instead uses the extra vanishing order at
    zero momentum.

    Scalar division requires vanishing when the divided leg has zero
    momentum; positive exterior degree requires only closedness. For one leg
    let <math|\<delta\>=\<iota\><rsub|q>> be contraction on exterior tensors.
    Split a <math|\<delta\>>-closed tensor as <math|f=e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>u+v>,
    with <math|u,v> containing no <math|e<rsub|a>>. Define

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|a>*f<around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<frac|f<around|(|\<xi\>|)>-f<around|(|\<xi\>|)>\|<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|a>=0>|q<rsub|a><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>,<space|2em>h<rsub|a>*f=e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>D<rsub|a>*v,<space|2em>R<rsub|a>*f=v\|<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|a>=0><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|D<rsub|a>> is the one-leg exterior
    division<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-exterior-division>. The identity
    <math|\<delta\>*f=0> gives <math|\<delta\><rprime|'>*v=-q<rsub|a>*u>,
    where <math|\<delta\><rprime|'>> contracts the other axes. Since
    <math|q<rsub|b>> is independent of <math|\<xi\><rsub|a>> for
    <math|b\<ne\>a>, <math|D<rsub|a>> commutes
    with<nbsp><math|\<delta\><rprime|'>>, and
    <math|D<rsub|a>*<around|(|q<rsub|a>*u|)>=u>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\>*h<rsub|a>*f=q<rsub|a>*D<rsub|a>*v-e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>D<rsub|a>*\<delta\><rprime|'>*v=v-v\|<rsub|\<xi\><rsub|a>=0>+e<rsub|a>\<wedge\>u=f-R<rsub|a>*f<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The remainder <math|R<rsub|a>*f> is again closed and has no component
    containing<nbsp><math|e<rsub|a>>. Applying this in the four axes gives
    the homotopy

    <\equation*>
      f=\<delta\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|4>h<rsub|a>*R<rsub|a-1>*\<cdots\>*R<rsub|1>*f|)>+R<rsub|4>*R<rsub|3>*R<rsub|2>*R<rsub|1>*f<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The final remainder vanishes in positive exterior degree because it
    contains none of the four axes. In degree zero it is the restriction to
    zero momentum of this leg, which must vanish for scalar division. In that
    case the formula reads <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|a>q<rsub|a>*D<rsub|a>*<around|(|R<rsub|a-1>*\<cdots\>*R<rsub|1>*f|)>>.

    The quotients are analytic even at the zeros of<nbsp><math|q<rsub|a>>. On
    a Fourier monomial the identity

    <\equation*>
      <frac|e<rsup|i*n*\<xi\><rsub|a>>-1|e<rsup|i*\<xi\><rsub|a>>-1>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|v=0><rsup|n-1>e<rsup|i*v*\<xi\><rsub|a>>,>|<cell|n\<gtr\>0,>>|<row|<cell|-<big|sum><rsub|v=n><rsup|-1>e<rsup|i*v*\<xi\><rsub|a>>,>|<cell|n\<less\>0,>>|<row|<cell|0,>|<cell|n=0>>>>>
    </equation*>

    bounds the quotient on a smaller strip by a constant times
    <math|<around|\||n|\|>> times its original exponential weight. The strip
    loss absorbs <math|<around|\||n|\|>>, so the Fourier series converges
    absolutely. Each division loses at most one momentum order.

    For independent legs, their remaining transverse subspaces do not depend
    on the divided momentum, so each restriction and division preserves them.
    The dependent-leg induction has the following claim: with <math|r>
    independent momenta and dependent exterior degree <math|d\<geqslant\>1>,
    a tensor closed in the dependent leg and transverse in all independent
    legs has a dependent-leg primitive preserving those transverse
    conditions, provided <math|d+2*r\<gtr\>4>. Analytic bounds hold after a
    strip reduction depending only on <math|r,d> and the original strip. For
    <math|r=0> the condition gives <math|d\<gtr\>4>, so the tensor is zero.
    The induction step below reduces <math|<around|(|r,d|)>> to
    <math|<around|(|r-1,d+2|)>>, preserving the condition. Initially
    <math|r=k-1,d=1>, so it applies for <math|k\<geqslant\>3>.

    For the dependent momentum <math|\<xi\><rsub|0>=-<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|k-1>\<xi\><rsub|i>>,
    first shear the coordinates to replace <math|\<xi\><rsub|1>>
    by<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|0>>. The other independent contractions remain
    unchanged. The one-leg formula gives <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*X=f>,
    retaining the contractions in legs <math|2,\<ldots\>,k-1>. Write
    <math|c<rsub|1>> for contraction in leg<nbsp><math|1>. Its defect obeys
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*<around|(|c<rsub|1>*X|)>=c<rsub|1>*f=0>. Solve
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*Y=c<rsub|1>*X> by the same formula. After
    <math|c<rsub|1>>, leg<nbsp><math|1> has exterior degree zero, so
    restoring that leg will require only scalar division.

    On <math|\<xi\><rsub|1>=0>, the restriction <math|Y<rsub|0>> is
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>>-closed, since <math|c<rsub|1>*X> vanishes there.
    The induction hypothesis with one fewer independent momentum and
    dependent degree two higher gives <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>*Z=Y<rsub|0>>,
    preserving the other transverse conditions. Extend<nbsp><math|Z>
    independently of<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|1>>. Then
    <math|Y<rprime|'>=Y-\<delta\><rsub|0>*Z> vanishes on
    <math|\<xi\><rsub|1>=0>, and scalar division gives
    <math|c<rsub|1>*D=Y<rprime|'>>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      V=X-\<delta\><rsub|0>*D,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|0>*V=f,<space|2em>c<rsub|1>*V=c<rsub|1>*X-\<delta\><rsub|0>*Y<rprime|'>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    All corrections preserve the other independent contractions. The two
    successive solves <math|f\<mapsto\>X> and <math|c<rsub|1>*X\<mapsto\>Y>
    each raise the dependent exterior degree by one; contraction
    <math|c<rsub|1>> changes only leg<nbsp><math|1>. Thus the restricted
    problem for <math|Y<rsub|0>> has one fewer independent momentum and
    dependent degree two higher. For three or more legs it terminates above
    exterior degree four, where the tensor is zero. The independent-leg
    divisions then finish the factorization.

    For two legs, the restriction <math|Y<rsub|0>> is at zero external
    momentum. Starting from order <math|s+2>, the first division loses one
    order, contraction restores one, and the solve for<nbsp><math|Y> leaves
    order <math|s+1\<geqslant\>1>. Hence <math|Y<rsub|0>=0> directly. The
    correction and final division leave order<nbsp><math|s>. This verifies
    the scalar vanishing hypothesis where it is needed. For the Taylor
    differences of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>, gauge invariance
    supplies transversality because all lower Taylor differences vanish.
  </proof>

  For <math|z=<around|(|z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|k-1>|)>>, contour
  displacement gives <math|<around|\||<wide|g|\<check\>><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-\<zeta\>*<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|><rsub|1>>>
  for the Fourier coefficients <math|<wide|g|\<check\>>> of <math|g>. Choose
  the anchor at zero and coordinate paths to<nbsp><math|z<rsub|\<nu\>>>.
  Their total slot length is at most <math|4*k+<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>><around|\||z<rsub|\<nu\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
  Every path width below<nbsp><math|\<zeta\>> therefore gives a convergent
  weighted sum. Reality and simultaneous adjoint invariance pass to the
  coefficients by conjugation and invariant projection.

  <paragraph|A finite-dimensional model.> The classical matching below is the
  following calculation, with locality estimates attached. Let
  <math|S*<around|(|v,z|)>> be smooth near
  <math|0\<in\><R><rsup|a>\<times\><R><rsup|b>> with
  <math|D*S*<around|(|0,0|)>=0> and <math|<math-ss|Q>=D<rsub|z><rsup|2>*S*<around|(|0,0|)>\<gtr\>0>,
  and let <math|z<around|(|v|)>> solve <math|D<rsub|z>*S*<around|(|v,z<around|(|v|)>|)>=0>,
  <math|z<around|(|0|)>=0>. Put <math|W*v=<around|(|v,D*z<around|(|0|)>*v|)>>
  and <math|<math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><around|[|u|]>=D<rsup|3>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|W*v,W*v,<around|(|0,u|)>|]>>.
  Along <math|\<gamma\><around|(|t|)>=<around|(|t*v,z*<around|(|t*v|)>|)>>
  one has <math|\<gamma\><rprime|'><around|(|0|)>=W*v> and
  <math|\<gamma\><rprime|''><around|(|0|)>=<around|(|0,w|)>> with
  <math|w=D<rsup|2>*z<around|(|0|)><around|[|v,v|]>>. Differentiating the
  equation for <math|z> once gives <math|D<rsup|2>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|W*v,<around|(|0,u|)>|]>=0>,
  and twice gives <math|w=-<math-ss|Q><rsup|-1><math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)>>.
  In the third derivative of <math|S<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|v|)>=S*<around|(|v,z<around|(|v|)>|)>>
  the only term containing <math|w> is <math|3*D<rsup|2>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|W*v,<around|(|0,w|)>|]>=0>,
  and the term containing <math|D<rsup|3>*z> is multiplied by
  <math|D*S<around|(|0|)>=0>, so the cubic term is simple:
  <math|D<rsup|3>*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|0|)><around|[|v<rsup|3>|]>=D<rsup|3>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|W*v|)><rsup|3>|]>>.
  In the fourth derivative the term <math|4*D<rsup|2>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|W*v,<around|(|0,D<rsup|3>*z<around|(|0|)><around|[|v<rsup|3>|]>|)>|]>>
  vanishes by the first identity and the term containing <math|D<rsup|4>*z>
  is multiplied by <math|D*S<around|(|0|)>=0>; the terms containing <math|w>
  are

  <\equation*>
    6<math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><around|[|w|]>+3*<around|\<langle\>|w,<math-ss|Q>w|\<rangle\>>=-3<math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><math-ss|Q><rsup|-1><math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  an exchange correction: the three ways of splitting four legs into two
  pairs, each pair joined through the fluctuation propagator
  <math|<math-ss|Q><rsup|-1>>. Degree four is the stopping point. In the
  continuum the curvature square <math|<around|\||d*a+<tfrac|1|2><around|[|a\<wedge\>a|]>|\|><rsup|2>>
  has degree at most four in the connection, so matching through degree four
  captures the classical marginal term; the Taylor remainder of degree five
  is of order <math|\<beta\>*t<rsub|m><rsup|5>> per site and is absorbed by
  the activity bounds (proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>).

  On the lattice the model applies with <math|z=\<psi\>>: <math|v> is the
  coarse chart variable and <math|\<psi\>> the fine axial variable;
  <math|S*<around|(|v,\<psi\>|)>> is the fine leading action plus
  <math|<blockpenalty><big|sum><rsub|e><wilsonterm>> of the predictor
  discrepancy, without the input coupling
  <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>>, and it excludes the
  inherited loop action. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> supplies the
  stationary branch <math|\<psi\><around|(|v|)>> with
  <math|<math-ss|Q>\<geqslant\>\<nu\><rsub|j,\<ell\>>*I>, and we write
  <math|W<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*v=<around|(|v,D*\<psi\><around|(|0|)>*v|)>> for
  the axial tangent and <math|S<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|v|)>=S*<around|(|v,\<psi\><around|(|v|)>|)>>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5> gives the vertex bounds
  <math|C<rsub|3>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|3>+\<ell\><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|3><rprime|'>>,
  <math|C<rsub|4>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|4>+\<ell\><rsup|-1>*c<rsub|4><rprime|'>>
  and <math|<around|\||D<rsup|4>*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|4>+3*C<rsub|3><rsup|2>*E<rsub|<math-up|ins>>>,
  with <math|E<rsub|<math-up|ins>>=max <around|(|1,C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|ins>>|)>>.
  The stationary identities hold for every block ratio of
  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>; only the uniform bound on the
  new arrays needs <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|j>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Classical matching><label|lem:C4> With
    <math|<math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><around|[|u|]>=D<rsup|3>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|W<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*v,W<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*v,<around|(|0,u|)>|]>>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|D<rsup|3>*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|0|)><around|[|v<rsup|3>|]>>|<cell|=D<rsup|3>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|W<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*v|)><rsup|3>|]>,>>|<row|<cell|D<rsup|4>*S<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|0|)><around|[|v<rsup|4>|]>>|<cell|=D<rsup|4>*S<around|(|0|)><around|[|<around|(|W<rsub|<math-up|ax>>*v|)><rsup|4>|]>-3<math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><math-ss|Q><rsup|-1><math-ss|B><around|(|v,v|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    For <math|\<ell\>> above a threshold depending on the input path data,
    cubic and quartic arrays satisfying the bounds of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1> make <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|N,m>> agree
    with <math|S<rsub|<math-up|stat>>> through degree four. Their bound
    depends only on fixed stage data.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The identities are the finite-dimensional calculation above applied to
    the analytic stationary branch of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>; in the
    joint coordinates of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5> the same identities
    hold with smooth interpolated legs. The kernel bounds are those of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>.

    Increase <math|\<ell\>> until <math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|3><rprime|'>,\<ell\><rsup|-1>*c<rsub|4><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>1>.
    The output action built from <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>> and the rows of
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>> alone agrees with <math|S<rsub|<math-up|stat>>>
    at degree two (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>). Both are gauge invariant,
    so their cubic Taylor difference is annihilated by linearized gauge
    transformations in each leg; that is, it is transverse.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C3> with <math|k=3> writes it through curls;
    placing its coefficients on coordinate paths gives cubic atoms, bounded
    through <math|C<rsub|3>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|3>+1>. Adding these atoms, with
    their full nonlinear transport, changes the quartic Taylor coefficients.
    The remaining quartic difference is again transverse, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C3> with <math|k=4> gives quartic atoms bounded
    through <math|C<rsub|4>+3*C<rsub|3><rsup|2>*E<rsub|<math-up|ins>>>. This
    makes the two actions agree through degree four. At <math|m=N>, the free
    form acts as the identity on curls, so the same construction matches the
    Wilson energy through degree four and starts with zero loop atoms.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3> places the remaining top-level difference,
    including clipping and unhealthy configurations, in the initial
    activities.
  </proof>

  The common factor<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rprime|'>> in the penalty and fine
  leading action cancels from the stationary equation; hence the arrays are
  independent of<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>. In <math|log det
  <around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'><math-ss|Q>|)>>, the term <math|<around|(|dim
  <math-ss|Q>|)>*log \<beta\><rprime|'>> is independent of the field and
  contributes only to the vacuum scalar.

  <subsection|The determinant comparison><label|subsec:C-determinant>

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B4> gives the one-loop action

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
    det <math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>-log J<rsub|<math-up|ax>><around|(|v|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  with the stationary fine configuration <math|U<rsub|v>>, the inherited
  quadratic loop action <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1>>, the axial Hessian
  <math|<math-ss|Q><around|(|v|)>> without<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>,
  and the Haar chart ratio <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>>, normalized to one at
  zero.

  We measure the kinetic coefficient by a transverse plane wave: momentum
  <math|t*e<rsub|1>>, link polarization <math|e<rsub|2>> and one fixed unit
  colour vector. For a quadratic Taylor kernel <math|Y>, including the Taylor
  factor<nbsp><math|1/2>!, recall from Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:B-inverse>
  the functional <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y|)>=2<math-up|Re><around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*Y<rsub|<around|(|2,b|)>,<around|(|2,b|)>>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|1>|)>>,
  where <math|<around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*g<around|(|t|)>=<tfrac|1|2>g<rprime|''><around|(|0|)>>,
  and define

  <\equation*>
    \<alpha\><rsub|N,m>=\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsup|<math-up|blk>><rsub|N,m>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|a=e<rsup|i*t*x<rsub|1>>*e<rsub|2>\<otimes\>e<rsub|b><rsup|<g>>>
  one has <math|<around|\||d<rsub|1>*a|\|><rsup|2>=t<rsup|2>+O<around|(|t<rsup|4>|)>>
  per site, so <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y|)>> is the coefficient of
  <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\||d<rsub|1>*a|\|><rsup|2>> in <math|Y>, the
  curvature-square coefficient; the factor <math|2> compensates for the
  <math|1/2>!. The kernel <math|Y<rsup|<math-up|blk>><rsub|N,m>> is the
  infinite-volume quadratic kernel of <math|<math-ss|T>>. The prescribed
  kinetic kernel has <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>=1>. Simplicity makes
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> independent of<nbsp><math|b>, as shown in the
  proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>; by construction it is independent
  of<nbsp><math|p> and<nbsp><math|\<beta\><rprime|'>>.

  We use the joint left Hessian <math|<math-ss|J>>, the gauge insertion
  <math|\<cal-R\>>, the slice embedding <math|\<cal-I\>> and the frame
  derivative <math|X> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B4>, and the covariant
  difference <math|d<rsub|U>>, the Laplacian
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|U>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>>, the regulator
  <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|9>> with
  <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>>,
  the fine-link projection <math|P<rsub|f>> and the gauge condition
  <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>=d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X>
  defined before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>. Recall <math|X*\<cal-R\>=I>
  and <math|det <around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)><rsup|2>=1>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B4>). Put

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>=<math-ss|J>+F<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|\<ast\>>*F<rsub|\<mu\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>=F<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-R\>=\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The next lemma is the Faddeev\UPopov identity for the gauge condition
  <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>>: <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>> is the gauge-fixed
  Hessian, <math|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>> the ghost operator, and <math|-log
  det <math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>> the ghost contribution.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Gauge completion><label|lem:C5> For <math|v> in a neighbourhood of
    zero,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:C-FP><math-ss|T><around|(|v|)>=\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>log
      det <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)>-log det
      <math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|v|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Gauge invariance and stationarity give <math|<math-ss|J>\<cal-R\>=0>, and
    <math|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-R\>=<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)>=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>>|<cell|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-I\>>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|I>>>>><rsup|<space|-0.17em>\<ast\>><matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>\<cal-I\>>>>>><matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>>|<cell|F<rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-I\>>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|I>>>>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|det <around|(|\<cal-R\>,\<cal-I\>|)><rsup|2>=1>, this gives
    <math|det <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|(|det
    <math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|2>*det
    <around|(|\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>\<cal-I\>|)>>. The proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B4> gives <math|<math-ss|Q>=<around|(|D*s|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>\<cal-I\>|)>*D*s>
    and <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>=<around|\||det D*s|\|>>, where <math|s>
    changes axial coordinates to left increments at the stationary point.
    Hence <math|<frac|1|2>*log det <math-ss|Q>-log
    J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>=<frac|1|2>*log det
    <around|(|\<cal-I\><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|J>\<cal-I\>|)>>. Positivity of
    <math|<math-ss|Q>>, <math|J<rsub|<math-up|ax>>> and
    <math|<math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>> near zero permits the logarithms and
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-FP>; together these identities are the three
    lines of<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-one-loop-box>.
  </proof>

  The comparison uses the auxiliary fine operator and determinant

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|A><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>=<math-ss|A>+d<rsub|U>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>+<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>,<space|2em>\<Pi\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|U|)>=<tfrac|1|2>log
    det <math-ss|A><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|U|)>-log det
    <around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|U>+\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|U|)>|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<math-ss|A>> is the left-coordinate Hessian of the fine leading
  action and <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>> applies
  <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>> to each link component. Let
  <math|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>> be the infinite-volume quadratic Taylor kernel of
  <math|\<Pi\><rsub|\<mu\>>>. Write <math|u<rsub|j>> for the ratio threshold
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>; items<nbsp>5 and<nbsp>8 of
  Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices> choose odd ratios above it.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Block-to-fine coefficient comparison><label|lem:C6> For every odd
    <math|\<ell\>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|j>> as in items<nbsp>5 and<nbsp>8 of
    Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>, put
    <math|\<mu\>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>>. There is a constant
    <math|d<rsub|<math-up|cmp>>\<geqslant\>0>, depending only on the fixed
    stage bounds, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>-\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|<math-up|cmp>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The constant is independent of <math|N,p,\<ell\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    At one coarse momentum both operators act on the same joint space. Their
    difference either factors through a coarse space of dimension at most
    <math|4<dimG>> per cell or contains the regulator
    <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2><around|(|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|)><rsup|3>>.
    The first kind is bounded by finite rank, the second by the uniform trace
    bound for <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>>, so the logarithmic Hessians
    differ by a trace-class amount bounded independently
    of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>; no trace is estimated by the fine-cell dimension
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>>. We (1)<nbsp>fold <math|\<Pi\><rsub|\<mu\>>> to
    coarse momentum, which leaves <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>> unchanged;
    (2)<nbsp>remove the contact term, which leaves <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>>
    unchanged by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B6>; (3)<nbsp>bound the difference
    of logarithmic Hessians; (4)<nbsp>apply Cauchy's estimate. The input
    arrays satisfy Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>
    by<nbsp><math|<around|(|I<rsub|m+1>|)>>.

    <em|Step 1: folding.> Folding the auxiliary operator into coarse momentum
    does not alter<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>>. The symbol of the weights
    <math|w<rsup|<math-up|av>>> in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-predictor> vanishes at
    every nonzero alias to order at least two in each external leg, so these
    aliases contribute <math|O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|4>|)>>.
    The zero alias in the tested entry is

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsup|4>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*Y<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|\<xi\>/\<ell\>|)>+O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|3>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The factors are the cell volume and <math|\<ell\><rsup|-1>> per
    interpolated field. Extracting the quadratic coefficient supplies
    another<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>>, so
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>=1>.

    <em|Step 2: the contact term.> The full completed Hessian contains a
    contact term: the second derivative of the predictor multiplied by the
    first derivative of the penalty, the sum
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>d<rsup|\<lambda\>>*C<rsub|\<lambda\>>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-contact>. Along the free interpolation, write
    <math|<math-ss|a><around|(|\<xi\>|)>> for this penalty derivative and
    <math|<math-ss|b><around|(|\<xi\>|)>> for the mixed fine/frame trace.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B6> gives <math|<math-ss|a><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|2>|)>>
    and <math|<math-ss|b><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>|)>>.
    The change in the tested entry on removing the contact term consists of
    <math|<math-ss|ab>>, its reflected counterpart, and a term quadratic
    in<nbsp><math|<math-ss|a>>, with bounded analytic factors. It is
    <math|O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|3>|)>+O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|4>|)>>
    and has zero<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>>. Write
    <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>> for
    <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>> with the contact term removed.

    <em|Step 3: the joint-space difference.> At one coarse momentum let
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|f>> be the fine-link fibre and
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|0>> the coarse-frame fibre, of
    dimension<nbsp><math|<dimG>>. Let <math|P<rsub|f>:\<cal-V\><rsub|f>\<oplus\>\<cal-V\><rsub|0>\<to\>\<cal-V\><rsub|f>>
    and <math|Z:\<cal-V\><rsub|f>\<oplus\>\<cal-V\><rsub|0>\<to\>\<cal-V\><rsub|0>>
    be the coordinate projections, and put

    <\equation*>
      <wide|<math-ss|A>|~><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>=P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|A><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>P<rsub|f>+Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z=<matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|A><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>>|<cell|0>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|I>>>>>,<space|2em>det
      <wide|<math-ss|A>|~><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>=det
      <math-ss|A><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Thus adjoining the frame identity leaves the auxiliary determinant
    unchanged. Let <math|H> be the differential of the logarithm of the
    framed predictor and <math|B> its coarse-link penalty Hessian; these are
    the two factors in the second-derivative chain rule after removal of the
    contact term. Put <math|K=\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*X>, and let
    <math|J<rsub|a>> select direction<nbsp><math|a> from fine links, so
    <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|\<mu\>>=<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsub|a><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*J<rsub|a>>.
    Then

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>>|<cell|=P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|A>P<rsub|f>+H<rsup|\<ast\>>*B*H+<around|(|d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+K|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+K|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<wide|<math-ss|A>|~><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>>|<cell|=P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|A>P<rsub|f>+P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsub|a><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*J<rsub|a>|)>*P<rsub|f>+Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z,>>>>
    </align*>

    and their difference is

    <\equation*>
      \<Theta\>=<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>-<wide|<math-ss|A>|~><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>=H<rsup|\<ast\>>*B*H-Z<rsup|\<ast\>>*Z+P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U>*K+K<rsup|\<ast\>>*d<rsub|U><rsup|\<ast\>>*P<rsub|f>+K<rsup|\<ast\>>*K-P<rsub|f><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>J<rsub|a><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>*J<rsub|a>|)>*P<rsub|f><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The fine Hessians cancel exactly. The first term factors through coarse
    links, the second through coarse frames. For compatible maps <math|V,W>
    through a coarse space of dimension<nbsp><math|r>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|V*W|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>r<around|\<\|\|\>|V|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|W|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em><around|\||<tr><rsub|<math-up|joint>><around|(|V*W|)>|\|>=<around|\||<tr><rsub|<math-up|coarse>><around|(|W*V|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<around|\<\|\|\>|W|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|V|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|r=4<dimG>> or <math|<dimG>>, independently
    of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>. Differentiating these factorizations through
    order two preserves the intermediate coarse spaces.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7> bounds their factors in the mutually dual
    block norms.

    The remaining terms keep a regulator factor.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>(i) bounds the trace norm
    of<nbsp><math|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>> on a complex strip and bounds
    <math|D<rsup|r>*P<rsub|\<mu\>>> for <math|r\<leqslant\>2>, independently
    of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>; Step<nbsp>6 of its proof sums the fine aliases
    <math|k<rsub|z>=<around|(|\<xi\>+2*\<pi\>*z|)>/\<ell\>> with weights
    <math|<around|(|1+<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-3>>. Two field
    derivatives of <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|\<mu\>>=\<mu\><rsup|2><around|(|P<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|3>|)><rsup|3>>
    leave one cube undifferentiated. Put that cube in trace norm and the
    other factors in operator norm; the bounds on
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*D<rsup|r><around|(|A<rsup|-1>|)>> in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B7> cancel the inverse scale before taking a
    trace. Applied to the factors of<nbsp><math|\<Theta\>>, these estimates
    give a constant<nbsp><math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>> for its trace norm through
    two field insertions, in the dual block norms, depending only on the
    fixed stage bounds. At each coarse momentum these bounds sum the fine
    aliases without a factor<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsup|4>>.

    Let <math|F<rsub|\<ast\>>,A<rsub|\<ast\>>> bound the inverses and the
    operators through two normalized insertions in the compatible block norms
    (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B5> and<nbsp><reference|lem:B7>). In
    <math|D<rsub|v>*D<rsub|w>*log det A=<tr><around|(|A<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|v*w>*A-A<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|v>*A*<space|0.17em>A<rsup|-1>*D<rsub|w>*A|)>>,
    telescoping the difference between <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>>
    and <math|<wide|<math-ss|A>|~><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>> with
    <math|<around|(|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|-1>-<around|(|<wide|<math-ss|A>|~><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|-1>=-<around|(|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|rem>><rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\><around|(|<wide|<math-ss|A>|~><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>|)><rsup|-1>>
    gives terms with exactly one factor <math|\<Theta\>>, or a derivative of
    it, in trace norm and only bounded factors otherwise; cyclically moving
    factors identifies the required dual norms. Hence the difference of the
    logarithmic Hessians is at most a fixed polynomial in
    <math|A<rsub|\<ast\>>,B<rsub|\<ast\>>,F<rsub|\<ast\>>>. The common ghost
    determinant cancels. Including the Taylor factors gives a
    bound<nbsp><math|C<rsub|\<ast\>>> for the analytic difference kernel on a
    strip of width<nbsp><math|\<zeta\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>, both
    independent of<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>. The inherited loop kernel has order
    four by<nbsp><math|<around|(|I<rsub|m+1>|)>>; pullback by the analytic
    interpolation <math|W<rsub|0>> preserves that order, so its
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>> is zero.

    <em|Step 4: Cauchy's estimate.> Restrict the tested entry <math|g> of the
    difference to <math|\<xi\>=t*e<rsub|1>>. Cauchy's coefficient inequality
    on <math|<around|\||t|\|>=\<zeta\><rsub|\<ast\>>/2> gives
    <math|<around|\||<around|[|t<rsup|2>|]>*g|\|>\<leqslant\>4*C<rsub|\<ast\>>/\<zeta\><rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>>
    and hence <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|g|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>8*C<rsub|\<ast\>>/\<zeta\><rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>>.
    This gives<nbsp><math|d<rsub|<math-up|cmp>>>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The one-loop coefficient><label|subsec:C-coefficient>

  The vertices of the fine action differ from Wilson's in momentum orders
  <math|3,2,1> for the quadratic, cubic and quartic terms
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>); the input arrays satisfy its hypotheses
  by<nbsp><math|<around|(|I<rsub|m+1>|)>>. The resulting logarithmic term is
  the part of the one-step drift which remains after the block ratio has been
  chosen.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One-loop coefficient><label|lem:C7> There is a constant
    <math|d<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>0>, depending only on the fixed stage data,
    such that, for every step <math|m+1\<to\>m> at stage<nbsp><math|j> with
    odd <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>u<rsub|j>> as in
    Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+<slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|j><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    It is independent of <math|N,p,\<ell\><rsub|m>> and the running coupling.
  </lemma>

  Thus one blocking step lowers the curvature-square coefficient by
  <math|<slope>log \<ell\><rsub|m>+O<around|(|1|)>>, with
  <math|<slope>=2*b<rsub|0>>: the one-loop coefficient of asymptotic freedom.
  The proof is the background-field computation<nbsp><cite|Abbott1982> on the
  lattice: gauge-field (vector) and ghost loops with two background
  insertions, where the vector vertex has an orbital part, equal to the ghost
  vertex, and a spin part.

  <\proof>
    The calculation pairs the one-loop vertices of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C5>, computes the leading bubble exactly, and
    compares the lattice integrand with it; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C6>
    then transfers the result to <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>. Choose a colour
    basis index <math|b>, external polarization<nbsp><math|e<rsub|2>> and
    external momentum <math|t*e<rsub|1>>. Signed permutations reduce other
    transverse axis pairs to this one. For nonzero loop momentum <math|k> the
    leading ghost vertex is <math|-2*i*k<rsub|2><math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|b><rsup|<g>>|)>>.
    The vector vertex has the same orbital term on its four components and
    the spin term

    <\equation*>
      2*i*t*<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|c*2>*\<delta\><rsub|c<rprime|'>*1>-\<delta\><rsub|c*1>*\<delta\><rsub|c<rprime|'>*2>|)><math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|b><rsup|<g>>|)><space|0.17em>,
    </equation*>

    with vector indices <math|c,c<rprime|'>>. Simplicity and the trace metric
    give <math|<tr><rsub|<g>><around|(|<math-up|ad><around|(|e<rsub|b><rsup|<g>>|)><rsup|2>|)>=-<casimir>>.
    The bubble pairs vertices at <math|t*e<rsub|1>> and <math|-t*e<rsub|1>>.
    The orbital factor <math|-2*i*k<rsub|2>> is unchanged when
    <math|k\<mapsto\>k+t*e<rsub|1>> and <math|t*e<rsub|1>\<mapsto\>-t*e<rsub|1>>.
    Put <math|S<rsub|<math-up|sp>>=E<rsub|21>-E<rsub|12>>. Since
    <math|<tr><space|0.17em>S<rsub|<math-up|sp>>=0>, the orbital\Uspin cross
    terms vanish; the spin product is <math|<around|(|2*i*t*S<rsub|<math-up|sp>>|)>*<around|(|-2*i*t*S<rsub|<math-up|sp>>|)>=4*t<rsup|2>*S<rsub|<math-up|sp>><rsup|2>>,
    with trace <math|-8*t<rsup|2>>. The bubble factors are

    <\equation*>
      <tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|contribution>>|<cell|<text|directional
      trace>>|<cell|<text|weight in ><tfrac|1|2>log det
      <math-ss|C><rsub|\<mu\>>-log det <math-ss|G><rsub|\<mu\>>>>|<row|<cell|<text|vector>>|<cell|4*<around|(|-2*i*k<rsub|2>|)><rsup|2>-8*t<rsup|2>>|<cell|-1/4>>|<row|<cell|<text|ghost>>|<cell|<around|(|-2*i*k<rsub|2>|)><rsup|2>>|<cell|+1/2>>>>>
    </equation*>

    Multiply by the colour trace, divide by
    <math|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>*<around|\||k+t*e<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|2>>, and
    take the coefficient of <math|t<rsup|2>>; the extraction of
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>> supplies the factor<nbsp><math|2>. Before taking
    the coefficient, ghost minus half vector is

    <\equation*>
      -<frac|4<casimir><around|(|k<rsub|2><rsup|2>+t<rsup|2>|)>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>*<around|\||k+t*e<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|2>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its quadratic coefficient is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:C-pole>h<around|(|k|)>=4<casimir><around*|(|<frac|k<rsub|2><rsup|2>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|6>>-<frac|4*k<rsub|2><rsup|2>*k<rsub|1><rsup|2>|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|8>>-<frac|1|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|4>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Expanding the denominator gives <math|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|-4>-2*t*k<rsub|1>\|k\|<rsup|-6>+t<rsup|2>*<around|(|4*k<rsub|1><rsup|2>\|k\|<rsup|-8>-<around|\||k|\|><rsup|-6>|)>+O<around|(|t<rsup|3>|)>>;
    substitution yields<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-pole> with its sign.

    Let <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>,b><around|(|k|)>> be the integrand of
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>, obtained with these
    weights and Taylor factors. Then

    <\equation*>
      \<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<big|int><rsub|<around|[|-\<pi\>,\<pi\>|]><rsup|4>><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>I<rsub|\<mu\>,b><around|(|k|)>*<space|0.17em>d*k<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For constants <math|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>,D<rsub|<math-up|int>>\<geqslant\>0>
    depending only on the fixed fine-stage bounds, uniformly in
    <math|\<mu\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:C-integrand><around|\||I<rsub|\<mu\>,b><around|(|k|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>|<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|4>>,<space|2em><around|\||I<rsub|\<mu\>,b><around|(|k|)>-h<around|(|k|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>D<rsub|<math-up|int>><around*|(|<around|\||k*<around|\||<rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|\|>*k|\|><rsup|-6>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||k|\|>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    To obtain them, use <math|q<rsub|a><around|(|k|)>=i*k<rsub|a>+O<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>|)>>
    and the vertex orders above. For the free inverse
    <math|R<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|k|)>>, differentiation of
    <math|R<rsub|\<mu\>><math-ss|A><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|\<mu\>>=I> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-2>,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\>*R<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-3>,<space|1em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|2>*R<rsub|\<mu\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Each first-order vertex has order <math|<around|\||k|\|>>, with bounded
    first external derivative. In a bubble, two momentum derivatives on one
    inverse give <math|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-2>*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4>\<leqslant\><around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4>>;
    moving derivatives to vertices gives the same bound. On
    <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>>, a lattice-to-leading
    replacement gains one power of<nbsp><math|<around|\||k|\|>>, giving
    <math|C<around|\||k|\|><rsup|-3>>. A regulator insertion contributes at
    most <math|C*\<mu\><rsup|2><around|\||k|\|><rsup|-6>>.

    The terms with one two-insertion (seagull) vertex are absent
    from<nbsp><math|h>. Their quadratic coefficients have bounds

    <\equation*>
      C*<around*|(|<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-2>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-6>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The first term comes from one inverse and a bounded seagull-vertex
    derivative; the second retains the regulator
    factor<nbsp><math|\<mu\><rsup|2>> with the differentiated inverse bounds.
    On the cube, <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>2*\<pi\>> and
    <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>, so this is at most
    <math|C<rprime|'>*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-4>>. On
    <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<geqslant\>\<mu\>>, it is at most
    <math|C*<around|(|2*\<pi\>*<around|\||k*<around|\||<rsup|-3>+\<mu\><rsup|2>|\|>*k|\|><rsup|-6>|)>>.
    Thus these terms satisfy both inequalities
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand>. The product rule gives the same bounds
    for the ninth-power regulator. Summing
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand>.

    On the spherical shell <math|\<mu\>\<less\><around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>1>,
    rotational symmetry gives angular means
    <math|k<rsub|2><rsup|2>/<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>=1/4> and
    <math|k<rsub|2><rsup|2>*k<rsub|1><rsup|2>/<around|\||k|\|><rsup|4>=1/24>.
    For the latter, write the fourth moments as
    <math|m<rsub|4>=\<bbb-E\>*\<theta\><rsub|1><rsup|4>> and
    <math|m<rsub|22>=\<bbb-E\>*\<theta\><rsub|1><rsup|2>*\<theta\><rsub|2><rsup|2>>
    on the unit sphere: rotation by <math|\<pi\>/4> gives
    <math|m<rsub|4>=3*m<rsub|22>>, and <math|<around|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<theta\><rsub|i><rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>=1>
    gives <math|4*m<rsub|4>+12*m<rsub|22>=1>. Since the sphere has area
    <math|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>>,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<big|int><rsub|\<mu\>\<less\><around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>1>h<around|(|k|)>*<space|0.17em>d*k>|<cell|=<frac|8*\<pi\><rsup|2><casimir>|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|4>>*<around*|(|<frac|1|4>-<frac|1|6>-1|)>*log
      \<mu\><rsup|-1><eq-number><label|eq:C-slope>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=-<frac|11<casimir>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*log
      \<mu\><rsup|-1>=-<slope>log \<mu\><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align>

    The errors in<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand> have shell integrals at most
    <math|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>*D<rsub|<math-up|int>>> and
    <math|\<pi\><rsup|2>*D<rsub|<math-up|int>>>. On
    <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>> the bound is
    <math|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>*\<mu\><rsup|-4>> and the volume is
    <math|\<pi\><rsup|2>*\<mu\><rsup|4>/2>; outside
    <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<leqslant\>1> the bound is
    <math|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|b><around|(|Y<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>+<slope>log
      \<mu\><rsup|-1>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4>*<around|{|A<rsub|<math-up|int>>*\<pi\><rsup|2>/2+A<rsub|<math-up|int>>*<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|4>+3*\<pi\><rsup|2>*D<rsub|<math-up|int>>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Combine this bound with Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C6> and put
    <math|\<mu\>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>>. Their sum gives<nbsp><math|d<rsub|j>>.
    The computed slope is <math|<slope>=2*b<rsub|0>> of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>, with its stated trace metric.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>>
    The row normalization comes from the free forms of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>; the loop cancellations from the symmetry
    of the one-loop kernel and the two-leg curl division of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C3>; the bounds from the classical matching of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4> and the one-loop bound of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>. Start at <math|m=N> and descend. The
    condition <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,k>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|k>>
    supplies the positive row factor at every level. Its zero-momentum
    entries give

    <\equation*>
      <wide|<math-ss|H>|^><around|(|0|)><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*\<delta\><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<frak-p\>>s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-q\>>*s<rsub|\<frak-p\>*\<frak-r\>>=\<delta\><rsub|\<frak-q\>*\<frak-r\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This proves the first cancellation and also forces
    <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\>1> by taking a diagonal
    entry.

    Subtract <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> times the kinetic kernel from the
    quadratic kernel of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|T>>. The residual is transverse.
    Its colour bilinear form is scalar: its self-adjoint representing
    operator commutes with the adjoint action, and its eigenspaces are
    ideals, so simplicity leaves one eigenvalue. Signed permutations identify
    the diagonal orientation coefficients. Reflection in an axis belonging to
    exactly one of two distinct orientations cancels the off-diagonal
    coefficient. Thus there is one degree-two coefficient, removed by the
    subtraction. Reflection through the origin removes degree three. The
    residual link kernel consequently vanishes to order four.

    Apply the two-leg part of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C3>. Removing its two
    curl symbols leaves a plaquette kernel of order two. If its Fourier
    coefficients for a fixed orientation-colour pair are
    <math|a<rsub|<math-up|loop>><around|(|z|)>>, then

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|z>a<rsub|<math-up|loop>><around|(|z|)>=0,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|z>z<rsub|a>*a<rsub|<math-up|loop>><around|(|z|)>=0*<space|1em><around|(|a=1,\<ldots\>,4|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Exponential decay permits termwise differentiation. Put the coefficients
    on coordinate paths to obtain the quadratic atoms and their absolutely
    convergent moments. Inherited loop atoms already have these moments,
    hence a link kernel of order four. Pullback by the free interpolation
    preserves this order. Only the quadratic Taylor kernel
    of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|T>> is assigned to these atoms; its higher terms
    enter the preparatory expansion of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>.

    For the bounds, contour displacement and the path-length estimate after
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C3> give a positive common decay rate for the
    top construction and the two possible incoming stages <math|k,k+1>.
    Choose <math|0\<less\>w<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|path>>\<less\>w<rsub|k><rprime|'>>
    below that rate, and bound the series at <math|w<rsub|k><rprime|'>> when
    choosing <math|A<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|path>>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4> permits this choice before the
    corresponding ratios, by making the input-dependent terms at most one.
    Lowering the width gives the bounds at
    <math|w<rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|path>>>. Make the following loop estimates
    at <math|w<rsub|k><rprime|'>> as well, so that the quadratic bound
    retains the same strict inequality between widths.

    The new loop contribution has a weighted bound
    <math|a<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>*\<ell\><rsup|4>+b<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>*log
    \<ell\>+c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>>. Here <math|\<ell\><rsup|4>> bounds
    the trace dimension per coarse cell, and the logarithm comes from the
    subtracted coefficient. For an inherited order-four link kernel,
    rescaling gives

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsup|4>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*O<around|(|<around|\||\<xi\>/\<ell\>|\|><rsup|4>|)>=O<around|(|\<ell\><rsup|-2><around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|4>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its weighted pullback is therefore bounded by
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pb>>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>.
    Choose <math|\<ell\>> so that <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pb>>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1/4>,
    then raise the coupling entry threshold until

    <\equation*>
      2*<around|(|a<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>*\<ell\><rsup|4>+b<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>*log
      \<ell\>+c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|lp>>|)>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>\<leqslant\>2*\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The sum is at most <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>+<tfrac|1|4>\<cdot\>2*\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>=\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>.
    These choices are in the order of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>:
    widths and amplitudes, ratios, then entry thresholds. The first
    inequality is possible because <math|2<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>\<to\>\<infty\>>
    as <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> with
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>,J<rsub|m>> fixed. For the second, antitonicity
    of<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|k>>, <math|J<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>+1>,
    and <math|\<theta\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>1> give, once
    <math|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>\<gtr\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>|\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>\<leqslant\><around*|(|<frac|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>>|)><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>\<leqslant\><frac|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>>\<longrightarrow\>1
    </equation*>

    as <math|<refbeta><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> with <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>>
    fixed. The descent starts with zero loops and the fourth-order Wilson
    matching of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>. The kernels and paths are
    chosen once for all periods. Finally <math|<around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|s>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>
    and <math|<around|\||\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|U,x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|k><around|\||\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>|\|>>
    give absolute convergence of the action.
  </render-proof>

  <section|Activities and the inductive step><label|sec:partII-D>

  An exact block integral contains interactions on arbitrarily large
  supports. We express them as a sum of regular activities and a
  disjoint-support sum of forced activities, the large-field polymer
  activities. Regular terms are smooth where their supports are healthy;
  forced terms retain configurations with flagged plaquettes. Their norms
  serve different purposes: derivatives are required of the first family,
  whereas the second is estimated uniformly over configurations. The
  estimates and the exact density identity enter
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>; the Wilson representation starts the
  recursion and supplies the initial curve of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>.

  The step has five stages, and the sizes explain why each is needed. Write
  <math|\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>>; the output regular activities must
  be bounded by <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-11/10>>.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><em|Connected expansion> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>).
    The block integral is a polymer gas whose elementary factors are
    <math|O<around|(|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>|)>>. Its logarithm
    has regular terms of total size <math|O<around|(|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>|)>>,
    which is too large, and forced terms of size
    <math|O<around|(|e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>|)>>.

    <item><em|Linear terms> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited>). An
    incoming regular activity has zero value, gradient and affine Hessian, so
    after blocking its leading part has scaling weight five:
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-5>> per fine anchor, a contraction
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>> per coarse anchor.

    <item><em|Low-order terms> (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>\U<reference|lem:D-preliminary>).
    Terms with one or two elementary factors are only
    <math|O<around|(|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>|)>> individually. Their sum is the
    logarithm of a local Gaussian integral; after flat subtraction, parity
    and the classical and one-loop terms of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>, it is
    <math|O<around|(|\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>|)>> up to subpower factors. We
    localize it by a fourth difference over five reflected tori. Terms with
    three or more factors are already <math|O<around|(|\<frak-b\><rsup|-19/16>|)>>
    up to subpower factors.

    <item><em|Curvature-square subtraction>
    (Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-subtraction>). The remaining affine
    Hessians are subtracted locally; lattice symmetry collects them into one
    coefficient<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>, added to the coupling.

    <item><em|Fusion and reset> (Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-fusion>).
    The resulting mask discrepancies vanish on healthy supports and are
    absorbed into the forced activities, giving the exact density
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5>). The scalar reset
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>) then reads <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> off
    at one test configuration and bounds the drift.
  </enumerate>

  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-continuity> proves continuity at fixed
  volume, Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-Wilson> treats the Wilson initial
  condition, and Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-recursion> fixes the step
  on whole curves and proves Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>.
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-analytic> proves the analytic estimates
  used in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E> for endpoint tuning and the
  fixed-volume limit.

  Throughout, the group and trace metric are those of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>. Scales, flags, path actions and
  densities are those of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization> and
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D06>, <reference|def:D22>\U<reference|def:D23>.
  At a step <math|m+1\<to\>m> put <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>,
  <math|\<Lambda\>=\<Lambda\><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>>, <math|t=t<rsub|m>>,
  <math|B=B<rsub|m>> and <math|\<sigma\>=\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>. Set
  <math|h<rsub|X><around|(|U|)>=\<b-1\>*<around|{|X\<cap\><flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=\<varnothing\>|}>>.
  Constants may depend on the fixed stage data and, when indicated, on the
  chosen ratio, but are independent of the volume. Here <math|C<rsub|j>> is a
  generic stage constant.

  <paragraph|Local terms and their norms.>

  An activity is a local function of the links, attached to a finite support,
  whose sums make up the effective density. Locality on <math|X> means
  dependence only on links with both endpoints in <math|X>. Write
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|o><around|(|y|)>> for the centered lift of <math|y-o> to
  <math|<Z><rsup|4>>. All lattice distances in this section use the maximum
  norm. Set <math|N<rsub|R><around|(|X|)>=<around|{|y:<math-up|dist><around|(|y,X|)>\<leqslant\>R|}>>
  and <math|N<rsub|R><around|(|o|)>=N<rsub|R><around|(|<around|{|o|}>|)>>. A
  link internal to <math|X> has both endpoints in <math|X>. The nonwrapping
  condition is <math|10<math-up|dist><around|(|y,o|)>\<less\>L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>
  for every <math|y\<in\>X>; it permits local affine tests without crossing a
  choice of lift.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Regular activities><label|def:D24> A regular activity is a Borel
    real gauge-invariant function <math|E<rsub|i>>, local on <math|X<rsub|i>>
    and anchored at <math|o<rsub|i>\<in\>X<rsub|i>>. It satisfies
    <math|E<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>=0> and is
    <math|C<rsup|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>> in exponential left-link
    increments on healthy configurations, with all ordered derivatives. On
    nonwrapping supports the Hessian of <math|A\<mapsto\>E<rsub|i><around|(|exp
    A|)>> at zero vanishes on every pair of affine directions

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|<around|(|y,a|)>>=u<rsub|a>+<big|sum><rsub|c>\<xi\><rsub|o<rsub|i>><around|(|y|)><rsup|c>*v<rsub|a*c>,<space|2em>u<rsub|a>,v<rsub|a*c>\<in\><g><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|<around|(|exp A|)><rsub|e>=exp <around|(|A<rsub|e>|)>>. This
    removes the affine quadratic contribution from the error.
  </definition>

  Together with <math|E<rsub|i><around|(|1|)>=0> and the vanishing first
  derivative, which is automatic by gauge invariance (proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-local-subtraction>), this says that
  <math|E<rsub|i>> has no relevant or marginal part: its local constant and
  its curvature-square coefficient have been extracted, the analogue of the
  localization operator of<nbsp><cite|BauerschmidtBrydgesSlade2019>.

  For forced terms the inventory specifies exactly the flags inside the
  support. Additional tests distinguish local alternatives with the same
  support and inventory.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Forced activities><label|def:D25> A forced activity with address
    <math|<around|(|Y<rsub|i>,I<rsub|i>,\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|extra>>|)>>,
    where <math|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I<rsub|i>\<subset\>Y<rsub|i>>, is a Borel
    real gauge-invariant function <math|K<rsub|i>> that depends only on the
    links internal to <math|Y<rsub|i>> and the flags in <math|Y<rsub|i>>, and
    vanishes outside the eligibility event
    <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|elig>>>
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-forced-eligibility>. Its extra test is Borel, gauge
    invariant, and reads the same links and flags. These conditions restrict
    the forced sum to the prescribed large-field inventory.
  </definition>

  The address of a regular activity is the pair
  <math|<around|(|X<rsub|i>,o<rsub|i>|)>>; that of a forced activity is the
  triple <math|<around|(|Y<rsub|i>,I<rsub|i>,\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|extra>>|)>>,
  and its eligibility indicator is <math|\<b-1\><rsub|\<cal-E\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|elig>>>>.

  Three sets attached to the large-field sites, the flags, play different
  roles. The support <math|Y<rsub|i>> carries the forced activity. The
  inventory <math|I<rsub|i>\<subset\>Y<rsub|i>> lists the flagged sites that
  the eligibility event requires in <math|Y<rsub|i>>, exactly those and no
  others. The complete read support of a factor in the expansion (defined
  before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>) is the region that factor inspects;
  polymers are formed through overlaps of complete read supports, and a
  support <math|Y<rsub|i>> need not be connected. Deciding the flags at the
  sites of <math|Y<rsub|i>> reads plaquettes within distance one of
  <math|Y<rsub|i>>, so the read regions of two disjoint supports may overlap
  although the supports do not. For example, let exactly two distant
  plaquettes have chord above <math|t<rsub|m>>, so that the flags are their
  vertex sets <math|F<rsub|1>> and <math|F<rsub|2>>. A selection may use one
  forced term whose support contains both, with inventory
  <math|F<rsub|1>\<cup\>F<rsub|2>>, or two terms with disjoint supports and
  inventories <math|F<rsub|1>> and <math|F<rsub|2>>. Both alternatives occur
  in the exact-cover sum of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>; in each
  selection every flag is covered exactly once, and no canonical covering is
  chosen. A term whose support contains <math|F<rsub|1>\<cup\>F<rsub|2>> but
  whose inventory is <math|F<rsub|1>> is ineligible at this configuration and
  vanishes. Forced fusion (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion>) groups
  overlap components together with the logarithmic factors meeting them and
  recovers every selection uniquely.

  We use the ordered derivative seminorm and weighted sums of
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>. Regular sums
  are bounded at their anchors; forced sums are bounded at every site of
  their supports, so the factor <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Y|)>>>
  charges each support for its size. For a function <math|f> on <math|Z>
  anchored at <math|o>, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,Z>> denotes
  the seminorm of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a> with that support and
  anchor.

  Continuity concerns the coefficients at fixed addresses. Health sets remain
  fixed as the curve parameter varies.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Continuity of representations><label|def:D25a> A continuous curve
    has fixed activity addresses and tests, and continuous coupling and
    vacuum scalar. At each <math|u<rsub|0>>, differences of regular
    derivatives and forced values admit nonnegative majorants
    <math|e<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>,k<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>> in the sense of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, with

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*e<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i><around|(|u|)>\<longrightarrow\>0*<space|1em><around|(|u\<to\>u<rsub|0>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Both sums run over all addresses. This is continuity for coupling tuning,
    with no derivative of a flag indicator.
  </definition>

  At fixed volume these sums control the density:

  <\gather*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-E\><rsub|m>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i>sup
    <around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-K\><rsub|m>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|<math-up|selections>><big|prod><rsub|i>sup
    <around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|i><around|(|1+sup
    <around|\||K<rsub|i>|\|>|)>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-K\><rsub|m>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </gather*>

  The first inequality sums the regular bounds over anchors, the second the
  forced bounds over sites, since each forced support is nonempty; the third
  sum runs over all finite selections. Restricting to disjoint supports and
  exact coverage preserves the third bound, so the density sums of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06> converge. No volume-independent bound
  on the full density follows.

  <paragraph|Coefficient algebras.> Several estimates are applied to factors
  with coefficients in a commutative algebra; this is how observable
  insertions are carried through the expansion in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>. Let
  <math|z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|n>> be commuting marks with
  <math|z<rsub|i><rsup|2>=0>, the nilpotent external fields
  of<nbsp><cite|BauerschmidtBrydgesSlade2019>. An element of the coefficient
  algebra is <math|a=<big|sum><rsub|I>a<rsub|I>*z<rsub|I>>, where
  <math|I\<subset\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>,
  <math|z<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>z<rsub|i>> is a mode and
  <math|a<rsub|I>> is real or complex. With mark weights
  <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> and <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I><math-ss|w><rsub|i>>,
  put <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>>=<big|sum><rsub|I><around|\||a<rsub|I>|\|><math-ss|w><rsub|I>>.
  Since <math|z<rsub|I>*z<rsub|J>=z<rsub|I\<cup\>J>> for disjoint <math|I,J>
  and <math|z<rsub|I>*z<rsub|J>=0> otherwise,
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|a*b|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|a|\<\|\|\>><around|\<\|\|\>|b|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Taking the coefficient of a mode is a finite linear operation and commutes
  with integration; for one mark, the coefficient of <math|z> in
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|K<rsub|j>+z*L<rsub|j>|)>> is
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>L<rsub|j>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<ne\>j>K<rsub|i>>. The
  case <math|n=0> is the algebra of complex coefficients.

  <paragraph|Support exponents.> The output norms use
  <math|\<sigma\>=\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>=100>. Intermediate estimates
  carry up to three spare units: Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> gives
  regular terms at <math|\<sigma\>+3> and forced terms at <math|\<sigma\>+2>,
  and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary> keeps <math|\<sigma\>+3> and
  <math|\<sigma\>+1>. The spare units pay for converting anchored sums to
  site sums (a support with covering number <math|n> has at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4>> possible
  anchors; the factor <math|<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|n>>
  is absorbed by one spare unit, while <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>> is
  paid by the smallness of the activities), for collecting logarithmic
  corrections into the forced sum, and for fusion. Independently, the rooted
  clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> holds with every exponent
  lowered by two; Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E> uses this in its
  marked estimates.

  <subsection|The local expansion><label|subsec:D-local>

  Fix a compact parameter interval and a continuous input curve
  <math|\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>> in the sense of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25a>. Its prescribed path satisfies
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>; its regular and forced
  families satisfy Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D25>
  and the caps of Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>;
  and <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>. Write
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u>=<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>>,
  and split the path action as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>. Write
  <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|2,m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>> for
  <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>> without its cubic and
  quartic atoms; it is still nonlinear in the links. The coefficient
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> is the quadratic coefficient of the determinant
  comparison in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>. It is independent of
  the curve parameter and the period.

  For <math|a\<less\>b> define the clover curvature, the clover-leaf field
  strength of lattice gauge theory, by

  <\equation*>
    F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|a*b>*<around|(|U,o|)>=<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,\<eta\>=\<pm\>1>\<varepsilon\>*\<eta\><space|0.17em><math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><frac|U<rsub|o;\<varepsilon\>*a,\<eta\>*b>-U<rsub|o;\<varepsilon\>*a,\<eta\>*b><rsup|-1>|2><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The loop starts at <math|o>, takes steps
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*e<rsub|a>,\<eta\>*e<rsub|b>,-\<varepsilon\>*e<rsub|a>,-\<eta\>*e<rsub|b>>,
  and returns to <math|o>. Thus each term transforms by the adjoint action at
  <math|o> and uses links within distance two. For an affine field as in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>, differentiating the four link factors
  gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-clover-linear><around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*z>|\|><rsub|z=0>*F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|a*b>*<around|(|e<rsup|z*A>,o|)>=v<rsub|b*a>-v<rsub|a*b><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Each inverse contributes the negative link field; the four orientations
  have the same signed curl.

  Expanding each row square <math|<frac|1|2><around|\||\<cal-L\><rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|\<frak-p\>>|\|><rsup|2>>
  into the products <math|<frac|1|2>*r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>*r<rsub|\<frak-p\>*k>*<around|\<langle\>|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*i>,\<cal-F\><rsub|\<frak-p\>*k>|\<rangle\>>>
  and adding the direct plaquette terms gives a countable family of local
  energy terms indexed by <math|\<lambda\>>; translations and the
  <math|<hypercubic>> average, with weight
  <math|<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|><rsup|-1>>, are included in the addresses.
  Each term has three versions: <math|L<rsub|\<lambda\>>>, the actual clipped
  or threshold-tested term; <math|L<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|\<lambda\>>>,
  the same quadratic curvature function without clip or test; and
  <math|L<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|\<lambda\>>>, the function
  <math|L<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|\<lambda\>>> with each curvature replaced
  by the clover curvature of its orientation at the anchor. For a direct term
  <math|L<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|\<lambda\>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>>
  and <math|L<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|\<lambda\>>=<frac|1|2>*c<rsub|<math-up|dir>>*<around|\||F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|q>*<around|(|U,o<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>|\|><rsup|2>>.

  Choose <math|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>> to contain the slot footprints and the
  radius-<math|B> neighborhood of the anchor needed to decide the relaxed
  threshold test. Queries outside that neighborhood are automatic once

  <\equation*>
    t*exp <around|(|<around|(|B-2|)>/R<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|rel>>|)>\<geqslant\>2<space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  since chords are at most two. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>
  give absolute convergence of <math|<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>L<rsub|\<lambda\>>+\<cal-A\><rsub|m>>,
  where <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|m>> is the sum of the cubic and quartic atoms.
  Define the masked quadratic functional and record the clover identity:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-low-sums>\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>=<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*L<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|\<lambda\>>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|\<lambda\>>|}>>*L<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|\<lambda\>>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|o>h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*<big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|q>*<around|(|U,o|)>|\|><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  A signed image in these formulas uses the pulled-back anchor, support and
  clover; the second equality follows from the coefficient identity of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> and reindexing orientations. On
  <math|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>> healthy, <math|L<rsub|\<lambda\>>=L<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|\<lambda\>>>;
  without flags, <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>=<math-ss|K><rsub|2,m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>>.

  The local integral is expanded before any regular term is normalized. Its
  elementary factors <math|F<rsub|a>> are: (i) the remainder of the
  stationary action; (ii) the variation <math|<math-up|e><rsup|-<around|[|\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m+1><around|(|U<rsub|v>|)>|]>>>
  of the inherited loop action about the stationary configuration
  <math|U<rsub|v>>; (iii) the determinant and covariance ratios and the Haar
  Jacobian; (iv) the incoming regular and forced activities; (v) the cutoff
  remainders of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>, that is, the failures of the
  fluctuation, chart, tag and inventory tests. Put
  <math|W<rsub|a>=F<rsub|a>-1>, except <math|W<rsub|a>=K<rsub|a>> for an
  incoming forced activity. The support of a factor includes every field,
  flag and covariance window it reads. We join two factors when their
  complete read supports, as defined before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>,
  intersect. A polymer is a finite product of factors whose complete read
  supports have connected intersection graph; we use only the polymers
  allowed by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>, which have disjoint incoming
  forced supports, inventories covered by the corners of the selected fine
  plaquettes <math|A<rsub|f><around|(|M|)>>, and the component compatibility
  there. The selection retains these supports and inventories. A polymer is
  <em|ordinary> if its complete read supports also avoid the output flags.

  For an ordinary polymer <math|M> on a healthy output support, its
  integrated weight and an absolute derivative majorant are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-seed-required>z<rsub|M><around|(|U|)>=<big|int><big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>M>W<rsub|a>*<around|(|U,\<zeta\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<zeta\>|)>,<space|2em><big|int>G<rsub|M>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>M>f<rsub|a><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<nu\>> is the single sector Gaussian/Haar law of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>, <math|G<rsub|M>> majorizes the product and
  its required ordered derivatives, and <math|f<rsub|a>> are that lemma's
  factor bounds. The integer <math|s<rsub|M>> is the sum of the numbers of
  radius-<math|B> blocks covering the complete read supports, including
  retained path lengths in the same units. The function <math|P<rsub|j>> has
  subpower growth after the ratio has been fixed. The formula is used only on
  compatible sectors; their sum retains the exact allocation indicators.
  Terms whose reads meet an output flag component remain attached to that
  component and have a nonempty forced inventory. The ordinary connected
  integrals are taken on the complement, where their read supports are
  disjoint from the output flags. Components with empty inventory are used in
  the regular logarithm on their healthy supports.

  The tree sums of Sections<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-local>\U<reference|subsec:D-fusion>
  are instances of one estimate. A support covered by <math|n>
  radius-<math|B> balls has at most <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*n>
  sites; this converts a site bound into an attachment bound.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Rooted polymer bound><label|lem:D-rooted> Let
    <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|a>,v<rsub|a>|)><rsub|a>> be a countable family of
    nonempty finite supports with weights <math|v<rsub|a>\<geqslant\>0>, and
    let <math|\<eta\>\<gtr\>0> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:<space|0.17em>x\<in\>Z<rsub|a>>v<rsub|a>*e<rsup|\<eta\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|a>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For a support <math|Z<rsub|\<star\>>> with weight
    <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>\<geqslant\>0>, let <math|T<rsub|\<star\>>> be the
    sum over finite rooted trees with root
    <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|\<star\>>,v<rsub|\<star\>>|)>> and other vertices
    labelled by the family, repetitions allowed, in which every child support
    meets its parent's; a tree contributes the product of its weights, and
    each set of <math|k> children of a vertex the factor <math|1/k>!. Then
    <math|T<rsub|\<star\>>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|\<star\>>*e<rsup|\<eta\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|\<star\>>|)>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Let <math|T<rsup|<around|(|h|)>>> denote the sum over trees of height at
    most <math|h>. The exponential formula for unordered children gives
    <math|T<rsub|\<star\>><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=v<rsub|\<star\>>> and
    <math|T<rsub|\<star\>><rsup|<around|(|h+1|)>>=v<rsub|\<star\>>*exp
    <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|a:Z<rsub|a>\<cap\>Z<rsub|\<star\>>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>T<rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|h|)>>|)>>.
    If <math|T<rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|h|)>>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|a>*e<rsup|\<eta\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|a>|)>>>
    for every label, the exponent is at most
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>Z<rsub|\<star\>>><big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a>>v<rsub|a>*e<rsup|\<eta\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|a>|)>>\<leqslant\><around|\||Z<rsub|\<star\>>|\|>*\<eta\>*<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|-4>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|\<star\>>|)>>.
    Induction on <math|h> and monotone convergence prove the bound.
  </proof>

  Every connected family of labels containing a distinguished label has a
  spanning tree rooted there, so the lemma dominates connected sums of
  nonnegative majorants. In applications the factor
  <math|e<rsup|\<eta\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|a>|)>>> is paid by one
  reserved unit of support exponent.

  We now give the connected identity with its initial estimates. For a
  support exponent <math|\<tau\>> let <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|\<tau\>>,\<cal-K\><rsub|\<tau\>>>
  be the sums of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05b> with <math|\<sigma\>>
  replaced by <math|\<tau\>>. In the rooted clause below,
  <math|W<rsub|\<star\>>> is a further elementary factor with complete read
  support <math|Z<rsub|\<star\>>>, <math|n<rsub|\<star\>>=<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|\<star\>>|)>>,
  and integrated majorant <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>>, including its support
  weight and required derivatives; any retained forced suppression of a
  selected large-field test is kept separately. The other factors have
  nonnegative majorants <math|v<rsub|a>> on their complete read supports
  <math|Z<rsub|a>>, including the reserved support weights. Lowering the
  support exponents in (d) includes the one in <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>>. Let
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|<math-up|reg>><around|(|W<rsub|\<star\>>|)>> and
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|<math-up|for>><around|(|W<rsub|\<star\>>|)>> be the
  absolute sums, at exponents <math|\<sigma\>+3> and <math|\<sigma\>+2>, of
  the connected terms containing one distinguished occurrence of
  <math|W<rsub|\<star\>>>, counted once. The expansion produces a scalar
  <math|\<tau\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|old>>>, local functions
  <math|f<rsub|a><rsup|0>> on <math|X<rsub|a>>, regular before flat-value and
  Hessian subtraction, and forced activities
  <math|k<rsub|i,u><rsup|<math-up|con>>> with exact-cover sum
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|con>>>; weighted-continuous means
  continuous in the weighted norms of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25a>.
  The stronger regular estimate needed for subtraction is obtained in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Connected local expansion><label|lem:D-connected> There are
    <math|C<rsub|j>,r<rsub|j>,\<eta\>\<gtr\>0>, depending only on the stage
    and ratio, such that for every input curve:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(a)>These exist, with fixed countable addresses, equivariant and
      weighted-continuous, such that everywhere

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-H\><rsub|u>=exp <space|-0.17em><around*|{|\<tau\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|old>>\|\<Lambda\>\|-\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m>-\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>-<big|sum><rsub|a>h<rsub|X<rsub|a>>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>|}>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|con>>,
      </equation*>

      with absolutely convergent sums.

      <item*|(b)><math|\<cal-E\><rsub|\<sigma\>+3><around|(|f<rsup|0>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|m+1><rsup|-7/16>>
      and <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<sigma\>+2><around|(|k<rsup|<math-up|con>>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>>.

      <item*|(c)>If <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a>>v<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>>, then
      <math|max <around|{|\<cal-R\><rsub|<math-up|reg>><around|(|W<rsub|\<star\>>|)>,e<rsup|r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>*\<cal-R\><rsub|<math-up|for>><around|(|W<rsub|\<star\>>|)>|}>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|\<star\>>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*n<rsub|\<star\>>>>.

      <item*|(d)>Parts (b) and (c) hold coefficientwise for factors in a
      coefficient algebra, and with every support exponent lowered by two.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The block kernel of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04> turns the density
    into a product of elementary factors; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>
    integrates their products under one Gaussian law and factorizes disjoint
    supports, and the local energy of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1> pays
    for the large-field components.

    <em|Step 1: polymer gas.> Expand finite products first. Fubini's theorem
    applies to each term by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-seed-required>. Disjoint
    complete read supports use disjoint Gaussian marginal blocks and Haar
    variables, so their integrals multiply. This gives a gas of the connected
    weights <math|z<rsub|M>>. Overlapping factors are always integrated
    together.

    <em|Step 2: elementary factors.> Put <math|\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>>
    and <math|g=<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>|)><rsup|-1/2>>. For a
    healthy local chart write its link logarithm as <math|m<rsub|Y>+g*z>; the
    mean is <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>|)>> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>. The following bounds include the
    prescribed finite number of ordered derivatives. A factor
    <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>> below absorbs fixed powers of
    <math|B> and <math|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>> after the ratio is fixed;
    read-count powers remain in <math|<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>>
    as in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-seed-required>:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-factor-rates><tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|<text|factor>>|<cell|<text|integrated
      majorant>>>|<row|<cell|<text|stationary-action
      remainder>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*g<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>P<rprime|'><rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|centered
      determinant remainder>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>\<leqslant\>P<rprime|'><rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|Gaussian-density
      and stationarity residuals>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|res>>>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|tail>>*B>>>|<row|<cell|<text|Haar-Jacobian
      remainder>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)><rsup|2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|inherited
      loop variation>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>*g\<leqslant\>P<rprime|'><rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>>|<row|<cell|<text|incoming
      regular sum>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>>|<row|<cell|<text|incoming
      forced component>>|<cell|P<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|for>>>>|<row|<cell|<text|cutoff
      remainder>>|<cell|M<rsub|w>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>>>>>
    </equation>

    For the first row, Taylor's formula after the stationary quadratic part
    starts with <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*<around|(|m<rsub|Y>+g*z|)><rsup|3>>;
    substituting <math|m<rsub|Y>=O<around|(|g<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)>> gives
    the displayed bound. For the determinant, subtract its flat value and the
    quadratic term already assigned to <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>>.
    The inverse identity bounds the remaining variation by
    <math|P<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>>. The covariance and
    stationary residuals are exponentially small by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>; their rescaling costs only the
    displayed fixed power. The chart density has value one and zero first
    derivative at zero, giving its quadratic bound. The inherited loop action
    is quadratic in slot curvatures with weighted coefficient sum at most
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>; on the chart each slot is
    <math|O<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g<around|\||z|\|>|)>> and moves by
    <math|O<around|(|g<around|\||z|\|>|)>>, which gives its row. Incoming
    sums retain their own allowances. Chart-test failures, fine-plaquette and
    mismatch failures, and tag or inventory departures are precisely the
    cutoff remainders of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>.

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> permits every fixed exponent
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|w>\<gtr\>0> for the cutoff remainders. Take
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|w>=1/2>, so <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>
    and the simultaneous probability bound is
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|w><rsup|2*r>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-r>> for <math|r>
    distinct cutoff addresses, including the coordinate-choice multiplicity.
    The linear Chernoff bound there gives each cutoff remainder the exponent
    <math|c<rsub|j>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>/g|)>/B<rsup|4>> for some
    <math|c<rsub|j>\<gtr\>0>. Since <math|t<rsub|m+1>/g\<geqslant\><cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>/<sqrt|2>>,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|t<rsub|m+1>/g|B<rsup|4>*log \<frak-b\>>\<longrightarrow\>\<infty\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The fixed amplitudes <math|M<rsub|w>> enter <math|P<rsub|j>>. All rows
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-factor-rates> are consequently at most
    <math|P<rprime|'><rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>
    with <math|P<rprime|'><rsub|j>> of subpower growth. After the coupling
    threshold is raised, <math|P<rprime|'><rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|1/16>>,
    so every factor majorant is at most <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>. Path
    lengths are summed with the exponential bounds of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>. Every fixed polynomial in
    <math|1+s<rsub|M>> is bounded by <math|C<rsub|j,\<eta\>>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*s<rsub|M>>>;
    choose <math|\<eta\>> from the unused support exponent. These choices
    precede the coupling threshold.

    <em|Step 3: large-field components.> Large-field components are the
    connected components of the neighbourhoods of the selected large-field
    tests in the allocation sector of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>, as
    defined before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>. Their selections
    <math|A=<around|(|A<rsub|f>,A<rsub|n>,A<rsub|c>|)>> consist of the
    selected fine plaquettes, mismatch edges and coarse flags; each retains
    <math|exp <around|(|-\<kappa\><rsub|B>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>|)>>
    before integration, <math|\<kappa\><rsub|B>> being the constant of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>. Fix the retained exponent
    <math|0\<less\>\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>\<less\>\<kappa\><rsub|B>> in
    the large-field component bound of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>, and
    then choose <math|0\<less\>r<rsub|j>\<less\>\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>>.
    When <math|A<rsub|c>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>, keep all reads meeting this
    component in its local factor and apply that bound. Old measurable
    factors use their supremum envelopes, while the Gaussian variables are
    integrated. The frozen Haar integrals are probability integrals. After
    adjoining every large-field component met by the reads,
    <math|A<rsub|c>=\<varnothing\>> means that these reads avoid output
    flags. In this case the selected-test clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> retains
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i>a<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>>
    through the required derivative order; apply its ordinary polymer bound
    to the remaining factors. With <math|F<rsub|A>> denoting this component
    product, its integrable majorant satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F<rsub|A>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|A>,<space|2em><big|int>g<rsub|A>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|A>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2><around|\||A|\|>>*<big|prod><rsub|a<math-up|attached>>f<rsub|a><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|s<rsub|A>> counts its radius-<math|B> blocks, and
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>> was fixed before <math|r<rsub|j>>. The
    product contains the other attached factors' bounds. Its covariance
    scores and residual quadratic exponentials are estimated by square
    completion under the common Gaussian law there. The surrounding ordinary
    factors have supports disjoint from the output flags and use that same
    law. The read-count polynomial uses the unused support exponent: for
    every fixed <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,
    <math|<around|(|1+s<rsub|A>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,\<delta\>>*e<rsup|\<delta\>*s<rsub|A>>>.
    Choose <math|\<delta\>> within that reserve, as
    for<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-seed-required>; this leaves the output support
    weights intact. The local sector multiplicity of the selected tests uses
    a fixed part of the energy exponent between
    <math|\<kappa\><rprime|'><rsub|B>> and <math|r<rsub|j>>. The prescribed
    derivative costs are already included in the read-count polynomial and
    the subpower factor. A simultaneous set of <math|r> cutoff addresses
    contributes <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|w>><rsup|2*r>> to the
    Gaussian probability in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>; Cauchy\USchwarz
    leaves <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|w>><rsup|r>> after the other
    factors have been estimated under that same law. Thus each cutoff
    remainder still has its own factor in <math|<big|prod>f<rsub|a>>, also
    when distinct ones share Gaussian coordinates.

    <em|Step 4: tree sums.> The coupling threshold makes
    <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*sup<rsub|x>
    <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a>>v<rsub|a>> at most <math|\<eta\>>, with
    <math|\<eta\>> smaller than the reserved exponent, which pays for the
    factors <math|e<rsup|\<eta\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|a>|)>>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-rooted> then bounds the rooted sums by
    <math|v<rsub|a>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*n<rsub|a>>>. This proves absolute
    summability, including all sectors and all differentiated terms. For a
    distinguished factor <math|W<rsub|\<star\>>>, the same lemma with root
    <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|\<star\>>,v<rsub|\<star\>>|)>> gives
    <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>*e<rsup|\<eta\>*n<rsub|\<star\>>>>. Rooting there
    counts each distinguished occurrence once. A forced component keeps
    <math|exp <around|(|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>|)>>
    in this sum, proving both forced bounds. For coefficient-valued factors
    take the norms of the coefficient algebra before integration; they are
    subadditive and submultiplicative, so every product and sum reduces to
    the same nonnegative tree calculation. Finite-product identities hold
    coefficient by coefficient, and absolute convergence permits passage to
    their limits. This proves the coefficientwise assertions without changing
    the health or inventory tests. Increasing the fine support exponent
    before choosing the ratio leaves the output exponents <math|\<sigma\>+3>
    and <math|\<sigma\>+2>. The same tree calculation applies with two
    subtracted from every support exponent, including the exponent in
    <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>>: covering subadditivity still applies and the
    attachment sum decreases. This proves the bounds with the reduced
    weights.

    <em|Step 5: logarithm.> There are two connectivity operations. First,
    elementary factors whose complete read supports intersect are integrated
    together; their connected products are the polymers <math|M>, with
    weights <math|z<rsub|M>>. Second, the hard-core gas of these polymers is
    expanded logarithmically; its connected graphs, the clusters, have
    polymers as vertices and support overlaps as edges.
    In<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-cluster> connectedness refers to this second graph.
    The first operation handles dependence under the Gaussian law; the second
    produces the additive regular interaction. Write
    <math|\<Xi\><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-M\>><big|prod><rsub|M\<in\>\<cal-M\>>z<rsub|M>>,
    where <math|\<cal-M\>> runs over finite selections with disjoint
    supports, including the empty selection. This is the ordinary hard-core
    gas. Its logarithm has the connected coefficients

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-cluster>

      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|log \<Xi\><around|(|z|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><frac|1|n!>*<big|sum><rsub|M<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,M<rsub|n>>\<phi\><rsup|T>*<around|(|M<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,M<rsub|n>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|h=1><rsup|n>z<rsub|M<rsub|h>>,>>|<row|<cell|\<phi\><rsup|T>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<Gamma\><text|connected>><big|prod><rsub|<around|{|h,k|}>\<in\>\<Gamma\>><around|[|-\<b-1\><rsub|Z<rsub|M<rsub|h>>\<cap\>Z<rsub|M<rsub|k>>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>|]><space|0.17em>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation>

    The graph in this formula has vertex set
    <math|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>, and the one-vertex coefficient is one.
    Apply<nbsp><cite-detail|Ueltschi2004|Theorem<nbsp>1,
    equations<nbsp>(3)--(4)> to the counting measure with masses
    <math|z<rsub|M>> and interaction <math|\<zeta\>*<around|(|M,M<rprime|'>|)>=-\<b-1\><rsub|Z<rsub|M>\<cap\>Z<rsub|M<rprime|'>>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>>.
    Its hypotheses are <math|<around|\||1+\<zeta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, finite
    total variation, finite exponential mass, and

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|M<rprime|'>:Z<rsub|M<rprime|'>>\<cap\>Z<rsub|M>\<ne\>\<varnothing\>><around|\||z<rsub|M<rprime|'>>|\|>*e<rsup|\<frak-w\><around|(|M<rprime|'>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<frak-w\><around|(|M|)>,<space|2em>\<frak-w\><around|(|M|)>=\<eta\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|M>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The rooted estimate just proved verifies the last condition. At fixed
    volume the site sum gives finite total exponential mass.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-cluster> and its rooted estimate therefore
    apply. Fixing the distinguished occurrence changes the symmetry factor
    from <math|1/n>! to <math|1/<around|(|n-1|)>>!, matching that theorem's
    rooted sum. Keep the components joined to forced polymers as coefficients
    of the exact-cover sum; take the logarithm only of the remaining ordinary
    gas. Expanding and regrouping finite selections proves this separation.
    Absolute convergence then proves the identity for the full integral. The
    deterministic part is exactly <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>>:
    the stationary terms and one-loop quadratic term are those of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>\U<reference|lem:C7>, with the masks
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-low-sums> retained.

    <em|Step 6: continuity and covariance.> For two inputs at fixed
    addresses, telescope each product with one difference factor. Activity
    differences use their own difference norms under
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>. For a coupling change, hold physical
    coordinates fixed and compare the Gaussian densities explicitly. On
    <math|d> active coordinates with fixed mean and positive precision
    <math|<math-ss|A>>, put <math|Q<around|(|a|)>=<around|\<langle\>|a-m,<math-ss|A><around|(|a-m|)>|\<rangle\>>/2>.
    The normalized laws satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <frac|d*\<nu\><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|2>>|d*\<nu\><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|1>>><around|(|a|)>=<around*|(|<frac|\<beta\><rsub|2>|\<beta\><rsub|1>>|)><rsup|d/2>*e<rsup|-<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|2>-\<beta\><rsub|1>|)>*Q<around|(|a|)>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its derivative in <math|\<beta\><rsub|2>> is the same ratio times
    <math|d/<around|(|2*\<beta\><rsub|2>|)>-Q<around|(|a|)>>. On a small
    relative coupling interval its absolute value is dominated by a
    polynomial times a Gaussian exponential with precision strictly below
    that of the base law. Square completion bounds its integral. Mean and
    precision changes are treated by the inverse difference identity and the
    Schur bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>; their density
    derivatives are again polynomial Gaussian scores. No old measurable
    factor is differentiated in this comparison. The tree bound with the same
    attachment margin controls the weighted discrepancy by a constant times
    the input activity and coupling discrepancies; this constant may depend
    on the fixed volume. Continuity holds locally and, by a finite covering
    of the compact interval, on fixed addresses throughout. Translation and
    signed permutations transport complete read supports, factors and
    sectors; their finite average gives equivariance.
  </proof>

  The terms linear in an incoming regular activity must be treated separately
  from products of small factors. Their supports can be large, so they are
  not replaced by auxiliary tori. The function <math|E<rsub|i>> and its
  derivative norm in this paragraph are at level <math|m+1>. For such an
  address <math|i>, let <math|T<rsub|i>> be its contribution to the negative
  connected logarithm. The local Gaussian substitution of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>, restricted to the windows incident to
  <math|X<rsub|i>>, reconstructs a fine configuration
  <math|U<rsub|i><rsup|0>*<around|(|V,z|)>>. With the same inner chart
  protector <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i>>, define

  <\equation*>
    P<rsub|i><around|(|V|)>=<frac|1|<around|\||\<cal-T\><rsub|i>|\|>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<theta\>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|i>><math-up|Ext><rsub|Y<rsub|i>><big|int>\<Gamma\><rsub|i,\<theta\>><around|(|z|)>*E<rsub|i>*<around*|(|U<rsub|i,\<theta\>><rsup|0>*<around|(|V,z|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<gamma\><rsub|C<rsub|i,\<theta\>><around|(|V|)>><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>R<rsub|i>=T<rsub|i>-P<rsub|i><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|i>> is the finite set of rooted frames and tags
  in that local substitution. The operator
  <math|<math-up|Ext><rsub|Y<rsub|i>>> extends the function from the healthy
  domain by its local Borel representative; on that domain it changes no
  value. The coarse support <math|Y<rsub|i>> contains the projected fine
  support and every incident window. This principal Gaussian average retains
  the incoming function itself.

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited> is the contraction estimate for the
  linear part of the step. An incoming regular activity has zero value, zero
  gradient (by gauge invariance) and zero affine Hessian
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>). Its Gaussian average
  <math|P<rsub|i>> at fixed coarse field therefore sees only background terms
  of scaling weight at least five, each costing
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-5>>; summing over the
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|4>> fine anchors above a coarse anchor gives
  <math|V<rsub|j>/\<ell\><rsub|m>>. The remainder <math|R<rsub|i>>, the
  difference between the true local integral and this Gaussian average, is
  smaller by a factor <math|<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Inherited regular terms><label|lem:D-inherited> The functions
    <math|P<rsub|i>,R<rsub|i>> are regular before flat-value and Hessian
    subtraction, on the same fixed coarse addresses as <math|T<rsub|i>>. At
    exponent <math|\<sigma\>+3> their anchored derivative sums satisfy

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><around|(|P|)>\<leqslant\><frac|V<rsub|j>|\<ell\><rsub|m>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,<space|2em>\<cal-E\><around|(|R|)>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j,\<ell\><rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|inh>><around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|V<rsub|j>> is fixed before the ratio and
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|j,\<ell\>><rsup|<math-up|inh>>> has upper rate
    <math|-1/2>. The identity <math|T<rsub|i>=P<rsub|i>+R<rsub|i>> and the
    corresponding weighted comparisons hold on the entire input curve.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The principal average is estimated by a weight count in the radial gauge;
    the remainder by the Gaussian comparison of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> and the unrefined bound of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. For the principal average use the
    same normalized directions as in the incoming norm. The linear background
    lifts a unit coarse curl to fine curvature of size
    <math|O<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>|)>>. Replace the background on
    <math|X<rsub|i>> by its affine part at the anchor. In the Taylor formula
    for <math|E<rsub|i>> the constant vanishes by
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>. The first derivative also vanishes:
    invariance under constant gauge transformations makes its coefficient on
    each link an <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant vector of
    <math|<g>>, which is zero since <math|<g>> is simple and nonabelian
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>). Its Hessian on two such affine
    fields vanishes by Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>. Since
    <math|E<rsub|i>> is gauge invariant, evaluate it in the radial gauge at
    its fine anchor; at fine distance <math|\<rho\>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>
    the lifted field is then at most <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>*<around|(|t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>|)>>
    times a permitted fine direction, using
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<geqslant\>\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>2>.
    In the normalized curvature directions, the linear prediction
    <math|<math-ss|P>> and its affine approximation
    <math|<math-ss|P><rsub|<math-up|aff>>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|P>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|P>-<math-ss|P><rsub|<math-up|aff>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>\<to\>\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-1-r>>
    for <math|0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>2>. Taking its curl and one further
    fine difference gives the two bounds above, with constants fixed before
    the ratio. For supports with <math|d<rsub|i><rsup|3>\<less\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>
    the weight count below shows that every surviving term carries
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-5>>; larger supports are treated without
    affine subtraction.

    For every support extending beyond the radius <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>>, use
    the incoming covering number <math|d<rsub|i>=<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m+1>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>.
    Split at <math|d<rsub|i><rsup|3>\<less\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>. In this class
    <math|3*d<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>; together with
    <math|B<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m>>, this bounds the inspected fine
    radius by <math|6*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>|)>*\<ell\><rsub|m>>. The support
    is nonwrapping because the coarse side is at least
    <math|100*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>|)>>. Perform the radial Taylor expansion
    on this whole support. In the incoming weighted direction norm, each
    differentiated lifted potential decomposes into a constant part, an
    affine part and a remainder with respective bounds

    <\equation*>
      C*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>C*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-2>,<space|2em>C*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-3>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|C> is bounded by a stage constant plus the normalized
    nonlinear and fluctuation errors. After the ratio is fixed these errors
    are made at most one by the entry choice: their bounds are fixed powers
    of <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>,B<rsub|m>> times
    <math|t<rsub|m+1>+g/t<rsub|m+1>>, which tends to zero. The spatial
    weights are retained in this decomposition, using
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<geqslant\>\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>2>
    from item<nbsp>4 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>.

    Assign weights one, two and three to the three parts. In the local
    radial-gauge expansion, a site gauge removes the weight-one part of the
    potential and produces terms of weight at least two; commutators of
    constant parts first occur at weight two. Thus the first possible
    curvature has weight two. The terms through weight four are therefore a
    constant, linear terms in curvature and its spatial derivatives, and a
    quadratic curvature term. The first is zero by flat normalization, the
    second is zero by constant colour invariance, and the last is zero by the
    affine Hessian condition. Equivalently, multilinearity after the
    three-part decomposition cancels every selection of total weight at most
    four. A selection of larger weight has at least five inverse powers of
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>>. Taylor's integral formula along the healthy
    radial segment gives the same gain at the evaluation field: integrate the
    next derivative from the flat field, repeating up to the available order
    <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>6>. The ordered chain rule
    has only stage-dependent many placements. The common Gaussian majorant of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> bounds their moments.

    For the complementary class <math|d<rsub|i><rsup|3>\<geqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>,
    including winding supports, apply the derivative bound without an affine
    subtraction. Its direction and read-count losses are polynomial in
    <math|1+d<rsub|i>>. Both cases are expressed by

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|d|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<ell\><rsup|-5>,>|<cell|d<rsup|3>\<less\>\<ell\>,>>|<row|<cell|1,>|<cell|d<rsup|3>\<geqslant\>\<ell\>,>>>>><space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|\<ell\>><around|(|d|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsup|-5>*<around|(|1+d|)><rsup|15>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|\<ell\><rsup|5>\<leqslant\>d<rsup|15>> in the second case.
    After the entry choice absorbs factors polynomial in <math|B<rsub|m>>, if
    <math|a<rsub|i>> is an incoming derivative majorant, the integrated
    principal bound is at most <math|C<rsub|j>*a<rsub|i>*\<delta\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|m>><around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>*<around|(|1+d<rsub|i>|)><rsup|q<rsub|j>>>,
    for a fixed integer <math|q<rsub|j>>; the polynomial comes from the
    ordered chain rule and the Gaussian moments just described.

    For support compression, fix an integer <math|r> with
    <math|Y<rsub|i>\<subset\>N<rsub|r*B<rsub|m>><around|(|<math-up|parent><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>|)>>
    and put <math|T<rsub|r>=<around|(|2*r+13|)><rsup|4>>. Choose the ratio to
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      20*<around|(|\<sigma\>+3|)>*T<rsub|r>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>*\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>.
    </equation*>

    Together with <math|B<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m>>, this leaves half
    the incoming exponent:

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<sigma\>+3|)><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<sigma\>+3|)>*T<rsub|r>+<tfrac|1|2>\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*d<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>

    The remaining half bounds <math|<around|(|1+d<rsub|i>|)><rsup|q<rsub|j>+15>>,
    since <math|sup<rsub|d\<geqslant\>0><around|(|1+d|)><rsup|q<rsub|j>+15>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*d/2>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    There are <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|4>> fine anchors above a coarse
    anchor. Multiplying their count by the per-address factor
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-5>> proves the bound
    <math|V<rsub|j>/\<ell\><rsub|m>>, with <math|V<rsub|j>> fixed before the
    ratio.

    For <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|m>>,
    compare the actual local integral with this principal Gaussian average
    before integrating. Subtract their means, covariances and transported
    kernels using the inverse difference identity, and telescope the
    remaining factor product with one marked difference. The inverse
    difference identity <math|C<rsup|-1>-<wide|C|~><rsup|-1>=C<rsup|-1>*<around|(|<wide|C|~>-C|)>*<wide|C|~><rsup|-1>>
    puts a reconstruction difference in one factor. Taylor's formula for the
    link exponential makes that factor at most
    <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|t<rsub|m+1>+g|)>> in
    each of the requested normalized derivatives; stationarity and window
    residuals contribute <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*g<rsup|-c<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-c<rprime|'><rsub|j>*B>>.
    Consequently their common bound is

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|{|t<rsub|m+1>+g+g<rsup|-c<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-c<rprime|'><rsub|j>*B>|}>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2+o<around|(|1|)>>,<space|2em>\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    A second occurrence of the incoming activity has bound
    <math|C<rsub|j><around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|)><rsup|2>>,
    which is also bounded by an upper-rate-<math|-1/2> factor times
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>. In every term of the
    difference, one of these factors is retained before the common Gaussian
    majorant is applied. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> bounds its remaining
    moments by <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|1+s<rsub|M>|)><rsup|d<rsub|j>>>,
    where <math|P<rsub|j>> has upper rate zero after the ratio is fixed and
    <math|s<rsub|M>> is the block count defined above. Summing with the
    unused incoming support exponent gives a function
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|j,\<ell\>><rsup|<math-up|inh>>> of rate <math|-1/2>.
    This proves the remainder estimate in the weighted norm. For the
    remaining supports use the unrefined bound for <math|T<rsub|i>> from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> and the same Gaussian majorant for
    <math|P<rsub|i>>. Support compression implies that the incoming covering
    number is at least <math|c*B<rsub|m>-C<rsub|j>>. Retaining a further
    fixed part of its exponent gives an extra <math|e<rsup|-c*B<rsub|m>>> in
    the weighted sum, for some <math|c\<gtr\>0> fixed before the coupling
    threshold. Since <math|B<rsub|m>\<geqslant\><around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>>, this factor, times any fixed polynomial in
    <math|B<rsub|m>>, is bounded by <math|<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>> after
    the threshold is raised. This supplies the stated remainder bound on
    large supports.

    Local Gaussian marginals read only <math|Y<rsub|i>>. A gauge
    transformation acts orthogonally on their colour coordinates and leaves
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|i>> invariant; changing Gaussian variables proves
    gauge invariance of <math|P<rsub|i>>. The frame average transports the
    support and anchor under lattice symmetries. The local extension
    preserves these identities, measurability and the healthy derivatives.
    The same properties hold for <math|R<rsub|i>> by subtraction. For a
    parameter difference use that incoming difference in the displayed
    integral and in the marked comparison. The covariance score and
    protected-coordinate change are those of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>,
    so the same weighted estimates give continuity at fixed addresses.
    Finally choose the ratio so <math|V<rsub|j>/\<ell\><rsub|m>> is less than
    the prescribed pre-ratio fraction, and then the threshold so
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|j,\<ell\><rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|inh>>> is less than
    that fraction.
  </proof>

  The bound <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> does not fit
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-11/10>>.
  The obstruction comes from connected terms with one or two elementary
  factors, each of size <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>. They improve only
  through cancellations in their sum, which is the logarithm of a local
  Gaussian integral: the lone cubic term vanishes by parity, the quartic
  terms belong to the classical action and the quadratic terms to the
  one-loop action. An estimate of each separate local term would miss these
  cancellations. We therefore evaluate the finite sum on small auxiliary
  tori, where the logarithm can be expanded directly, and recover a local
  density from the auxiliary sums. On the torus <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> of
  side <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>>, sum the local formula over all sites
  <math|y>, each evaluated at the configuration reflected about
  <math|x-<wide|y|~>>, so that <math|y> sits over <math|x>. The points of
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> fall into classes according to their distances
  from the reflection faces. When the configuration is healthy near <math|x>,
  the summand is the same on each class for every <math|i>, and the number of
  points in a class is a polynomial in <math|i> of degree at most four; only
  the interior class has degree four, with leading coefficient
  <math|2<rsup|4>> (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:D-five-tori>). The fourth
  difference with the weights <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>>
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-five-weights> therefore returns the interior summand
  <math|F<rsub|x><around|(|V|)>-F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>> exactly, because

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>i<rsup|k>=0*<space|1em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>k\<less\>4|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>i<rsup|4>=4!<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The discrepancy of the health masks is supported near flags and will be
  included in the forced activities.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-7.svg|382.013011pt|158.815532pt||><label|fig:D-five-tori>>|The
  five reflected tori <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>, of sides
  <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>>, drawn for <math|i=0> and <math|i=4>. A point
  <math|y> of <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> is classified by its distance,
  through the read radius <math|R>, from each reflection face; at the shifted
  configurations <math|V<rsub|i,x-<wide|y|~>>>, healthy near <math|x>, the
  local formula takes the same value on a class for all <math|i>. The
  boundary layers have fixed width, so a boundary class has a number of
  points of degree at most three in <math|i>, while the interior class grows,
  with degree four and leading coefficient <math|2<rsup|4>>. The fourth
  difference <math|<big|sum><rsub|i><around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i><around|(|\<cdummy\>|)>>
  annihilates degrees below four, so it removes every boundary class from the
  total and keeps the interior one. The tori are auxiliary evaluation
  domains, not physical volumes, and the cancellation takes place in the sum
  over all points, not term by term.>

  Write <math|f<rsub|a><rsup|0>> for a regular summand of the negative
  logarithm in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-cluster>, including its coefficient. Keep the
  inherited terms of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited> separately. Call
  a remaining term <em|low-order> if it is a connected term with at most two
  polymers of at most two elementary factors each, every path atom having
  length at most <math|B>, with no incoming activity factor or large-field
  component, or a path term of length at most <math|B>. Such terms have
  complete read radius at most a fixed stage multiple of <math|B>. Low-order
  terms read only the prescribed path, the coupling and the addresses, not
  the values of the incoming activities. Fix an exhaustion of the low-order
  addresses by finite sets invariant under translations and signed
  permutations, and select the low-order terms in one of these sets. Its
  index and the truncation error <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> are
  fixed in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>. Give an
  ordinary term the shares <math|p<rsub|x*a>=\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|a>><around|(|x|)>/<around|\||X<rsub|a>|\|>>,
  and a path term the share <math|\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|a>|}>><around|(|x|)>>;
  these shares sum to one. Put <math|F<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|a<math-up|selected>>p<rsub|x*a>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>>,
  the selected low-order sum. The remaining terms form
  <math|f<rsup|<math-up|tail>>>. Let <math|R> contain every complete read
  support of this finite sum relative to a site receiving a nonzero share.
  Its radius is a stage multiple of <math|B>. Set
  <math|w=4*R+100*<around|(|1+B|)>>, <math|<wide|w|\<bar\>>=w+5>, and let
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> have side <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>>,
  <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>4>. A configuration on <math|\<Lambda\>>
  gives a configuration <math|V<rsub|i,x>> on <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> by
  repeated reflection of its cube about <math|x>. The direction of a
  reflected link is reversed when necessary. Put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-five-weights>\<lambda\><rsub|i>=<frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|i><binom|4|i>|2<rsup|4>*<space|0.17em>4!>,<space|2em>G<rsub|x><around|(|V|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>F<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|y><around|(|V<rsub|i,x>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|F<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>> is the same finite local formula on the
  auxiliary torus, with its value at <math|1> subtracted at each site. The
  mask discrepancy and the constant of the redistribution are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-mask-localization>L<rsub|x>=<big|sum><rsub|a<text|selected>>p<rsub|x*a>*h<rsub|X<rsub|a>>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>-F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<big|sum><rsub|y\<in\>\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>h<rsub|N<rsub|<wide|w|\<bar\>>>*<around|(|x-<wide|y|~>|)>>*F<rsup|<around|(|i|)>><rsub|y><around|(|V<rsub|i,x-<wide|y|~>>|)>,<space|2em>c=F<rsub|0><around|(|1|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation>

  where <math|<wide|y|~>> is the integer representative used in the
  reflection.

  For the estimates below put <math|\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>>;
  <math|P<rsub|j>> has subpower growth.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite-period localization><label|lem:D-localization> The function
    <math|G<rsub|x>> is local on <math|N<rsub|<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>>,
    and

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|a>h<rsub|X<rsub|a>>*f<rsub|a><rsup|0>=c\|\<Lambda\>\|+<big|sum><rsub|a>h<rsub|X<rsub|a>>*f<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tail>>+<big|sum><rsub|x>h<rsub|N<rsub|<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>>*G<rsub|x>+<big|sum><rsub|x>L<rsub|x><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Each <math|L<rsub|x>> reads only links and flags in
    <math|N<rsub|2*<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>> and vanishes when that
    set is healthy, and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,N<rsub|<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|(|\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|)>,<space|2em>sup
      <around|\||L<rsub|x>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The identity is a rearrangement; the vanishing of <math|L<rsub|x>> is the
    fourth-difference cancellation; the bound on <math|G<rsub|x>> comes from
    the auxiliary logarithm, estimated with the classical and one-loop
    subtractions of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>.
    Summing<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-mask-localization> over <math|x> and changing
    <math|x> to <math|x-<wide|y|~>> gives exactly the asserted identity.
    Translation symmetry makes <math|F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>> independent of
    <math|x>.

    Use the polynomial period requirement in item<nbsp>7 of
    Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>, in addition to
    <math|100*<around|(|B+1|)>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>. Write
    <math|n=L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>>. The radius <math|R> of the local
    construction is affine in <math|B> with stage-dependent coefficients,
    computed from the low-order radius; hence
    <math|<wide|w|\<bar\>>=4*R+100*<around|(|1+B|)>+5> is affine in <math|B>
    with positive coefficients. Put <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>>=2*<wide|w|\<bar\>>\|<rsub|B=1>>.
    The chosen stage has integers <math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>,h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>
    with <math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>\<geqslant\><wide|a|\<bar\>>>,
    <math|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>\<geqslant\>1> and

    <\equation*>
      n\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>.
    </equation*>

    These are the minimum-period choices made after the ratios. Since
    <math|B\<geqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      2*<wide|w|\<bar\>>\<leqslant\><wide|a|\<bar\>>*B\<less\><wide|a|\<bar\>>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>\<leqslant\>n,<space|2em>4*R+16\<less\>n.
    </equation*>

    The reflection cubes, whose side is at most
    <math|2*<around|(|w+4|)>\<less\>2*<wide|w|\<bar\>>>, therefore embed, as
    does every radius-<math|R> read set. The larger health set
    <math|N<rsub|2*<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>> is a set on the original
    torus and need not embed; the argument uses only its containment of the
    queried links and flags.

    For the local comparison, suppose that the field is healthy on the
    radius-<math|R> neighbourhood. Translate its cube to
    <math|<around|[|0,2*R|]><rsup|4>>. On integer representatives
    <math|0\<leqslant\>k\<less\>n>, fold each coordinate by
    <math|q<around|(|k|)>=k> for <math|k\<leqslant\>2*R>,
    <math|q<around|(|k|)>=4*R-k> for <math|2*R\<less\>k\<leqslant\>4*R>, and
    <math|q<around|(|k|)>=0> thereafter. Pull back links along this map,
    reversing them on decreasing edges and setting them to the identity on
    collapsed edges. A plaquette becomes a plaquette of the original cube,
    with possible reversal and conjugation, or has identity holonomy. The
    field is therefore globally healthy and agrees with the original field on
    the cube. The bound <math|4*R\<less\>n> permits the construction without
    a divisibility assumption on <math|n>. Locality then permits this
    replacement when comparing the common local formulas on different
    periods.

    We verify the vanishing of <math|L<rsub|x>> on a healthy
    <math|N<rsub|2*<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>>. The complete read
    supports then lie in that set, every mask
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-mask-localization> is one, and every reflected
    configuration occurring there is healthy. Classify a point of
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>> by its distance, through <math|R>, from each
    reflection face. In the <math|y>-summand
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-mask-localization> the reflection cube is centred at
    <math|x-<wide|y|~>>, so its site <math|y> corresponds to <math|x>. For a
    fixed class the local formula is therefore independent of <math|i>. In
    one coordinate the number of points in an interior class is
    <math|2*<around|(|w-2*R+1|)>+2*i>; for a specified boundary class it is a
    fixed integer. Consequently each class has a multiplicity polynomial in
    <math|i> of degree at most four. Its degree is four only when all four
    coordinates are interior. The binomial identity displayed
    before<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-five-weights> cancels every boundary class and
    retains the interior class with coefficient
    <math|2<rsup|4>*4!/<around|(|2<rsup|4>*4!|)>=1>. Locality and gauge
    invariance identify its value with <math|F<rsub|x><around|(|V|)>-F<rsub|x><around|(|1|)>>.
    This proves <math|L<rsub|x>=0>. It also proves its stated read support:
    the largest displacement is at most <math|w+4\<less\><wide|w|\<bar\>>>,
    and each mask reads a further radius <math|<wide|w|\<bar\>>>.

    <em|Classification of the terms.> Here are the quantitative estimates on
    the sum before this localization. Put
    <math|\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>> and
    <math|\<frak-h\>=\<frak-b\><rsup|3/8>>. In the convergent connected sums
    multiply the majorant of each ordinary elementary factor by
    <math|\<frak-h\>>. The rooted attachment parameter still tends to zero,
    since <math|\<frak-h\>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/16>>. A
    term with at least three such factors across its polymers gains
    <math|\<frak-h\><rsup|-3>> upon restoring its original weights. A term
    with a retained large-field overlap has the same gain because its
    suppression is at least <math|\<frak-h\><rsup|-3>>. The sum after
    multiplication has size at most a subpower factor times
    <math|\<frak-h\>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>. Thus the terms in these two
    classes have bound

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-height-gain>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-h\><rsup|-3>*<around|(|\<frak-h\>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>|)>=P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-19/16>\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>
    </equation>

    after the coupling threshold is raised. The reserve is
    <math|19/16-23/20=3/80>. This estimate also bounds fixed ordered
    derivatives: their placement polynomial was included before the rooted
    sum.

    The connected sum separates into three parts. First retain the inherited
    terms using Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited>; their principal sum
    is made smaller than a prescribed multiple of
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>> by choosing the ratio, and
    their comparison remainder by raising the threshold. Among all other
    connected terms retain also those whose total support size exceeds the
    low-order radius: their support decay is at most
    <math|\<frak-h\><rsup|-3>> after the threshold is raised, so the estimate
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-height-gain> applies to them. A term with an incoming
    regular factor and at least one further factor is not low-order. The
    rooted clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>, with
    <math|W<rsub|\<star\>>> that factor and at least one attached factor,
    bounds these terms by <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>>.
    The remaining terms are low-order: the selected connected contributions
    and path terms of length at most <math|B>, to which the reflected-period
    construction is applied, and the low-order tail, of weighted norm at most
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>>. Long paths have an additional
    factor <math|e<rsup|-w<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*B/4>> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>; it converts their
    <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>> bound to <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>>.
    Low-order connected terms with three or four elementary factors already
    obey<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-height-gain>; the remaining connected terms have at
    most two.

    <em|The auxiliary logarithm.> For the finite sum use the stationary
    integral on each <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i>>. Their sides
    <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>> satisfy <math|2*<around|(|w+i|)>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B+1|)>>.
    The entry choice <math|B\<geqslant\>j+4> also gives
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>*<space|0.17em>2*<around|(|w+i|)>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\><rsub|m>+1|)>>
    for their fine periods. Thus the coarse-period form of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>\U<reference|lem:B-D2> applies, without
    requiring these auxiliary periods to belong to the chosen physical-volume
    sequence. Subtract its flat value and the classical cubic, quartic and
    quadratic one-loop terms of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>\U<reference|lem:C7>.
    Differentiation of its logarithm gives connected expectations under the
    common Gaussian law. For the terms not involving incoming errors, write
    the normalized local integral as <math|\<bbb-E\>*exp
    <around|(|V<rsub|3>+V<rsub|4>+V<rsub|\<geqslant\>5>|)>>, after the
    stationary action and its Gaussian determinant have been extracted. The
    subscripts refer to the Taylor degree in the link chart. Gaussian parity
    removes a lone odd cubic term. The fourth-order stationary terms are
    already in the classical path action, and the field-dependent quadratic
    part of the determinant is already in <math|\<alpha\>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>>
    and the quadratic loop atoms. Subtracting the value at <math|1> removes
    the remaining vacuum terms. Expanding <math|log \<bbb-E\>*e<rsup|V>>
    through two factors gives <math|\<bbb-E\>*V+<frac|1|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-E\>*V<rsup|2>-<around|(|\<bbb-E\>*V|)><rsup|2>|)>>;
    its remaining terms have at least three factors. In normalized
    fluctuation coordinates each nonquadratic factor starts at order
    <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>. The common Gaussian majorant bounds the
    last terms by <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-3/2>>.
    In the one- and two-factor terms that remain after the stated
    cancellations, Taylor's integral remainder and the healthy field bound
    give

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*<around|{|\<frak-b\><rsup|-3/2>+\<frak-b\>*<space|0.17em>t<rsub|m><rsup|5>+\<frak-b\><rsup|-1>*t<rsub|m>+t<rsub|m><rsup|3>+e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*B>|}>\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Every radius and chart loss is included in the subpower factor. Each of
    the four power terms has exponent at most <math|-3/2> before that factor;
    the path exponential decreases faster than any fixed power. Terms
    involving incoming activities are not low-order; apart from the inherited
    terms they obey<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-height-gain> or the bound
    <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|reg>>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/2>>
    above. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> bounds these terms simultaneously,
    so the derivative bound for the centered total auxiliary sum is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-aux-bound>C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+2<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*<around|{|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>\<gtr\>0> is the chosen
    truncation error. The first term is the centered local integral; the two
    volume terms bound the omitted regular sum at the field and at <math|1>.
    Approaching a boundary point of the healthy domain by radial contraction
    preserves these estimates, so they hold on the closed domain used in
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>.

    A direction of that definition, restricted to
    <math|N<rsub|<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>> and reflected, is bounded
    by <math|2*t*<around|(|1+<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)><rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>>.
    Rescaling each of at most <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>> directions
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-aux-bound> therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|x>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,N<rsub|<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>*<around|(|1+<wide|w|\<bar\>>|)><rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>*\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>*<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|4><around|\||\<lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*<around|[|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+2<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|i>|\|>*<around|(|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-23/20>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|)>|]><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    All the factors outside the negative powers are fixed powers of
    <math|1+B>. Also <math|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|N<rsub|<wide|w|\<bar\>>><around|(|x|)>|)>>
    is bounded by a stage constant. The regular bound divided by
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> tends to zero, since
    <math|23/20\<gtr\>11/10>. Finally,<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-mask-localization>
    and the uncentered <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>> bound give <math|sup
    <around|\||L<rsub|x>|\|>\<leqslant\>P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>,
    with the same polynomial read radius. These logarithmic corrections tend
    to zero in the site norm at exponent <math|\<sigma\>+2> and vanish on
    healthy supports. This proves all the estimates claimed for the
    localization.
  </proof>

  To specify the small coefficient, let <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|sub>>>,
  <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|low>>> and
  <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|high>>> be the operator bounds for local
  subtraction, low path replacement and the part of high path replacement
  proportional to <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>. These are
  the three linear operations in the following subsections; they are fixed
  from the output derivative norm and path bounds. Put
  <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>=C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|sub>>+C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|low>>+C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|high>>>.
  These constants use only derivative orders, direction powers, support
  exponents, and the output path bounds. For a prescribed
  <math|0\<less\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>, choose before the ratios

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|j>=<frac|1|64*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j><rsup|0>|)>>,<space|2em>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>=\<theta\>*c<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The quantity <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>> depends on
  <math|m> through <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>. The next
  assertion concerns the very same block integral and the masks
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-low-sums>. Its forced sum
  <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0>> is the disjoint-support exact-cover sum of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06> formed from the <math|k<rsub|i,u>>, and
  <math|\<frak-e\><rsub|i,u>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|i,u>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,X<rsub|i>>>,
  <math|\<frak-k\><rsub|i,u>\<geqslant\>sup<rsub|U><around|\||k<rsub|i,u><around|(|U|)>|\|>>
  are majorants.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Preparatory activities><label|lem:D-preliminary> For every input
    curve above there are a continuous scalar <math|\<tau\><rsub|u>>, local
    regular functions <math|f<rsub|i,u>>, with the regularity of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24> before flat-value and Hessian
    subtraction, and forced activities <math|k<rsub|i,u>>
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25>), all with fixed addresses,
    equivariant and continuous in the weighted norms with exponents
    <math|\<sigma\>+3> and <math|\<sigma\>+1>, such that

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=e<rsup|\<tau\><rsub|u>\|\<Lambda\>\|-\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|X<rsub|i>><around|(|U|)>*f<rsub|i,u><around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0><around|(|U|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:D-early>>>|<row|<cell|sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=x>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>+3|)><cover><rsub|B><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>>*\<frak-e\><rsub|i,u>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>,<eq-number><label|eq:D-early-bound>>>|<row|<cell|sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|i:x\<in\>Y<rsub|i>>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>+1|)><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*\<frak-k\><rsub|i,u>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>,<eq-number><label|eq:D-preliminary-forced>>>>>
    </align>

    with <math|\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>>\<gtr\>0> fixed after the
    ratio. Restricting the interval preserves all this.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The connected expansion of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>
    supplies the terms, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited> and the
    classification in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>
    bound all but the low-order ones, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization> localizes the latter; the
    choices below split <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>> among
    them. First choose the ratio so that the inherited principal terms of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited> use at most
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/32>, and then raise the
    threshold so that their comparison remainders use at most
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/32>. Bound the terms
    retained in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-height-gain> and the long paths together by
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/16>. The localization bound
    is <math|o<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>|)>> with the
    stronger support exponent. This fixes a coupling threshold independent of
    the period.

    Next fix the truncation index. The low-order tail reads only the
    prescribed path, the coupling and the incoming addresses. For each
    coupling in <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>> summability gives a tail smaller
    than <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>/2>; weighted continuity in
    the coupling gives a neighbourhood on which the same tail is smaller than
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>>. Finitely many neighbourhoods
    cover the compact <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>; take the largest index.
    This index depends only on the incoming address lists, so it serves every
    input with these lists. Increasing the index preserves the tail bound.
    Choose the tail bound to be at most <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/16>.
    The regular terms outside the finite sum then have total at most
    <math|<around|(|1/16+2/16|)>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>.
    Choose <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> so that the two
    localization errors in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-aux-bound>, including the
    direction and support losses, have total at most
    <math|12*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/16>. The resulting bound
    is <math|15*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/16\<less\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>.

    The change in the scalar is <math|\<tau\><rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|old>>-F<rsub|0><around|(|1|)>>,
    with the sign fixed by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-mask-localization>. Multiply the
    preliminary forced sum by <math|exp <around|(|-<big|sum><rsub|x>L<rsub|x>|)>>.
    Expand each <math|e<rsup|-L<rsub|x>>> about one and collect intersecting
    supports, as in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion>. The
    logarithmic factors vanish unless their supports contain flags, so each
    resulting connected component still contains a forced term. Their site
    bound at exponent <math|\<sigma\>+2> is a subpower factor times
    <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>>, whereas each forced component retains
    <math|C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>>.
    Choose a smaller positive <math|r<rprime|'><rsub|j>\<less\>r<rsub|j>>.
    The site attachment parameter tends to zero and the remaining exponential
    gives

    <\equation*>
      C<rprime|'><rsub|j>*e<rsup|-r<rprime|'><rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>
    </equation*>

    for some <math|\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>>\<gtr\>0>, since
    <math|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>\<geqslant\><refbeta><rsub|m+1>/2>. One
    extra support power is used in this collection, leaving
    <math|\<sigma\>+1>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-rooted> proves the
    asserted site bound and absolute convergence. No scalar depends on the
    output configuration, and all identities hold also when a chord is
    exactly at a threshold.

    For continuity, the truncation and all reflected tori are now fixed.
    Finite linear combinations preserve the weighted comparison from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. A difference of forced products
    has one marked difference factor; the rooted sum has the same strict
    attachment margin. Dominated convergence proves the weighted comparison
    after the collection. Flat evaluation gives continuity of the scalar. The
    support enlargement, inventories and conjunctions of tests are fixed. The
    same constructions commute with translations and signed permutations by
    reindexing. Every assertion is pointwise in the interval parameter or a
    convergence assertion within the interval, so restriction to a closed
    subinterval preserves it.
  </proof>

  The preparatory regular sum is <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>>=<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i,u>>
  on the healthy domain. Its weighted bound is the anchored sum of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05b>, before flat-value and Hessian
  subtraction. The next identity supplies the scalar reset.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Pre-reset expansion><label|lem:D4> For every input curve above and
    every <math|u>, there is a preparatory regular family with sum
    <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>>> and weighted bound
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> such that, when
    <math|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=\<varnothing\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>=exp
      <around|{|\<tau\><rsub|u>\|\<Lambda\>\|-\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>><around|(|U|)>-\<alpha\><rsub|N,m><math-ss|K><rsub|2,m><rsup|<math-up|kin>><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|U|)>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>,\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>\<gtr\>0>
    on this set.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Restrict the identity<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-early> to configurations without
    flags. Set <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>>=<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i,u>>
    on configurations without flags. Every health mask is one, and nonempty
    forced inventories give <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0>=1>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-low-sums> gives
    <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>=<math-ss|K><rsub|2,m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>>.
    Dropping the extra three support powers gives the stated bound, since
    <math|\<theta\>*c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/64>. Absolute convergence makes
    the exponent finite and real, proving positivity, including at <math|1>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Local quadratic subtraction><label|subsec:D-subtraction>

  The subtraction is local and uses no bound on the eventual coupling
  correction. It extracts from each preliminary regular function its response
  to constant curls, written with the clover curvature
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-clover-linear>.

  For a preliminary <math|f> on <math|X> anchored at <math|o>, put
  <math|Z=X\<cup\>N<rsub|2><around|(|o|)>>. For <math|q=<around|(|a,b|)>> and
  <math|T\<in\><g>>, set <math|A<rsup|q,T><rsub|<around|(|y,c|)>>=\<b-1\><rsub|c=b>*\<xi\><rsub|o><around|(|y|)><rsup|a>*T>,
  and fix a unit vector <math|T<rsub|\<ast\>>\<in\><g>>. Define

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)>>|<cell|=D<rsup|2>[f(exp\<cdot\><around|)||]><around|(|0|)><around|[|A<rsup|q,T<rsub|\<ast\>>>,A<rsup|r,T<rsub|\<ast\>>>|]>,>>|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|f><around|(|U|)>>|<cell|=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|q,r>q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)><around|\<langle\>|F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|q>*<around|(|U,o|)>,F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|r>*<around|(|U,o|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|<g>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Set <math|q<rsub|f>=0> when <math|Z> wraps. The matrix extracts the
  response to constant curls; its color independence is proved below. Define
  the regular remainder and the discrepancy of masks by

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|f>>|<cell|=f-f<around|(|1|)>-Q<rsub|f>,>>|<row|<cell|\<zeta\><rsub|f>>|<cell|=\<b-1\><rsub|Z<space|0.17em><math-up|unhealthy>>*<around|{|f<around|(|1|)>+h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*Q<rsub|f>-h<rsub|X>*f|}><space|0.17em>.<eq-number><label|eq:D-local-split>>>>>
  </align>

  The following identity is checked before any activities are summed.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local subtraction><label|lem:D-local-subtraction> For every
    preliminary regular <math|f>,

    <\equation*>
      h<rsub|X>*f=f<around|(|1|)>+h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*Q<rsub|f>-\<zeta\><rsub|f>+h<rsub|Z>*R<rsub|f><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The function <math|R<rsub|f>> is regular on <math|Z>, vanishes at
    <math|1>, and has zero affine Hessian when <math|Z> is nonwrapping. The
    Borel gauge-invariant function <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>> depends only on
    internal links and flags in <math|Z> and vanishes when <math|Z> is
    healthy.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Gauge invariance reduces the affine Hessian to its response to constant
    curls, Schur's lemma makes that response colour-independent, and the
    clover formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-clover-linear> reproduces it. The first
    derivative of <math|f> at the flat field vanishes: constant gauge
    transformations make its coefficient on each internal link an
    adjoint-invariant vector of <math|<g>>, hence a central vector, which is
    zero. Differentiating local gauge invariance again makes its Hessian
    annihilate an infinitesimal gradient in either argument. On a nonwrapping
    support an affine field with zero curl is a local gradient. Its constant
    and symmetric linear parts are differences of a quadratic site field,
    with a constant adjustment for the discrete difference. Multiply this
    site field by a periodic cutoff equal to one on <math|Z>. The internal
    links read by <math|f> are unchanged. The second differentiation of gauge
    invariance uses <math|D*f<around|(|1|)>=0>, as proved above, so no second
    derivative of the gauge orbit remains. Hence the affine Hessian depends
    only on the constant curls.

    For each pair of orientations the colour form
    <math|<around|(|S,T|)>\<mapsto\>D<rsup|2>[f(exp\<cdot\><around|)||]><around|(|0|)><around|[|A<rsup|q,S>,A<rsup|r,T>|]>>
    is <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant. The complexified adjoint
    representation of the compact simple Lie algebra <math|<g>> is
    irreducible, so every invariant bilinear form on <math|<g>> is a multiple
    of the trace form (Schur's lemma); testing on <math|T<rsub|\<ast\>>>
    identifies the multiple.

    The side satisfies <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>\<gtr\>20>,
    which permits the clover loops and affine tests without degeneracies.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-clover-linear> identifies the affine Hessian of
    <math|Q<rsub|f>> with that of <math|f>. Subtracting gives zero diagonal
    affine Hessian for <math|R<rsub|f>>; polarization gives zero on pairs.
    The value and first derivative of <math|R<rsub|f>> vanish at <math|1>. If
    <math|Z> wraps, no Hessian cancellation is required. The clover
    polynomial is smooth, Borel, gauge invariant and local on
    <math|N<rsub|2><around|(|o|)>>, which gives the other regularity
    assertions on <math|Z>.

    All indicators in <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>> read flags in <math|Z>. If
    <math|Z> is healthy, every mask in the identity is one and
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>=0>; the identity reduces to the definition of
    <math|R<rsub|f>>. If <math|Z> is unhealthy, <math|h<rsub|Z>*R<rsub|f>=0>
    and the definition of <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>> gives the identity
    directly. This proves the equality on every configuration and the stated
    locality and cancellations.
  </proof>

  The same subtraction has a quantitative bound. If <math|a<rsub|f>>
  majorizes <math|f>, the diagonal tests <math|t*A<rsup|q,T<rsub|\<ast\>>>>
  and their half-sum are permitted directions in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>. They give

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-projection-bound>t<rsup|2>*<around|\||q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,q|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|f>,<space|2em>t<rsup|2>*<around|\||q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>3*a<rsub|f><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Indeed four times the half-sum's diagonal Hessian is
  <math|t<rsup|2>*<around|(|q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,q|)>+q<rsub|f>*<around|(|r,r|)>+2*q<rsub|f>*<around|(|q,r|)>|)>>.
  Its absolute value and the two diagonal bounds give the second estimate.
  Product differentiation of the four-link formula bounds every permitted
  derivative of <math|F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|q>> by <math|C<rsub|j>*t> at
  a healthy anchor. For order zero this follows from the plaquette chord
  bound; at positive order the direction factors supply <math|t>, and all
  undifferentiated group matrices are bounded. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-remainder-bound><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|f>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,Z>+sup<rsub|U><around|\||\<zeta\><rsub|f><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*a<rsub|f><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  For <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>>, the terms <math|Q<rsub|f>> and <math|f> occur
  only under their own relaxed threshold tests, so the estimates still apply
  when <math|Z> is unhealthy.

  Enlargement costs at most <math|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z|)>\<leqslant\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|X|)>+1>:
  one radius-<math|B> ball centered at <math|o\<in\>X> contains
  <math|N<rsub|2><around|(|o|)>> and meets the original connected cover. Also
  <math|<math-up|dist><around|(|x,o|)>\<leqslant\>C*B<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z|)>>
  for <math|x\<in\>Z>, by following the connected cover. For a support of
  covering number <math|n> through <math|x>, its anchor lies within distance
  <math|C*B*n> of <math|x>, giving at most
  <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4>> possible
  anchors. The inequality <math|<around|(|1+n|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|n>>
  converts exponent <math|\<sigma\>+3> at the anchor to exponent
  <math|\<sigma\>+1> at a site: one support power bounds this polynomial and
  one remains unused. At exponent <math|\<sigma\>> the enlargement itself
  costs only a stage constant. Without these extra powers the conversion
  would leave a polynomial in <math|n> at the original exponent.

  Sum <math|q<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>> at each anchor. Translation covariance makes
  the result independent of the anchor. Reflection in an axis belonging to
  exactly one of two distinct orientations changes the sign of the
  off-diagonal entry, so that entry is zero. Permuting axes identifies the
  diagonal entries. Thus a single real <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> satisfies

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=o>q<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>*<around|(|q,r|)>>|<cell|=\<Delta\><rsub|u>*\<delta\><rsub|q*r>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|i>|}>>*Q<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>>|<cell|=<frac|\<Delta\><rsub|u>|2>*<big|sum><rsub|o>h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*<big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|q>*<around|(|U,o|)>|\|><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.<eq-number><label|eq:D-isotropy>>>>>
  </align>

  The coefficient sums converge absolutely
  by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-projection-bound>, and
  <math|t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>.
  In particular <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/t<rsub|m><rsup|2>=\<theta\>*c<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/10><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|-2>\<less\>1>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> recovers <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> explicitly
  from one healthy test configuration; this gives its continuity in <math|u>
  and, with Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>, the drift bound.

  <subsection|Forced fusion, activity bounds and the
  reset><label|subsec:D-fusion>

  Put <math|\<alpha\>=\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>. After
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-subtraction> the exponent contains
  <math|\<alpha\>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|m>+<frac|\<Delta\><rsub|u>|2>*<big|sum><rsub|o>h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*<big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|q>*<around|(|U,o|)>|\|><rsup|2>>,
  whereas the output density must contain
  <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)><math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m>>.
  The difference consists of clover versus plaquette curvature, the missing
  cubic and quartic atoms, and the clips and health masks. The first two
  become regular activities, <math|e<rsub|\<lambda\>,u>> below and
  <math|-<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*\<frak-a\><rsup|<around|(|k|)>><rsub|i>>;
  the third, <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>, vanishes on healthy supports
  and, with the mask discrepancies <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-local-subtraction>, is absorbed into the forced
  activities by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion>. Define

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|e<rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>|<cell|=\<Delta\><rsub|u>*<around|(|L<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|\<lambda\>>-L<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>|<cell|=<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*<around|(|L<rsub|\<lambda\>>-h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*L<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>*<around|(|h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>-h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|\<lambda\>>|}>>|)>*L<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|\<lambda\>><space|0.17em>.<eq-number><label|eq:D-kinetic-split>>>>>
  </align>

  The regular function is used with mask <math|h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>>.
  Its affine Hessian vanishes because its two terms have the same
  constant-curl response. Add also the regular cubic and quartic functions
  <math|-<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>>,
  with their prescribed masks; they have zero Hessian because
  <math|k\<geqslant\>3>. Every <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u>> vanishes on
  healthy <math|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>.

  Write the logarithmic corrections <math|\<zeta\><rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>> and
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u>> as <math|\<ell\><rsub|a>> on nonempty
  supports <math|Z<rsub|a>>. A connected selection <math|P> consists of
  preliminary forced labels and logarithmic labels, contains at least one
  forced label, and has pairwise disjoint preliminary forced supports.
  Connected means connected in the graph joining intersecting supports. Its
  support <math|Y<rsub|P>> is the union of all supports, including
  logarithmic supports; its inventory <math|I<rsub|P>> is the union of the
  preliminary forced inventories. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-fused-term>K<rsub|P><around|(|U|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|Y<rsub|P>\<cap\><flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=I<rsub|P>|}>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>P<rsub|<math-up|for>>>k<rsub|i,u><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|a\<in\>P<rsub|<math-up|log>>><around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>>-1|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The extra test is the conjunction of the preliminary forced tests.
  Selections and tests are fixed as <math|u> varies.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Forced fusion><label|lem:D-fusion> Suppose
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>sup<rsub|U><around|\||\<ell\><rsub|a><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    For <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>,\<gamma\>\<geqslant\>0> assume
    nonnegative <math|v<rsub|a>> for all forced and logarithmic labels with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||k<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>*v<rsub|i>,<space|1em><around|\||e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>v<rsub|a>,<space|1em>sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a>>e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>+1|)><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|a>|)>>*v<rsub|a>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\>,<space|1em><around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Then the exact-cover gas of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fused-term> equals
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0>*exp <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<ell\><rsub|a>|)>>
    and its forced norm of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05b> is at most
    <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1|)>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The identity is a regrouping of finite selections into overlap
    components; the bound is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-rooted>. Expand
    <math|exp <around|(|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<ell\><rsub|a>|)>=<big|prod><rsub|a><around|(|1+<around|(|e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a>>-1|)>|)>>.
    Decompose a finite selection, together with a selection from
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><rsup|0>>, into support-overlap components. A
    nonzero logarithmic factor meets a flag. Exact coverage assigns that flag
    to a preliminary forced label, whose support meets the logarithmic
    support. Every component therefore contains a forced label. Their union
    supports are disjoint; their union inventories cover all flags.
    Conversely the components recover the original selection uniquely. The
    eligibility indicator in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fused-term> equals one on any
    such nonzero product: another component cannot supply a flag in
    <math|Y<rsub|P>>, since its support is disjoint. This proves the identity
    for finite expansions.

    For the absolute bound retain <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>>
    from one forced label per connected selection. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-rooted>, with <math|\<eta\>=1>, to the
    weights <math|\<frak-v\><rsub|i>=v<rsub|i>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>>>;
    its hypothesis follows from <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1>
    and the extra exponent in the hypothesis here. It bounds the rooted tree
    sums by <math|\<frak-v\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>>>,
    and the sum of these bounds over roots whose supports contain a fixed
    site is at most <math|\<gamma\>>. Every connected selection has a
    spanning tree rooted at a label whose support contains that site, so its
    sum is at most <math|\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1>. The
    covering number is subadditive on intersecting unions, which retains
    <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B><around|(|Y<rsub|P>|)>>> in this
    comparison. Restoring <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>> gives
    the claimed bound. The resulting summable envelope justifies passage from
    finite expansions to the infinite identity.
  </proof>

  Let <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>>> be the masked sum of the
  <math|R<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>>, the <math|e<rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>, and the cubic
  and quartic corrections. Let <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u>> be the fused gas.
  Hold these functions fixed while a trial parameter <math|\<vartheta\>>
  varies, and put

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>>=e<rsup|-<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<vartheta\>|)><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The parameter <math|\<vartheta\>> is the free coupling correction of the
  scalar reset. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> shows that
  <math|\<vartheta\>=\<Delta\><rsub|u>> gives the density identity at every
  configuration; at one healthy test configuration <math|V> with
  <math|<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>\<gtr\>0>,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> uses the affine dependence on
  <math|\<vartheta\>> to read off <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>, which gives its
  continuity in <math|u> and, with Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>, the drift
  bound. The output representation is the following single list: regular
  addresses <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|i>,o<rsub|i>|)>> with functions
  <math|R<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>>, addresses <math|<around|(|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>,o<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>>
  with functions <math|e<rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>, the cubic and quartic addresses
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D22> with functions
  <math|-<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*\<frak-a\><rsub|i><rsup|<around|(|k|)>>>,
  and forced addresses <math|<around|(|Y<rsub|P>,I<rsub|P>,\<cal-E\><rsub|P><rsup|<math-up|extra>>|)>>
  with functions <math|K<rsub|P>>. Each regular function carries its own
  health mask. We prove the inequalities

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-fusion-small>e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>,<space|2em>\<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> before using them for the density
  identity. They impose no condition on the trial coupling.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Activity bounds and covariance><label|lem:D1> For every coarse
    output constructed above, both activity families are countable and
    equivariant under translations and <math|<hypercubic>>, including
    supports, anchors, inventories and tests. There exist nonnegative
    majorants as in Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>
    whose weighted series converge and which satisfy

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3> states the same bounds for the Wilson
  representation, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2> extends them to all the
  chosen classes.

  <\render-proof|Proof for a coarse output>
    The regular bound adds the costs of the three linear operations to the
    preparatory bound of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>; the forced
    bound applies Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-remainder-bound> controls each subtracted
    function. The low regular terms cost <math|C<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>,
    since <math|t<rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>.
    The high terms cost <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>+\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>|)>>:
    each has at least three curvature factors, and
    <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>*t<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>*t\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>.
    A derivative of a word of length <math|l> costs a fixed power of
    <math|1+l>, by the product rule and the allowed spatial growth of the
    directions. The support obeys <math|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>\<leqslant\>C+l/B>.
    Raise the threshold until <math|\<sigma\>/B\<leqslant\>w<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|J<rsub|m>>/4>.
    A quarter of the path exponent then bounds the support weight, and
    another quarter bounds this polynomial, since
    <math|sup<rsub|l\<geqslant\>0><around|(|1+l|)><rsup|k>*e<rsup|-w*l/2>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Summing Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1> gives the previously defined
    constant <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>> and a constant <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|1>>
    with

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j><rsup|0>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>+C<rsub|j><rsup|1><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The constant <math|c<rsub|j>=<around|[|64*<around|(|1+C<rsub|j><rsup|0>|)>|]><rsup|-1>>
    was chosen before the ratios in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>.
    After the ratio is fixed, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> bounds
    <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>>; raise the entry threshold until
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|1><around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>*t<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/2048>.
    This is possible because <math|t<rsup|3>/\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-2/5><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|3>\<to\>0>.
    Take <math|\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1/32> before the ratio. Since
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>*c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/64>, the sum is at most
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024>. The same argument
    allows any prescribed smaller coefficient by choosing <math|\<theta\>>
    first and raising the threshold for the second term.

    For the logarithmic corrections, conversion from anchored sums to site
    sums costs at most <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>>, after the
    extra support powers pay the powers of the covering number. The local
    subtraction costs <math|C<rsub|j>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>.
    The low terms in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-kinetic-split> cost

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<around|{|<around|(|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>+\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/t<rsup|2>|)>*t+\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>|}>=:m<rsub|j><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The linear clipped tail is bounded by <math|2*c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t>
    times its coefficient, since chords are at most two. For a failed relaxed
    threshold test, the remote threshold supplies
    <math|e<rsup|l/R<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|rel>>>> in a path of
    length <math|l>. It is paid by the path decay because
    <math|R<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|rel>>*w<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>\<geqslant\>12>.
    The common-anchor clover term costs <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>*t<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>.
    These estimates give exactly the displayed bound, including unhealthy
    configurations. Since <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/t=O<rsub|j><around|(|<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-3/5>/<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)>>
    and <math|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>> is fixed after the ratio,
    <math|<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*m<rsub|j>\<to\>0>.

    Let <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>=\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>/2>.
    The majorants in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-preliminary-forced>, multiplied by
    <math|e<rsup|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>>, have rooted total at most
    <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>>. For each logarithm use
    <math|<around|\||e<rsup|\<ell\><rsub|a>>-1|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||\<ell\><rsub|a>|\|>>
    once its individual majorant is at most <math|log 2>; this follows by
    increasing the entry threshold until <math|m<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>log 2>.
    Their rooted total is at most <math|2*m<rsub|j>>. Increase the entry
    threshold so that <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1/2>
    for <math|\<gamma\>=e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>+2*m<rsub|j>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion> applies. Increase the threshold
    further so that <math|\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1|)>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>/1024>.
    Indeed <math|e<rsup|\<gamma\>>-1\<leqslant\>2*\<gamma\>> and
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|<math-up|fus>>\<gg\><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|1/5>>.
    This proves the forced inequality and summability
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fusion-small>.

    Every address is a finite combination of countable preliminary and path
    addresses, so the families are countable. Subtraction and path
    replacement transport the entire address under translations. Signed
    permutations transform the curvature orientations with their signs; the
    finite group average includes every transformed term. These permutations
    act on supports, anchors and functions. On a connected selection they
    also transform the union inventory and conjunction of tests.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fused-term> therefore has the required
    covariance. For an inverse link, <math|<around|(|e<rsup|z*A>*U|)><rsup|-1>=e<rsup|-z<math-up|Ad><rsub|U<rsup|-1>><space|0.17em>A>*U<rsup|-1>>.
    Differentiating this identity repeatedly gives only finitely many
    product-rule terms through order <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>; the
    adjoint-invariant norm bounds them by a constant depending on that order.
    This proves the complete address covariance and the convergent weighted
    bounds for a coarse output. The Wilson start is established below;
    induction in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2> then gives the class
    assertion.
  </render-proof>

  The identity below fixes the whole density before the scalar estimate of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>. Its proof uses the bounds just established.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Exact density representation><label|lem:D5> The final activities
    satisfy Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>, vanish at the flat field in their
    regular part, and are independent of <math|\<vartheta\>>. For every
    <math|u> there is <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>\<in\><R>> such that

    <\equation*>
      q<rsub|u,\<Delta\><rsub|u>><around|(|U|)>=<frac|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>><space|2em><text|for
      every >U<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Moreover <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>=1> when
    <math|<flags><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>=\<varnothing\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Local subtraction rewrites the preliminary regular functions, path
    replacement turns the masked quadratic functional into the kinetic
    action, and fusion absorbs the logarithmic corrections; the
    bounds<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-fusion-small> make every sum converge. Choose
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-isotropy> and put
    <math|c<rsub|u>=<big|sum><rsub|i>f<rsub|i,u><around|(|1|)>>. Local
    subtraction gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|X<rsub|i>>*f<rsub|i,u>=c<rsub|u>+<frac|\<Delta\><rsub|u>|2>*<big|sum><rsub|o>h<rsub|<around|{|o|}>>*<big|sum><rsub|q><around|\||F<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|q>|\|><rsup|2>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<zeta\><rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>+<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|Z<rsub|i>>*R<rsub|f<rsub|i,u>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    All sums converge absolutely. For each low
    term<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-kinetic-split> gives

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*L<rsub|\<lambda\>>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>*h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*<around|(|L<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|\<lambda\>>-L<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|\<lambda\>>|)>-\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|2em>=\<alpha\>*h<rsub|Z<rsub|\<lambda\>>>*L<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|\<lambda\>>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>*h<rsub|<around|{|o<rsub|\<lambda\>>|}>>*L<rsup|<math-up|cl>><rsub|\<lambda\>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Sum and apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-low-sums>. The high corrections cancel
    <math|<around|(|\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>*\<cal-A\><rsub|m>>. Thus
    the exponent in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-early> becomes

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|u>-c<rsub|u>/<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>|)>\|\<Lambda\>\|-<around|(|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)><math-ss|K><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|kin>>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|2em>+<big|sum><rsub|i>\<zeta\><rsub|f<rsub|i,u>>+<big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>>\<ell\><rsub|\<lambda\>,u><space|0.17em>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion> incorporates the last two sums into
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u>>. Hence, on every configuration,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-unnormalized>\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|u>-c<rsub|u>/<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>|)><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>*q<rsub|u,\<Delta\><rsub|u>><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    At <math|1> all path and final regular terms vanish. The only exact-cover
    forced selection is empty, so <math|q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>><around|(|1|)>=1>
    for every <math|\<vartheta\>>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-unnormalized>
    proves the quotient identity and positivity of its denominator. The same
    empty-selection argument gives <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u>=1> whenever there
    are no flags.

    The regular cancellations were proved in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-local-subtraction> and
    after<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-kinetic-split>; the weighted bounds are those of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>. The value <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> used in
    the activity construction is already fixed by the preliminary functions.
    The trial variable <math|\<vartheta\>> occurs only in the exponential
    defining <math|q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>>>. This proves the exact identity
    without using Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> or its reset estimate.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|The scalar reset.><with|the-label|13.3|<label|subsec:C-reset>>

  The density identity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> holds with a value
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> that the construction fixes before the bound on it
  proved here is used. The proof order is

  <\padded-center>
    <image|figures/fig-8.svg|409.572254pt|46.620171pt||>
  </padded-center>

  Before the estimate below the following are available: the path arrays and
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>), the
  activity families with their bounds (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>), and
  the exact identity <math|q<rsub|u,\<Delta\><rsub|u>>=\<cal-H\><rsub|u>/\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5>). Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-subtraction>
  already gives <math|t<rsub|m><rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>,
  which the activity bounds use. The reset gives a second description of the
  same coefficient: evaluating the identity at one configuration with
  positive kinetic energy identifies <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> as the unique
  root of an affine scalar equation. This explicit formula, with the
  continuity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D6> in
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-continuity> (whose proof does not use
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>), gives the continuity of
  <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> in <math|u>; with Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> it
  gives the drift bound.

  For an input curve <math|\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>> of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, recall
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u>=<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>>,
  and put <math|b<rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> supplies regular and forced sums
  <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>>,\<cal-F\><rsub|u>>, independent of
  <math|\<vartheta\>>, with trial density

  <\equation*>
    q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|-<around|(|b<rsub|u>+\<vartheta\>|)><math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>><around|(|U|)>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>,
  </equation*>

  the flat-field normalizations <math|q<rsub|u,\<vartheta\>><around|(|1|)>=1>,
  and <math|q<rsub|u,\<Delta\><rsub|u>>=\<cal-H\><rsub|u>/\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4> gives a preparatory sum
  <math|E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>>> with bound
  <math|c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/4>, for
  a stage constant <math|c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>\<geqslant\>4>, and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> gives the final
  bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps>. The remaining scalar correction
  can be read from a single configuration with a definite kinetic energy.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Coupling reset><label|lem:C8> For each input curve of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, take the families and real
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> supplied by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5>, with
    no bound on <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> assumed. Then
    <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, and
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>>
    is continuous and satisfies

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>-<slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>+e<rsub|u>,<space|2em><around|\||e<rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|j>+1\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The bound is independent of <math|N,p,u> and the input curve.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We evaluate the exact identity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> at one
    configuration <math|V> with kinetic energy of order
    <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*t<rsub|m><rsup|2>>; the identity is
    affine in the reset, which is then read off. Recall
    <math|t=t<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1> from item<nbsp>7 of
    Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>. Put a group element of chord
    <math|h<rsub|V>=\<tau\>*t> on direction-<math|1> links whose second
    coordinate is even, and the identity elsewhere. Continuity along a
    nonconstant one-parameter subgroup gives
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|G>\<gtr\>0> such that every chord in
    <math|<around|[|0,\<varepsilon\><rsub|G>|]>> occurs. Choose

    <\equation*>
      0\<less\>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>min <around*|(|\<varepsilon\><rsub|G>,<frac|1|4>,<frac|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>|512*<around|(|1+A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Every <math|<around|(|1,2|)>> plaquette has chord <math|h<rsub|V>> and
    all other plaquettes have chord zero. Thus this
    configuration<nbsp><math|V> is everywhere healthy. All periods here are
    even because <math|4\<mid\>L<rsub|p>>. Signed permutations permute the
    plaquettes and preserve their chords, so the direct contribution to
    <math|<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>> is at least
    <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*h<rsub|V><rsup|2>/2>.
    Row squares are nonnegative. Since <math|<around|\||<math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><around|(|U-U<rsup|-1>|)>/2|\|>\<leqslant\><chord><around|(|U|)>>
    and adjoint transport is isometric, every slot has norm at most
    <math|h<rsub|V>\<leqslant\>1/4>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1> bounds the
    absolute sum of cubic and quartic terms by

    <\equation*>
      2<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>*h<rsub|V><rsup|3>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*h<rsub|V><rsup|2>/4<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Each signed image of<nbsp><math|V> has the same plaquette chords with a
    single nonzero orientation. Hence the bounds apply to every term of the
    hypercubic average. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|V>=<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>\<geqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*c<rsub|V>*t<rsup|2>,<space|2em>c<rsub|V>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>*\<tau\><rsup|2>/4\<gtr\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4> gives <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>,\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>\<gtr\>0>.
    Put <math|v<rsub|u>=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1>*log
    \<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>>. Taking the logarithmic contrast in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:C-prereset>log <frac|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u><math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m><math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|2,m><around|(|V|)>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>+E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|V|)>-E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The pre-reset bound of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>, summed over anchors
    as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05b>, bounds each of the last two
    values by <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/4>.
    No vanishing condition at<nbsp><math|1> is needed for this preparatory
    sum.

    On everywhere-healthy configurations every curvature slot has norm at
    most<nbsp><math|t>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1> and
    <math|t\<leqslant\>1> therefore give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|m>-<math-ss|K><rsup|<math-up|kin>><rsub|2,m>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>*t<rsup|3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    After <math|\<ell\>> is fixed, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> bounds
    <math|<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>|\|>> by a stage constant. Raise the
    coupling entry threshold until <math|2<around|\||\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>|\|>*A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>*t<rsup|3>\<leqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/2>.
    This is possible since <math|t<rsup|3>/\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-2/5><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|3>\<to\>0>.
    Together with<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-prereset> this yields

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||b<rsub|u>*K<rsub|V>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>-log
      <frac|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For the final regular sum, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps> give

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>><around|(|V|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|x><big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=x>e<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|maj>>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Since the activity families are fixed as <math|\<vartheta\>> varies, the
    scalar discrepancy is affine:

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|f*<around|(|u,\<vartheta\>|)>>|<cell|=<around|(|b<rsub|u>+\<vartheta\>|)>*K<rsub|V>+\<cal-L\><rsub|m><around|(|V|)>+E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>><around|(|V|)>-log
      <frac|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=f*<around|(|u,0|)>+\<vartheta\>*K<rsub|V><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    Since <math|V> has no flagged sites, <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|V|)>=1>.
    Evaluating the exact identity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> at
    <math|V> gives <math|f*<around|(|u,\<Delta\><rsub|u>|)>=0>. As
    <math|K<rsub|V>\<gtr\>0>, this determines

    <\equation*>
      \<Delta\><rsub|u>=-f*<around|(|u,0|)>/K<rsub|V>,<space|2em><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\><frac|<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|pre>>+1|)>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>|c<rsub|V>*t<rsub|m><rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Increase the entry threshold to obtain the final inequality, using
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/t<rsub|m><rsup|2>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/10><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m>|)><rsup|-2>\<to\>0>.
    The block integral and activity sums are continuous in<nbsp><math|u> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D6> of Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-continuity>,
    whose proof does not use the present lemma; so <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>
    is continuous.

    Multiplication by <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>=e<rsup|v<rsub|u><around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|>>>
    gives the density identity at<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>. Its coupling
    is <math|\<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<Delta\><rsub|u>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7> and <math|<around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>
    prove the claimed drift.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Continuity at fixed volume><label|subsec:D-continuity>

  The scalar reset of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> and the step on curves
  need continuity of the integral and both final sums. The next deduction
  uses the weighted comparison in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>
  and does not use Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Continuity of the activity sums><label|lem:D6> For input curves of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, the final activity families have fixed
    addresses and are continuous in the weighted sense of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25a>. For every fixed configuration
    <math|U>,

    <\equation*>
      u\<longmapsto\>\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>u\<longmapsto\>E<rsub|u><rsup|<math-up|sum>><around|(|U|)>,<space|2em>u\<longmapsto\>\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>
    </equation*>

    are continuous.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Every operation of the step is linear at fixed addresses or a convergent
    product with a strict attachment margin, so the weighted continuity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary> carries over. The maps
    <math|f\<mapsto\>f<around|(|1|)>,q<rsub|f>,R<rsub|f>,\<zeta\><rsub|f>>
    are linear on functions with fixed address.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-projection-bound> applied to a difference
    bounds its projection coefficients by its derivative majorant divided by
    <math|t<rsup|2>>. Weighted preliminary continuity therefore gives
    continuity of <math|c<rsub|u>>, <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>, and the
    subtracted regular and logarithmic series. The low and high corrections
    are linear in <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>>, with fixed summable path
    coefficients, so they retain this continuity. Every mask is a fixed
    function of <math|U>.

    For fusion each coefficient is a finite product and hence continuous in
    supremum norm. To justify summing weighted differences, work first along
    subsequences with one summable majorant. Given
    <math|u<rsub|n>\<to\>u<rsub|0>>, pass to a subsequence whose total
    weighted preliminary discrepancies are at most
    <math|2<rsup|-n>*\<varepsilon\>>. Add their nonnegative majorants to
    those at <math|u<rsub|0>>; the extra mass is at most
    <math|\<varepsilon\>>. The margin <math|<around|(|2*B+1|)><rsup|4>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1/2>
    arranged in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> permits this
    addition for small <math|\<varepsilon\>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion> gives one summable envelope for the
    fused coefficients along this subsequence. Dominated convergence gives
    convergence of their weighted differences. If the original sequence
    failed to converge, a subsequence with discrepancies bounded away from
    zero would admit such a further subsequence, a contradiction. This proves
    weighted continuity without assuming a common envelope on an arbitrary
    neighborhood. Constants here may depend on the fixed volume.

    For the pointwise gas, put <math|M<rsub|i>=max <around|(|sup
    <around|\||K<rsub|i,u>|\|>,sup <around|\||K<rsub|i,u<rsub|0>>|\|>|)>>.
    Telescoping finite products and summing selections gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>-\<cal-F\><rsub|u<rsub|0>><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i>M<rsub|i>>*<big|sum><rsub|i>sup
      <around|\||K<rsub|i,u>-K<rsub|i,u<rsub|0>>|\|><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The restrictions on exact coverage use fixed inventories. Weighted
    continuity makes the right side tend to zero. The regular sum is
    continuous by the analogous sum of order-zero differences. The same
    bounds give continuity in <math|u>, uniform in <math|U>, for the input
    density at fixed volume, since its path action is affine in the
    continuous coupling.

    Finally the kernel of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04> is uniformly
    bounded on a compact coupling interval at this volume. The tag masses sum
    to one; its single-link normalizing integrals are positive and continuous
    in the coupling, and its link factors are bounded on the compact group.
    Dominated convergence against product probability Haar measure gives
    continuity of <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>>. This proves all
    three pointwise continuities and weighted continuity, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|The Wilson start><label|subsec:D-Wilson>

  At the finest level the identity free form permits a local choice. Put
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>=c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|N>>> and
  <math|F<around|(|u|)>=<math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><around|(|u-u<rsup|-1>|)>/2>.
  To define the quartic term, let <math|\<psi\>> be the local inverse of
  <math|X\<mapsto\>F<around|(|e<rsup|X>|)>> at zero and set

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|4><around|(|Y|)>=<frac|1|4!>*D<rsup|4><around|[|<wilsonterm><around|(|e<rsup|\<psi\><around|(|\<cdummy\>|)>>|)>|]><around|(|0|)><around|[|Y,Y,Y,Y|]><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The inverse exists since <math|D*F<around|(|e<rsup|\<cdot\>>|)><around|(|0|)>=I>.
  This invariant quartic polynomial is chosen so that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-Wilson-remainder>R<around|(|u|)>=<wilsonterm><around|(|u|)>-<tfrac|1|2>\|F<around|(|u|)>\|<rsup|2>-T<rsub|4><around|(|F<around|(|u|)>|)>
  </equation>

  has zero Taylor terms through degree four at <math|1>. This is the case
  <math|m=N> in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>. The path action
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23> at <math|m=N> has row
  <math|<sqrt|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>>*<space|0.17em>F>, no cubic or
  loop terms, and quartic term <math|<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*T<rsub|4><around|(|F|)>>
  with its prescribed mask. These finite path sums satisfy
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>.

  The initial activities retain the difference between Wilson energy and this
  action also on flagged configurations. The proof constructs the full curve.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Wilson initialization><label|lem:D3> For every <math|N,p> there is
    a continuous curve <math|\<beta\>\<mapsto\>\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>>
    of representations at level <math|N> on <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|N>>, with
    coupling <math|\<beta\>>, vacuum scalar zero, and

    <\equation*>
      <density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>=e<rsup|-\<beta\>*E<rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Its activity families are countable and equivariant, with majorants
    satisfying <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>
    and <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|N>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>,
    and the curve is continuous in the sense of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25a>.
  </lemma>

  <\render-proof|Proof of the initialization identity, bounds and continuity>
    The Wilson energy is split exactly into the path action of level
    <math|N>, a fifth-order regular remainder, and a nonnegative unassigned
    action that is charged to the flags. Everything here is at level
    <math|N>: <math|j=J<rsub|N>>, <math|t=t<rsub|N>>, <math|B=B<rsub|N>> and
    <math|\<sigma\>=\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>>. For a plaquette <math|q>,
    write <math|k<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|kin>>=<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)><around|\||F<around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>|\|><rsup|2>/2>,
    <math|h<rsub|q>=h<rsub|<math-up|vertices><around|(|q|)>>>, and
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|q>> for its relaxed threshold test. Define its
    unassigned action by

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-W\><rsub|q><around|(|U|)>=<choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>-c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>*t<chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>,>|<cell|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>\<gtr\>t,>>|<row|<cell|h<rsub|q>*k<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|kin>>+<around|(|1-h<rsub|q>|)>*<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>-\<chi\><rsub|q>*k<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|kin>>,>|<cell|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>\<leqslant\>t.>>>>>
    </equation*>

    The relaxed threshold test <math|\<chi\><rsub|q>=1> implies
    <math|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>\<leqslant\>t>. The identity
    <math|<around|(|u-u<rsup|-1>|)>/2=<around|(|u-1|)>*<around|(|1+u<rsup|-1>|)>/2>
    and contraction of the projection give
    <math|<around|\||F<around|(|u|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><chord><around|(|u|)>>.
    Together with <math|<chord><around|(|u|)><rsup|2>=2<wilsonterm><around|(|u|)>>
    this gives <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|q>\<geqslant\>0>. For
    <math|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>\<gtr\>t>,
    <math|c<rsub|<math-up|lin>>\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>/16\<leqslant\>1/16>
    gives <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|q>\<geqslant\><chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)><rsup|2>/4\<geqslant\>t<rsup|2>/4>.
    Substitution in the two cases gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-start-identity><wilsonterm><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>=L<rsub|q><around|(|U|)>+<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*h<rsub|q>*T<rsub|4><around|(|F<around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>|)>+<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*h<rsub|q>*R<around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>+\<cal-W\><rsub|q><around|(|U|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|L<rsub|q>> is the direct clipped term plus the
    threshold-tested row square. The identity includes small plaquettes next
    to a flag and both sides of the clipping threshold.

    Take the regular activities <math|\<beta\>*<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|dir>>|)>*R<around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>>
    on their plaquette supports, anchored at the starting vertex, with the
    signed average of the path action. They vanish at <math|1> with their
    Hessians. Through the prescribed order their normalized healthy
    derivatives are bounded by <math|C<rsub|j>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|5>>. To verify
    this without assuming small links, use the plaquette holonomy as one
    coordinate and the other three links as compact parameters. The remainder
    vanishes to order five in the holonomy chart. At derivative order
    <math|k\<leqslant\>5>, Taylor's formula leaves order <math|5-k> and the
    direction factors supply <math|t<rsup|k>>. For <math|k\<gtr\>5>, bounded
    derivatives and <math|t<rsup|k>\<leqslant\>t<rsup|5>> give the same
    estimate. The four-link formula controls the remaining compact factors.
    There are finitely many plaquettes at each anchor, so the regular sum is
    at most <math|C<rsub|j>*\<beta\>*t<rsup|5>>. On the top band,

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<refbeta><rsub|N>t<rsub|N><rsup|5>|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>=<refbeta><rsub|N><rsup|-2/5><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|N>|)><rsup|5>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Increasing the entry threshold gives the regular bound
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024> uniformly in volume.

    Let <math|\<frak-F\>=<flags><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>>. Join two flags when
    their radius-<math|2*B> neighborhoods overlap. For each nonempty
    connected inventory <math|I> use <math|Y<rsub|I>=N<rsub|2*B><around|(|I|)>>
    and

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|I><around|(|U|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|Y<rsub|I>\<cap\>\<frak-F\>=I|}>>*e<rsup|-\<beta\>*d<rsub|I><around|(|U|)>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The charge <math|d<rsub|I>> is the signed average of those
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|q>> whose anchors lie within distance <math|B> of
    <math|I>, in the coordinates of that signed image. Every nonzero
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|q>> is assigned: a failure of the relaxed threshold
    test has a witnessing flag within this distance, since <math|t*exp
    <around|(|<around|(|B-2|)>/R<rsub|J<rsub|N>><rsup|<math-up|rel>>|)>\<geqslant\>2>.
    The other cases already meet a flag at a plaquette vertex. The anchors
    lie within <math|B> of <math|I>, and each nonautomatic relaxed threshold
    test reads within <math|B-2> of its anchor. Thus
    <math|N<rsub|2*B><around|(|I|)>> contains every link and flag used in the
    charge. Distinct actual components have disjoint supports, so each
    nonzero term is assigned once. Hence the sum of the component charges is
    the signed average of <math|<big|sum><rsub|q>\<cal-W\><rsub|q>>.

    Every flag touches a plaquette with <math|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|q>|)>\<gtr\>t>,
    and a plaquette has at most four vertices. Retaining the identity
    orientation in the nonnegative signed average gives

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|I><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\><frac|t<rsup|2><around|\||I|\|>|16<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|>>*<space|2em><around|(|I\<subset\>\<frak-F\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    A nonzero exact-cover product of <math|K<rsub|I>> selects precisely the
    overlap components of <math|\<frak-F\>>. Indeed any two flags in one
    selected connected inventory are connected in <math|\<frak-F\>>.
    Conversely a flag joined to a selected inventory cannot lie in another
    selected inventory, since their radius-<math|2*B> supports would
    intersect. Exact coverage then forces every component to be selected. The
    actual components satisfy eligibility. Thus the forced sum has exactly
    one nonzero term, equal to the exponential of minus <math|\<beta\>> times
    the unassigned action. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-start-identity>, summed
    and averaged, proves the Wilson density identity for all configurations
    with scalar zero.

    For the forced norm, a connected inventory of <math|r> sites is encoded
    by a rooted spanning walk of length at most <math|2*<around|(|r-1|)>> in
    the overlap graph. Each step has at most
    <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>> choices, and
    <math|<cover><rsub|B><around|(|N<rsub|2*B><around|(|I|)>|)>\<leqslant\>5<rsup|4>*r>.
    Counting possible roots for a support through a specified site costs
    another <math|C*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>>. With
    <math|c=1/<around|(|16<around|\||<hypercubic>|\|>|)>>, the weighted sum
    is therefore bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|4>*<big|sum><rsub|r\<geqslant\>1><around*|[|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|8>*e<rsup|5<rsup|4>*\<sigma\>-c*\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>>|]><rsup|r><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The top band gives <math|\<beta\>*t<rsup|2>\<geqslant\><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|N>|)><rsup|2>/2>.
    This dominates both <math|log <around|(|1+B|)>> and the exponent in
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>, so the geometric sum is
    eventually at most <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>/1024>,
    uniformly in volume. Both normalized bounds can be made arbitrarily
    smaller by increasing the entry threshold.

    Addresses and tests are independent of <math|\<beta\>>. Regular
    differences have the same majorants with <math|\<beta\>> replaced by
    <math|<around|\||\<beta\>-\<beta\><rsub|0>|\|>>. For the forced functions
    <math|d<rsub|I>\<geqslant\>0> is bounded at fixed volume and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||e<rsup|-\<beta\>*d<rsub|I>>-e<rsup|-\<beta\><rsub|0>*d<rsub|I>>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<beta\>-\<beta\><rsub|0>|\|>*sup<rsub|U>
      d<rsub|I><around|(|U|)><space|2em><around|(|\<beta\>,\<beta\><rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    There are finitely many subsets of this torus, so the weighted difference
    sums tend to zero. Translations and signed permutations transport
    complete plaquette terms, component supports, inventories and tests. This
    gives covariance as well as continuity. We have proved the Wilson
    representation, both bounds, and its full continuous curve with scalar
    zero. The one-step assertion for the later levels is proved in
    Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-recursion>.
  </render-proof>

  <subsection|The specified recursion><label|subsec:D-recursion>

  The shooting argument of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E> restricts
  parameter intervals repeatedly, so the step must be a fixed construction on
  curves that is compatible with restriction. We therefore fix, once for each
  continuous input curve, all choices of Sections<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-local>\U<reference|subsec:D-fusion>:
  the tree gauge, the address identifications, the truncation index and the
  auxiliary tori; the last two depend only on the incoming address lists. The
  step on a closed subinterval is the restriction of the step on the larger
  interval. The estimates used to repeat the step are the following explicit
  ones. At level <math|m> the path is the fixed <math|<pathaction><rsub|N,m>>
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23>, with the bounds of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>. The activity functions
  satisfy Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D24>\U<reference|def:D25> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>. At an adjacent pair the output is exactly
  the functions in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5>, its scalar is
  <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1>*log \<cal-H\><around|(|1|)>>, and
  its correction obeys

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-retained>t<rsub|m><rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>,<space|2em>\<cal-E\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>/1024,<space|2em><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The first three bounds precede the scalar reset; the last is
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8>, and it also follows from the first. The
  input coupling belongs to <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>; no output-band
  condition is imposed.

  The classes are defined inductively from the Wilson start. Put
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>=<around|{|\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\>>:\<beta\>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|N>|}>>,
  with the curve of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>, and
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,N,p>=\<cal-I\><rsub|N,p>>. For <math|m\<less\>N>, a
  representation <math|\<rho\>> at level <math|m> belongs to
  <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>> if there are
  <math|\<rho\><rprime|'>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m+1,p>> with
  <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>=\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\><rprime|'>|)>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>
  and a real <math|\<Delta\>> with <math|t<rsub|m><rsup|2><around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>
  such that: the path of <math|\<rho\>> is <math|<pathaction><rsub|N,m>>; its
  regular activities satisfy Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24> and are
  produced by local subtraction and path replacement, and its forced
  activities satisfy Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25> and are produced by
  fusion (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fusion>), from preliminary families
  with the properties of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary>,
  including<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-early-bound>\U<eqref|eq:D-preliminary-forced>;
  its coupling is <math|\<beta\><rprime|'>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<Delta\>>;
  its vacuum scalar is <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1>*log
  \<cal-H\><around|(|1|)>> with <math|\<cal-H\>=<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'>><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rprime|'>>;
  and its density equals <math|\<cal-H\>>. The relation
  <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>> consists of the pairs
  <math|<around|(|\<rho\>,\<rho\><rprime|'>|)>> so obtained. Membership is a
  property of a single representation. The
  caps<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps> are not imposed in this definition;
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> derives them. In particular the classes
  contain the outputs of the construction of
  Sections<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-local>\U<reference|subsec:D-fusion> on
  continuous curves and on their restrictions to closed subintervals. The
  analytic construction of Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-analytic> uses
  the same choices on the common address spaces; its step map and its
  restrictions are used for the selected recursion and endpoint tuning. Thus
  an adjacent pair always uses the actual input density and its own coupling
  in the kernel.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Specified recursion><label|lem:D2> For fixed
    <math|G,<blockpenalty>>, every member of <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>>
    satisfies Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2> and the
    activity bounds in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-retained>. For every continuous curve
    in <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m+1,p>> with couplings in
    <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>, including curves assembled from earlier
    outputs, the step gives a continuous curve in
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>>, related to it pointwise by
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>>. Its restriction to a closed subinterval is
    the step on the restricted curve.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The Wilson start (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>) supplies the initial
    curve and both caps; Sections<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-local>\U<reference|subsec:D-continuity>
    construct the step on a curve and prove its bounds, identity and
    continuity. At a step, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary> uses only
    the prescribed paths, the local regularity and forced eligibility, the
    bounds and the weighted continuity of its input, so the input curve need
    not coincide with an earlier chosen curve. Its truncation index depends
    only on the incoming address lists, so the same selection serves every
    input curve with these lists. Local subtraction, path replacement and
    fusion preserve the read supports, gauge invariance, cancellations and
    countability. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> proves their numerical
    bounds, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D6> proves weighted continuity on
    the interval.

    The normalized identity of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> and the reset of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> identify the same <math|\<Delta\>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> identifies <math|\<Delta\>> with the
    continuous scalar root and gives <math|<around|\||\<Delta\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1>
    and the drift in Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>. Defining the vacuum
    scalar by flat evaluation gives the exact integral, since the normalized
    representation is one at <math|1>. Hence every output is a member of the
    next class, <eqref|eq:D-retained> is inherited, and the prescribed paths
    are unchanged.

    Restricting a chosen step to a closed parameter subinterval preserves its
    supports, tests, weighted continuity and estimates; weighted continuity
    becomes continuity in the relative topology of the smaller interval. We
    use this restricted step whenever the input curve is restricted. The
    construction applies to every continuous curve in the classes, regardless
    of how that curve was obtained. Thus the classes are closed under the
    step and under interval restriction, as required for the shooting
    argument in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1> supplies the analytic estimates for this
    same recursion.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>>
    The step is the construction of Sections<nbsp><reference|subsec:D-local>\U<reference|subsec:D-fusion>
    applied to the input curve. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>,
    <reference|lem:D1>, <reference|lem:D5> and<nbsp><reference|lem:D6> give
    the final functions and their exact normalized identity. Put

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|u>=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1>*log
      \<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<Delta\><rsub|u><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The denominator is positive by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D6> gives continuity of <math|v<rsub|u>> and of
    the activity families. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> identifies this same
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|u>> with its continuous scalar root, and its drift
    estimate gives

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|u>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>-<slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>+e<rsub|u>,<space|2em><around|\||e<rsub|u>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|<math-up|RG>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Define <math|\<rho\><rsub|u>> by these scalars and the activities in
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>. Multiply the identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> by <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|1|)>>
    to obtain <math|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>=\<cal-H\><rsub|u><around|(|U|)>>
    for every configuration. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1> gives the activity
    caps and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D6> gives continuity.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2> gives <math|\<rho\><rsub|u>\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|N,m,p>>
    and <math|<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|u>,\<rho\><rprime|'><rsub|u>|)>\<in\>\<cal-R\><rsub|N,m,p>>.
    The Wilson construction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3> supplies the
    common initial curve with scalar zero.
  </render-proof>

  <subsection|Weighted analytic estimates><label|subsec:D-analytic>

  This subsection writes the specified recursion in coordinates
  <math|<around|(|\<frak-x\><rsub|m>,A<rsub|m>|)>>, a normalized coupling and
  the activities, and proves the following. The step maps are real-analytic
  on balls of fixed radius, with multilinear coefficients of degree at least
  one bounded by <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>=1/128>
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-analytic-step>). Hence the whole history is
  the unique fixed point of a contraction on power series in the endpoint
  coupling (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>), which gives endpoint tuning in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>. Each fixed-level coordinate
  converges along the full cutoff sequence
  (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D-cutoff-continuity>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fixed-level>), which drives the fixed-volume
  limit. Field differentiability does not give this parameter analyticity,
  because some activity functions are only measurable.

  <paragraph|What is held fixed.> A coupling derivative is never taken at
  fixed Gaussian coordinates. In each sector integral the physical link
  variables are held fixed, so every flag, allocation, tag and chart test,
  whose thresholds are <math|t<rsub|m+1>> and fixed stage constants rather
  than <math|\<beta\><rsup|-1/2>>, is independent of the coupling, and the
  supports, masks and local function domains of the activities are fixed.
  What varies with the coupling includes the Gaussian density in these fixed
  coordinates, its normalizing determinant, the stationary corrections and
  large-field energy factors such as <math|e<rsup|-\<beta\><around|(|s|)>*d>>,
  and these are analytic in the coupling (proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-coupling-stencil>). The fields themselves are
  not complexified, since the clips and threshold tests are not analytic in
  the links. Analyticity in the coupling and in the activity coordinates is
  therefore obtained from real evaluations.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-coupling-stencil> bounds finite combinations
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*f<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>> of
  values along real lines at a radius growing with
  <math|<refbeta><rsub|m+1>>; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-real-lines> uses
  finite differences to bring these bounds to a fixed radius; and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-stencil-recovery> recovers multilinear power
  series from them, on balls of radius <math|1/4> centred at each point of
  the real domain of the recursion. In the fixed-level limit
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fixed-level>) candidate limits are compared by
  geometric truncation of the activities; no compactness of a ball in the
  infinite-dimensional activity space is used.

  We compare cutoffs using activity functions on fixed domains. At level
  <math|m> and period <math|p>, put all regular addresses into one countable
  list and all forced addresses into a second list. A missing term has
  coefficient zero. The lists contain every finite support, anchor and
  inventory used above, and every finite conjunction of their eligibility
  tests. The output of a fusion is indexed by its finite selection, so the
  lists are closed under the construction. Keep the same permutations for
  translations and signed permutations. This identifies different cutoffs
  without changing their health domains.

  For a difference <math|A=<around|(|E,K|)>> of activity families set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-common-norm><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,p>=max
    <around*|{|<\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<frac|sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|o<rsub|i>=x>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X<rsub|i>|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<frac|sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|x\<in\>Y<rsub|i>>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*sup<rsub|U><around|\||K<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|\|>|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>>>>>>
    </aligned>|}><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The seminorms in this formula are the least majorants in
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>. Quotient by
  zero seminorm and complete the space with the prescribed local
  cancellations, eligibility and permutations. Denote the resulting real
  Banach space by <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>>. This retains regular
  derivatives on healthy configurations and forced values on every
  configuration. The vacuum scalar is excluded.

  The coupling coordinate must allow relative changes of <math|\<beta\>>
  while keeping a uniform analytic radius. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-coupling-coordinate>\<chi\><rsub|m>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/32>,<space|1em>b<rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m><refbeta><rsub|m>,<space|1em>\<frak-x\><rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>*<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|m>/<refbeta><rsub|m>>,<space|1em>\<beta\><rsub|m>=<refbeta><rsub|m><around|(|\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-x\><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Thus <math|b<rsub|m>> converts a change of <math|\<frak-x\><rsub|m>> into a
  change of <math|\<beta\><rsub|m>>, up to the factor
  <math|2*\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-x\><rsub|m>>. Monotonicity gives
  <math|b<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|0><refbeta><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>.
  Item<nbsp>7 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices> gives
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<geqslant\>2<rsup|224>>; then
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1/128>. In the following paragraphs write
  <math|b<rsub|min>=\<chi\><rsub|0><refbeta><rsub|0>>,
  <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>=1/128> and <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>=1/16>.

  Let <math|F<rsub|N,m>> denote the selected activity map and
  <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>> its scalar correction, so that

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|m>=F<rsub|N,m>*<around|(|\<frak-x\><rsub|m+1>,A<rsub|m+1>|)>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|m>=\<beta\><rsub|m+1>+\<alpha\><rsub|N,m>+\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<frak-x\><rsub|m+1>,A<rsub|m+1>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The top map is denoted by <math|F<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|\<frak-x\><rsub|N>|)>>.
  The maps are constructed for <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1>
  and <math|33/40\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-x\>\<leqslant\>47/40>.
  All inputs in this domain have the common address lists, so the truncation
  index and auxiliary tori of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary> are
  the same at every point. Hence <math|F<rsub|N,m>> and
  <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>> are single-valued, and their restriction to a
  line is the construction on that line. We use expansions centred in the
  smaller domain

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-analytic-domain><around|\<\|\|\>|A|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/8,<space|2em>17/20\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-x\>\<leqslant\>23/20<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  A ball of radius <math|1/4> about such a centre stays in the larger domain:
  its activity norm is at most <math|3/8>, and the change of
  <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-x\>> is at most <math|1/512\<less\>1/40>. For
  a retained history with <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|0>-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/4>,
  summing the drift and the error bound in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants> gives
  <math|<around|\||\<beta\><rsub|m>-<refbeta><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><refbeta><rsub|m>/4>.
  Its square root therefore lies in <math|<around|[|17/20,23/20|]>>. Every
  activity in the history, including the Wilson start, has norm at most
  <math|1/1024> by the proofs of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>. Thus the entire history lies
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-analytic-domain>.

  For a finite parameter evaluation formula with real weights
  <math|c<rsub|\<nu\>>> and nodes <math|s<rsub|\<nu\>>\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>,
  define its moment bound

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-real-stencil>\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;R|)>=sup<rsub|k\<geqslant\>0><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>/R|)><rsup|k>|\|><space|0.17em>,<space|2em>R\<gtr\>1<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  It expresses analyticity through real evaluations: if
  <math|f<around|(|s|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k>a<rsub|k>*s<rsup|k>> with
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|a<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>*R<rsup|k>\<leqslant\>M>,
  then <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*f<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>M*<space|0.17em>\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;R|)>>,
  and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-stencil-recovery> below proves a converse,
  up to halving the radius. The nodes lie on a parameter interval; they are
  unrelated to the spatial stencils of local fields.

  Uniform line estimates at nearby centres control mixed finite differences;
  this supplies the multilinear coefficients for the endpoint equations.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Analyticity from real parameter
    estimates><label|lem:D-stencil-recovery> Let <math|X> be a real normed
    space, <math|Y> a real Banach space, <math|r\<gtr\>0>, <math|R\<gtr\>1>,
    <math|C\<geqslant\>0>, and let <math|f> be defined on a set containing
    <math|x<rprime|'>+<around|[|-1,1|]>*w> for all <math|x<rprime|'>> near
    <math|x> and all <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|w|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r>. Assume
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*f*<around|(|x<rprime|'>+s<rsub|\<nu\>>*w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<space|0.17em>\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;R|)>>
    for all such <math|x<rprime|'>,w> and all parameter evaluation formulas
    with nodes in <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>. Then there are continuous
    symmetric <math|k>-linear maps <math|T<rsub|k>:X<rsup|k>\<to\>Y> with

    <\equation*>
      f*<around|(|x+h|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>T<rsub|k>*<around|(|h,\<ldots\>,h|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C<around*|(|<frac|4*e|r*R>|)><rsup|k>
    </equation*>

    whenever <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>r> and
    <math|4*e<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>r*R>. The <math|T<rsub|k>>
    extend with the same bounds to the completion of <math|X>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Associate to a parameter evaluation formula the sequence
    <math|<around|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>/R|)><rsup|k>|)><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>>
    with supremum norm. The assumed inequality defines a bounded linear map,
    of norm at most <math|C>, from their span to <math|Y>. Extend it to the
    closure. The <math|k>th coordinate vector lies in that closure: the
    <math|k>th finite difference at <math|0>, divided by
    <math|k!<around|(|a/R|)><rsup|k>>, has zero moments below <math|k>,
    moment one at <math|k>, and higher moments tending uniformly to zero as
    its mesh <math|a\<to\>0>. To see the uniformity, split the higher moments
    at any fixed order and use <math|<around|\||j*a/R|\|><rsup|n>\<leqslant\><around|(|k*a/R|)><rsup|n>>
    for the remaining tail. Evaluation on a single point then gives the exact
    line series. Its coefficient sum at radius <math|R/2> is at most
    <math|2*C>. Uniqueness follows by successively dividing a vanishing
    series by its first possible power.

    Write <math|P<rsub|k><around|(|h|)>> for the coefficient of
    <math|s<rsup|k>> in <math|f*<around|(|x+s*h|)>>. Rescaling <math|s> gives
    <math|P<rsub|k>*<around|(|a*h|)>=a<rsup|k>*P<rsub|k><around|(|h|)>> and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|k><around|(|h|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C<around*|(|<frac|2<around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>>|r*R>|)><rsup|k><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    We must prove the polynomial identity in the direction <math|h>. Put
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|h>*g<around|(|a|)>=g*<around|(|a+h|)>-g<around|(|a|)>>.
    Uniform line remainders at nearby centres imply

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|s*h><rsup|<space|0.17em>n>*f*<around|(|x+s*a|)>=O<around|(|s<rsup|n>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|s\<to\>0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed insert the line series at <math|x+s*a> into the <math|n>th forward
    difference. The coefficients below degree <math|n> cancel by
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|n><around|(|-1|)><rsup|n-j><binom|n|j>j<rsup|k>=0>
    for <math|k\<less\>n>; the uniformly bounded tail is
    <math|O<around|(|s<rsup|n>|)>>.

    For <math|h<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|n-1>> and
    <math|T\<subset\><around|{|0,\<ldots\>,n-1|}>> set

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|T>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>T><frac|h<rsub|i>|n-i>,<space|2em>w<rsub|T>=-<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>T><frac|i*h<rsub|i>|n-i>.
    </equation*>

    There is the exact mixed-difference identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-mixed-difference>\<delta\><rsub|s*h<rsub|0>>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|s*h<rsub|n-1>>*f*<around|(|x+s*a|)>=<big|sum><rsub|T><around|(|-1|)><rsup|n-<around|\||T|\|>>*\<delta\><rsub|s*v<rsub|T>><rsup|<space|0.17em>n>*f*<around|(|x+s*<around|(|a+w<rsub|T>|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Expand the right side as a sum over its <math|n+1> forward-difference
    points. At the point indexed by <math|j\<less\>n>, pairing sets <math|T>
    differing by the index <math|i=j> cancels the terms, since that index has
    coefficient <math|<around|(|j-i|)>/<around|(|n-i|)>=0>. At <math|j=n> the
    coefficient of each <math|h<rsub|i>> is one, leaving precisely the left
    side. Hence every mixed <math|n>th difference above is
    <math|O<around|(|s<rsup|n>|)>>.

    Expand each of its finitely many values in its line series at <math|x>
    and compare powers of <math|s>. It follows that

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|h<rsub|0>>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|h<rsub|n-1>>*P<rsub|k><around|(|a|)>=0*<space|2em><around|(|k\<less\>n|)>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|n=k+1> this makes the <math|k>th difference of <math|P<rsub|k>>
    independent of its base point. Define

    <\equation*>
      T<rsub|k>*<around|(|h<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,h<rsub|k>|)>=<frac|1|k!>*\<delta\><rsub|h<rsub|1>>*\<cdots\>*\<delta\><rsub|h<rsub|k>>*P<rsub|k><around|(|0|)>.
    </equation*>

    The identity <math|\<delta\><rsub|u+v>=\<delta\><rsub|u>+\<delta\><rsub|v>+\<delta\><rsub|u>*\<delta\><rsub|v>>
    proves additivity in each slot, since the extra <math|<around|(|k+1|)>>st
    difference vanishes. The displayed bound on <math|P<rsub|k>> makes these
    additive maps locally bounded, hence continuous and real linear in each
    slot. The operators commute, giving symmetry. Homogeneity gives
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|h><rsup|k>*P<rsub|k><around|(|0|)>=k!P<rsub|k><around|(|h|)>>,
    so their diagonal is the original line coefficient.

    There are <math|2<rsup|k>> terms in this polarization sum, and the
    argument in each has norm at most <math|k> on unit directions. Thus
    <math|k<rsup|k>/k!\<leqslant\>e<rsup|k>> gives the stated operator bound.
    The diagonal series equals <math|f*<around|(|x+h|)>> by the line
    recovery; the norm bound proves its convergence on the stated domain.
    Continuous multilinear maps extend uniquely through the dense inclusion
    into the completion, with unchanged norm.
  </proof>

  We evaluate the maps along lines <math|<around|(|\<frak-x\><around|(|s|)>,A<around|(|s|)>|)>=<around|(|\<frak-x\>+s*v,A<rsub|0>+s*A<rsub|1>|)>>,
  with <math|<around|\||v|\|>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|0>+s*A<rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1> for
  <math|<around|\||s|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. Supports, flag and threshold tests
  and local function domains are fixed along the line. Write
  <math|F<around|(|s|)>=F<rsub|N,m>*<around|(|\<frak-x\><around|(|s|)>,A<around|(|s|)>|)>>
  and <math|\<cal-D\><around|(|s|)>=\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<frak-x\><around|(|s|)>,A<around|(|s|)>|)>>.
  Let <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|<math-up|pre>>> be the anchored regular norm at
  exponent <math|\<sigma\>+3>, before division by
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>> and before subtraction. Let
  <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|<math-up|for>>> be the corresponding forced site norm
  at exponent <math|\<sigma\>+1>.

  The coupling varies through a square of an affine function. The next
  estimate controls that dependence at a radius growing with the reference
  coupling; a bound at one fixed field-derivative order would not suffice.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Estimates on coupling lines><label|lem:D-coupling-stencil> Put
    <math|\<frak-b\>=<refbeta><rsub|m+1>> and
    <math|\<frak-R\>=\<frak-b\><rsup|1/256>>. For every line
    <math|s\<mapsto\><around|(|\<frak-x\>+s*v,A<rsub|0>+s*A<rsub|1>|)>>,
    <math|<around|\||s|\|>\<leqslant\>1>, with
    <math|<around|\||v|\|>\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|0>+s*A<rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|33/40\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m+1>*\<frak-x\>\<leqslant\>47/40>,
    the preparatory families satisfy

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-N\><rsub|<math-up|pre>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*f<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/4>*\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;\<frak-R\>/2|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-N\><rsub|<math-up|for>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*k<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>*\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;\<frak-R\>/4|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    For any prescribed <math|\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>\<gtr\>0>, the Wilson
    map satisfies the analogous normalized estimate with radius
    <math|\<frak-R\>> and bound <math|4*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>> after
    the entry threshold is raised.
  </lemma>

  The proof keeps physical fields fixed and then applies the connected and
  localization operations.

  <\proof>
    The parameter coefficients of the connected expansion of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> are bounded by the same rooted
    estimate, with the Gaussian rescaling expanded in convergent Neumann
    series; the five tori of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization> then
    act on these coefficients. Put <math|\<upsilon\>=\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/128>>
    and raise the threshold until

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-coupling-aperture>128*\<frak-R\>*\<chi\><rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>\<upsilon\>\<leqslant\>1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    This is possible since <math|1/256-1/32+1/128=-5/256\<less\>0>. Set
    <math|\<zeta\>=\<chi\><rsub|m+1>*\<frak-x\>> and
    <math|L=\<zeta\>-6*\<frak-R\>*\<chi\><rsub|m+1>>. For
    <math|<around|\||s|\|>\<leqslant\>6*\<frak-R\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><around|(|s|)>=\<frak-b\>*L<rsup|2>*<around*|(|<frac|\<zeta\>|L>+<frac|\<chi\><rsub|m+1>*v|L>*s|)><rsup|2>,<space|2em><frac|3|4>\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|m+1>*<around|(|\<frak-x\>+s*v|)>\<leqslant\><frac|4|3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The displayed margins follow from<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-aperture> and
    <math|33/40\<leqslant\>\<zeta\>\<leqslant\>47/40>. In particular the real
    couplings lie in <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m+1>>. The relative scale change
    is at most a fixed multiple of <math|\<upsilon\>>.

    To estimate parameter coefficients, use physical link variables in each
    sector integral. All flag, allocation, tag and chart tests are then
    independent of <math|s>: their thresholds are <math|t<rsub|m+1>> and the
    prescribed stage constants, not <math|\<beta\><around|(|s|)><rsup|-1/2>>.
    Old measurable forced functions occur as <math|K<rsub|0>+s*K<rsub|1>> at
    the same physical links. Gaussian rescaling is used to estimate the
    coefficients, after these functions have been held fixed. Thus parameter
    coefficients do not differentiate their field arguments or any test.

    Here is the coefficient bound for this change of Gaussian scale. For a
    positive local precision matrix <math|<math-ss|A>> and affine source
    <math|<math-ss|b><rsub|0>>, put <math|m<rsub|0>=-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1><math-ss|b><rsub|0>>
    and <math|Q<around|(|a|)>=<around|\<langle\>|a-m<rsub|0>,<math-ss|A><around|(|a-m<rsub|0>|)>|\<rangle\>>/2>.
    This is the centred quadratic energy in the sector Gaussian density. The
    parameter occurs through <math|\<beta\><around|(|s|)>*Q>, its normalizing
    determinant, and the local stationary corrections. For a precision
    perturbation <math|H>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<math-ss|A>+H|)><rsup|-1>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>0><around|(|-<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>H|)><rsup|n><math-ss|A><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>log
      det <around|(|<math-ss|A>+H|)>-log det
      <math-ss|A>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geqslant\>1><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|n+1>|n><math-up|Tr><around*|(|<around|(|<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>H|)><rsup|n>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Both series are estimated through the same dimensionless perturbation
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsup|-1>H>. The inverse bound and exponential Schur
    sums of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>, together
    with<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-aperture>, make their
    radius-<math|\<frak-R\>> coefficient sums less than a fixed constant. For
    the Gaussian density, expansion of its exponential is dominated by
    <math|e<rsup|c<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>> with <math|c> smaller than half
    its precision lower bound. Indeed the coefficient of
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>> changes by
    <math|O<around|(|\<frak-R\>*\<chi\><rsub|m+1>|)>>. Integrating this
    majorant bounds every parameter degree at once. The local number of
    coordinates is a fixed power of <math|1+B>; hence its additional loss is
    bounded because <math|<around|(|1+B|)><rsup|C>*\<frak-R\>*\<chi\><rsub|m+1>\<to\>0>.
    The same Neumann series solves each stationary correction with the
    uniformly bounded inverse. Ordered field derivatives only add the fixed
    read polynomials already present in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2>.

    Each incoming factor is affine in <math|s>. For a regular occurrence its
    exponential has radius-<math|\<frak-R\>> majorant <math|exp
    <around|(|\<frak-R\><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|1>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>; for a
    forced occurrence the majorant is <math|<around|\||K<rsub|0>|\|>+\<frak-R\><around|\||K<rsub|1>|\|>>.
    Products use Cauchy products of coefficients. Reserve the support powers
    before summing these expressions. The initial small factor
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-factor-rates> loses at most one factor
    <math|\<frak-R\>> for an incoming affine occurrence; the change of
    Gaussian scale has the bounded sums just proved. Thus the weighted
    attachment parameter is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-R\>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-7/16>=P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-111/256>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The connected logarithm therefore has an absolutely summable parameter
    series by the same rooted estimate as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. A forced component retains a
    smaller fixed portion of the energy in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D1>;
    coefficient multiplication and sector counting use the rest. Its
    coefficient majorant is <math|C<rsub|j>*e<rsup|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pre>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>>>.

    Apply the five reflected tori of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>
    to these same parameter coefficients. A parameter evaluation formula
    commutes with their finite linear combinations. The direction rescaling,
    auxiliary volumes, support conversion and flat subtraction cost a
    subpower factor. The resulting regular coefficient sum has bound
    <math|P<rsub|j><around|(|\<frak-b\>|)>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-3/8>>: the
    preceding attachment bound is smaller, since <math|111/256\<gtr\>3/8>.
    Raise the threshold until this is at most <math|\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/4>>.
    The regular logarithm and the forced collection may use radii
    <math|\<frak-R\>/2> and <math|\<frak-R\>/4>, respectively; their Cauchy
    products then have geometric reserve. Multiplying degree <math|n> by
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*s<rsub|\<nu\>><rsup|n>> proves
    the asserted bounds.

    For the Wilson map use the formulas in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>. Its regular term is
    <math|\<beta\><around|(|s|)>> times a fixed fifth-order remainder. Its
    radius-<math|\<frak-R\>> coefficient sum is at most
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<frak-b\>*t<rsub|N><rsup|5>/\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>,
    which tends to zero. For a forced charge <math|d<rsub|I>\<geqslant\>0>,
    write <math|\<beta\><around|(|s|)>=\<beta\><around|(|0|)>+b<rsub|1>*s+b<rsub|2>*s<rsup|2>>.
    Its exponential has coefficient sum at most

    <\equation*>
      exp <around|{|-\<beta\><around|(|0|)>*d<rsub|I>+<around|(|<around|\||b<rsub|1>|\|>*\<frak-R\>+<around|\||b<rsub|2>|\|>*\<frak-R\><rsup|2>|)>*d<rsub|I>|}>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-\<beta\><around|(|0|)>*d<rsub|I>/2>
    </equation*>

    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-aperture>. The inventory count in that proof
    is still summable with this half exponent. Both normalized coefficient
    sums can be made at most <math|\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>>; enlarging
    the bound to <math|4*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>> gives the stated
    estimate. All bounds were taken before the period was counted, so they
    are uniform in the period.
  </proof>

  We now reduce the growing radius to one fixed radius chosen before the
  ratios. Choose a radius <math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>1>, and then
  positive <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>/2>
  and <math|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>>.
  Put <math|\<varphi\><rsub|m>=\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/t<rsub|m><rsup|2>>
  and <math|<wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|m>=2*\<varphi\><rsub|m>>; here
  <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>=\<theta\>*c<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>
  is the coefficient for this step. The small fraction <math|\<theta\>> is
  chosen below before the ratios.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Real parameter estimates><label|lem:D-real-lines> For every line
    as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-coupling-stencil>, the selected local
    maps satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-stencil-bound><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*F<around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>*\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;\<frak-R\><rsub|0>/2|)>,<space|2em><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|\<nu\>>c<rsub|\<nu\>>*\<cal-D\><around|(|s<rsub|\<nu\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varphi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-s\><around|(|c,s;\<frak-R\><rsub|0>|)>,
    </equation>

    with <math|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m><wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|m>>.
    The radius <math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>> can be arbitrarily large. The Wilson
    map satisfies the first estimate with the same
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Finite differences recover the low line coefficients from the bound of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-coupling-stencil>, as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-stencil-recovery>; the high ones are paid by
    the growing radius; the three linear conversions and fusion then pass the
    bound to the output. Recover the regular line coefficients by finite
    differences at zero. Put <math|\<frak-Q\>=\<frak-b\><rsup|1/256>/2>.
    Choose an integer <math|K> with <math|<around|(|K+1|)>/256+1/4\<gtr\>11/10>;
    we take <math|K=300>. Split after degree <math|K> and interpolate the
    finite part at <math|K+1> fixed distinct points of
    <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>>. The norm of this finite interpolation matrix
    gives a constant <math|L<rsub|0>>, depending only on
    <math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>> and <math|K>, independent of the coupling and
    volume. The radius-<math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>> coefficient sum is bounded
    by

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|0>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>+<around|(|1+L<rsub|0>|)>*<around|(|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>/\<frak-Q\>|)><rsup|301>*\<frak-b\><rsup|-1/4><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For the tail each degree <math|n\<geqslant\>301> contributes
    <math|<around|(|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>/\<frak-Q\>|)><rsup|n>>; its geometric
    denominator is bounded once <math|\<frak-Q\>\<geqslant\>2*\<frak-R\><rsub|0>>.
    The tail has rate

    <\equation*>
      -301/256-1/4=-365/256\<less\>-11/10<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Construct the preparatory families with <math|\<theta\>> replaced by
    <math|\<theta\>*\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>/<around|[|4*<around|(|1+L<rsub|0>|)>|]>>,
    so that<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-early-bound> holds with
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|aux>><rsub|m>=\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/<around|[|4*<around|(|1+L<rsub|0>|)>|]>>
    in place of <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>>. Since this
    only decreases the bound, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-preliminary> holds
    as stated, and no identity changes. Choose this fraction before the
    ratio, and raise the threshold until the tail is at most
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/4>.
    The radius-<math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>> bound is then at most
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/2>.
    Projection by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-projection-bound> gives a scalar bound at
    most <math|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|pre>><rsub|m>/t<rsub|m><rsup|2>>.
    The scalar reset in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C8> is exactly
    this extracted coefficient.

    For the regular output, apply its three linear conversions with total
    bound <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>>. Choose <math|\<theta\>> so the
    radius-<math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>> preparatory contribution is at most
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024> after this
    conversion, and raise the threshold so the high path term contributes at
    most another <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|reg>>/1024>.
    Explicitly it suffices to choose the real-interval coefficient so that
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|0>*L<rsub|0>*\<theta\>*c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/2048>
    and then to use the tail reserve just proved. For the forced output,
    retain its coefficient majorant from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-coupling-stencil>;
    subtraction adds logarithmic factors with the bounds in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>. At fixed <math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>>, their
    radius-<math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>> sums have the same small attachment
    parameter, with a constant depending on <math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>>. Fusion
    therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-K\><rsub|m><around|(|K|)><rsub|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>/2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,0>*exp
      <around|{|-\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|out>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>|}>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>/1024
    </equation*>

    for a smaller <math|\<kappa\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|out>>\<gtr\>0> and a
    larger threshold. Here <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|m>> is the forced norm at the
    output exponent <math|\<sigma\>>, and the subscript denotes the parameter
    coefficient sum at that radius. The exponent <math|\<sigma\>+1> of the
    preparatory bound supplies the extra power used in fusion. The maximum
    norm<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-common-norm> is thus at most <math|1/512>, and can
    be made smaller than the prescribed <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>>.
    This derives both the order-zero bound and the full bound on parameter
    evaluation formulas. For Wilson choose
    <math|4*\<theta\><rsub|<math-up|W>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>>
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-coupling-stencil> and reduce its radius to
    <math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>/2>.

    Since <math|\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1> and <math|c<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1/64>,
    the scalar normalization gives

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>/32|)><refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|-1/10>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m><wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|G>*\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>><refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|9/10><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The second inequality follows by comparison with an integral, since the
    reference-coupling increments have a fixed positive lower bound. Divide
    by <math|b<rsub|m>=<refbeta><rsub|m><rsup|31/32>> and raise the entry
    threshold until <math|C<rsub|G>*\<eta\><rsub|<math-up|par>>*<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>><rsup|-11/160>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    This proves the weighted scalar bound.
  </proof>

  The real parameter estimates yield norm-convergent multilinear expansions
  in the common completed spaces.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local analytic estimates><label|lem:D-analytic-step> At every
    point of the real domain above, the centered maps <math|F<rsub|N,m>>,
    <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>> and <math|F<rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|W>>> have
    convergent real multilinear expansions on the ball of radius <math|1/4>.
    Their degree-<math|k> coefficients, <math|k\<geqslant\>1>, have norms at
    most <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>>, <math|<wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|m>> and
    <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>>, respectively, where
    <math|<wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>0> is independent of
    <math|N,p> and

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m><wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|m><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-stencil-recovery> turns the bounds of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-real-lines> into coefficient bounds. The
    larger real domain preceding<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-analytic-domain> supplies
    the neighbouring centres required in the recovery lemma. Apply it with
    <math|r=1/4> and <math|R=\<frak-R\><rsub|0>/2> for the activity map, and
    <math|R=\<frak-R\><rsub|0>> for the scalar correction. Their coefficient
    bounds are respectively

    <\equation*>
      2*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>*<around|(|32*e/\<frak-R\><rsub|0>|)><rsup|k>,<space|2em>2*\<varphi\><rsub|m>*<around|(|16*e/\<frak-R\><rsub|0>|)><rsup|k><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Choose <math|\<frak-R\><rsub|0>\<geqslant\>128*e> before the ratios.
    Since <math|2*\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|map>>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
    these are the asserted bounds. The same argument applies to Wilson.
    Completion preserves each coefficient, its norm and its diagonal
    identity. The fixed permutation subspace preserves equivariance.
  </proof>

  The strict path reserve also permits a comparison across cutoffs at a fixed
  level.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Continuity in the cutoff><label|lem:D-cutoff-continuity> Fix
    <math|m,p>. If <math|<around|(|\<frak-x\><rsup|<around|(|N|)>>,A<rsup|<around|(|N|)>>|)>\<to\><around|(|\<frak-x\>,A|)>>
    in <math|<R>\<times\>\<cal-X\><rsub|m+1,p>> within the domain of the
    maps, then <math|F<rsub|N,m>*<around|(|\<frak-x\><rsup|<around|(|N|)>>,A<rsup|<around|(|N|)>>|)>>
    converges in <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>> and
    <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m><around|(|\<frak-x\><rsup|<around|(|N|)>>,A<rsup|<around|(|N|)>>|)>>
    converges in <math|<R>>. The path coefficients of
    <math|<pathaction><rsub|N,m>> converge in the weighted path norm with
    exponent <math|w<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>, being bounded
    uniformly in <math|N> at the strictly larger exponent of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The path coefficients converge degree by degree through the affine
    coefficient maps below; the connected weights converge by dominated
    convergence, and the remaining operations are linear or have a strict
    attachment margin. Compare first the quadratic rows and then the cubic,
    quartic and quadratic loop kernels in that order. The free forms, their
    row factors and the kernels <math|W<rsub|0>>, <math|T> converge by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>; placing the coefficients of
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|N,m>> on coordinate paths
    (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-C>) gives convergence of the
    quadratic rows. The resolvent identity and the uniform inverse bound of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows> give convergence of the remaining
    fixed-window inverses. In each subsequent comparison fix these rows and
    all preceding kernels. Let <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|a>> be the affine
    coefficient space for the kernel of the current degree, with its
    prescribed symmetries and cancellation identities. Divide its weighted
    coefficient norm by the stage allowance. The corresponding coefficient
    map, denoted <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a>>, obeys

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-affine-kernel>\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a>*<around|(|K+\<lambda\>*<around|(|H-K|)>|)>=\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K|)>+\<lambda\>*<around|(|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|H|)>-\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For the cubic coefficient this follows by retaining degree three in the
    stationary substitution: it is linear in the incoming cubic kernel. At
    degree four, terms quadratic in the cubic kernel are fixed, and the
    incoming quartic kernel occurs once. The quadratic loop coefficient is
    affine in its incoming loop kernel, with the preceding classical kernels
    fixed. These are the degree extractions in
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>\U<reference|lem:C7>.

    Take the ratio threshold of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4> for the
    enlarged input path bound <math|64*A<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>,
    and, for the enlarged quadratic loop bound
    <math|64*\<Lambda\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|loop>>\<leqslant\>128*\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>,
    the threshold in the proof of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>
    with <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|pb>>*\<ell\><rsup|-2>\<leqslant\>1/256>,
    so that <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>+<tfrac|1|256>\<cdot\>128*\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>=\<Lambda\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>.
    These choices precede the coupling threshold. With the preceding kernels
    fixed, the coefficient constructions then accept normalized input size
    <math|64> and give normalized output size at most one. The enlarged input
    must still belong to <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|a>> and satisfy these bounds.
    For inputs <math|K,H\<in\>\<cal-V\><rsub|a>> of normalized size at most
    one put <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>=<around|\<\|\|\>|H-K|\<\|\|\>>>. If
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>\<gtr\>0>, the extrapolated input
    <math|K<rsub|\<ast\>>=K+<around|(|32/\<delta\><rsub|K>|)>*<around|(|H-K|)>>
    belongs to the same affine space and has norm at most
    <math|33\<less\>64>. Its linear cancellation and symmetry conditions hold
    because their homogeneous differences form a linear subspace. Hence its
    stage bound applies. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-affine-kernel> gives

    <\equation*>
      <frac|32|\<delta\><rsub|K>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|H|)>-\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>|\<\|\|\>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-T\><rsub|m><rsup|a><around|(|K|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The output difference is at most <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>/16\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|K>/4>;
    at <math|\<delta\><rsub|K>=0> it is zero by the same affine identity.
    This calculation concerns one kernel, with all preceding kernels fixed.

    For two cutoffs, the change of those fixed preceding kernels contributes
    an error <math|e<rsub|N,N<rprime|'>,i>> tending to zero at each fixed
    level <math|i>, once their preceding comparisons have been made. The
    normalized difference of the current kernels therefore satisfies
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|i+1>/4+e<rsub|N,N<rprime|'>,i>>.
    Truncate this coefficient recursion at level <math|k>, retaining any
    terminal kernel in <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|a>> of normalized size at most
    one there. Its terminal difference is at most two, and iteration gives

    <\equation*>
      \<delta\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1/4|)><rsup|k-m>+<big|sum><rsub|i=m><rsup|k-1><around|(|1/4|)><rsup|i-m>*e<rsub|N,N<rprime|'>,i><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    At fixed <math|k> the sum tends to zero; then <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>
    proves the Cauchy property, successively for each degree.

    Retain now finitely many addresses and finite path lengths. Window
    covariances and means on these sets converge with their derivatives by
    the uniform inverse bound and the resolvent identity. Stationary
    corrections use this same inverse, and determinant differences are
    integrals of traces of resolvent differences.
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>\U<reference|lem:C7> express the path
    coefficients by these kernels. The stronger-weight clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1> bounds these coefficients uniformly in
    <math|N> at an exponent <math|w<rprime|'>\<gtr\>w>, with
    <math|w=w<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>. A tail of length at
    least <math|l> at exponent <math|w> is bounded by its uniform
    <math|w<rprime|'>> norm times <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|w<rprime|'>-w|)>*l>>.
    Thus finite-coefficient convergence and uniformly small tails prove path
    convergence with a strict reserve.

    For the connected weights, write both integrals in the same fixed
    physical coordinates and subtract their densities. The converging means
    and precisions make these Gaussian densities converge pointwise. Their
    strict positive lower bounds give a common integrable Gaussian
    exponential majorant, including the fixed derivative scores; combine it
    with <math|G<rsub|M>> from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-D2> and the
    weighted input discrepancy. Dominated convergence therefore gives
    convergence for a finite collection. The rooted estimates bound the
    discarded collections uniformly. Localization uses only five fixed
    auxiliary tori, finite linear combinations and such uniformly bounded
    tails. Subtraction is linear, and fusion has a strict attachment margin.
    Consequently input coordinates converging
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-common-norm> and converging normalized couplings give
    outputs and corrections converging in the same common spaces.
  </proof>

  For two histories at the same <math|N,p>, let
  <math|\<frak-x\><rsub|m>,A<rsub|m>> and
  <math|\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|m>,A<rprime|'><rsub|m>> be these
  coordinates and set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:D-history-distance>\<frak-d\>=max<rsub|0\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>
    max <around|{|<around|\||\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|m>-\<frak-x\><rsub|m>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rprime|'><rsub|m>-A<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,p>|}>,<space|2em>\<delta\>=\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|0>-\<beta\><rsub|0><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The parameter series below belong to <math|\<frak-S\><around|(|X|)>=<around|{|<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>z<rsup|k>*X<rsub|k>:<big|sum><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>\<infty\>|}>>,
  with norm <math|<big|sum><rsub|k><around|\<\|\|\>|X<rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>>.
  Evaluation has norm at most one for real
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. This space is complete, and its
  Cauchy product is bounded by the product of the norms.

  For the series equations fix a history reaching
  <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>, whose existence is proved in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.2>, and set
  <math|z<rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-x\><rsub|m>>. Write
  <math|\<frak-u\><rsub|m>> for the perturbation of
  <math|\<frak-x\><rsub|m>>, <math|W<rsub|m>> for that of <math|A<rsub|m>>,
  and <math|\<frak-e\>> for the endpoint series. For any map <math|F> let
  <math|<wide|F|^>> be its centered series. The simultaneous equations are

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|N>>|<cell|=<wide|F|^><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|W>><around|(|\<frak-u\><rsub|N>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|W<rsub|m>>|<cell|=<wide|F|^><rsub|N,m>*<around|(|\<frak-u\><rsub|m+1>,W<rsub|m+1>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<less\>N|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<frak-u\><rsub|m>>|<cell|=<around|(|2*z<rsub|m>|)><rsup|-1>*<around*|[|b<rsub|m><rsup|-1>*<around*|(|\<frak-e\>-<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m><wide|\<cal-D\>|^><rsub|N,i><around|(|\<frak-u\><rsub|i+1>,W<rsub|i+1>|)>|)>-\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-u\><rsub|m><rsup|2>|]><space|0.17em>.<eq-number><label|eq:D-series-map>>>>>
  </align>

  The last line is obtained by expanding the square
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-coordinate>; it retains its quadratic term.

  These right sides define a map on the product of the spaces
  <math|\<frak-S\><around|(|<R>|)>> and <math|\<frak-S\><around|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>|)>>,
  with the maximum of their series norms. The real endpoint interval is

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-J\>=<around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>-\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|min>/4,<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|min>/4|]><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The following is the estimate used by Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>.
  It applies to the selected step maps, their actual Wilson initial family
  and the same fixed address spaces.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Analytic endpoint equations><label|lem:D-E1> The histories above
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-d\>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<delta\>|\|>/b<rsub|min>+8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-e\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<frak-S\><around|(|<R>|)>>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|min>/4>,
    equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-series-map> preserve the closed
    radius-<math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>> ball in the product of the spaces
    <math|\<frak-S\><around|(|<R>|)>> and
    <math|\<frak-S\><around|(|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>|)>> and contract by at most
    <math|1/2>. Their unique solution evaluates to the selected real
    recursion.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The small coefficients of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-analytic-step> make
    every step map nearly constant, and summing the scalar recursion from the
    endpoint cancels the coefficients <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>>. The
    centered expansions of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-analytic-step> give

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rprime|'><rsub|m>-A<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*max
      <around|{|<around|\||\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|m+1>-\<frak-x\><rsub|m+1>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rprime|'><rsub|m+1>-A<rsub|m+1>|\<\|\|\>>|}>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|N,m>-\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|m>*max
      <around|{|<around|\||\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|m+1>-\<frak-x\><rsub|m+1>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rprime|'><rsub|m+1>-A<rsub|m+1>|\<\|\|\>>|}><space|0.17em>.<eq-number><label|eq:D-local-lipschitz>>>>>
    </align>

    For distances at most <math|1/4> these follow by subtracting the series
    and summing <math|k*<around|(|1/4|)><rsup|k-1>>. For larger distances the
    separate bounds <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>/2>
    and <math|<around|\||\<cal-D\><rsub|N,m>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|m>/2>
    give the displayed, weaker constants. The Wilson top map has the first
    bound as well.

    Write <math|z<rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-x\><rsub|m>> and
    <math|z<rprime|'><rsub|m>=\<chi\><rsub|m>*\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|m>>
    in this proof. Both are in <math|<around|[|17/20,23/20|]>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-coupling-coordinate> gives the exact identity

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|m>-\<beta\><rsub|m>=b<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|m>-\<frak-x\><rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|z<rprime|'><rsub|m>+z<rsub|m>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Summing the scalar recursion from the endpoint cancels
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,i>> between the two histories, and hence

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|m>*<around|\||\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|m>-\<frak-x\><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<delta\>|\|>+<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m><around|\||\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|N,i>-\<cal-D\><rsub|N,i>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<delta\>|\|>+4*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|m>*\<frak-d\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Take the maximum together with the activity estimates and use
    <math|b<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|min>>. This proves the asserted
    inequality before absorbing its last term. In particular
    <math|\<delta\>=0> gives <math|\<frak-d\>=0>, and otherwise
    <math|\<frak-d\>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||\<delta\>|\|>/b<rsub|min>>. Thus
    real evaluation can be identified by uniqueness.

    On the product ball of radius <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>> the series
    pair <math|<around|(|\<frak-u\><rsub|m>,W<rsub|m>|)>> has norm at most
    <math|2*\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>1/4>. A centered series with
    coefficients bounded by <math|c> consequently has norm at most
    <math|c/2>, and the difference of its two evaluations is bounded by
    <math|2*c> times the difference of its arguments. For the scalar prefix
    the latter gives <math|4*<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m><wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|i>>
    times the product distance, because the two coordinates contribute at
    most twice that distance. Also

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-u\><rsub|m><rsup|2>-\<frak-v\><rsub|m><rsup|2>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-u\><rsub|m>-\<frak-v\><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|2*z<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1>, the scalar part
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-series-map> has norm at most

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>/4+g<rsub|\<ast\>>+\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|2>\<less\>\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>
    </equation*>

    and Lipschitz constant at most <math|4*g<rsub|\<ast\>>+2*\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>=5/32\<less\>1/2>.
    The activity part has norm at most <math|g<rsub|\<ast\>>/2\<less\>\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>>
    and Lipschitz constant at most <math|4*g<rsub|\<ast\>>\<less\>1/2>. Thus
    the map preserves the closed ball and contracts there. Starting at zero,
    its successive differences are bounded by a geometric series with ratio
    <math|1/2>; completeness gives its unique fixed point.

    Evaluation commutes with sums, products and each absolutely convergent
    multilinear series. It turns the fixed point into the real recursion and
    Wilson start. The comparison already proved identifies it with the
    selected history. The endpoint width <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|min>/4>
    is smaller than the real shooting width:
    <math|b<rsub|min>=\<chi\><rsub|0>*<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/128>.
    Hence the whole closed real interval stays in that shooting interval.
  </proof>

  Only the endpoint coupling is compact in the next argument; candidate
  limits are compared by geometric truncation of the activities, and no
  compactness of a ball in the activity Banach space is used.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Fixed-level limits of couplings and
    activities><label|lem:D-fixed-level> For fixed <math|p,m>, the selected
    scalar couplings and activity coordinates in <math|\<cal-X\><rsub|m,p>>
    converge along every full cutoff sequence with convergent endpoints in
    the interval of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>. The prescribed paths
    converge with the strict reserve of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-cutoff-continuity>.
    The limits are independent of subsequences. The additive vacuum scalars
    are excluded from this convergence statement.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Compactness is used only for the scalar coordinates; the activities are
    controlled by truncating the recursion at a fixed level, which costs a
    geometric factor by<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-local-lipschitz>, and by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-cutoff-continuity> for the truncated orbit.
    First extract a subsequence on which every scalar coordinate
    <math|\<frak-x\><rsub|i>> converges. Each fixed coordinate is in a
    compact real interval by <math|17/20\<leqslant\>\<chi\><rsub|i>*\<frak-x\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>23/20>;
    a diagonal selection gives the simultaneous subsequence.

    Fix a stopping index <math|k\<gtr\>m>. At level <math|k> replace the
    activity coordinate by zero and run the same selected maps downward using
    the unchanged scalar coordinates. Write
    <math|A<rsub|m><rsup|<around|[|k|]>>> for the resulting coordinate. The
    first inequality in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-local-lipschitz>, applied
    successively at equal scalar inputs, gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-truncated-orbit><around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|m>-A<rsub|m><rsup|<around|[|k|]>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m,p>\<leqslant\><around|(|8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|k-m><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The terminal difference is at most one. For fixed <math|k> the truncated
    coordinates converge by a finite downward induction, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-cutoff-continuity>. The geometric bound is
    uniform in the cutoff and tends to zero as <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>. Given
    <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, choose <math|k> to make both truncation
    errors smaller than <math|\<varepsilon\>/3>, then choose the cutoffs so
    that the truncated difference is smaller than <math|\<varepsilon\>/3>.
    Thus the actual activity coordinates are Cauchy in their common completed
    spaces on the extracted subsequence. The scalar corrections converge by
    the second inequality in<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-local-lipschitz> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-cutoff-continuity>.

    It remains to identify two possible subsequential limits. Denote them by
    <math|\<frak-x\><rsub|i>,A<rsub|i>,\<cal-D\><rsub|i>> and
    <math|\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|i>,A<rprime|'><rsub|i>,\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|i>>.
    Their common endpoint is <math|\<beta\><rsub|0>>. Passing to the limit in
    the finite scalar recursion gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:D-limit-prefix>\<beta\><rsub|i>-\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|i>=-<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>i><around|(|\<cal-D\><rsub|j>-\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|j>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The limits of <math|\<alpha\><rsub|N,j>> cancel because the prescribed
    paths have one limit. Put

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>=sup<rsub|i\<geqslant\>0> max
      <around|{|<around|\||\<frak-x\><rsub|i>-\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|i>|\|>,<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|i>-A<rprime|'><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|i,p>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This supremum is finite with bound <math|1/4>. Indeed the activity
    difference is at most <math|1/4>, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-limit-prefix>
    together with <math|<around|\||\<cal-D\><rsub|j>|\|>,<around|\||\<cal-D\><rprime|'><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide|\<varphi\>|\<bar\>><rsub|j>/2>
    gives <math|<around|\||\<frak-x\><rsub|i>-\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
    after division by <math|b<rsub|i>> as in the previous proof. Passing the
    one-step estimates to the limit gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A<rsub|i>-A<rprime|'><rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
    Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-limit-prefix>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:D-local-lipschitz> give
    <math|<around|\||\<frak-x\><rsub|i>-\<frak-x\><rprime|'><rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
    Hence <math|\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>>,
    so <math|\<frak-d\><rsub|\<infty\>>=0>.

    Every subsequence therefore has a further subsequence with these same
    scalar and activity limits. If the full sequence failed to converge at
    some fixed level, a subsequence staying a positive distance from the
    limit would contradict the preceding construction. This proves full
    convergence. The continuous map <math|x\<mapsto\><refbeta><rsub|m><around|(|\<chi\><rsub|m>*x|)><rsup|2>>
    gives convergence of <math|\<beta\><rsub|m>>. The path assertion is
    already part of the cutoff comparison, with a strictly larger exponent
    than the one defining the local charts. None of these arguments compares
    the vacuum scalars, which disappear from the normalized activity and
    coupling equations.
  </proof>

  <section|Trajectories, marked fields and ultraviolet
  convergence><label|sec:partII-E>

  This section constructs the trajectories and the renormalized insertions
  used in Part<nbsp>I, and proves their volume-uniform bounds and full
  fixed-volume limits: Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>,
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> and Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>.
  It also supplies the cutoff equation identities, coefficient corrections
  and finite-rank comparisons from which Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>
  builds the quotient fields. All these results concern the Wilson measures
  of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D02> and the insertions of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>.

  Three different tasks are involved, and each has its own mechanism. A
  continuous shooting argument chooses the bare coupling so that the
  trajectory reaches one prescribed coarse coupling
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.1>). Weighted estimates for the
  specified recursion give uniqueness and analytic dependence near that
  endpoint. Finally, at fixed volume, convergence of the effective
  coordinates at each fixed level is combined with a bound on the error made
  by stopping the insertion transfer there
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.3>). The additive vacuum scalar
  cancels from normalized expectations, so its convergence is never needed.

  The main difficulty in the bounds of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2>
  is differentiation in position. Each lattice difference introduces an
  inverse spacing, so a bound for undifferentiated insertions does not
  suffice. Local comparison replaces a differentiated insertion by one of
  higher degree, with a negligible chessboard error. Exact equation
  identities remove its equation component. A finite matrix of free Maxwell
  correlation tests bounds the remaining coefficient. This argument takes
  place at finite cutoff, before any continuum field space has been
  constructed.

  Throughout, the group, metric, penalty, scales and admissible classes are
  those of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization> and
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. The trajectories are built from the
  path actions of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>\U<reference|lem:C2>, which
  fix the classical and one-loop terms, the Wilson initialization of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>, and the inductive step of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, which carries an admissible
  representation one level down. Additional dependence of constants is
  indicated. Write <math|M<rsub|N,p>=<latmoment><rsub|N,p>> and
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|F|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>=<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p>>.

  For an insertion label <math|v=<around|(|d,c|)>> put
  <math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y|)>=O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y|)>>.
  Physical points <math|x> in a moment are read as lattice sites
  <math|x/a<rsub|N>>, modulo <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>>. We use the torus
  separation

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|sep><rsub|<math-up|tor>><around|(|y|)>=min<rsub|i\<ne\>j>
    min<rsub|z\<in\>L<rsub|p><Z><rsup|4>><around|\||a<rsub|N>*<around|(|y<rsub|i>-y<rsub|j>|)>+z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>,
  </equation*>

  with value <math|+\<infty\>> for at most one slot. Multilinear
  interpolation on the physical grid means the affine interpolation in each
  coordinate of each grid cube, using its vertex values.

  <subsection|Trajectories and tuning><label|sec:partII-E.1>

  The entry levels allow the ratios and stage constants to increase
  sufficiently slowly. Besides the conditions of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>, we use the following quantitative
  choices. Let <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>> be the direct clipping
  coefficient at stage<nbsp><math|j>, and let
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|G><rsup|<math-up|mat>>> be the real dimension of the
  ambient matrix space. Put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-vacuum-majorant>Q<rsub|j><around|(|b|)>=2*<around|(|2+4*A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)>+1+6*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>+<around|(|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)><rsup|2>|)>+4*\<nu\><rsub|G><rsup|<math-up|mat>>*<around|(|log
    16+<tfrac|1|2>log b|)>,<space|2em>q<rsub|m>=Q<rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>> is the path bound of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>. For an admissible unmarked representation
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>> on <math|V> sites write
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m>=v<rsub|<math-up|vac>,m>*V-<pathaction><rsub|N,m>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>
  and <math|K<rsub|m,Y>> for its forced activities. The next estimate bounds
  what it costs, per site, to replace the positive density by absolute values
  in a normalized integral.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Normalization cost><label|lem:E-normalization-cost> For every
    sufficiently late level <math|m> and every admissible unmarked
    representation <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>> on <math|V> sites,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-vacuum-ratio><frac|<big|int>e<rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>>*<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-A\>><big|prod><rsub|Y\<in\>\<cal-A\>><around|\||K<rsub|m,Y><around|(|U|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*U|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|m>*<space|0.17em>d*U>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|V*q<rsub|m>>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|<wide|g|^><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|-1/2>>. The
    scalar-free unmarked exponent is at most <math|V*E<rsub|m>>, and its
    absolute forced sum is at most <math|e<rsup|V*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>>>
    with <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>\<leqslant\>1>; at
    every sufficiently late level the path bounds give
    <math|E<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2+4*A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>.
    Restrict the denominator to links of chord at most
    <math|<wide|g|^><rsub|m>/4>. There are no flags there, the forced sum is
    one, and its scalar-free exponent is at least
    <math|-V*<around|(|E<rsub|m>+6*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>+<around|(|A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)><rsup|2>|)>|)>>.
    The elementary Haar-ball bound gives this set measure at least
    <math|<around|(|<wide|g|^><rsub|m>/16|)><rsup|4*V*\<nu\><rsub|G><rsup|<math-up|mat>>>>.
    The common vacuum scalar in numerator and denominator cancels, so the
    ratio is at most

    <\equation*>
      exp <space|-0.17em><around*|{|V*<around*|[|2*E<rsub|m>+\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>+6*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|m>>+<around|(|A<rsub|J<rsub|m>><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)><rsup|2>|)>+4*\<nu\><rsub|G><rsup|<math-up|mat>>*log
      <around|(|16/<wide|g|^><rsub|m>|)>|]>|}>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|V*q<rsub|m>>.
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  The per-site cost <math|q<rsub|m>=O<around|(|1+log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>|)>> comes from the Haar-ball loss
  <math|<wide|g|^><rsub|m><rsup|-\<nu\><rsub|G><rsup|<math-up|mat>>>> on
  every link. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-integral> improves it to a
  constant depending only on the stage: in an axial gauge the denominator
  pays the Haar-ball loss on <math|3*V+1> links and the numerator recovers it
  on <math|3*V-3> one-link integrals, so only a loss of order <math|log
  <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>> for the whole torus remains. The source
  comparison needs that improvement, because its gain per cell is only a
  power of <math|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>>. Enlarge <math|Q<rsub|j>> at the
  finitely many initial levels, so that <math|q<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>1>
  and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-ratio> hold at every level. The choices include

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-marker-scales>B<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|i+1>=o<around|(|s<rsub|i+1>|)>,<space|2em>\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|i>|)>.
  </equation>

  Define <math|k<around|(|m|)>> to be the first <math|k\<geqslant\>m> with
  <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>.

  The short-distance comparison uses the following comparison windows of
  levels. For <math|b\<gtr\>0> and a level<nbsp><math|t> in
  stage<nbsp><math|j>, write <math|L=\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>> and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-window>h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|<around|(|b+2|)>*log
    <cutoffsize><rsub|t>|log L>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>v<rsub|t>=<around*|\<lceil\>|<frac|10*log
    B<rsub|t>|log L>|\<rceil\>>,<space|1em>k<rsub|t>=t-h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>-v<rsub|t>.
  </equation>

  The numerical requirements of the marked proofs serve three purposes:
  contraction of one-insertion remainders
  (items<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns>\U<reference|it:E-match>), control of
  replicated sources and comparison with the free field
  (items<nbsp><reference|it:E-log>\U<reference|it:E-entries>), and the
  initialization of insertions (item<nbsp><reference|it:E-birth>). At each
  stage the finite-dimensional and support constants are fixed first, then
  the block ratio, and finally the coupling entry. The constants named here
  are introduced with their estimates; we list them so that the order of
  choice is visible in one place.

  For each stage<nbsp><math|j>, let <math|K<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> be a
  nondecreasing bound for the leading coefficient maps and their inverses
  through that stage's finitely many degrees and symmetry averages. These
  bounds are fixed before the column bounds<nbsp><math|C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|j>>.
  Let <math|A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>> bound the finite basis
  recombination in degree<nbsp><math|d>, and let
  <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> bound the local marked error of the
  one-step estimate before the ratio is chosen (proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>). Each step below has
  <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>> and <math|L=\<ell\><rsub|m>>; in
  items<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph>\U<reference|it:E-match> and in the proofs
  of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>,
  <reference|lem:E-local-comparison> and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-coarse-bounded>
  we write <math|K=K<rsub|j>>, <math|S=C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|j>> and
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>=L<rsup|-3/8>>. For the retained incoming
  regular term, and for the unsubtracted incoming contribution in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-J>, let
  <math|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|supp>>\<in\><N>> bound the enlargement of its
  projected incoming read support in units of <math|B<rsub|m>>, over the
  finitely many adjacent stage pairs with fine stage<nbsp><math|j>. Put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-support-conversion>\<cal-A\><rsub|m>=exp
    <space|-0.17em><around*|{|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2|)>*<around|(|2*R<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|<math-up|supp>>+13|)><rsup|4>|}>.
  </equation>

  This factor converts an incoming support weight into the outgoing weight.
  Indeed, for fine and coarse covering numbers <math|s<rsub|f>,s<rsub|c>>,
  the connected-cover bound gives

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L*s<rsub|c>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|(|L+10*s<rsub|f>|)>*<around|(|2*R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|supp>>+13|)><rsup|4>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2|)>*s<rsub|c>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>log
    \<cal-A\><rsub|m>+<tfrac|1|2>\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*s<rsub|f>,>>>>
  </align*>

  provided <math|20*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2|)>*<around|(|2*R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|supp>>+13|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>.
  Thus half the incoming exponent remains for the other support factors. The
  constant <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>2> includes these bounds and
  the finite source comparison constants. In
  item<nbsp><reference|it:E-birth>, <math|C<rsub|d>> is the section bound
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-global-bound>. For the source estimates, choose an
  integer <math|q<rsup|<math-up|ht>><rsub|d>> bounding the powers of the
  inverse health threshold in degree<nbsp><math|d>, and put

  <\equation*>
    \<zeta\><rsub|d,i>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-<around|(|q<rsup|<math-up|ht>><rsub|d>+4|)>>.
  </equation*>

  This allowance controls attachments of other marked factors to a
  distinguished mark. Let <math|A<rsub|j,d>> bound the finite section,
  derivative and support-conversion constants in these attachments at
  stage<nbsp><math|j>, and let <math|\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>
  be the attachment threshold in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> for
  the step at level<nbsp><math|i>. Decrease it so that
  <math|\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1/4>. All these
  constants are fixed before the coupling entry.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments> uses

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-attachment-allowance>2*A<rsub|j,d>*<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|q<rsup|<math-up|ht>><rsub|d>>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>/2,<space|2em>j=J<rsub|i+1>,<space|1em>d\<leqslant\>J<rsub|i>.
  </equation>

  The factor <math|<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>> bounds the spatial sum
  from marked anchors to a site; it is derived in the proof of that lemma.
  For transport to finer levels we also require

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-source-allowance-transport><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>*\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>.
  </equation>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><label|it:E-columns> <em|Coefficient and support bounds.> Choose
    nondecreasing columns with <math|C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>64*K<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>>,
    above the derivative factorials and finite basis-recombination constants
    at stage<nbsp><math|j>. The support exponent is
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>=100>; we also retain the estimates at every
    exponent in <math|<around|[|98,100|]>>.

    <item><label|it:E-graph> <em|One-insertion contraction>
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>). Choose
    <math|L<rsup|3/5>\<gtr\>2*C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>,
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>L> and
    <math|20*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2|)>*<around|(|2*R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|supp>>+13|)><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>L*\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>.
    The local marked error <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>>
    satisfies <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-2/5>>,
    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-graph-reserve>\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>*<around|(|1+S|)>*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>,<space|1em>2*K*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*<around|(|1+S|)>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>4*S*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>2*K\<leqslant\>S,<space|1em>4*K<rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m>>.
    </equation>

    <item><label|it:E-match> <em|Backward matching>
    (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-coarse-bounded>). Require

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-matching-choices>4*K*<space|0.17em>32*<around|(|1+S|)><rsup|2>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|4*K<rsup|2>+1|)>*L<rsup|1/8>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation>

    For each auxiliary section through the stage's degrees, require also
    <math|2*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|j>|)>*H<rsub|j,d>*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>*L<rsup|-1/8>\<leqslant\>1/4>,
    where <math|H<rsub|j,d>> bounds its normalized healthy derivatives.

    <item><label|it:E-log> <em|Accumulated transport losses.> Choose ratios
    to satisfy<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>, including every constant
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>>.

    <item><label|it:E-entries> <em|Gaussian comparison and source bounds>
    (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>\U<reference|lem:E-source-integral>).
    On a comparison window, for an arity bound <math|n\<geqslant\>2>, put
    <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|t>=<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|1/<around|(|8*n|)>>>.
    Let <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>> be the sum of the
    covariance, rounding, Taylor and localization errors in the fixed finite
    source comparison. Require

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-Gaussian-entry><math-ss|w><rsub|t><rsup|n>\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>+2>*\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em>Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>log
      <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/<around|(|16*n|)>,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<in\><around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>/2,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|]>*<space|1em><around|(|k<rsub|t>\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t|)>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>> is the stage normalization
    constant in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-comparison-cost>, enlarged to cover the
    source modes and amplitudes of the finitely many arity bounds
    <math|n\<geqslant\>2> used at stage<nbsp><math|j>, and the second bound
    is required for each of these<nbsp><math|n>. These constants are fixed
    first; the entry is then raised until <math|log
    <cutoffsize><rsub|t>\<geqslant\>16*n*<space|0.17em>Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>>.
    The first bound is imposed with the stronger smallness used
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-iteration>. The entry also meets the local
    marked bound in item<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph>, the forced-source
    bounds at every exponent between <math|98> and <math|100>,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance>\U<eqref|eq:E-source-allowance-transport>.
    The unmarked attachment sum in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> is
    at most <math|\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>/2>, and <math|64*log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>/B<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>1/4>.

    <item><label|it:E-birth> <em|Initialization of insertions>
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>). At every level<nbsp><math|m> and
    <math|d\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>>, require <math|B<rsub|m>> at least the
    radius of<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and
    <math|C<rsub|d>*t<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|\|>*\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>>-1|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>.

    <item><label|it:E-ratio-log> <em|Ratio below the logarithmic coupling>
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>). At every level<nbsp><math|m>,
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>>.
  </enumerate>

  The finite comparisons in item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries> exhaust
  bounded configurations with positive point separation, fixed arities and
  finite derivative orders as<nbsp><math|j> increases. Their errors and
  constants are calculated in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>;
  none depends on the period.

  The following choice gives both the sparse insertion grids and the windows
  used for free comparison.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Scale and period choices><label|lem:E1.1> All
    requirements<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns>\U<reference|it:E-ratio-log>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> can be met with

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<geqslant\>3<text|
      odd>,<space|2em>log \<ell\><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>,<space|2em>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0,<space|2em>4\<mid\>L<rsub|p>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    The sides <math|L<rsub|p>> increase strictly; every positive integer
    divides infinitely many. For every finite set of exponents
    <math|b\<geqslant\>1> and finite comparison requests, arbitrarily late
    terminal levels<nbsp><math|t> meet the comparison requirements
    simultaneously on their windows<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window>, with
    <math|k<rsub|t>\<to\>\<infty\>> and <math|J<rsub|i>=J<rsub|t>> throughout
    each window.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The choices follow the order of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>:
    stage constants, then ratios, then entries. First fix the entire sequence
    <math|K<rsub|j>> and the finite section bounds. Choose
    <math|C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>64*K<rsub|j+1><rsup|2>>,
    and then fix <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>> to include these columns and all
    source constants. Since <math|J<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m>+1>, this
    gives <math|4*K<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m>>>
    even at a stage transition. Next choose nondecreasing odd stage ratios
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>\<geqslant\>max <around|(|3,j|)>>
    large enough that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-log-cost><frac|\<omega\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|RG>>|log
      \<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>>\<leqslant\>min
      <around|(|<slope>/4,j<rsup|-2>|)>,<space|2em><frac|log
      \<cal-C\><rsub|j>|log \<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>>\<leqslant\>j<rsup|-2>.
    </equation>

    Each denominator can be increased after its numerator is fixed. The ratio
    requirements in items<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph>
    and<nbsp><reference|it:E-match> are lower bounds on this same later
    choice: <math|L<rsup|-2/5>/L<rsup|-3/8>=L<rsup|-1/40>\<to\>0> and
    <math|L<rsup|1/8>*L<rsup|-3/8>=L<rsup|-1/4>\<to\>0>. Choose also
    <math|L<rsup|3/5>\<gtr\>2*C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>>, so
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>/L\<less\><tfrac|1|2>L<rsup|-2/5>>.
    For this fixed <math|L>, raise the coupling entry until the absolute
    value of the <math|o<rsub|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>><around|(|1|)>> term
    in the one-step estimate is at most <math|<tfrac|1|2>L<rsup|-2/5>>. The
    local error <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>/L+o<rsub|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>><around|(|1|)>>
    is then at most <math|L<rsup|-2/5>>. The column and ratio choices also
    give the fixed auxiliary-section inequality. The support radii and
    exponents in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-support-conversion> are fixed
    before<nbsp><math|L>, so its compression condition and
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>L> are further fixed lower bounds on
    the ratio. Thus no column is chosen in response to a ratio or to a
    later-stage column.

    Choose consecutive constant-stage intervals, postponing each new stage
    until all its fixed coupling requirements hold. On a fixed stage
    <math|s<rsub|i>> grows linearly, while
    <math|B<rsub|i>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    <around|(|2+s<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>> and
    <math|q<rsub|i>=O<around|(|1+log <around|(|2+s<rsub|i>|)>|)>>. Thus

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<ell\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|i+1>/s<rsub|i+1>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|1em><text|on
      that stage as its entry is postponed>.
    </equation*>

    For the initial stage, choose <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> after its
    fixed ratio<nbsp><math|L<rsub|0>>. For
    <math|B<around|(|b|)>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    b|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>>, the quotient <math|B*<around|(|b+<slope>log
    L<rsub|0>|)><rsup|2>/B<around|(|b|)><rsup|2>> tends to one as
    <math|b\<to\>\<infty\>>. Choose <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> beyond a
    threshold where it is at most two for every
    <math|b\<geqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>. This covers every step of
    the first stage, including <math|i=0>. At a transition to a later ratio,
    impose the same bound at its entry and thereafter, with that ratio fixed.
    Impose the second condition of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> with bound
    <math|1/j> after entry to stage<nbsp><math|j>. Impose also
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|i>/j> and <math|log
    \<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>\<leqslant\>s<rsub|i>/j> at its entry.
    These are lower bounds on that entry. By item<nbsp>9 of
    Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>,
    <math|t<rsub|m><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>> and
    <math|<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m><rsup|d+1>\<to\>0>,
    so item<nbsp><reference|it:E-birth>, including the finite stencil-radius
    requirement, is another entry bound. Since <math|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> at a fixed ratio,
    item<nbsp><reference|it:E-ratio-log> is also an entry bound, imposed for
    both ratios that occur at the levels of a stage. At each fixed ratio, the
    same threshold formula gives <math|t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>\<to\>1> as
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>>; impose
    <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2> at entry
    and thereafter, with <math|t<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1> supplied by
    item<nbsp>7 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>. The preceding
    entry restrictions give both little-oh assertions and <math|log
    \<ell\><rsub|i>/s<rsub|i>\<to\>0>; <math|s<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>i*log 3> at
    every level.

    For each stage, the length <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> is <math|O<rsub|j><around|(|<around|(|log log
    <around|(|2+s<rsub|t>|)>|)><rsup|2>+log log
    <around|(|2+s<rsub|t>|)>|)>=o<around|(|t|)>>. Lengthen that
    constant-stage interval to contain a terminal point with the whole
    window<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> in the same stage and with its left
    endpoint beyond all earlier requested levels. Do this with the longest
    window <math|b=j>, all arities <math|n\<leqslant\>j> and the finitely
    many comparison orders at stage<nbsp><math|j>, on the compact sets of
    test configurations with point separations at least <math|1/j> and
    coordinates of absolute value at most<nbsp><math|j>. Every
    <math|1\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>j> uses a subinterval of this window. The
    length and error bounds are uniform for <math|b> in any fixed compact
    subinterval of <math|<around|[|1,j|]>>; take the maximum of the finite
    comparison constants before postponing the common terminal level. Their
    finitely many derivative bounds are uniform on each such compact set. The
    estimates<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-loss>\U<eqref|eq:E-source-iteration>
    show that the first requirement in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-Gaussian-entry> holds
    once that entry is late enough. The second is a lower bound on <math|log
    <cutoffsize><rsub|t>> after <math|Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>> is
    fixed. The total window length is <math|o<around|(|t|)>>, so its coupling
    variation is <math|o<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)>> and gives the
    third requirement. These are lower bounds on a later entry or interval
    length. Each fixed request is then met at arbitrarily large stages. The
    source requirements are also entry bounds. On a fixed stage, the left
    side of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance> is a fixed constant times
    <math|<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|-4>>
    and tends to zero. In<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-allowance-transport>,
    division by <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d,i+1>> leaves

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|-1>*<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)><rsup|q<rsup|<math-up|ht>><rsub|d>+4>,
    </equation*>

    which tends to zero at fixed ratio. Include both adjacent stages at a
    transition. There are finitely many degrees at each entry. The unmarked
    attachment sum tends to zero by the factor rates in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>, so its half-threshold requirement
    is another entry bound. These choices do not alter earlier intervals.
    Taking <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>>
    gives odd ratios, and <math|\<ell\><rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|st>>\<geqslant\>j>
    gives <math|log \<ell\><rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

    The level <math|k<around|(|m|)>> exists:
    <math|a<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> decreases by at least the factor
    <math|2/3> at each step. If <math|k<around|(|m|)>=u+1\<gtr\>m>,
    minimality gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-first-open>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|u>*B<rsub|u><rsup|2>.
    </equation>

    Consequently<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> gives
    <math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)>>.
    The case <math|k<around|(|m|)>=m> satisfies the same conclusion directly
    from <math|q<rsub|m>=o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>.

    Choose <math|L<rsub|p>=4*<around|(|M<rsub|<math-up|per>>+p+1|)>>. For
    <math|j=J<rsub|m+1>>, the required period inequalities, including
    item<nbsp>7 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>, are

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|L<rsub|p>*e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>,<space|2em>L<rsub|p>*e<rsup|s<rsub|m+1>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|<around|(|j+3|)>*\<ell\><rsub|m>+1|)>,>>|<row|<cell|L<rsub|p>*e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    The first follows by choosing <math|M<rsub|<math-up|per>>> large, since
    <math|<around|(|B<rsub|m>+1|)>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|m>>> is bounded. After
    canceling <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>> in the second, postpone each stage until
    <math|e<rsup|s<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>100*<around|(|j+4|)>>. For the third,
    <math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>> and
    <math|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>> are fixed before entry to
    stage<nbsp><math|j>, and <math|v<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>+B<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|h<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|per>>>*e<rsup|-s<rsub|m>>\<to\>0>
    on that stage. Postpone each later stage until this expression is at most
    one thereafter, and enlarge <math|M<rsub|<math-up|per>>> for the finite
    initial interval. This proves all three inequalities. The third supplies
    the period bound for the five-torus localization in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-localization>. The periods are strictly
    increasing, divisible by four, and every integer divides infinitely many
    of them.
  </proof>

  The next identities retain the normalization of the original Wilson
  integral through every integration.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Exact trajectory identities><label|lem:E1.2> For the trajectories
    constructed below with endpoint <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>> and every
    <math|m\<less\>N>,

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<rho\><rsub|N,p,N>=\<rho\><rsup|<math-up|W>><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>,<space|2em><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>-\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><rsup|<math-up|RG>>,>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>=Z<rsub|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|N>,\<beta\><rsub|N,p>>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    All representations are admissible, and the terminal integral has the
    same value.
  </lemma>

  The following proof also proves Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>.

  <\render-proof|Proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.2> and
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>>
    The trajectory is obtained by bracketing, level by level, along the
    continuous Wilson curve of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D3>, carried down by
    the continuous step of the specified recursion in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D2>; the drift margin comes
    from<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>. Set

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|m><rsup|->=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<tfrac|1|2><slope>s<rsub|m>,<space|2em>b<rsub|m><rsup|+>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+2<slope>s<rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    These intervals lie in the coupling bands <math|\<frak-B\><rsub|m>>.
    Suppose the fine endpoint couplings are <math|b<rsub|m+1><rsup|->> and
    <math|b<rsub|m+1><rsup|+>>. The drift estimate
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost> give coarse endpoint bounds

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|m><rsup|->\<leqslant\>b<rsub|m><rsup|->-<tfrac|1|4><slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>\<less\>b<rsub|m><rsup|->,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|m><rsup|+>\<geqslant\>b<rsub|m><rsup|+>+<tfrac|3|4><slope>log
      \<ell\><rsub|m>\<gtr\>b<rsub|m><rsup|+>.
    </equation*>

    For a continuous function whose endpoints bracket
    <math|<around|[|b<rsub|m><rsup|->,b<rsub|m><rsup|+>|]>>, restrict between
    its last lower hit before its first upper hit. Its values then remain in
    this interval. Restrict the parameter interval accordingly. Every
    preceding representation is restricted along with the new one, so all
    already obtained identities persist.

    Start at level<nbsp><math|N> with the Wilson curve restricted to
    <math|<around|[|b<rsub|N><rsup|->,b<rsub|N><rsup|+>|]>> and repeat this
    operation down to zero. Since <math|b<rsub|0><rsup|->=b<rsub|0><rsup|+>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>,
    it produces a trajectory with the required endpoint. The case <math|N=0>
    is the Wilson representation at <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>>. All
    retained representations and adjacent pairs belong to the chosen classes
    and relation because Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG> returns admissible
    representations whose adjacent pairs lie in the chosen relation.

    The exact density identity at each step is the one in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>, now applied only on the retained
    interval. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>, integration of the block
    kernel of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04> in its coarse argument is
    one. Fubini therefore preserves the integral, and the Wilson start gives
    its displayed value at every level. The path arrays were fixed
    before<nbsp><math|p> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>; restricting the
    parameter interval does not change them.

    Summing the drift from zero gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-running><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m>-<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>-<slope>s<rsub|m>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>m>\<omega\><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>><rsup|<math-up|RG>>.
    </equation>

    For a fixed splitting level<nbsp><math|k>, the tail is at most
    <math|sup<rsub|i\<geqslant\>k> J<rsub|i+1><rsup|-2>*s<rsub|m>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>. The initial sum is fixed. Divide by
    <math|s<rsub|m>>, let <math|m\<to\>\<infty\>> and then
    <math|k\<to\>\<infty\>>. Since <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0>,
    this proves the uniform running law in Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>;
    neither bound depends on <math|N\<geqslant\>m> or<nbsp><math|p>.
  </render-proof>

  The uniqueness estimate concerns the specified recursion, rather than
  arbitrary representations of a density. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>
  supplies its weighted analytic coordinates and their real power-series
  extension. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.3> uses the fixed-level
  convergence of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fixed-level>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Analytic endpoint tuning><label|lem:E1.3> There is
    <math|w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>\<gtr\>0>, depending only on the construction,
    such that, for every <math|N,p> and <math|s\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>,
    exactly one bare coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|s|)>> starts
    a trajectory of the specified recursion ending at
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>*s>. Moreover,

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|0|)>=\<beta\><rsub|N,p>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|N,p><around|(|s|)>=b<rsub|N,p><around|(|s|)>*<space|1em><around|(|-1\<less\>s\<less\>1|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|b<rsub|N,p>> is holomorphic on
    <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>1>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Uniqueness and the analytic extension both come from the weighted
    contraction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>, applied to histories of
    the specified recursion (Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D06>
    and<nbsp><reference|def:D25a>). Let <math|\<frak-d\>> be the history
    distance of two histories in the domain of that lemma, including the
    coupling coordinate, and <math|\<delta\>> their coarse endpoint
    difference. With the constants <math|b<rsub|min>,\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>
    fixed before that lemma, independent of <math|N,p>, it gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-analytic-input>\<frak-d\>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<delta\>|\|>/b<rsub|min>+8*g<rsub|\<ast\>>*\<frak-d\>,<space|2em>g<rsub|\<ast\>>=1/128.
    </equation>

    For equal endpoints, <math|\<frak-d\>\<leqslant\>\<frak-d\>/16> implies
    equality of the histories and hence of their bare couplings.

    Take <math|w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>=\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>*b<rsub|min>/4>,
    where <math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>=1/16> is fixed before
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>. The local action is affine in the
    varying coupling; the masks and sector domains are fixed in its analytic
    coordinates. Its power-series map preserves the
    radius-<math|\<lambda\><rsub|\<ast\>>> ball and contracts by <math|1/2>
    for endpoint series <math|w<rsub|<math-up|tun>>*z>. Successive
    substitution converges in the norm <math|<big|sum><rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>>
    of absolutely summable series. The map preserves real coefficients, so
    evaluation gives a real trajectory in that domain for every
    <math|s\<in\><around|[|-1,1|]>>, pointwise in <math|s>. This supplies
    existence on the whole stated interval, including its endpoints. The
    selected coefficient at <math|s=0> is the trajectory of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.2>, by uniqueness. The bare-coupling series
    is holomorphic in <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>1> and has the claimed
    real values. All clipping and health conditions are covered by the
    analytic neighbourhood in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-E1>; they are not
    differentiated across their boundaries.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Marked fields and volume-uniform
  estimates><label|sec:partII-E.2>

  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> needs bounds on differentiated
  insertions, uniform in the cutoff and the volume. The argument has four
  parts. (i)<nbsp>One marked insertion is carried to coarser levels as a
  polynomial coordinate plus remainders, with a two-sided bound on the
  coordinate that loses no factor <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>> in the
  inverse direction (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2.1>).
  (ii)<nbsp>Placing one insertion in every reflected cell of a tiling of side
  <math|a<rsub|m>> and integrating down to a level<nbsp><math|k> with
  <math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>> sites per cell gives the
  chessboard bound <math|A*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<varepsilon\>>>
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2.2>). (iii)<nbsp>A lattice
  difference of a degree-<math|d> insertion is a
  degree-<math|<around|(|d+1|)>> insertion up to a negligible error; its
  coefficient splits into an equation part, which vanishes in separated
  correlations, and a part in a space of dimension at most
  <math|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d+1>> (Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2.3>\U<reference|sec:partII-E.2.4>).
  (iv)<nbsp>That part is bounded through a nondegenerate matrix of free
  Maxwell tests (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2.5>).
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.4> combines (ii)\U(iv) and proves
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2.6>).
  The bounded approximations of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2.7>
  serve the phase argument of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-H>.

  <subsubsection|One marked insertion><label|sec:partII-E.2.1>

  Fix a degree<nbsp><math|d> and suppress <math|N,p,d> while describing one
  transfer. We use the marked densities of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-evolution>,
  the local section <math|P<rsub|d>=<localsection|d>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>, and the factor-count scaling
  <math|t<rsup|<factorcount>>> of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>. Write
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>> for the unmarked representation,
  <math|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>,m>> for its vacuum scalar and
  <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,m>>> for its site torus. An anchor is the
  lattice site at which the insertion is placed. Denote the marked regular
  supports by <math|Z<rsub|i>>, to distinguish them from the unmarked
  supports<nbsp><math|X<rsub|i>>. For a bare polynomial coordinate
  <math|\<b-up-v\>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-marked-evolution>H<rsub|N><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>=<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>*<space|0.17em>P<rsub|d>*<around|[|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>>*\<b-up-v\>|]>,<space|2em>H<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d><blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>>H<rsub|m+1><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>,
  </equation>

  so that <math|H<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>=H<rsup|<math-up|ins>><rsub|N,p,m;d><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>>.
  These densities keep track of one insertion under exact integration. The
  equality is exact; the estimates below control the coordinates in which it
  is written.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Marked density><label|def:D26> A representation of
    <math|H<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>> at level<nbsp><math|m> consists
    of a coordinate <math|\<b-up-x\>=x<rsub|0>*\<b-1\>+\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>\<in\>V<rsub|d>>,
    complex regular remainders <math|R<rsub|i>> on
    supports<nbsp><math|Z<rsub|i>>, and forced remainders <math|Q<rsub|i>> on
    forced addresses of<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|m>>, such that the exact
    decomposition<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-representation> holds with
    <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|m>=\<b-up-x\>>,
    <math|R<rsup|<math-up|reg>><rsub|m,i>=R<rsub|i>> and
    <math|Q<rsup|<math-up|for>><rsub|m,i>=Q<rsub|i>>. The section and the
    stencil<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> are translated to the anchor.
  </definition>

  The remainders restore the exact density; eligibility is that of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>. The marked regular norm below is
  anchored at the insertion site, taken to be zero; it is not anchored
  separately at each support. For selected majorants
  <math|e<rsub|i>\<geqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>>,
  <math|k<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>sup <around|\||Q<rsub|i>|\|>>, set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-marked-mass>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>>*e<rsub|i>,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>=<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|Y<rsub|i>|)>>*k<rsub|i>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>>|<cell|=\<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>+<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-K\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  The derivative seminorm is Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>. A
  coefficient graph at level<nbsp><math|m> is a linear choice
  <math|\<b-up-x\>\<mapsto\>g<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>=<around|(|R<around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>,Q<around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>|)>>
  of such remainders, with the supports fixed and
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
  As in the graph transform for invariant manifolds, the marked step maps
  coefficient graphs at level <math|m+1> to graphs at level<nbsp><math|m> and
  halves the distance between two of them
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>); the current polynomial
  coordinate thus bounds the whole inserted density.

  The following step retains the complete inserted density while reexpressing
  its remainders in the new polynomial coordinate. The inverse estimate is
  essential: a bound in the original bare coordinate would accumulate at
  every step.

  To leading order a step multiplies a homogeneous coordinate of engineering
  weight <math|w> by <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d-w>>: the factor
  <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>> comes from the normalization
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-evolution> and <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-w>>
  from blocking. These factors form the diagonal map

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|<math-ss|S><rsub|L>\<b-up-x\>|)><rsub|i>=L<rsup|d-w<rsub|i>>*x<rsub|i>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d,
  </equation*>

  where <math|<around|(|w<rsub|i>,k<rsub|i>|)>> are the engineering weight
  and factor count of the basis polynomial<nbsp><math|b<rsub|d,i>>; the unit
  has <math|w<rsub|0>=0>. What remains is a change of basis <math|T<rsub|m>>,
  bounded with its inverse uniformly in <math|N>, <math|p> and <math|m>
  within a stage, obtained by reexpressing the fine section, evaluated on the
  free interpolation of a coarse jet, in the coarse basis. Precisely, for a
  coarse radial jet<nbsp><math|<math-ss|j>>, let
  <math|U<rsup|W<rsub|0>><around|(|<math-ss|j>|)>> be the fine stencil
  configuration obtained by exponentiating the free
  interpolation<nbsp><math|W<rsub|0>> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> of the
  coarse potential of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|j>>, the degree-<math|d> Taylor
  polynomial, in the jet coordinates, of the coarse-link logarithms. Define
  <math|<math-ss|R><rsup|<math-up|lead>><rsub|m>> by

  <\equation*>
    <around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|q>><around|(|<math-ss|R><rsup|<math-up|lead>><rsub|m>b|)><around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>|\|><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=0>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d<rsup|q>|d*h<rsub|<math-up|lat>><rsup|q>>*P<rsub|d><around|[|b|]><around*|(|0;U<rsup|W<rsub|0>><around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>><math-ss|j>|)>|)>|\|><rsub|h<rsub|<math-up|lat>>=0>*<space|2em><around|(|0\<leqslant\>q\<leqslant\>d|)>,
  </equation*>

  put <math|<around|(|<math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|eng>><rsub|L>\<b-up-x\>|)><rsub|i>=L<rsup|w<rsub|i>>*x<rsub|i>>,
  and set

  <\equation*>
    T<rsub|m>=t<rsub|m><rsup|<factorcount>><math-ss|R><rsup|<math-up|lead>><rsub|m><math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|eng>><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|m>>t<rsub|m+1><rsup|-<factorcount>>.
  </equation*>

  Taylor matching gives <math|<around|(|<math-ss|R><rsup|<math-up|lead>><rsub|m>|)><rsub|b*i>=\<delta\><rsub|b*i>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-w<rsub|i>>>
  for <math|w<rsub|b>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|i>> and <math|=0> for
  <math|k<rsub|b>\<less\>k<rsub|i>>. To bound the other entries, use the
  kernel form of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>: at averaging order
  <math|j\<geqslant\>d+3>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|\||\<nabla\><rsub|f><rsup|r>*W<rsub|0>*<around|(|\<ell\><rsub|m>*y+a,y<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|j,r>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-1-r>*e<rsup|-c<rsub|j>*<around|\||y-y<rprime|'>|\|>>,<space|2em>0\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>d.
  </equation*>

  The choice of the reference level ensures this order. A jet potential grows
  polynomially, so the weighted bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:B-weighted-interp> with
  <math|c<rprime|'>=0> and <math|h=d> applies to it. Thus its kernel sum and
  the required differences converge absolutely. Applying the displayed bound
  to each factor of a homogeneous jet monomial gives
  <math|<around|\||<around|(|<math-ss|R><rsup|<math-up|lead>><rsub|m>|)><rsub|b*i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-w<rsub|i>>>,
  with <math|C> depending only on <math|d> and the stage. Periodization gives
  the same bound on a torus by summing over its translates. The threshold
  formula in item<nbsp>9 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices> and
  the entry choices of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> give
  <math|0\<less\>t<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>1> and
  <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2>. Hence
  <math|T<rsub|m>> sends each basis vector to its own and higher weights,
  with nonzero diagonal <math|<around|(|T<rsub|m>|)><rsub|i*i>=<around|(|t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|k<rsub|i>>>,
  and back substitution in increasing weight bounds
  <math|T<rsub|m><rsup|\<pm\>1>> uniformly in <math|N>, <math|p> and <math|m>
  within a stage.

  The marked step constructs, from a coefficient graph at level <math|m+1>,
  the coarse coordinate and a coefficient graph at level<nbsp><math|m> for
  which the marked identity holds again. Its statement is in the coordinate
  <math|\<b-up-u\>=<math-ss|M><rsub|m>\<b-up-x\>> extracted by the step.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One marked step><label|lem:E-marked-step> For
    <math|m\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>> and each coefficient graph
    <math|g<rsub|m+1>> at level<nbsp><math|m+1>, there are an invertible
    linear <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|m>> on <math|V<rsub|d>> and a coefficient
    graph <math|g<rsub|m>> at level<nbsp><math|m> such that every
    representation of <math|H<rsub|m+1><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>> with
    coordinate<nbsp><math|\<b-up-x\>> and remainders
    <math|g<rsub|m+1><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>> yields one of
    <math|H<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>> with coordinate
    <math|\<b-up-u\>=<math-ss|M><rsub|m>\<b-up-x\>> and remainders
    <math|g<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-u\>|)>>. Moreover:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)><math|<around|\||\<b-up-u\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d><around|\||\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>>
      and <math|<around|\||\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><around|\||\<b-up-u\>|\|><rsub|1>>;

      <item*|(ii)><math|<math-ss|M><rsub|m>> and <math|g<rsub|m>> respect
      conjugation and signed permutations, and
      <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|m>\<b-1\>=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>> with
      zero remainders;

      <item*|(iii)>fine graphs differing by at most
      <math|\<tau\>*C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><around|\||\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>>
      give coarse graphs differing by at most
      <math|<tfrac|\<tau\>|2>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||\<b-up-u\>|\|><rsub|1>>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  The proof gives the construction and the bounds, including the contraction
  between two choices of incoming remainders.

  <\proof>
    The new coordinate is read off by Taylor extraction after one exact block
    integration (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>) in the local sections
    of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>. The connected expansion of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>, in a nilpotent algebra, controls
    the marked terms; the pre-reset estimates of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D4>, the subtraction of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-local-subtraction> and the density identity
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D5> provide the unmarked part of the step on
    which the marked terms ride. Put <math|L=\<ell\><rsub|m>>,
    <math|S=C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>,
    <math|K=K<rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>> and <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m>=<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>.
    All norms in this proof are coefficient sums or the anchored
    norm<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-mass>.

    <em|Step 1: Marked terms in the local integration.> Introduce
    <math|z<rsup|2>=0>. Replace the regular exponential factor by its product
    with <math|1+z*<around|(|x<rsub|0>+\<b-1\><rsub|\<Sigma\><rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*P<rsub|d>*<around|[|t<rsub|m+1><rsup|-<factorcount>>*\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|]>+<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|Z<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*R<rsub|i>|)>>
    and replace a forced factor <math|K<rsub|i>> by
    <math|K<rsub|i>+z*Q<rsub|i>>. The coefficient of <math|z> in the eligible
    covering sum is exactly the representation of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D26>. Apply the coefficientwise rooted
    clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> in this nilpotent
    algebra. Each connected term now contains one distinguished factor. For a
    term with ordinary factors <math|a<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,a<rsub|h>>, its
    marked coefficient is

    <\equation*>
      <around|[|z|]>*<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|h><around|(|a<rsub|i>+z*b<rsub|i>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|h>b<rsub|i>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>i>a<rsub|j>.
    </equation*>

    Root its connected support at this distinguished factor. The sums over
    all other factors are bounded by the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>; there is no sum over possible
    translates of the marked anchor. Absolute convergence justifies the
    displayed extraction and integration term by term. A regular mark has its
    derivative majorant <math|e<rsub|i>>; a forced mark has its amplitude
    majorant <math|k<rsub|i>>. The latter retains its original eligibility
    event, so the large-field estimate used in the forced expansion still
    applies.

    The three contributions to a basis input are bounded as follows. The map
    <math|T<rsub|m>> fixes the unit and preserves the nonconstant subspace.
    It is triangular in the homogeneous weights:
    <math|<around|(|T<rsub|m>|)><rsub|i*j>\<ne\>0> and <math|i\<ne\>j> imply
    <math|w<rsub|j>\<less\>w<rsub|i>>. Its diagonal entries are nonzero, so
    inversion proceeds in increasing weight, with the finite stage bounds
    included in<nbsp><math|K>. Let <math|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>\<geqslant\>1>
    bound its two operator norms and the following local remainder estimates
    before symmetry averaging; the leading stage bound was chosen with
    <math|K\<geqslant\>2*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>*C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>>.
    Write <math|\<epsilon\>=\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>> for the
    local marked error of item<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph>. Taylor
    subtraction of a primary mark leaves coefficient error at most
    <math|\<epsilon\><around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>> and
    remainder at most <math|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|\||<math-ss|S><rsub|L>\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.
    A regular remainder of mass <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|Z|)>>*e<rsub|i>>
    contributes at most <math|\<epsilon\>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|Z|)>>*e<rsub|i>>
    to each of these two bounds. A forced remainder contributes at most
    <math|\<epsilon\>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|Y|)>>*k<rsub|i>/\<kappa\><rsub|m+1>>.

    For the primary mark, the local polynomial is subtracted through
    degree<nbsp><math|d> before rescaling. Each remaining term has at least
    one additional scaled jet weight, and the block rescaling gives the
    inverse block factor in its norm. The finite connected sums and the
    ordered derivative product rule introduce only stage constants.

    An incoming regular mark is never moved to the forced family. A forced
    remainder must vanish off an eligibility event with a nonempty inventory
    of flagged sites (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25>), whereas the
    support of a regular mark may wrap around the torus while every plaquette
    in it is healthy. The integrated contribution of a regular mark consists
    of regular coarse remainders and, from those terms of its connected
    expansion that carry a flagged inventory at level<nbsp><math|m>, of
    forced coarse remainders on their own eligibility events. Wrapping plays
    no role in this assignment; it only decides whether the radial
    cancellation can be used. Fix such a mark <math|R<rsub|i>> on
    <math|Z<rsub|i>>, with derivative majorant <math|e<rsub|i>>, let
    <math|<wide|Z|^><rsub|i>> be the coarse support of its contribution, and
    put

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|i>=<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m+1>><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>,<space|2em><wide|Z|^><rsub|i>\<subset\>N<rsub|R<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|supp>>*B<rsub|m>><around|(|<math-up|parent><around|(|Z<rsub|i>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Call <math|Z<rsub|i>> short if <math|d<rsub|i><rsup|3>\<less\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>.
    Three classes of supports are estimated separately.

    (i) If <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|Z|^><rsub|i>|)>\<gtr\>B<rsub|m>>,
    the contribution is bounded at a coarse support exponent at least
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+3>, one more than the exponent
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2> in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-support-conversion>.
    Half of this unit excess absorbs the derivative losses, which are
    polynomial in <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|Z|^><rsub|i>|)>>.
    The other half gives a factor <math|e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/2>>, since
    <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|Z|^><rsub|i>|)>\<gtr\>B<rsub|m>>.
    As for the large supports in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited>, the resulting factor tends to
    zero as the coupling entry is raised at fixed stage and ratio; it is part
    of the term <math|o<rsub|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>><around|(|1|)>> below.

    (ii) If <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|Z|^><rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|m>>
    and <math|Z<rsub|i>> is short, then <math|Z<rsub|i>> is nonwrapping about
    the anchor. Indeed, as in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-inherited>,
    <math|3*d<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>> and
    <math|B<rsub|m+1>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|m>> bound the fine radius of
    <math|Z<rsub|i>> by <math|6*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>|)>*\<ell\><rsub|m>>,
    while the coarse side is at least <math|100*<around|(|1+B<rsub|m>|)>>.
    The radial-jet coefficients of <math|R<rsub|i>> through
    degree<nbsp><math|d> therefore vanish: this is the format of the incoming
    remainders, recorded in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a> at
    level<nbsp><math|m+1>. Taylor's formula along the healthy radial segment
    gives the factor <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>>, with
    derivative losses polynomial in <math|1+d<rsub|i>>.

    (iii) If <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|Z|^><rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|m>>
    and <math|d<rsub|i><rsup|3>\<geqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m>>, no radial
    cancellation is used. By<nbsp>(ii), this class contains every wrapping
    support with <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|Z|^><rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|m>>;
    it also contains nonwrapping supports that are not short. The derivative
    bound without subtraction has losses polynomial in <math|1+d<rsub|i>>,
    and

    <\equation*>
      1\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>*<around|(|1+d<rsub|i>|)><rsup|3*<around|(|d+1|)>>
    </equation*>

    gives the same factor <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>> at
    the cost of a further polynomial in <math|1+d<rsub|i>>.

    In classes (ii) and (iii), the support
    conversion<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-support-conversion> gives
    <math|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>+2|)><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|<wide|Z|^><rsub|i>|)>\<leqslant\>log
    \<cal-A\><rsub|m>+<tfrac|1|2>\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*d<rsub|i>>. The
    remaining half of the incoming exponent bounds the polynomial in
    <math|1+d<rsub|i>>, since <math|sup<rsub|s\<geqslant\>0><around|(|1+s|)><rsup|q>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*s/2>\<less\>\<infty\>>
    for each fixed<nbsp><math|q>. After multiplication by
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>>, the contribution of the mark is at most
    <math|C*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|-1>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*d<rsub|i>>*e<rsub|i>>,
    with <math|C> fixed before the ratio. Thus the smallness of a healthy
    wrapping mark comes from its unused support weight, not from a Taylor
    cancellation.

    An incoming forced mark retains its own eligibility event, whether or not
    its support wraps. Its support sum keeps half the original forced
    suppression: its normalization is <math|\<kappa\><rsub|m+1>>, not
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|m+1><rsup|<math-up|for>>>. The remaining
    suppression absorbs the same derivative and support factors. Thus the
    bounds before choosing parameters have the form
    <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>/L+o<rsub|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>><around|(|1|)>>,
    with <math|C<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|loc>>> fixed before <math|L> and the
    second term tending to zero at fixed stage and block factor; the ratio
    and entry choices of item<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph>, made in the proof
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>, make it at most
    <math|\<epsilon\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|-2/5>>.
    The degree-<math|d> Taylor extraction is on healthy charts; the
    indicators themselves are never differentiated.

    Let <math|\<b-up-u\>> be the extracted coarse polynomial coordinate,
    <math|r> the incoming anchored remainder norm and <math|r<rprime|'>> the
    resulting coarse remainder norm, including its forced normalization.
    Summing the contributions gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-atom-estimates><around|\||\<b-up-u\>-T<rsub|m><math-ss|S><rsub|L>\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\>*<around|(|<around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>+r|)>,<space|2em>r<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>\|<math-ss|S><rsub|L>\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>\|<rsub|1>+\<epsilon\>*r,<space|2em>r=<around|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|m+1><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>.
    </equation>

    The regular-remainder terms in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates> use the
    vanishing Taylor coefficients through degree<nbsp><math|d> on short
    supports, and the unused support weight on the other supports. Retained
    incoming regular terms use the support conversion
    factor<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-support-conversion>. The same sums with the
    primary mark canceled give the Lipschitz bound
    <math|\<epsilon\>*<around|\<\|\|\>|g-<wide|g|~>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>>
    for the coefficient and remainder differences; this uses the entire
    forced marked family at once.

    <em|Step 2: Linear choice and inverse bound.> Perform the construction on
    each homogeneous basis vector and on all its signed and conjugated
    images. Take their union of supports, with zero entries where necessary,
    and recombine using the basis coefficients. Average over the finite
    signed-permutation group and over conjugation. This gives a
    complex-linear family with the required symmetries and exact density
    identity. Let <math|A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>>\<geqslant\>1> bound the
    sum of absolute recombination coefficients in this finite averaging.
    After averaging, the error in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates> is at most
    <math|\<epsilon\>*<around|(|1+S|)>*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>><around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>,
    and the remainder is at most <math|<around|(|C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>+\<epsilon\>*S|)>*A<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|sym>><around|\||<math-ss|S><rsub|L>\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>.

    Use<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-graph-reserve>. The cross-stage inequality follows
    from item<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns>, fixed before the ratios. Since
    <math|\<epsilon\>*S\<leqslant\>1\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|loc>>>, the
    averaged remainder constant is at most <math|K>. In the notation
    <math|\<b-up-y\>=<math-ss|S><rsub|L>\<b-up-x\>>, the resulting
    inequalities are

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<b-up-u\>-T<rsub|m>*\<b-up-y\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*<around|(|<around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>+r|)>,<space|2em>r<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>K\|\<b-up-y\><rsup|\<circ\>>\|<rsub|1>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*r,<space|2em>r\<leqslant\>S<around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|<around|\||\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||<math-ss|S><rsub|L>\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|d><around|\||\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>>,
    the first two conditions in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-graph-reserve> imply

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<b-up-y\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\||\<b-up-u\>|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||\<b-up-u\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\||\<b-up-y\>|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||\<b-up-y\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\||\<b-up-u\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    For example, apply <math|T<rsub|m><rsup|-1>> and use
    <math|2*K*<around|\||\<b-up-u\>-T<rsub|m>*\<b-up-y\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<b-up-y\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>
    to absorb the error; the same argument on the nonconstant subspace gives
    the last bound. Moreover <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*r\<leqslant\>K<around|\||\<b-up-y\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>,
    so

    <\equation*>
      r<rprime|'>\<leqslant\>2*K<around|\||\<b-up-y\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>4*K<rsup|2><around|\||\<b-up-u\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||\<b-up-u\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    The linear map <math|\<b-up-x\>\<mapsto\>\<b-up-u\>=<math-ss|M><rsub|m>\<b-up-x\>>
    is injective by <math|<around|\||\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>S<around|\||\<b-up-u\>|\|><rsub|1>>,
    hence invertible in finite dimension. This defines the new graph by
    <math|g<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-u\>|)>=g<rsub|m><around|(|<math-ss|M><rsub|m>\<b-up-x\>|)>>;
    the last display is the graph bound required of a coefficient graph at
    level<nbsp><math|m>. The two coefficient estimates of<nbsp>(i) now
    follow; property<nbsp>(ii) is inherited from the symmetric construction.
    In particular the inverse estimate has no factor <math|L<rsup|d>>:
    <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|L><rsup|-1>> has norm at most one.

    For the unit, the primary extraction is exact, <math|T<rsub|m>> fixes it
    and its nonconstant coordinate is zero. All the preceding remainder
    bounds are then zero, and the unit is sent to <math|L<rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>
    with zero remainders. Finally, when two fine graphs differ by at most
    <math|\<tau\>*S<around|\||\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>> in the remainder norm,
    the Lipschitz estimate above and rebasing by the inverse bound
    of<nbsp>(i) give a coarse difference at most
    <math|<tfrac|1|2>\<tau\>*C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||\<b-up-u\>|\|><rsub|1>>:
    the two contributions are the changed remainder at fixed input and the
    changed input coordinate, each bounded by <math|\<tau\>/4>
    using<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-graph-reserve>. This is the graph contraction used
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level>.
  </proof>

  Choose the reference level <math|<reflevel|d>> with
  <math|J<rsub|<reflevel|d>>\<geqslant\>d+3>,
  <math|\<frak-q\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d+1> and
  <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<geqslant\>d> for
  <math|m\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>. Apply
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> along the trajectory, starting
  from the representation at the cutoff constructed in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>, and let <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|m>> be its
  coefficient maps. Define, before normalizing the lattice fields,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-terminal-map><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>=<math-ss|M><rsub|<reflevel|d>>\<cdots\><math-ss|M><rsub|N-1><space|1em><around|(|N\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>|)>.
  </equation>

  An empty product is the identity. For <math|N\<less\><reflevel|d>> choose
  an arbitrary invertible map, as allowed in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>.

  <paragraph|The curvature square at the terminal map.> We continue the
  example of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-example>. The curvature square
  <math|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>> is a label of degree <math|d=4> with engineering
  weight four and factor count two, the top weight in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|4>>.
  Its column of the leading map is therefore diagonal,
  <math|T<rsub|m>*c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>=<around|(|t<rsub|m>/t<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>*c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>>,
  and <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|m>>> fixes it. With the leading
  maps alone, the terminal map would multiply <math|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>> by the
  single factor <math|<around|(|t<rsub|<reflevel|4>>/t<rsub|N>|)><rsup|2>>.
  The coefficient maps, however, are controlled only up to an error in the
  full coefficient norm, <math|<around|\||<math-ss|M><rsub|m>\<b-up-x\>-T<rsub|m><math-ss|S><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|m>>\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|\<ell\><rsub|m>>*<around|(|<around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|g<rsub|m+1><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>|)>>
  (Step<nbsp>2 in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>), so
  at each step the curvature-square input may acquire a component along the
  unit <math|\<b-1\>>, the only other real <math|<hypercubic>>-invariant
  label in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|4>> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-example>),
  for instance from integrated remainders. The estimates therefore do not
  force the unit coefficient<nbsp><math|\<mu\><rsub|N,p>> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-example> to vanish. The reference
  normalization instead prescribes the whole bare coefficient vector
  <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,4><rsup|-1>c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>>;
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1> bounds it by
  <math|<around|\||c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|\|><rsub|1>*<big|prod><rsub|<reflevel|4>\<leqslant\>i\<less\>N>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-o<around|(|1|)>><around|\||c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|\|><rsub|1>>,
  by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>
  continues the example with the quotient field.

  The next lemma carries one insertion from the cutoff to the reference
  level. Its content is the representation at the cutoff, whose forced part
  needs the initialization requirement, and the transport bounds obtained by
  iterating the marked step.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Marked trajectory><label|lem:E2.1><label|lem:E2.2> Let
    <math|N\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>> and <math|<reflevel|d>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>.
    The densities<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-evolution> have representations at
    level<nbsp><math|m> with coordinates <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>>
    and remainder families linear in<nbsp><math|\<b-up-v\>>, with fixed
    supports, such that <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|N><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>=\<b-up-v\>>,
    <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|m>=<math-ss|M><rsub|m>\<b-up-x\><rsub|m+1>> for
    <math|m\<less\>N>, <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|<reflevel|d>><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>\<b-up-v\>>
    with <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> invertible, and

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-E\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>+<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-K\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m>><around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>,
    </equation*>

    also with <math|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>> replaced by any exponent in
    <math|<around|[|98,100|]>>. For <math|m\<less\>N>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsub|m+1><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>><around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>|\|><rsub|1>,<space|2em><around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>*\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d><around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsub|m+1><around|(|\<b-up-v\>|)>|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    At level<nbsp><math|N>, split the unmasked section into its healthy part
    and its complement <math|f<rsub|\<b-up-v\>>=\<b-1\><rsub|\<Sigma\><rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|unhealthy>>*P<rsub|d>*<around|[|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>>*\<b-up-v\><rsup|\<circ\>>|]>>.
    The healthy part is the polynomial term
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-representation>; the complement must be
    carried by the forced family, whose eligibility events
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>) cover the flagged configurations.
    For an eligible family<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\>> let
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>> be its forced terms whose supports
    meet<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>>. If
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>=\<varnothing\>>, exact coverage leaves no
    flag in<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and <math|f<rsub|\<b-up-v\>>=0>.
    Otherwise replace <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>> by one new marked
    forced address with support <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>>Y<rsub|i>>,
    inventory <math|<big|cup><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>>I<rsub|i>>,
    zero unmarked activity, and <math|Q=f<rsub|\<b-up-v\>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>>K<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>>
    on the event that the flags in that support are exactly this inventory,
    <math|Q=0> otherwise. An index of the new marked family records the
    cluster<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|\<Sigma\>>>, even when two clusters
    have the same support and inventory; retain all original forced factors
    in<nbsp><math|Q>. The remaining supports are disjoint from the merged
    support, since they avoid<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and were disjoint
    from every merged support. This is a bijection between eligible families
    meeting<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> and eligible marked families
    containing one new address, so the forced sum restores
    <math|f<rsub|\<b-up-v\>><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>> exactly.
    The regular remainder is zero and <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|N>=\<b-up-v\>>.

    The support weight of the merged address is at most
    <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|N>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|)>>>
    times the product of the old support weights: their connected covers all
    meet the stencil cover. The activity caps bound the sum of the weighted
    majorants of addresses meeting<nbsp><math|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>> by
    <math|<around|\||\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|\|>*\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>.
    Dropping disjointness and summing their products over nonempty subsets
    gives at most <math|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|\|>*\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>-1>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-global-bound>, <math|<around|\||f<rsub|\<b-up-v\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d>*t<rsub|N><rsup|-d><around|\||\<b-up-v\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>;
    with<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps> and
    <math|<cover><rsub|B<rsub|N>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|)>\<leqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-K\><rsup|<math-up|mk>><rsub|N>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*C<rsub|d>*t<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*e<rsup|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>>*<around|(|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Sigma\><rsub|d>|\|>*\<epsilon\><rsub|N><rsup|<math-up|for>>>-1|)><around|\||\<b-up-v\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|\||\<b-up-v\><rsup|\<circ\>>|\|><rsub|1>>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    by item<nbsp><reference|it:E-birth>. This is the bound of the lemma at
    <math|m=N>, since <math|C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|N>>\<geqslant\>1>.

    Iterate the marked step on the same unmarked trajectory.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> gives
    <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|m>=<math-ss|M><rsub|m>\<b-up-x\><rsub|m+1>>, the
    exact equality in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-evolution>, the graph bound and
    the two transport bounds; the factor <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>>
    agrees with <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|d>=\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>*a<rsub|m+1><rsup|d>>.
    Each <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|m>> is invertible, so their product is
    invertible and <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|<reflevel|d>>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>\<b-up-v\>>.
    No choice of a different unmarked representation is made. For fixed
    supports, take the union of the addresses selected for the finitely many
    basis inputs and fill unused addresses by zero; the basis construction in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> then makes the coefficients and
    both remainders linear in<nbsp><math|\<b-up-v\>>. Finally, for
    <math|s\<geqslant\>0> and <math|98\<leqslant\>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>100> we
    have <math|e<rsup|\<tau\>*s>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|100*s>>, so lowering the
    support exponent in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-mass> only decreases both
    sums.
  </proof>

  The normalization retains the symmetries of the local sections. Its unit
  includes the degree-dependent spacing factor.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Symmetry and the unit><label|lem:E2.1a> For every <math|d>,
    <math|N\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>> and <math|p>, the maps
    <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> commute with conjugation
    and<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>. For <math|<reflevel|d>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>,

    <\equation*>
      \<b-up-x\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>|)>=a<rsub|m><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>.
    </equation*>

    Consequently <math|\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>=a<rsub|<reflevel|d>><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>
    has insertion one.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Complex conjugation and signed permutations commute with integration
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>), with Taylor extraction in the
    symmetric sections of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>, and with the
    equivariant coefficient graph of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>.
    Each operation therefore commutes with every <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|m>>
    and their product.

    For a unit input, the marked density is
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>>. Exact
    integration and multiplication by <math|\<ell\><rsub|m><rsup|d>> give
    <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|d><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|m>> at
    level<nbsp><math|m>. The selected extraction has zero nonconstant
    coordinate and zero remainder for this density. Induction proves the
    displayed identity. In particular <math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>=a<rsub|N><rsup|d>*\<b-1\>>.
    Substitute into Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>; factor scaling fixes
    the polynomial unit, so its insertion equals
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*a<rsub|N><rsup|d>=1>.
  </proof>

  The remainders have the cancellations of the marked
  format<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-marked-representation> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:renormalization>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Marked local format><label|lem:E2.2a> For
    <math|N\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>> and <math|<reflevel|d>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>N>,
    the complex regular remainders are Borel, gauge invariant, local and
    smooth as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>. Their supports contain
    the anchor. Their values at the identity, and their radial-jet
    coefficients through degree<nbsp><math|d> on nonwrapping supports,
    vanish. The forced remainders satisfy
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25>, and

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|i>\<ne\>0<space|1em>\<Longrightarrow\><space|1em><text|the
      anchor belongs to >Y<rsub|i>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The anchored extraction in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>
    subtracts the value at the identity and the radial Taylor polynomial;
    this gives exactly the two stated regular cancellations. On wrapping
    supports no radial Taylor cancellation is imposed. In the connected
    expansion every marked component contains the original insertion anchor.
    The support is the union of the supports in that component, so it still
    contains that anchor after enlargement. A component reaches a forced
    address only through terms that carry a flagged inventory at that level,
    and then only on the eligibility event of that address
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D25>); an incoming regular mark is never
    moved to the forced family as a whole (Step<nbsp>1 in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>). The anchor argument applies to
    these terms as well. Locality, gauge invariance and Borel measurability
    are preserved by local integration and by these tests. The regular
    derivatives are taken on healthy configurations, as in
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D24>; no derivative of a relaxed threshold
    test is used. The fixed basis construction and its support unions retain
    these properties under iteration.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Chessboard seminorms and replicated
  sources><label|sec:partII-E.2.2>

  A bond tiling has cell boundaries through link midpoints. Its reflected
  copies place an observable in every cell, with conjugation on odd
  reflections. The copy <math|<around|(|F|)><rsub|C>> is the pullback of
  <math|F> under the composition of bond reflections taking the base cell to
  <math|C>, conjugated when that composition has odd parity.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Chessboard seminorm><label|def:D27> For a tiling
    <math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>> into congruent reflected cells and
    an observable <math|F> in its base cell, set

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>>=<around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>><around|(|F|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|><rsup|1/<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>|\|>>.
    </equation*>

    Interior means dependence only on links with both endpoints strictly
    inside the base cell. The centered tiling
    <math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m><around|(|y|)>> has physical
    cell side <math|a<rsub|m>> and base cell centered at<nbsp><math|y>.
  </definition>

  The seminorm measures the cost of repeating one observation throughout the
  box; it converts reflected-product estimates into bounds on one insertion.
  For interior complex observables these quantities are seminorms and satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-chess><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>><around|(|F<rsub|C>|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|C>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>>.
  </equation>

  Apply the abstract chessboard theorem, <cite-detail|FrohlichIsraelLiebSimon1978|Theorem<nbsp>4.1>,
  successively in the four axes. The real vector space is the space of
  complex cell functions, and the reflection includes conjugation.
  Translation invariance gives cyclicity; reflection positivity gives the
  required Schwarz inequality. For the Wilson measure this positivity is
  <cite-detail|Seiler1982|Theorem<nbsp>2.1, pp.<nbsp>14--15>, with compact
  matrix group, positive coupling and observables on one side of the bond
  planes. For <math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>>, there are
  <math|L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|m>> cells per axis, an even integer. The cell side
  <math|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|N>=D<rsub|N>/D<rsub|m>> is odd, so the centered
  boundaries pass through link midpoints. Bounded Borel cell functions follow
  by approximation in the finite-dimensional configuration measure.

  The centered seminorm estimate must also control anchors near arbitrary,
  separated configurations. Write <math|v=<around|(|d,c|)>> for the insertion
  label. A signed image means the image under<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>;
  conjugation is also allowed. Two tilings are aligned when their cells have
  the same axes and every coarse boundary is a fine boundary.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Interior seminorm bound><label|lem:F1a> Fix
    <math|m\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N>, <math|N\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>,
    and<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>\<geqslant\>0>. Suppose, for every
    anchor<nbsp><math|y<rprime|'>>, every signed image or image under
    conjugation of<nbsp><math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y<rprime|'>|)>> has
    seminorm at most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>>
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k><around|(|y<rprime|'>|)>>
    and stencil within<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>> of<nbsp><math|y<rprime|'>>. In
    every translated bond tiling<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\>> of
    side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>>, an anchor at distance at
    least<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k>> from its cell boundary satisfies

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-uncentered>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y|)><text| is
      interior>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v,y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-T\>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    At an aligned fine-cell center the bound follows
    from<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>; between centers it follows from
    log-convexity in each axis, using the symmetry of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>. Since<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|N>>
    is odd, the fine-cell centers are lattice sites. At an aligned fine-cell
    center, reflect all coarse copies into distinct fine cells. Their signs
    and conjugations are among the assumed images.
    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> with<nbsp><math|1> in every unoccupied fine
    cell. Taking the coarse seminorm bounds it
    by<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>>.

    For intermediate anchors, let<nbsp><math|H<around|(|z|)>> be the coarse
    seminorm after<nbsp><math|z> lattice translations in one axis, with the
    other coordinates fixed. Translation invariance and reflection give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-logconvex>H<around|(|z|)><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>H*<around|(|z-1|)>*H*<around|(|z+1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Indeed, translate the reflected product by one lattice step. Alternate
    cells then contain shifts<nbsp><math|z-1> and<nbsp><math|z+1>;
    apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>. On a finite interval a nonnegative
    sequence satisfying this inequality cannot exceed its endpoint maximum:
    an interior positive maximum extends to both neighbours and hence to an
    endpoint. This includes zero endpoints.

    Bracket each coordinate of<nbsp><math|y> by its two neighbouring
    fine-center coordinates. All anchors in the resulting rectangle have
    margin at least<nbsp><math|2*a<rsub|k>>, so their stencils are interior.
    Induct on<nbsp><math|i=0,\<ldots\>,4>: the seminorm is at
    most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>> when the first<nbsp><math|i>
    coordinates are arbitrary lattice coordinates in these intervals and the
    remaining coordinates are endpoints. The case<nbsp><math|i=0> is the
    aligned-center bound. In the next axis, the endpoint bounds hold
    uniformly in the coordinates already moved;<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-logconvex>
    supplies the intervening bounds. The case<nbsp><math|i=4>
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-uncentered>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Replicated sources.> Several insertions are carried at once by
  giving each a nilpotent mark. This is the device of the observable field
  in<nbsp><cite-detail|BauerschmidtBrydgesSlade2015|Section<nbsp>2.3>, where
  only terms of order at most one in each external field are retained. For a
  finite set of slots <math|I<rsub|0>>, work in the commutative algebra
  <math|<C><around|[|z<rsub|i>:i\<in\>I<rsub|0>|]>/<around|(|z<rsub|i><rsup|2>:i\<in\>I<rsub|0>|)>>.
  Write <math|z<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>z<rsub|i>> for the
  mode<nbsp><math|I>. The exact source density starts at

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-multiple-density>\<cal-H\><rsub|N><around|(|z;U|)>=<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N><around|(|U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|0>><around|(|1+z<rsub|i>*F<rsub|i><around|(|U|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-H\><rsub|m>=<blockmap|m><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m+1>>\<cal-H\><rsub|m+1>.
  </equation>

  Thus its normalized <math|z<rsub|I>> integral is the cutoff moment of the
  corresponding physical insertions. At level<nbsp><math|m> write its local
  representation as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-source-form>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|e<rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|m><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*f<rsub|i,m><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|X>H<rsub|m,X>*<around|(|z;U|)>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<space|1em>\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|\<cal-A\><math-up|eligible>><big|prod><rsub|Y\<in\>\<cal-A\>><around*|(|K<rsub|m,Y><around|(|U|)>+<big|sum><rsub|i>z<rsub|i>*k<rsub|i,m,Y><around|(|U|)>+K<rsup|\<geqslant\>2><rsub|m,Y>*<around|(|z;U|)>|)>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<Phi\><rsub|m>> is the unmarked exponent, <math|f<rsub|i,m>>
  and <math|k<rsub|i,m,Y>> are the complete one-insertion exponential and
  forced terms, including their physical spacing factors. The polynomials
  <math|H<rsub|m,X>> and <math|K<rsup|\<geqslant\>2><rsub|m,Y>> contain only
  modes <math|z<rsub|I>> with <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>2>. Their
  support contains every anchor indexed by a nonzero mode. The regular terms
  include their health masks, and each forced term retains its eligibility
  test. For a single mark, <math|z<rsub|1><rsup|2>=0> gives
  <math|e<rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|m>+z<rsub|1>*f<rsub|1,m>>=e<rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|m>>*<around|(|1+z<rsub|1>*f<rsub|1,m>|)>>,
  and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form> is the representation of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D26> with

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|1,m>=a<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*<around*|{|x<rsub|0>+\<b-1\><rsub|\<Sigma\><rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*P<rsub|d>*<around|[|t<rsub|m><rsup|-<factorcount>>*\<b-up-x\><rsub|m><rsup|\<circ\>>|]>+<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|Z<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*R<rsub|i>|}>,<space|2em>k<rsub|1,m,Y<rsub|i>>=a<rsub|m><rsup|-d>*Q<rsub|i>,
  </equation*>

  at <math|\<b-up-v\>=<math-ss|Z><rsup|-1><rsub|N,p,d>c>. The higher modes
  record the connected terms that integration creates between different
  marks. Rescaling mark<nbsp><math|h> by <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|h>\<gtr\>0>,
  its <em|mark weight>, multiplies the coefficient of <math|z<rsub|I>> by
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|h\<in\>I><math-ss|w><rsub|h>>; a bound of order one
  on the rescaled source therefore bounds that coefficient by
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|h\<in\>I><math-ss|w><rsub|h><rsup|-1>>.

  Connected terms with at least two marks need large supports, because
  distinct marked anchors are far apart. We record this once, since the
  stopping estimate uses it again.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Marked covering estimate><label|lem:E-cover> Let
    <math|B\<geqslant\>8>. If a support <math|X> contains
    <math|n\<geqslant\>2> anchors at pairwise periodic max-distance at least
    <math|B<rsup|2>>, then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-marked-cover>B*n\<leqslant\>64<cover><rsub|B><around|(|X|)>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take a minimal cover of <math|X> by <math|M=<cover><rsub|B><around|(|X|)>>
    radius-<math|B> balls with connected union. Join their centers by a tree
    with edges of length at most <math|2*B+1>. Its doubled traversal has
    length at most <math|6*B*<around|(|M-1|)>>. Assign each anchor to one
    covering center. Distinct anchors have distinct centers, and the distance
    between any two assigned centers is at least <math|B<rsup|2>-2*B>. Visit
    these centers in their cyclic order in the traversal. The triangle
    inequality gives <math|n*<around|(|B<rsup|2>-2*B|)>\<leqslant\>6*B*<around|(|M-1|)>>,
    so <math|n*<around|(|B-2|)>\<leqslant\>6*M>. For <math|B\<geqslant\>8> we
    have <math|B-2\<geqslant\><tfrac|3|4>B>, hence
    <math|B*n\<leqslant\>8*M\<leqslant\>64*M>. The argument uses the periodic
    max-distance and needs no nonwrapping assumption.
  </proof>

  The estimate permits each source weight to be enlarged by
  <math|e<rsup|\<delta\>*B/64>> per mark when <math|\<delta\>> units of the
  support exponent are removed.

  The rooted expansion needs a bound on all the other marked factors. We
  verify it for the primary insertions of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, with their selected coefficient
  graphs, before taking any replicated integral. For mark<nbsp><math|h> at
  level<nbsp><math|i> let <math|\<b-up-x\><rsub|h,i>> be its dimensionless
  singleton coefficient, let <math|b<rsub|h,i>=a<rsub|i><rsup|-d><around|\||\<b-up-x\><rsub|h,i>|\|><rsub|1>>,
  and let <math|v<rsub|a,i><rsup|<math-up|src>>> be the weighted integrated
  majorants of the other marked factors on their complete read supports
  <math|Z<rsub|a,i>>, with derivative and support weights as in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Marked attachments><label|lem:E-source-attachments> Let
    <math|<reflevel|d>\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>N>. Consider finitely many
    degree-<math|d> primary insertions whose anchors have level-<math|i>
    ancestors pairwise at periodic distance at least <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>>
    for <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>N>. Give mark<nbsp><math|h> weights
    with <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|h,i+1>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|-1><math-ss|w><rsub|h,i>>
    and <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|h,k>b<rsub|h,k>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>>.
    Then, for <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>N>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a,i>>v<rsub|a,i><rsup|<math-up|src>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>/2,
    </equation*>

    uniformly in the cutoff, the period and the number of marks.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The proof is an induction down the levels: the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> bounds each step, the covering
    estimate pays for the changing mark weights, and the
    allowance<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance> closes the bound. The
    singleton coefficients are controlled by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> and their format by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a>.

    For the local expansion, give a mode <math|z<rsub|I>> the weight
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>w<rsub|i>>, with <math|w<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>.
    The resulting coefficient norm is submultiplicative: multiplication sums
    over disjoint splittings of<nbsp><math|I>, exactly the splittings in the
    product of the two weighted norms. The coefficientwise rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> applies in this algebra, with the
    attachment bound at the preceding stage of the construction. Derivative
    and support weights are included in the separate majorants
    <math|v<rsub|a,i>>; no submultiplicativity is asserted for them.
    Connected terms with at least two marks
    obey<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-cover> with <math|B=B<rsub|k>>, because
    distinct anchors are separated by <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>> and
    <math|B<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>8>.

    The weight change from <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|h,i+1>> to
    <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|h,i>> costs <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>>
    on a mode<nbsp><math|I>. For <math|<around|\||I|\|>\<geqslant\>2>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-cover> at level<nbsp><math|i> gives

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|{|<frac|64*log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>|B<rsub|i>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|i>><around|(|X|)>|}>.
    </equation*>

    The coefficient of the covering number tends to zero at fixed stage,
    since <math|B<rsub|i>\<asymp\><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>> and
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i+1>/<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<to\>1>. The
    source entry bounds therefore pay this increase from the support reserve
    at each step. The singleton terms use the coefficient and graph bounds
    established below. This pays for the varying weights on all modes with at
    least two marks.

    The weights <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|h,i>> estimate the intermediate source
    polynomials; the weight in the final integral remains
    <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|h,k>>. The inverse estimate of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> gives
    <math|b<rsub|h,i+1>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|d>*C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>*b<rsub|h,i>>.
    Therefore<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-allowance-transport> proves by
    induction that

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|w><rsub|h,i>b<rsub|h,i>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>*<space|2em><around|(|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>N|)>.
    </equation*>

    The graph norm has the same bound with its column constant included in
    <math|A<rsub|j,d>>. The normalized forced terms retain their original
    eligibility events. Their remaining large-field suppression pays the
    fixed derivative factors before the forced normalization is divided out.

    Here is the conversion from a marked-anchor bound to the site sum
    required by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. Each support contains
    its marked anchor. If it also contains a site<nbsp><math|x> at distance
    <math|R> from that anchor, its connected covering number is at least
    <math|R/<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)>>. One reserved unit of support
    exponent thus gives a factor at most <math|e<rsup|-R/<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)>>>.
    Distinct anchors have occupancy one, so summing this factor over them is
    bounded by

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|z\<in\><Z><rsup|4>>e<rsup|-<around|\||z|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>/<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>.
    </equation*>

    This estimate also bounds the periodic sum, by lifting the distinct
    anchors to nearest representatives. The finite derivative products and
    polynomial evaluations cost at most <math|A<rsub|j,d>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|q<rsup|<math-up|ht>><rsub|d>>>.
    Rooted attachment sums use the other reserved support unit to absorb
    <math|e<rsup|\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>*n<rsub|\<star\>>>>;
    summing their convergent tree majorants enlarges the bound by at most
    two, after decreasing <math|\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>> if
    necessary. For the weighted majorants
    <math|v<rsub|a,i><rsup|<math-up|src>>> of all other marked factors,
    including the connected source terms already formed, this gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*sup<rsub|x>
      <big|sum><rsub|a:x\<in\>Z<rsub|a,i>>v<rsub|a,i><rsup|<math-up|src>>\<leqslant\>2*A<rsub|j,d>*<around|(|2*B<rsub|i>+1|)><rsup|4>*<around|(|1+B<rsub|i>|)><rsup|4>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i><rsup|q<rsup|<math-up|ht>><rsub|d>>*\<zeta\><rsub|d,i>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>/2.
    </equation*>

    Start with finite source products at level<nbsp><math|N>, where there are
    no connected higher modes. In each descending step, apply the rooted
    bound to the input majorants, change the mark weights using the covering
    estimate, and use the displayed site bound for the output majorants. This
    closes the induction, with a bound independent of the number of marks and
    of the truncation. The unmarked factors contribute at most the other
    <math|\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>/2>, by
    item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries>. Thus the complete attachment sum is
    at most <math|\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>> at every level
    <math|i\<in\><around|[|k,N|]>>. Passing to the absolutely convergent
    limit preserves the bound. In particular the rooted clause applies to the
    other marked factors as well as to the ordinary factors.
  </proof>

  The next estimate is the source of all volume-uniform bounds. Place one
  copy of the insertion in every cell of the level-<math|m> tiling, give each
  copy a nilpotent mark, and integrate down to a level<nbsp><math|k> at which
  every cell contains <math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>> sites.
  The marks stay at distance at least <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> at every
  intermediate level, so the attachment condition of the rooted clause holds
  uniformly in the number of marks (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments>).
  Each mark then costs <math|e<rsup|6>/<math-ss|w><rsub|k>>, where
  <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|k><rsup|-1>=a<rsub|k><rsup|-d>*e<rsup|o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>>
  is the size of the transported insertion, and each level-<math|k> site
  costs the unmarked normalization <math|e<rsup|q<rsub|k>>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-normalization-cost>. Taking the
  <math|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>|\|>>-th root gives
  the per-insertion bound <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|k><rsup|-1>e<rsup|6+<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k>>>.

  We also use the estimate for a residual observable<nbsp><math|E> with an
  exact source representation. Call <math|<math-ss|w>E> controlled at
  level<nbsp><math|k> when its weighted singleton exponential bound is at
  most<nbsp><math|3>, its forced singleton bound is at most<nbsp><math|1>,
  and the connected regular and forced bounds contribute at most one each per
  mark, with the original eligibility tests. The source evolution from its
  initial level down to<nbsp><math|k> must retain these bounds and satisfy
  the attachment bounds<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance> at every
  intervening level. This condition concerns the complete source, including
  its forced terms. Fix <math|d> and a bounded coefficient ball. A stopping
  pair is a pair of levels <math|<reflevel|d>\<leqslant\>m\<leqslant\>k> with
  <math|B<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Replicated source bound><label|lem:E-replication> There are mark
    weights <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|k>\<gtr\>0>, independent of<nbsp><math|p>,
    such that, for each stopping pair, some
    <math|N<rsub|0>*<around|(|d,<text|ball>,m,k|)>> gives, for all
    <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and all<nbsp><math|p>,

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|w><rsub|k><rsup|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>|\|>><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>><around|(|O<around|(|c|)>|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k>>|\|>*q<rsub|k>+6<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>|\|>>.
    </equation*>

    For every <math|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>0>, there is
    <math|k<rsub|0><around|(|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>> with <math|6-log
    <math-ss|w><rsub|k>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>*s<rsub|k>>
    for all <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0><around|(|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>>.
    The same integral bound holds with <math|O<around|(|c|)>,<math-ss|w><rsub|k>>
    replaced by <math|E,<math-ss|w>> whenever <math|<math-ss|w>E> is
    controlled at<nbsp><math|k>.
  </lemma>

  The proof bounds the complete source before taking its replicated integral.

  <\proof>
    The marks are attached through Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments>,
    the expansion of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> bounds the higher
    modes, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-normalization-cost> bounds the
    unmarked ratio; Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a> control the singleton coefficients and
    their format. Enlarge the fixed coefficient radius to include its signed
    and conjugated images. Put

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|k>=R<rprime|'>*C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|k>>*<big|prod><rsub|i\<less\>k>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>,<space|2em><math-ss|w><rsub|k>=a<rsub|k><rsup|d>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-w<rsub|d>>/E<rsub|k>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|R<rprime|'>\<gtr\>0> depends on the coefficient ball and
    <math|w<rsub|d>> is a fixed nonnegative integer large enough for its
    finite polynomial evaluations and forced counterparts. The transport
    inequality of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>, iterated from
    <math|<reflevel|d>>, bounds the level-<math|k> coefficients by the
    product in <math|E<rsub|k>>. Threshold powers introduce only a fixed
    power of <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>; enlarging <math|w<rsub|d>>
    includes them. Increase <math|w<rsub|d>> to at least
    <math|q<rsup|<math-up|ht>><rsub|d>+4>. Multiplying every mark by
    <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|k>> then bounds its full singleton size by
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-w<rsub|d>>\<leqslant\>\<zeta\><rsub|d,k>>.

    The reflected anchors at level<nbsp><math|k> have spacing
    <math|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|k><rsup|2>>, and their
    parents agree under each block map. Since
    <math|B<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> and
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|i>\<geqslant\>3>, their spacing is at least
    <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> at every finer level. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments> with initial weight
    <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|k>>. Its full-interval bound verifies the
    hypothesis of the rooted clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>.
    Use the mode-weighted coefficient norm of that proof; derivative and
    support weights remain in the separate majorants.

    Singleton terms are bounded directly by their one-mark norms. Taylor's
    product rule introduces at most the factorial of the derivative order;
    the stage column constant was chosen above this factorial. With
    <math|j=J<rsub|k+1>>, the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> supplies the derivative bound at
    support exponent <math|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|k>>+3> and the forced bound
    <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>*e<rsup|\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|k>*n<rsub|\<star\>>>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|k+1><rsup|2>>>
    at exponent <math|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|k>>+2>, where
    <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>> is the integrated majorant of the distinguished
    factor and <math|n<rsub|\<star\>>> its covering number. Multiplying by a
    mark weight changes only the distinguished factor in its tree sum.
    Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns> pays the derivative factorial. The
    weighted sums therefore retain those bounds; decreasing the final
    exponent from <math|100> to any value in <math|<around|[|98,100|]>> only
    decreases the sums. The forced normalization uses half the suppression,
    leaving the other half for the marked derivative and attachment losses.
    Their spatial anchor is always one of the marks; a nonzero mode is
    assigned only to a support containing all its marked anchors. This proves
    the support assertion in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>, as well as its
    absolute convergence. Restricting the set of marks commutes with the
    construction, since every operation takes disjoint unions of their modes.

    At the stopping level, the weighted supremum of each complete singleton
    exponential term is at most <math|3>, and that of its forced profile is
    at most<nbsp><math|1>. Indeed, its polynomial part is at most its
    coefficient bound, its marked regular sum is at most that bound, and the
    normalization of the forced part is at most one;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1> and the definition of <math|E<rsub|k>>
    bound the sum by<nbsp><math|3>. The forced profile follows from its
    <math|\<kappa\><rsub|k>> normalization.
    For<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>, the local absolute sums give an
    additional two per mark: exponentiation of the connected regular terms
    and the eligible forced sum each contribute at most one. Integrating the
    remaining scalar-free unmarked exponent and dividing by the unmarked
    partition function gives at most <math|e<rsup|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k>>|\|>*q<rsub|k>>>,
    by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-normalization-cost>. Thus coefficient
    extraction in the weighted source norm yields

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|w><rsub|k><rsup|<around|\||I<rsub|0>|\|>><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I<rsub|0>>F<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k>>|\|>*q<rsub|k>+<around|(|3+1+2|)><around|\||I<rsub|0>|\|>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Exact integration preserves these normalized coefficients, proving the
    first assertion. The ratio of site and cell counts is
    <math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>>. For a residual source
    controlled at weight<nbsp><math|<math-ss|w>>, the same coefficient
    extraction uses <math|<math-ss|w>> in place
    of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|w><rsub|k>>; only the bounds <math|3+1+2> and the
    unmarked ratio enter this calculation. Taking the cell root gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-residual-root><around|\<\|\|\>|E|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m>>\<leqslant\><math-ss|w><rsup|-1>exp
      <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k>+6|}>.
    </equation>

    In particular <math|<math-ss|w>=c<math-ss|w><rsub|0>> gives a bound
    <math|C<rsub|m,k,<math-ss|w><rsub|0>>/c>, uniformly in the cutoff and
    period. This proves the residual clause, for which <math|E> need not be a
    primary insertion.

    Finally<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>, summed as
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-running>, gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>k>log C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>,<space|2em>log
      <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The separate final column is included in this sum for <math|k\<gtr\>0>.
    Hence, for every <math|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>0>, there is
    <math|k<rsub|0><around|(|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>> with <math|6-log
    <math-ss|w><rsub|k>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>*s<rsub|k>>
    for all <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0><around|(|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>>.
    All choices precede the period, and all the estimates are on full cutoff
    tails.
  </proof>

  The centered estimate fixes its constant before the physical cell size. A
  sufficiently fine cutoff then places the fixed insertion stencil strictly
  inside that cell.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Centered bound><label|lem:E2.3> For every <math|d> and
    <math|R,\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, there is
    <math|A*<around|(|d,R,\<varepsilon\>|)>\<geqslant\>0> such that, for
    every <math|m\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>, some
    <math|N<rsub|0>*<around|(|d,R,\<varepsilon\>,m|)>\<geqslant\>m> gives,
    for <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>, every <math|p,y> and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>R>,

    <\equation*>
      O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c,y|)><text| is
      interior>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c,y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m><around|(|y|)>>\<leqslant\>A*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<varepsilon\>>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The replicated source bound of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>
    gives the seminorm at the stopping level <math|k=k<around|(|m|)>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>; the choice of <math|k<around|(|m|)>>
    makes the per-cell site cost negligible against <math|s<rsub|k>>. For
    <math|0\<less\>\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>\<less\>1>,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|O<around|(|c,0|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m><around|(|0|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k>+6-log
      <math-ss|w><rsub|k>|}>,>>|<row|<cell|6-log
      <math-ss|w><rsub|k>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|(|d+\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>*s<rsub|k>*<space|1em><around|(|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0><around|(|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>*s<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>*<space|1em><around|(|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0><around|(|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:E-centered-input>>>>>
    </align>

    The cutoff threshold is uniform in the coefficient ball and
    in<nbsp><math|p>.

    Put <math|\<tau\>=s<rsub|k<around|(|m|)>>>, <math|\<upsilon\>=s<rsub|m>>
    and <math|W=<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)><rsup|4>>. Since
    <math|q<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      \<tau\>-\<upsilon\>=log <around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k>|)>\<leqslant\>W\<leqslant\>W*q<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>*\<tau\>.
    </equation*>

    The exponent in the first line of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-centered-input> is
    therefore at most <math|<around|(|d+2*\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>*\<tau\>\<leqslant\><around|(|d+2*\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>*\<upsilon\>/<around|(|1-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>>.
    Choose <math|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>> so that
    <math|<around|(|d+2*\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>/<around|(|1-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>d+\<varepsilon\>>.
    This proves the required estimate with constant one for all sufficiently
    large<nbsp><math|m>. For the finitely many remaining
    <math|m\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>, use the first line
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-centered-input> at the selected fixed
    <math|k<around|(|m|)>> and take the maximum of its ratio to
    <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<varepsilon\>>> and<nbsp>one. This
    fixes<nbsp><math|A> before<nbsp><math|m>.

    Finally the stencil of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08> has fixed
    lattice radius. For fixed<nbsp><math|m> its physical radius tends to zero
    as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>. Translation
    covariance moves the centered calculation to any<nbsp><math|y>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Negligible errors and local
  matching><label|sec:partII-E.2.3>

  The following estimates concern the insertions of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, with the terminal
  maps<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>> of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>. They are used to identify the quotient
  fields in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>. A statement holds on full
  cutoff tails if it holds for all <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and
  every<nbsp><math|p>. A nonnegative family <math|b<rsub|N,p>> has subpower
  growth when, for every <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
  <math|b<rsub|N,p>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|N><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>> on such a
  tail. A coefficient family is tame if

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:F-subpower>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0\<exists\>N<rsub|0>\<forall\>N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>\<forall\>p:<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|N><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>.
  </equation>

  For later volume extraction we also use strictly increasing subsequences
  <math|N=<math-ss|n><around|(|j|)>>, <math|p=P<around|(|k|)>> and the
  ordered limit

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E2-tails><math-up|olim><space|0.17em>X<rsub|N,p>=x<space|1em>\<Longleftrightarrow\><space|1em>\<forall\>\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0\<exists\>k<rsub|0>\<forall\>k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>\<exists\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>\<forall\>j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>:<around|\||X<rsub|<math-ss|n><around|(|j|)>,P<around|(|k|)>>-x|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>.
  </equation>

  The ordered versions of tameness and negligibility replace full cutoff
  tails by these quantifiers. We prove the full-tail versions of the bounds
  below; the same constructions work for ordered-tame families, with their
  ordered thresholds, and this is the form used in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>.

  An anchor is the lattice site at which an insertion is placed; translates
  of a bond tiling are by lattice vectors. In a base cell of
  side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>>, let<nbsp><math|z<rsup|a>> be the anchor's
  integer coordinate measured from the first site in direction<nbsp><math|a>,
  and put

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E2-margin>M<rsub|n<rsub|\<ast\>>,k,N>=<around*|\<lfloor\>|<frac|a<rsub|k>|8*<around|(|n<rsub|\<ast\>>+1|)>*a<rsub|N>>|\<rfloor\>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|n<rsub|\<ast\>>,k,N>\<leqslant\>z<rsup|a>,<space|1em>z<rsup|a>+M<rsub|n<rsub|\<ast\>>,k,N>+1\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|N><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  These inequalities specify a relative interior margin.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Negligible errors><label|def:E2-small> An observable
    family<nbsp><math|E<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>> is negligible if there are
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|N,p>\<geqslant\>0> with <math|sup<rsub|p>
    \<eta\><rsub|N,p>\<to\>0> as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>> such that, for every
    arity<nbsp><math|n<rsub|\<ast\>>> and level<nbsp><math|m>, there are
    <math|k\<geqslant\>m> and <math|N<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>k> with the
    following property: for all <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>,
    all<nbsp><math|p>, every translated bond
    tiling<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>> of
    side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>> and every base-cell anchor<nbsp><math|y>
    satisfying<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-margin>,

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)><text| is interior>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\><rsub|N,p><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </definition>

  The tolerance is chosen before <math|n<rsub|\<ast\>>> and<nbsp><math|m>;
  the level and the cutoff tail may depend on these two requests. Here
  <math|n<rsub|\<ast\>>> is the number of insertions in a later product test,
  and the lower level<nbsp><math|m> requires the chosen cell scale to be
  arbitrarily fine. All residuals below are translation covariant, and their
  signed and conjugated images obey the same estimates. To pass from centered
  to arbitrary interior seminorms, choose <math|k<rprime|'>\<geqslant\>k>
  with <math|24*<around|(|n<rsub|\<ast\>>+1|)>*a<rsub|k<rprime|'>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>>
  and apply the finite reflection argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>.
  It uses only fixed stencils and the chessboard inequality, so it also
  applies to these residuals on full cutoff tails.

  Local matching converts a local observable of controlled size into an
  insertion of the same degree. It makes no assumption on the size of the
  physical coefficient it produces; it bounds its inverse terminal
  coordinate, which is the quantity in<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-subpower>. The
  derivative bound below controls Taylor extraction, and the supremum bound
  controls the forced complement.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Local comparison><label|lem:E-local-comparison>

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)><em|Local matching.> Let <math|F<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>> be
      local observables such that <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d<rprime|'>>*F<rsub|N,p>>
      is continuous on a fixed stencil and regular on healthy configurations
      in the sense of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>, with derivative
      bounds and a supremum bound over all configurations of subpower growth.
      Then there are tame <math|b<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|d<rprime|'>>> with
      <math|O<rsup|<around|(|d<rprime|'>|)>><around|(|b|)>-F> negligible.

      <item*|(ii)><em|Auxiliary equation insertions.> For the auxiliary
      section of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4>, there are invertible
      <math|P<rsub|N,p>>, with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|N,p><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2>
      eventually uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>, such that, with
      <math|B<rsub|d,N,p>=<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>P<rsub|N,p>*t<rsub|N><rsup|<factorcount>><math-ss|R><rsub|d>>,
      the comparison<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-equation-comparison> holds for every
      equation label <math|\<pi\><rsub|N,p>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> with tame
      <math|B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<pi\><rsub|N,p>>.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  Here <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> is the equation ideal described in the proof
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>, and the comparison is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-equation-comparison>O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<pi\><rsub|N,p>,y|)>-a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>\<pi\><rsub|N,p>|]><around|(|y|)><space|1em><text|is
    negligible>.
  </equation>

  Both parts use the same backward coefficient correction.

  <\proof>
    We represent the observable as a one-mark source at the cutoff, carry the
    difference with the selected primary insertion backwards with the marked
    step (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>), and bound its replicated
    seminorm by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>. Write
    <math|h<rsub|N>=a<rsub|N><rsup|d<rprime|'>>*F<rsub|N,p><around|(|0|)>>.
    Enlarge its stencil by the section stencil. The radial Taylor
    coefficients through degree<nbsp><math|d<rprime|'>> (in the sections of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>) give a coefficient <math|v<rsub|N>> and a
    remainder. They are bounded by a fixed multiple of the derivative bound:
    it is a linear map on a finite jet space. Subtract the section with that
    coefficient on the healthy part. Its regular remainder has zero value and
    zero radial jet through degree<nbsp><math|d<rprime|'>>. Assign the
    complementary observable to the forced supports meeting the enlarged
    stencil. This gives an exact one-mark representation of
    <math|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>*h<rsub|N>>, with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-local-start><around|\||v<rsub|N>|\|><rsub|1>+r<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>E<rsub|N,p>,<space|2em><text|forced
      majorant>\<leqslant\>E<rprime|'><rsub|N,p>,
    </equation>

    where <math|E,E<rprime|'>> have subpower growth. The support weight of
    this fixed enlarged stencil is bounded by
    <math|e<rsup|81*\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>>> eventually; its
    <math|B<rsub|N>> covering number is at most<nbsp><math|81>. Threshold
    powers, the Taylor-coefficient bound and this support weight are included
    in <math|E,E<rprime|'>>. The entry conditions make each of them subpower.
    No division by the forced allowance is made on the uncontrolled
    complement: it is retained with its original eligibility tests and the
    supremum bound <math|E<rprime|'>>.

    To compare with the selected graph, carry this representation backwards
    and at the reference level choose the primary coefficient with the same
    polynomial coordinate. Subtract the corresponding selected inserted
    density. Its polynomial coordinate at the reference level is zero. The
    following calculation carries this matching condition towards the fine
    scale.

    For one step, let <math|u,v> be the coarse and fine coordinates,
    <math|R,T> their remainder bounds, <math|Y=<around|\||<math-ss|S><rsub|L>v|\|><rsub|1>>,
    <math|K> the norm bound for the leading map and its inverse,
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>=L<rsup|-3/8>> and
    <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|L>=L<rsup|1/8>>. The marked-step inequalities
    give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-match-cone>Y\<leqslant\>K*<around*|(|<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*<around|(|Y+T|)>|)>,<space|2em>R\<leqslant\>K*Y+\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*T.
    </equation>

    Suppose <math|a<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>R>, with
    <math|a=32*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>4*K<rsup|2>>.
    Then <math|4*K<around|\||u|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>Y+\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*T>.
    Substitution in the first inequality, using
    <math|4*K*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1>, gives
    <math|Y\<leqslant\>4*K*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>*T>.
    Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-match> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> gives

    <\equation*>
      4*K*<space|0.17em>32*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>|)><rsup|2>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1,<space|2em><around|(|4*K<rsup|2>+1|)>*\<vartheta\><rsub|L>*\<varepsilon\><rsub|L>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    Consequently <math|32*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>|)><rsup|2><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>T>
    and <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|L>*R\<leqslant\>T>. The matching condition
    starts this induction with <math|u=0> at the reference level. Multiplying
    the second inequality through the interval gives the inverse-scale gain,
    while the finest-level bound is closed by absorbing the coefficient
    contribution into its remainder bound. In particular, with subpower
    <math|E<rprime|''>> absorbing <math|E,E<rprime|'>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-backward-match><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|N>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|2*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|N>>|)>*E<rprime|''><rsub|N,p>|\<ell\><rsub|N-1><rsup|1/8>>,<space|2em>R<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|N>>|)>*E<rprime|''><rsub|N,p>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|N>-s<rsub|k>|)>/8>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<Delta\><rsub|N>> is the difference from the selected fine
    polynomial coordinate. The same bounds hold when a local source is first
    represented at any level <math|n\<leqslant\>N>, with its polynomial
    coordinate matched to the selected graph at a lower reference level. If
    <math|E<rprime|''><rsub|n;N,p>> bounds its starting data after the
    coefficient contribution is absorbed, then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-backward-match-level><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|n>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|2*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|n>>|)>*E<rprime|''><rsub|n;N,p>|\<ell\><rsub|n-1><rsup|1/8>>,<space|2em>R<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|n>>|)>*E<rprime|''><rsub|n;N,p>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|n>-s<rsub|k>|)>/8>*<space|1em><around|(|<reflevel|d<rprime|'>>\<leqslant\>k\<less\>n|)>.
    </equation>

    Indeed the cone starts at the same zero matched coordinate, and its
    induction uses only the steps through<nbsp><math|n>. The boundary
    absorption uses the starting bound there. The product
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=k><rsup|n-1>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-1/8>> is
    exactly <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|n>-s<rsub|k>|)>/8>>. Taking
    <math|n=N> recovers<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match>. The factor
    <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|n>-s<rsub|k>|)>/8>> is the inverse-scale
    gain: each matched step contracts the remainder by
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|-1/8>>.

    To estimate the replicated residual, for a source of size <math|E>
    started at <math|N>, use at level<nbsp><math|k> the mark weight

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|w>=<frac|\<zeta\><rsub|d<rprime|'>,k>/4|a<rsub|k><rsup|-d<rprime|'>>*E*exp
      <around|{|-<around|(|s<rsub|N>-s<rsub|k>|)>/8|}>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d<rprime|'>,k>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|q<rsup|<math-up|ht>><rsub|d<rprime|'>>+4|)>>>
    is the source allowance fixed before<nbsp><math|N,p>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance>. Its reciprocal is a fixed
    power of the reference coupling. At every finer step multiply this weight
    by the source contraction factor of that step. Its product with the
    dimensionless singleton size is at most one quarter of the available
    source allowance; the other three quarters bound the graph remainder, the
    forced complement and their sum. This follows inductively from the two
    inequalities in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates>. For the first step the
    forced complement is bounded by <math|E<rprime|'>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-local-start>; the large-field suppression absorbs its
    fixed support and derivative factors before division by the new forced
    normalization. The radius transport<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-allowance-transport>
    and the site-sum calculation in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments> verify the attachment
    bound at every finer level. Thus the residual is controlled at
    level<nbsp><math|k> in the sense of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>.
    The singleton polynomial coordinate at the reference level is zero, so
    the representation constructed this way is exactly the difference with
    the selected primary coefficient.

    Here <math|m> labels the tiling and <math|k> the finer stopping level at
    which its replicated sources are integrated. Replicate the difference at
    a stopping pair <math|m\<leqslant\>k>, and use the weighted source
    representation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>. Coefficient extraction
    contributes <math|<math-ss|w><rsup|-1>> per mark; the complete singleton
    bounds and the normalized unmarked ratio<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-ratio>
    contribute a constant depending on <math|m,k>. Consequently, for every
    fixed such pair,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-local-error><around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsup|<around|(|d<rprime|'>|)>><around|(|b|)>-F|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|m><around|(|0|)>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|m,k>*\<frak-e\><rsub|N,p>*a<rsub|N><rsup|1/8>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d<rprime|'>><rsup|-1>b<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>2*E<rprime|'''><rsub|N,p>,
    </equation>

    where <math|\<frak-e\>,E<rprime|'''>> have subpower growth. The factor
    <math|e<rsup|s<rsub|k>/8>> and the fixed weight factor
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|d<rprime|'>,k><rsup|-1>> belong to
    <math|C<rsub|m,k>>. The same argument applies after each signed
    permutation and conjugation, with translation covariance and the union of
    their fixed stencils. Absorbing two factors <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1/32>>
    bounds the first expression by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|1/16>> on every fixed
    pair. The interior-seminorm comparison after
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small> gives the common tolerance
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|1/32>>, chosen before all cell requests. This proves
    negligibility and tameness on full cutoff tails, and part<nbsp>(i).

    For part<nbsp>(ii), perform the construction on all its basis polynomials
    together, with the original Taylor normalization. Let <math|u<rsub|j>> be
    the primary bare coefficient matching its <math|j>th basis vector, and
    set <math|P*x=<big|sum><rsub|j>x<rsub|j>*u<rsub|j>>, after the finite
    symmetry averaging. For this fixed section the regular derivative bound
    <math|H<rsub|j,d>> at stage<nbsp><math|j> is fixed before its ratio,
    independently of the later coupling. The sharper coefficient estimate
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match> therefore has numerator bounded by
    <math|2*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|j>|)>*H<rsub|j,d>> on each
    basis input. The ratio condition, including the finite
    basis-recombination factor, gives

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||x-P*x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|4><around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed, before summing over the fixed basis the right side is the
    last-step factor <math|\<ell\><rsub|N-1><rsup|-1/8>> times the stage
    coefficient bound; the stage ratio was chosen after that bound. The
    matrix is therefore invertible and <math|<around|\||P<rsup|-1>*x|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2<around|\||x|\|><rsub|1>>.
    The construction is common to all later cell requests. Multiplication by
    <math|t<rsub|N><rsup|<factorcount>><math-ss|R><rsub|d>> changes a
    polynomial label into its auxiliary bare insertion coordinate;
    multiplication by <math|<math-ss|Z>> changes that coordinate to the
    reference normalization. These are exactly the maps <math|B> in the
    statement. The inverse bound preserves tameness,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-local-error> compares the two sections on its
    transported equation ideal. Finite combinations use the same subpower
    factors. The argument only replaces eventual bounds on coefficients, so
    ordered-tame families obey the corresponding ordered version as well.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Equation identities and coefficient
  corrections><label|sec:partII-E.2.4>

  The equation projections separate the coefficient directions controlled by
  exact cutoff identities. The free equation space consists of polynomial
  labels that vanish on curvature jets satisfying the Maxwell equations. Its
  transported image is the kernel of the projections chosen below, and the
  free quotient has dimension<nbsp><math|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>. The
  projections and the equation observables in
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>\U<reference|lem:E2.7> are chosen together,
  before the spacing and volume subsequences.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Equation projections><label|lem:E2.6> For every<nbsp><math|d>,
    there are complex linear idempotents <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>>
    and <math|C<rsup|\<Pi\>><rsub|d>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|\<Pi\>><rsub|d>,<space|2em>dim
      <math-up|ran><space|0.17em>\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>\<leqslant\>dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>,<space|2em>ker
      \<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>=B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>,
    </equation*>

    with the maps <math|B> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>(ii).
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The kernel is prescribed by the transported equation ideal, and the bound
    comes from choosing orthogonal projections in a fixed norm. Let
    <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>\<subset\>V<rsub|d>> be the polynomials of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07> belonging to the ideal generated by
    the linear Maxwell equations in radial jet coordinates. Radial
    coordinates already satisfy the linear Bianchi identities. Taking
    invariant polynomials commutes with the on-shell quotient: average any
    representative over the compact adjoint group to obtain an invariant
    representative. Thus the on-shell map is onto <math|<maxwellspace|d>>
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11>) and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-equation-count>dim V<rsub|d>-dim
      \<cal-I\><rsub|d>=dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>.
    </equation>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>(ii) constructs invertible
    coefficient maps <math|B<rsub|d,N,p>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>>. They
    transport <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|d>> to the coefficient equations for
    auxiliary local representatives, including their change to the selected
    insertion coordinates. Set <math|U<rsub|d,N,p>=B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>.
    In the Euclidean norm on the fixed basis take the orthogonal projection
    <math|Q<rsub|d,N,p>> onto <math|U<rsub|d,N,p>> and set
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>=1-Q<rsub|d,N,p>>. Then
    <math|\<Pi\><rsup|2>=\<Pi\>> and <math|ker \<Pi\>=U<rsub|d,N,p>>.
    Equivalence with the fixed coefficient norm bounds
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>|\<\|\|\>>> independently of <math|N,p>,
    even when <math|B> is poorly conditioned. Since <math|B> is invertible,
    <math|dim ker \<Pi\>=dim \<cal-I\><rsub|d>>,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-equation-count> gives the dimension bound.

    The same local comparison maps will be used in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.7>. They and these projections are chosen
    for all cutoffs before any subsequences.
  </proof>

  The next estimate converts a coefficient equation into an observable with
  exact separated moment identities.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Equation observables><label|lem:E2.7> For these same projections,
    every tame family<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p>\<in\>ker \<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>>
    admits a measurable observable family<nbsp><math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>>,
    bounded at each anchor and cutoff on a full cutoff tail, for which
    <math|O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c<rsub|N,p>,y|)>-\<cal-Q\><rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>>
    is negligible. For every fixed spectator list
    and<nbsp><math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, on a full cutoff tail,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>\<cal-Q\><rsub|N,p><around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|h\<ne\>i>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|h>,y<rsub|h>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p>=0*<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|sep><rsub|<math-up|tor>><around|(|y|)>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The observable is the auxiliary equation section of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4>, whose separated moments vanish exactly;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>(ii) compares it with the
    insertion. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4> supplies
    <math|<wide|P|~><rsub|d>=V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>>> and
    <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>>. Using the kernel identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>, write
    <math|c<rsub|N,p>=B<rsub|d,N,p>*\<pi\><rsub|N,p>> with
    <math|\<pi\><rsub|N,p>\<in\>\<cal-I\><rsub|d>>, and set

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-equation-section>\<cal-Q\><rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-d>*V<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|aux>><around|[|<math-ss|R><rsub|d>\<pi\><rsub|N,p>|]><around|(|y|)>.
    </equation>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>,
    specifically<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-equation-comparison>, proves that
    <math|O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c|)>-\<cal-Q\>> is negligible. Its
    inverse change has subpower growth for tame<nbsp><math|c>. The common
    tolerance and interior-seminorm comparison were proved there and apply to
    this family.

    The exact zero identity is<nbsp><eqref|eq:A-aux-zero> in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4>: every auxiliary equation term is a
    Wilson action derivative whose multiplier and transport avoid the varied
    link, so Haar invariance makes its exterior moment zero. The auxiliary
    section has one finite equation radius. At prescribed positive physical
    separation, every fixed spectator stencil is exterior to it for all
    sufficiently fine cutoffs, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>. Its product is
    bounded and measurable, as required by that lemma. Apply the identity
    with that product as spectator. Finally <math|<wide|P|~><rsub|d>> is
    continuous on a finite product of compact groups. At each fixed cutoff
    its evaluation with the chosen finite coefficients is bounded.
  </proof>

  Changing the degree changes the insertion normalization. The next estimate
  corrects that change before taking limits.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Corrected inclusion><label|lem:E2.8> For every
    <math|d\<leqslant\>d<rprime|'>> and <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, there is a
    tame family<nbsp><math|b<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|d<rprime|'>>> such that

    <\equation*>
      O<rsup|<around|(|d<rprime|'>|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|b<rsub|N,p>,y|)>-O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y|)><space|1em><text|is
      negligible><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The target observable satisfies the hypotheses of local matching
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>(i)) in
    degree<nbsp><math|d<rprime|'>>. After multiplication by
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d<rprime|'>>>, it is

    <\equation*>
      a<rsub|N><rsup|d<rprime|'>-d>*P<rsub|d><around|[|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]><around|(|y|)>.
    </equation*>

    It is a local continuous function on the fixed degree-<math|d> stencil.
    Its ordered derivatives in the allowed link directions and its supremum
    have subpower bounds. Indeed, the inverse terminal coordinate has this
    growth by the first transport inequality of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>; threshold
    powers have subpower growth because <math|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|N>=o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>>. The factor
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d<rprime|'>-d>\<leqslant\>1> can only decrease these
    bounds.

    Local matching extracts a degree-<math|d<rprime|'>> coefficient
    <math|b<rsub|N,p>> whose inverse terminal coordinate still has subpower
    growth. Its residual seminorm is bounded by
    <math|C<rsub|m,k>*\<frak-e\><rsub|N,p>*a<rsub|N><rsup|1/8>> at every
    fixed stopping pair of levels, with <math|\<frak-e\>> of subpower growth.
    The absorption and interior-seminorm argument in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison> gives one tolerance
    <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|1/32>> for all the later cell requests. The residual
    is therefore negligible. For <math|d=d<rprime|'>> one may simply take
    <math|b<rsub|N,p>=c>, with zero residual.
  </proof>

  The physical difference quotient requires a correction in the next degree.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Corrected difference><label|lem:E2.9> For every
    degree<nbsp><math|d>, <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>> and axis<nbsp><math|a>,
    there is a tame family<nbsp><math|b<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|d+1>> such that

    <\equation*>
      O<rsup|<around|(|d+1|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|b<rsub|N,p>,y|)>-<frac|O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y+e<rsub|a>|)>-O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y|)>|a<rsub|N>><space|1em><text|is
      negligible><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Multiplying the target difference by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d+1>> removes
    the inverse spacing and gives exactly

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|d><around|[|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]>*<around|(|y+e<rsub|a>|)>-P<rsub|d><around|[|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]><around|(|y|)>,
    </equation*>

    to which local matching (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>(i))
    applies in degree <math|d+1>. Its stencil is the union of one fixed
    stencil and its unit translate. It is continuous and local. Moving an
    ordered derivative anchor by one lattice site multiplies its spatial
    weight by at most a stage-dependent factor. The factor is at most
    <math|2<rsup|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>>> per derivative. The logarithmic
    condition <math|\<varpi\><rsub|J<rsub|N>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>> in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> makes each fixed product of these factors
    subpower. The other bounds are those in the preceding proof. Local
    matching in degree <math|d+1> now supplies a tame coefficient family and
    the same negligible residual. The inverse spacing has been removed before
    matching, so it causes no further loss in that residual. The four axes
    use translates of this one computation and their fixed spatial-weight
    bounds.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Comparison with free fields><label|sec:partII-E.2.5>

  Fix<nbsp><math|d> and a real basis <math|\<frak-m\><rsub|1>=1,\<frak-m\><rsub|2>,\<ldots\>,\<frak-m\><rsub|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>>
  of<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d>>. Choose finitely many one- or two-point
  tests. In test<nbsp><math|i>, the first label is the
  variable<nbsp><math|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>>; the optional second label is a
  fixed basis element. Let <math|y<rsup|i>\<in\><Gen|n<rsub|i>>>,
  <math|n<rsub|i>\<in\><around|{|1,2|}>>, be the test's configuration. The
  free test matrix is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E2-free-matrix>\<cal-G\><rsub|i*j>=<maxwellcorr><rsub|n<rsub|i>><around|(|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>,<text|
    test >i<text| spectator>;y<rsup|i>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The next lemma supplies a finite family that distinguishes every free field
  of degree at most<nbsp><math|d>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Free tests><label|lem:E2-free-tests> For every<nbsp><math|d> and
    real basis<nbsp><math|\<frak-m\><rsub|1>=1,\<frak-m\><rsub|2>,\<ldots\>,\<frak-m\><rsub|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>>,
    there are finitely many one- or two-point tests at generic
    configurations, with every second label a basis element, for which the
    matrix<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-free-matrix> has
    rank<nbsp><math|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>.
  </lemma>

  The proof uses only the Maxwell correlations of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11> and the free positivity in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, independently of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F>.

  <\proof>
    Let<nbsp><math|c\<ne\>0> in<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1> gives a strictly positive reflected
    two-point value for<nbsp><math|c> and the reflection
    of<nbsp><math|<wide|c|\<bar\>>>. Expand that second label in the
    basis<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>|)>>. At least one of the
    resulting two-point functionals is nonzero on<nbsp><math|c>. Each free
    Wick correlation is a finite sum of products of derivatives of
    <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    between distinct sites, hence is smooth there. This permits a
    perturbation of the pair to<nbsp><math|<Gen|2>> preserving this nonzero
    value. Thus the common kernel of all permitted test functionals is zero.
    Starting with<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d>>, choose a test nonzero on a
    nonzero vector in the current common kernel. Each choice strictly lowers
    its dimension. After finitely many choices the common kernel is zero,
    so<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\>> has rank<nbsp><math|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>.
  </proof>

  Fix such a family for the rank comparison below.

  For an index<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> of the comparison sequence, a common
  coefficient list<nbsp><math|b<rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|j,N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|d>> and
  lattice-compatible configuration <math|x<rsup|\<nu\>,i>=r<rsub|\<nu\>>*y<rsup|\<nu\>,i>>
  define the test insertions <math|F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|tst>>=O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|b<rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|j<rsub|h>,N,p>,x<rsub|h><rsup|\<nu\>,i>/a<rsub|N>|)>>,
  with<nbsp><math|j<rsub|1>=j> and the spectator index fixed by
  test<nbsp><math|i>. Physical points are read modulo the lattice period.
  Let<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\><rsub|J><rsup|\<nu\>>> be the Maxwell moment of the
  labels<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<frak-m\><rsub|j<rsub|h>>|)><rsub|h\<in\>J>>
  at<nbsp><math|<around|(|y<rsub|h><rsup|\<nu\>,i>|)><rsub|h\<in\>J>>, with
  <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<varnothing\>><rsup|\<nu\>>=1>. Define the relative
  errors by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:F-relative>R<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1,<space|2em>R<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|h\<in\>I>F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|tst>>-<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>J\<subseteq\>I>\<cal-G\><rsub|J><rsup|\<nu\>>*R<rsub|I\<setminus\>J><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The rank argument uses only the singleton and pair cases. Suppressing the
  superscript<nbsp><math|\<nu\>>, these are

  <\equation*>
    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|R<rsub|<around|{|1|}>>>|<cell|=F<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|tst>>-\<cal-G\><rsub|<around|{|1|}>>,>>|<row|<cell|R<rsub|<around|{|1,2|}>>>|<cell|=<around|(|F<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|tst>>-\<cal-G\><rsub|<around|{|1|}>>|)>*<around|(|F<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|tst>>-\<cal-G\><rsub|<around|{|2|}>>|)>-<around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|<around|{|1,2|}>>-\<cal-G\><rsub|<around|{|1|}>>*\<cal-G\><rsub|<around|{|2|}>>|)>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation*>

  Thus the pair error subtracts the connected free two-point value from the
  product of the centered insertions. The lifts<nbsp><math|b<rsup|\<nu\>>>
  include the scale normalization, so<nbsp><math|F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|tst>>>
  carries no additional power of<nbsp><math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>>. A lift is the
  reference coefficient whose Wick-ordered leading polynomial at
  scale<nbsp><math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>> is the specified basis
  element<nbsp><math|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>>. The construction uses
  <math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>=a<rsub|t<rsub|\<nu\>>><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t<rsub|\<nu\>>>|)><rsup|b>>
  for one fixed <math|b\<gtr\>0> chosen from the degree and the finite tests,
  before<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> varies.

  The source estimates below also apply to tests with any fixed arity
  bound<nbsp><math|n\<geqslant\>2>. For a slot set <math|I<rsub|0>> with
  <math|<around|\||I<rsub|0>|\|>\<leqslant\>n>, define the free generating
  polynomial and its logarithm, the free cumulant generating function,

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|G><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>=<big|sum><rsub|J\<subseteq\>I<rsub|0>>\<cal-G\><rsub|J>*z<rsub|J>,<space|2em><math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>=log
    <math-ss|G><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|i><rsup|2>=0*<space|1em><around|(|i\<in\>I<rsub|0>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The logarithm is a finite polynomial because the constant term of
  <math|<math-ss|G><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> is one and its nonconstant part is
  nilpotent. Its coefficient on a nonempty<nbsp><math|I> is

  <\equation*>
    <around|[|z<rsub|I>|]><math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>=<big|sum><rsub|\<cal-P\><text|
    partition of >I><around|(|-1|)><rsup|<around|\||\<cal-P\>|\|>-1>*<around|(|<around|\||\<cal-P\>|\|>-1|)>!<big|prod><rsub|J\<in\>\<cal-P\>>\<cal-G\><rsub|J>.
  </equation*>

  For two slots these are the two singleton means and the connected pair
  moment; the rank comparison uses just this case. In the source estimates,
  the test positions may range over any fixed compact set
  <math|K\<subset\><Conf|<around|\||I<rsub|0>|\|>>> with
  <math|<sepinf>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. Constants may depend on
  <math|K,\<delta\>,n> and the finite degree and derivative orders; the lifts
  are common to these positions. The coefficients to be estimated are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-corrected-coefficients>\<frak-c\><rsub|I>=<around|[|z<rsub|I>|]>*<around*|(|e<rsup|-<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|h><around|(|1+z<rsub|h>*F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|tst>>|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  For the source comparison, use the intervals<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window>. Fix
  <math|b\<gtr\>0> large enough for the degree and the finite tests, put
  <math|l=t-h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>>, <math|k=l-v<rsub|t>> and
  <math|r=a<rsub|t><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|b>>. The interval
  from <math|t> to<nbsp><math|l> separates the reading points from the last
  fluctuation scale; the interval from <math|l> to<nbsp><math|k> lets the
  mark weights of the corrected sources increase while their remainders
  decrease. With <math|L=\<ell\><rsub|J<rsub|t>><rsup|<math-up|st>>>, these
  intervals satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-window-geometry>L<rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>>\<geqslant\><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|b+2>,<space|2em>L<rsup|v<rsub|t>>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|t><rsup|10>,<space|2em><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<in\><around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>/2,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|]>*<space|1em><around|(|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t|)>
  </equation>

  on sufficiently late intervals. The last assertion follows from
  <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>=o<around|(|t|)>> at fixed stage and
  the entry choices. All rounded test points lie strictly inside the
  level-<math|k> cell: their distance from the origin is
  <math|O<around|(|r|)>>, while <math|a<rsub|l>/r\<geqslant\><around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>>.
  Rounding changes their rescaled coordinates by
  <math|O<around|(|<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-b>|)>>.

  Fix the arity bound <math|n\<geqslant\>2> and the compact separated
  position set specified above. Put <math|\<cal-M\>=<cutoffsize><rsub|t>>.
  Let <math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>> bound the required derivative orders, and
  let <math|c,\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|G>>\<gtr\>0> be the localization and
  healthy-chart exponents in the estimates below. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-source-initial>\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>=\<cal-M\><rsup|-b>+C<rsup|<math-up|G>><rsub|j>*\<cal-M\><rsup|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>*<around|(|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t><rsup|-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|G>>>+e<rsup|-c*B<rsub|t>>|)>.
  </equation>

  <paragraph|Comparison windows.> A comparison window is a triple of levels
  <math|k\<less\>l\<less\>t>; put <math|\<cal-M\>=<cutoffsize><rsub|t>>. The
  proofs of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>\U<reference|lem:E-source-integral>
  use the window only through the following conditions:

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(Win1)><math|log <around|(|a<rsub|l>/a<rsub|t>|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|b+2|)>*log
    \<cal-M\>>, <math|log <around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|l>|)>\<geqslant\>10*log
    B<rsub|t>>, and <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<in\><around|[|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>/2,<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|]>>
    for <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t>;

    <item*|(Win2)><math|\<cal-C\><rsub|J<rsub|t>>*<big|prod><rsub|k\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t>\<cal-C\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|1/64>>;

    <item*|(Win3)>every level of <math|<around|[|k,t|]>> is past the entries
    of item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries> for the finitely many requests, and
    <math|Q<rsup|<math-up|cmp>><rsub|J<rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>log
    \<cal-M\>/<around|(|16*n|)>>.
  </enumerate>

  Late windows<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> satisfy (Win1)\U(Win3)
  by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window-geometry>, <eqref|eq:E-source-loss> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. A window need not lie in one stage: under
  (Win1)\U(Win3) the three lemmas hold with
  <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+2>> replaced by
  <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|J<rsub|t>>*<big|prod><rsub|l\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t>\<cal-C\><rsub|J<rsub|i>>>.

  The polynomial comparison supplies the initial error for the subsequent
  source iteration.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Middle-level source comparison><label|lem:E-source-polynomial> For
    each fixed finite label family, sufficiently late intervals admit common
    real lifts, fixing the unit and bounded in <math|N,p> for that interval.
    For every comparison window satisfying (Win1)\U(Win3) and uniformly over
    the prescribed compact position set, the coefficients of
    <math|z<rsub|I>>, <math|I\<ne\>\<varnothing\>>, of
    <math|e<rsup|-<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>*<big|prod><rsub|h><around|(|1+z<rsub|h>*F<rsup|<math-up|tst>><rsub|h>|)>>,
    represented at level<nbsp><math|l>, have total regular and normalized
    forced bound

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+2>*\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>.
    </equation*>

    The assertion holds on a full cutoff tail, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>.
  </lemma>

  The proof gives the Wick correction and the inverse-separation gain.

  <\proof>
    The free covariance is compared with the lattice covariance of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B2> and<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows> at the
    reading scale, the internal contractions are removed by Wick ordering,
    and the connected expansion of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>
    carries the corrected sources from <math|t> to<nbsp><math|l>, with the
    marked step of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> supplying the
    singleton coefficients. The sources have at most <math|n> marks in each
    cell; the number of cells is unrestricted. Use the coefficientwise rooted
    clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> in their weighted
    nilpotent algebra. Only point separation is needed for the covariance
    comparison; coincident coordinates of distinct points are allowed.

    On a fixed healthy chart, subtract the radial Taylor polynomial of each
    local insertion. The coefficient norm of the remainder is bounded by its
    next ordered derivative times the corresponding power of the coordinate
    radius. All derivatives needed here are among the finitely many stage
    orders. Express each polynomial in curvature and differentiated-curvature
    variables, carrying along their powers of<nbsp><math|r>. In the finite
    jet coordinates <math|\<xi\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<xi\><rsub|n<rsub|\<xi\>>>>
    write the covariance as <math|C=<around|(|C<rsub|a*b>|)>> and put
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|C>=<big|sum><rsub|a,b>C<rsub|a*b>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsup|2>/\<partial\>*\<xi\><rsub|a>*\<partial\>*\<xi\><rsub|b>>.
    Gaussian contraction and Wick ordering are the mutually inverse operators

    <\equation*>
      e<rsup|\<Delta\><rsub|C>/2>*\<pi\>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<geqslant\>0><frac|1|2<rsup|j>*j!>*\<Delta\><rsub|C><rsup|j>*\<pi\>,<space|2em>:\<pi\>:<rsub|C>=e<rsup|-\<Delta\><rsub|C>/2>*\<pi\>.
    </equation*>

    The series terminate on the chosen polynomial space, since each
    contraction removes two factors. We Wick-order each insertion with
    respect to its internal contractions; contractions between distinct
    insertions remain. The leading coefficient change <math|T<rsub|m>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> and Wick ordering, in the jet
    normalization of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>, are invertible and
    preserve the unit and real form. The error in replacing the selected
    coefficient map by this leading map has norm less than <math|1/4> after
    its inverse, by the marked estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates> and
    the entry condition. Solving this finite linear system gives one lift for
    each free basis polynomial, common to every row. Transport only between
    the fixed levels <math|<reflevel|d>> and<nbsp><math|t> bounds these
    reference coefficients independently of<nbsp><math|N,p> for that
    interval. All matrices are real on the real form, and the unit equation
    has the exact solution<nbsp><math|\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>>.

    The covariance calculation involves finitely many derivatives of
    <math|\<Gamma\><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||x|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    For each pair of coloured plaquette stencils the rescaled Fourier chart
    <math|K<around|(|\<xi\>|)>> is supported in
    <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>. Its leading symbol is the
    Maxwell curvature symbol <math|H<around|(|\<xi\>|)>>. For every fixed
    required order<nbsp><math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>, the quadratic-form
    calculation of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B2>, differentiated in
    frequency, gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<partial\><rsup|j>*K<around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|-j>,<space|2em><around|\||\<partial\><rsup|j>*<around|(|K-H|)><around|(|\<xi\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\||\<xi\>|\|><rsup|1-j><space|1em><around|(|0\<less\><around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<leqslant\>1,j\<leqslant\>q<rsub|<math-up|der>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    These bounds follow by differentiating the inverse quadratic symbol: its
    curl factors remove the inverse-square singularity; replacing each
    lattice difference by its linear term leaves one extra power
    of<nbsp><math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>>. The regulator and its derivatives
    obey the same fixed bounds; different colours have zero cross covariance.
    To convert the second estimate to position space, decompose a cutoff of
    <math|K-H> into annuli <math|<around|\||\<xi\>|\|>\<asymp\>2<rsup|-v>>. A
    derivative of order<nbsp><math|s> in position inserts <math|s> powers
    of<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. After <math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>\<gtr\>5+s>
    integrations by parts on each annulus its transform at<nbsp><math|R*x>,
    with <math|x> in a compact separated set, is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C*2<rsup|-v*<around|(|5+s|)>>*min <around|{|1,<around|(|R*2<rsup|-v>|)><rsup|-q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Splitting the sum at <math|2<rsup|v>\<asymp\>R> gives
    <math|C*R<rsup|-5-s>>. The smooth part away from zero decreases faster
    than this. The leading homogeneous symbol is the corresponding curvature
    derivative of<nbsp><math|\<Gamma\>> away from the origin, with the
    Fourier normalization of Part<nbsp>I. Including the curvature scaling
    powers therefore gives relative error <math|O<around|(|R<rsup|-1>|)>>.

    For the finite jet samples, terms of the matching homogeneity give this
    leading derivative exactly. Every other term has at least one extra
    inverse power of<nbsp><math|R>; summing their finitely many absolute
    coefficients preserves the <math|O<around|(|R<rsup|-1>|)>> bound. Here
    <math|R=<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|b>>, and rounding in the
    rescaled variables gives an additional <math|O<around|(|R<rsup|-1>|)>>.
    Localizing the sector covariance introduces
    <math|C<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>*e<rsup|-c*B<rsub|t>>>
    by the inverse-covariance bound in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B-windows>,
    with smaller <math|c>, since <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>\<geqslant\><wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>/2>
    throughout the interval. Its nonlinear healthy-chart error is
    <math|C<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>>*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t><rsup|-\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|G>>>>
    for some fixed <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|G>>\<gtr\>0>. The derivative
    orders and powers<nbsp><math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>> are fixed by the
    degree and the finite row family; their stage constants are fixed before
    entry. Since <math|log <cutoffsize><rsub|t>=o<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)>> and <math|B<rsub|t>\<asymp\><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>>, both errors are smaller than any
    prescribed fixed negative power of<nbsp><math|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>> after
    entry. Choose <math|b> so that the inverse-separation error is smaller
    than the finite power required by the mark weight below. The chart and
    inspected supports lie inside the nonwrapping comparison interval
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window-geometry>, so these local estimates hold on
    every period, with the same constants.

    The finite-polynomial estimate combining these errors is as follows. Let
    <math|U> bound the Gaussian comparison polynomials, <math|T> the
    one-factor error, and <math|Z> the two-factor error in their coefficient
    norm. For the connected two-point term, subtract the product of the
    one-point terms. Expanding that product gives the bound

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-Gaussian-polynomial>T+Z+<around|(|U+T|)>*T+T*U.
    </equation>

    For example, if <math|a<rsub|i>> and <math|a<rsub|i><rsup|0>> are the two
    one-point terms, then <math|a<rsub|1>*a<rsub|2>-a<rsub|1><rsup|0>*a<rsub|2><rsup|0>=<around|(|a<rsub|1>-a<rsub|1><rsup|0>|)>*a<rsub|2>+a<rsub|1><rsup|0>*<around|(|a<rsub|2>-a<rsub|2><rsup|0>|)>>,
    whose norm is at most <math|<around|(|U+T|)>*T+U*T>. The terms <math|T,Z>
    are the errors in the remaining one- and two-mark polynomial terms.
    Gaussian contraction expands into pairings, so the bound for any fixed
    polynomial is a finite polynomial in the bounds for its contractions.
    After <math|b> has been fixed sufficiently large, the preceding
    covariance and Taylor estimates make<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-Gaussian-polynomial>
    smaller than every source bound required at<nbsp><math|l>.

    For more slots, let <math|\<cal-G\><rprime|'><rsub|J>> be the Gaussian
    polynomial moment before replacing its covariance by the Maxwell
    covariance. On the compact separated position set, all these moments are
    bounded by a fixed<nbsp><math|U\<geqslant\>1>, and their discrepancies
    are at most <math|\<varepsilon\>>, a constant times the covariance and
    Taylor error above. For a partition with <math|q> blocks, telescoping its
    product bounds the discrepancy by <math|q*\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|U+\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|q-1>>.
    The partition formula for <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>
    therefore bounds the sum of all nonempty corrected coefficients by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|n>*\<varepsilon\>*<around|(|1+U+\<varepsilon\>|)><rsup|n-1>.
    </equation*>

    There are finitely many subsets and partitions for fixed<nbsp><math|n>.
    Their constants are included in<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>>.
    Subtracting <math|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>> cancels every leading
    free coefficient in the logarithm; for a pair this is precisely the
    mean-product subtraction in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-Gaussian-polynomial>. A
    support carries a mode only when it contains all its anchors. The
    support-independent scalar correction is retained separately per cell.
    Thus no local term is assigned to a support lacking one of its marks.

    For terms meeting a flagged region, keep that region's complete source
    support and its original eligibility test. At a step <math|i+1\<to\>i> in
    stage<nbsp><math|j>, let <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>> be the integrated
    majorant of the distinguished marked factor and <math|n<rsub|\<star\>>>
    its covering number, as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. Its
    rooted forced bound is <math|v<rsub|\<star\>>*e<rsup|\<eta\><rsup|<math-up|att>><rsub|i>*n<rsub|\<star\>>>*e<rsup|-r<rsub|j>*\<beta\><rprime|'><rsub|u>*t<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>>>
    before division by the forced source allowance. Half this suppression
    pays the derivative and support factors of the finite marks; the other
    half bounds their normalized forced remainder by the error above after
    entry. The finite polynomial contractions, Taylor subtractions and
    connected attachments cost at most <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>> at each of
    the <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>> steps. Their product is the factor in
    the statement. At each step the mode-weighted coefficient norm is
    submultiplicative; the derivative and support weights remain in the
    separate integrated majorants. The rooted sums are anchored at marks, so
    their bounds have no factor counting the replicated cells.
  </proof>

  The next estimate increases the mark weight while retaining the full
  regular and forced representation.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Corrected Gaussian sources><label|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>
    Uniformly on that set, arbitrarily late intervals admit common real lifts
    of the free basis, bounded in <math|N,p> for each fixed interval and
    fixing the unit, such that the corrected source
    <math|e<rsup|-<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|z|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|h><around|(|1+z<rsub|h>*F<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|tst>>|)>>,
    carried to level<nbsp><math|k>, has a
    representation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form> there with mark weight

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|w><rsub|t>=exp <around|(|log
      <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/<around|(|8*n|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    At this weight its regular and forced source sums are each at most one,
    with scalar source bound at most one per cell. For each such interval
    there is <math|N<rsub|0>> such that the assertion holds for all
    <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and all<nbsp><math|p>.
  </lemma>

  Its proof iterates the middle-level error over the second part of the
  interval.

  <\proof>
    Starting from the error of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>
    at<nbsp><math|l>, a quadratic recurrence from the connected expansion of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> carries the bound
    to<nbsp><math|k>; the extra length <math|v<rsub|t>> of the window absorbs
    the per-step losses. Work in the submultiplicative mode-weighted
    coefficient norm defined in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-attachments>. Keep derivative and
    support weights in separate integrated majorants and retain the forced
    eligibility tests.

    We now bound the loss over all steps; their number grows
    with<nbsp><math|t>. Use<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-initial>. Increasing the
    fixed power <math|q<rsub|<math-up|der>>> includes the finite Taylor and
    rounding bounds above. The mark weight contributes at most
    <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|t><rsup|n>=\<cal-M\><rsup|1/8>> to any mode in one
    cell. Enlarge <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>> to include all per-step finite
    polynomial and support constants. From<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost> and the
    two ceilings in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window>, on sufficiently late intervals
    with fixed<nbsp><math|b>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-source-loss>\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>+2>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|<around|(|b+6|)>/j<rsup|2>>*B<rsub|t><rsup|10/j<rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|1/64>.
    </equation>

    Indeed <math|log B<rsub|t>=2*log log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>+o<around|(|1|)>>
    and <math|log \<cal-M\>=<around|(|log log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>>, so
    <math|B<rsub|t>=\<cal-M\><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. The two ceilings and
    the <math|+2> contribute at most <math|L<rsup|4/j<rsup|2>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|4/j<rsup|2>>>,
    since <math|\<ell\><rsup|<math-up|st>><rsub|J<rsub|t>>\<leqslant\><cutoffsize><rsub|t>>
    by item<nbsp>6 of Table<nbsp><reference|tab:constant-choices>; the final
    inequality holds at all sufficiently large stages for each
    fixed<nbsp><math|b>.

    We use the following one-step source estimate. For the fixed lifts of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>, let <math|S<rsub|i>>
    majorize the corrected singleton terms, and <math|P<rsub|i>> the
    corrected terms with at least two marks, in the weighted regular norm at
    mark weight<nbsp><math|<math-ss|w><rsub|t>>, including their scalar terms
    per cell. Use the same quantities for the forced norm after division by
    its positive source allowance, and take the larger values. For
    <math|k\<leqslant\>i\<less\>l>, the connected product expansion gives

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>*S<rsub|i+1>+\<cal-M\><rsup|1/2>*S<rsub|i+1><rsup|2>,>>|<row|<cell|P<rsub|i>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>*P<rsub|i+1>+\<cal-M\><rsup|1/2>*<around|(|S<rsub|i+1>+P<rsub|i+1>|)><rsup|2>.<eq-number><label|eq:E-source-step>>>>>
    </align>

    Here <math|K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>\<geqslant\>1> is fixed at the
    stage, and <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|j>\<geqslant\>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>+2>.
    To obtain these bounds, separate connected terms linear in the incoming
    corrected sources from those with at least two source factors. The former
    use the one-mark integration bound. In the latter, the product rule
    leaves a product of two source norms; summing the additional attachments
    uses the rooted clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>. Its
    finite derivative and localization losses are bounded by
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsup|1/2>> after entry. Every higher product is absorbed
    in the same quadratic bound while the source norm is at most
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsup|-1/2>>. For the forced terms the retained
    eligibility event supplies the forced suppression before its allowance is
    divided out. The entry requirement in item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries>
    includes this normalized forced bound. Disjoint cell modes use the
    multiplicative weights of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>; their
    connecting supports keep the same estimate. No factor counts the number
    of cells.

    Set <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>=S<rsub|i>+P<rsub|i>>.
    Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-step> imply
    <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|src>>*\<frak-a\><rsub|i+1>+2*\<cal-M\><rsup|1/2>*\<frak-a\><rsub|i+1><rsup|2>>.
    The first part of the interval gives <math|\<frak-a\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\><math-ss|w><rsub|t><rsup|n>\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+2>*\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-loss>, for <math|b\<geqslant\>1> and
    sufficiently late entry,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-source-iteration><math-ss|w><rsub|t><rsup|n>\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|h<rsub|t><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|t>+2>*\<cal-E\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|G>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|-3/4>,<space|2em>\<frak-a\><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>\<cal-C\><rsub|j><rsup|l-i>*\<frak-a\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<cal-M\><rsup|-3/4>\<less\>\<cal-M\><rsup|-1/2>.
    </equation>

    The second inequality follows by backward induction: below
    <math|\<cal-M\><rsup|-1/2>> the quadratic term in the recurrence is at
    most <math|2*\<frak-a\><rsub|i+1>>. For the first, the covariance
    contribution is at most <math|\<cal-M\><rsup|1/8+1/64-b>>, whose exponent
    is at most <math|-55/64>. The other contributions tend to zero faster
    than every fixed negative power of<nbsp><math|\<cal-M\>>, since <math|log
    \<cal-M\>=o<around|(|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)>> and
    <math|B<rsub|t>\<asymp\><around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>>. Thus the initial-error condition
    is compatible with the whole interval, although its length grows
    with<nbsp><math|t>.

    The extra length <math|v<rsub|t>> also provides the support margin: a
    level-<math|l> neighborhood of radius <math|B<rsub|t><rsup|2>> has
    level-<math|k> radius at most <math|B<rsub|t><rsup|2>/L<rsup|v<rsub|t>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|t><rsup|-8>>.
    It therefore fits inside the protected cell around its rounded reading
    points. The successive kernel neighborhoods add a bounded geometric sum
    in those units; their fixed margin was included in the choice of the
    comparison cell. Thus the inspection and attachment tests used in the
    iteration stay inside the stated interval. The unit has no remainder by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>.

    The support margin in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window-geometry> makes every
    replicated inspected stencil fit its own cell. Terms meeting flagged
    regions keep their original forced eligibility and the suppression used
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>; their source amplitude
    replaces only the amplitude of the distinguished factor. Thus the local
    source sums at the enlarged mark weight are at most one in both regular
    and forced norms. The constant source contribution is at most one per
    cell. These estimates prove the lemma on arbitrarily late intervals, with
    all thresholds independent of<nbsp><math|p>.
  </proof>

  The remaining normalization uses the complete positive unmarked density. In
  particular it cannot discard the forced sum or replace it by its healthy
  part.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Normalized source integral><label|lem:E-source-integral> For the
    corrected sources of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>, put
    <math|u<rsub|t>=log <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/<around|(|16*n|)>>. For each
    such interval there is <math|N<rsub|0>> such that, for all
    <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>> and all<nbsp><math|p>, for every nonempty
    mode<nbsp><math|I>,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>><around|(|\<frak-c\><rsub|I>|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>|\|>*q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>><math-ss|w><rsub|t><rsup|-<around|\||I|\|><around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>|\|>>,<space|2em>q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|t>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The bound compares the source integral with the complete positive
    unmarked density of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>, whose positivity
    follows from the density identity of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RG>,
    the positive Wilson start and the probability kernels of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B1>; the source sizes come from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>. Let <math|\<rho\>> be the
    unmarked representation at the width level<nbsp><math|k>,
    <math|V=<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k>>|\|>=<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>|\|>>,
    <math|g=\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\>|)><rsup|-1/2>>,
    <math|H<rsub|<math-up|path>>=A<rsub|J<rsub|k>><rsup|<math-up|path>>>, and
    let <math|\<delta\>,\<Lambda\><rsup|<math-up|loop>>> bound its regular
    and loop terms. The admissibility inequalities give
    <math|0\<less\>g\<leqslant\>t<rsub|k>>, <math|\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsup|<math-up|loop>>\<leqslant\>\<Lambda\><rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|loop>>>
    and <math|\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>\<leqslant\>4*<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>>.

    Choose constants <math|s<rsub|H>\<in\><around|(|0,1|]>>,
    <math|b<rsub|H>\<geqslant\>1> such that small Haar balls have measure at
    least <math|s<rsub|H>*r<rsup|<dimG>>> and the clipped one-link integrals
    are at most <math|b<rsub|H>*<around|(|g/\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|<dimG>>>,
    where <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>> is the smaller of one and the linear
    clip coefficient. These follow from the nonsingular exponential chart and
    compactness of the group, with constants chosen before all levels. Write

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-h\>=log b<rsub|H>-<dimG>log \<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>\<cal-P\>*<around|(|\<rho\>,X,g|)>=2*E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>+6+\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|base>>+X+3*\<frak-h\>-4*log
      s<rsub|H>.
    </equation*>

    Here <math|E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>> bounds the loop and regular exponent per
    site, including the exponent discrepancy <math|\<delta\>> in the clipped
    comparison, and on links of size at most <math|g/4>,
    <math|\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|base>>=3*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,J<rsub|k>>+H<rsub|<math-up|path>><rsup|2>|)>>.
    For any integrable complex<nbsp><math|F> with
    <math|z<around|\||F<around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|\<rho\>><around|(|U|)>+V*X>>,
    <math|z\<geqslant\>0>, the ratio estimate is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-positive-ratio><frac|z<around|\||<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*U|\|>|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\>*<space|0.17em>d*U>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|V*\<cal-P\>*<around|(|\<rho\>,X,g|)>-4<dimG>log
      g>.
    </equation>

    The gauge fixing cancels the small-coupling powers in this ratio. The
    denominator has <math|3*V+1> free links and the numerator will integrate
    <math|3*V-3> selected plaquette factors, so their Haar-ball powers differ
    only by <math|g<rsup|<dimG><around|(|3*V-3|)>>/g<rsup|<dimG><around|(|3*V+1|)>>=g<rsup|-4<dimG>>>,
    a cost that is not extensive in<nbsp><math|V>; all remaining constants
    are exponential in<nbsp><math|V>. For the denominator, fix an axial
    spanning tree with <math|V-1> links. Gauge invariance removes those
    links, leaving <math|3*V+1> free links. Restrict each free link to the
    ball of chord <math|g/4>. All plaquettes are then healthy, so the forced
    covering sum equals one. The lower action bound and the Haar-ball bound
    give

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\>*<space|0.17em>d*U\<geqslant\>e<rsup|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>>*V-V*<around|(|E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>+\<beta\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|base>>|)>>*<around|(|s<rsub|H>*g<rsup|<dimG>>|)><rsup|3*V+1>.
    </equation*>

    For the numerator, the clipped plaquette inequality is

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>*E<rsub|<math-up|plaq>><around|(|U|)>\<geqslant\><frac|<chord><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>|g/\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>>-1.
    </equation*>

    It follows in the quadratic branch from
    <math|<around|(|a/g-1|)><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0> and
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>min
    <around|(|1,c<rsup|<math-up|dir>>|)>>, and in the linear branch from
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>c<rsup|<math-up|lin>>> and
    <math|g> below the threshold. Summing over the six plaquettes per site
    retains the product of <math|e<rsup|-<chord><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\>>|)>/<around|(|g/\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>|)>>>
    in the upper integrand.

    Keep the <math|3*V-3> plaquette factors selected as follows and discard
    the others, whose values lie in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. The axial tree
    returns each site towards zero in its highest nonzero coordinate. Order
    free links by that coordinate, treating wrap links first in each
    coordinate, except for the four links at zero, and then transverse links
    in decreasing height. Associate to each treated link its plaquette
    towards the tree. That plaquette contains the link once; every other
    remaining selected plaquette is independent of that link in this order.
    Haar invariance therefore replaces its factor by the one-link integral.
    The four untreated links account for the difference
    <math|<around|(|3*V+1|)>-4=3*V-3>. All discarded plaquette factors lie in
    <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, including at the periodic boundary. Thus

    <\equation*>
      z<around|\||<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*U|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>>*V+V*<around|(|E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>+6+X|)>>*<around*|(|b<rsub|H>*<around|(|g/\<sigma\><rsub|\<ast\>>|)><rsup|<dimG>>|)><rsup|3*V-3>.
    </equation*>

    Divide these inequalities. The powers of <math|g> differ by
    <math|-4<dimG>>. Using <math|3*V-3\<leqslant\>3*V>,
    <math|3*V+1\<leqslant\>4*V>, <math|b<rsub|H>\<geqslant\>1> and
    <math|s<rsub|H>\<leqslant\>1> gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-positive-ratio>.

    One may use the clipped comparison exponent in this calculation even if
    it is not the exponent used for the source bound. Their difference has
    absolute value at most <math|V*\<delta\>>. Multiplying the upper source
    bound by <math|e<rsup|V*\<delta\>>> and bounding the two positive
    partition integrals in the opposite direction by the same factor accounts
    for this change. These two factors are included in
    <math|2*E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>> because each occurrence of
    <math|E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>> includes <math|\<delta\>>.
    Thus<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-positive-ratio> applies to the actual source
    integral on the original exact history.

    The enlarged-weight bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>
    give <math|X\<leqslant\>1+n>: one is the scalar bound, and each of at
    most <math|n> marks contributes at most one. The local coefficient
    estimate before integration includes <math|z=<math-ss|w><rsub|t><rsup|<around|\||I|\|>*V>>.
    The entry inequalities imply

    <\equation*>
      E<rsub|<math-up|ex>>\<leqslant\>8*H<rsub|<math-up|path>>+2,<space|2em>-4<dimG>log
      g/V\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    For the second, <math|-4<dimG>log g=2<dimG>log
    \<beta\><around|(|\<rho\>|)>> is <math|O<around|(|log
    <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>|)>>, whereas
    <math|V\<geqslant\>D<rsub|k>=e<rsup|s<rsub|k>>>. The first follows from
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>*t<rsub|k><rsup|3>\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|k>*t<rsub|k><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|loop>>*t<rsub|k><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>1>
    on sufficiently late levels. Hence choose a stage constant

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-comparison-cost>Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<geqslant\>2*<around|(|8*A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>+2|)>+6+3*<around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>+<around|(|A<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|path>>|)><rsup|2>|)>+<around|(|1+n|)>+3*\<frak-h\><rsub|j>-4*log
      s<rsub|H>+1
    </equation>

    for each arity used at stage<nbsp><math|j>. It bounds the exponent per
    cell in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-positive-ratio>. All its quantities are fixed
    before the entry to stage<nbsp><math|j>.
    Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>
    requires <math|Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>log
    <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/<around|(|16*n|)>> on the chosen intervals. This
    proves <math|q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|t>> and the
    asserted coefficient estimate. In particular the volume appears only
    through the number of cells, so the cutoff threshold is independent
    of<nbsp><math|p>.
  </proof>

  The next estimate uses the corrected source comparison in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>. Each relative error is
  interior whenever its insertions are interior,
  since<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-relative> is a polynomial in those insertions.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Relative rank comparison><label|lem:E2.10> For every such finite
    test family, there are <math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>*\<downarrow\>*0>,
    <math|y<rsup|\<nu\>,i>\<to\>y<rsup|i>>, levels
    <math|k<rsub|\<nu\>>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|\<nu\>>> tending to infinity,
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>\<geqslant\>0> tending to zero, and real
    lifts<nbsp><math|b<rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|j,N,p>> of the free
    basis<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>|)>>, bounded
    in<nbsp><math|N,p> for fixed<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> and common to all tests.
    They satisfy <math|b<rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|1,N,p>=\<b-up-u\><rsub|d>>.
    Each<nbsp><math|x<rsup|\<nu\>,i>> is generic on the
    spacing-<math|a<rsub|t<rsub|\<nu\>>>> grid. Eventually uniformly
    in<nbsp><math|p>, all test insertions are interior
    to<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k<rsub|\<nu\>>><around|(|0|)>>,
    and, for every nonempty<nbsp><math|I>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k<rsub|\<nu\>>><around|(|0|)>>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10>>
    The relative errors are the corrected
    coefficients<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-corrected-coefficients>, and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>\U<reference|lem:E-source-integral>,
    with the free correlations of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11>, bound
    their seminorms by a negative power of the cutoff size. Apply those
    lemmas with arity bound <math|n=2>, so
    <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|t>=<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|1/16>=e<rsup|2*u<rsub|t>>>
    and <math|u<rsub|t>=log <cutoffsize><rsub|t>/32>. The normalized source
    lemma gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-rank-input><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|C\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>><around|(|\<frak-c\><rsub|I>|)><rsub|C>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>|\|>*q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>|}><math-ss|w><rsub|t><rsup|-<around|\||I|\|><around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>|\|>>,<space|2em>q<rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>u<rsub|t>.
    </equation>

    The cutoff threshold is independent of<nbsp><math|p>.

    Taking the <math|<around|\||\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>|\|>>-th root
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-rank-input> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<frak-c\><rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|u<rsub|t>-2<around|\||I|\|>*u<rsub|t>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-u<rsub|t>>=<around|(|<cutoffsize><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1/32>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|e<rsup|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>> has coefficient
    <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|J>> at <math|z<rsub|J>>, multiplication by
    <math|e<rsup|<math-ss|C><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>> shows that
    <math|\<frak-c\><rsub|I>> is exactly <math|R<rsub|I>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-relative>. Choose successive comparison windows with
    both levels increasing, reading radii strictly decreasing to zero, and
    <math|e<rsup|-u<rsub|t>>> decreasing to zero. The windows of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> allow these finitely many requests at
    each step. Call the resulting levels <math|k<rsub|\<nu\>>\<leqslant\>t<rsub|\<nu\>>>,
    radii <math|r<rsub|\<nu\>>> and errors
    <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>=e<rsup|-u<rsub|t<rsub|\<nu\>>>>>. Rounding
    on the finer grid preserves genericity for each fixed<nbsp><math|\<nu\>>
    once that grid is fine enough. Interior insertion supports imply
    interiority of every <math|R<rsub|I>>, by its polynomial formula.
  </render-proof>

  <subsubsection|Bounds for lattice differences><label|sec:partII-E.2.6>

  Positional differences are a separate problem. A difference quotient
  multiplies the insertion by the inverse spacing,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-inverse-spacing><frac|O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y+e<rsub|a>|)>-O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y|)>|a<rsub|N>>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>*<around*|(|P<rsub|d><around|[|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]>*<around|(|y+e<rsub|a>|)>-P<rsub|d><around|[|t<rsub|N><rsup|-<factorcount>><math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d><rsup|-1>c|]><around|(|y|)>|)>,
  </equation>

  so Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>, applied to the two terms on the left,
  bounds its seminorm only by <math|2*A*a<rsub|m><rsup|-d-\<varepsilon\>>/a<rsub|N>>,
  which diverges as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>. The remedy has three steps.
  Local matching represents the difference quotient as a
  degree-<math|<around|(|d+1|)>> insertion up to a negligible error
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.9>); the factor
  <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-<around|(|d+1|)>>> becomes the normalization of the
  new insertion, but its coefficient is only tame, not bounded. The equation
  projection removes the directions of that coefficient whose insertions
  vanish in separated correlations (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>\U<reference|lem:E2.7>).
  The remaining coefficient lies in a space of dimension at most
  <math|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d+1>> and is bounded by testing it against
  the finite free matrix (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10>).

  The tests of the difference must be bounded without any bound on
  differentiated insertions. Averaging the difference quotient over
  <math|Q<rsub|N>\<approx\>h<rsub|<math-up|av>>/a<rsub|N>> consecutive
  translates telescopes to a difference over the fixed physical distance
  <math|Q<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>>, which is bounded by undifferentiated insertions
  alone; this is why the argument is not circular. Comparing averaged tests
  at nearby positions uses the following displacement estimate, which also
  involves only undifferentiated insertions. For a grid
  configuration<nbsp><math|x> define its minimum coordinate separation on the
  physical torus by

  <\equation*>
    g<rsub|p><around|(|x|)>=min<rsub|i\<ne\>j> min<rsub|a=1,\<ldots\>,4>
    min<rsub|z\<in\>L<rsub|p><Z>><around|\||x<rsub|i><rsup|a>-x<rsub|j><rsup|a>+z|\|>,
  </equation*>

  with value <math|+\<infty\>> for one slot. This separation supplies the
  empty slabs for reflection positivity. A coordinate hybrid of <math|x,y>
  chooses each coordinate from either configuration. Assume the insertions
  and their single-axis reflections are interior at a centered
  level<nbsp><math|D\<leqslant\>N>, with all their seminorms at
  most<nbsp><math|A\<geqslant\>0>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Displacement of correlation tests><label|lem:E-displacement> For
    every <math|r\<gtr\>0> with <math|a<rsub|D>\<less\>r>, a one-coordinate
    grid move <math|x\<mapsto\>y> of size at most<nbsp><math|r> from
    <math|g<rsub|p><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>4*r> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>-M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y/a<rsub|N>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>A<rsup|n>*<around|\||x-y|\|>/r.
    </equation*>

    If <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<leqslant\>r> and every coordinate hybrid has
    coordinate separation at least<nbsp><math|4*r>, then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>-M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y/a<rsub|N>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>4*n*A<rsup|n>*<around|\||x-y|\|>/r.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    A move changes the lengths of the two empty slabs next to the moved
    insertion, and the spectral theorem for the transfer operator bounds the
    effect of that change; the reflection inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>
    bounds what remains. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> supplies the assumed
    seminorms for bounded coefficient lists on full cutoff tails. The
    separation hypothesis gives every directed coordinate distance at
    least<nbsp><math|4*r>, at any period. No fixed test neighbourhood is
    used.

    Fix an axis and all other coordinates. Reflection positivity and
    translation invariance give a positive transfer operator, rescaled to
    <math|0\<leqslant\>T\<leqslant\>1>. On the periodic axis the expectation
    is a normalized trace. If <math|X> denotes the insertion being moved, its
    two adjacent empty slabs have lengths
    <math|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>,n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>> in fine
    sites. A move by <math|l\<geqslant\>0> changes their factor by

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>+l>*X*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>-l>-T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>*X*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>>|<cell|=T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>*<around|(|T<rsup|l>*X-X*T<rsup|l>|)>*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>-l>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=-T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>*<around|(|1-T<rsup|l>|)>*X*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>-l>+T<rsup|n<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|sl>>>*X*<around|(|1-T<rsup|l>|)>*T<rsup|n<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|sl>>-l>.<eq-number><label|eq:E-trace-displacement>>>>>
    </align>

    Both expressions are inserted in the same remaining cyclic product and
    divided by the same partition trace. The stencil is contained in a
    centered cell of lattice side <math|d<rsub|<math-up|cell>>=a<rsub|D>/a<rsub|N>>.
    Since <math|a<rsub|D>\<less\>r>, the directed separations exceed
    <math|2*d<rsub|<math-up|cell>>+2*r/a<rsub|N>>, and
    <math|l*a<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>r> by assumption. Splitting the two
    surrounding empty slabs at their reflection planes leaves
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|sl>>+1\<geqslant\>2*r/a<rsub|N>> fine sites next to
    each occurrence of <math|1-T<rsup|l>>. The spectral theorem gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|1-T<rsup|l>|)>*T<rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|sl>>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>sup<rsub|0\<leqslant\>t\<leqslant\>1>
      l*<around|(|1-t|)>*t<rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|sl>>>\<leqslant\>l/<around|(|n<rsub|<math-up|sl>>+1|)>\<leqslant\>l*a<rsub|N>/<around|(|2*r|)>.
    </equation*>

    For each normalized trace in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-trace-displacement>,
    reflection Schwarz at the slab ends and the chessboard inequality bound
    the remaining factors by the product of their centered seminorms, at
    most<nbsp><math|A<rsup|n>>. One applies this inequality to the complete
    cyclic product, so no supremum norm of<nbsp><math|X> is required. The two
    terms together are bounded by <math|A<rsup|n>*l*a<rsub|N>/r>. An
    arity-one moment is translation invariant and has change zero.

    For a negative move reverse the chosen axis. Coordinate separation is
    unchanged and the assumed reflected seminorms have the same bound, so the
    same slab estimate applies. Replace the <math|4*n> coordinates one at a
    time. Each starting configuration is a coordinate hybrid and hence has
    the required separation. Each move is at most
    <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<leqslant\>r>. Summing proves the second bound.
    The constants depend only on the seminorms, arity and slab margin,
    uniformly in<nbsp><math|N,p>.
  </proof>

  All the preceding comparison lemmas hold on full cutoff tails, uniformly
  in<nbsp><math|p>. Local matching also applies to any fixed iterated
  difference: after multiplying by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|d+h>>, a difference
  of order<nbsp><math|h> is a finite sum of translates of the degree-<math|d>
  section, with subpower derivative bounds. Matching in degree <math|d+h>
  leaves a seminorm error tending to zero at every fixed cell size, uniformly
  in<nbsp><math|p>. This finite-cutoff argument supplies the difference
  bounds required in Part<nbsp>I.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Difference estimate><label|lem:E2.4> For every arity <math|n>,
    insertion-label list <math|v>, difference list <math|\<b-l\>> and
    <math|r\<gtr\>0>, the constants in Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB>
    can be chosen independently of<nbsp><math|p>:

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|p> sup<rsub|<math-up|sep><rsub|<math-up|tor>><around|(|y|)>\<geqslant\>r><around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B*<around|(|n,v,\<b-l\>,r|)>*<space|2em><around|(|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>*<around|(|n,v,\<b-l\>,r|)>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We first bound the chessboard seminorm of one differentiated insertion at
    a fixed fine cell size, uniformly in <math|N,p> (Steps<nbsp>1\U4), and
    then apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> (Step<nbsp>5). Let
    <math|F=O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|c|)>>, let
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-a\>>*F> be a fixed iterated normalized
    difference in its anchor, and put <math|d<rprime|'>=d+<around|\||\<b-a\>|\|>>.

    <em|Step 1: Matching and the equation part.> Local matching gives tame
    coefficients <math|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>> in degree <math|d<rprime|'>> with
    <math|O<rsup|<around|(|d<rprime|'>|)>><around|(|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>|)>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-a\>>*F>
    of vanishing seminorm. Write <math|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>=\<Pi\><rsub|d<rprime|'>,N,p>*c<rsup|\<b-a\>>>.
    For a tame coefficient in <math|ker \<Pi\><rsub|d<rprime|'>>>, the
    auxiliary equation section has seminorm zero. To see this, take all the
    other reflected copies as the spectator in the exact Haar identity in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A-R4>. For a fixed physical cell, its side in
    lattice units eventually exceeds twice the equation radius plus two; all
    those copies are then exterior. The section-comparison residual of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.7> tends to zero in seminorm. In particular
    it applies to <math|z=<around|(|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>-c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>.
    Indeed, <math|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
    is tame, and <math|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
    has norm at most one. Every bounded coefficient family is tame, because
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d<rprime|'>><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|d<rprime|'>>*<big|prod><rsub|i=<reflevel|d<rprime|'>>><rsup|N-1>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>=a<rsub|N><rsup|-o<around|(|1|)>>
    </equation*>

    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>. Subtracting these two tame families proves
    tameness of<nbsp><math|z> before<nbsp><math|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>> is
    bounded.

    <em|Step 2: The averaged tests are bounded.> It remains to
    bound<nbsp><math|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>>. Use a fixed sufficiently
    short-distance element of the comparison sequence of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10> for degree<nbsp><math|d<rprime|'>>. Let
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> be the minimum coordinate separation of
    its configuration on the physical torus. Fix a slab margin
    <math|0\<less\>b\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|0>/8> and a centered
    level<nbsp><math|D> with <math|a<rsub|D>\<less\>b>. Average the variable
    slot of every test over <math|Q<rsub|N>=<around|\<lceil\>|h<rsub|<math-up|av>>/a<rsub|N>|\<rceil\>>>
    sites in each direction occurring in<nbsp><math|\<b-a\>>, where
    <math|0\<less\>h<rsub|<math-up|av>>\<less\>b/<around|[|2*<around|(|<around|\||\<b-a\>|\|>+1|)>|]>>.
    Eventually <math|Q<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>2*h<rsub|<math-up|av>>>,
    so the total shift in each coordinate is less than<nbsp><math|b> and
    every coordinate hybrid used in the averaging has coordinate separation
    at least<nbsp><math|4*b>. Denote this linear map on coefficients by
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,p>>. Telescoping in one direction gives

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|Q<rsub|N>>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|Q<rsub|N>-1><frac|f*<around|(|z+<around|(|j+1|)>*e<rsub|a>|)>-f*<around|(|z+j*e<rsub|a>|)>|a<rsub|N>>=<frac|f*<around|(|z+Q<rsub|N>*e<rsub|a>|)>-f<around|(|z|)>|Q<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>>.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|Q<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>\<geqslant\>h<rsub|<math-up|av>>>,
    iterating bounds the tests of<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-a\>>*F> by
    <math|<around|(|2/h<rsub|<math-up|av>>|)><rsup|<around|\||\<b-a\>|\|>>>
    times the supremum of tests of<nbsp><math|F>. The latter is bounded by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>, the interior-seminorm comparison
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>. Matching errors tend to zero in these tests:
    put the error and the bounded spectator insertions in distinct cells with
    fixed margins, use Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> for their interior
    seminorms, and apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>. All these bounds hold on
    full cutoff tails and use no positional difference estimate. Thus
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,p>*c<rsup|\<b-a\>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|test>>>
    eventually, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>.

    <em|Step 3: Invertibility of the tests.> The finite-dimensional lower
    bound is obtained before any cutoff limit. The free matrix has a positive
    lower bound on the unit sphere. The relative-error estimate and the
    equation-seminorm bound put the tests of the projected lifts within
    <math|O<around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>|)>+o<rsub|N\<to\>\<infty\>><around|(|1|)>>
    of the free matrix, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>. Choose the
    fixed<nbsp><math|\<nu\>> with this error smaller than its matrix lower
    bound. Averaging over sufficiently small<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|av>>>
    preserves the lower bound: the undifferentiated correlation tests have
    uniform positional continuity by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement>,
    with margin<nbsp><math|b>, the centered level<nbsp><math|D> and the
    coordinate-hybrid separations just chosen. The level and its seminorm
    bounds are fixed before reducing<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|av>>>, so the
    displacement bound tends to zero with<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|av>>>.
    The projected lifts are therefore linearly independent. Their number
    equals <math|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d<rprime|'>>>, whereas
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6> bounds <math|dim
    <math-up|ran><space|0.17em>\<Pi\><rsub|d<rprime|'>>> by that number. They
    consequently span the range. Their uniform coefficient bounds give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-detection><around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|det>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,p>*c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>
    </equation>

    with <math|C<rsub|<math-up|det>>> independent of<nbsp><math|N,p> on the
    retained tails. Choose an invertible square minor <math|H<rsub|0>> of the
    free matrix and the corresponding interacting minor<nbsp><math|H>. For
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H-H<rsub|0>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|2<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|0><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>>,
    the inverse bound <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|0><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>>>
    follows.

    <em|Step 4: The bound on <math|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>>.> The equation
    coefficient <math|z=<around|(|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>-c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
    is tame. Its tests tend to zero, so eventually
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|det>>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,p>*z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1/2>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-detection> yields

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|<math-up|det>>*C<rsub|<math-up|test>>+<tfrac|1|2><around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*C<rsub|<math-up|det>>*C<rsub|<math-up|test>>+1.
    </equation*>

    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> now bounds the seminorms
    of<nbsp><math|O<rsup|<around|(|d<rprime|'>|)>><around|(|c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>|)>>
    at each fixed sufficiently fine scale. Now
    <math|c<rsup|\<b-a\>>-c<rsup|<math-up|pr>>> is tame, so its equation
    residual has vanishing seminorm. Together with the matching residual, the
    triangle inequality for seminorms gives the same type of bound
    for<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-a\>>*F>. All thresholds and constants
    in this argument are independent of<nbsp><math|p>.

    <em|Step 5: Products at separated points.> Distribute the differences in
    <math|\<b-l\>> among their indicated slots. A difference in
    slot<nbsp><math|i> acts only on that insertion, so the resulting moment
    is exactly the expectation of the product of these differentiated
    insertions. Choose a fixed tiling side smaller than<nbsp><math|r> and
    translate its boundaries away from all anchors. In each axis the excluded
    boundary strips occupy less than one full cell length if their widths are
    chosen below <math|a<rsub|m>/<around|[|4*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>>. For
    sufficiently large<nbsp><math|N> a lattice translation with these margins
    exists. Distinct anchors occupy distinct cells, since their torus
    separation exceeds the cell side. The interior-seminorm comparison, with
    a finer centered scale, bounds all the factors uniformly.
    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> and put one in every unused cell. Their
    product gives the asserted<nbsp><math|B>. Empty difference lists and
    arities at most one obey the same argument. This proves the lemma and
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB>.
  </proof>

  <subsubsection|Bounded approximations><label|sec:partII-E.2.7>

  The later phase argument needs bounded approximations with a bound
  independent of the cutoff. Compactness at each cutoff gives no such bound.
  We obtain it by stopping at one fixed coarse lattice and pulling bounded
  observables back through the probability block kernels.

  In the next two lemmas, a statement holds eventually in the ordered limits
  when it holds for all sufficiently large indices of the chosen volume
  sequence and, at each such volume, all sufficiently fine cutoffs of the
  chosen spacing sequence. No common cutoff threshold in other subsequently
  fixed parameters is asserted. The chessboard seminorms used below are
  defined in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Bounded coarse approximations><label|lem:E-coarse-bounded> For
    every <math|d>, <math|k\<geqslant\><reflevel|d>>, real
    <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>> and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, there are
    measurable real observables <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|c,y|)>>
    and <math|M<rsub|<math-up|bnd>>\<less\>\<infty\>>, independent of
    <math|N,p,y>, with <math|<around|\||\<cal-B\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|c,y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|<math-up|bnd>>>
    and

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|c,y|)>-O<rsup|<around|(|d|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c,y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k><around|(|y|)>>\<leqslant\>\<delta\>
    </equation*>

    eventually in the ordered limits. Both observables are interior to that
    cell, and the same holds for their signed and reflected images.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We stop at a fixed coarse level<nbsp><math|h>, replace the insertion by a
    bounded coarse polynomial pulled back through the probability block
    kernels (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D04>), and bound the discrepancy
    by replicating it in every cell (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>);
    the marked step of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> matches the
    coefficients. The construction is independent of the phase decomposition.
    Choose a fixed <math|m\<geqslant\>k> with
    <math|B<rsub|m><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|m>>, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. At a finer level <math|h\<geqslant\>m>,
    the bounded coarse polynomial with coordinate<nbsp><math|v> is

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|V><rsub|h><around|(|v;U|)>=a<rsub|h><rsup|-d>*<around*|(|v<rsub|0>+\<b-1\><rsub|\<Sigma\><rsub|d><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*P<rsub|d>*<around|[|t<rsub|h><rsup|-<factorcount>>*v<rsup|\<circ\>>|]><around|(|U|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Its supremum for each basis input is bounded by a constant depending
    on<nbsp><math|d,h>, uniformly in<nbsp><math|N,p>: the healthy polynomial
    uses a fixed compact coordinate range, and the unit term is constant. Its
    inspected stencil has a fixed radius, including the links needed for the
    relaxed threshold test.

    Let <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|N,h>*<around|(|U,d*V|)>> be the probability
    kernel obtained by composing the block kernels from <math|N>
    to<nbsp><math|h>, and set

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h>*F|)><around|(|U|)>=<big|int>F<around|(|V|)>*<space|0.17em>\<cal-K\><rsub|N,h>*<around|(|U,d*V|)>.
    </equation*>

    It preserves real values and does not increase the supremum norm. The
    locality of the block kernels confines its dependence to the fine
    preimage of the inspected stencil. For reflected cells these ancestor
    cones are disjoint once <math|h> has the stated margin. At each
    intermediate level the block kernels are products in the coarse-link
    variables given the preceding field; integrating disjoint ancestor cones
    therefore gives conditional independence, and hence
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|C>\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h>*F<rsub|C>=\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h><around|(|<big|prod><rsub|C>F<rsub|C>|)>>.
    Choose <math|h> sufficiently large that this preimage lies strictly
    inside the centered level-<math|k> cell, including its boundary-link
    margin.

    To compare with the actual insertion, choose auxiliary fine coefficients
    <math|u<rsub|j>> whose selected level-<math|h> polynomial coordinates
    differ by at most <math|1/4> from the basis vectors. They are obtained by
    carrying the pure coarse polynomial back through the inverse graph
    construction in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>.
    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-match-cone> to the difference with this primary
    coarse polynomial. Write <math|S<rsub|h>=C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|h>>>,
    and let <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|h>> be the matrix of the selected
    level-<math|h> coordinates of these trials. For a basis input, the cone
    and the finest-level boundary estimate give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-coarse-inversion>32*<around|(|1+S<rsub|h>|)><rsup|2>*<around|\||e<rsub|j>-\<cal-J\><rsub|h>*e<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|h>,<space|2em>R<rsub|h>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+S<rsub|h>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||e<rsub|j>-\<cal-J\><rsub|h>*e<rsub|j>|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\><frac|1|16*<around|(|1+S<rsub|h>|)>>\<leqslant\><frac|1|4>.
    </equation>

    Taking the maximum over columns gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-J\><rsub|h>-1|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>1/4>,
    which implies <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-J\><rsub|h><rsup|-1>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>2>.
    If <math|c> is the required reference coefficient, linear recombination
    therefore gives

    <\equation*>
      O<around|(|c|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j>t<rsub|j>*O<around|(|<math-ss|Z><rsub|N,p,d>u<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|j><around|\||t<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>h>log
      C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>><around|\||c|\|><rsub|1>.
    </equation*>

    Replace each term on the right by <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h><math-ss|V><rsub|h><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>>.
    This defines a bounded complex observable
    <math|\<cal-B\><rprime|'><rsub|N,p>>; its bound depends on <math|h,d,c>,
    and hence may depend on <math|\<delta\>>, but is independent
    of<nbsp><math|N,p>.

    The discrepancy is controlled by replicating all its reflected copies
    before taking the marginal kernel. Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-multiple-density>
    applies to the fine insertions; at level<nbsp><math|h> multiply the
    source density by the corresponding coarse factors. The conditional
    factorization above identifies the coefficient at each mark with
    <math|\<cal-P\><rsub|N,h><math-ss|V><rsub|h><around|(|e<rsub|j>|)>-O<around|(|<math-ss|Z>u<rsub|j>|)>>.
    The probability kernel supplies no additional supremum factor. Weighted
    coefficient extraction and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-ratio> then give,
    after recombination,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-bounded-error><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-B\><rprime|'><rsub|N,p>-O<around|(|c|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|k,m,d,c>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>h>log
      C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>>*<around|(|1+C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|h>>|)>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|h>-s<rsub|m>|)>/8>.
    </equation>

    The prefactor has subpower growth in<nbsp><math|a<rsub|h><rsup|-1>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>; the last factor tends to zero. Choose
    <math|h> so that this expression is at most<nbsp><math|\<delta\>>, before
    choosing the cutoff. This also meets the stencil-fit condition.

    For real<nbsp><math|c>, the original insertion is real by
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:A1> and<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>. Set
    <math|\<cal-B\>=<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<cal-B\><rprime|'>>. In the
    centered cell,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\>-O<around|(|c|)>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|\<cal-B\><rprime|'>-O<around|(|c|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><wide|\<cal-B\><rprime|'>-O<around|(|c|)>|\<bar\>>.
    </equation*>

    The chessboard seminorm and its invariance under conjugation show that
    this does not increase<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-bounded-error>; the supremum
    bound also does not increase. Translation, signed permutation and
    conjugation use the same finite collection of stencils and bounds. This
    proves the lemma on the stated ordered tails.
  </proof>

  Fix a degree bound<nbsp><math|d>, a lower level<nbsp><math|m> and an
  ordered extraction. A real grid word is a list
  <math|w=<around|(|<around|(|d<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>|)>,x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>>
  with <math|d<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>d>, <math|<wide|c|\<bar\>><rsub|i>=c<rsub|i>>
  and distinct spacing-<math|a<rsub|k>> positions. We assume its
  configuration is generic. Let <math|\<vartheta\>> denote linear time
  reflection on positions and labels, without complex conjugation of scalar
  coefficients. For an ordered word <math|w>, write <math|w<rsup|\<ast\>>>
  for conjugation and reversal of order, and
  <math|\<theta\>*w=\<vartheta\>*w<rsup|\<ast\>>> for the antilinear
  reflection of Osterwalder and Schrader. Put

  <\equation*>
    F<rsub|N,p><around|(|w|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i>O<rsup|<around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>/a<rsub|N>|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>=T*j*a<rsub|k>*<around|(|1,1,1,1|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    f<rsub|j,N,p,T>=F<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>>*w|)>-F<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>>*\<vartheta\>*w|)>.
  </equation*>

  These are the real word differences used in the off-diagonal variance
  estimate in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-H>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Bounded real word approximations><label|lem:E2-bounded-real> There
    is <math|k\<geqslant\>m>, chosen before<nbsp><math|w>, such that every
    such word has, for each <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>, measurable real
    approximations <math|g<rsub|j,N,p,T>> and
    <math|R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<less\>\<infty\>> with
    <math|<around|\||g<rsub|j,N,p,T>|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>
    for all <math|N,p,T,j>. For each <math|i\<less\>j> and every sufficiently
    large integer<nbsp><math|T>, eventually in the ordered limits,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<around|\<langle\>|g<rsub|i,N,p,T>*g<rsub|j,N,p,T>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>-<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i,N,p,T>*f<rsub|j,N,p,T>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Each insertion of the word is replaced by its bounded approximation from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-coarse-bounded>; the chessboard
    inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>, with the seminorm bounds of
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> and<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>, controls
    the replacement error by seminorms alone. Choose <math|k\<geqslant\>max
    <around|(|m,max<rsub|e\<leqslant\>d><reflevel|e>|)>>. It depends only
    on<nbsp><math|d,m>, before the length, labels and positions
    of<nbsp><math|w>. All its finer comparison levels remain available by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>.

    Include every label in<nbsp><math|w> and its signed and conjugated images
    in a finite list. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> gives a bound <math|C>
    for their centered seminorms at any one fixed sufficiently fine level.
    Choose that level so that its cells and the margins required by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> fit between distinct
    spacing-<math|a<rsub|k>> points. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-coarse-bounded> with
    error<nbsp><math|\<delta\>> at a still finer centered level, preserving
    these margins. Denote its real bounded approximations
    by<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>>. The same family, translated to any
    anchor, is used for every copy of the word. Its seminorms are at most
    <math|C+\<delta\>>, independently of its possibly much larger supremum
    bound.

    For a product of <math|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>> such insertions at
    distinct grid sites, telescope the products, replacing one factor at a
    time. Put all <math|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>> factors into distinct cells
    with the chosen margins. The interior-seminorm comparison
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-word-error><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>>\<cal-B\><rsub|a>-<big|prod><rsub|a=1><rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>>O<rsub|a>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>n<rsub|<math-up|word>>*\<delta\>*<around|(|C+\<delta\>|)><rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>-1>.
    </equation>

    Only the seminorms, not the supremum bounds
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\><rsub|a>>, occur on the right. For each fixed
    finite configuration, sufficiently large periods avoid wrapping
    identifications; its stencils then lie in the prescribed cells on the
    inner cutoff tail. This explains the order of the two eventualities.

    Let <math|G<rsub|j,N,p,T>> be the product of the <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|i>>
    for the translated word, and let <math|G<rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|j,N,p,T>>
    be the corresponding product for its reflected word. Set
    <math|g<rsub|j>=G<rsub|j>-G<rsup|\<theta\>><rsub|j>>. Choose
    <math|M<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bnd>>> to bound both the original and
    reflected approximations in slot<nbsp><math|i>. Then

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||g<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*<big|prod><rsub|i>M<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|bnd>>=:R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,
    </equation*>

    independently of<nbsp><math|N,p,T,j>. Every<nbsp><math|g<rsub|j>> is real
    and measurable. Set <math|g<rsub|j>=0> outside the ordered tails on which
    the coarse approximations are defined, so this supremum bound holds for
    all <math|N,p,T,j>. For fixed <math|i\<less\>j>, sufficiently large
    <math|T> separates the two translated finite lists, for each of the four
    combinations of a word and a reflected word. Indeed their first
    coordinates differ by <math|T*<around|(|j-i|)>*a<rsub|k>> plus a fixed
    finite difference. Each list is itself distinct because the original word
    is generic and reflection is injective.

    Expand <math|g<rsub|i>*g<rsub|j>> and <math|f<rsub|i>*f<rsub|j>> into
    their four product terms and apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-word-error> with
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|word>>=2*n>. Choose
    <math|0\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1> so that
    <math|4*<around|(|2*n|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|C+1|)><rsup|2*n-1>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>.
    Taking real parts cannot increase the error. Intersecting the four
    eventual sets proves the assertion. The empty word has zero difference
    and may be assigned <math|g<rsub|j>=0>. There is no common cutoff
    threshold in <math|T,i,j>; a finite list of pairs is handled by
    intersecting its eventual sets. The choice gives such a grid level above
    every prescribed lower level<nbsp><math|m>, which is the cofinality
    required in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-H>.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Full ultraviolet convergence><label|sec:partII-E.3>

  We prove convergence as the ultraviolet cutoff increases, at fixed volume.
  The argument is a triangle inequality through a common fixed
  level<nbsp><math|m>. At a separated configuration<nbsp><math|x> on a fixed
  common grid,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-three-term><around|\||M<rsub|N,p>-M<rsub|N<rprime|'>,p>|\|>\<leqslant\><wide*|<around|\||M<rsub|N,p>-C<rsub|N,p,m>|\|>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|<text|stopping
    error>>+<wide*|<around|\||C<rsub|N,p,m>-C<rsub|N<rprime|'>,p,m>|\|>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|<text|fixed-level
    difference>>+<wide*|<around|\||C<rsub|N<rprime|'>,p,m>-M<rsub|N<rprime|'>,p>|\|>|\<wide-underbrace\>><rsub|<text|stopping
    error>>,
  </equation>

  where <math|M<rsub|N,p>=M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>> is the
  cutoff moment and <math|C<rsub|N,p,m>=C<rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|v;x|)>> is
  its approximation computed at level<nbsp><math|m> from the unmarked
  representation and the one-mark families<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-coarse-integral>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-stopping> bounds each stopping error by
  <math|2*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>>, with <math|F<rsub|0>> independent
  of<nbsp><math|m>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level> shows that the
  fixed-level difference tends to zero as <math|N,N<rprime|'>\<to\>\<infty\>>
  at fixed<nbsp><math|m>. So choose <math|m> to make both stopping errors
  small, hold it fixed, and then send both cutoffs to infinity.

  Three separate facts make the middle term converge. The unmarked
  coordinates converge at each fixed level, by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fixed-level>. The singleton marked graphs
  converge because the marked step halves graph distances
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>), which
  gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-cross-cutoff>. The denominator stays bounded away
  from zero, by restriction to small links in an axial gauge. The vacuum
  scalar cancels between numerator and denominator. Fix<nbsp><math|p>. The
  normalized density at a fixed level is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-normalized-density><frac|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>>=<frac|e<rsup|-<pathaction><rsub|N,m>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|N,p,m>|<big|int>e<rsup|-<pathaction><rsub|N,m>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<b-1\><rsub|X<rsub|i><space|0.17em><math-up|healthy>>*E<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|reg>>>*\<cal-F\><rsub|N,p,m>>.
  </equation>

  Here <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|N,p,m>> is the forced sum of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D06>. The factor
  <math|e<rsup|v<rsub|<math-up|vac>,m><around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,m>>|\|>>>
  has canceled, so no convergence of that factor is proved or used.

  For a fixed finite insertion-label list and a separated common-grid
  configuration<nbsp><math|x>, let <math|o<rsub|i,m>=x<rsub|i>/a<rsub|m>> be
  its sites at all sufficiently fine levels. They are read modulo the period;
  their parents agree at consecutive levels.
  In<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>, form <math|\<cal-H\><rsup|0><rsub|N,p,m>>
  by setting <math|H<rsub|m,X>=K<rsup|\<geqslant\>2><rsub|m,Y>=0>, keeping
  the complete singleton functions <math|f<rsub|i,m>>
  and<nbsp><math|k<rsub|i,m,Y>>. Define

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:E-coarse-integral>C<rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|v;x|)>=<frac|<big|int><around|[|z<rsub|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>|]>*\<cal-H\><rsup|0><rsub|N,p,m><around|(|z;U|)>*<space|0.17em>d*U|<big|int><density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N,p,m>*<space|0.17em>d*U>.
  </equation>

  The numerator is specified by the unmarked representation and the one-mark
  families alone. The difference from the exact source is the stopping error,
  estimated in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-stopping>.

  The first lemma treats one fixed coarse level. The unmarked trajectories
  converge at that level; contraction of the marked recursion then gives
  convergence of the singleton insertions.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Fixed-level marked convergence><label|lem:E-fixed-level> For every
    fixed <math|p,v,x> as above and every sufficiently fine fixed
    level<nbsp><math|m>, the scalar-free unmarked density and the complete
    singleton functions in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form> are Cauchy in their
    common function spaces as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>. Their normalized
    integrals satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-coarse-cauchy>C<rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|v;x|)><text| is
      Cauchy as >N\<to\>\<infty\>.
    </equation>

    The scalar-free partition integral is bounded below by a positive
    constant depending on<nbsp><math|p,m>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The three facts listed at the start of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.3> are proved in turn.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-fixed-level> gives full-sequence convergence
    of the scalar couplings and scalar-free activities in fixed weighted
    spaces at each fixed<nbsp><math|p,m>, and includes the prescribed paths
    with the strict reserve. Thus the cutoff-dependent path arrays converge
    in the same charts. The vacuum scalar is excluded, as
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-normalized-density>.

    Use <math|\<kappa\><rsub|i>=<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>
    in the remainder norm<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-mass>. For coefficient
    graphs <math|g,<wide|g|~>> at level<nbsp><math|i> put

    <\equation*>
      <math-up|dist><rsup|<math-up|gr>><rsub|i><around|(|g,<wide|g|~>|)>=sup<rsub|\<b-up-x\>\<ne\>0>
      <frac|<around|\<\|\|\>|g<around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>-<wide|g|~><around|(|\<b-up-x\>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|rem>>|C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i>><around|\||\<b-up-x\>|\|><rsub|1>>,
    </equation*>

    and let <math|<math-up|dist><rsup|<math-up|gr>><rsub|i><around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>>
    be this distance between the graphs selected at cutoffs
    <math|N,N<rprime|'>>, after the fixed-level identification of their
    unmarked coordinates. The graph bound in the definition of a coefficient
    graph gives <math|<math-up|dist><rsup|<math-up|gr>><rsub|i>\<leqslant\>2>;
    by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>(iii), one step on a common
    unmarked representation halves it. For two different cutoffs, first
    compare both steps with a step on their limiting unmarked coordinates.
    The one-mark local integrals, their Taylor coefficients and the forced
    regrouping are continuous in these coordinates. To check this continuity,
    retain the common summable majorants in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates>;
    each finite local integral is continuous in its coefficients and chart,
    and the remaining tails are bounded by these same summable majorants. The
    inverse coefficient maps are continuous because their inverses have the
    common bound already proved. Hence for each fixed <math|i> the extra
    discrepancy <math|\<delta\><rsub|i>*<around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>> tends to
    zero as both cutoffs tend to infinity.

    Iterating from a remote fixed level<nbsp><math|h> down to<nbsp><math|m>
    gives the estimate

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-cross-cutoff><math-up|dist><rsup|<math-up|gr>><rsub|m><around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|1-<around|(|h-m|)>>+<big|sum><rsub|i=m><rsup|h-1>2<rsup|-<around|(|i-m|)>>*\<delta\><rsub|i>*<around|(|N,N<rprime|'>|)>.
    </equation>

    First choose <math|h-m> large enough to make the geometric term small.
    Keeping this <math|h> fixed, let <math|N,N<rprime|'>\<to\>\<infty\>>; the
    finite sum of discrepancies then tends to zero. This proves the Cauchy
    property at level<nbsp><math|m>. The finitely many coefficient maps
    between the reference level and<nbsp><math|m> converge as well. Solving
    their fixed-dimensional linear systems therefore gives Cauchy polynomial
    coordinates for each fixed reference label. Substituting in the graph
    gives the complete singleton functions.

    On the fixed finite torus, weighted convergence bounds the sums of the
    regular terms uniformly and bounds the forced terms after grouping
    identical supports and inventories. There are only finitely many such
    supports and inventories. The resulting scalar-free density and the
    singleton functions are uniformly Cauchy on their fixed health domains.
    The health threshold at level<nbsp><math|m> is fixed; no moving indicator
    is being differentiated or replaced. Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>,
    with its higher modes set to zero, is a finite polynomial in the grouped
    functions and their exponentials. Its scalar-free coefficients are
    consequently uniformly Cauchy and integrable against Haar probability
    measure.

    The denominator has a common positive lower bound. Restrict to an axial
    tree gauge with all free links of chord at most <math|g/4>, choosing
    <math|g\<gtr\>0> below every admitted
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,m><rsup|-1/2>> on the retained fixed-level tail.
    The forced sum on this set is one, and the scalar-free action is
    uniformly bounded above there. Its product Haar measure is positive. The
    same lower bound applies to all cutoffs on that tail. Dividing the Cauchy
    numerator integrals by these positive Cauchy denominators
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-coarse-cauchy>.
  </proof>

  The source density transfers exactly, but its singleton part does not:
  connected components with two or more marks remain. The next estimate
  bounds the effect of omitting all those components while keeping the bound
  independent of the stopping level.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stopping estimate><label|lem:E-stopping> For fixed <math|p,v,x>,
    there are <math|F<rsub|0>\<less\>\<infty\>> and <math|m<rsub|0>> such
    that, for each <math|m\<geqslant\>m<rsub|0>> and every sufficiently large
    <math|N>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-stop-error><around|\||M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>-C<rsub|N,p,m>*<around|(|v;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>.
    </equation>

    The constant <math|F<rsub|0>> is independent of <math|m,N>; the cutoff
    threshold may depend on<nbsp><math|m>.
  </lemma>

  Every mode with at least two marks has support covering number at least
  <math|B<rsub|m>/64>, by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-cover>. All such modes
  can therefore be multiplied by a complex<nbsp><math|z> with
  <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>>, at the cost of two
  units of support exponent. The normalized integral
  <math|F<rsub|m><around|(|z|)>> is then holomorphic and bounded by a
  constant <math|F<rsub|0>> fixed at a base level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>,
  independent of<nbsp><math|m>; <math|F<rsub|m><around|(|1|)>> is the cutoff
  moment and <math|F<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>=C<rsub|N,p,m>>, so Cauchy's
  estimate gives <math|<around|\||F<rsub|m><around|(|1|)>-F<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>>.

  <\proof>
    The expansion is that of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form> for the exact
    source evolution<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-multiple-density>; the bounds at the
    base level come from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication> and the
    singleton bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level>. Choose a
    base level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>> above the reference levels and the grid
    containing<nbsp><math|x>. At all sufficiently fine levels, distinct
    ancestral sites have separation at least <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>>: their
    separation is a fixed positive physical number divided
    by<nbsp><math|a<rsub|i>>, while <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|2>> grows only as a
    power of <math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>>. Their occupancy is one.
    Enlarge <math|k<rsub|0>> so these assertions and all required positive
    source allowances hold thereafter.

    Choose mark weights <math|<math-ss|w><rsub|i>\<gtr\>0> at this base level
    so the weighted complete singleton sizes lie below those allowances. This
    choice is independent of<nbsp><math|N>:
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level> bounds each singleton uniformly
    along the fixed-level cutoff tail. Let
    <math|s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|reg>>,s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|for>>> bound
    the weighted regular singleton supremum and the sum of forced singleton
    suprema at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>. Set

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-stopping-constant>F<rsub|0>=<frac|exp
      <around|{|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k<rsub|0>>>|\|>*q<rsub|k<rsub|0>>+n*<around|(|s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|reg>>+s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|for>>+2|)>|}>|<big|prod><rsub|i><math-ss|w><rsub|i>>.
    </equation>

    The numerator is the normalized-integral bound of the several-mark
    expansion, now with these fixed base weights. It depends on the base
    level, not on the later stopping level.

    At level<nbsp><math|m\<gtr\>k<rsub|0>>, every nonzero mode of
    <math|H<rsub|m,X>> or <math|K<rsup|\<geqslant\>2><rsub|m,Y>> contains at
    least two marks and its support contains their anchors. The covering
    estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-cover> gives

    <\equation*>
      <cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>\<geqslant\>B<rsub|m>/64.
    </equation*>

    Multiply every one of these higher-mode functions by a common complex
    variable<nbsp><math|z>. For <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>>,
    its amplitude increase is absorbed by decreasing the support exponent by
    two:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||z|\|>*e<rsup|<around|(|\<sigma\>-2|)><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><rsub|B<rsub|m>><around|(|X|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Singleton functions are not multiplied by<nbsp><math|z>. The source
    representation therefore remains integrable on
    <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>>, with its prescribed
    singleton functions, original forced eligibility tests and two fewer
    units of support exponent. Here <math|\<sigma\>=\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>=100>.
    For <math|s\<geqslant\>0> and <math|98\<leqslant\>\<tau\>\<leqslant\>100>,
    <math|e<rsup|\<tau\>*s>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|100*s>>, so every regular or
    forced weighted sum decreases when its exponent decreases. The
    coefficientwise rooted clause of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>
    bounds the connected regular terms at exponent <math|\<sigma\>+3> and the
    forced terms at <math|\<sigma\>+2>. The marked product factors are paid
    at the entry chosen in item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries>. Applying these
    bounds in the weighted source norm of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>
    leaves the two units used here, with final exponent <math|98>.

    Carry this modified density back to the same base
    level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>, using the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> with all support exponents lowered
    by two, as permitted in its statement. Every nonzero mode contains only
    marks whose anchors lie in its support, so multiplying modes retains
    their positive weights without adding a mark outside that support. At the
    base level, the singleton exponential and forced factors contribute at
    most <math|n*<around|(|s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|reg>>+s<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)>>
    to the logarithmic majorant; the remaining connected regular and forced
    sums contribute at most <math|2*n>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-ratio>
    contributes <math|<around|\||\<Lambda\><rsub|n<rsub|p,k<rsub|0>>>|\|>*q<rsub|k<rsub|0>>>.
    Coefficient extraction divides by <math|<big|prod><rsub|i><math-ss|w><rsub|i>>,
    giving exactly<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-stopping-constant>. Absolute convergence
    on <math|<around|\||z|\|>\<less\>e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>> makes the
    normalized full-mode integral <math|F<rsub|m><around|(|z|)>> holomorphic
    there, with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F<rsub|m><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>F<rsub|0>.
    </equation*>

    This avoids the level-<math|m> volume factor that would arise from
    estimating the normalized integral only where it was stopped.

    For <math|e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>\<gtr\>2>, the Cauchy derivative estimate
    on circles of radius <math|e<rsup|B<rsub|m>/64>/2> centered on
    <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||F<rsub|m><around|(|1|)>-F<rsub|m><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>.
    </equation*>

    At <math|z=1> this is the exact multiple-source integral. Its equality to
    the original cutoff moment follows from Fubini and the probability
    normalization of every block kernel in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-multiple-density>.
    At <math|z=0> it is exactly<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-coarse-integral>.
    Substitution proves the estimate. For each fixed<nbsp><math|m>, the
    required bound on its singleton functions holds on a cutoff tail by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level>; it changes the threshold but
    leaves<nbsp><math|F<rsub|0>> fixed.
  </proof>

  The fixed-level and stopping estimates, together with the positional bounds
  already proved, give the full limit.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Lattice-point convergence><label|lem:E3.1> For every <math|p,n,v>,
    there is <math|f<rsub|p,v>\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>|)>>
    such that, for every <math|\<b-l\>>, compact
    <math|K\<subset\><NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>> and <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>,
    some <math|N<rsub|0>*<around|(|p,n,v,\<b-l\>,K,\<varepsilon\>|)>> gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;x/a<rsub|N>|)>-\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<less\>\<varepsilon\>
    </equation*>

    at every spacing-<math|a<rsub|N>> point of <math|K> in the periodic lift,
    for every <math|N\<geqslant\>N<rsub|0>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Pointwise convergence on the common grids
    is<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-three-term>; the difference bounds of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.4> extend it to uniform convergence of all
    derivatives. By Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-fixed-level>\U<reference|lem:E-stopping>,
    the right side of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-three-term> is at most
    <math|4*F<rsub|0>*e<rsup|-B<rsub|m>/64>+<around|\||C<rsub|N,p,m>-C<rsub|N<rprime|'>,p,m>|\|>>;
    taking first <math|m> and then <math|N,N<rprime|'>> large proves the
    Cauchy property at every separated configuration on a fixed common grid.
    Since every ratio is an integer, each coarser grid is contained in every
    finer grid. The union of these grids is dense in the periodic
    noncollision domain.

    We next extend this limit and identify its derivatives. Choose a compact
    neighbourhood <math|K<rprime|'>> of<nbsp><math|K> contained in that
    domain, and a positive lower bound for its torus separation.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.4> bounds the lattice functions and all
    their fixed differences there. One more difference gives, by telescoping
    along coordinate lattice paths,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||G<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>-G<rsub|N><around|(|z<rprime|'>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B*<space|0.17em><around|\||z-z<rprime|'>|\|><rsub|1>
    </equation*>

    for nearby physical grid points, where
    <math|G<rsub|N>=\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>>. For order zero, a
    finite dense grid in <math|K<rprime|'>> and this estimate extend the
    pointwise Cauchy property to uniform convergence on<nbsp><math|K>. The
    multilinear interpolants obey the same bound, with an additional
    <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|N>|)>> displacement to their vertices. The
    resulting limit<nbsp><math|f<rsub|p,v>> is continuous and periodic.

    Suppose <math|G<rsub|N>> already converges locally uniformly
    to<nbsp><math|g>. Bounds for two more differences compare its first
    forward difference with a difference over a fixed physical interval. Put
    <math|h<rsub|N>=Q<rsub|N>*a<rsub|N>\<to\>h\<gtr\>0>, with
    <math|Q<rsub|N>\<in\><N>>. Summing successive first differences and
    bounding their variation by the second difference gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:E-discrete-taylor><around*|\||<frac|G<rsub|N>*<around|(|z+h<rsub|N>*e|)>-G<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>|h<rsub|N>>-\<Delta\><rsub|e>*G<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><tfrac|1|2>B*h<rsub|N>.
    </equation>

    Choose <math|h> small and then let <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>. The endpoint
    values converge uniformly, so<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-discrete-taylor> proves
    that <math|\<Delta\><rsub|e>*G<rsub|N>> is uniformly Cauchy
    on<nbsp><math|K>. Call its continuous limit<nbsp><math|g<rsub|e>>. The
    exact telescoping identity on grid segments, followed by Riemann
    summation of the uniformly convergent first differences, gives

    <\equation*>
      g*<around|(|z+t*e|)>-g<around|(|z|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>g<rsub|e>*<around|(|z+u*e|)>*<space|0.17em>d*u.
    </equation*>

    Thus <math|\<partial\><rsub|e>*g=g<rsub|e>>. Induct on the length of the
    difference list. Every required additional order is available from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.4>, so all derivative limits exist and
    <math|f<rsub|p,v>> is smooth. Finite covers by such neighbourhoods give
    the asserted uniform statement on<nbsp><math|K>.
  </proof>

  The same limit applies when observation points move across periodic
  representative boundaries. Smearing then follows from a compact-support
  Riemann sum.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Interpolation and smearing><label|lem:E3.2> For fixed
    <math|p,n,v,\<b-l\>> and <math|x<rsub|N>\<to\>x\<in\><NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>>,
    the multilinear interpolants of <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>>
    at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|N>> converge to <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v><around|(|x|)>>.
    For every <math|p>-admissible separated test<nbsp><math|t>,

    <\equation*>
      t*\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v>\<in\>L<rsup|1>,<space|2em>a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y>t<around|(|<wide|y|^>|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|v;y|)>\<longrightarrow\><big|int>t*\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Both assertions follow from the locally uniform convergence of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1>, with the placement and moment
    conventions of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D03> and admissibility from
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:continuum>. Choose a compact neighbourhood
    of<nbsp><math|x> inside the periodic noncollision domain. Every vertex
    used by the interpolant at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|N>> lies there eventually
    and tends to<nbsp><math|x>. The interpolant is a convex combination of
    the vertex values. Uniform convergence at these vertices and continuity
    of the limiting derivative give the moving-point assertion. Work on the
    periodic lift throughout; no continuity of the choice of centered
    representatives is needed.

    The closed support of<nbsp><math|t> is a compact subset of that domain,
    strictly inside the chosen period box. The smooth limiting derivative is
    bounded there, so its product with<nbsp><math|t> is integrable. On a
    fixed compact neighbourhood, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1> makes the
    supremum of the difference between the lattice values and the limiting
    derivative tend to zero. Its contribution to the sum is at most that
    supremum times <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|t|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>> times
    the total volume of the grid cubes meeting the support. This volume is
    bounded independently of<nbsp><math|N>. The remaining sum is the Riemann
    sum for the continuous compactly supported function
    <math|t*\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*f<rsub|p,v>> and converges to its
    integral.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>>
    Set <math|M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>=f<rsub|p,v>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1> gives its smoothness on the full periodic
    noncollision domain, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2> gives the
    asserted interpolation and smearing limits for every fixed derivative
    list. Thus every inner limit in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09>
    exists at every admissible fixed volume along the full cutoff sequence.
  </render-proof>

  <section|Infinite volume and field calculus><label|sec:partII-F>

  We pass from the fixed-volume kernels of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>
  (fixed-volume limit) to correlations on<nbsp><math|<R><rsup|4>>. The order
  of limits is that of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09> and
  Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:two-limits>: at every fixed admissible volume
  the spacing tends to zero along the full cutoff sequence; only then does
  the volume grow, along one refinement of the prescribed volume sequence
  that serves every field list, derivative and test.
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F.1> extracts this volume limit, with
  all derivatives, from the uniform bounds of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>).
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F.2> bounds separated correlations cell
  by cell and shows that negligible errors vanish in them
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b>). Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F.3>
  identifies the quotient fields: it constructs coefficient maps for
  inclusions and positional derivatives and proves <math|dim<rsub|<C>>
  F<rsub|d>=dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>,
  which proves Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>).
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F.4> passes reflection positivity to the
  limit and proves the power bounds of E7 (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F3>,
  which completes Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV>).
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-F.5> bounds repeated reflected
  insertions, the input for the slab operators of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>.

  The Wilson family and separated tests are those of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:continuum>, the insertion labels those of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:local-fields>, and the differences those of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:uv-limit>. Write
  <math|M<rsub|p,v>=M<rsup|<math-up|UV>><rsub|p,v>> and
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|F|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>=<big|int>F*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsub|N,p>>.
  Constants may depend on the fixed construction; further dependence is
  indicated.

  <subsection|Volume limits><label|sec:partII-F.1>

  Fix a strictly increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>:<N>\<to\><N>>.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> bounds the lattice kernels and their
  differences on separated sets, uniformly in the volume; one further
  difference makes these bounds equicontinuous.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Volume limits><label|lem:F1> There are a strictly
    increasing<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>> and a complex multilinear
    table<nbsp><math|S> such that, with <math|P=<math-ss|p>\<circ\><math-ss|q>>,
    for every<nbsp><math|n>, label list<nbsp><math|v> and difference
    list<nbsp><math|\<b-l\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-conf>S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;\<cdot\>|)>\<in\>C<rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>,v>\<longrightarrow\>\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;\<cdummy\>|)><space|1em><text|locally
      uniformly on ><Conf|n><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Every compact <math|K\<subset\><Conf|n>> eventually lies
    in<nbsp><math|<NoHit|L<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>>|n>>, and for every separated
    test<nbsp><math|f>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-ordered>C<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>><rsup|<math-up|UV>>*<around|(|v;f|)>\<longrightarrow\><big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
    </equation>

    Both convergences persist along every further refinement
    of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Fix compact sets <math|K\<subset\>K<rprime|'>\<subset\><Conf|n>>, with
    <math|K<rprime|'>> a neighbourhood of<nbsp><math|K>. For
    large<nbsp><math|p>, <math|K<rprime|'>> lies
    in<nbsp><math|<NoHit|L<rsub|p>|n>> with torus separation at least some
    <math|2*r\<gtr\>0> independent of<nbsp><math|p>.
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> bounds
    <math|\<Delta\><rsub|\<b-l\>><latmoment><rsub|N,p>> by
    <math|B*<around|(|v,\<b-l\>,r|)>> near<nbsp><math|K<rprime|'>> for
    large<nbsp><math|N>, uniformly in<nbsp><math|p>, and by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2> the multilinear interpolants converge at
    each fixed<nbsp><math|p> to <math|\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|p,v>>.
    Hence

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-local>sup<rsub|p\<geqslant\>p<rsub|0>>
      sup<rsub|x\<in\>K<rprime|'>><around|\||\<partial\><rsub|\<b-l\>>*M<rsub|p,v><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B*<around|(|v,\<b-l\>,r|)>,
    </equation>

    although the cutoff threshold may depend on<nbsp><math|p>. One more
    derivative gives equicontinuity on<nbsp><math|K>.
    Arzelà\UAscoli<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1976|Theorem<nbsp>7.25> and a
    diagonal extraction over compact exhaustions of<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>,
    arities, degree lists, basis coefficient lists and difference lists, a
    countable family, give<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>>. Multilinearity extends
    the limits to every label list, and integration by parts against
    compactly supported tests identifies the limit of each derivative with
    the derivative of the limit. This proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>.

    A compact separated set has bounded diameter, so it eventually lies
    in<nbsp><math|<NoHit|L<rsub|P<around|(|k|)>>|n>>.
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV> gives, at every
    admissible<nbsp><math|p>, the full inner limit
    <math|C<rsub|p><rsup|<math-up|UV>>*<around|(|v;f|)>=<big|int>f*<space|0.17em>M<rsub|p,v>>;
    the support of<nbsp><math|f> is eventually admissible
    along<nbsp><math|P>, and local uniform convergence
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-ordered>. Both properties pass to subsequences.
  </proof>

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> refines<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>> once more
  without changing<nbsp><math|S>; from then on<nbsp><math|P> denotes the
  final refinement. At each volume the kernel limits remain the full
  ultraviolet limits of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV>: no spacing
  subsequence enters<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-ordered>.

  <paragraph|Symmetries of the limit.> Permutation symmetry, the empty
  correlation, deletion of the distinguished unit, conjugation and
  signed-permutation covariance hold on the lattice once<nbsp><math|N>
  exceeds the reference levels (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a>). Approximating separated configurations
  by lattice points, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2>
  and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf> pass them to<nbsp><math|S>. For translations,
  approximate the translation vector on the same lattice; translation
  invariance and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-local> give arbitrary real translations.
  This proves E1\UE3, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf> gives E6.

  <subsection|Separated cells and negligible errors><label|sec:partII-F.2>

  Chessboard seminorms, interior observables, signed images, aligned tilings,
  anchors and margins are those of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27> and of
  the paragraphs before Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> and
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small>. For observables
  <math|F<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,F<rsub|n>> in distinct cells
  <math|C<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,C<rsub|n>> of a translated bond
  tiling<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\>>, each interior to its cell, write
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|h>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-T\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|h>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-T\>>>,
  where <math|G<rsub|h>> is the base-cell observable with
  <math|<around|(|G<rsub|h>|)><rsub|C<rsub|h>>=F<rsub|h>>. The chessboard
  inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>, with the constant one in the remaining
  cells, gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:F-chess><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|h=1><rsup|n>F<rsub|h>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\><big|prod><rsub|h=1><rsup|n><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|h>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-T\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The seminorm measures the cost of repeating one observation throughout the
  box, so this bound is independent of the volume and of the distances
  between the cells.

  Separated anchors must therefore be placed in distinct cells, away from the
  boundaries. In each axis, the translations of a tiling of
  side<nbsp><math|a> that bring one of<nbsp><math|n> anchors within
  distance<nbsp><math|w> of a cell boundary form, modulo<nbsp><math|a>, at
  most<nbsp><math|n> intervals of total length<nbsp><math|2*n*w>. If
  <math|2*n*w\<less\>a>, some lattice translation avoids them
  once<nbsp><math|a<rsub|N>> is small. We call this the strip count. Anchors
  at torus sup-distance greater than<nbsp><math|a> never share a cell.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Separated cells><label|lem:F-cells> Let <math|m\<leqslant\>k> and
    <math|n\<geqslant\>1> with <math|6*n*a<rsub|k>\<less\>a<rsub|m>>, and
    suppose the insertions<nbsp><math|O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|h>,\<cdot\>|)>>,
    <math|h\<leqslant\>n>, satisfy the hypothesis of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> at level<nbsp><math|k> with a common
    bound<nbsp><math|B>. For all sufficiently large<nbsp><math|N> and all
    anchors with pairwise torus sup-distance greater
    than<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|h=1><rsup|n>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|h>,y<rsub|h>|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>B<rsup|n>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The strip count with <math|a=a<rsub|m>> and <math|w=3*a<rsub|k>> gives a
    tiling of side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>> with the anchors in distinct cells,
    at distance at least<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k>> from their boundaries.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> bounds each seminorm by<nbsp><math|B>,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> gives the claim.
  </proof>

  A negligible error has a tolerance chosen before the cell scale
  (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small>), and this order makes it vanish
  against each fixed list of spectators.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Vanishing errors><label|lem:F1b> For every negligible
    error<nbsp><math|E> of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small>, fixed
    label list<nbsp><math|v>, distinguished slot<nbsp><math|i>
    and<nbsp><math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>, there is
    <math|C*<around|(|v,\<delta\>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that, eventually
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-small><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|E<rsub|N,p><around|(|y<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|h\<ne\>i>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|h>,y<rsub|h>|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|v,\<delta\>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|N,p>*<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|sep><rsub|<math-up|tor>><around|(|y|)>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Consequently its smear against any fixed separated test tends to zero in
    that order.
  </lemma>

  The error and the spectators receive distinct cells; the error pays its
  tolerance<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsub|N,p>>, and each spectator a constant.

  <\proof>
    Let<nbsp><math|n> be the arity. Take<nbsp><math|m> with
    <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>\<delta\>>, and let <math|k\<geqslant\>m> be the
    level that Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small> assigns to the
    arity<nbsp><math|n> and the lower level<nbsp><math|m>; the error tiling
    has side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>>. Choose
    <math|k<rsub|<math-up|sp>>\<geqslant\>k>, beyond the reference levels of
    the spectators, with <math|3*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/<around|[|8*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> bounds the centered seminorms of all
    signed and conjugated images of the spectators at
    level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|<math-up|sp>>> by
    some<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>. The strip count with
    <math|a=a<rsub|k>> and <math|w=a<rsub|k>/<around|[|4*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>>,
    and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>a<rsub|k>>, give a tiling of
    side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>> with the anchors in distinct cells at distance
    at least<nbsp><math|w> from their boundaries. This margin contains the
    integer margin<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-margin>, including its extra lattice
    unit, and exceeds<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>>. Translating
    by a multiple of<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>>, we may take the error's cell to
    be the base cell. Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small> bounds the
    seminorm of the error by<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsub|N,p>>, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>, with tiling side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>>
    and centered level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>, bounds those of the
    spectators by<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|sp>>>.
    Now<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-small> with
    <math|C=B<rsub|<math-up|sp>><rsup|n-1>>. On a compact test support the
    number of lattice tuples times<nbsp><math|a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>> is bounded
    independently of<nbsp><math|N,p>, which proves the smeared assertion.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Quotient fields><label|sec:partII-F.3>

  Two labels represent the same field when every separated correlation gives
  the same answer for them (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>). For a
  degree<nbsp><math|d>, a slot, fixed spectators<nbsp><math|v> and a
  separated test<nbsp><math|f>, the one-slot functionals
  on<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>> are

  <\equation*>
    L<rsub|N,p><around|(|c|)>=<latsmear><rsub|N,p><around*|(|<around|(|d,c|)>,v;f|)>,<space|2em>L<around|(|c|)>=<big|int>f*<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|n+1>*<around*|(|c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x<rsub|0>*<space|0.17em>d*x.
  </equation*>

  As <math|S> is smooth, <math|<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>> is the
  intersection of the kernels of all such<nbsp><math|L>: it consists exactly
  of the labels that no separated smear detects. Four inputs from
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.2> identify it, each with one task.
  (C1)<nbsp>Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.8> represents a degree-<math|d>
  insertion in a degree <math|e\<geqslant\>d>, and
  (C2)<nbsp>Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.9> its lattice difference quotient
  in degree<nbsp><math|d+1>, up to negligible errors.
  (C3)<nbsp>Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.7> replaces a tame family in
  <math|ker \<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>> by observables with vanishing separated
  moments, up to a negligible error; <em|tame> means the subpower
  bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-subpower>. (C4)<nbsp>The free test rows of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests> and the relative rank comparison
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10> keep the quotient from losing
  dimension. The families supplied by (C1)\U(C3) may have unbounded
  coefficients; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F-represent> turns them into fixed
  coefficient vectors.

  The coefficient limits are taken along a common spacing
  subsequence<nbsp><math|<math-ss|n>> at every volume and a further volume
  refinement, in the order<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>. They do not affect the
  kernel limits, which at each admissible volume remain the full ultraviolet
  limits required by Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D09>. A family in a
  finite-dimensional space is <em|bounded> if its norm is bounded eventually
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>; an <em|ordered limit point> of countably many
  bounded families is their joint limit along an ultrafilter containing the
  ordered tails. It exists by compactness, and families that converge along
  the ordered tails keep their limits, so passing to ordered limit points
  changes neither<nbsp><math|S> nor<nbsp><math|P>.

  The next lemma, finite-dimensional linear algebra, converts approximate
  representatives, which need not be bounded, into one exact representative.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Representation of limits><label|lem:F-represent> Let<nbsp><math|V>
    be a finite-dimensional complex normed space and<nbsp><math|\<cal-F\>> a
    proper filter on an index set. Let <math|\<Pi\><rsub|u>,\<Pi\>> be
    idempotents on<nbsp><math|V> with <math|\<Pi\><rsub|u>\<to\>\<Pi\>>, and
    <math|L<rsub|i,u>,L<rsub|i>>, <math|i\<in\>I>, linear functionals with
    <math|L<rsub|i,u>\<to\>L<rsub|i>> and <math|ker
    \<Pi\>=<big|cap><rsub|i>ker L<rsub|i>>. Suppose <math|x<rsub|u>\<in\>V>
    satisfy, for every<nbsp><math|i>, along<nbsp><math|\<cal-F\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-normalized>L<rsub|i,u><around|(|x<rsub|u>|)>\<longrightarrow\>t<rsub|i>,<space|2em>L<rsub|i,u>*<around*|(|<around|(|x<rsub|u>-\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>|\<\|\|\>>|)>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation>

    Then some <math|z\<in\>V> satisfies <math|\<Pi\>*z=z> and
    <math|L<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=t<rsub|i>> for every<nbsp><math|i>.
  </lemma>

  The second condition makes the null part of<nbsp><math|x<rsub|u>>
  negligible relative to its retained part. If the retained
  parts<nbsp><math|\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>> escaped to infinity, normalizing
  them would produce a unit vector in the range of<nbsp><math|\<Pi\>>
  annihilated by every<nbsp><math|L<rsub|i>>: a null vector in the retained
  range, which <math|ker \<Pi\>=<big|cap><rsub|i>ker L<rsub|i>> excludes.

  <\proof>
    Put <math|b<rsub|u>=<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|-1>>
    and <math|z<rsub|u>=b<rsub|u>*\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>>, so
    <math|b<rsub|u>+<around|\<\|\|\>|z<rsub|u>|\<\|\|\>>=1> and
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|u>*z<rsub|u>=z<rsub|u>>. The pairs
    <math|<around|(|b<rsub|u>,z<rsub|u>|)>> have a cluster
    point<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<lambda\>,z|)>> along<nbsp><math|\<cal-F\>>
    in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>\<times\><around|{|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>1|}>>.
    As <math|L<rsub|i,u><around|(|z<rsub|u>|)>=b<rsub|u>*L<rsub|i,u><around|(|x<rsub|u>|)>-L<rsub|i,u>*<around|(|b<rsub|u>*<around|(|x<rsub|u>-\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>|)>|)>>,

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|i><around|(|z<rsub|u>|)>-b<rsub|u>*t<rsub|i>=b<rsub|u>*<around*|(|L<rsub|i,u><around|(|x<rsub|u>|)>-t<rsub|i>|)>-L<rsub|i,u>*<around*|(|b<rsub|u>*<around|(|x<rsub|u>-\<Pi\><rsub|u>*x<rsub|u>|)>|)>+<around|(|L<rsub|i>-L<rsub|i,u>|)><around|(|z<rsub|u>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0,
    </equation*>

    and <math|\<Pi\>*z<rsub|u>-z<rsub|u>=<around|(|\<Pi\>-\<Pi\><rsub|u>|)>*z<rsub|u>\<to\>0>.
    Hence <math|L<rsub|i><around|(|z|)>=\<lambda\>*t<rsub|i>>,
    <math|\<Pi\>*z=z> and <math|\<lambda\>+<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>=1>.
    If <math|\<lambda\>=0>, then <math|z\<in\><big|cap><rsub|i>ker
    L<rsub|i>=ker \<Pi\>>, so <math|z=\<Pi\>*z=0>, contradicting
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|z|\<\|\|\>>=1>. Thus <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>,
    and <math|z/\<lambda\>> has the required properties.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Corrected coefficient maps><label|lem:F2> After a strictly
    increasing refinement of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|q>>, which does not
    change<nbsp><math|S>, there are complex linear maps
    <math|<wide|\<jmath\>|^><rsub|d,e>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|e>> for
    <math|d\<leqslant\>e> and <math|<wide|\<partial\>|^><rsub|d,a>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d+1>>
    such that, for every<nbsp><math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, spectator
    word<nbsp><math|v<around|(|x|)>> and <math|<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x|)>\<in\><Conf|n+1>>,

    <\gather>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|S<rsub|n+1>*<around*|(|<wide|\<jmath\>|^><rsub|d,e>*c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>=S<rsub|n+1>*<around*|(|c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|S<rsub|n+1>*<around*|(|<wide|\<partial\>|^><rsub|d,a>*c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>=\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|a>>*S<rsub|n+1>*<around*|(|c<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,v<around|(|x|)>|)>.<eq-number><label|eq:F-calculus>>>>>
    </gather>

    Moreover <math|dim<rsub|<C>> F<rsub|d>=dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>,
    and the induced maps <math|\<jmath\><rsub|d,e><around|[|c|]>=<around|[|<wide|\<jmath\>|^><rsub|d,e>*c|]>>
    and <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><around|[|c|]>=<around|[|<wide|\<partial\>|^><rsub|d,a>*c|]>>
    satisfy Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>.
  </lemma>

  The proof identifies the null space with the kernel of a limiting
  projection, then applies Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F-represent> to the
  families of (C1) and<nbsp>(C2).

  <\proof>
    <em|Limiting projections.> Bounded coefficient families are tame:
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> bounds their inverse terminal coordinates
    by a fixed factor times <math|<big|prod><rsub|m\<less\>N>C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>>,
    and <math|log C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1><rsup|-2>*log
    \<ell\><rsub|m>> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>) with
    <math|J<rsub|m>\<to\>\<infty\>> makes this product grow more slowly than
    every power of<nbsp><math|a<rsub|N><rsup|-1>>. A countable diagonal
    extraction, first in<nbsp><math|N> at every<nbsp><math|p>, then in the
    volume, retaining<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf>, makes the following converge:
    the projections of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>, which are bounded
    by<nbsp><math|C<rsup|\<Pi\>><rsub|d>>, the comparison lifts of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10>, and the couplings
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>\<in\>\<frak-B\><rsub|k>>, <math|k\<in\><N>>,
    of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>. In operator norm,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-projections>\<Pi\><rsub|d,<math-ss|n><around|(|j|)>,p>\<longrightarrow\>\<Pi\><rsub|d><rsup|<around|(|p|)>>,<space|2em>\<Pi\><rsub|d><rsup|<around|(|P<around|(|k|)>|)>>\<longrightarrow\>\<Pi\><rsub|d>,<space|2em>\<Pi\><rsub|d><rsup|2>=\<Pi\><rsub|d><space|0.17em>,
    </equation>

    and by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails> this iterated limit is an ordered limit.

    <em|The null space.> By finite dimensionality, the full fixed-volume
    limits and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-ordered>, <math|L<rsub|N,p>\<to\>L> in
    operator norm in the ordered limit. For fixed<nbsp><math|c>, the bounded
    family <math|c-\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>*c> is tame and lies in <math|ker
    \<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>>, so (C3) and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b> give
    <math|L<rsub|N,p>*<around|(|c-\<Pi\><rsub|d,N,p>*c|)>\<to\>0>. Thus
    <math|L*<around|(|<around|(|1-\<Pi\><rsub|d>|)>*c|)>=0> for
    every<nbsp><math|L>:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-null>ker \<Pi\><rsub|d>\<subset\><nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>.
    </equation>

    For the reverse inequality, use (C4).
    Rearranging<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-relative>
    isolates<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\><rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|I>>; every other term
    contains a nonempty<nbsp><math|R<rsub|J>>, whose expectation is at most
    its seminorm<nbsp><math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> with one occupied cell. Thus

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-rank><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|<big|prod><rsub|h\<in\>I>F<rsup|<math-up|tst>><rsub|h>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>-\<cal-G\><rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|I>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<big|sum><rsub|J\<subsetneq\>I><around|\||\<cal-G\><rsup|\<nu\>><rsub|J>|\|>,
    </equation>

    and the sum stays bounded as <math|y<rsup|\<nu\>,i>\<to\>y<rsup|i>>. Pass
    to the coefficient and kernel limits. Each entry of the resulting matrix
    is a fixed spectator functional, vanishing
    on<nbsp><math|<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>>, applied to the limiting lift
    of<nbsp><math|\<frak-m\><rsub|j>>; so the matrix factors
    through<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>> and has rank at most<nbsp><math|dim
    F<rsub|d>>. As <math|\<nu\>\<to\>\<infty\>> these matrices tend to the
    rank-<math|dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>> matrix<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\>>,
    so <math|dim F<rsub|d>\<geqslant\>dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>. Ranks
    of converging idempotents are eventually constant (if
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P-Q|\<\|\|\>>\<less\>1>, then <math|P> is
    injective on <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>Q>), so the dimension
    inequality of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6> passes
    to<nbsp><math|\<Pi\><rsub|d>>, and with<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-null>, <math|dim
    F<rsub|d>\<leqslant\>dim V<rsub|d>-dim ker
    \<Pi\><rsub|d>\<leqslant\>dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>. Hence

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-dimension><nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>=ker
      \<Pi\><rsub|d>,<space|2em>dim<rsub|<C>>
      F<rsub|d>=dim<rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>.
    </equation>

    <em|Targets.> For the inclusion of <math|c\<in\>V<rsub|d>>, (C1) and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b> give a tame family
    <math|w<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|e>> whose one-slot functionals tend to
    <math|t<rsub|L>=<big|int>f*S*<around|(|c,\<ldots\>|)>>. For the
    derivative in slot<nbsp><math|i> and axis<nbsp><math|a>, (C2) gives
    <math|w<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|d+1>>, and torus summation by parts gives
    exactly

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-parts>a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y>f<around|(|<wide|y|^>|)>*\<Delta\><rsub|i,a><latmoment><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>=a<rsub|N><rsup|4*n>*<big|sum><rsub|y><frac|f*<around|(|<wide|y|^>-a<rsub|N>*e<rsub|i,a>|)>-f<around|(|<wide|y|^>|)>|a<rsub|N>><latmoment><rsub|N,p><around|(|v;y|)>,
    </equation>

    with <math|e<rsub|i,a>> the coordinate vector of slot<nbsp><math|i>,
    axis<nbsp><math|a>. The backward quotient tends
    to<nbsp><math|-\<partial\><rsub|i,a>*f> with residual at most
    <math|a<rsub|N>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsub|i,a><rsup|2>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>/2>,
    which Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> at order zero controls, so the
    target is <math|t<rsub|L>=-<big|int><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|i,a>*f|)>*S*<around|(|c,\<ldots\>|)>>.

    <em|Representation.> Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F-represent>
    in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|e>>, with<nbsp><math|\<cal-F\>> the filter of
    ordered tails <math|<around|{|<around|(|j,k|)>:k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>,j\<geqslant\>j<rsub|0><around|(|k|)>|}>>,
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|u>=\<Pi\><rsub|e,<math-ss|n><around|(|j|)>,P<around|(|k|)>>>
    and the one-slot functionals of degree<nbsp><math|e>. They converge as
    shown above, and <math|ker \<Pi\><rsub|e>=<big|cap>ker L>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-dimension>. With <math|\<Pi\>=\<Pi\><rsub|e,N,p>>,
    the family <math|<around|(|w-\<Pi\>*w|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\>*w|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
    lies in <math|ker \<Pi\>> and is tame, being a bounded multiple
    of<nbsp><math|w> minus a family of norm at most one; (C3) and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b> give the second condition
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-normalized>. The lemma gives one vector representing
    the target against every spectator and test at once; no further
    subsequence is chosen for these uncountably many tests. Choose such
    vectors on a basis of<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>> and extend linearly;
    <eqref|eq:F-conf> turns the distributional identities
    into<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-calculus>.

    <em|Induced maps.> By<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-calculus>,
    <math|<wide|\<jmath\>|^><rsub|d,e>> and
    <math|<wide|\<partial\>|^><rsub|d,a>> carry
    <math|<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>> into null spaces, so they descend
    to<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>> and satisfy the identities of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>; with<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-dimension>
    this proves that proposition. The further properties listed after it
    (injective responses, units, conjugation, <math|<hypercubic>> covariance,
    the positional identity in every slot) follow from these identities, E1
    and E2, as explained there.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Example: the curvature square (continued).>
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:A-example> follows the label
  <math|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>\<in\>V<rsub|4>> of
  <math|<frac|1|4>*<big|sum><rsub|a,b,\<alpha\>>F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*b>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*b>>
  to its insertion; in degree four the quotient removes nothing. Every jet
  coordinate has weight at least two, and since<nbsp><math|<g>> is simple no
  nonzero <math|<math-up|Ad><around|(|G|)>>-invariant polynomial is linear in
  a <math|<g>>-valued jet; so an element of<nbsp><math|V<rsub|4>> is a
  constant plus an invariant quadratic form in the lowest
  jet<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|0>>, that is, in the curvature at the origin.
  The Maxwell equations do not constrain the curvature at one point, so no
  nonzero such polynomial vanishes on every Maxwell solution: the equation
  space<nbsp><math|\<cal-I\><rsub|4>> of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6> is
  zero. That lemma gives <math|\<Pi\><rsub|4,N,p>=1>,
  and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-dimension> gives <math|<nullspace|4><around|(|S|)>=0>.
  Distinct degree-four labels give distinct fields, and
  <math|<around|[|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|]>\<ne\>0> in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|4>>. The
  quotient first acts on derivatives: the lattice difference quotient of the
  insertion of<nbsp><math|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>> is not a degree-five insertion,
  (C2) represents it by a tame family in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|5>>, and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F-represent> replaces that family by one vector,
  with <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><around|[|c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|]>=<around|[|<wide|\<partial\>|^><rsub|4,a>*c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>|]>>.
  In degree five, <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|5>> contains the invariant contraction
  <math|<big|sum><rsub|a,b,c,\<alpha\>>F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*b>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|c>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|c*b>>
  of a curvature with the Maxwell expression. By
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-dimension>,
  <math|<nullspace|5><around|(|S|)>=ker \<Pi\><rsub|5>> is therefore nonzero,
  and <math|<wide|\<partial\>|^><rsub|4,a>*c<rsub|F<rsup|2>>> is determined
  only modulo these limits of transported equation directions.

  <subsection|Euclidean properties><label|sec:partII-F.4>

  Lattice symmetries and positivity pass through the ordered limits;
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F-cells> controls separated points as they
  approach one another, uniformly in their diameter.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Euclidean properties><label|lem:F3> The table<nbsp><math|S> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1> satisfies E1\UE7.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    E1\UE3 and E6 were proved after Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>; the
    positive derivative orders of E7 use<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-calculus>.

    <em|Reflection positivity.> We prove E5 and deduce E4. Fix joint smooth
    tests<nbsp><math|f<rsub|i>> with compact support on positive-side
    separated configurations for an axis<nbsp><math|a>. Use the bond
    reflection<nbsp><math|x<rsup|a>\<mapsto\>a<rsub|N>-x<rsup|a>>, a lattice
    symmetry with planes<nbsp><math|x<rsup|a>=a<rsub|N>/2>
    and<nbsp><math|x<rsup|a>=<around|(|a<rsub|N>+L<rsub|p>|)>/2>. For
    large<nbsp><math|p,N> the lattice smears<nbsp><math|F<rsub|i>> are
    supported strictly inside the positive half-torus, with stencils avoiding
    both planes, since the supports keep a positive distance from
    <math|x<rsup|a>=0> and the stencils have physical radius tending to zero.
    Periodic Wilson reflection positivity<nbsp><cite-detail|Seiler1982|Theorem<nbsp>2.1,
    pp.<nbsp>14--15> gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|z|\<bar\>><rsub|i>*z<rsub|j><around|\<langle\>|<wide|F<rsub|i>\<circ\><math-ss|r><rsub|a,N>|\<bar\>>F<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>\<geqslant\>0,
    </equation*>

    where<nbsp><math|<math-ss|r><rsub|a,N>> reflects configurations.
    Insertion covariance reflects the positions and
    applies<nbsp><math|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|refl>>> to the
    labels; conjugation acts on the labels of the first word. The reflected
    joint tests converge with all derivatives
    to<nbsp><math|<wide|f|\<bar\>><rsub|i>\<otimes\>f<rsub|j>> with the first
    group reflected through<nbsp><math|x<rsup|a>=0>, and all supports lie in
    one compact separated set. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:UB> at order
    zero bounds the effect of the moving tests by their uniform difference
    times a bounded weighted site count. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-ordered> then give a positive semidefinite limiting
    matrix; smoothness of<nbsp><math|S> makes every pairing absolutely
    integrable. This is E5. Concentrating normalized bumps at positive-side
    words gives E4 by continuity.

    <em|Power bound at order zero.> Take a nonempty label list and put
    <math|d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>=1+max<rsub|i> d<rsub|i>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> with <math|\<varepsilon\>=1>, for the
    finitely many signed and conjugated images of the labels, gives
    <math|k<rsub|0>> and <math|C<rsub|<math-up|ctr>>> such that their
    centered seminorms at levels <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>> are at most
    <math|C<rsub|<math-up|ctr>>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>>;
    these constants precede the separation. The block ratios grow slowly:
    <math|log \<ell\><rsub|u>/s<rsub|u>\<to\>0>
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>) gives
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|u>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|u><rsup|-1>> eventually, so for
    some <math|c<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>\<gtr\>0> and all<nbsp><math|u>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-thickness>a<rsub|u+1>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>*a<rsub|u><rsup|2>.
    </equation>

    Thus the first <math|u\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>> with
    <math|a<rsub|u>\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1> satisfies
    <math|a<rsub|u>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|0>*\<delta\><rsup|2>>, where
    <math|c<rsub|0>=min <around|(|c<rsub|<math-up|mesh>>,a<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>>:
    a discrete scale lies within a fixed power of any prescribed length. Fix
    <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1> and<nbsp><math|x> with
    <math|<sepinf><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>r>. Choose the first
    <math|m\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>> with <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>r/2>, then the
    first <math|k\<geqslant\>k<rsub|0>> with
    <math|a<rsub|k>\<less\>a<rsub|m>/<around|[|6*<around|(|n+1|)>|]>>; then
    <math|k\<geqslant\>m> and <math|a<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>c<rprime|'><around|(|n|)>*r<rsup|4>>.
    Lattice approximations of<nbsp><math|x> in large volumes have torus
    separation greater than<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>>, so
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F-cells> bounds the lattice correlation by
    <math|<around|(|C<rsub|<math-up|ctr>>*a<rsub|k><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>|)><rsup|n>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf> give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-power><around|\||S<rsub|n>*<around|(|v;x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|n,d,c|)>*r<rsup|-4*d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>*n>,
    </equation>

    with constants independent of the positions and the volume. Each
    insertion costs its centered seminorm at a cell scale comparable
    to<nbsp><math|r<rsup|4>>, and the chessboard bound makes the total
    independent of the diameter of<nbsp><math|x>.

    <em|Higher orders.> By<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-calculus>, every coordinate
    derivative is a correlation with fixed degree-raised labels, finitely
    many at each order. Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-power> to each, take the
    largest exponent, and bound the Fréchet norm by the sum of the coordinate
    coefficients in an orthonormal basis of<nbsp><math|<R><rsup|4*n>>. This
    proves E7; for arity zero, <math|S<rsub|0>=1>.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV>>
    Compose the volume refinements of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-ordered> proves
    ordered convergence, with the full inner limit of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:UV> at every admissible volume, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F3> proves E1\UE7. The coefficient corrections
    change no kernel limit.
  </render-proof>

  <subsection|Repeated reflected words><label|sec:partII-F.5>

  Use the OS space of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14>. For a
  word<nbsp><math|w>, let<nbsp><math|w<rsup|\<ast\>>> reverse it and apply
  conjugation to its labels, and let <math|\<theta\>*w=<math-ss|s><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|refl>>w<rsup|\<ast\>>>
  be its antilinear OS reflection. For <math|s\<gtr\>0> and a
  word<nbsp><math|w> with times in <math|<around|(|0,s|)>> set

  <\equation*>
    A<rsub|w>*v=w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|s*e<rsub|4>>*v,<space|2em>w<rsup|\<dagger\>>=\<tau\><rsub|s*e<rsub|4>>*\<theta\>*w;
  </equation*>

  <math|A<rsub|w>> acts on words, and boundedness on OS classes is not
  assumed. A word has buffer<nbsp><math|r> in a slab of
  width<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>> if its points have pairwise
  sup-distance at least<nbsp><math|r> and times in
  <math|<around|[|r,h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>-r|]>>. Fix a degree
  bound<nbsp><math|d> and a coefficient bound<nbsp><math|R\<geqslant\>0>, and
  put <math|Q=4*<around|(|d+1|)>>. The next estimate bounds repeated
  insertion before any operator extension.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Repeated reflected words><label|lem:F4> There is
    <math|C<rsub|0>=C<rsub|0>*<around|(|d,R|)>\<gtr\>0> such that every
    nonempty length-<math|n> word<nbsp><math|w> with these bounds and
    buffer<nbsp><math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1> in a slab of
    width<nbsp><math|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>> admits a width
    <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>> in which<nbsp><math|w>
    has buffer<nbsp><math|r/2> and, for every separated exterior
    word<nbsp><math|v> with positive times,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|<around|(|A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>*A<rsub|w>|)><rsup|j>*v|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|OS>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|v>*<around|[|C<rsub|0><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|Q*n>|]><rsup|4*j>*<space|2em><around|(|j\<geqslant\>0|)>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|B<rsub|v>=B<rsub|v>*<around|(|w,r,s|)>\<less\>\<infty\>> is
    independent of<nbsp><math|j>.
  </lemma>

  The operators use the chosen width<nbsp><math|s>, and<nbsp><math|s>,
  <math|C<rsub|0>> and<nbsp><math|Q> precede<nbsp><math|v>. The main
  insertions occupy cells of a size chosen from<nbsp><math|r> alone and the
  exterior insertions a finer aligned tiling, so the cost of the exterior
  does not grow with the number<nbsp><math|j> of repetitions.

  <\proof>
    Enlarge the coefficient ball to contain all signed and conjugated images
    in degrees at most<nbsp><math|d>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3> with
    <math|\<varepsilon\>=1> gives <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> and
    <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0> such that their centered seminorms at
    levels <math|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>\<geqslant\>k<rsub|\<ast\>>> are at most
    <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>>,
    <math|d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>=d+1>; this precedes <math|n,r,w>. Choose the
    first <math|m\<geqslant\>k<rsub|\<ast\>>> with
    <math|a<rsub|m>\<less\>r/32> and put <math|s=a<rsub|m>*<around|\<lfloor\>|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>/a<rsub|m>|\<rfloor\>>>.
    As<nbsp><math|w> is nonempty with buffer<nbsp><math|r>,
    <math|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>\<geqslant\>2*r>, so
    <math|0\<less\>s\<leqslant\>h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>> and
    <math|h<rsub|<math-up|sl>>-s\<less\>a<rsub|m>>, and<nbsp><math|w> has
    buffer<nbsp><math|r/2> in width<nbsp><math|s>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-thickness>, <math|a<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|1>*r<rsup|2>>
    with <math|c<rsub|1>\<gtr\>0> depending on<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    Fix <math|0\<less\>\<alpha\>\<leqslant\>1> with
    <math|20*\<alpha\>\<less\>c<rsub|1>>, and choose the first
    <math|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>\<geqslant\>k<rsub|\<ast\>>> with
    <math|a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>\<less\>\<alpha\>*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|2>>.
    Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:F-repeat-scales>20*n*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>\<less\>a<rsub|m>,<space|2em>a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>\<geqslant\>c<rsub|2>*<around|(|r/n|)><rsup|4>,<space|2em><around|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>|)><rsup|n>\<leqslant\><around|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*c<rsub|2><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>|)><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|4*d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>*n>.
    </equation>

    Expanding the OS norm gives a correlation of <math|4*j*n> main insertions
    and two copies of<nbsp><math|v>. The main insertions are separated from
    one another and from the exterior by at least <math|r/2\<gtr\>a<rsub|m>>.
    Translations by multiples of<nbsp><math|s> preserve residues
    modulo<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m><Z><rsup|4>>, so the main anchors have at
    most<nbsp><math|2*n> residue patterns, those of<nbsp><math|w>
    and<nbsp><math|\<theta\>*w>, for every<nbsp><math|j>. For
    nonempty<nbsp><math|v>, let <math|t<rsub|min>\<gtr\>0> be its minimum
    time and choose <math|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>\<geqslant\>k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>
    with <math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>\<less\>2*t<rsub|min>> and
    <math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>> below every distance between distinct
    anchors of<nbsp><math|v>. The two exterior copies have
    times<nbsp><math|\<pm\><around|(|2*j*s+t|)>>, <math|t> a time
    of<nbsp><math|v>, so they are more than
    <math|2*t<rsub|min>\<gtr\>a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>> apart for
    every<nbsp><math|j>. Choose <math|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>\<geqslant\>m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>,
    beyond the exterior reference levels, with
    <math|12<around|\||v|\|>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>>\<less\>a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>>,
    and let <math|X\<geqslant\>0> bound the centered seminorms of the
    exterior labels and their images at level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>>
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>). Neither <math|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>
    nor<nbsp><math|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>> depends on<nbsp><math|j>.

    The coarse tiling has side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>> and separates the main
    insertions; the fine tiling has side<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>>,
    shares its origin and separates the exterior insertions. Their side ratio
    is odd, so coarse reflections preserve the fine grid and its interior
    margins. The strip count for the <math|2*n> main residues
    modulo<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m>> with <math|w=5*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>>,
    possible by<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-repeat-scales>, keeps the main anchors at
    distance at least<nbsp><math|5*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>> from the
    coarse boundaries. A further translation by less
    than<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>>
    puts the <math|2<around|\||v|\|>> exterior anchors at distance at
    least<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>>> from the fine boundaries,
    since <math|12<around|\||v|\|>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|v>>>\<less\>a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>>,
    and leaves the main anchors margin at
    least<nbsp><math|3*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>>>. The main anchors occupy
    distinct coarse cells, none containing an exterior anchor.

    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> to the coarse tiling, treating the product
    of the exterior factors in one coarse cell as one observable.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> bounds each main factor by
    <math|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>>.
    For a coarse cell with<nbsp><math|n<rsub|C>> exterior factors, reflect
    their product into every coarse cell; in each copy the exterior anchors
    are more than<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|v>>>> apart and occupy
    distinct fine cells, so<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> for the fine tiling and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a> bound the coarse seminorm of that product
    by<nbsp><math|X<rsup|n<rsub|C>>>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|<around|(|A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>*A<rsub|w>|)><rsup|j>*v|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|OS>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>>*<around|(|B<rsub|\<ast\>>*a<rsub|k<rsub|<math-up|w>>><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>|)><rsup|4*j*n>.
    </equation*>

    For each fixed<nbsp><math|j>, lattice approximations of the anchors
    preserving these strict margins, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.2>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf> pass this bound to the limit; no cutoff
    threshold uniform in<nbsp><math|j> is needed. Take <math|B<rsub|v>=max
    <around|(|1,X<rsup|2<around|\||v|\|>>|)>>,
    <math|C<rsub|0>=B<rsub|\<ast\>>*c<rsub|2><rsup|-d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>>
    and <math|Q=4*d<rsub|<math-up|pow>>>, and
    use<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-repeat-scales>. For empty<nbsp><math|v> omit its
    factors and take <math|B<rsub|v>=1>.
  </proof>

  <section|Free fields, Ward identities and rotations><label|sec:partII-G>

  The only rotations that preserve the lattice, the Wilson action and the
  block-spin transformation of Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-B>\U<reference|sec:partII-F>
  are those of the hypercubic group<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>. Continuous
  rotational covariance of the limit is therefore an output of the
  construction. This section obtains it from a conserved stress tensor and
  proves Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>,
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST> and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>.
  The argument has three tasks.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(1)><em|Find the conserved line.> The Maxwell comparison
    identifies the fields of degrees four and five with their free
    counterparts. A three-dimensional space of candidate tensors maps by
    divergence onto a two-dimensional space of vectors, so conserved tensors
    form a line (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>).

    <item*|(2)><em|Make the translation shell act as differentiation.> Shell
    fluxes of a member of that line define charge maps on the field spaces
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.3>). Finite free Ward tests identify the
    translation charge with a multiple of differentiation
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>).

    <item*|(3)><em|Identify the angular action and recover rotations.>
    Coordinate reflection positivity controls the angular flux at infinity
    for collinear configurations, with no lower bound on the energy spectrum
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-collinear-flux>). Analyticity extends the
    angular identity to all configurations; finite internal tests remove the
    defect in the bracket relations, and the angular charges integrate to an
    <math|O<around|(|4|)>> action (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>).
  </enumerate>

  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G.1> treats the free field,
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:G-lattice>\U<reference|sec:partII-G.2> the
  comparison and the conserved line, Section<nbsp><reference|sec:G-coordinate>
  the coordinate reflection spaces, and Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G.3>
  the Ward identities. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:G-coordinate> uses no
  continuous rotation; continuous rotations are first available in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>. The free calculation of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1> also supplies the positivity used in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests>; its proof uses no interacting
  input.

  Two estimates go beyond convergence at fixed separated points. The lattice
  comparison must control tests uniformly in their supremum norm, including
  near configurations with a shared coordinate; narrow strips around those
  configurations are removed, with different scales for the kernel and for
  its variation. Later, conservation removes the inner boundaries in the
  angular identity but not the boundary at infinity, which an anisotropic
  cutoff and the coordinate spectral estimates remove.

  Except in the free calculation, fix the table, volume refinement, quotient
  fields and calculus of Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D09>,
  <reference|def:D10> and<nbsp><reference|def:D11> and
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>\U<reference|lem:F3>. Subsequences below
  change only comparison radii. Write <math|\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>> for the
  inclusions and <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>> for field differentiation.

  <subsection|Free fields><label|sec:partII-G.1>

  Put <math|z=x-y> and <math|<math-ss|h><rsub|a*b><around|(|z|)>=\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*\<Gamma\><around|(|z|)>>.
  The curvature covariance in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11> is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-curvature>C<rsub|a*b,i*j><around|(|z|)>=-\<delta\><rsub|b*j><math-ss|h><rsub|a*i>+\<delta\><rsub|b*i><math-ss|h><rsub|a*j>+\<delta\><rsub|a*j><math-ss|h><rsub|b*i>-\<delta\><rsub|a*i><math-ss|h><rsub|b*j>,<space|2em><math-ss|h><rsub|a*b>=<frac|8*z<rsub|a>*z<rsub|b>-2*\<delta\><rsub|a*b><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>|4*\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|6>>.
  </equation>

  Different colours have zero covariance. Wick correlations sum products of
  covariances over the pairings that join factors of different insertions;
  for instance <math|<maxwellcorr><rsub|2>(:F<rsub|1>*F<rsub|2>:,:F<rsub|3>*F<rsub|4>:)=C<rsub|13>*C<rsub|24>+C<rsub|14>*C<rsub|23>>,
  and no covariance at zero separation enters. Set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-free-tensor>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|a*b,i*j>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|i*j><rsup|\<alpha\>>:,<space|2em>Q<rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|c>Q<rsub|a*c,b*c>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>=<big|sum><rsub|a>Q<rsub|a*a>,>>|<row|<cell|<maxwellstress><rsub|a*b>>|<cell|=-Q<rsub|a*b>+<tfrac|1|4>\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>,<space|2em><curvaturesquare>=<tfrac|1|4>Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  The minus sign is fixed by the shell convention in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>.

  For the coordinate reflection <math|s<rsub|a>>, strict reflected positivity
  of <math|u\<in\><maxwellspace|d>> means

  <\equation*>
    <maxwellcorr><rsub|2><around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|s<rsub|a>|)>*<wide|u|\<bar\>>,u;s<rsub|a>*x,x|)>\<gtr\>0*<space|2em><around|(|x=t*e<rsub|a>,t\<gtr\>0|)>.
  </equation*>

  Write <math|<math-ss|L><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K|)>> for the
  derivative at zero of <math|R<rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>>.
  The two free shell Ward identities, with the fluxes of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>, are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-free-shells>\<cal-P\><rsub|b><rsup|T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>>=S<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*u|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-v\><around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|K><rsup|T<rsup|<math-up|Max>>>=S<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|L><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K|)>*u|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-v\><around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation>

  The next lemma gives the nondegeneracy of free correlation tests and the
  normalization used in the interacting comparison.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Free positivity and stress><label|lem:G1.1>

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)>The Maxwell table satisfies E1\UE7, the field calculus and
      <math|O<around|(|4|)>> covariance.

      <item*|(ii)>Every <math|u\<in\><maxwellspace|d>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>>
      has strict reflected positivity.

      <item*|(iii)>The centered real symmetric <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial
      conserved degree-four tensors form the line <math|<R><maxwellstress>>,
      and <math|<maxwellstress>> satisfies<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-free-shells>.

      <item*|(iv)>For <math|x\<ne\>y>,

      <\equation*>
        <maxwellcorr><rsub|2><around|(|<curvaturesquare>,<curvaturesquare>;x,y|)>=<frac|3<dimG>|\<pi\><rsup|4>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|8>>.
      </equation*>
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|(i)> Formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-curvature> satisfies Bianchi and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*C<rsub|a*b,i*j>=0> off zero,
    so Wick correlations descend to the on-shell quotient. It also gives
    permutation symmetry, reality, units, translations and orthogonal
    covariance. Each differentiated contraction is homogeneous of a fixed
    negative degree and bounded on the unit sphere; a finite product of these
    bounds proves E6\UE7. Differentiating the pairings proves the derivative
    law; inclusions retain the same polynomial. Reflection positivity E4\UE5
    is proved after<nbsp>(ii).

    <em|(ii)> Kelvin inversion <math|z\<mapsto\>z<rsup|\<vee\>>=z/<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>
    makes the reflected two-point pairing of curvature jets triangular with
    positive pivots<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-pivots>; strict positivity then follows
    from Wick's theorem. Let <math|<math-up|Harm><rsub|j>> be the real space
    of <math|<g>>-valued homogeneous polynomial two-forms of
    degree<nbsp><math|j> satisfying <math|d*f=\<delta\>*f=0>. Its Frobenius
    inner product identifies it with the dual of the order-<math|j> on-shell
    curvature jets. Use Taylor coefficients <math|D<rsup|j>/j>! and the inner
    product summing both ordered form indices and ordered derivative indices;
    <math|p<around|(|\<partial\>|)>> denotes contraction with this normalized
    derivative. For <math|z\<ne\>0> put <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|z>=\<b-1\><rsub|4>-2*z*z<rsup|<math-ss|t>>/<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2>>.
    Let <math|R<rsub|p><around|(|y|)>> be the covariance of
    <math|F<around|(|y|)>> with the jet probe<nbsp><math|p> at zero. Then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-kelvin>R<rsub|p><around|(|y|)>=\<varsigma\><rsub|j><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-4>*<around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|y>\<otimes\>\<cal-R\><rsub|y>|)>*p<around|(|y<rsup|\<vee\>>|)>,<space|2em>\<varsigma\><rsub|j>=<frac|<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>*<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j>|\<pi\><rsup|2>>.
    </equation>

    Indeed <math|p<around|(|\<partial\>|)>*\<Gamma\>=<around|(|-2|)><rsup|j><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-2*j>*p<around|(|y|)>*\<Gamma\>>;
    induction proves this, since traces of the coefficient tensor vanish by
    harmonicity. In the two curls, terms containing
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*p<rsub|a*b>> and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*p<rsub|b*c>+\<partial\><rsub|b>*p<rsub|c*a>+\<partial\><rsub|c>*p<rsub|a*b>>
    vanish. The remaining radial derivatives use
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>y<rsub|a>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*p=j*p> and
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-2*j-2>=-<around|(|2*j+2|)>*y<rsub|a><around|\||y|\|><rsup|-2*j-4>>,
    and give <math|\<varsigma\><rsub|0>=2/\<pi\><rsup|2>>,
    <math|<around|(|j+1|)>*\<varsigma\><rsub|j+1>=-2*<around|(|j+3|)>*\<varsigma\><rsub|j>>.

    For towers <math|p=<around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>> and
    <math|q=<around|(|q<rsub|j>|)>>, let <math|B<rsub|z>*<around|(|p,q|)>> be
    their two-site covariance at separation<nbsp><math|z>, with
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|z>> acting on all tensor indices of the second
    probe; this is the reflected jet pairing at <math|\<pm\>z/2>. Define the
    triangular map<nbsp><math|\<sigma\><rsub|z>> by replacing a single
    order-<math|j> probe<nbsp><math|p> with the tower whose order-<math|k>
    component is <math|<around|(|\<varsigma\><rsub|j>/\<varsigma\><rsub|k>|)>*T<rsub|k><around|[|p|(>\<cdot\>-z<rsup|\<vee\>>)]>,
    <math|k\<leqslant\>j>, where <math|T<rsub|k>> is the homogeneous Taylor
    term at zero. Its generated field is the right side
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-kelvin> with <math|p*<around|(|y<rsup|\<vee\>>-z<rsup|\<vee\>>|)>>.
    Since <math|D<around|(|z<rsup|\<vee\>>|)>=<around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2>*\<cal-R\><rsub|z>>,
    its Taylor terms at<nbsp><math|z> below<nbsp><math|j> vanish, and its top
    term is <math|\<varsigma\><rsub|j><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2*j-4>*\<cal-R\><rsub|z>*p>.
    Symmetry removes the entries above the diagonal. Thus

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-pivots>B<rsub|z>*<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|z>*p,\<sigma\><rsub|z>*q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j><frac|<around|(|j+1|)>*<around|(|j+2|)>*2<rsup|j>|\<pi\><rsup|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*j+4>>*<around|\<langle\>|p<rsub|j>,q<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation>

    Evaluating the second order-<math|j> probe supplies
    <math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|j>>, since its positional derivatives are
    opposite to derivatives of the separation; this cancels the sign
    in<nbsp><math|\<varsigma\><rsub|j>>. In coordinates diagonalizing this
    form, Wick pairing between two sites gives
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|X<rsup|\<mu\>>,X<rsup|\<nu\>>|\<rangle\>>=\<b-1\><rsub|\<mu\>=\<nu\>>*\<mu\>!<big|prod><rsub|i>b<rsub|i><rsup|\<mu\><rsub|i>>>
    with <math|b<rsub|i>\<gtr\>0>. Every polynomial uses a finite tower,
    proving strict positivity.

    For several positive-side sites the reflected curvature covariance is
    positive semidefinite: spatial Fourier transformation
    of<nbsp><math|\<Gamma\>> gives <math|e<rsup|-<around|\||p|\|>*<around|(|t+s|)>>/<around|(|2<around|\||p|\|>|)>>,
    and its two curls, with reflection of the form indices, give the sum of
    squares of the two transverse polarizations. Jet derivatives
    differentiate the same factors. Include the contractions within each
    positive-side word in its Fock vector; its reflected pairing is then the
    symmetric Fock pairing of this positive semidefinite form, so finite sums
    of words have nonnegative squared norm. On compact separated supports
    every pairing is continuous and bounded; integration gives E5 and point
    evaluation gives E4.

    <em|(iii)> Simplicity makes every invariant linear colour form zero and
    every invariant bilinear form a multiple of the fixed metric. Through
    weight four only the unit and the quadratics<nbsp><math|Q> remain, and
    sign changes and permutations give the basis

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>><rsub|a*b>=\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*Q<rsub|a*a>,<space|2em>Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><rsub|a*b>=\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*<around|(|Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>-2*Q<rsub|a*a>|)>,<space|2em>Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>><rsub|a*b>=<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|)>*Q<rsub|a*b>
    </equation*>

    of centered real symmetric tensors. Sign parity reduces their divergences
    to contractions of <math|F*\<partial\>*F> with the free
    index<nbsp><math|b>. The equations remove
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|d>\<partial\><rsub|d>*F<rsub|d*c>>, and Bianchi
    gives <math|2*<big|sum><rsub|c,d>F<rsub|c*d>*\<partial\><rsub|c>*F<rsub|b*d>=<big|sum><rsub|c,d>F<rsub|c*d>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|c*d>>.
    So the divergences lie in the plane spanned by

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|c>:F<rsub|b*c>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|b*c>\<assign\><tfrac|1|2>\<partial\><rsub|b>*Q<rsub|b*b>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|c\<less\>d,c,d\<ne\>b>:F<rsub|c*d>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|c*d>\<assign\><tfrac|1|4>\<partial\><rsub|b>*<around|(|Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>-2*Q<rsub|b*b>|)>,
    </equation*>

    with colour sums understood. Test the first components of the divergences
    of <math|Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>,Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>,Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>>
    (columns) at<nbsp><math|e<rsub|1>> against
    <math|Q<rsub|12,12><around|(|0|)>> and <math|Q<rsub|23,23><around|(|0|)>>
    (rows), dividing by <math|<dimG>/<around|(|4*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>>.
    For example <math|S<rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|2>*<around|(|Q<rsub|11>*<around|(|t*e<rsub|1>|)>,Q<rsub|12,12><around|(|0|)>|)>=2<dimG>/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|4>*t<rsup|8>|)>>,
    whose derivative gives the first entry;
    differentiating<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-curvature> gives the others:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-divergence-matrix><matrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|-256>|<cell|0>|<cell|128>>|<row|<cell|0>|<cell|-512>|<cell|-128>>>>>.
    </equation>

    Its first two columns are independent, so divergence maps the span of the
    <math|Z<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>> onto this plane. The test of
    <math|Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>><rsub|12>> against <math|Q<rsub|13,23>> gives
    <math|-16>, and zero for <math|Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>,Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>>,
    so the three tensors are independent. The kernel is the line of
    <math|<tfrac|1|2>Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>-<tfrac|1|4>Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>+Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>=-<maxwellstress>>.

    To compute its charge, polarize<nbsp><math|<maxwellstress>> between a
    smooth Maxwell wave<nbsp><math|f> and the field<nbsp><math|g> generated
    by a probe at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>>. Replace <math|\<Gamma\>> by
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>> with
    <math|-\<Delta\>*\<Gamma\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>=\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>,
    a smooth approximate identity. For a probe <math|F<rsub|i*j>> the
    divergence of<nbsp><math|g> is <math|\<delta\><rsub|b*j>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>-\<delta\><rsub|b*i>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>.
    The polarized tensor is <math|t<rsub|a*b>=-<big|sum><rsub|c><around|(|f<rsub|a*c>*g<rsub|b*c>+g<rsub|a*c>*f<rsub|b*c>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>\<delta\><rsub|a*b>*<big|sum><rsub|i,j>f<rsub|i*j>*g<rsub|i*j>>,
    and Bianchi gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*t<rsub|a*b>=-<big|sum><rsub|c>f<rsub|b*c>*<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*g<rsub|a*c>>.
    With the minus sign of the shell integral,
    <math|<big|int>\<chi\>*v<rsub|b>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*t<rsub|a*b>=-<big|int>\<chi\>*v<rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*c>*<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|c*j>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>-\<delta\><rsub|c*i>*\<partial\><rsub|j>*\<rho\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|)>>,
    and a second integration by parts gives

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|i>*<around|(|v<rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*j>|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>-\<partial\><rsub|j>*<around|(|v<rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*i>|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>=<around|(|<math-up|Lie><rsub|v><space|0.17em>f|)><rsub|i*j><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>.
    </equation*>

    No derivative of<nbsp><math|\<chi\>> appears at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>>,
    and the contraction with<nbsp><math|D*v> vanishes by symmetry. For
    <math|v=K*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-x<rsub|0>|)>> this is
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|c><around|(|K<rsub|c*i>*f<rsub|c*j>+K<rsub|c*j>*f<rsub|i*c>|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>>,
    the generator of the free action on curvature; for constant<nbsp><math|v>
    it is <math|v\<cdot\>\<nabla\>*f>. Higher probes follow by
    differentiating in<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>>. In a Wick contraction, the
    terms in which both stress legs end at<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>> contribute
    zero to the shell flux: dilation by<nbsp><math|2> multiplies their shell
    integral by <math|2<rsup|3+p-8-j-l>\<ne\>1> (probe
    orders<nbsp><math|j,l>, weight degree <math|p=0,1>), while the flux is
    independent of the cutoff. Terms with both legs outside vanish by
    conservation. The remaining contractions give the
    derivation<nbsp><math|<math-up|Lie><rsub|v>>,
    proving<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-free-shells>.

    <em|(iv)> At<nbsp><math|z=r*e<rsub|1>> the six unordered curvature
    components have diagonal covariances <math|\<pm\><around|(|\<pi\><rsup|2>*r<rsup|4>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    Since <math|<curvaturesquare>=<tfrac|1|2><big|sum><rsub|a\<less\>b,\<alpha\>>:<around|(|F<rsub|a*b><rsup|\<alpha\>>|)><rsup|2>>:,
    its two-point Wick sum is <math|<tfrac|1|4>\<cdot\>2\<cdot\>6<dimG>/<around|(|\<pi\><rsup|4>*r<rsup|8>|)>>.
    Orthogonal covariance gives the formula for every<nbsp><math|z\<ne\>0>.
  </proof>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-9.svg|282.83479pt|118.478526pt||><label|fig:G-shell>>|The
  shell flux of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>. The
  cutoff<nbsp><math|\<chi\>> equals one near the insertion
  <math|A<around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>> and vanishes near the spectators
  <math|B<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>; the current is integrated against
  <math|\<nabla\>*\<chi\>> on the shaded annulus. Conservation makes the flux
  independent of<nbsp><math|\<chi\>>, so the annulus may shrink
  towards<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>> or grow until it meets a spectator. It says
  nothing about flux through large spheres. The vanishing of the total
  angular flux at infinity is proved separately: on coordinate lines in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-collinear-flux>, and for all configurations in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>.>

  The sign convention can be seen in one free example
  (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:G-shell>). By
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>(iv), <math|<maxwellcorr><rsub|2><around|(|<curvaturesquare><around|(|x|)>,<curvaturesquare><around|(|y|)>|)>=c*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|-8>>
  with <math|c=3<dimG>/\<pi\><rsup|4>>. The translation shell Ward
  identity<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-free-shells> and the free derivative law give,
  for a shell around<nbsp><math|x>,

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-P\><rsub|b><rsup|<maxwellstress>>=\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|b>>*<around*|(|c*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|-8>|)>=-8*c*<space|0.17em><around|(|x<rsub|b>-y<rsub|b>|)>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|-10>.
  </equation*>

  Replacing <math|<maxwellstress>> by <math|\<lambda\><maxwellstress>>
  multiplies this flux by<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>>, so the requirement that the
  flux be the derivative fixes both the normalization and the sign of the
  stress tensor. This is a free-field calculation; nothing is asserted here
  about the form of interacting correlations.

  The next lemmas concern only the invariant polynomial algebra of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D11>, realized by the Taylor sections of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>. A curvature jet of order<nbsp><math|j> has
  weight<nbsp><math|j+2>. Let <math|\<frak-P\>> be the complex polynomial
  algebra on all on-shell jets, <math|\<frak-P\><rsub|\<leqslant\>j>> its
  subalgebra using orders at most<nbsp><math|j>, and
  <math|\<frak-P\><rsup|G>> the invariant subalgebra. The invariant
  polynomials of weight at most<nbsp><math|d> identify
  with<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d>>. For a spectator degree<nbsp><math|d> put

  <\equation*>
    \<frak-N\><rsub|d>=<around|{|P\<in\>\<frak-P\>:S<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|P<around|(|0|)>,u<rsub|1><around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|n><around|(|x<rsub|n>|)>|)>=0<text|
    for all >n,u<rsub|i>\<in\><maxwellspace|d>,<around|(|0,x|)>\<in\><Conf|n+1>|}>,
  </equation*>

  with every polynomial Wick ordered at its own site. The degree
  of<nbsp><math|P> is unrestricted. The next result says that spectators of
  one bounded degree detect every invariant polynomial, however high its jet
  order.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Detection by bounded spectators><label|lem:G-detection> There is
    <math|d<rsub|0>\<in\><N>> such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-detection>\<frak-N\><rsub|d<rsub|0>>\<cap\>\<frak-P\><rsup|G>=<around|{|0|}>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  The proof has two stages. At a fixed jet order, finite generation and
  reflected positivity give a bound on the spectator degree. To make the
  bound independent of the jet order, a quadratic spectator isolates a
  highest-order jet derivative of<nbsp><math|P> by its harmonic pole, and
  induction on weight removes that jet order.

  The extraction rests on the difference between a single harmonic pole and a
  product of poles. In four dimensions <math|K<around|(|z|)>=\<partial\><rsub|1>*\<partial\><rsub|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2>=8*z<rsub|1>*z<rsub|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-6>>
  is harmonic and homogeneous of degree <math|-4>; multiplication by
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|4>> leaves <math|8*z<rsub|1>*z<rsub|2><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2>>,
  which is not analytic at zero, and only <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|6>>
  clears it. The product of two poles <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2>\<cdot\><around|\||z|\|><rsup|-2>=<around|\||z|\|><rsup|-4>>
  has the same degree but is already cleared
  by<nbsp><math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|4>>. In general a product of poles of
  orders <math|r<rsub|1>,r<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>2> is cleared by
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r<rsub|1>+r<rsub|2>-2|)>>>, while a
  harmonic pole of order <math|r<rsub|1>+r<rsub|2>> needs
  <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r<rsub|1>+r<rsub|2>-1|)>>>.

  <\render-proof|Proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-detection>>
    <em|Step 1: bounded jet order.> Fix<nbsp><math|j>. The
    algebra<nbsp><math|\<frak-P\><rsub|\<leqslant\>j>> is a polynomial
    algebra on finitely many variables, hence Noetherian by the Hilbert basis
    theorem<nbsp><cite-detail|Stacks2026|Lemma<nbsp>10.31.1>. Its ideal
    generated by invariant polynomials of positive ordinary degree therefore
    has finitely many invariant, ordinary-homogeneous
    generators<nbsp><math|f<rsub|\<alpha\>>>. Every invariant
    ordinary-homogeneous polynomial is a sum of products of invariant
    homogeneous polynomials of weight at most some<nbsp><math|b<rsub|j>>:
    write <math|P=<big|sum>a<rsub|\<alpha\>>*f<rsub|\<alpha\>>>, keep only
    terms of total degree<nbsp><math|deg P>, and replace
    <math|a<rsub|\<alpha\>>> by its gauge average, whose degree is smaller;
    induction on the degree concludes.

    Spectators of degree<nbsp><math|b<rsub|j>> detect:
    <math|\<frak-N\><rsub|b<rsub|j>>\<cap\>\<frak-P\><rsup|G>\<cap\>\<frak-P\><rsub|\<leqslant\>j>=<around|{|0|}>>.
    Otherwise take its lowest nonzero ordinary-homogeneous
    part<nbsp><math|P<rsub|r>> and split a product of homogeneous factors of
    total ordinary degree<nbsp><math|r> into distinct nearby spectator sites.
    Every leg of<nbsp><math|P> must pair with a spectator leg, so terms with
    internal spectator contractions, or with higher-degree parts
    of<nbsp><math|P>, vanish. As the spectator sites coalesce, Wick's rule
    gives the two-site pairing of<nbsp><math|P<rsub|r>> with their ordinary
    product. Each factor has weight at most<nbsp><math|b<rsub|j>>, so for
    <math|P\<in\>\<frak-N\><rsub|b<rsub|j>>> this limit is zero. Choose the
    product sum to be the reflection of<nbsp><math|<wide|P<rsub|r>|\<bar\>>>;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>(ii) makes this pairing strictly positive,
    a contradiction.

    <em|Step 2: pole extraction.> Put <math|g=<dimG>> and choose
    <math|d<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>max <around|{|b<rsub|g>,2*g+5|}>>. In a real
    colour basis write <math|X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)>> for the jet
    coordinate tested by a scalar order-<math|j> probe<nbsp><math|p>. For
    scalar probes<nbsp><math|p,q> of orders <math|k,l>, the invariant
    quadratic <math|<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:X<rsub|k,\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)>*X<rsub|l,\<alpha\>><around|(|q|)>>:
    has weight<nbsp><math|k+l+4>. Its collision with a polynomial using jets
    through order<nbsp><math|j> has, in the coefficient where just the
    order-<math|k> leg contracts at order<nbsp><math|j>, the polynomial

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-collision><big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>X<rsub|l,\<alpha\>><around|(|q|)>*<big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>>C<rsub|k,j>*<around|(|p,e<rsub|\<mu\>>;z|)><frac|\<partial\>*P|\<partial\>*X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>><around|(|e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>,
    </equation>

    where <math|<around|(|e<rsub|\<mu\>>|)>> is a basis of order-<math|j>
    probes and <math|C<rsub|k,j>> their differentiated curvature covariance.

    <em|Claim.> If <math|P\<in\>\<frak-N\><rsub|d<rsub|0>>>,
    <math|l\<less\>k> and <math|k+l+4\<leqslant\>d<rsub|0>>,
    then<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collision> lies
    in<nbsp><math|\<frak-N\><rsub|d<rsub|0>>>. Put <math|r=4+k+j>. Tested
    against fixed exterior spectators, the term<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collision>
    is harmonic and homogeneous of degree<nbsp><math|-r> in<nbsp><math|z>, so
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r-1|)>>> times it is analytic at
    zero. The other terms, expanded in Taylor series at each singular order,
    give at order<nbsp><math|r> homogeneous functions that become analytic
    after multiplication by <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r-2|)>>>:
    a double contraction is a product of poles of orders
    <math|r<rsub|1>,r<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>2>, cleared by
    <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r<rsub|1>+r<rsub|2>-2|)>>>, and
    Taylor terms keep this improvement. The contraction of the order-<math|l>
    leg alone has order at most <math|4+l+j\<less\>r>. The claim now follows
    from:

    <\equation*>
      <text|a harmonic <math|K>, homogeneous of degree
      <math|-r\<leqslant\>-2>, with <math|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*<around|(|r-2|)>>*K>
      analytic at <math|0>, vanishes.>
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|\<Delta\>*<around|(|<around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*s>*K|)>=4*s*<around|(|s+1-r|)><around|\||z|\|><rsup|2*s-2>*K>
    in four dimensions; successive Laplacians show that<nbsp><math|K> extends
    analytically, and negative homogeneity makes it zero. Expand the
    collision through an order exceeding <math|8+k+l+2*j>. Since its
    correlation with the quadratic test and further bounded-degree spectators
    vanishes identically, comparing successive radial homogeneities makes the
    order-<math|r> harmonic pole zero, so<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collision> belongs
    to<nbsp><math|\<frak-N\><rsub|d<rsub|0>>>.

    <em|Step 3: induction on weight.> Take <math|k=g+1> and
    <math|l=0,\<ldots\>,g-1>. For each<nbsp><math|l> choose a
    probe<nbsp><math|q<rsub|l>> and a jet value<nbsp><math|v<rsub|l>> with
    <math|q<rsub|l><around|(|v<rsub|l>|)>=1>. The matrix
    <math|<around|(|X<rsub|l,\<alpha\>><around|(|q<rsub|l>|)>|)><rsub|l,\<alpha\>>>
    has nonzero polynomial determinant: assigning<nbsp><math|v<rsub|l>> to
    colour<nbsp><math|l> and zero to the other colours in order<nbsp><math|l>
    makes it the identity. For <math|j\<gtr\>g> the
    polynomial<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collision> replaces an order-<math|j> jet by
    an order-<math|l> jet, so its weight is smaller, and the induction
    hypothesis makes it zero. Multiplication by the transposed cofactor
    matrix shows that every one-colour contraction
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collision> vanishes. As <math|z,p> vary, the forms
    <math|C<rsub|k,j>*<around|(|p,\<cdot\>;z|)>> span the dual of the
    order-<math|j> jets: their common annihilator generates a Maxwell wave
    whose order-<math|k> jets vanish everywhere, a polynomial of degree
    below<nbsp><math|k> with negative homogeneity, hence zero,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-kelvin> makes the probe zero. Hence every
    order-<math|j> partial derivative of<nbsp><math|P> vanishes. Descending
    from the top order removes all orders above<nbsp><math|g>; Step<nbsp>1
    with spectator bound<nbsp><math|b<rsub|g>> treats the rest.
  </render-proof>

  Let <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|d>> be the real space of complex linear tuples
  <math|<math-ss|X><rsub|a>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\><maxwellspace|d+1>> that
  commute with conjugation, kill the unit, are vector-covariant
  under<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, and send <math|<maxwellspace|e>>
  into<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|e+1>> for <math|e\<leqslant\>d>. For
  <math|<math-ss|X>\<in\>\<cal-C\><rsub|d>> the Ward sum is

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-E\><rsub|b><around|(|<math-ss|X>;\<b-u\>,x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,<math-ss|X><rsub|b>u<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|n>;x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Finitely many tests on lines determine its kernel.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite vector Ward tests><label|lem:G1-vector> For all
    sufficiently large<nbsp><math|d>, finitely many positional derivatives of
    <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b><around|(|<math-ss|X>;\<b-u\>,x|)>>, with
    <math|u<rsub|i>\<in\><maxwellspace|d>> and<nbsp><math|x> on a coordinate
    line perpendicular to<nbsp><math|e<rsub|b>>, define a real linear
    map<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\><rsub|d>> with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-free-tests>ker \<cal-T\><rsub|d>=<R>\<partial\><rsup|<math-up|Max>><space|1em><text|in
      >\<cal-C\><rsub|d>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Take <math|d\<geqslant\>d<rsub|0>> from
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-detection>. Suppose all the stated jets of
    the Ward sums of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|X>> vanish.

    <em|Step 1: continuation off the lines.> Free correlations are real
    analytic off the diagonals by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-curvature>, so every Ward
    sum vanishes near each testing line. For a separated configuration choose
    distinct ordered line coordinates respecting the strict order of its
    coordinates in that axis. The straight segment to the line configuration
    stays separated: strict inequalities persist, and coordinate ties are
    separated by the line coordinates. Analytic continuation along it gives
    <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b>=0> on<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>.

    <em|Step 2: weight four.> Let <math|<math-ss|J><rsub|j>> be the scalar
    on-shell order-<math|j> jet space. For
    <math|p\<in\><math-ss|J><rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>> put
    <math|U<around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:X<rsub|0,\<alpha\>><around|(|p|)><rsup|2>>:.
    Simplicity, the filtration and inversion parity force
    <math|<math-ss|X><rsub|b>U<around|(|p|)>> to be a colour contraction of
    one order-zero and one order-one jet, represented by a linear map
    <math|A<rsub|p>:<math-ss|J><rsub|0>\<to\><math-ss|J><rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>>.
    In the Ward identity of <math|U<around|(|p|)>,U<around|(|q|)>,U<around|(|w|)>>
    let the first two points coalesce along<nbsp><math|t*u> and multiply
    by<nbsp><math|t<rsup|5>>. The term with <math|<math-ss|X><rsub|b>> at the
    distant point tends to zero, and the two remaining triangular Wick
    contractions give, for every <math|X\<in\><math-ss|J><rsub|0>> and with
    <math|C<rsub|1,0>> taken in one colour,

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|1,0>*<around|(|A<rsub|p>*X,q;u|)>*<space|0.17em>q<around|(|X|)>=C<rsub|1,0>*<around|(|A<rsub|q>*X,p;u|)>*<space|0.17em>p<around|(|X|)>.
    </equation*>

    Arbitrary<nbsp><math|X> are reached by choosing the distant
    probe<nbsp><math|w> through the invertible order-zero
    covariance<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-kelvin>. If <math|p<around|(|X|)>=0>, choose
    <math|q<around|(|X|)>\<ne\>0>; varying <math|q,u> gives
    <math|A<rsub|p>*X=0>. Thus <math|A<rsub|p>*X=p<around|(|X|)>*f<rsub|b>*p>,
    and the same identity shows that <math|f<rsub|b>:<math-ss|J><rsub|0><rsup|\<ast\>>\<to\><math-ss|J><rsub|1><rsup|\<ast\>>>
    is real linear with <math|C<rsub|1,0>*<around|(|f<rsub|b>*p,q;u|)>=C<rsub|1,0>*<around|(|f<rsub|b>*q,p;u|)>>.

    <em|Calculation of<nbsp><math|f<rsub|b>>.> Write <math|e<rsup|i*j>> for
    the curvature coordinate and <math|\<partial\><rsub|h>*e<rsup|i*j>> for
    its first jet. For <math|f<rsub|1>*e<rsup|12>>, sign parity permits only
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|12>,\<partial\><rsub|3>*e<rsup|23>,\<partial\><rsub|4>*e<rsup|24>>;
    interchange of axes<nbsp><math|3,4> and
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>*e<rsup|i*2>=0> reduce their
    span to <math|<R>\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|12>>. For
    <math|f<rsub|1>*e<rsup|23>> the candidates
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|23>,\<partial\><rsub|2>*e<rsup|13>,\<partial\><rsub|3>*e<rsup|12>>
    reduce, by antisymmetry in axes<nbsp><math|2,3> and Bianchi, to
    <math|<R>\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|23>>. Permutations leave two
    constants, which agree because <math|C<rsub|1,0>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|1>*e<rsup|12>,e<rsup|23>;e<rsub|1>+e<rsub|3>|)>=-1/<around|(|2*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>\<ne\>0>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-curvature>. Therefore
    <math|f<rsub|b>=c<rsub|<math-up|jet>>*\<partial\><rsub|b>>, and since
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>*U<around|(|p|)>> has two identical terms,
    <math|<math-ss|X><rsub|b>=c*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|b>>
    on weight four with <math|c=c<rsub|<math-up|jet>>/2>.

    <em|Step 3: higher weights.> Set <math|<math-ss|X><rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|b>=<math-ss|X><rsub|b>-c*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsup|<math-up|Max>><rsub|b>>.
    Translation invariance makes its Ward sums zero, and it kills every free
    stress component. Insert into such a sum a free stress shell for the
    dilation field <math|v<around|(|y|)>=y-x<rsub|0>>, surrounding only the
    distinguished point. The term where <math|<math-ss|X><rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|b>>
    acts on the stress is zero. The other shell terms are given by the
    polarized calculation in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, now with
    this<nbsp><math|v>; the extra derivative of<nbsp><math|v> contracts with
    the trace of the free stress, which is zero
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-free-tensor>. The local Lie derivative of a two-form
    under dilation is <math|y\<cdot\>\<nabla\>+2>, and on jets it gives the
    weight operator <math|<math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|wt>>X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>>=<around|(|j+2|)>*X<rsub|j,\<alpha\>>>.
    Subtracting the Ward sum with distinguished
    label<nbsp><math|<math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|wt>>u> shows
    <math|<around|[|<math-ss|D><rsup|<math-up|wt>>,<math-ss|X><rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|b>|]>*u\<in\>\<frak-N\><rsub|d>>,
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-detection> makes it zero. So
    <math|<math-ss|X><rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|b>> preserves each weight.
    Inversion acts there by<nbsp><math|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|<math-up|weight>>>,
    whereas vector covariance makes <math|<math-ss|X><rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|b>>
    odd under inversion; hence <math|<math-ss|X><rsup|\<circ\>><rsub|b>=0>.

    <em|Step 4: finite selection.> Conversely translation invariance
    annihilates every test on<nbsp><math|\<partial\><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>.
    The real space<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsub|d>> is finite-dimensional;
    successively choose a test nonzero on the current kernel modulo this
    line. Each choice lowers its dimension, so finitely many tests have
    precisely the asserted kernel.
  </proof>

  The internal tests rest on one elementary fact about the plane generators
  <math|K<rsub|a*b>=E<rsub|b*a>-E<rsub|a*b>> of
  <math|<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>. Call a coordinate quarter
  turn the rotation <math|e<rsub|a>\<mapsto\>e<rsub|b>>,
  <math|e<rsub|b>\<mapsto\>-e<rsub|a>> in one coordinate plane.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Alternating planes><label|lem:G-planes> Let
    <math|\<beta\>:<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>\<times\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>\<to\>V>
    be real bilinear and alternating, with
    <math|\<beta\>*<around|(|h*K*h<rsup|-1>,h*L*h<rsup|-1>|)>=\<beta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>>
    for every coordinate quarter turn<nbsp><math|h>. Then <math|\<beta\>=0>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The quarter turns generate the determinant-one signed permutations: they
    project onto the transpositions of <math|S<rsub|4>>, and squares of
    quarter turns give the diagonal sign changes with two minus signs.
    Conjugation by a diagonal sign matrix<nbsp><math|s> multiplies
    <math|K<rsub|a*b>> by <math|s<rsub|a>*s<rsub|b>>; conjugation by a
    permutation matrix permutes the planes. <em|Overlapping planes:>
    <math|s=<math-up|diag><around|(|1,1,-1,-1|)>> fixes <math|K<rsub|12>> and
    reverses the sign of <math|K<rsub|13>>, so
    <math|\<beta\>*<around|(|K<rsub|12>,K<rsub|13>|)>=-\<beta\>*<around|(|K<rsub|12>,K<rsub|13>|)>=0>.
    <em|Disjoint planes:> the even permutation
    <math|<around|(|13|)><around|(|24|)>> interchanges <math|K<rsub|12>> and
    <math|K<rsub|34>>, so <math|\<beta\>*<around|(|K<rsub|12>,K<rsub|34>|)>=\<beta\>*<around|(|K<rsub|34>,K<rsub|12>|)>=-\<beta\>*<around|(|K<rsub|12>,K<rsub|34>|)>=0>.
    Every pair of distinct planes is carried to one of these two pairs by a
    signed permutation of determinant one, and
    <math|\<beta\>*<around|(|K,K|)>=0>; bilinearity gives <math|\<beta\>=0>.
  </proof>

  Let <math|\<cal-B\><rsub|d>> consist of alternating real-bilinear families
  <math|\<Delta\>:<with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)><rsup|2>\<to\><math-up|End><rsub|<C>><maxwellspace|d>>
  which commute with conjugation, kill the unit, preserve every lower-degree
  subspace, and satisfy

  <\equation*>
    R<rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|h|)>*\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>=\<Delta\>*<around|(|h*K*h<rsup|-1>,h*L*h<rsup|-1>|)>*R<rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|h|)><space|2em><around|(|h\<in\><hypercubic>,det
    h=1|)>.
  </equation*>

  The next result removes the defect in the rotation commutator.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite internal Ward tests><label|lem:G1-internal> For every
    <math|d\<geqslant\>4>, finitely many sums

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-erasure-test><big|sum><rsub|i>S<rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|u<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>*u<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>,u<rsub|n>;x|)>,<space|2em>u<rsub|i>\<in\><maxwellspace|d>,<space|1em>x\<in\><Conf|n>,
    </equation>

    separate<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\><rsub|d>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Suppose all these sums vanish and fix
    <math|<math-ss|Z>=\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>>. On the weight-four space
    let <math|b<rsub|x>*<around|(|u,v|)>=S<rsub|2><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|u,v;x,0|)>>.
    The order-zero Kelvin formula and Wick's rule give
    <math|b<rsub|x>*<around|(|u,v|)>=b<rsub|y>*<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|y>*\<cal-R\><rsub|x>|)>*u,v|)>>
    for <math|<around|\||x|\|>=<around|\||y|\|>\<ne\>0>. Both
    <math|<math-ss|Z>> and the orthogonal action preserve this space.
    Applying <math|b<rsub|x>*<around|(|<math-ss|Z>u,v|)>+b<rsub|x>*<around|(|u,<math-ss|Z>v|)>=0>
    at<nbsp><math|x> and<nbsp><math|y>, and nondegeneracy
    of<nbsp><math|b<rsub|y>>, shows that<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Z>> commutes
    with <math|R<rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|y>*\<cal-R\><rsub|x>|)>>,
    in particular with every coordinate quarter turn. With the signed
    covariance of<nbsp><math|\<Delta\>>, the function
    <math|<around|(|K,L|)>\<mapsto\>\<Delta\>*<around|(|K,L|)>*u> satisfies
    the hypothesis of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-planes> for each
    weight-four<nbsp><math|u>, so<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Z>> vanishes on weight
    four, including the stress.

    Insert a free angular stress shell around the distinguished point only,
    as in Step<nbsp>3 of the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1-vector>.
    Subtracting the Ward sum with the angularly differentiated label gives
    <math|<around|[|<math-ss|L><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|M|)>,<math-ss|Z>|]>*u\<in\>\<frak-N\><rsub|d>>.
    This polynomial has weight at most<nbsp><math|d>, so
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>(ii) makes it zero. Hence
    <math|<math-ss|Z>> commutes with all infinitesimal rotations, and
    differentiating <math|e<rsup|t<math-ss|L><around|(|M|)>><math-ss|Z>-<math-ss|Z>e<rsup|t<math-ss|L><around|(|M|)>>>
    gives commutation with coordinate quarter turns in every degree.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-planes> again gives <math|\<Delta\>=0>.
    Finite-dimensionality selects a finite separating subfamily by
    successively reducing the common kernel.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Lattice inputs><label|sec:G-lattice>

  The insertions are those of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>, with
  coefficient polynomials from Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D19> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1>. The inverse coefficient estimate of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> inverts the marked coefficient
  map; the corrected sources and normalized integral of
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-integral> bound the remainders; the
  uniform displacement estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement>
  controls positions, with coordinate separations at least four times the
  displacement margin. Chessboard seminorms are those of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D27>. Cutoff limits are taken in the
  order<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>, followed by the fixed extraction of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>; an ordered limit point of a bounded
  coefficient family is its limit along an ultrafilter containing the ordered
  tails.

  Fix arity and degree bounds <math|n\<geqslant\>2,D\<geqslant\>1> and
  <math|R\<geqslant\>1>. A basis word is a list of members of fixed real
  bases of <math|<maxwellspace|d>>, <math|d\<leqslant\>D>. Put

  <\equation*>
    K<rsub|R,m>=<around|{|x:<around|\||x|\|>\<leqslant\>R,<sepinf><around|(|x|)>\<geqslant\>R<rsup|-1>|}>,<space|2em>E<rsub|\<delta\>>=<around|{|x:<around|\||x<rsub|i><rsup|a>-x<rsub|j><rsup|a>|\|>\<gtr\>\<delta\>*<around|(|i\<ne\>j,a=1,\<ldots\>,4|)>|}>.
  </equation*>

  The comparison exponents <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|1>,\<alpha\><rsub|2>\<gtr\>0>
  and <math|c\<less\>0> have the following roles: <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>>
  sets the comparison radius, <math|\<alpha\><rsub|1>> the cells used for
  moment bounds, <math|\<alpha\><rsub|2>> the smaller cells used for
  displacement bounds, and<nbsp><math|c> the width of the excluded coordinate
  strips. They satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-exponents>\<alpha\><rsub|1>+6\<less\>\<alpha\><rsub|0>,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|2>+5\<less\>\<alpha\><rsub|0>+c,<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D*n+c\<less\>0,<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D*n-\<alpha\><rsub|0>-c\<less\>0.
  </equation>

  The first two inequalities place the chessboard cells; the last two make
  the excluded strips negligible. One explicit choice, with
  <math|n<rsub|max>=max <around|(|n,D|)>>, is
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|1>=7*n<rsub|max>*<around|(|n<rsub|max>+2|)>+1>,
  <math|\<kappa\><rsub|2>=6+\<kappa\><rsub|1>>,
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>=17+n<rsub|max>+\<kappa\><rsub|1>+\<kappa\><rsub|2>*n<rsub|max>*<around|(|n<rsub|max>+2|)>+\<kappa\><rsub|2>>,
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|1>=\<alpha\><rsub|0>-7>,
  <math|\<alpha\><rsub|2>=\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<kappa\><rsub|2>> and
  <math|c=-\<kappa\><rsub|1>>. Write <math|M<rsub|k>=<cutoffsize><rsub|k>>
  and set

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-balance>r<rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>*M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|0>>,<space|2em>\<frak-s\><rsub|k>=M<rsub|k><rsup|-\<alpha\><rsub|0>>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|k>=M<rsub|k><rsup|c>,<space|2em>R<rsup|<math-up|cell>><rsub|k>=C*M<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D>,<space|1em>R<rsup|<math-up|var>><rsub|k>=C*M<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D>,
  </equation>

  where <math|C\<geqslant\>1> depends only on <math|n,D,R> and the
  construction and is fixed in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-lattice>. Thus
  <math|r<rsub|k>*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>=a<rsub|k>>: the normalized grid is the
  physical construction grid. For an ordered limit point
  <math|c<rsub|k,d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>> of the lifts in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-lattice>, and the quotient
  <math|\<pi\><rsub|d>:V<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, define the rescaled correlation

  <\equation*>
    S<rsup|<math-up|sc>><rsub|k>*<around|(|\<b-u\>;x|)>=S<rsub|m>*<around|(|\<pi\><rsub|d<rsub|i>>*c<rsub|k,d<rsub|i>>*u<rsub|i>;r<rsub|k>*x|)>.
  </equation*>

  The following comparison uses one coefficient family for every word within
  the fixed bounds. Its constants may depend on these bounds, the word and
  the construction. The arity, degree and radius bounds are fixed first, then
  the level and the lifts; the estimates then hold for every basis word
  within these bounds.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Lattice comparison at small radii><label|lem:G-lattice> There are
    arbitrarily large levels<nbsp><math|k>, real
    <math|<hypercubic>>-equivariant lifts
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|k,N,p,d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>V<rsub|d>>
    preserving the degree-zero unit, and <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>\<to\>0>
    such that every ordered limit point of these lifts satisfies, for every
    basis word with <math|m\<leqslant\>n>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-lattice-input>

      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\||S<rsup|<math-up|sc>><rsub|k>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\><around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|cell>><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|on
        >K<rsub|R,m>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<nabla\>*S<rsup|<math-up|sc>><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>4*m<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|var>><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>/\<delta\><rsub|k>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|on
        >K<rsub|2*R,m>\<cap\>E<rsub|4*\<delta\><rsub|k>>,>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||S<rsup|<math-up|sc>><rsub|k>-S<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|Max>>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>>|<cell|>|<cell|<text|on
        ><around|(|\<frak-s\><rsub|k><Z>|)><rsup|4*m>\<cap\>K<rsub|2*R,m>\<cap\>E<rsub|0>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation>

    At fixed<nbsp><math|k> the lifts are bounded on ordered tails.
  </lemma>

  The lifts are chosen so that the rescaled insertions have the free Wick
  polynomials as Gaussian leading part<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-lift-equation>. A
  subset recursion removes the continuum Gaussian moments; for exact Gaussian
  variables each remainder has mean zero, and the source estimates of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E> make each remainder small in
  chessboard seminorm<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-source-root>. The chessboard
  inequality then gives the comparison on the grid, and the coarser
  normalizations <math|\<alpha\><rsub|1>,\<alpha\><rsub|2>> give the size and
  gradient bounds off the grid.

  <\proof>
    <em|Step 1: lifts.> Choose a real <math|<hypercubic>>-equivariant
    polynomial representative map <math|\<iota\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|free>>>
    for the on-shell quotient, using the Taylor sections of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1> and averaging a real splitting
    over<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>. Let <math|<math-ss|C><rsub|k,N,p,d>> be
    the marked coefficient map from level<nbsp><math|k> down
    to<nbsp><math|<reflevel|d>>, after the Wick-ordering correction of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>. Write
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|t><rsup|<math-up|wt>>> for multiplication of a
    weight-<math|j> monomial by<nbsp><math|t<rsup|j>>; the factor-count
    scaling<nbsp><math|t<rsup|<factorcount>>> is that of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>. The Gaussian field divided by the
    small-field threshold has scale

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-t\><rsub|k,N,p>=<around|(|t<rsub|k>*<sqrt|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>0\<less\>\<frak-t\><rsub|k,N,p><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>2*M<rsub|k>,
    </equation*>

    since <math|t<rsub|k>=M<rsub|k>/<sqrt|<refbeta><rsub|k>>> is the
    small-field threshold of Part<nbsp>I and
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,k>\<leqslant\>2<refbeta><rsub|k>>. Solve

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-lift-equation><math-ss|C><rsub|k,N,p,d>\<Lambda\><rsub|k,N,p,d>*u=\<frak-t\><rsub|k,N,p><rsup|-<factorcount>>*\<delta\><rsub|M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|0>>><rsup|<math-up|wt>>*\<iota\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|free>>*u.
    </equation>

    The solution exists and obeys

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-lift-bound><around|\||v|\|><rsub|1>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|k,d>*a<rsub|k><rsup|d><around|\||<math-ss|C><rsub|k,N,p,d>v|\|><rsub|1>,
    </equation>

    with <math|K<rsub|k,d>> independent of<nbsp><math|N,p> at
    fixed<nbsp><math|k>. Indeed, apply the inverse estimate of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> at each step <math|m+1\<to\>m>
    from level<nbsp><math|k> down to<nbsp><math|<reflevel|d>>, and the
    coefficient comparison in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>, whose relative error is
    less than<nbsp><math|1/4>, so its inverse costs at most<nbsp><math|2>.
    One may take <math|K<rsub|k,d>> to be twice the product of the finitely
    many column and block factors up to<nbsp><math|k>, enlarged by the fixed
    basis norms. The coefficient map is injective, hence invertible on its
    finite coefficient space, and its unit, conjugation and signed laws pass
    to the inverse. The bound on<nbsp><math|\<frak-t\><rsup|-1>> bounds the
    right side at fixed<nbsp><math|k>, so the lifts are bounded on cutoff
    tails.

    <em|Step 2: subset recursion.> Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>\U<reference|lem:E-source-integral>
    hold for an arity bound<nbsp><math|n\<geqslant\>2>, at most<nbsp><math|n>
    marks per cell and any compact position set with positive point
    separation; apply them to the interval<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> with
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>> and position
    set<nbsp><math|K<rsub|2*R,m>>. Their covariance and Taylor bounds are
    uniform on <math|K<rsub|2*R,m>>, whose point separations are at least
    <math|<around|(|2*R|)><rsup|-1>>, and need no lower bound on coordinate
    differences; only the reflected placement below
    requires<nbsp><math|E<rsub|0>>. Write
    <math|I<rsub|i>=O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>><around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|k,N,p,d<rsub|i>>*u<rsub|i>,r<rsub|k>*x<rsub|i>/a<rsub|N>|)>>
    at compatible lattice points. For a subset<nbsp><math|J> put
    <math|G<rsub|J>=S<rsub|<around|\||J|\|>><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*<around|(|<around|(|u<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\>J>;<around|(|x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|i\<in\>J>|)>>
    and define

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|\<varnothing\>>=1,<space|2em>R<rsub|I>=<big|prod><rsub|i\<in\>I>I<rsub|i>-<big|sum><rsub|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>J\<subseteq\>I>G<rsub|J>*R<rsub|I\<setminus\>J>.
    </equation*>

    This subtracts the full continuum Gaussian moment on every subset of
    slots; for exact continuum Gaussian variables its nonempty coefficients
    have expectation zero, by induction on<nbsp><math|<around|\||I|\|>>. The
    Wick-ordering correction and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-lift-equation> identify the
    leading Gaussian correlations with these continuum moments at the
    normalized grid points, as calculated in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>. Covariance, Taylor and
    localization errors remain in the regular and forced source terms.

    <em|Step 3: chessboard seminorm of the remainders.> The finite-set clause
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> gives arbitrarily late common terminal
    levels<nbsp><math|k> for the three exponents
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|1>,\<alpha\><rsub|2>>. Use the
    interval<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> with
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>>, its width level
    <math|w<rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|0>><around|(|k|)>=k-h<rsub|k><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>|)>-v<rsub|k>>
    and its centered tiling <math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|w<rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|0>><around|(|k|)>><around|(|0|)>>.
    Put <math|q<rsub|k>=log M<rsub|k>/<around|(|16*n|)>> and give each mark
    weight <math|e<rsup|2*q<rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|k><rsup|1/16>>. The
    weighted mode norm in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>
    multiplies mark weights over subsets, so coefficient extraction for a
    nonempty<nbsp><math|I> contributes <math|e<rsup|-2*q<rsub|k><around|\||I|\|>>>
    in each reflected cell, and the corrected-source iteration of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources> keeps this mark weight on
    the selected comparison intervals. Work in
    <math|<C><around|[|z<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,z<rsub|m>|]>/<around|(|z<rsub|i><rsup|2>|)>>
    and multiply by the reciprocal of the continuum free moment polynomial;
    its coefficient of<nbsp><math|z<rsub|I>> is
    exactly<nbsp><math|R<rsub|I>>. After the finite Gaussian contractions on
    every subset, the remaining source modes have zero leading Gaussian
    coefficient. In the source iteration, terms linear in these modes cost
    the finite stage constant, and every other connected term contains at
    least two residual modes and is bounded by their squared norm times a
    fixed power of<nbsp><math|M<rsub|k>>; this
    is<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-step>. The covariance and localization errors
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial> are below the
    corresponding source tolerance at entry<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-iteration>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>, applied to the
    coefficients<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-corrected-coefficients> of these finitely
    many subset modes, gives this mark weight with constants depending only
    on <math|n,D,R> and the construction; its sums are rooted at a mark and
    do not count the replicated cells.

    The normalized positive integral costs, per cell, at most the stage
    constant <math|Q<rsub|J<rsub|k>><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>>
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-comparison-cost> (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-integral>),
    which contains the term <math|1+n> for at most<nbsp><math|n> marks.
    Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-entries> imposes
    <math|Q<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>log
    M<rsub|t>/<around|(|16*n|)>> for each arity bound, hence
    <math|Q<rsub|J<rsub|k>><rsup|<math-up|cmp>>\<leqslant\>q<rsub|k>>; the
    entry is chosen after the finitely many source constants. Taking the root
    over the reflected cells gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-source-root><around|\<\|\|\>|R<rsub|I>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|w<rsub|\<alpha\><rsub|0>><around|(|k|)>><around|(|0|)>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|q<rsub|k>-2*q<rsub|k><around|\||I|\|>>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*n|)>>*<space|2em><around|(|\<varnothing\>\<ne\>I\<subseteq\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,m|}>|)>.
    </equation>

    The smallness comes from the corrected density at large coupling: its
    source terms stay bounded at a growing mark weight, while the unmarked
    normalization grows more slowly than that weight. The estimate concerns
    the complete reflected array, before absolute values are taken, and needs
    no independence of the insertions.

    <em|Step 4: comparison on the grid.> Distinct coordinate differences at
    normalized grid points are at least <math|\<frak-s\><rsub|k>>, so their
    physical differences are at least<nbsp><math|a<rsub|k>>. The reading map
    assigns the corresponding level-<math|k> sites without rounding, and the
    interval margin<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window-geometry> contains the inspected
    stencils and their reflected copies. Expanding the recursion,
    applying<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> to each<nbsp><math|R<rsub|I>> and
    bounding the free submoments on<nbsp><math|K<rsub|2*R,m>> gives
    <math|C<rsub|R,\<b-u\>>*M<rsub|k><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*n|)>>>, the last
    line of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-lattice-input>.

    <em|Step 5: size and variation off the grid.> At its own normalization
    the singleton comparison gives a chessboard seminorm at most
    <math|1+<around|\||S<rsub|1><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|u|)>|\|>>. For
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|1>>, changing normalization
    to<nbsp><math|M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|0>>> multiplies a
    weight-<math|j> coefficient by <math|M<rsub|k><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*j>>,
    so the finite coefficient sum bounds these seminorms
    by<nbsp><math|R<rsup|<math-up|cell>><rsub|k>> for<nbsp><math|C> large;
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|2>>
    gives<nbsp><math|R<rsup|<math-up|var>><rsub|k>>. Include every reflected
    basis label and its complex conjugation in these maxima. The two interval
    lengths of<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window> give, with the stage block
    ratio<nbsp><math|L>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-root-sides><frac|a<rsub|w<rsub|b><around|(|k|)>>|a<rsub|k>>=L<rsup|h<rsub|k><around|(|b|)>+v<rsub|k>>\<leqslant\>L<rsup|2>*M<rsub|k><rsup|b+2>*B<rsub|k><rsup|10>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|k><rsup|b+5>*<space|2em><around|(|b=\<alpha\><rsub|1>,\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>,
    </equation>

    after the stage entry, where <math|L> is fixed and
    <math|B<rsub|k>=M<rsub|k><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. So the
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|1>> cell is at most
    <math|a<rsub|k>*M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|1>+6>=o<around|(|r<rsub|k>|)>>
    and the <math|\<alpha\><rsub|2>> cell is
    <math|o<around|(|r<rsub|k>*\<delta\><rsub|k>|)>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-exponents>. The chessboard inequality bounds
    separated continuum moments by<nbsp><math|<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|cell>><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>>.
    The physical diameter on<nbsp><math|r<rsub|k>*K<rsub|2*R,m>> is at most
    <math|4*R*r<rsub|k>\<less\>L<rsub|p>/2> on the ordered tails, so the
    torus separation<nbsp><math|g<rsub|p>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement> is the minimum ordinary
    coordinate separation, which exceeds <math|4*r<rsub|k>*\<delta\><rsub|k>>
    on<nbsp><math|E<rsub|4*\<delta\><rsub|k>>>. The uniform clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement>, with margin
    <math|r<rsub|k>*\<delta\><rsub|k>> and the reflected seminorms included
    in<nbsp><math|R<rsup|<math-up|var>><rsub|k>>, gives
    <math|4*m<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|var>><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>/\<delta\><rsub|k>>
    after rescaling. The three normalizations use the same coefficient map
    and lifts and the same terminal levels; the finitely many cutoff tails
    can be intersected.

    <em|Step 6: limits.> Take any ordered limit point of the bounded lifts.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-conf> pass the
    moment estimates to the fixed table, and
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> with Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>
    passes the difference bound. No extraction of the table or the volume
    sequence is made.
  </proof>

  For the shell estimate take <math|\<varkappa\>\<in\><around|{|0,1|}>>,
  <math|A\<in\>F<rsub|d>>, <math|B\<in\>F<rsub|4>> and
  <math|D=d+1-\<varkappa\>>. The shell factor has scaling degree
  <math|3+\<varkappa\>>: integration over four dimensions contributes four
  powers of the radius, the differentiated cutoff removes one, and the
  constant or linear vector field contributes<nbsp><math|\<varkappa\>>. Since
  the two inserted fields have total degree <math|d+4>, the expected output
  degree is <math|<around|(|d+4|)>-<around|(|3+\<varkappa\>|)>=D>. A grid
  test is a separated configuration <math|<around|(|0,x<rsub|i>|)>> on
  <math|a<rsub|j<rsub|0>><Z><rsup|4>> for some level<nbsp><math|j<rsub|0>>,
  generic in each coordinate, with a fixed spectator
  list<nbsp><math|\<b-B\><rsub|i>>. Its response functional and marked
  response are

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|r><rsub|i><around|(|v|)>=S*<around|(|v<around|(|0|)>,\<b-B\><rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<frak-h\><rsub|i><around|(|y|)>=S*<around|(|B<around|(|y|)>,A<around|(|0|)>,\<b-B\><rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Finite collections may use different grids, and the union of all these
  configurations is dense. The next lemma shows that every finite combination
  of tests annihilating <math|F<rsub|D>> also annihilates the limiting shell
  response.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Annular annihilator estimate><label|lem:G-annular> For every
    finite list of grid tests and coefficients <math|g<rsub|i>\<in\><C>> with
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i><math-ss|r><rsub|i>=0>
    on<nbsp><math|F<rsub|D>>, there are <math|\<rho\><rsub|l>*\<downarrow\>*0>
    such that

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-annular-input>sup<rsub|1/4\<leqslant\><around|\||z|\|>\<leqslant\>2><around*|\||<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|l><rsup|3+\<varkappa\>>*\<frak-h\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|l>*z|)>|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The local sections of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:A1> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>\U<reference|lem:E2.3> represent the
    product of two nearby marked insertions by one marked insertion of
    degree<nbsp><math|D>; Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> pass to the quotient. Put
    <math|h=3+\<varkappa\>>, so <math|D+h=d+4>. Recall <math|s<rsub|m>=log
    <around|(|a<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|)>> from<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-levels>. Use the
    support radius <math|B<rsub|m>=<around|\<lceil\>|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>|\<rceil\>>> of Part<nbsp>I and take
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>=128*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>*a<rsub|m+1>>, so <math|log
    \<rho\><rsub|m>=-s<rsub|m>+o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>.

    <em|Step 1: two-mark representation.> Fix a centered cell around the
    distinguished point, disjoint from all spectator stencils, and a finer
    stopping level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>\<geqslant\><reflevel|D>> at which its
    replicated source estimate applies. Choose
    levels<nbsp><math|j<around|(|m|)>> with

    <\equation*>
      <frac|s<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>|s<rsub|m>>\<longrightarrow\>t=1+<frac|1|64*<around|(|d+12|)>>;
    </equation*>

    they exist since <math|s<rsub|m+1>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>1>
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>). At a grid point
    <math|y\<in\>a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>><Z><rsup|4>> with
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|m>/16\<less\><around|\||y|\|>\<less\>4*\<rho\><rsub|m>>,
    choose representatives <math|a\<in\>V<rsub|d>,b\<in\>V<rsub|4>>
    of<nbsp><math|A,B>, block their two marked insertions at<nbsp><math|y,0>
    together, and retain the Taylor terms of weight at most<nbsp><math|D>.
    Their coefficient vector<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>\<in\>V<rsub|D>>
    is chosen by inverting the marked coefficient map. The exact difference
    is

    <\equation*>
      \<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>*O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|4|)>><around|(|b,y/a<rsub|N>|)>*O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|d|)>><around|(|a,0|)>-O<rsub|N,p><rsup|<around|(|D|)>><around|(|c<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>,0|)>.
    </equation*>

    Let <math|u<rsub|m,1>,u<rsub|m,2>> be the available mark weights of the
    degree-<math|d> and rescaled degree-four marks at
    level<nbsp><math|j<around|(|m|)>>. The one-mark bounds,
    including<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>>, give <math|log
    u<rsub|m,1>=<around|(|-d*t+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>> and <math|log
    u<rsub|m,2>=<around|(|-4*t+h+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>>; support
    weights, coefficient columns and threshold powers have
    logarithm<nbsp><math|o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marker-scales> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>.
    Transporting each source to level<nbsp><math|m+1> adds the volume factor
    <math|<around|(|a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>/a<rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|4>> to its
    weight. So the joint weight<nbsp><math|v<rsub|m>> and the dimensionless
    coefficient bound<nbsp><math|e<rsub|m>> of its Taylor remainder satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-annular-cost>log v<rsub|m>=<around|[|-D-<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|t-1|)>+o<around|(|1|)>|]>*s<rsub|m>,<space|2em>e<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|[|<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|t-1|)>+o<around|(|1|)>|]>*s<rsub|m>|}>.
    </equation>

    Indeed the transported exponents sum to
    <math|-<around|(|d+4|)>*t+h+8*<around|(|1-t|)>=-D-<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|t-1|)>>,
    and Taylor extraction costs the reciprocal joint weight
    times<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|D>>, which cancels the<nbsp><math|D>
    term. This is the two-mark use of the source
    representation<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-form>, retaining its forced terms
    and their eligibility tests.

    <em|Step 2: backward matching.> Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match-level>
    from<nbsp><math|m+1> to the fixed stopping level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>.
    The matched remainder gains <math|<big|prod><rsub|i=k<rsub|0>><rsup|m>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|1/8>=e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|m+1>-s<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>/8>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|s<rsub|m>-s<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>/8>>
    in its inverse bound. Put <math|c<rsub|m>=e<rsup|s<rsub|m>/32>> and
    multiply the source by<nbsp><math|c<rsub|m>>; its size is then at most

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|m>*e<rsub|m>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|m>-s<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>/8>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|{|<around|[|-5/64+o<around|(|1|)>|]>*s<rsub|m>|}>,<space|2em><tfrac|1|32>+<around|(|d+12|)>*<around|(|t-1|)>-<tfrac|1|8>=-<tfrac|5|64>,
    </equation*>

    which eventually fits every fixed source allowance
    at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>>. The residual-source
    estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-residual-root> at the fixed cell and
    level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|0>> then bounds the chessboard seminorm of the
    unamplified residual by<nbsp><math|2*C/c<rsub|m>>, with<nbsp><math|C>
    independent of<nbsp><math|m,N,p>. The inverse
    estimate<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-lift-bound> in degree<nbsp><math|D>
    bounds<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p><around|(|y|)>> on cutoff tails for each
    fixed<nbsp><math|m,y>. Interior support follows from
    <math|32*\<rho\><rsub|m>\<to\>0> and the support diameter
    <math|a<rsub|m+1>*<around|(|B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>+1+2048*B<rsub|m+1><rsup|2>|)>\<to\>0>.

    <em|Step 3: cancellation at grid points.> Pass to an ordered limit
    point<nbsp><math|c<around|(|y|)>> and project it
    to<nbsp><math|F<rsub|D>>. The chessboard
    inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> for the residual and the fixed
    exterior stencils gives, simultaneously for the finite tests,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>*\<frak-h\><rsub|i><around|(|y|)>-<math-ss|r><rsub|i><around|(|\<pi\><rsub|D>*c<around|(|y|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|i>*c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>,
    </equation*>

    with the same coefficient vector for every spectator. Multiplication
    by<nbsp><math|g<rsub|i>> and summation cancel the response term, so
    <math|f<rsub|m><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>*\<frak-h\><rsub|i><around|(|y|)>>
    is <math|O<around|(|c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>|)>> at all grid points of the
    enlarged annulus whose four coordinates are nonzero.

    <em|Step 4: interpolation across coordinate planes.> For a derivative
    order<nbsp><math|q>, Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> replaces the
    moving label by one of degree <math|4+q>. The centered and reflected
    bounds of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication>, with<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess>,
    bound a field of fixed degree<nbsp><math|e> by <math|exp
    <around|[|<around|(|e+2+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>|]>>, with a
    field-dependent constant of logarithm<nbsp><math|o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>.
    The finitely many later levels required for the fixed spectator word have
    logarithmic scale <math|s<rsub|m>+o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>. Choose degree
    bounds<nbsp><math|e<rsub|i*j>> for the spectator labels and put
    <math|C<rsub|0>=8+d+max<rsub|i> <big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|e<rsub|i*j>+2|)>>.
    Since <math|\<rho\><rsub|m><rsup|h>\<leqslant\>1>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-annular-derivatives><around|\||\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|q>*f<rsub|m><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>exp
      <around|[|<around|(|q+C<rsub|0>+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>|]>*<space|2em><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|m>/16\<less\><around|\||y|\|>\<less\>4*\<rho\><rsub|m>|)>,
    </equation>

    with <math|C<rsub|0>> independent of<nbsp><math|q>. Choose <math|q> with
    <math|q*<around|(|t-1|)>\<gtr\>C<rsub|0>>; since
    <math|a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>=exp <around|[|-<around|(|t+o<around|(|1|)>|)>*s<rsub|m>|]>>,
    the <math|q>th derivatives along grid-sized segments tend to zero. On
    each coordinate segment of length <math|4*<around|(|2<rsup|q>+2|)>*a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>>
    choose <math|q> grid nodes with nonzero coordinates, mutually separated
    by at least <math|a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>>. Lagrange interpolation gives
    <math|<around|\||g|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|q><around|(|max<rsub|<math-up|nodes>><around|\||g*<around|\||+a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>><rsup|q>*sup|\|>*\<partial\><rsup|q>*g|\|>|)>>
    on the segment, with <math|C<rsub|q>> independent of<nbsp><math|m>. Apply
    it to <with|font-family|rm|Re><math|<space|0.17em>f<rsub|m>> and
    <with|font-family|rm|Im><math|<space|0.17em>f<rsub|m>> in each of the
    four axes; the segments stay in the enlarged annulus since
    <math|4*<around|(|2<rsup|q>+2|)>*a<rsub|j<around|(|m|)>>=o<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|m>|)>>.
    Four successive interpolations give <math|sup<rsub|\<rho\><rsub|m>/4\<leqslant\><around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>2*\<rho\><rsub|m>><around|\||f<rsub|m><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<to\>0>.
    Select a decreasing subsequence of<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|m>>; it
    depends on the fixed tests and<nbsp><math|g>, as allowed.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Free comparison and the conserved line><label|sec:partII-G.2>

  The next lemma converts Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-lattice> into the
  comparison of Part<nbsp>I, measured uniformly over the supremum-norm unit
  ball of tests. No volume refinement is needed.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stable weak comparison><label|lem:G2.1> There is one stable
    scaling sequence such that, for every arity<nbsp><math|n>, degree list,
    <math|u<rsub|i>\<in\><maxwellspace|d<rsub|i>>> and compact
    <math|\<cal-K\>\<subset\><Conf|n>>,

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>1,<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f\<subset\>\<cal-K\>><around*|\||<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*<around*|[|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|<scaleframe|k|d<rsub|i>>u<rsub|i>;r<rsub|k>*x|)>-<maxwellcorr><rsub|n><around|(|\<b-u\>;x|)>|]>*d*x|\|>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-lattice> supplies the three estimates,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1> the free modulus of continuity and the
    free separating tests, and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> the
    dimension bound. The set <math|K<rsub|R,m>\<setminus\>E<rsub|8*\<delta\><rsub|k>>>
    lies in finitely many strips about coordinate coincidence hyperplanes and
    has volume at most <math|C<rsub|m,R>*\<delta\><rsub|k>>; its contribution
    to a tested difference is at most <math|C<rsub|m,R><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<around|(|<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|cell>><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<maxwellcorr><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|K<rsub|R,m>>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|k>>.
    Outside these strips, rounding to the grid moves a point by at most
    <math|4*m*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|k>>, so the
    segment stays in <math|K<rsub|2*R,m>\<cap\>E<rsub|4*\<delta\><rsub|k>>>.
    The mean value bound and the free modulus of
    continuity<nbsp><math|\<varpi\>> give total tested error at most

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-tested-error>C<rsub|m,R><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<around*|[|\<varepsilon\><rsub|k>+<around|(|<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|cell>><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>+<around|\<\|\|\>|<maxwellcorr><rsub|m>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|K<rsub|R,m>>|)>*\<delta\><rsub|k>+<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|var>><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>/\<delta\><rsub|k>+\<varpi\>*<around|(|4*m*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>|)>|]>.
    </equation>

    Every term tends to zero by the strict
    inequalities<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-exponents>: the strips cost
    <math|<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|cell>><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>*\<delta\><rsub|k>>,
    the rounding costs <math|<around|(|R<rsup|<math-up|var>><rsub|k>|)><rsup|m>*\<frak-s\><rsub|k>/\<delta\><rsub|k>>,
    and both are negative powers of<nbsp><math|M<rsub|k>>. Arbitrary fields
    expand in the finitely many basis words, with coefficient sums bounded by
    the product of their norms.

    At a fixed comparison level the lifts are bounded in a finite-dimensional
    space. Take an ordered limit point and project to<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>>;
    ordered convergence holds also for convergent coefficients, by
    multilinearity. This proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-tested-error> for linear
    maps <math|U<rsub|k,d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>, for every
    ordered limit point and the same table (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1>).

    Choose successively arity, degree and radius bounds tending to infinity,
    tolerances tending to zero, and strictly decreasing radii tending to
    zero; this is possible because <math|log M<rsub|k>=o<around|(|log
    a<rsub|k><rsup|-1>|)>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-scales>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-running>, whence
    <math|a<rsub|k>*M<rsub|k><rsup|\<alpha\><rsub|0>>\<to\>0>. The sequence
    is selected before any field list. To impose inclusions exactly, choose
    real <math|<hypercubic>>-equivariant left inverses <math|L<rsub|d>> of
    the free inclusions<nbsp><math|i<rsub|d>> and replace the maps
    recursively by

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-correct-frames><wide|U|~><rsub|k,0>=U<rsub|k,0>,<space|2em><wide|U|~><rsub|k,d+1>=\<jmath\><rsub|d,d+1>*<wide|U|~><rsub|k,d>*L<rsub|d>+U<rsub|k,d+1>*<around|(|I-i<rsub|d>*L<rsub|d>|)>.
    </equation>

    The first summand is exact on included fields and the second agrees with
    <math|U<rsub|k,d+1>> on a complement; since free and interacting
    inclusions preserve correlations, the comparison is preserved, as are
    reality and signed covariance, and the degree-zero unit gives all units.
    Strict free positivity supplies finitely many separating tested
    correlations in each degree; their matrices converge, so a nonzero
    full-rank minor makes <math|<wide|U|~><rsub|k,d>> eventually injective,
    and Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> makes it an isomorphism. Fill
    the finitely many earlier indices in each degree with any of these
    isomorphisms.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>>
    By the equivalence after Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC>, uniform
    tested convergence on each compact set is local<nbsp><math|L<rsup|1>>
    convergence; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1> gives it for one stable
    sequence.
  </render-proof>

  The comparison upgrades to smooth convergence, which the identification of
  the current needs.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Smooth comparison><label|cor:G-smooth> For the stable frames of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-derivative-limit><math-ss|d><rsub|k,a>=r<rsub|k><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d+1>|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a><scaleframe|k|d>\<longrightarrow\>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>
    </equation>

    in operator norm, and, whenever <math|u<rsub|k,i>\<to\>u<rsub|i>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-smooth>S<rsub|n>*<around|(|<scaleframe|k|d<rsub|i>>u<rsub|k,i>;r<rsub|k><space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em>|)>\<longrightarrow\><maxwellcorr><rsub|n><around|(|u<rsub|i>;\<cdummy\>|)><space|1em><text|in
      >C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|<math-up|loc>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)>.
    </equation>
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    Choose finitely many tested free responses injective
    on<nbsp><math|<maxwellspace|d+1>>; their interacting matrices converge to
    this injective matrix by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1>. Testing
    <math|<math-ss|d><rsub|k,a>u> gives, by integration by parts, minus the
    correlation tested against <math|\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|a>>*f>,
    which converges to the test of <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*u>.
    An injective finite matrix has a bounded left inverse on all small
    perturbations, proving<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> on a basis.

    The chain rule and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> make every
    positional derivative converge locally in<nbsp><math|L<rsup|1>>, also for
    convergent label lists. For <math|g=\<chi\>*\<cdummy\><around*|(|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|<scaleframe|k|d<rsub|i>>u<rsub|k,i>;r<rsub|k>*<space|0.17em>\<cdummy\><space|0.17em>|)>-<maxwellcorr><rsub|n>(u<rsub|i>;\<cdummy\><around*|)||)>|\<nobracket\>>>,
    with <math|\<chi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)>>
    equal to one on a compact <math|\<cal-K\>>, and <math|M\<gtr\>4*n+j>,
    integration by parts gives <math|<around|\||<wide|g|^><around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|M>*<around|(|1+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|-M>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>M><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*g|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>>.
    Fourier inversion<nbsp><cite-detail|Folland1999|Theorem<nbsp>8.26>
    applies since <math|g> and <math|<wide|g|^>> are integrable; multiplying
    by<nbsp><math|p> through order<nbsp><math|j> and integrating proves
    uniform convergence of those derivatives on<nbsp><math|\<cal-K\>>.
  </proof>

  The rank calculation<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-divergence-matrix> transfers to the
  mixed fields; the proof also fixes the sign of one current.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Conserved line><label|lem:G2.2> The real space of centered real
    symmetric <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial tensors
    <math|J<rsub|a*b>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> with
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>=0> is
    one-dimensional. For some nonzero<nbsp><math|J>, a refinement of the
    sequence of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:MC> and
    positive<nbsp><math|c<rsub|k>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<scaleframe|k|4>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|c<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*J<rsub|a*b>|)>\<longrightarrow\><maxwellstress><rsub|a*b>
    </equation*>

    componentwise.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Transport a free tensor by<nbsp><math|<scaleframe|k|4>> and subtract its
    mixed one-point value times the unit. On centered tensors this is a real
    isomorphism<nbsp><math|C<rsub|k>>; the inverse transports back and
    subtracts the free expectation, and unit preservation makes the two
    composites identities. Let<nbsp><math|V<rsub|k>> be transport
    by<nbsp><math|<scaleframe|k|5>> on real covariant vectors.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit>,

    <\equation*>
      V<rsub|k><rsup|-1><around|(|r<rsub|k><math-up|div>|)>*C<rsub|k>\<longrightarrow\><math-up|div><rsup|<math-up|Max>>,
    </equation*>

    the centering term disappearing since derivatives kill the unit.
    Composition with a right inverse of free divergence tends to the
    identity, so mixed divergence is onto. The domain and target have
    dimensions three and two by these isomorphisms
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-divergence-matrix>, so the kernel is a real line.

    Choose a nonzero<nbsp><math|B> on this line and set
    <math|c<rsub|k>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|<scaleframe|k|4>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*B|)>|\<\|\|\>>/<around|\<\|\|\>|<maxwellstress>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    with norms on whole tensors. The normalized preimages have a convergent
    subsequence on this sphere. Stability preserves symmetry, reality and
    signed tensoriality; <eqref|eq:G-smooth> at arity one gives centering of
    the limit, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> gives zero free
    divergence. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>(iii) makes the limit
    <math|<maxwellstress>> or <math|-<maxwellstress>>; choose <math|J=B> or
    <math|-B> once to obtain the stated limit with
    positive<nbsp><math|c<rsub|k>>.
  </proof>

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-annular> now applies to each component of the
  current, and the resulting fields represent both fluxes of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>. The proof combines it with
  finite-dimensional duality, conservation, translation covariance and
  continuity of separated responses.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Raw shell charges><label|lem:G2.3> For the chosen<nbsp><math|J>
    there are complex linear maps <math|<math-ss|p><rsub|b>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d+1>>
    and <math|<math-ss|Y><rsub|K>:F<rsub|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>, the latter real
    linear in<nbsp><math|K>, such that, for every shell cutoff, field,
    spectator list and separated configuration,

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-P\><rsub|b><rsup|J>=S*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|p><rsub|b>A|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-Y\><rsub|K><rsup|J>=S*<around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|Y><rsub|K>A|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Use a constant Killing field<nbsp><math|v> (<math|\<varkappa\>=0>) or
    <math|v<around|(|y|)>=K*y> (<math|\<varkappa\>=1>). Independence of the
    shell cutoff follows by integration by parts: the divergence vanishes,
    and <math|<big|sum><rsub|a,b><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*v<rsub|b>|)>*J<rsub|a*b>=0>
    by symmetry.

    Fix finitely many grid response tests<nbsp><math|<math-ss|r><rsub|i>> and
    coefficients<nbsp><math|g<rsub|i>> annihilating<nbsp><math|F<rsub|D>>,
    <math|D=d+1-\<varkappa\>>. Shrink a common shell cutoff to
    <math|\<chi\>*<around|(|y/\<rho\><rsub|l>|)>>, with <math|\<chi\>=1> near
    zero and <math|\<nabla\>*\<chi\>> supported in
    <math|1/2\<leqslant\><around|\||y|\|>\<leqslant\>1>. Changing variables
    gives the sum of their fluxes as

    <\equation*>
      -<big|sum><rsub|a,b><big|int><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>|)><around|(|z|)>*v<rsub|b><around|(|z|)>*<big|sum><rsub|i>g<rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|l><rsup|3+\<varkappa\>>*S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|l>*z|)>,A<around|(|0|)>,\<b-B\><rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*z,
    </equation*>

    which is independent of<nbsp><math|l> and tends to zero
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-annular-input>. So every linear relation between the
    response tests is a relation between their flux values, and
    finite-dimensional duality gives a field in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|D>>
    matching these finitely many values: the annihilator of the image of the
    response matrix annihilates the flux vector.

    Choose a finite separating family of grid tests; it exists because the
    common kernel of all separated responses is zero, grid configurations are
    dense, and responses are continuous. A field matching this family is
    unique, and adjoining any further grid test, the same field matches it.
    Continuity of the responses and of shell integrals, with the cutoff fixed
    locally, extends the identity to all separated configurations with
    distinguished point zero; translation covariance moves that point to
    arbitrary<nbsp><math|x<rsub|0>>. Linearity of the integral and
    injectivity of responses give complex linearity in<nbsp><math|A> and real
    linearity in<nbsp><math|K>. Taking <math|v=e<rsub|b>> and
    <math|v=K*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-x<rsub|0>|)>> gives the two raw shell
    identities.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Coordinate reflection spaces and spectral
  estimates><label|sec:G-coordinate>

  Coordinate reflection positivity alone gives a reflection Hilbert space for
  each coordinate axis. This subsection constructs these spaces, bounds the
  slab insertion operators, and bounds the low-energy spectral mass of
  collinear words of mixed reflection parity and of the components of a
  conserved tensor. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G.3> uses these bounds
  to remove the boundary at infinity before continuous rotations are known.
  Nothing here uses rotations or Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G.1>\U<reference|sec:partII-G.2>:
  the inputs are E1\UE7, Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> and
  Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:F3>\U<reference|lem:F4>.

  A word is <math|w=<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>
  with distinct points, and <math|\<tau\><rsub|z>*w=<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>+z|)>>.
  Fix a norm <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdummy\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|V<rsub|d>>> on
  each coefficient space; insertion-label bounds refer to representatives in
  the spaces<nbsp><math|V<rsub|d>> of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>,
  and the resulting operators depend only on their classes
  in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|d>>. Write <math|w<rsup|\<ast\>>> for the word with
  reversed order and conjugated labels, extended antilinearly to word
  combinations, <math|\<vartheta\>> for linear time reflection, acting on
  labels through the hypercubic action of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|s><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|refl>>>,
  and <math|\<theta\>*w=\<vartheta\>*w<rsup|\<ast\>>>; thus <math|\<theta\>>
  is the antilinear reflection in the form of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14>. The insertion rule and buffer are
  those preceding Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F4>:
  <math|A<rsub|w>*v=w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|h*e<rsub|4>>*v>, and an
  <math|r>-buffered word in width<nbsp><math|h> has mutual sup-distance at
  least<nbsp><math|r> and times in <math|<around|[|r,h-r|]>>. Write
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|i,a>*w> for the word with <math|A<rsub|i>> replaced
  by <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*A<rsub|i>>.

  Repeated reflected insertions bound the operator that inserts a word into a
  time slab. Taking arbitrarily high powers removes the dependence of the
  bound on the exterior vector.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Slab operators><label|lem:H1.1> For <math|d\<in\><N>> and
    <math|R\<geqslant\>0> there are <math|C\<gtr\>0> and <math|Q\<in\><N>>
    such that every <math|r>-buffered word<nbsp><math|w> of
    length<nbsp><math|n>, with degrees at most<nbsp><math|d> and coefficient
    norms at most<nbsp><math|R>, in a slab of width<nbsp><math|h\<gtr\>0>,
    has a bounded insertion operator <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>>
    extending<nbsp><math|A<rsub|w>>, with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|Q*n>>
    for <math|0\<less\>r\<leqslant\>1>. For widths <math|h,k> in which the
    words are buffered,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>|<cell|=<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|h*e<rsub|4>>*\<theta\>*w|)>,<space|2em><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)><math-ss|B><rsub|k><around|(|v|)>=<math-ss|B><rsub|h+k><around|(|w*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|h*e<rsub|4>>*v|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:H-slab-laws>>>|<row|<cell|\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|i><rsup|a>><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>>|<cell|=<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|i,a>*w|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:H-slab-derivative>>>>>
    </align>

    with derivatives in operator norm; the empty word
    gives<nbsp><math|e<rsup|-h*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Replace<nbsp><math|R> by <math|max <around|(|1,R|)>>. <em|The heat
    semigroup.> On the span of positive words,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<tau\><rsub|t*e<rsub|4>>*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|v,\<tau\><rsub|2*t*e<rsub|4>>*v|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<tau\><rsub|2*t*e<rsub|4>>*v|\<\|\|\>>>.
    E7 bounds the separated orbit, so iteration gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<tau\><rsub|t*e<rsub|4>>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>
    and null vectors are preserved. Translation invariance gives symmetry and
    commuting unitary spatial translations; Gram continuity gives strong
    continuity. Since <math|e<rsup|-H>*\<psi\>=0> implies
    <math|e<rsup|-2<rsup|-j>*H>*\<psi\>=0>, strong continuity excludes
    spectral mass at zero of the heat operator. Spectral
    calculus<nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorem<nbsp>2.5.1 and
    Proposition<nbsp>4.2.3> defines <math|H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>=<big|int><rsub|<around|(|0,1|]>>-log
    v*<space|0.17em>d*P<around|(|v|)>> from the spectral
    measure<nbsp><math|P> of the time-one heat operator; positive roots and
    continuity give <math|e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>\<to\>P<rsub|4><rsup|0>>
    strongly.

    <em|The power argument.> For a nonempty word,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F4> supplies a width<nbsp><math|s> and
    <math|D=C<rsub|0><rsup|n>*<around|(|n/r|)><rsup|Q*n>>. On the word span
    put <math|A=A<rsub|w>>, <math|B=A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>> with
    <math|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>=\<tau\><rsub|s*e<rsub|4>>*\<theta\>*w>, and
    <math|T=B*A>. The OS form gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|A*v,u|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|v,B*u|\<rangle\>>>,
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F4> gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsup|j>*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|v>*D<rsup|2*j>>.
    Set

    <\equation*>
      d<rsub|j>=D<rsup|-2*j>*<around|\<\|\|\>|T<rsup|j>*v|\<\|\|\>>,<space|2em>M<rsub|v>=sup<rsub|j\<geqslant\>1>
      d<rsub|j>\<less\>\<infty\>.
    </equation*>

    Symmetry of<nbsp><math|T> gives <math|d<rsub|j><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>*d<rsub|2*j>>,
    hence <math|M<rsub|v><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>*M<rsub|v>>
    and <math|M<rsub|v>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>>. In
    particular <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|A*v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\<langle\>|v,T*v|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em><around|\<\|\|\>|T*v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>D<rsup|2><around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
    The bound is independent of<nbsp><math|K<rsub|v>>, and density gives the
    unique bounded extension. Right multiplication by
    <math|e<rsup|-<around|(|h-s|)>*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>> gives the
    required width.

    <em|Identities.> Positive-word matrix entries
    prove<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-laws>. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>
    bounds differentiated-label operators in a smaller buffer; integrating
    their matrix entries twice along segments gives an operator-norm Taylor
    remainder, and induction proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-derivative> and
    norm smoothness.
  </proof>

  With <math|e<rsub|\<mu\>>> as time, the same construction gives
  <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>,
  <math|e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>> and commuting
  transverse translations <math|<math-ss|T><rsub|\<perp\>><around|(|y|)>>,
  <math|y<rsup|\<mu\>>=0>, using only coordinate reflection positivity and
  signed permutations. Their joint projection-valued measure
  <math|P<rsub|\<mu\>>> lives on <math|<around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>\<times\><R><rsup|3>>,
  with variables <math|<around|(|E,p|)>>: apply Gelfand theory to the
  commutative unital C<rsup|<math|\<ast\>>>-algebra generated by
  <math|e<rsup|-H<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>> and the translation
  representation of <math|C<rsub|0><around|(|<R><rsup|3>|)>><nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorems<nbsp>2.4.1,
  3.7.11, equation<nbsp>(3.89) and Proposition<nbsp>4.2.3>, then take the
  logarithm of the heat coordinate. For a word combination<nbsp><math|U>, let
  <math|v<rsub|\<mu\>,u><around|(|U|)>> be its OS vector translated
  by<nbsp><math|u*e<rsub|\<mu\>>> into positive times, and undo the damping
  by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-coordinate-measure>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>*<around|(|E,p|)>=e<rsup|2*u*E>*<space|0.17em>d*<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|\<mu\>,u><around|(|U|)>,P<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>*v<rsub|\<mu\>,u><around|(|U|)>|\<rangle\>>.
  </equation>

  This measure is independent of<nbsp><math|u> and finite on
  <math|E\<leqslant\>M>; point-field measures may have infinite total mass
  before damping. Polarize to<nbsp><math|\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U*V>> on
  integrable tests. For nonnegative integrable<nbsp><math|g<rsub|1>,g<rsub|2>>,
  spectral Cauchy\USchwarz gives

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-coordinate-CS><around*|\||<big|int>f*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U*V>|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\><big|int>g<rsub|1>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>*<big|int>g<rsub|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|V><space|1em><text|if
    ><around|\||f|\|><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|1>*g<rsub|2>.
  </equation>

  If <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>*U=\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>*U> with
  <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>\<in\><around|{|1,-1|}>>, then, for
  <math|x<rsup|\<mu\>>=0> and <math|t> separating the words,

  <\equation*>
    S*<around*|(|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*<space|0.17em>\<tau\><rsub|t*e<rsub|\<mu\>>+x>*U|)>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<big|int>e<rsup|-t*E+i*p\<cdot\>x>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>*<around|(|E,p|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Both point fields and collinear words are represented.

  Opposite reflection signs compare coordinate spectra. Fix a set of axes
  <math|\<cal-A\>\<subset\><around|{|1,2,3,4|}>> and a finite word
  combination<nbsp><math|U> whose offsets have zero coordinates
  in<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\>>, with <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|j>*U=\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>*U>
  for <math|j\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, and assume that both signs occur. For even
  nonnegative <math|f<rsub|j>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>>
  set <math|f<rsub|j,v><around|(|p|)>=v<rsup|-1>*f<rsub|j>*<around|(|p/v|)>>.
  Throughout, <math|\<lambda\>> ranges over <math|e<rsup|<N>>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Opposite reflection signs><label|lem:H-coordinate-signs> For every
    <math|i\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, <math|q\<less\>0>,
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>0> and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>f<rsub|j,\<lambda\><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|j>>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|E+\<lambda\><rsup|q>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i><rsup|U>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Constants may depend on <math|U,\<cal-A\>,q,\<beta\>,f,\<delta\>>.
  </lemma>

  Tested against a product of even profiles, the reflected correlation
  of<nbsp><math|U> has a positive spectral representation in each time axis
  <math|i\<in\>\<cal-A\>>, with sign<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>>. For
  two axes of opposite parity, these representations of the same number
  differ by a sign; after the annular corrections below, this gives the
  transfer inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-transfer>. A narrow momentum
  profile is replaced by an annular profile with the same low moments; the
  residual has a nonnegative Laplace response, so the low-energy response in
  axis<nbsp><math|i> is bounded by two responses, one with each of the
  profiles in<nbsp><math|i> and in the next axis<nbsp><math|j> replaced by
  its annular correction, which the moment bounds control. Each transfer
  replaces one narrow profile by one whose width is bounded below; once every
  axis has been treated, the moment bound applies directly.

  <\proof>
    <em|Moments.> For a placement of margin<nbsp><math|r\<gtr\>0>, E7 bounds
    the <math|2*m>th transverse derivative of the reflected-pair correlation
    by <math|C*r<rsup|-D>>. Its centered difference has spectral integrand
    <math|<around|[|2*sin <around|(|t*p<rsub|j>/2|)>/t|]><rsup|2*m>*e<rsup|-2*u*E>>,
    and the integral remainder formula and Fatou give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-coordinate-moments><big|int>e<rsup|-2*u*E><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>\<leqslant\>C*r<rsup|-D>.
    </equation>

    Extra heat supplies energy powers, so for every damping <math|u> as
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-measure> and <math|m\<in\><N>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-all-moments><big|int>e<rsup|-2*u*E>*<around|(|1+E+<around|\||p|\|>|)><rsup|m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>\<less\>\<infty\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For offsets perpendicular to<nbsp><math|\<mu\>> take <math|u=t/2>,
    <math|r\<asymp\>t>; integrating <math|C*t<rsup|-D>> against
    <math|t<rsup|D>*<space|0.17em>d*t> on <math|<around|(|0,1|)>> gives,
    after enlarging<nbsp><math|D>, <math|<big|int><around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-D><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*m>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>\<less\>\<infty\>>.

    <em|Annular correction.> Choose even <math|\<sigma\>\<geqslant\>0> of
    integral one supported in <math|1\<leqslant\><around|\||v|\|>\<leqslant\>2>.
    Let <math|L<rsub|j>>, <math|0\<leqslant\>j\<less\>m>, be the Lagrange
    polynomials of degree <math|m-1> with
    <math|L<rsub|j><around|(|<around|(|i+2|)><rsup|2>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|i*j>>.
    For radius<nbsp><math|\<varrho\>\<gtr\>0> define

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<around|(|q|)>*g<around|(|q|)>*<space|0.17em>d*q=<big|int>\<sigma\><around|(|v|)>*<big|int>f<around|(|p|)>*<big|sum><rsub|j\<less\>m>L<rsub|j><around|(|<around|(|p/<around|(|\<varrho\>*v|)>|)><rsup|2>|)>*g*<around|(|\<varrho\>*v*<around|(|j+2|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p*<space|0.17em>d*v.
    </equation*>

    Since <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>L<rsub|j><around|(|x|)>*<around|(|j+2|)><rsup|2*i>=x<rsup|i>>
    for <math|i\<less\>m>, the residual <math|f-\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f>
    has vanishing moments <math|<big|int>p<rsup|2*i><around|(|\<cdummy\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p>
    for <math|i\<less\>m>, hence <math|2*m> vanishing moments when <math|f>
    is even; the correction is an annular profile whose coefficients contain
    <math|\<varrho\><rsup|-2*r>>. Put <math|<math-up|Lap><space|0.17em>f<around|(|E|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-E<around|\||t|\|>><fourierplus|f><around|(|t|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t>.
    Interpolating <math|<around|(|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>> gives the
    residual response

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-residual-response>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<around|(|E|)>=2*E*<big|int><frac|f<around|(|p|)>|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<big|int>\<sigma\><around|(|v|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<less\>m><frac|<around|(|\<varrho\>*v*<around|(|j+2|)>|)><rsup|2>-p<rsup|2>|<around|(|\<varrho\>*v*<around|(|j+2|)>|)><rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>>*<space|0.17em>d*v*<space|0.17em>d*p=<math-up|Lap><around|(|f-\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f|)><around|(|E|)>.
    </equation>

    Its value at <math|E=0> is <math|2*\<pi\>*f<around|(|0|)>>, and for
    <math|f\<geqslant\>0> supported in <math|<around|[|-\<varrho\>,\<varrho\>|]>>
    all factors lie in <math|<around|[|0,1|]>>. Let <math|f\<geqslant\>0> be
    even, supported in <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> and equal to one on
    <math|<around|[|-<tfrac|1|2>,<tfrac|1|2>|]>>, and let
    <math|0\<less\>v\<leqslant\>1\<leqslant\>\<varrho\>>. Then

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<frac|c<rsub|m>|E+v>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<rsub|v><around|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<pi\><around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|v>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<rsub|v><around|(|E|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|\<varrho\>/E|)><rsup|D>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|E\<geqslant\>1,m\<geqslant\>D/2|)>,>>>>
    </align*>

    with <math|C=C*<around|(|m,D,<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>|)>>:
    the upper bounds follow from <math|2*E*<big|int><around|(|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*d*p=2*\<pi\>>
    and from bounding the product by <math|<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|2*\<varrho\>*<around|(|j+2|)>/E|)><rsup|2>>;
    for the lower bound the product is at least
    <math|2<rsup|-m>*<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2*m>> for
    <math|<around|\||p|\|>\<leqslant\>v>, and one integrates over
    <math|0\<less\>p\<less\>v*E/<around|(|2*<around|(|E+v|)>|)>>. With the
    moment bound, for <math|\<varrho\>\<geqslant\>1>,
    <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f<rsub|v>\<subset\><around|[|-\<varrho\>,\<varrho\>|]>>
    and <math|m\<geqslant\>D/2> chosen after the moment
    exponent<nbsp><math|D>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-residual-cost><big|int>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*f<rsub|v><around|(|E|)><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*r>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|v<rsup|-1>+\<varrho\><rsup|D>|)>.
    </equation>

    <em|Transfer between two axes.> For <math|i,j\<in\>\<cal-A\>> write
    <math|<math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g|)>=<big|int><math-up|Lap><space|0.17em>g<rsub|i><around|(|E|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<setminus\><around|{|i|}>>g<rsub|k><around|(|p<rsub|k>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i><rsup|U>>.
    For <math|\<cal-A\>> containing all axes, the same product test gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-3>*<big|int>S*<around|(|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<tau\><rsub|x>*U|)>*<big|prod><rsub|k><fourierplus|g<rsub|k>><around|(|x<rsub|k>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x=\<varepsilon\><rsub|i><math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g|)>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|j><math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g|)>,
    </equation*>

    since <math|<big|int>e<rsup|i*p*x><fourierplus|g><around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x=2*\<pi\>*g<around|(|p|)>>
    for even profiles, while the time integral is
    <math|<math-up|Lap><space|0.17em>g<around|(|E|)>>; both temporal profiles
    have the zero moments required at collisions. For
    smaller<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\>>, put <math|g<rsub|k><around|(|p|)>=h*<around|(|p/T|)>>
    in omitted axes, with <math|h\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>>
    even and equal to one near zero, and let <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-cost>. Hence
    <math|<math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g|)>=-<math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g|)>>
    when <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|i>=-\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>>. Subtract
    annular corrections <math|a<rsub|i>,a<rsub|j>> from the two profiles in
    the current axes; positivity of the residual responses gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-sign-transfer><math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>;g<rsub|\<ne\>i>|)>\<leqslant\><math-up|Resp><rsub|i><around|(|g<rsub|i>-a<rsub|i>;g<rsub|j>\<mapsto\>a<rsub|j>|)>+<math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g<rsub|j>-a<rsub|j>;g<rsub|i>\<mapsto\>a<rsub|i>|)>,
    </equation>

    after expanding the signed identity and discarding
    <math|<math-up|Resp><rsub|j><around|(|g<rsub|j>-a<rsub|j>;g<rsub|\<ne\>j>|)>\<geqslant\>0>.

    <em|The walk.> Choose a finite walk <math|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|L>>
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\>> with opposite signs at consecutive entries,
    <math|i<rsub|0>=i>, every axis among <math|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|L-1>>,
    and <math|i<rsub|L>> already visited; it exists because both signs occur
    (insert an axis of the opposite sign between two entries of the same
    sign). Put <math|V<rsub|s>=<around|{|i<rsub|0>,\<ldots\>,i<rsub|s-1>|}>>.
    Fix <math|K\<geqslant\>0> and <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>. The induction
    claim is that there is <math|A<rsub|s>\<gtr\>0> such that, for every
    fixed family of even nonnegative compactly supported
    profiles<nbsp><math|f<rsub|j>>, all exponents with
    <math|0\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|s>>
    (<math|j\<in\>V<rsub|s>>) and <math|-K\<leqslant\>\<omega\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|s>>
    (<math|j\<nin\>V<rsub|s>>), every <math|0\<leqslant\>\<eta\>\<leqslant\>A<rsub|s>>
    with <math|\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|s>>\<leqslant\>\<eta\>>, and
    <math|R<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>1> such that the profile in
    axis<nbsp><math|i<rsub|s>> is supported in
    <math|<around|[|-R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>,R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>|]>>,
    all large subtraction orders<nbsp><math|m> satisfy

    <with|equation-nr|<value|equation-nr>|the-equation|<macro|<math-up|H><rsub|s>>|<\equation>
      <label|eq:H-sign-induction>0\<leqslant\><big|int>\<cal-R\><rsub|m,R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>>*f<rsub|i<rsub|s>,\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|s>>>><around|(|E|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<in\>\<cal-A\>\<setminus\><around|{|i<rsub|s>|}>>f<rsub|j,\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|j>>><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|i<rsub|s>><rsup|U>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>,
    </equation>>

    with <math|C> and the threshold in<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>> depending on
    the fixed data. Small positive exponents on unvisited axes permit later
    visits.

    At <math|s=L> every width is at least one, so all transverse suprema are
    bounded, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-cost> with <math|r=0> bounds the
    integral by <math|C*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|i<rsub|L>>>+\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>*D>|)>>;
    <math|A<rsub|L>*D\<less\>\<delta\>> gives<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-induction>.
    For the step from <math|s+1> to<nbsp><math|s> put <math|i=i<rsub|s>>,
    <math|j=i<rsub|s+1>> and <math|p<rsub|\<ast\>>=K*<around|\||\<cal-A\>\<setminus\><around|{|i,j|}>|\|>>.
    The parameters are chosen in this order:

    <padded-center|<with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|3|3|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|6|6|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|choice>|<cell|depends
    on>>|<row|<cell|1>|<cell|<math|\<alpha\><rprime|'>=A<rsub|s+1>>>|<cell|the
    case <math|s+1> of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-induction>>>|<row|<cell|2>|<cell|<math|r\<in\><N>>
    with <math|2*r*\<alpha\><rprime|'>\<gtr\>p<rsub|\<ast\>>+max
    <around|(|K,\<delta\>|)>+1>>|<cell|<math|\<alpha\><rprime|'>,K,\<delta\>,<around|\||\<cal-A\>|\|>>>>|<row|<cell|3>|<cell|moment
    exponent <math|D> of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-cost> for
    <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*r>>>|<cell|<math|r,U>>>|<row|<cell|4>|<cell|<math|0\<less\>A<rsub|s>\<less\>min
    <around|(|\<alpha\><rprime|'>,\<delta\>/<around|(|D+1|)>|)>>>|<cell|<math|\<alpha\><rprime|'>,\<delta\>,D>>>|<row|<cell|5>|<cell|subtraction
    orders <math|m>>|<cell|<math|D> and the collision orders>>>>>>>

    Correct the profile in axis<nbsp><math|i> at radius
    <math|R<rsub|0>*\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>> and the one in
    axis<nbsp><math|j> at radius <math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<alpha\><rprime|'>>>.
    The correction in<nbsp><math|i> is bounded by a fixed even nonnegative
    annular profile of width<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<eta\>>>, since its
    coefficients contain only factors <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2*r<rprime|'>*<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|i>-\<eta\>|)>>\<leqslant\>1>;
    so the second term of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-transfer> is bounded by the
    case <math|s+1>, the exponent in axis<nbsp><math|i> having become
    nonnegative. In the first term the correction in<nbsp><math|j> is bounded
    by <math|C<rsub|r>*\<lambda\><rsup|-2*r*\<alpha\><rprime|'>><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|><rsup|2*r>>,
    with bounded coefficients since <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>\<less\>\<alpha\><rprime|'>>,
    and the other transverse suprema cost at
    most<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsup|p<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-cost> bounds this term by

    <\equation*>
      C*\<lambda\><rsup|p<rsub|\<ast\>>-2*r*\<alpha\><rprime|'>+max
      <around|(|-\<omega\><rsub|i>,\<eta\>*D|)>>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\><rsup|p<rsub|\<ast\>>-2*r*\<alpha\><rprime|'>+max
      <around|(|K,A<rsub|s>*D|)>>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Both residual responses are nonnegative,
    so<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-transfer> with the positive annular majorants
    proves the case<nbsp><math|s>.

    <em|Conclusion.> Apply the case <math|s=0> with <math|K\<geqslant\>max
    <around|(|-q,max<rsub|j><around|(|-\<beta\><rsub|j>|)>|)>>,
    <math|\<omega\><rsub|i>=q>, <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>=\<beta\><rsub|j>> for
    <math|j\<ne\>i>, and <math|\<eta\>=0>, the profile in axis<nbsp><math|i>
    being the bump of the lower response bound. On <math|E\<leqslant\>1> that
    bound makes the stated integral at most <math|c<rsub|m><rsup|-1>> times
    the integral in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-induction>.
  </proof>

  Fix <math|a\<ne\>b> and a line axis<nbsp><math|l>. Set
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|a>=\<omega\><rsub|b>=-100>,
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|l>=-150> when <math|l\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>>, and
  <math|\<omega\><rsub|j>=-300> for <math|j\<nin\><around|{|a,b,l|}>>. For a
  fixed even nonnegative <math|\<psi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>>
  equal to one on <math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> put

  <\equation*>
    w<rsub|j>=\<lambda\><rsup|1+\<omega\><rsub|j>>,<space|2em>\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|p|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>\<mu\>>w<rsub|j><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>*<around|(|p<rsub|j>/w<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|U;\<upsilon\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>><around|(|p|)>|E+\<lambda\><rsup|\<upsilon\>>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U>.
  </equation*>

  These are the scales of the box on which the vector field of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-collinear-flux> equals<nbsp><math|K*y>:
  <math|<around|\||y<rsub|a>|\|>,<around|\||y<rsub|b>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|100>>,
  <math|<around|\||y<rsub|l>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|150>>,
  <math|<around|\||y<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|300>>. Set
  <math|B=2<rsup|-4>*<big|prod><rsub|j><around|(|1+\<varepsilon\><rsub|j>*\<vartheta\><rsub|j>|)>*U>
  for a word combination<nbsp><math|U> with offsets on <math|<R>e<rsub|l>>,
  with <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|a>=\<varepsilon\><rsub|b>=-1> and the other
  signs positive. Let <math|J<rsub|i*j>\<in\>F<rsub|4>> be real, symmetric,
  <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial and conserved; no Ward normalization is
  assumed. The index <math|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b>> in a measure denotes the
  one-point word <math|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b><around|(|0|)>>. Consider also tests
  <math|\<Psi\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>> such that, for some fixed
  <math|K> and every <math|L\<geqslant\>0>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-scaled-tests><around|\||\<Psi\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|E,p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|L>*\<lambda\><rsup|K>*e<rsup|-\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>*E>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>\<mu\>><around|(|1+\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|j>><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>|)><rsup|-L><space|0.17em>,
  </equation>

  and put <math|X<rsub|\<lambda\>>=<around|{|E\<gtr\>1|}>\<cup\><big|cup><rsub|j\<ne\>\<mu\>><around|{|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<gtr\>w<rsub|j>|}>>.
  The scaled Fourier\ULaplace tests of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-collinear-flux>,
  with fixed polynomial prefactors, have this bound. Constants below may
  depend on the fixed words, tensor, profiles and exponents.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Coordinate spectral estimates><label|lem:H-G3> For every projected
    collinear<nbsp><math|B>, <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|M\<geqslant\>0>, with <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>=1> if
    <math|\<mu\>=l> and <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>=-\<omega\><rsub|l>>
    otherwise, and <math|W=\<lambda\><rsup|-99>>,

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|B;-3000|)>>|<cell|=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>>|)>,<space|2em>I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b>;\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>*<space|1em><around|(|\<mu\>\<ne\>b|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:G-spectral-input>>>|<row|<cell|<big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|E*W<rsup|4>|<around|(|E+W|)><rsup|4>>*\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>>>|<cell|=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|4>|)>,<eq-number><label|eq:G-mass-input>>>>>
    </align>

    and <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>;0|)>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|900>|)>>
    and <math|<big|int><rsub|X<rsub|\<lambda\>>>\<Psi\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|U*V>=O<rsub|M><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-M>|)>>.
  </lemma>

  The sign estimates come from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-coordinate-signs>;
  the diagonal estimate comes from conservation. Recall
  also<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS>, <eqref|eq:H-all-moments>, and the
  holomorphy for <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>t\<gtr\>0> of one-insertion
  heat matrix elements, which follows from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>
  and spectral calculus.

  <\proof>
    <em|Sign estimates.> In direction<nbsp><math|l>, choose <math|2*u>
    separating the offsets and use <math|R<rsub|v><around|(|E|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|-E<around|\||t|\|>>*<around|(|1-\<chi\><around|(|t|)>|)>*\<phi\>*<around|(|v*t|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t>,
    where <math|0\<leqslant\>\<chi\>\<leqslant\>1> is compactly supported and
    equals one on <math|<around|[|-2*u,2*u|]>>, and
    <math|\<phi\>=<fourierplus|<around|(|\<sigma\>\<ast\>\<sigma\>|)>>\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<phi\><around|(|0|)>=1>. It satisfies
    <math|C/<around|(|E+v|)>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|v><around|(|E|)>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*v<rsup|-1>*e<rsup|-2*u*E>>
    for <math|E\<leqslant\>1> and small<nbsp><math|v>: integrate over
    <math|R\<less\>t\<less\>R+c/<around|(|E+v|)>> beyond
    <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>> for the lower bound, and drop
    <math|1-\<chi\>> and scale for the upper. The removed profile has bounded
    supremum, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-moments> permits the
    transfer<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-transfer> through an opposite transverse
    sign; <math|\<delta\>=1> gives <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\>|)>> when
    <math|\<mu\>=l>. For <math|\<mu\>\<ne\>l>, omit the <math|l> profile,
    apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-coordinate-signs> on
    <math|\<cal-A\>=<around|{|j:j\<ne\>l|}>>, where both signs occur for the
    specified parity, and restore its supremum
    <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-1-\<omega\><rsub|l>>|)>>;
    <math|\<delta\>=1> gives <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|l>>|)>>.
    The same lemma for the point word<nbsp><math|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b>>,
    <math|\<mu\>\<ne\>b>, gives the second estimate.

    <em|Diagonal tensor: moment-subtracted extension.> Write
    <math|C<rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|x|)>=S*<around|(|J<rsub|a*b><around|(|0|)>*J<rsub|c*d><around|(|x|)>|)>>,
    <math|x\<ne\>0>. Choose <math|D<rsub|<math-up|col>>> bounding the
    collision powers of these pairs and their first derivatives. For even
    <math|\<chi\>=1> near zero with compact support put
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|D<rsub|<math-up|col>>>*f<around|(|t|)>=\<chi\><around|(|t|)>*<big|sum><rsub|r\<less\>D<rsub|<math-up|col>>>f<rsup|<around|(|r|)>><around|(|0|)>*t<rsup|r>/r!>,
    and define

    <\equation*>
      R<rsup|0><rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>C<rsub|a*b,c*d><around|(|x|)>*<around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|j>f<rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>-<big|prod><rsub|j>\<Pi\><rsub|D<rsub|<math-up|col>>>*f<rsub|j><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>|]>*d*x.
    </equation*>

    Taylor vanishing gives integrability, and a zero Taylor factor recovers
    the unsubtracted integral. Average under signed symmetries and pair
    exchange to obtain<nbsp><math|R>. Conservation makes
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>*R<rsub|i*b,c*d>> a jet at
    zero, and inversion <math|x\<mapsto\>-x> makes<nbsp><math|R> even,
    eliminating even divergence orders. To remove the linear orders, put
    <math|\<chi\><rsub|0><around|(|x|)>=<big|prod><rsub|j>\<chi\><around|(|x<rsub|j>|)>>
    and <math|q<rsub|a*b,c*d>=<big|sum><rsub|i>R<rsub|i*b,c*d>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|a>*\<chi\><rsub|0>|)>|)>>,
    and replace<nbsp><math|R> by <math|R<rsub|a*b,c*d>-q<rsub|a*b,c*d>*\<delta\><rsub|0>>;
    reflections kill coefficients unless indices occur in pairs, and
    permuting them gives the pair symmetries. Write
    <math|<wide|R|^><rsub|a*b,c*d>> for the Fourier transform of this
    corrected extension. Its divergence is the polynomial
    <math|F<rsub|b,c*d><around|(|p|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i>p<rsub|i>*<wide|R|^><rsub|i*b,c*d><around|(|p|)>>,
    with no terms of degree below three. Symmetry gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j,k\<ne\>\<mu\>>p<rsub|j>*p<rsub|k>*<wide|R|^><rsub|k*\<mu\>,j*\<mu\>>=p<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|2>*<wide|R|^><rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>,\<mu\>*\<mu\>>+<big|sum><rsub|j\<ne\>\<mu\>>p<rsub|j>*F<rsub|\<mu\>,j*\<mu\>>-p<rsub|\<mu\>>*F<rsub|\<mu\>,\<mu\>*\<mu\>>,
    </equation*>

    and the same two contractions on the other index pair give

    <\gather>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-Q\><rsub|0>=<wide|R|^><rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>,\<mu\>*\<mu\>>,<space|2em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|1>=<big|sum><rsub|j,k\<ne\>\<mu\>>p<rsub|j>*p<rsub|k>*<wide|R|^><rsub|k*\<mu\>,j*\<mu\>>=p<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|2>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|0>+D<rsub|1>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-Q\><rsub|2>=<big|sum><rsub|j,k,r,s\<ne\>\<mu\>>p<rsub|j>*p<rsub|k>*p<rsub|r>*p<rsub|s>*<wide|R|^><rsub|s*k,r*j>=p<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|2>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|1>+D<rsub|2>,<eq-number><label|eq:H-tensor-responses>>>>>
    </gather>

    where multiplication by <math|p<rsub|j>> acts on the test and
    <math|D<rsub|1>,D<rsub|2>> are polynomial remainders.

    <em|Diagonal tensor: annular bounds.> On a fixed temporal annulus,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-coordinate-signs>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS> bound each
    <math|<wide|R|^><rsub|a*b,c*d>> by <math|O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>>.
    For a diagonal entry, divide the annular profile by <math|p<rsub|\<mu\>>>
    and move the derivative to the label, which then has both signs; moment
    subtraction justifies this at the collision. To remove the remaining
    zero-moment restrictions, expand one transverse profile into its residual
    and its fixed annular correction. For an odd profile<nbsp><math|f>
    supported in <math|<around|[|-r<rsub|0>,r<rsub|0>|]>> use
    <math|p<rsup|-1>*\<cal-C\><rsub|m,\<varrho\>>*<around|(|p*f|)>> as
    correction; for <math|f<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>=v<rsup|-1>*f*<around|(|p/v|)>>,
    splitting at <math|E=v> gives <math|<big|int><rsub|<around|\||p|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|0>*v><around|\||p*f<rsub|v><around|(|p|)>|\|>/<around|(|p<rsup|2>+E<rsup|2>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*p\<leqslant\>C/<around|(|E+v|)>>
    from <math|<around|\||f<around|(|p|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\||p|\|>>.
    The factor <math|<around|(|1+E|)><rsup|-2*m>> makes the even lower bound
    dominate this residual, including at zero,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS> with<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-sign-transfer>
    controls it. Each correction fixes one more transverse annulus with
    bounded coefficients <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2*r*\<beta\>>>,
    <math|\<beta\>\<leqslant\>0>; the induction ends after at most three
    coordinates, and finite Taylor terms are bounded on normalized shrinking
    profiles. Since each transverse momentum costs at most<nbsp><math|W>, the
    annular bounds for <math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|0>,\<cal-Q\><rsub|1>,\<cal-Q\><rsub|2>>
    are <math|O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>|)>>,
    <math|O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>
    and <math|O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|4>|)>>.
    The errors cost <math|O<around|(|W<rsup|4>|)>> and
    <math|O<around|(|W<rsup|6>|)>>, since divergence has degree at least
    three and the contractions add one and three powers; one subtraction
    order<nbsp><math|m> above these degrees annihilates them by its zero
    temporal moments.

    <em|Diagonal tensor: positivity.> Put
    <math|g<rsub|v,r><around|(|p|)>=v<rsup|-1>*<around|(|p/v|)><rsup|-2*r>*\<sigma\>*<around|(|p/v|)>>,
    so <math|p<rsup|2>*g<rsub|v,r+1>=v<rsup|2>*g<rsub|v,r>>. Keep
    <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>> fixed and set

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|r><around|(|t|)>=W<rsup|-2*r><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|r><around|(|g<rsub|t*W,r>|)>,<space|2em>d<rsub|r>=W<rsup|-2*r><math-up|Re><space|0.17em>\<cal-Q\><rsub|r>*<around*|(|<around|(|1-\<cal-C\><rsub|m,8>|)>*<around|(|g<rsub|W,r>-g<rsub|2*W,r>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Conservation gives <math|c<rsub|r+1><around|(|t|)>=t<rsup|2>*c<rsub|r><around|(|t|)>+O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>
    for <math|t=1,2>, <math|r=0,1>. The correction of
    <math|g<rsub|W,r>-g<rsub|2*W,r>> has zero zeroth moment and other even
    moments <math|O<around|(|W<rsup|2>|)>>, so
    <math|d<rsub|r>=c<rsub|r><around|(|1|)>-c<rsub|r><around|(|2|)>+O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>.
    Reflection positivity for<nbsp><math|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>> makes the
    spectral representation of<nbsp><math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|0>> positive; on
    corrected profiles it gives <math|d<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|d<rsub|1>\<leqslant\>0>, <math|d<rsub|2>\<geqslant\>0>, since
    multiplication by <math|p<rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|2>> becomes
    <math|-E<rsup|2>>. Consequently <math|4*d<rsub|0>=5*d<rsub|1>-d<rsub|2>+O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>
    and <math|d<rsub|0>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>>.
    Subtracting <math|<around|(|E<rsup|2>+4*W<rsup|2>*v<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    from <math|<around|(|E<rsup|2>+W<rsup|2>*v<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-residual-response>, with
    <math|1\<leqslant\><around|\||v|\|>\<leqslant\>2>, bounds the response
    difference below by <math|c<rsub|m>*E*W<rsup|2>/<around|(|E+W|)><rsup|4>>
    for <math|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>1>; both vanish at zero. Since the
    response difference and <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>> are
    nonnegative,

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|m>*<big|int><rsub|E\<leqslant\>1><frac|E*W<rsup|2>|<around|(|E+W|)><rsup|4>>*\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>>\<leqslant\>d<rsub|0>=O<rsub|\<delta\>><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<delta\>>*W<rsup|2>|)>,
    </equation*>

    and multiplying by <math|W<rsup|2>/c<rsub|m>> gives the third estimate.

    <em|Crude and far-region bounds.> Finiteness of
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|\<mu\>><rsup|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>>*<around|(|E\<leqslant\>1|)>>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|897>|)>>
    give the crude bound. On <math|E\<gtr\>1>,<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-scaled-tests>
    gives <math|C<rsub|M>*\<lambda\><rsup|-M>*e<rsup|-t<rsub|0>*E>> for a
    fixed <math|t<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>; on <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<gtr\>w<rsub|j>>,
    the inequality <math|\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|j>><around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<gtr\>\<lambda\>>
    gives arbitrary decay. Integrate by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-all-moments> and
    use<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS> for mixed terms.
  </proof>

  <subsection|Ward identities and rotations><label|sec:partII-G.3>

  Write <math|\<b-q\>=<around|(|A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>|)><rsub|i>>
  and define the angular sum

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-angular><math-up|Ang><rsub|K><around|[|<math-ss|Y>,<math-ss|p>|]><around|(|\<b-q\>;x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i><around*|[|S*<around|(|\<ldots\>,<math-ss|Y><rsub|K>A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>;x|)>+<big|sum><rsub|b><around|(|K*x<rsub|i>|)><rsub|b>*S*<around|(|\<ldots\>,<math-ss|p><rsub|b>A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>;x|)>|]>.
  </equation>

  It is the total angular flux of the raw charges of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.3>. Fix distinct axes <math|a,b>, a line
  axis<nbsp><math|l>, and the exponents<nbsp><math|\<omega\><rsub|j>>,
  profile<nbsp><math|\<psi\>> and weights<nbsp><math|\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>>
  of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:G-coordinate>. For a word<nbsp><math|B>
  on<nbsp><math|<R>e<rsub|l>> projected to odd parity in <math|a,b> and even
  parity in the other axes, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3> gives the sign
  estimates<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-spectral-input>, the diagonal
  bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-mass-input>, the crude bound
  <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>;0|)>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|900>|)>>,
  and <math|O<rsub|M><around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-M>|)>> from the
  region<nbsp><math|X<rsub|\<lambda\>>>. These estimates use coordinate
  reflection positivity, signed permutations and conservation only; no
  continuous rotation enters them.

  The next lemma removes the angular boundary at infinity before continuous
  rotations are known. Its mechanism is as follows. The shell identities and
  conservation write the angular sum as the integral of the conserved tensor
  against the derivative of a vector field equal to<nbsp><math|K*y> on a
  large box; the sum does not depend on the box, so it suffices that this
  integral tends to zero. No decay of correlations is assumed, so the
  smallness must come from positivity. After a translation the configuration
  lies on a line<nbsp><math|<R>e<rsub|l>>. A coordinate reflection in an axis
  other than<nbsp><math|l> fixes every point of this line, and the reflection
  in<nbsp><math|l> maps it to itself; so reflecting the integration variable
  replaces the spectator word by its parity projections, which are again
  combinations of words on the line, odd in<nbsp><math|a,b> and even in the
  other axes. Such a word has positive spectral representations of opposite
  signs in two coordinate time axes, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-coordinate-signs>
  turns this into a bound on its low-energy spectral mass; the diagonal
  components of the tensor are controlled by conservation instead
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>). The box has the anisotropic extents
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|100>>, <math|\<lambda\><rsup|150>>,
  <math|\<lambda\><rsup|300>> of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:G-coordinate>,
  chosen so that in each case of the proof these bounds give a power
  of<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>> below<nbsp><math|-101>, which beats the
  prefactor<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsup|100>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Collinear angular identity><label|lem:G-collinear-flux> For every
    separated configuration<nbsp><math|x> on a coordinate line, every field
    list<nbsp><math|\<b-q\>> and every <math|K\<in\><with|math-font|Euler|s*o><around|(|4|)>>,
    the raw charges satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-collinear><math-up|Ang><rsub|K><around|[|<math-ss|Y>,<math-ss|p>|]><around|(|\<b-q\>;x|)>=0.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    It suffices to treat <math|K=E<rsub|b*a>-E<rsub|a*b>>, <math|a\<ne\>b>.
    Choose even <math|\<eta\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>>
    equal to one on<nbsp><math|<around|[|-1,1|]>> and put
    <math|\<ell\><around|(|t|)>=t*\<eta\><around|(|t|)>>. Set

    <\equation*>
      f<rsub|a*b><around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\><rsup|100>*\<ell\>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-100>*y<rsub|a>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j\<ne\>a>\<ell\><rprime|'>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|j>>*y<rsub|j>|)>,<space|2em>V<rsub|b>=f<rsub|a*b>,<space|1em>V<rsub|a>=-f<rsub|b*a>,<space|1em>V<rsub|j>=0*<around|(|j\<ne\>a,b|)>.
    </equation*>

    The vector field<nbsp><math|V> equals<nbsp><math|K*y> on the box
    <math|<around|\||y<rsub|a>|\|>,<around|\||y<rsub|b>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|100>>,
    <math|<around|\||y<rsub|l>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|150>>,
    <math|<around|\||y<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|300>>, hence
    near every insertion for large<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>>. Disjoint shell
    cutoffs, the raw shell identities of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.3> and
    conservation give <math|<math-up|Ang><rsub|K>=-<big|int><big|sum><rsub|\<mu\>,b>\<partial\><rsub|\<mu\>>*V<rsub|b>*<space|0.17em>S*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b><around|(|y|)>,\<b-q\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y>.
    The two terms with <math|\<mu\>=a,b> cancel exactly, because
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*f<rsub|a*b>=\<partial\><rsub|b>*f<rsub|b*a>>
    and <math|J<rsub|a*b>=J<rsub|b*a>>. Since the sum is independent
    of<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>>, it suffices to show that the remaining terms
    are <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-101>|)>> before the
    prefactor<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsup|100>>.

    Consider the <math|V<rsub|b>> terms with <math|\<mu\>\<ne\>a>. Reflecting
    the integration variable in each axis, including the tensor signs,
    replaces the spectator word by its odd projection in<nbsp><math|a,b> and
    its even projections in the other axes; if the line axis is reflected,
    the projection is a finite linear combination of collinear words, to
    which Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3> applies. The differentiated profile
    is supported where <math|<around|\||y<rsub|\<mu\>>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>>,
    so its Laplace transform has the factor
    <math|e<rsup|-E/\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>>>. For each of
    the three spatial factors <math|f<rsub|j>=\<ell\>>
    or<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rprime|'>>, Fourier scaling gives, on
    <math|<around|\||p<rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>w<rsub|j>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||<wide|f<rsub|j>*<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|j>><space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em>|)>|^><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|-\<omega\><rsub|j>><around|\<\|\|\>|f<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\>*w<rsub|j><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>*<around|(|p<rsub|j>/w<rsub|j>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Their product gives <math|\<lambda\><rsup|3>*\<cal-K\><rsub|\<lambda\>>>,
    and the time integral contributes<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsup|\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>>.
    For an off-diagonal component, <math|e<rsup|-t>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+t|)><rsup|-1>>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-coordinate-CS> bound the integral by
    <math|C*\<lambda\><rsup|3+\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>>*<around|[|I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|B;-3000|)>*I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*b>;\<omega\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>|]><rsup|1/2>>,
    with <math|\<delta\>=1>. For <math|\<mu\>=b> split at
    <math|E=\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>>. Above it use
    <math|e<rsup|-E/\<lambda\><rsup|-100>>\<leqslant\>4*<around|[|W/<around|(|E+W|)>|]><rsup|2>>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-mass-input>; below it
    <math|E+\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>\<leqslant\>2*\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>>
    supplies <math|\<lambda\><rsup|-1500>> in Cauchy\USchwarz, and the crude
    bound <math|I<rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|J<rsub|\<mu\>*\<mu\>>;0|)>=O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|900>|)>>
    completes the estimate. The four cases give the following powers
    of<nbsp><math|\<lambda\>> before the prefactor:

    <padded-center|<with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-halign|l>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|5|5|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|component
    and region>|<cell|power of <math|\<lambda\>>>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<mu\>=l\<nin\><around|{|a,b|}>>>|<cell|<math|3-150+<around|(|1+1|)>/2=-146>>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<mu\>\<nin\><around|{|a,b,l|}>>>|<cell|<math|3-300+<around|(|150+1|)>/2=-221.5>>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<mu\>=b>,
    <math|E\<geqslant\>\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>>>|<cell|<math|3+<around|(|150+1-396|)>/2=-119.5>>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<mu\>=b>,
    <math|E\<less\>\<lambda\><rsup|-3000>>>|<cell|<math|3-1500+<around|(|150+900|)>/2=-972>>>>>>>>

    Every row is below <math|-101>. The region <math|X<rsub|\<lambda\>>> is
    <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-M>|)>> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-G3>, with <math|M\<gtr\>201>, and the
    <math|V<rsub|a>> terms are identical after exchanging<nbsp><math|a,b>,
    since <math|\<omega\><rsub|a>=\<omega\><rsub|b>>; this includes their
    diagonal component<nbsp><math|J<rsub|a*a>>. So the angular sum is
    <math|O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|-1>|)>>, hence zero. Linearity
    in<nbsp><math|K> proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collinear>.
  </proof>

  To pass from lines to arbitrary configurations, the rotation argument needs
  analyticity of a vector-valued function, obtained from its Gram kernel.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Analyticity from a stationary Gram kernel><label|lem:G-analytic>
    Let <math|v:<R>\<to\>\<cal-H\>> be a smooth curve in a Hilbert space with
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|v<around|(|s|)>,v<around|(|s<rprime|'>|)>|\<rangle\>>=A*<around|(|s<rprime|'>-s|)>>,
    and suppose that the derivatives of<nbsp><math|A> at zero coincide with
    those of a function analytic at zero. Then <math|v> is real analytic at
    every point of<nbsp><math|<R>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Differentiating the Gram identity gives
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|v<rsup|<around|(|m|)>><around|(|s|)>,v<rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|s<rprime|'>|)>|\<rangle\>>=<around|(|-1|)><rsup|m>*A<rsup|<around|(|k+m|)>>*<around|(|s<rprime|'>-s|)>>.
    Let <math|\<rho\>\<gtr\>0> be smaller than the radius of the analytic
    function; Cauchy estimates give <math|<around|\||A<rsup|<around|(|2*m|)>><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsup|-2*m>*<around|(|2*m|)>>!.
    Hence

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsup|<around|(|m|)>><around|(|s|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||A<rsup|<around|(|2*m|)>><around|(|0|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<rho\><rsup|-2*m>*<around|(|2*m|)>!\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|2/\<rho\>|)><rsup|2*m><around|(|m!|)><rsup|2>
    </equation*>

    for every<nbsp><math|s>, using <math|<around|(|2*m|)>!\<leqslant\>4<rsup|m><around|(|m!|)><rsup|2>>.
    Taylor's remainder in the Hilbert space then tends to zero on the
    interval of radius <math|\<rho\>/2> around every point.
  </proof>

  For a finite-dimensional rotation action write
  <math|<math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K|)>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*t>*R<rsub|d><around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>|\|><rsub|t=0>>
  for its generator. The shell charges are now identified without assuming
  rotation covariance; the proof fixes their common scale and then constructs
  an orthogonal-group action.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Rotation and charge identification><label|lem:G3.1> There are
    <math|c<rsub|\<star\>>\<in\><R>\<setminus\><around|{|0|}>> and continuous
    complex linear <math|O<around|(|4|)>> representations <math|R<rsub|d>>
    extending<nbsp><math|<hypercubic>>, fixing units and commuting with
    conjugation and inclusions, with

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|p><rsub|b>=c<rsub|\<star\>>*\<partial\><rsub|b>,<space|2em><math-ss|Y><rsub|K>=c<rsub|\<star\>><math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K|)>,<space|2em>R<rsub|d+1><around|(|h|)>*\<partial\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|i>h<rsub|i*a>*\<partial\><rsub|i>*R<rsub|d><around|(|h|)>,>>|<row|<cell|S<rsub|n>*<around|(|R<rsub|d<rsub|i>><around|(|h|)>*A<rsub|i>;h*x|)>=S<rsub|n>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>*<space|2em><around|(|h\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>|)>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    For every<nbsp><math|d>, an isomorphism
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>> intertwines the
    free and interacting <math|O<around|(|4|)>> actions.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We first identify the translation charge up to one common scalar. After
    this normalization the angular charge satisfies the derivative commutator
    needed to extend the collinear identity. We then prove that identity on
    all separated configurations, remove the bracket defect by finite free
    tests, and construct an intertwiner with the free rotation action.

    <em|Step 1: shell commutators.> Fix a shell cutoff and move its
    distinguished point. Differentiating the two shell identities of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.3> under the compact integral gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-shell-commutators>\<partial\><rsub|b><math-ss|p><rsub|a>=<math-ss|p><rsub|a>\<partial\><rsub|b>,<space|2em>\<partial\><rsub|b><math-ss|Y><rsub|K>+<big|sum><rsub|i>K<rsub|i*b><math-ss|p><rsub|i>=<math-ss|Y><rsub|K>\<partial\><rsub|b>,
    </equation>

    the second term coming from the derivative of
    <math|K*<around|(|y-x<rsub|0>|)>>. Testing the unit, applying conjugation
    or a signed permutation, and including a field show that the charges kill
    units, are real, have vector or angular covariance, and respect
    inclusions; injectivity of responses turns these tested identities into
    field identities.

    <em|Step 2: the common normalization.> Translate a collinear
    configuration by<nbsp><math|e<rsub|l>> in<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-collinear>,
    with <math|K=E<rsub|b*l>-E<rsub|l*b>>; subtraction gives
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>S*<around|(|\<ldots\>,<math-ss|p><rsub|b>A<rsub|i>,\<ldots\>;x|)>=0>
    for <math|b\<ne\>l>, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-shell-commutators> gives every
    positional jet of these identities. Translation invariance gives the same
    identities for<nbsp><math|\<partial\>>. Apply the finite free vector
    tests<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-free-tests> to the transported mixed vector maps;
    their test matrices converge by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth>. Adjoining a
    real functional nonzero on<nbsp><math|\<partial\><rsup|<math-up|Max>>>,
    the free matrix becomes injective and stays so under small perturbations,
    so the mixed kernel has dimension at most one. It
    contains<nbsp><math|<math-ss|p>> and<nbsp><math|\<partial\>>, so
    <math|<math-ss|p>=c<rsub|d>*\<partial\>> in every large degree;
    inclusions and nonzero free differentiation make the constants equal and
    give the identity in lower degrees. Rescale the two shell identities
    by<nbsp><math|r<rsub|k>/c<rsub|k>> and<nbsp><math|1/c<rsub|k>>; changing
    variables <math|y=r<rsub|k>*z> and using<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:G-charge-limits><frac|r<rsub|k>|c<rsub|k>><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d+1>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|p><rsub|a><scaleframe|k|d>\<to\>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>,<space|2em>c<rsub|k><rsup|-1><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|Y><rsub|K><scaleframe|k|d>\<to\><math-ss|L><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K|)>,
    </equation>

    since finite separating tests recover operator limits from the shell
    integrals. In particular <math|c<rsub|\<star\>>=c<rsub|d>\<ne\>0> and
    <math|c<rsub|k>\<to\>c<rsub|\<star\>>>. Put
    <math|<math-ss|D><rsub|K>=c<rsub|\<star\>><rsup|-1><math-ss|Y><rsub|K>>;
    then<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-shell-commutators> reads
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|b><math-ss|D><rsub|K>+<big|sum><rsub|i>K<rsub|i*b>*\<partial\><rsub|i>=<math-ss|D><rsub|K>\<partial\><rsub|b>>.

    <em|Step 3: analyticity of one-insertion vectors.> By this commutator,
    every positional derivative of an angular sum is again an angular sum,
    with differentiated labels; so all jets of the collinear identity vanish
    on a line. In a coordinate plane write
    <math|G<rsub|u,v>*<around|(|t,s|)>=S*<around|(|u*<around|(|t*e<rsub|a>+s*e<rsub|b>|)>,v<around|(|0|)>|)>>,
    <math|<around|(|\<varrho\>,\<theta\>|)>> for polar coordinates, and
    <math|G<rsup|<math-up|rot>><rsub|u,v>*<around|(|t,s|)>=S*<around|(|<around|(|e<rsup|-\<theta\><math-ss|D><rsub|K>>*u|)>*<around|(|\<varrho\>*e<rsub|a>|)>,<around|(|e<rsup|-\<theta\><math-ss|D><rsub|K>>*v|)><around|(|0|)>|)>>.
    For positive separation the axial function is a matrix coefficient of
    <math|e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>, after fixed
    positive-time damping of its end vectors, hence analytic by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1> and spectral calculus;
    so<nbsp><math|G<rsup|<math-up|rot>>> is analytic. The angular equation
    holds for<nbsp><math|G<rsup|<math-up|rot>>>, and
    <math|\<cal-F\>=G-G<rsup|<math-up|rot>>> vanishes on the axis. Its
    angular jets give the recurrence

    <\equation*>
      t*\<partial\><rsub|s><rsup|m+1>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u,v>*<around|(|t,0|)>=-\<partial\><rsub|s><rsup|m>*<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|<math-ss|D><rsub|K>u,v>+\<cal-F\><rsub|u,<math-ss|D><rsub|K>v>|)>*<around|(|t,0|)>+m*\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<partial\><rsub|s><rsup|m-1>*\<cal-F\><rsub|u,v>*<around|(|t,0|)>,
    </equation*>

    the last term omitted for <math|m=0>; induction, for all labels at once,
    makes every transverse Taylor coefficient of<nbsp><math|\<cal-F\>> zero.
    In the reflection Hilbert space of axis<nbsp><math|a>, let
    <math|v<around|(|s|)>> be a one-insertion vector translated by
    <math|s*e<rsub|b>>; by the derivative law of
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> its difference quotients converge,
    against a total set of vectors, to the vector of the differentiated
    label, which is continuous in<nbsp><math|s> by Gram continuity; hence
    <math|v> is smooth. Its Gram kernel depends on <math|s<rprime|'>-s>, and
    its Taylor coefficients at zero are those of
    <math|G<rsup|<math-up|rot>>>, which is analytic.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G-analytic> makes<nbsp><math|v> real analytic.
    Pairing with a fixed exterior word proves that an angular sum is analytic
    in the position of an insertion that is strictly earliest or latest in
    some coordinate.

    <em|Step 4: from a line to arbitrary configurations.> If the angular sums
    vanish for all labels at one end of a segment along which each of them is
    analytic, they vanish along it, and so do all their jets at the other
    end. Fix a configuration with distinct first coordinates; such
    configurations are dense. Treat its points one at a time, always taking
    the unmoved point with the smallest first coordinate; it is strictly
    earliest in axis<nbsp>1, since every moved point has first coordinate
    larger than all original ones. Move it along<nbsp><math|e<rsub|2>> until
    its second coordinate exceeds all others; then
    along<nbsp><math|e<rsub|1>> until its first coordinate exceeds all
    others, remaining strictly latest in axis<nbsp>2; then set its
    coordinates <math|2,3,4> to zero in turn, remaining strictly latest in
    axis<nbsp>1. Each segment is separated and moves an insertion that is
    strictly extreme in some axis, so Step<nbsp>3 applies along it. The final
    configuration lies on the first axis, where all jets vanish. Reversing
    the path proves the angular identity at the given configuration, and
    continuity extends it to<nbsp><math|<Conf|n>>.

    <em|Step 5: the bracket identity.> The defect
    <math|<around|[|<math-ss|D><rsub|K>,<math-ss|D><rsub|L>|]>-<math-ss|D><rsub|<around|[|K,L|]>>>
    has vanishing correlation sums by the commutator of two angular
    identities; it is real, alternating in<nbsp><math|K,L>, covariant under
    signed permutations of determinant one, kills the unit and preserves the
    filtration. The finite free tests<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-erasure-test> are
    injective on this defect space, and their injectivity transfers
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth>; pulling the mixed defect back by the stable
    frames makes it zero in large degree, and injective inclusions give the
    lower degrees. Thus <math|<around|[|<math-ss|D><rsub|K>,<math-ss|D><rsub|L>|]>=<math-ss|D><rsub|<around|[|K,L|]>>>.

    <em|Step 6: the orthogonal-group representation.> Let
    <math|C<rsub|i>=<math-ss|L><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K<rsub|i>|)>>
    and <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,i>=<around|(|<scaleframe|k|d>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|D><rsub|K<rsub|i>><scaleframe|k|d>>
    for the six plane generators; <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k>\<to\>C>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-charge-limits>. On the endomorphism space define the
    relative generators and their quadratic operator

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-A\><rsub|k,i><around|(|X|)>=<math-ss|B><rsub|k,i>X-X*C<rsub|i>,<space|2em><math-up|Cas><rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i><rsup|2>,
    </equation*>

    and <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>>, <math|<math-up|Cas>> with <math|C> in place
    of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k>>. By Step<nbsp>5,
    <math|K<rsub|i>\<mapsto\>\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i>> is a Lie-algebra
    representation, so <math|<math-up|Cas><rsub|k>> commutes with every
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i>>: in the commutator, the antisymmetric
    structure constants cancel in pairs. An invariant inner product makes
    <math|<math-up|Cas>> self-adjoint, with kernel the commutant of the free
    action, and a real polynomial<nbsp><math|z> with <math|z<around|(|0|)>=1>
    gives the kernel projection <math|P=z<around|(|<math-up|Cas>|)>>. We
    first construct projections <math|P<rsub|k>\<to\>P> commuting with the
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i>>, then show that the relative generators vanish
    on their ranges. Iterate <math|\<varphi\><around|(|t|)>=3*t<rsup|2>-2*t<rsup|3>>
    on <math|z<around|(|<math-up|Cas><rsub|k>|)>>. The identities

    <\equation*>
      \<varphi\><around|(|t|)>-t=<around|(|1-2*t|)>*<around|(|t<rsup|2>-t|)>,<space|2em>\<varphi\><around|(|t|)><rsup|2>-\<varphi\><around|(|t|)>=<around|(|4*<around|(|t<rsup|2>-t|)>-3|)>*<around|(|t<rsup|2>-t|)><rsup|2>
    </equation*>

    show that, near<nbsp><math|P>, the errors square and the displacements
    have a convergent geometric sum; the limits are projections
    <math|P<rsub|k>\<to\>P>, polynomial limits in
    <math|<math-up|Cas><rsub|k>>, hence commuting with
    every<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i>>. The restrictions of the
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i>> to <math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P<rsub|k>>
    satisfy the plane commutator relations and tend to zero, since
    <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|i>> vanishes on<nbsp><math|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P>.
    Every plane generator is a commutator of two others, so

    <\equation*>
      m<rsub|k>=max<rsub|i>\<\|\|\>\<cal-A\><rsub|k,i>\|<rsub|<math-up|ran><space|0.17em>P<rsub|k>>\<\|\|\>\<longrightarrow\>0,<space|2em>m<rsub|k>\<leqslant\>2*m<rsub|k><rsup|2>,
    </equation*>

    and <math|m<rsub|k>=0> eventually. Therefore
    <math|f<rsub|k>=P<rsub|k><around|(|I|)>\<to\>I> is invertible and
    <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|k,i>f<rsub|k>=f<rsub|k>*C<rsub|i>>. All operations
    preserve the real signed commutant. Set
    <math|\<cal-V\><rsub|d>=<scaleframe|k|d>f<rsub|k>> for one
    large<nbsp><math|k>, and

    <\equation*>
      R<rsub|d><around|(|h|)>=\<cal-V\><rsub|d>*R<rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|h|)>*\<cal-V\><rsub|d><rsup|-1>.
    </equation*>

    This is continuous and real, extends the signed action, and has
    generator<nbsp><math|<math-ss|D>>. The global angular identity makes
    <math|S*<around|(|R<around|(|e<rsup|t*K>|)>*A;e<rsup|t*K>*x|)>> constant
    in<nbsp><math|t>; plane rotations and coordinate reflections
    generate<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>>, proving covariance. Covariance,
    unit deletion, inclusion and differentiation give the unit, inclusion and
    vector laws by injectivity of responses, with
    <math|<math-ss|L>=<math-ss|D>> and the same<nbsp><math|c<rsub|\<star\>>>
    in every degree.
  </proof>

  Put <math|J<rsub|0>=c<rsub|\<star\>><rsup|-1>*J> and define, with
  probability Haar measure on<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:G-average>T<rsub|a*b>=<big|int><rsub|O<around|(|4|)>><big|sum><rsub|i,j>h<rsub|a*i>*h<rsub|b*j>*R<rsub|4><around|(|h|)><around|(|J<rsub|0>|)><rsub|i*j>*<space|0.17em>d*h.
  </equation>

  This average imposes exact tensor covariance while retaining the normalized
  shell actions.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Normalized tensor><label|lem:G3.2> The tensor<nbsp><math|T>
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-average> satisfies
    <math|R<rsub|4><around|(|h|)>*T<rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>h<rsub|i*a>*h<rsub|j*b>*T<rsub|i*j>>
    for <math|h\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>>. It is centered, nonzero, real,
    symmetric and conserved, and has both normalized shell Ward identities of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The integrand is a continuous function on a compact group with values in
    a finite-dimensional space; Haar invariance gives the tensor law. Reality
    and symmetry pass through the integral, and the vector law for
    derivatives gives conservation. Changing variables <math|y=h*z> in each
    transformed shell and using covariance gives the translation and angular
    actions <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>> and
    <math|<math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K|)>>; for the latter use
    <math|R<around|(|h|)><math-ss|L><around|(|h<rsup|-1>*K*h|)>=<math-ss|L><around|(|K|)>*R<around|(|h|)>>.
    Integration preserves both identities, and the positional derivative law
    of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> identifies the translation action
    with <math|\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|0><rsup|b>>*S>. The tensor is
    centered: covariance gives <math|S<rsub|1>*<around|(|R<rsub|4><around|(|h|)>*J<rsub|i*j>|)>=S<rsub|1><around|(|J<rsub|i*j>|)>=0>
    in every integrand. If <math|T=0>, its translation shell identity would
    give <math|S*<around|(|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b>*A|)><around|(|x<rsub|0>|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|x|)>|)>=0>
    for every label and separated spectator list;
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10> would give
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|b>=0> in degree four,
    contradicting<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> and nonzero free
    differentiation of a quadratic curvature polynomial.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1> gives the representations and covariance,
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2> gives the tensor with the stated
    properties.
  </render-proof>

  Finally the charge normalization removes the factors<nbsp><math|c<rsub|k>>
  from stress insertions; no new choice depending on the arity or derivative
  order is needed.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Transported infinitesimal actions><label|lem:G3.3> Along the
    sequence of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>, for every fixed
    <math|d,a,K>,

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|k><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d+1>|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a><scaleframe|k|d>\<longrightarrow\>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>,<space|2em><around|(|<scaleframe|k|d>|)><rsup|-1><math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K|)><scaleframe|k|d>\<longrightarrow\><math-ss|L><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|Max>><around|(|K|)>
    </equation*>

    in operator norm, and <math|<around|(|<scaleframe|k|4>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*T<rsub|a*b>|)>\<to\><maxwellstress><rsub|a*b>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The derivative limit is<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit>; the rotation
    limit follows from<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-charge-limits> and
    <math|c<rsub|k>\<to\>c<rsub|\<star\>>>. By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2> the tensor<nbsp><math|T> is centered,
    nonzero, signed-tensorial and conserved; since <math|J\<ne\>0> spans the
    line of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>, <math|J=c*T> for a real
    <math|c\<ne\>0>, and comparing translation shells gives
    <math|c=c<rsub|\<star\>>>. Consequently

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|<scaleframe|k|4>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*T<rsub|a*b>|)>=<frac|c<rsub|k>|c<rsub|\<star\>>><around|(|<scaleframe|k|4>|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|c<rsub|k><rsup|-1>*r<rsub|k><rsup|4>*J<rsub|a*b>|)>\<longrightarrow\><maxwellstress><rsub|a*b>.
    </equation*>
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>>
    Use the convergent free preimages of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.3> in
    the chosen stress slots and the fixed free labels in the others.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-smooth> gives convergence of every positional
    derivative, uniformly on every separated compact set. The derivative
    differentiates <math|x\<mapsto\>S*<around|(|\<b-A\><rsub|k>;r<rsub|k>*x|)>>,
    so all chain-rule factors are included. The sequence is the refinement
    chosen in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>; each fixed arity, degree and
    derivative order is covered on a tail of that single sequence.
  </render-proof>

  <section|Phases and reconstruction><label|sec:partII-H>

  We prove the phase decomposition of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH> and
  the reconstruction of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>, starting from the
  correlations of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:IV>, the quotient fields of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>, the slab operators of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>, the rotations of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1> and the tensor of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>. Three different objects appear, and only
  the first two are used here.

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-lborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-rborder|0ln>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|4|4|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-width|0.36par>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-width|0.55par>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|2|2|cell-hyphen|t>|<table|<row|<cell|object>|<cell|role>>|<row|<cell|separated
    correlations <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>> of a phase>|<cell|Euclidean
    correlations, with reflection positivity on positive-time words
    (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-H.1>)>>|<row|<cell|ordered-time
    continuations <math|<math-ss|F><rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>> and boundary
    distributions <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>>>|<cell|construction of the
    tempered Wightman functions and the relativistic fields
    (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-H.2>)>>|<row|<cell|contact extensions
    of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT>>|<cell|a specified Euclidean
    extension through collisions with controlled local order
    (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-J>); not used in the
    reconstruction>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The reconstruction thus proceeds from separated Euclidean correlations and
  slab bounds; it does not require a positive Euclidean extension across
  collision diagonals.

  We keep the words, reflections <math|\<vartheta\>,\<theta\>>, insertion
  rule and slab operators <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:G-coordinate>.

  <subsection|Compression and phases><label|sec:partII-H.1>

  Put <math|P<rsub|0>=P<rsub|4><rsup|0>> and
  <math|\<Omega\>=\<Omega\><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> in this subsection,
  and write <math|U<rsub|y>>, <math|y<rsup|4>=0>, for spatial translations on
  the OS space. Compression does the following. A long empty time interval
  removes the positive-energy components:
  <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h+t><around|(|w|)>=<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>*e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>
  and <math|e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>\<to\>P<rsub|0>>. The
  surviving insertions <math|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>=P<rsub|0><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>\|<rsub|ker
  H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>> commute (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>),
  and a character of the algebra they generate assigns a compatible scalar to
  every compressed insertion; integrating over characters against the vacuum
  state gives the mixture formula. A phase is such a character, not a set of
  field configurations. Commutativity alone does not show that each character
  is reflection invariant, or that no field direction is lost in it: a
  lattice variance inequality gives the first, and the chessboard inequality
  preserves the rank of finite families of field tests
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>).

  Rotations make spatial translations trivial at zero energy; compression
  then removes slab width and placement.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Compression algebra><label|lem:H1.2> The operators
    <math|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>> are independent of the slab width and
    placement. They generate a separable commutative unital
    C<rsup|<math|\<ast\>>>-algebra<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> on
    <math|ker H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>, with cyclic and separating
    vector<nbsp><math|\<Omega\>>. Its character space<nbsp><math|<phases>> is
    compact metric, the vacuum state is given by a full-support probability
    measure<nbsp><math|\<nu\>>, and

    <\equation*>
      S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>=\<xi\><around|(|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>|)>,<space|2em>S<around|(|w|)>=<big|int><rsub|<phases>>\<xi\><around|(|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    For fixed words and large<nbsp><math|z<rsup|4>> the correlation is
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,U<rsub|<wide|z|\<vect\>>>*e<rsup|-<around|(|z<rsup|4>-c|)>*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>*v<rprime|'>|\<rangle\>>>,
    and strong heat convergence makes fixed time shifts negligible, uniformly
    in<nbsp><math|<wide|z|\<vect\>>>. Choose an orthogonal
    reflection<nbsp><math|K> taking <math|y+e<rsub|4>> to
    <math|<around|\||y+e<rsub|4>|\|>*e<rsub|4>>. Since
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|K*<around|(|y+e<rsub|4>|)>,K*e<rsub|4>|\<rangle\>>=1>,
    the ray <math|t*K*e<rsub|4>> has positive time component. By the
    covariance of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>, rotating the displacements
    <math|t*e<rsub|4>+y+e<rsub|4>> and<nbsp><math|t*e<rsub|4>> makes their
    difference purely temporal, so their correlations become equal in the
    limit; removing the extra<nbsp><math|e<rsub|4>> gives
    <math|U<rsub|y>*P<rsub|0>=P<rsub|0>> by density.

    The identities <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h+t><around|(|w|)>=<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>*e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>
    and <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h+t><around|(|\<tau\><rsub|t*e<rsub|4>>*w|)>=e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>>
    give width and placement independence of <math|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>>,
    and <math|M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>=P<rsub|0><around|[|w|]>>,
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=S<around|(|w|)>>.
    Successive use of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-laws> proves the cluster identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-clusters>lim<rsub|j\<to\>\<infty\>>
      S*<around*|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|1><around|(|j|)>>*w<rsub|1>*\<cdots\>*\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|m><around|(|j|)>>*w<rsub|m>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|1>|)>*\<cdots\>*M<rsup|0><around|(|w<rsub|m>|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation>

    whenever <math|<around|(|z<rsub|i+1><around|(|j|)>-z<rsub|i><around|(|j|)>|)><rsup|4>\<to\>\<infty\>>;
    in the induction, <math|\<psi\><rsub|j>\<to\>\<psi\>> and
    <math|t<rsub|j>\<to\>\<infty\>> imply
    <math|U<rsub|y<rsub|j>>*e<rsup|-t<rsub|j>*H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>*\<psi\><rsub|j>\<to\>P<rsub|0>*\<psi\>>
    for arbitrary<nbsp><math|y<rsub|j>>.

    Place four words at <math|0>, <math|j*<around|(|e<rsub|4>+2*e<rsub|1>|)>>,
    <math|j*<around|(|2*e<rsub|4>+e<rsub|1>|)>> and
    <math|3*j*<around|(|e<rsub|4>+e<rsub|1>|)>>. Exchanging the first and
    fourth axes interchanges the two middle time orders. Covariance
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-clusters> equate the resulting products after
    simultaneous rotation of their labels and points, and a second
    application with all displacements along <math|e<rsub|4>+e<rsub|1>>
    removes that rotation. So the middle factors commute on the dense span
    <math|P<rsub|0><around|[|w|]>=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>>. This
    also gives cyclicity, and the empty word gives the unit. If
    <math|a*\<Omega\>=0>, then <math|a*b*\<Omega\>=0> on this span, so
    <math|a=0>. Basis labels and countable dense position sets
    generate<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> by norm continuity. Gelfand
    theory and the vacuum state give the compact metric spectrum and the
    probability measure<nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorems<nbsp>2.4.1
    and<nbsp>2.8.2>; separation gives full support, since
    <math|<big|int><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|a|)>|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<nu\>=<around|\<\|\|\>|a*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
    vanishes only for <math|a=0>.
  </proof>

  Moment bounds in the vacuum state bound a compressed operator in every
  phase. This is the form in which lattice chessboard estimates enter here
  and in Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-I>\U<reference|sec:partII-J>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Vacuum moments bound compressed operators><label|lem:H-roots> Let
    <math|U\<in\>\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> and <math|B\<geqslant\>0> satisfy
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><rsup|n>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\>B<rsup|2*n>>
    for every <math|n\<in\><N>>. Then <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U|\<\|\|\>>=sup<rsub|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>B>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>, <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><rsup|n>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|><rsup|2*n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>>.
    If <math|<around|\||\<xi\><rsub|0><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<gtr\>b\<gtr\>B>,
    continuity gives an open neighbourhood<nbsp><math|O>
    of<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|0>> on which
    <math|<around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|>\<gtr\>b>, and full support
    gives <math|\<nu\><around|(|O|)>\<gtr\>0>. Then
    <math|\<nu\><around|(|O|)>*b<rsup|2*n>\<leqslant\>B<rsup|2*n>> for
    every<nbsp><math|n>, which is impossible. Gelfand theory identifies
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U|\<\|\|\>>> with
    <math|sup<rsub|\<xi\>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|>>.
  </proof>

  The moment hypothesis comes from the lattice as follows. Let a lattice word
  difference be interior with centered chessboard seminorm at
  most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|0>> in one cell, multiplied by exterior insertions
  with centered chessboard seminorms at most
  <math|B<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,B<rsub|L>> in disjoint finer cells, and let
  <math|U\<in\>\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> be its compressed limit along the ordered
  limit. Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> to <math|2*n> widely translated
  copies, alternately conjugated; take the ordered limits and then infinite
  translation separation, using<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-clusters>; reflection
  identifies the alternating copies with adjoints. This gives
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,<around|(|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*U|)><rsup|n>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|B<rsub|0>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l>|)><rsup|2*n>>,
  hence <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l>>.

  A generic grid word has positions in <math|a<rsub|k><Z><rsup|4>> with
  pairwise distinct coordinates along each axis. For real coefficient
  representatives<nbsp><math|<around|(|d<rsub|i>,c<rsub|i>|)>> write
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>> for the Wilson
  expectation of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:wilson>, and put
  <math|\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p><around|(|w|)>=<big|prod><rsub|i>O<rsup|<around|(|d<rsub|i>|)>><rsub|N,p><around|(|c<rsub|i>,x<rsub|i>/a<rsub|N>|)>>.
  The word differences used below are

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-real-approx>f<rsub|j,N,p,T>=\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>>*w|)>-\<cal-O\><rsub|N,p>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>>*\<vartheta\>*w|)>,<space|2em>z<rsub|j><around|(|T|)>=T*j*a<rsub|k>*<around|(|1,1,1,1|)>.
  </equation>

  For each degree bound and lower grid level<nbsp><math|m>,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-bounded-real> chooses <math|k\<geqslant\>m>
  before<nbsp><math|w>; these are the cofinal grids used below. For
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> it supplies real <math|g<rsub|j,N,p,T>> and a
  finite <math|R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>, independent of <math|N,p,T,j>, with
  <math|<around|\||g<rsub|j,N,p,T>|\|>\<leqslant\>R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>>
  eventually in the ordered cutoffs for each fixed<nbsp><math|T,j>, and, for
  <math|i\<less\>j> and large<nbsp><math|T>, eventually in the ordered
  cutoffs, <math|<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|g<rsub|i>*g<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>-<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>*f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>><rsub|N,p>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\>>;
  the threshold may depend on <math|T,i,j>. Bounded<nbsp><math|g<rsub|i>>
  control the diagonal variance terms, for which <math|f<rsub|i><rsup|2>> has
  no uniform bound.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Phase fields and selection><label|lem:H1.3> For every
    <math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>, its table satisfies E1\UE7, retains the
    inclusions, derivatives, conjugation, rotations and stress identities of
    the mixture, and

    <\equation*>
      <nullspace|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>=<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>*<space|2em><around|(|d\<in\><N>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Each separated value is continuous in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. For
    <math|\<nu\>>-almost every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, the time-axis OS ground
    space is one-dimensional.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|Reflection and rotation invariance.> We first prove

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-reflection>M<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<vartheta\>*w|)>=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>,<space|2em>M<rsup|0>*<around|(|h*w|)>=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*<space|1em><around|(|h\<in\>O<around|(|4|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For a real grid word set <math|Z=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>-M<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<vartheta\>*w|)>>.
    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-laws> gives <math|Z<rsup|\<ast\>>=-Z>, so
    <math|a=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,Z<rsup|2>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=-<around|\<\|\|\>|Z*\<Omega\>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>0>.
    Lattice variance gives

    <\equation*>
      0\<leqslant\><around*|\<langle\>|<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i=0><rsup|q>g<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>|\<rangle\>>\<leqslant\><around|(|q+1|)>*R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>+2*<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j><around*|(|<math-up|Re><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>*f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>+\<varepsilon\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For fixed <math|q,T>, intersect the finitely many eventual sets, take the
    ordered cutoffs, then <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>. Each pair expands into
    four separated moments, whose signed sum tends to<nbsp><math|a> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E3.1>, <eqref|eq:F-conf>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-clusters>. Thus <math|0\<leqslant\><around|(|q+1|)>*<around|[|R<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|2>+q*<around|(|a+\<varepsilon\>|)>|]>>;
    first <math|q\<to\>\<infty\>>, then <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>,
    gives <math|a\<geqslant\>0>, and separation of<nbsp><math|\<Omega\>>
    gives <math|Z=0>. Grid density, norm continuity and multilinearity give
    all words. For the second identity, the cluster identity gives
    simultaneous orthogonal invariance of two-factor vacuum entries along
    rays with positive time before and after rotation; such a ray exists
    unless time is reversed, when composition with<nbsp><math|\<vartheta\>>
    suffices. With <math|v=M<rsup|0><around|(|u|)>*\<Omega\>> and
    <math|x=M<rsup|0><around|(|w|)>*\<Omega\>>, simultaneous invariance and
    time-reflection invariance give <math|<around|\<langle\>|v,x|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|g<rsup|-1>*v,\<vartheta\>*g<rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|v,g*\<vartheta\>*g<rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>>.
    Density gives every hyperplane reflection, and an orthogonal map is a
    product of at most four reflections.

    <em|Euclidean properties.> Testing on positive words gives the
    permutation, unit and linearity identities and annihilates mixed null
    labels, so <math|<nullspace|d><around|(|S|)>\<subset\><nullspace|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>>.
    Character evaluation gives reality, covariance and smooth phase values,
    continuous in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. For positive
    words<nbsp><math|w<rsub|i>> in a common slab set
    <math|Y<rsub|i>=<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w<rsub|i>|)>\|<rsub|ker
    H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>; direct evaluation of the OS form gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|i,j><wide|c|\<bar\>><rsub|i>*c<rsub|j>*M<rsup|0>*<around|(|\<theta\>*w<rsub|i>*w<rsub|j>|)>=<around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>c<rsub|i>*Y<rsub|i>|)><rsup|\<ast\>><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|j>c<rsub|j>*Y<rsub|j>|)>\<geqslant\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Spectral permanence and character evaluation preserve
    positivity<nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorems<nbsp>2.5.1(1)
    and<nbsp>2.6.4>, and coordinate rotations give every axis; integrating
    the vectors of this positive Gram kernel against compact positive-time
    joint tests expresses the smeared OS form as a norm square. Inclusions
    and derivatives pass through <math|T\<mapsto\>\<xi\>*<around|(|P<rsub|0>*T\|<rsub|ker
    H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>|)>>. Put a stress shell and its insertions
    in one buffered slab; its weights vanish near collisions, so the operator
    integral exists, and testing against exterior positive words gives the
    identities of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>, which character
    evaluation transfers to every phase. The bound
    <math|<around|\||S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    also for derivative labels, gives E7 and separated-support integrability.

    <em|Equal null spaces.> Use the rows of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2-free-tests>, corrected lifts of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.10> and coefficient limits of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. Each row of trial<nbsp><math|\<ell\>>
    consists of a singleton<nbsp><math|u> and optional
    spectator<nbsp><math|v>, placed at the comparison points
    <math|r<rsub|\<ell\>>*y<rsup|\<ell\>,i>>. Write
    <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|I><rsup|\<ell\>>> for the Maxwell moment of the row
    labels in slots<nbsp><math|I> at the unscaled
    points<nbsp><math|y<rsup|\<ell\>,i>>, and set
    <math|s=\<cal-G\><rsup|\<ell\>><rsub|<around|{|u|}>>>,
    <math|s<rprime|'>=\<cal-G\><rsup|\<ell\>><rsub|<around|{|v|}>>> and
    <math|c=\<cal-G\><rsup|\<ell\>><rsub|<around|{|u,v|}>>-s*s<rprime|'>>,
    omitting terms with<nbsp><math|v> in singleton rows.
    In<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> put

    <\equation*>
      \<Delta\><rsub|1>=M<rsup|0><around|(|u|)>-s*1,<space|1em>\<Delta\><rsub|1><rprime|'>=M<rsup|0><around|(|v|)>-s<rprime|'>*1,<space|1em>\<Delta\><rsub|2>=M<rsup|0>*<around|(|u*v|)>-s*M<rsup|0><around|(|v|)>-s<rprime|'>*M<rsup|0><around|(|u|)>+<around|(|s*s<rprime|'>-c|)>*1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The polynomials of<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-relative> have centered chessboard
    seminorms at most <math|\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>+\<varepsilon\>>,
    preserved under signed images and conjugation by translating the periodic
    pattern; the lattice moment argument after
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-roots>, followed by
    <math|\<varepsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>, gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-moments><big|int><rsub|<phases>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|\<Delta\>|)>|\|><rsup|2*j>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>><rsup|2*j>*<space|2em><around|(|j\<in\><N>|)><space|0.17em>,
    </equation>

    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-roots> gives
    <math|<around|\||\<xi\><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|1>|)>|\|>,<around|\||\<xi\><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|1><rprime|'>|)>|\|>,<around|\||\<xi\><around|(|\<Delta\><rsub|2>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>>
    in every phase. Expanding <math|\<Delta\><rsub|2>> gives
    <math|<around|\||\<xi\>*<around|(|M<rsup|0>*<around|(|u*v|)>|)>-s*s<rprime|'>-c|\|>\<leqslant\>\<gamma\><rsub|\<ell\>>*<around|(|1+<around|\||s|\|>+<around|\||s<rprime|'>|\|>|)>>.
    Phase singleton and pair evaluations of the corrected lifts therefore
    converge to the injective free row map, whose positive minimum on the
    unit sphere makes them eventually injective. They factor through
    <math|V<rsub|d>/<nullspace|d><around|(|S<rsup|\<xi\>>|)>>; the first null
    inclusion and<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-dimension> bound its dimension above by
    <math|dim <maxwellspace|d>>, and injectivity bounds it below. Thus the
    null spaces coincide.

    <em|Almost every ground space.> Reflection positivity and E7 construct
    the phase heat semigroup as in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>. Write
    <math|<around|[|b|]><rsub|\<xi\>>> for a positive-word class and
    <math|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> for its ground projection.
    Strong heat convergence and the mixture identity give, by dominated
    convergence,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|<phases>><around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>><around|[|b|]><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0><around|[|b|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<big|int><rsub|<phases>><around|\||S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)><space|0.17em>,
    </equation*>

    the heat entries decreasing from their zero-time bounds. The integrands
    differ by <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>><around|[|b|]><rsub|\<xi\>>-S<rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>,
    which therefore vanishes almost everywhere. Rational configurations and
    basis coefficients give a countable dense family in every phase OS space,
    by Gram continuity; outside the union of its null sets the ground range
    is <math|<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>.
  </proof>

  Choose <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> from the full-measure set of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>; only its ground-space dimension enters
  this choice, and it is retained throughout. The slab bound passes to every
  phase with the same constants, by transferring the repeated insertion
  estimate through the character and applying the power argument of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1>.

  <\corollary>
    <dueto|Phase slab operators><label|cor:H-phase-slabs><label|cor:H-phase-slab>
    For every <math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>> the operators <math|A<rsub|w>>
    extend to bounded <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>>
    on <math|\<cal-H\><rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    satisfying<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-laws>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-slab-derivative>.
  </corollary>

  <\proof>
    In the phase OS space put <math|v<rsub|j>=<around|(|A<rsub|w<rsup|\<dagger\>>>*A<rsub|w>|)><rsup|j>*v>.
    The mixed-space map <math|Y<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>=<math-ss|B><around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>\|<rsub|ker
    H<rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>> equals
    <math|<around|(|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)><rsup|\<ast\>><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|)><rsup|j>*Y<around|(|v|)>>,
    so <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|[|v<rsub|j>|]><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=\<xi\>*<around|(|Y<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)><rsup|\<ast\>>*Y<around|(|v<rsub|j>|)>|)>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|Y<around|(|v|)><around|\<\|\|\>|<rsup|2>|\<\|\|\>><math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|4*j>>.
    The power argument gives <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|w|)>>
    of norm at most <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<math-ss|B><rsub|h><around|(|w|)>|\<\|\|\>>>,
    and the dense-vector argument gives its identities and norm derivatives.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH>>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2> gives the character space, the
    full-support probability measure and the mixture formula.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3> gives E1\UE7, equal null spaces, the
    common identities of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC> and
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>, covariance and phase continuity.
  </render-proof>

  <subsection|Reconstruction><label|sec:partII-H.2>

  Use the response labels<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\>> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>. With time reflection acting on labels
  through<nbsp><math|R<rsub|d>>, the star is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-star><around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d>*A|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=\<iota\><rsub|d>*<around*|(|R<rsub|d><around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|4><rsup|<math-up|refl>>|)>*<wide|A|\<bar\>>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  The boost generators <math|-i<math-ss|L><rsub|d><around|(|K<rsub|4*i>|)>>
  and the spatial rotations integrate to <math|S*L*<around|(|2,<C>|)>>, the
  simply connected cover of <math|S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>>; a full
  spatial turn acts trivially, so the action descends to
  <math|S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>>. Inclusions intertwine the actions, and
  the time-reflection sign of <math|K<rsub|4*i>> and antilinearity make the
  star<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-star> commute with boosts. Take
  <math|\<cal-P\>=S*O<rsup|+><around|(|1,3|)>\<ltimes\><R><rsup|4>> itself in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14b>, and
  <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|lab>>*<around|(|\<Lambda\>,z|)>> the continued
  label action of<nbsp><math|\<Lambda\>>. Let <math|\<frak-B\>> be the
  Borchers algebra of finite sequences <math|F=<around|(|F<rsub|0>,F<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>|)>>,
  <math|F<rsub|n>\<in\>\<cal-L\><rsup|\<otimes\>n>\<otimes\>\<cal-S\><rsub|n>>;
  its product concatenates label tensors and tensors the tests, and its
  antilinear star reverses order, applies the star<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-star> to
  labels and complex conjugation to tests. For a phase write
  <math|U<rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>>> for spatial translation
  on<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\><rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>, and put

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-V\><rsub|n>=<around*|{|p\<in\><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>:<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>=0,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|k=j><rsup|n>p<rsub|k>\<in\><forwardcone><around|(|j\<geqslant\>2|)>|}><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Fix a phase<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. The reconstruction follows the scheme of
  Osterwalder and Schrader<nbsp><cite|OsterwalderSchrader1973|OsterwalderSchrader1975>,
  with one change: in place of their growth condition, the slab bounds of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1> and Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:H-phase-slab>
  give the strip bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-poly>, from which the boundary
  distributions are obtained directly. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>
  constructs the continuation and the boundary distributions, with
  covariance, spectral support, positivity, continuity in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>
  and the barycenter formula. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-locality> proves
  locality, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-fields> realizes the fields,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.2> compares the Hilbert spaces,
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.3> proves the uniform low-energy bound and
  vacuum uniqueness, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-irreducible> proves
  irreducibility, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.4> continues the stress
  tensor.

  The requirements of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC> come from three
  sources. Reflection positivity gives the OS spaces, their heat semigroups
  and the positivity of the boundary distributions. Slab growth gives bounded
  insertions, hence the strip bound, tempered boundary values and continuity
  in the phase. The rotations of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>, continued
  to boosts, give Lorentz covariance and, through imaginary boosts, locality.
  Vacuum uniqueness first holds only for <math|\<nu\>>-almost every phase
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>). The low-energy estimate of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.3> is uniform in the phase; with the
  continuity in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>> of the vacuum-subtracted heat pairings and
  the full support of<nbsp><math|\<nu\>>, it removes the exceptional set.
  That estimate, <math|\<mu\><rsub|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>*<around|{|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>>,
  is polynomial in the energy: no positive lower bound on nonzero energies
  (spectral gap) is used or proved.

  Positive-height operators give positive boundary distributions without a
  Euclidean extension across collisions.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Continuation and boundary distributions><label|lem:H2.1> For every
    <math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>> and label list<nbsp><math|a>, the functions
    <math|<math-ss|F><rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n><around|(|a;\<cdot\>|)>>
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-continuation> satisfy
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D15>, and their boundary values in
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14f> define joint Schwartz distributions
    <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<psi\>|)>>, bounded by one
    Schwartz seminorm uniformly in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. These are Poincaré
    covariant for the label action above, their Fourier transforms are
    supported in<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\><rsub|n>>, and
    <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F|)>\<geqslant\>0> for
    <math|F\<in\>\<frak-B\>>. Each <math|\<xi\>\<mapsto\>W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<psi\>|)>>
    is continuous, and <math|<big|int><rsub|<phases>>W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<psi\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>=W<rsup|<math-up|mix>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<psi\>|)>>.
  </lemma>

  Here <math|W<rsup|<math-up|mix>><rsub|n>> is the boundary distribution of
  the mixed continuation, defined below.

  <\proof>
    <em|Positive imaginary times.> Write <math|e<rsup|-t*H<rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>>
    for the phase heat semigroup. For a label<nbsp><math|a> put
    <math|B<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>><around|(|a|)>=<math-ss|B><rsub|2*\<epsilon\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a*<around|(|\<epsilon\>*e<rsub|4>|)>|)>>;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1> and Corollary<nbsp><reference|cor:H-phase-slab>
    give <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|B<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>><around|(|a|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|a>*\<epsilon\><rsup|-q<rsub|a>>>
    for <math|0\<less\>\<epsilon\>\<less\>1>, uniformly
    in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. For <math|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z\<gtr\>2*\<epsilon\>>
    set <math|G<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|z,y|)>=e<rsup|-<around|(|<math-up|Im><space|0.17em>z-2*\<epsilon\>|)>*H<rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>*e<rsup|i<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>z*H<rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>*U<rsub|y><rsup|\<xi\>>>,
    and for <math|n\<geqslant\>1>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-continuation><math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a;z,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>,B<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>><around|(|a<rsub|1>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j=2><rsup|n><around*|[|G<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>>*<around|(|z<rsub|j>-z<rsub|j-1>,<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|j>-<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|j-1>|)>*B<rsub|\<xi\>,\<epsilon\>><around|(|a<rsub|j>|)>|]>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>,
    </equation>

    with <math|0\<less\>\<epsilon\>\<less\>1> and
    <math|4*\<epsilon\>\<less\>min<rsub|j><math-up|Im><around|(|z<rsub|j+1>-z<rsub|j>|)>>;
    set <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|0><rsup|\<xi\>>=1>. At imaginary times the
    insertion rule gives<nbsp><math|S<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>>, independently
    of<nbsp><math|\<epsilon\>>, and the identity theorem extends this
    agreement. The function is holomorphic in the times and continuous in
    space; extend it by zero outside ordered times. Taking
    <math|\<epsilon\>=\<delta\>/8> gives, uniformly in<nbsp><math|x>
    and<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-poly><around|\||<math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a;x<rsup|4>+i*y,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<delta\><rsup|-m>*<space|1em><around*|(|0\<less\>\<delta\>\<leqslant\>1,y<rsub|j+1>-y<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>\<delta\>|)>.
    </equation>

    <em|Boundary distributions.> Write <math|\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y;f|)>=<big|int><math-ss|F><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|a;x<rsup|4>+i*y,<wide|x|\<vect\>>|)>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>
    and <math|\<cal-G\>=-i*<big|sum><rsub|j>j*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|j><rsup|4>>>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-poly> and holomorphy, integration by parts gives
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y+t*\<varrho\>;f|)>=\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y+t*\<varrho\>;\<cal-G\>*f|)>>
    with <math|\<varrho\><rsub|j>=j>, and repeated integration gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-boundary>\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y;f|)>=<big|sum><rsub|k=0><rsup|m><frac|<around|(|-1|)><rsup|k>|k!>*\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y+\<varrho\>;\<cal-G\><rsup|k>*f|)>-<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><frac|<around|(|-t|)><rsup|m>|m!>*\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y+t*\<varrho\>;\<cal-G\><rsup|m+1>*f|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The integrand is bounded by <math|C*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-G\><rsup|m+1>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>/m>!,
    uniformly in<nbsp><math|y,\<xi\>>. At <math|y=0> this
    defines<nbsp><math|W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>>, and dominated convergence
    gives every ordered boundary limit and a common Schwartz seminorm bound.

    <em|Dependence on the phase.> The mixed operators of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.1> define
    <math|<math-ss|F><rsup|<math-up|mix>>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-continuation>
    and <math|W<rsup|<math-up|mix>>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary>, with the
    same bound. The mixture formula at imaginary times extends to strict
    times by the identity theorem and integration of Cauchy's formula. For
    continuity set <math|z<rsub|j>=i*y<rsub|j>+\<varsigma\>*x<rsub|j><rsup|4>>;
    the common strip bound gives a normal family in<nbsp><math|\<varsigma\>>,
    and if <math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ell\>>\<to\>\<xi\>>, each subsequential limit
    agrees on the imaginary segment by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>, hence
    everywhere. Fubini and the common integrable bound
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary> give continuity of the distributions and
    the barycenter formula.

    <em|Lorentz covariance.> Display the <math|j>th label in
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y;f;\<ldots\>,a<rsub|j>,\<ldots\>|)>>.
    Differentiating Euclidean <math|<around|(|4,i|)>> covariance and using
    the time Cauchy\URiemann identity gives

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j>\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|y;f;\<ldots\>,-i<math-ss|L><around|(|K<rsub|4*i>|)>*a<rsub|j>,\<ldots\>|)>=\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around*|(|y;<big|sum><rsub|j><around|[|x<rsub|j><rsup|i>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|j><rsup|4>>+<around|(|x<rsub|j><rsup|4>+i*y<rsub|j>|)>*\<partial\><rsub|x<rsub|j><rsup|i>>|]>*f|)>,
    </equation*>

    first at imaginary times, then by holomorphy. At the
    boundary<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary> removes<nbsp><math|y>, so along a
    real boost the derivative of simultaneously transformed labels and tests
    is zero. Spatial rotations and translations keep their Euclidean
    identities.

    <em|Spectral support.> Define <math|<wide|W|~><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>=W<rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>(<fourierplus|b>(-\<cdot\><around|)||)>>.
    For compactly supported smooth frequency tests<nbsp><math|b>,
    differentiating the height and the exponential multiplier gives
    cancellation, hence

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-Laplace><wide|W|~><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>=\<phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around*|(|y;<fourierplus|<around|(|e<rsup|<big|sum><rsub|j>y<rsub|j>*p<rsub|j><rsup|4>>*b|)>>(-\<cdot\><around*|)||)><space|0.17em>,|\<nobracket\>>
    </equation>

    including the zero endpoint. Sending ordered heights to infinity kills
    negative time-tail momenta, and translation and Lorentz covariance give
    support in<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\><rsub|n>>.

    <em|Positivity.> Apply the propagators and insertions
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-continuation> to the vacuum, with an initial heat
    interval, and smear against a word test<nbsp><math|b>. Reflection and the
    insertion rule identify mixed-height Gram entries with
    <math|<math-ss|F><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>>, whose
    limits as both height lists vanish are
    <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|b<rsup|\<ast\>>*c|)>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary>. The vectors are therefore Cauchy, and
    their limits <math|v<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>\<in\>\<cal-H\><rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
    have this Gram form; the common seminorm bound and product-test density
    extend it to joint tests. Hence <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|F<rsup|\<ast\>>*F|)>=<around|\<\|\|\>|<big|sum><rsub|b>c<rsub|b>*v<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\>0>.
  </proof>

  Locality is the exchange of two adjacent spacelike points. For
  <math|1\<leqslant\>j\<less\>n> let <math|s> be the transposition of
  <math|j> and <math|j+1>, write <math|x<rsup|s>> and <math|a<rsup|s>> for
  configurations and label lists with these entries exchanged, and put
  <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|j>=<around|{|x\<in\><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>:<minksquare><around|(|x<rsub|j+1>-x<rsub|j>|)>\<less\>0|}>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Exchange of adjacent spacelike points><label|lem:H-locality> For
    every <math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>, label list<nbsp><math|a>,
    <math|1\<leqslant\>j\<less\>n> and <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|n>>
    supported in<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\><rsub|j>>,

    <\equation*>
      W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<psi\>|)>=W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a<rsup|s>;\<psi\><rsup|s>|)>,<space|2em>\<psi\><rsup|s><around|(|x|)>=\<psi\><around|(|x<rsup|s>|)>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  The two orders give two holomorphic functions on two different tubes.
  Opposite imaginary boosts move a configuration with tied heights into the
  two tubes and so define two holomorphic germs at that configuration. At
  zero real times both germs restrict to the same Euclidean correlation, by
  permutation symmetry, so they coincide; the equality is carried to
  arbitrary real times, and partial boundary values give the claim.

  <\proof>
    <em|The tube function.> Let <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>=<around|{|w\<in\><around|(|<C><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>:<math-up|Im><around|(|w<rsub|k+1>-w<rsub|k>|)>\<in\><openforwardcone>,k\<less\>n|}>>,
    where <math|<openforwardcone>> is the interior
    of<nbsp><math|<forwardcone>>. Let <math|\<chi\>=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<math-up|dist><around|(|p,\<cal-V\><rsub|n>|)>\<less\>2|}>>\<ast\>\<varrho\><rsub|1>>
    with <math|\<varrho\><rsub|1>\<geqslant\>0> smooth, of unit integral and
    supported in the unit ball; then <math|\<chi\>=1>
    near<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\><rsub|n>>, has bounded derivatives, and vanishes
    at distance greater than three. For <math|w\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>
    define

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-W\><rsub|a><around|(|w|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-4*n>*<wide|W|~><rsub|n><rsup|\<xi\>>*<around*|(|\<chi\><around|(|p|)>*e<rsup|i*<big|sum><rsub|k>p<rsub|k>\<cdot\>w<rsub|k>>|)>.
    </equation*>

    With <math|q<rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|l\<geqslant\>k>p<rsub|l>>, one has
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|k>p<rsub|k>\<cdot\><math-up|Im><space|0.17em>w<rsub|k>=<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>2>q<rsub|k>\<cdot\><math-up|Im><around|(|w<rsub|k>-w<rsub|k-1>|)>\<geqslant\>c<around|\||p|\|>>
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\><rsub|n>>, and up to a bounded subtraction on
    <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<chi\>>; so the test is a holomorphic
    family of Schwartz functions and <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|a>> is holomorphic
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>. By<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-Laplace>,
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|a><around|(|w|)>=<math-ss|F><rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n><around|(|a;w<rsup|4>,<wide|w|\<vect\>>|)>>
    when the imaginary parts are purely temporal and strictly ordered; in
    particular <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|a>> equals
    <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<cdot\>|)>> at ordered
    Euclidean points <math|w<rsub|k>=<around|(|i*t<rsub|k>,<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|k>|)>>.
    For nondecreasing heights<nbsp><math|y>, the damping multipliers
    <math|e<rsup|-<big|sum><rsub|k>y<rsub|k>*p<rsup|4><rsub|k>>=e<rsup|-<big|sum><rsub|k\<geqslant\>2><around|(|y<rsub|k>-y<rsub|k-1>|)>*q<rsub|k><rsup|4>>>
    are bounded with bounded derivatives on<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\><rsub|n>>, so
    they act continuously on Schwartz tests. This defines partial boundary
    distributions <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n,y>*<around|(|a;\<cdot\>|)>>,
    continuous in<nbsp><math|y>, equal to
    <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<cdot\>|)>> at <math|y=0>, and
    equal to <math|<big|int>\<cal-W\><rsub|a>*<around|(|x+i*y|)>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>
    for strictly increasing<nbsp><math|y>. Here <math|x+i*y> means
    <math|<around|(|x<rsub|k><rsup|4>+i*y<rsub|k>,<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|k>|)><rsub|k>>.

    <em|Complex boosts.> Real Lorentz covariance in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1> gives
    <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|a>*<around|(|\<Lambda\>*w|)>=\<rho\><rsub|n><rsup|\<vee\>><around|(|\<Lambda\>|)>*\<cal-W\><rsub|a><around|(|w|)>>
    for real<nbsp><math|\<Lambda\>> and <math|w\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>,
    where <math|\<rho\><rsub|n><rsup|\<vee\>>> acts on label tensors. For a
    spatial unit vector<nbsp><math|e> and rapidity<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>, the
    label action of the boost <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<beta\>>>
    along<nbsp><math|e> is <math|exp <around|(|-i*\<beta\>*<big|sum><rsub|i>e<rsub|i><math-ss|L><around|(|K<rsub|4*i>|)>|)>>
    on finite-dimensional spaces, an entire function of<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>.
    Hence the covariance identity holds for complex <math|\<beta\>>, by the
    identity theorem in<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>, whenever
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|\<beta\><rprime|'>>*w\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>> along
    a path from <math|0> to<nbsp><math|\<beta\>>.

    <em|Two germs.> Fix heights with <math|y<rsub|j>=y<rsub|j+1>> and
    <math|y<rsub|k>\<less\>y<rsub|k+1>> for the other<nbsp><math|k>, and
    <math|x\<in\>\<cal-G\><rsub|j>>, so <math|w<rsub|0>=x+i*y> lies on the
    boundary of<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>. Write
    <math|x<rsub|j+1>-x<rsub|j>=<around|(|\<tau\>,<wide|v|\<vect\>>|)>> with
    <math|<around|\||\<tau\>|\|>\<less\><around|\||<wide|v|\<vect\>>|\|>> and
    put <math|e=<wide|v|\<vect\>>/<around|\||<wide|v|\<vect\>>|\|>>. The
    imaginary boost <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i*b>> along<nbsp><math|e> gives
    this difference the imaginary part <math|sin
    b*<space|0.17em><around|(|<around|\||<wide|v|\<vect\>>|\|>,\<tau\>*e|)>>,
    in time and space components, which lies in<nbsp><math|<openforwardcone>>
    for <math|0\<less\>b\<less\>\<pi\>>; for small<nbsp><math|b> the other
    differences stay in<nbsp><math|<openforwardcone>>. So, for a small
    <math|b<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> and a small neighbourhood<nbsp><math|N>
    of<nbsp><math|w<rsub|0>>, <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i*b<rsub|0>>*w\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>
    for <math|w\<in\>N>, and <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|i*b>*w\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>
    for <math|0\<leqslant\>b\<leqslant\>b<rsub|0>> if moreover
    <math|w\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>; the opposite boost
    <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|-i*b>> moves the exchanged configuration
    into<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>. Define, near<nbsp><math|w<rsub|0>>,

    <\equation*>
      G<rsub|+><around|(|w|)>=\<rho\><rsub|n><rsup|\<vee\>><around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|i*b<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|-1>*\<cal-W\><rsub|a>*<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|i*b<rsub|0>>*w|)>,<space|2em>G<rsub|-><around|(|w|)>=\<rho\><rsub|n><rsup|\<vee\>><around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|-i*b<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|-1>*\<cal-W\><rsub|a<rsup|s>><around*|(|<around|(|\<Lambda\><rsub|-i*b<rsub|0>>*w|)><rsup|s>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Both are holomorphic in a neighbourhood of<nbsp><math|w<rsub|0>>. By the
    complex boost identity along <math|<around|[|0,b<rsub|0>|]>>,
    <math|G<rsub|+>=\<cal-W\><rsub|a>> on the part of that neighbourhood
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>, and
    <math|G<rsub|-><around|(|w|)>=\<cal-W\><rsub|a<rsup|s>><around|(|w<rsup|s>|)>>
    where <math|w<rsup|s>\<in\>\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>.

    <em|The Wick slice.> Suppose first that all real times of<nbsp><math|x>
    vanish. Then <math|w<rsub|0>=<around|(|i*y<rsub|k>,<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|k>|)><rsub|k>>
    is a separated Euclidean configuration, since
    <math|<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|j>\<ne\><wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|j+1>>. The
    Wick slice <math|<around|{|<around|(|i*t<rsub|k>,<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|k>|)>|}>>
    is a real subspace of dimension <math|4*n> whose complex span is
    <math|<around|(|<C><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>. Near<nbsp><math|w<rsub|0>> on
    the slice, points with <math|t<rsub|j>\<less\>t<rsub|j+1>> lie
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>, where
    <math|G<rsub|+>=S<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<cdot\>|)>>; points
    with <math|t<rsub|j>\<gtr\>t<rsub|j+1>> have exchanged configuration
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>>, where
    <math|G<rsub|->=S<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n><around|(|a<rsup|s>;|(>\<cdot\><around|)|<rsup|s>|)>=S<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<cdot\>|)>>
    by permutation symmetry. Since <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>> is smooth
    at<nbsp><math|w<rsub|0>>, all jets of <math|G<rsub|+>> and
    <math|G<rsub|->> along the slice at<nbsp><math|w<rsub|0>>, computed as
    limits from these open half-spaces, equal those of
    <math|S<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>*<around|(|a;\<cdot\>|)>>. Real jets along
    the slice are restrictions of complex jets, and the slice spans
    <math|<around|(|<C><rsup|4>|)><rsup|n>>; so all complex derivatives of
    <math|G<rsub|+>-G<rsub|->> vanish at<nbsp><math|w<rsub|0>>, and
    <math|G<rsub|+>=G<rsub|->> near<nbsp><math|w<rsub|0>>.

    <em|Propagation.> For general <math|x\<in\>\<cal-G\><rsub|j>> consider
    <math|x<around|(|t|)>=<around|(|t*x<rsub|k><rsup|4>,<wide|x|\<vect\>><rsub|k>|)><rsub|k>>,
    <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,1|]>>; it stays
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\><rsub|j>>, since
    <math|<around|\||t*\<tau\>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|\||\<tau\>|\|>\<less\><around|\||<wide|v|\<vect\>>|\|>>,
    and the strict height margins persist. Along this compact path both germs
    are defined at every point. Two germs of the same kind at nearby points
    agree on the overlap of their domains, since both extend the same
    function from the open set <math|\<cal-T\><rsub|n>> (respectively its
    exchanged image), which accumulates at each point. A finite chain of
    overlapping neighbourhoods carries <math|G<rsub|+>=G<rsub|->> from
    <math|t=0> to <math|t=1>.

    <em|Partial boundary values.> For <math|f> supported in a small ball
    around <math|x\<in\>\<cal-G\><rsub|j>>, the representation of
    <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n,y>> by the tube function and continuity up to
    the tied heights give <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n,y>*<around|(|a;f|)>=<big|int>G<rsub|+>*<around|(|x<rprime|'>+i*y|)>*f<around|(|x<rprime|'>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x<rprime|'>>,
    and likewise <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n,y<rsup|s>>*<around|(|a<rsup|s>;f<rsup|s>|)>=<big|int>G<rsub|->*<around|(|x<rprime|'>+i*y|)>*f<around|(|x<rprime|'>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x<rprime|'>>.
    These agree, and a partition of unity gives
    <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n,y>*<around|(|a;\<psi\>|)>=W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n,y<rsup|s>>*<around|(|a<rsup|s>;\<psi\><rsup|s>|)>>
    for compactly supported <math|\<psi\>> with support
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\><rsub|j>>; for general
    <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|n>> with support
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-G\><rsub|j>>, multiply by cutoffs equal to one on
    growing balls, which converge in<nbsp><math|\<cal-S\><rsub|n>> without
    enlarging the support. Let the heights tend to zero along such tied
    height lists; continuity of the partial boundary distributions gives the
    claim.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Relativistic fields><label|lem:H-fields> For every
    <math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>, the boundary distributions of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1> define a faithful theory
    on<nbsp><math|\<cal-L\>> satisfying W1\UW3 and the spectral
    representation and spectral condition
    <math|<math-ss|E><around|(|<R><rsup|4>\<setminus\><forwardcone>|)>=0> of
    W0, with continuation the functions <math|<math-ss|F><rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>>
    of that lemma. Its adjoint and Lorentz label maps are the common maps
    specified above.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Complete the form <math|W<rsup|\<xi\>>*<around|(|F<rsup|\<ast\>>*G|)>>
    on<nbsp><math|\<frak-B\>> modulo null vectors to
    obtain<nbsp><math|\<cal-H\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, with
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|a,f|)><around|[|b|]>=<around|[|<around|(|a,f|)>*b|]>>.
    For null<nbsp><math|x> the inserted vector pairs with<nbsp><math|c> as
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|x,<around|(|a<rsup|\<ast\>>,<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>*c|\<rangle\>>=0>;
    taking <math|c> to be that vector preserves nullity and gives the adjoint
    law W1. Test continuity gives strongly continuous covariance unitaries,
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1> gives W2. Translations integrate to a
    nondegenerate representation of <math|C<rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>=C<rsub|0><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>><nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Theorem<nbsp>3.7.11
    and equation<nbsp>(3.89)> with spectral measure
    from<nbsp><cite-detail|Landsman1998|Proposition<nbsp>4.2.3>, and the
    Fourier support in<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\><rsub|n>> puts the spectrum
    in<nbsp><math|<forwardcone>>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-locality>,
    applied to adjacent pairs inside vacuum expectations of words, gives W3
    on the word domain. The boundary condition of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D14f> is the definition
    of<nbsp><math|W<rsup|\<xi\>><rsub|n>> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>.
    A label acting as zero has zero Euclidean correlations
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-Laplace>, symmetry and continuity;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3> and injectivity of responses give
    faithfulness.
  </proof>

  Surjectivity of the comparison map transfers ground-space information
  between the two Hilbert spaces.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|OS comparison><label|lem:H2.2> For every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, there
    is a unique linear isometry <math|<math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>:\<cal-H\><rsub|\<xi\>>\<to\>\<cal-H\><rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
    with the boundary-word values specified before
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>. It is surjective, hence unitary, and
    intertwines spatial translations and the heat semigroups:
    <math|<math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>=e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>><math-ss|J><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>
    for <math|s\<geqslant\>0>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The map <math|J:<around|[|b|]>\<mapsto\>v<rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|b|)>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1> is an isometry taking vacuum to vacuum
    and intertwines spatial translations and heat
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-Laplace>. Two linear isometries with these
    boundary-word values agree on the dense word span, hence everywhere. For
    compact frequency<nbsp><math|b> let <math|f<rsub|y>=<fourierplus|<around|(|e<rsup|-<big|sum><rsub|j>y<rsub|j>*p<rsub|j><rsup|4>>*b|)>><around|(|-\<cdot\>|)>>;
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-Laplace> the norms and mixed pairing of its
    boundary-word image under<nbsp><math|J> and the corresponding
    positive-height OS vector agree, so the two vectors coincide.
    Frequency-test density and closed range include every smeared
    positive-height vector, approximate identities give point vectors, and
    permutation and perturbation of equal times give all positive words. So
    <math|J> is surjective.
  </proof>

  The low-energy estimate is the infrared input of the reconstruction. For a
  fixed compactly supported local word<nbsp><math|A>, locality makes a
  commutator vanish outside a bounded spatial region for bounded times. An
  odd time average turns this into a positive spectral weight comparable to
  the energy<nbsp><math|E> near zero. A momentum cutoff at
  scale<nbsp><math|u> costs<nbsp><math|u<rsup|3>> in three spatial
  dimensions, and the cone <math|<around|\||<wide|p|\<vect\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>E>
  gives <math|u*<space|0.17em>\<mu\>*<around|{|u/2\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>\<lesssim\>u<rsup|3>>;
  dyadic summation gives<nbsp><math|u<rsup|2>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Uniform low-energy bounds and vacuum uniqueness><label|lem:H2.3>
    For every finite complex combination<nbsp><math|A> of field words smeared
    with compactly supported smooth tests, there are
    <math|M<rsub|A>,M<rprime|'><rsub|A>\<geqslant\>0>, depending on its
    words, labels and tests, such that for
    every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><rsub|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>><around|(|<around|{|0\<less\>\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>u|}>|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M<rsub|A>*u<rsup|2>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1|)>,>>|<row|<cell|0\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>-P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>|)>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>M<rprime|'><rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|s\<geqslant\>1|)>,>>>>
    </align*>

    where <math|P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>> projects
    onto<nbsp><math|<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>. Moreover <math|ker
    H<rsub|\<xi\>>=<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>> and <math|ker
    H<rsub|4,\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>=<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>><rsup|<math-up|OS>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|The low-energy measure.> Let <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>=<math-ss|E><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|<around|{|\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>=0|}>|)>>
    be the full zero-energy projection. Let <math|A<rsup|\<ast\>>> be the
    word adjoint, reversing order and applying the
    star<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-star> to labels and complex conjugation to tests,
    and put <math|\<alpha\><rsub|z>*A=<math-ss|T><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|z|)>*A<math-ss|T><rsub|\<xi\>><around|(|z|)><rsup|\<ast\>>>.
    Bound all support times and spatial norms by<nbsp><math|r> and set
    <math|R=4*r+1>, <math|\<mu\>=\<mu\><rsub|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>> and
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>=\<mu\><rsub|A<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>>.
    The common distribution bounds give <math|\<mu\><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>\<leqslant\>M>,
    uniformly in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. Covariance and locality give

    <\equation*>
      a*<around|(|y,t|)>=<fourierplus|\<mu\>><around|(|y,t|)>-<fourierplus|\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>><around|(|-y,-t|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|[|A<rsup|\<ast\>>,\<alpha\><rsub|<around|(|y,t|)>>*A|]>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=0*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||y|\|>\<gtr\>R,<around|\||t|\|>\<leqslant\>1|)><space|0.17em>,
    </equation*>

    with <math|<fourierplus|\<mu\>><around|(|z|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*p\<cdot\>z>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><around|(|p|)>>.
    Write <math|E=p<rsup|4>>. The time average
    <math|<wide|a|\<bar\>><around|(|y|)>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|[|a*<around|(|y,t|)>-a*<around|(|y,-t|)>|]>/<around|(|2*i|)>*<space|0.17em>d*t>
    is bounded by<nbsp><math|M> and supported
    in<nbsp><math|<wide|B|\<bar\>><rsub|R>>. It is the Fourier transform of
    two positive measures, the pushforwards of
    <math|\<varpi\><around|(|E|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>> under
    <math|p\<mapsto\><wide|p|\<vect\>>> and of
    <math|\<varpi\><around|(|E|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\><rsup|\<ast\>>> under
    <math|p\<mapsto\>-<wide|p|\<vect\>>>, where
    <math|\<varpi\><around|(|E|)>=<around|(|1-cos E|)>/E>,
    <math|\<varpi\><around|(|0|)>=0>; on <math|0\<leqslant\>E\<leqslant\>1>,
    <math|\<varpi\><around|(|E|)>\<geqslant\>E/\<pi\>>. Choose a smooth
    momentum bump <math|b<rsub|u>> with <math|0\<leqslant\>b<rsub|u>\<leqslant\>1>,
    equal to one on <math|B<rsub|u>> and supported
    in<nbsp><math|B<rsub|2*u>>; <math|<around|\||<fourierplus|b<rsub|u>><around|(|-y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>8<around|\||B<rsub|1>|\|>*u<rsup|3>>.
    Since <math|<around|\||<wide|p|\<vect\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>E> on the
    spectral cone, <math|b<rsub|u>=1> on the shell
    <math|<around|{|u/2\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>>, and Fourier inversion and
    positivity give

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|3><frac|u|2*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><around|(|<around|{|u/2\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>|)>\<leqslant\><around*|\||<big|int><wide|a|\<bar\>><around|(|y|)><fourierplus|b<rsub|u>><around|(|-y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y|\|>\<leqslant\>8<around|\||B<rsub|1>|\|><around|\||B<rsub|R>|\|>*M*u<rsup|3><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Summing the shells at <math|u*2<rsup|-j>> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-low>\<mu\><around|(|<around|{|0\<less\>E\<leqslant\>u|}>|)>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|3>*M*u<rsup|2>*<space|2em><around|(|0\<less\>u\<leqslant\>1|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For <math|u\<gtr\>1> use the total mass. Since
    <math|e<rsup|-s*E>=<big|int><rsub|E><rsup|\<infty\>>s*e<rsup|-s*t>*<space|0.17em>d*t>,
    Tonelli gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:H-heat>0\<leqslant\><around|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,<around|(|e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>-Q<rsub|\<xi\>>|)>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|E\<gtr\>0>e<rsup|-s*E>*<space|0.17em>d*\<mu\>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|A>*s*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>t<rsup|2>*e<rsup|-s*t>*<space|0.17em>d*t=2*C<rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    <em|From almost every phase to every phase.> By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.2>, <math|<math-ss|J><rsup|<math-up|OS>><rsub|\<xi\>>>
    is unitary and intertwines heat, so it identifies <math|ker
    H<rsub|\<xi\>>> with <math|ker H<rsup|<math-up|OS>><rsub|\<xi\>>>; by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3> both are <math|<C>\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>
    for <math|\<nu\>>-almost every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, and there
    <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>=P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>>. The vacuum-subtracted heat
    pairing <math|<around|\<langle\>|A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>-<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>|\|><rsup|2>>
    is continuous in<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>: by<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-boundary> it is
    a continuous Schwartz pairing, with error <math|M*e<rsup|-s*N>> outside
    radius<nbsp><math|2*N> on<nbsp><math|<forwardcone>>. It is bounded by
    <math|2*C<rsub|A>*s<rsup|-2>> for almost every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, hence,
    by continuity and full support of<nbsp><math|\<nu\>>, for
    every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>. Letting <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>> gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>=<around|\||<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>|\|><rsup|2>>.
    Since <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>=\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>,
    density of compact word vectors gives
    <math|Q<rsub|\<xi\>>=P<rsub|0,\<xi\>>>, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-heat>
    becomes the second bound. Unitarity and heat intertwining give the OS
    vacuum line.
  </proof>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Irreducibility><label|lem:H-irreducible> For
    every<nbsp><math|\<xi\>>, a bounded operator<nbsp><math|B> is scalar if
    and only if, for every smeared field<nbsp><math|\<Phi\>> and
    <math|u,v\<in\>\<cal-D\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, one has
    <math|u\<in\><math-up|Dom><space|0.17em>\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>> and
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsup|\<ast\>>*u,B*v|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|u,B*\<Phi\>*v|\<rangle\>>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    A scalar satisfies the relation by the adjoint law. Conversely, the
    adjoint law and iteration move words across matrix entries without
    requiring <math|B> to preserve the domain. For a word<nbsp><math|A> put
    <math|u=A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>, <math|v=A<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>
    and, in the upper half-plane,

    <\equation*>
      p<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<langle\>|u,e<rsup|i*z*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>q<around|(|z|)>=<around|\<langle\>|B<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,e<rsup|i*z*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*v|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    These are bounded and continuous to the boundary, where covariance and
    the weak relation give <math|p<around|(|t|)>=q*<around|(|-t|)>>. Morera
    and Liouville make their bounded pasted continuation constant, so
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|u,e<rsup|-s*H<rsub|\<xi\>>>*B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|u,B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Heat convergence and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.3> give
    <math|B*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>=b*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>>. Moving words
    again gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|A<rprime|'>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,B*A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>=b*<around|\<langle\>|A<rprime|'>*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>,A*\<Omega\><rsub|\<xi\>>|\<rangle\>>>,
    so <math|B=b*1> by density.
  </proof>

  For the charge law set <math|\<eta\>=<math-up|diag><around|(|-1,-1,-1,1|)>>,
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|i>=1> for <math|i\<leqslant\>3>,
  <math|\<zeta\><rsub|4>=-i>, and

  <\equation*>
    T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b>=-\<zeta\><rsub|a>*\<zeta\><rsub|b>*T<rsub|a*b>,<space|2em>T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b><around|(|f|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b>,f|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|K<around|(|x|)>=k+V*x> with <math|V<rsup|<math-ss|T>>*\<eta\>+\<eta\>*V=0>,
  and let <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>> be its infinitesimal Lorentz label action.
  For real <math|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R>|)>> of
  integral one and <math|c\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R><rsup|3>|)>>
  put <math|Q<rsub|K>*<around|(|f,c|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b>T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|4*b>*<around|(|K<rsup|b>*<around|(|c\<otimes\>f|)>|)>>.
  Suppose <math|g\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R><rsup|4>|)>>,
  and <math|c=1> on a ball such that all <math|<around|(|y,s|)>> over its
  complement, <math|s\<in\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f>, are spacelike to
  <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>g>. The charge law is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:H-charge>i*<around|[|Q<rsub|K>*<around|(|f,c|)>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d>*A,g|)>|]>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>*\<iota\><rsub|d>*A,g|)>-<big|sum><rsub|a>\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d>*A,\<partial\><rsub|a>*<around|(|K<rsup|a>*g|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  A common polynomial bound permits the continuation of local stress
  identities.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Continued stress><label|lem:H2.4> In every phase, <math|T>
    continues to a symmetric Hermitian conserved Lorentz tensor of
    operator-valued distributions, with the local charge
    law<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-charge> under its support hypothesis.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <em|Tensor properties.> The factors give
    <math|T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|44>=T<rsub|44>>,
    <math|T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|4*i>=i*T<rsub|4*i>>,
    <math|T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|i*j>=-T<rsub|i*j>>. Euclidean symmetry
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-star> give symmetry and Hermiticity, and
    differentiation and exponentiation of covariance give the Lorentz action.
    Continuing the derivative identity gives
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d+1>*\<partial\><rsub|a>*A,f|)>=-\<zeta\><rsub|a><rsup|-1>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<xi\>>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|d>*A,\<partial\><rsub|a>*f|)>>,
    so Euclidean conservation gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<eta\><rsub|a*a>*T<rsup|<math-up|M>><rsub|a*b>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|a>*f|)>=0>
    on the common word domain.

    <em|The Euclidean jump.> For real<nbsp><math|c>, insert
    <math|c<around|(|y|)>*h*<around|(|t-t<rsub|0>|)>> into the shell
    identities of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>, transferred to the phase
    by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>, and integrate conservation on each
    side of<nbsp><math|t<rsub|0>>. The <math|\<nabla\>*c> term stays away
    from the distinguished point and remains bounded, so
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|b><big|int>c<around|(|y|)>*K<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|b>*<around|(|y,t|)>*T<rsub|4*b>*<around|(|y,t|)>*<space|0.17em>d*y>
    is Cauchy on each side, with jump <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|K>*A+<big|sum><rsub|b>K<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|b>*\<partial\><rsub|b>*A>,
    where <math|K<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|b><around|(|y|)>=\<zeta\><rsub|b>*K<rsup|b>*<around|(|<wide|y|\<vect\>>,i*y<rsup|4>|)>>.

    <em|Continuation of the jump.> Fix strictly ordered
    heights<nbsp><math|y<rsub|j>> for all fields other than the current,
    including the distinguished field with index<nbsp><math|0>. For their
    real times<nbsp><math|t<rsub|j>> put <math|z<rsub|j><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>=i*y<rsub|j>+\<varsigma\>*t<rsub|j>>,
    and place the current at <math|z<rsub|0><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>\<pm\>i*\<varepsilon\>>,
    retaining Euclidean separation<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. Write

    <\equation*>
      I<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<pm\>><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|b><big|int>c<around|(|x|)>*K<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|b>*<around|(|x,-i*z<rsub|0><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>\<pm\>\<varepsilon\>|)><space|0.17em><math-ss|F><rsub|\<pm\>><rsup|\<xi\>><around|(|\<ldots\>,T<rsub|4*b>*<around|(|x,z<rsub|0><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>\<pm\>i*\<varepsilon\>|)>,\<ldots\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x,
    </equation*>

    where <math|<math-ss|F><rsub|\<pm\>>> inserts the current immediately
    after or before the distinguished field. Choose a complex
    neighbourhood<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>> of <math|<around|[|0,1|]>> preserving
    the other strict height differences. On
    <math|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>\<nabla\>*c> the spacelike germs of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-locality> bound the side integrals, uniformly
    for small<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>> and <math|\<varsigma\>> in compact
    <math|L\<subset\>\<cal-U\>>, and conservation gives
    <math|sup<rsub|\<varsigma\>\<in\>L><around|\||I<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><rsup|\<pm\>><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>-I<rsub|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>><rsup|\<pm\>><around|(|\<varsigma\>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|L>*<around|\||\<varepsilon\>-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|\|>>.
    The limits are holomorphic; at small imaginary<nbsp><math|\<varsigma\>>
    the Euclidean calculation identifies their jump, and the identity theorem
    extends it to <math|\<varsigma\>=1>. Test against <math|g,f> and take the
    continuous partial boundaries; integration by parts differentiates
    <math|K<rsup|a>*g>, giving<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-charge>. Schwartz cutoffs and
    the joint bounds extend it from compact spectators. For
    complex<nbsp><math|c>, the charge of its imaginary part commutes with the
    distinguished field by locality. Constant and rotational<nbsp><math|K>
    give the required actions.
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>>
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>, <reference|lem:H-locality>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:H-fields> give, in every phase, a faithful theory
    satisfying W1\UW3 and the spectral condition, with continuations
    satisfying Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D15>
    and<nbsp><reference|def:D14f>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.2> gives the
    unitary comparison, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.3> the vacuum lines and
    hence W0, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-irreducible> the scalar weak
    commutant. Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.3>\U<reference|lem:H2.4> also
    give the uniform rates and the continued stress, at the retained
    phase<nbsp><math|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>> as at every other.
  </render-proof>

  <section|The trace law and non-free comparisons><label|sec:partII-I>

  This section proves Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR> and the non-free
  comparisons. The stress tensor<nbsp><math|T> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-G> is normalized by its comparison with
  the free Maxwell stress (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>), and the
  free stress is traceless. So this comparison cannot determine the first
  nonzero trace term, which is smaller by the inverse running coupling
  (Table<nbsp><reference|tab:I-orders>). That term corresponds to the
  one-loop trace anomaly<nbsp><cite|CollinsDuncanJoglekar1977>; we compute
  its coefficient from a lattice Ward identity, with a rate. The proof of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR> has five steps.

  <\enumerate>
    <item*|(1)><math|L=<maxwellstress>>, <math|D=\<delta\><rsub|a*b><curvaturesquare>>
    and<nbsp><math|\<Xi\>> span the centered real symmetric
    <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial tensors of degree four; a
    functional<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> reads the trace from
    the divergence<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-rho>.

    <item*|(2)>A lattice stress current (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:I-current>)
    has divergence equal to the action variation up to a defect, which block
    transfer<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-transfer-map> makes small; block transfer also
    keeps the trace and antisymmetric part of the current lattice
    differences.

    <item*|(3)>Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator> computes the regulated
    Ward coefficient<nbsp><math|-12<slope>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-transfer> carries it to continuum fields
    along the radii of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>, with the rate of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-stability>.

    <item*|(4)>A conserved combination of these fields is a multiple
    of<nbsp><math|T>; its <math|D>-coefficient tends to
    <math|d<rsub|2>=-<slope>/4> (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:I-coefficient>).

    <item*|(5)>Positivity makes <math|<tracetwopoint>> nonincreasing. This
    turns comparison along subsequences, which uses frames, into the
    frame-free limit at every radius (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:I-squeeze>).
  </enumerate>

  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-I.2> proves the non-free comparisons,
  each with its comparison class: no two-point statement excludes a
  generalized free field with the same spectral measure. In the selected
  phase, on no interval <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> does
  <math|<tracetwopoint>> agree with a finite Wick profile
  (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:NF>).

  Throughout, retain the fields, inclusions and units of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10> and
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>, the tensor of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:ST>, and the selected reconstruction of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>. The symbols
  <math|\<tau\>,e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>,\<Theta\>,<tracetwopoint>> and
  <math|<traceamplitude>> have the meanings of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D16>. Write
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>=\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>,f|)>>,
  and use the word domain of item<nbsp><reference|def:W1> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:wightman>.

  <subsection|Trace and spectrum><label|sec:partII-I.1>

  Centering must hold on the common operator domain, as well as in separated
  correlations. The proof below identifies the subtraction after
  continuation.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Centering as an operator identity><label|lem:I1.1> For every
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, on the word domain,

    <\equation*>
      \<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>=\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<tau\>,f|)>-e<rsub|<math-up|tr>><around*|(|<big|int>f|)><math-up|Id><rsub|\<cal-H\>>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Unit deletion (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E1-E3>) and the quotient
    calculus of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> remove the unit insertion; the
    continuation of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1> and the field realization
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-fields> carry this to the operators.
    Deleting the unit insertion leaves a Euclidean correlation independent of
    its position. On each ordered-time region its continuation is therefore
    constant in that variable. The identity theorem on the connected
    continuation domain <math|\<cal-Z\><rsub|n>> of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D15>, applied successively in each time
    variable, identifies it with the correlation containing the unit.
    Boundary values and smearing give <math|<around|\<langle\>|u,\<Phi\>*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|4><unitfield|4>,f|)>*v|\<rangle\>>=<around|(|<big|int>f|)><around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>>
    for word vectors <math|u,v>. Density and linearity give the identity. The
    zero Euclidean one-point function continues to zero and has zero boundary
    distribution, proving the centering assertion.
  </proof>

  We use the Fourier convention of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-products>:

  <\equation*>
    <fourierplus|f><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*\<b-up-p\>\<cdot\>x>*f<around|(|x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x,<space|2em><around|(|<wavetransform>\<psi\>|)><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>=<big|int>e<rsup|i*\<b-up-p\>\<cdot\><around|(|y-x|)>>*\<psi\>*<around|(|x,y|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x*<space|0.17em>d*y<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  In particular, <math|<wavetransform><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>\<otimes\>h|)>=<wide|<fourierplus|f>|\<bar\>><fourierplus|h>>.
  Write <math|W<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;<wide|f|\<bar\>>\<otimes\>h|)>=<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|h|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>>
  for the joint tempered boundary distribution of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>. The next lemma constructs its positive
  measure.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Unique trace spectral measure><label|lem:I1.2> There is a unique
    positive tempered-growth Borel measure <math|<tracespectrum>>, supported
    in <math|<forwardcone>>, such that

    <\equation*>
      d*\<mu\><rsub|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>>=<around|\||<fourierplus|f>|\|><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum><space|2em><around|(|f\<in\>\<cal-S\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    It is Lorentz invariant in momentum variables, and

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;\<psi\>|)>>|<cell|=<big|int><wavetransform>\<psi\>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>>|<cell|=<big|int>e<rsup|-r*\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum>>|<cell|>|<cell|<around|(|r\<gtr\>0|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The measure is built from the positive boundary distributions of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>. For <math|v<rsub|f>=\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>>,
    translation covariance gives <math|<wide|h|^><rsub|+><around|(|P|)>*v<rsub|f>=<wide|f|^><rsub|+><around|(|P|)>*v<rsub|h>>.
    Here <math|<wide|h|^><rsub|+><around|(|P|)>=<big|int><wide|h|^><rsub|+><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<math-ss|E><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>>,
    with <math|<math-ss|E>> the joint translation spectral resolution of
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:RC>. Both sides smear the field with
    <math|f\<ast\>h>. Choose tests whose transforms do not vanish on
    successively larger balls. On such a ball define
    <math|d<tracespectrum>=<around|\||<wide|f|^><rsub|+>|\|><rsup|-2>*d*\<mu\><rsub|v<rsub|f>>>.
    The displayed identity makes these definitions agree on overlaps.
    Polarization gives <math|<around|\<langle\>|v<rsub|f>,v<rsub|h>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><wide|<wide|f|^><rsub|+>|\<bar\>><wide|h|^><rsub|+>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum>>.
    Product tests determine the joint distribution.

    The spectrum condition gives cone support. Schwartz continuity, applied
    to inverse transforms of cutoffs equal to one on
    <math|<around|{|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\>R|}>>, gives
    <math|<tracespectrum><around|(|<around|{|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\>R|}>|)>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|1+R|)><rsup|k>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.4> continues <math|T> to a symmetric
    Hermitian Lorentz tensor. Contraction and invariance of the unit
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>) make <math|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>>
    a Hermitian Lorentz scalar. Covariance of <math|W<rsub|2>> and uniqueness
    of the measure give Lorentz invariance.

    Tempered growth and <math|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\><sqrt|2>*\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>>
    on the cone make the Fourier\ULaplace integral holomorphic at positive
    imaginary time, with boundary <math|W<rsub|2>>. To identify it with the
    Euclidean continuation of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>, take enough
    primitives to obtain continuous boundary values, using the polynomial
    boundary bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:H-poly>. Their zero difference extends by
    Schwarz reflection<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorem<nbsp>11.14>;
    holomorphic uniqueness and differentiation give equality and the Laplace
    formula. In particular <math|<tracetwopoint>> is real, nonnegative and
    nonincreasing.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Comparison at prescribed radii.> Use the scaling frames of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D12>: <math|<scaleframe|i|d>:<maxwellspace|d>\<to\>F<rsub|d>>.
  Insert <math|<scaleframe|i|d>A> at <math|r<rsub|i>*x>, and read tensors by
  <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|4><around|(|<scaleframe|i|4>|)><rsup|-1>>. Write
  <math|x<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>> for nearest points to <math|x> in
  <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|n<rsub|i>><Z><rsup|4>>. Jets use positional
  derivatives of smooth interpolants and their normalized lattice
  differences, including derivatives of the inserted fields. A shell flux is
  the integral against <math|-\<partial\><rsub|a>*\<chi\>> in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D13>. Define
  <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>=lim<rsub|<around|(|N,p|)>>
  \<beta\><rsub|N,p,k<rsub|i>>> along the ordered limits retained in the
  proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>; the radius level <math|n<rsub|i>>
  precedes the insertion level <math|k<rsub|i>>.

  The next lemma extends Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1> to prescribed radii.
  Its proof also transfers the derivative argument
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> to these frames.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Prescribed radii><label|lem:I-radii> Every positive null sequence
    has a subsequence <math|<around|(|r<rsub|i>|)>>, with frames
    <math|<scaleframe|i|d>> making <math|<around|(|r<rsub|i>,<scaleframe|i|\<cdot\>>|)>>
    a stable scaling sequence (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D12>) and
    levels <math|n<rsub|i>\<less\>k<rsub|i>> tending to infinity, such that:

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(i)>the couplings satisfy

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:I-adjustable>\<beta\><rsub|i>\<to\>\<infty\>,<space|2em><frac|log
        <around|(|1/r<rsub|i>|)>|\<beta\><rsub|i>>\<to\><slope><rsup|-1><space|0.17em>;
      </equation>

      <item*|(ii)>for every arity <math|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>\<geqslant\>1>,
      degrees <math|d<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>D>, labels
      <math|A<rsub|j><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>\<to\>A<rsub|j>> and compact
      <math|\<cal-K\>\<subset\><Gen|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>>,

      <\equation*>
        sup<rsub|x\<in\>\<cal-K\>><around*|\||S<rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>*<around|(|<scaleframe|i|\<b-d\>>\<b-A\><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>;r<rsub|i>*x<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>|)>-<maxwellcorr><rsub|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>><around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>|\|>\<to\>0<space|0.17em>,
      </equation*>

      and the same holds for each fixed jet and translation-shell flux;

      <item*|(iii)>for <math|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>=2> without derivatives,
      (ii) holds uniformly over the phases.
    </enumerate>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The proof follows the finite-dimensional construction of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.1>, with radii placed in comparison windows.
    The source estimates of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication> control the windows, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement> moves the insertion points.

    <em|Radii and levels.> Put <math|M<rsub|n>=<cutoffsize><rsub|n>>. At
    radius level <math|n> allow

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-reading>r*<around|(|n,u|)>=a<rsub|n>*M<rsub|n><rsup|u>,<space|2em>u\<in\><around|[|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|]>,<space|2em>k<around|(|n|)>=min
      <around|{|m\<gtr\>n:log <around|(|a<rsub|n>/a<rsub|m>|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|log
      <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|48>|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    The separation in <math|k<around|(|n|)>> leaves room for all fixed-degree
    marked transports; its logarithmic size is negligible compared with
    <math|<refbeta><rsub|n>>. For degree <math|D> and an arity bound
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>\<geqslant\>2> choose comparison exponents
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|1>,\<alpha\><rsub|2>> and <math|c>
    with

    <\equation*>
      c\<less\>0,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|1>+6\<less\>\<alpha\><rsub|0>,<space|1em>\<alpha\><rsub|2>+5\<less\>\<alpha\><rsub|0>+c,<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>+c\<less\>0,<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>-\<alpha\><rsub|0>-c\<less\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Choose the differences <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>\<gtr\>6>,
    then <math|-c\<gtr\><around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>,
    then <math|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>\<gtr\>5-c>, and finally
    increase all three <math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>> together until
    <math|\<alpha\><rsub|2>\<geqslant\>D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>+10>.

    <em|Comparison windows.> We first obtain comparison windows at every
    sufficiently late radius level, rather than only the intervals selected
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. For each of the three exponents
    <math|b=\<alpha\><rsub|j>+u-\<alpha\><rsub|0>>, stop backwards from
    <math|n> when the accumulated logarithmic block length first exceeds
    <math|<around|(|b+2|)>*log M<rsub|n>>, then stop again after another
    <math|10*log B<rsub|n>>. The entry bounds fixed in
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.1> give
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>log <refbeta><rsub|m>> at every
    level<nbsp><math|m>. Each overshoot is therefore <math|O<around|(|log log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>|)>=o<around|(|log M<rsub|n>|)>>, so the resulting cell
    side is at most <math|a<rsub|n>*M<rsub|n><rsup|b+5>>. All levels in these
    windows tend to infinity and their couplings have ratio tending to one.
    The accumulated source loss satisfies

    <\equation*>
      log <big|prod><rsub|m<math-up|in the
      window>>\<cal-C\><rsub|J<rsub|m>>\<leqslant\><around*|(|sup<rsub|m<math-up|in
      the window>> J<rsub|m><rsup|-2>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|m<math-up|in the
      window>>log \<ell\><rsub|m>=o<around|(|log M<rsub|n>|)>,
    </equation*>

    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-log-cost>. Thus these windows satisfy the window
    hypotheses (Win1)\U(Win3) stated before
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-source-polynomial>: its initial rounding
    error is <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|-b>>, its covariance and healthy-chart
    errors are smaller than any required fixed inverse power of
    <math|M<rsub|n>> after entry, and its source
    recurrence<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-source-step> has total linear loss
    <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|o<around|(|1|)>>>. The entry bounds of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.1> include the comparison
    requirements<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-Gaussian-entry> for the finitely many
    arities admitted at each stage. At mark weight
    <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|1/<around|(|8*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>|)>>> the
    resulting error is at most <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|-3/4>>. The normalized
    integral is bounded by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-positive-ratio>; its stage
    constant is at most <math|log M<rsub|n>/<around|(|16*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>|)>>
    after entry. These estimates are uniform in
    <math|u\<in\><around|[|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|]>> and do
    not require a window to remain in one stage.

    <em|Chessboard seminorms.> At its own normalization a singleton therefore
    has chessboard seminorm at most <math|1+<around|\||<maxwellcorr><rsub|1><around|(|A|)>|\|>>.
    Indeed coefficient extraction at amplitude <math|e<rsup|2*q>>,
    <math|q=log M<rsub|n>/<around|(|16*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>|)>>, bounds its
    centered seminorm by <math|e<rsup|q-2*q>\<leqslant\>1>. Expand a
    degree-<math|D> label in weight-homogeneous basis vectors. Changing from
    exponent <math|\<alpha\><rsub|1>+u-\<alpha\><rsub|0>> to <math|u>
    multiplies a weight-<math|j> coordinate by
    <math|M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*j>>,
    hence bounds this seminorm by <math|C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D>>.
    Include all signed and conjugated basis vectors in the finite comparison
    request. The chessboard inequality<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-chess> then bounds
    their reflected products by the product of these seminorms. The same
    calculation with <math|\<alpha\><rsub|2>> gives seminorms
    <math|C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D>>.
    Their cell sides are <math|o<around|(|r*<around|(|n,u|)>*\<delta\><rsub|n>|)>>
    for <math|\<delta\><rsub|n>=M<rsub|n><rsup|c>>. Apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-displacement> only when every coordinate
    hybrid of the two physical configurations has periodic coordinate
    separation at least <math|4*r*<around|(|n,u|)>*\<delta\><rsub|n>>. The
    centered level <math|D<rsub|<math-up|cen>>> can be chosen with
    <math|a<rsub|D<rsub|<math-up|cen>>>\<less\>r*<around|(|n,u|)>*\<delta\><rsub|n>>;
    the insertions and their single-axis reflections are interior
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-window-geometry>. At small radii these periodic
    separations equal the ordinary coordinate separations on the fixed
    compact set. The lemma bounds the positional difference by the product of
    the seminorms times <math|4*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>/\<delta\><rsub|n>>. With
    scaled mesh <math|h<rsup|<math-up|mesh>><rsub|n>=M<rsub|n><rsup|-u>>, the
    two errors are consequently bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|1>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>*\<delta\><rsub|n>,<space|2em>C*M<rsub|n><rsup|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>-\<alpha\><rsub|2>|)>*D*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>>*h<rsup|<math-up|mesh>><rsub|n>/\<delta\><rsub|n><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    These are the contributions of coordinate-coincidence strips of width
    <math|4*\<delta\><rsub|n>> and interpolation across one mesh cell;
    include a strip for each coordinate hybrid, a finite union of volume
    <math|O<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|n>|)>> since
    <math|h<rsup|<math-up|mesh>><rsub|n>=o<around|(|\<delta\><rsub|n>|)>>.
    Both vanish uniformly in <math|u\<in\><around|[|\<alpha\><rsub|0>,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|]>>
    by the choice of the comparison exponents. For the remaining error, use
    the corrected Gaussian source of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-Gaussian-sources>
    on every subset of the <math|n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>> slots, as
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-source-root>. The weighted mode norm in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-replication> is submultiplicative at every
    arity. Coefficient extraction at the amplitudes just chosen gives
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>C*M<rsub|n><rsup|-1/<around|(|16*n<rsub|<math-up|ar>>|)>>\<to\>0>
    for the complete reflected array.

    <em|Phase uniformity.> By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-roots> and the
    lattice paragraph after it, these seminorm bounds on every repeated
    reflected product bound the norm of the corresponding compressed
    zero-energy operator, hence its value at every character of the phase
    algebra of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:PH>. This gives (iii), including
    moving coefficients by finite-dimensional multilinearity.

    <em|Every small radius lies in a window.> By the same entry bound
    <math|\<ell\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>log <refbeta><rsub|m>>, applied to both
    adjacent stage ratios, <math|\<ell\><rsub|n>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|n+1>>
    eventually. Monotonicity of <math|M<rsub|n>> gives
    <math|r*<around|(|n,\<alpha\><rsub|0>|)>\<leqslant\>r*<around|(|n+1,\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|)>>,
    while <math|r*<around|(|n,\<alpha\><rsub|0>|)>\<to\>0>. First crossing of
    any prescribed small radius, followed by continuity in <math|u>, puts
    that radius in one of these intervals. Diagonal extraction over degrees,
    arities, compact sets and jet orders gives
    <math|n<rsub|i>,k<rsub|i>=k<around|(|n<rsub|i>|)>> and frames, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1a> for symmetries and units and the
    recursive correction<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-correct-frames> for inclusions. Fix
    the exponents before each extraction, and require
    <math|<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|0>+1|)>*log
    M<rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<leqslant\><around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|48>>.

    <em|Jets and couplings.> For jets, the argument
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-derivative-limit> applies on these frames with
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:FC>: finitely many tested free responses
    have convergent matrices and bounded left inverses, integration by parts
    identifies the tested derivative <math|r<rsub|i><around|(|<scaleframe|i|d+1>|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|a><scaleframe|i|d>A>
    with the response of <math|\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*A>,
    and iteration and integration on the fixed compact shell give the jet and
    flux limits. Minimality of <math|k<around|(|n|)>> gives <math|log
    <around|(|a<rsub|n>/a<rsub|k<around|(|n|)>>|)>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|48>|)>=o<around|(|<refbeta><rsub|n>|)>>. The
    running law of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:MT>,
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-drift-sum>, gives
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>/<refbeta><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<to\>1>. Since
    <math|<refbeta><rsub|n>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+<slope>log
    <around|(|1/a<rsub|n>|)>>, the imposed bound on <math|u*log M<rsub|n>>
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-adjustable>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Tensor coordinates.> Set <math|L=<maxwellstress>>,
  <math|D<rsub|a*b>=\<delta\><rsub|a*b><curvaturesquare>>, and
  <math|\<Xi\><rsub|a*a>=0>, <math|\<Xi\><rsub|a*b>=<big|sum><rsub|c,\<alpha\>>:F<rsub|a*c><rsup|\<alpha\>>*F<rsub|b*c><rsup|\<alpha\>>>:
  for <math|a\<ne\>b>. In the notation of<nbsp><eqref|eq:G-free-tensor>,
  <math|L=-Q+<tfrac|1|4>\<delta\>*Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>>,
  <math|\<Xi\><rsub|a*b>=<around|(|1-\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|)>*Q<rsub|a*b>> and
  <math|D=<tfrac|1|4>\<delta\>*Q<rsub|<math-up|tot>>>; in the basis
  <math|Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>,Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>,Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>>
  of the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>,

  <\equation*>
    L=-<tfrac|1|2>Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>+<tfrac|1|4>Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>>-Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>,<space|2em>\<Xi\>=Z<rsup|<around|(|3|)>>,<space|2em>D=<tfrac|1|2>Z<rsup|<around|(|1|)>>+<tfrac|1|4>Z<rsup|<around|(|2|)>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  By that proof, these three tensors span the centered real symmetric
  <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial tensors of degree four.

  Write <math|<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>>:F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|a*c>*F<rsup|\<alpha\>><rsub|b*c>>:
  and similarly with derivatives. Before imposing the Maxwell equation,
  divergence has three coordinates, represented in component <math|b> by

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|b*c>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>>,<space|1em><big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|b*c>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For diagonal entries <math|z<rsub|0>*<big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,F<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>>+z<rsub|1>*<big|sum><rsub|b,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|b*c>,F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>>
  and off-diagonal entries <math|z<rsub|2>*<big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>>,
  the divergence coordinates are <math|<around|(|2*<around|(|z<rsub|0>-z<rsub|2>|)>,2*z<rsub|1>+z<rsub|2>/2,z<rsub|2>|)>>.
  Indeed Bianchi gives <math|<big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,\<partial\><rsub|a>*F<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>=<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|a,c><around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|a*c>,\<partial\><rsub|b>*F<rsub|a*c>|\<rangle\>>>.
  Thus the functional <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>*<around|(|x,y,z|)>=-2*x-8*y>
  satisfies

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I-rho>\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|raw>><space|0.17em>B|)><curvaturesquare>=-<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>B,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>L|)>=\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>\<Xi\>|)>=0,<space|2em>\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><space|0.17em>D|)>=-4<space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  It annihilates the third coordinate, the Maxwell equation direction. Write
  <math|R<rsub|5>\<cong\><R><rsup|3>> for this raw space and <math|V<rsub|5>>
  for the space of real <math|<hypercubic>>-covariant axis vectors in
  <math|<around|(|<maxwellspace|5>|)><rsup|4>>. The free Maxwell quotient map
  <math|s:R<rsub|5>\<to\>V<rsub|5>> is onto, with <math|ker
  s=<R>v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>>, <math|v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>=<around|(|0,0,1|)>>
  and <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>|)>=0>;
  thus <math|V<rsub|5>> is two dimensional.

  <paragraph|Lattice stress current.> We construct the lattice insertions
  from plaquette curvatures. Let <math|<math-ss|S>=<math-ss|S><rsub|N,m>> be
  the leading analytic path action without its coupling, defined in
  Section<nbsp><reference|subsec:C-matching>, and let
  <math|<math-ss|s><rsub|a>q<around|(|x|)>=q*<around|(|x+e<rsub|a>|)>> be the
  lattice shift.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Lattice stress current><label|def:I-current> At a
    site<nbsp><math|x> put <math|f<rsub|a*b><around|(|x|)>=<math-up|proj><rsub|<g>><around|(|U<rsub|\<square\><around|(|x;a,b|)>>-U<rsub|\<square\><around|(|x;a,b|)>><rsup|-1>|)>/2>
    and

    <\equation*>
      J<rsub|a*b>=<frac|\<delta\><rsub|a*b>|4>*<big|sum><rsub|c,d><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|c*d>,f<rsub|c*d>|\<rangle\>>-<big|sum><rsub|c><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|a*c>,f<rsub|b*c>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em><around|(|Y<rsub|b><rsup|x>|)><rsub|e>=\<b-1\><rsub|e<rsup|->=x>*f<rsub|b,e<rsup|<math-up|dir>>><around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    With <math|\<nabla\><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|4>c<rsup|\<nabla\>><rsub|j>*<around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|j>-<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|-j>|)>>
    and <math|<around|(|c<rsup|\<nabla\>><rsub|1>,c<rsup|\<nabla\>><rsub|2>,c<rsup|\<nabla\>><rsub|3>,c<rsup|\<nabla\>><rsub|4>|)>=<around|(|4/5,-1/5,4/105,-1/280|)>>,
    the Ward defect is <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b>=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>>.
  </definition>

  The current<nbsp><math|J> is the lattice form of <math|<maxwellstress>>,
  and <math|Y<rsub|b>> is the initial left displacement. Initially
  <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J=0> and <math|J<rsub|a*b>-J<rsub|b*a>=0>,
  so these data satisfy the identities (a)\U(b) of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator> below with <math|q=0> and
  <math|<math-ss|t>=0>.

  <paragraph|Block transfer.> The averaging kernels must respect the
  particular difference<nbsp><math|\<nabla\><rsub|a>>. For odd block ratio
  <math|\<ell\>>, define commuting finite sums

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|M><rsub|a>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|j=0><rsup|\<ell\>-1><math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|j>,<space|2em><math-ss|G><rsub|a>=<big|sum><rsub|j=1><rsup|4>c<rsup|\<nabla\>><rsub|j>*<big|sum><rsub|v=-j><rsup|j-1><math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|v>,<space|2em><math-ss|N><rsub|a>=<math-ss|G><rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|\<ell\>|)>><math-ss|M><rsub|a>,<space|2em><math-ss|K><rsub|0>=<big|prod><rsub|a><math-ss|M><rsub|a><math-ss|N><rsub|a><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<math-ss|G><rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|\<ell\>|)>>> is
  <math|<math-ss|G><rsub|a>> with every shift replaced by its
  <math|\<ell\>>th power, and <math|<math-ss|G><rsub|a><space|0.17em><around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|a>-1|)>=\<nabla\><rsub|a>>.
  Put <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|a>=<math-ss|G><rsub|a><math-ss|M><rsub|a><big|prod><rsub|c\<ne\>a><math-ss|M><rsub|c><math-ss|N><rsub|c>>
  and <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|edge>>=<math-ss|N><rsub|a><big|prod><rsub|c\<ne\>a><math-ss|M><rsub|c><math-ss|N><rsub|c>>.
  Since <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|a><around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|a>-1|)>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>-1|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|K><rsub|0>\<nabla\><rsub|a>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*\<nabla\><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|coarse>><math-ss|K><rsub|a>,<space|2em><math-ss|K><rsub|0><around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|a>-1|)>=\<ell\><rsup|-1>*<around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>-1|)><math-ss|K><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|edge>>,<space|2em><math-ss|K><rsub|a>-<math-ss|K><rsub|0>=<around|(|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>-1|)><math-ss|K><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tr>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The last quotient is a finite Laurent polynomial:
  <math|<math-ss|G><rsub|a>-<math-ss|G><rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|\<ell\>|)>><math-ss|M><rsub|a>>
  vanishes modulo <math|<math-ss|s><rsub|a><rsup|\<ell\>>-1> after
  multiplication by <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|a>>, because
  <math|<math-ss|M><rsub|a>> averages all residue classes and both factors
  <math|<math-ss|G><rsub|a>,<math-ss|G><rsub|a><rsup|<around|(|\<ell\>|)>>>
  have mass one. Polynomial division defines
  <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tr>>>. The support radius of
  <math|<math-ss|K><rsub|0>> is at most <math|56*\<ell\>>, its mass is one
  and its absolute coefficient sum is at most <math|32<rsup|4>>.

  For Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>'s stationary background
  <math|U<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|V|)>>, stationary substitution is
  <math|\<cal-I\>*F<around|(|V|)>=F<around|(|U<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|V|)>|)>>.
  The stationary equation is <math|D<rsub|U>*<around|(|<math-ss|S>+<math-ss|S><rsub|<math-up|block>>|)>=0>
  on the gauge-fixed fine variations. Retain the stationary coarse frame
  <math|h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>> from the coordinates preceding
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B4>, and write
  <math|Q<rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|U|)>=<around|(|Q<rsub|e><rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|U|)>|)><rsub|e>>
  and <math|<wide|Q|~>*<around|(|U,h|)>=<around|(|Q<rsup|<math-up|pred>><around|(|U|)>|)><rsup|h>>
  for the predictor in that frame. Its fine-coordinate differential is

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|f>*\<eta\>=<around*|\<nobracket\>|<frac|d|d*s>|\|><rsub|s=0>*log
    <around*|(|<wide|Q|~>*<around|(|e<rsup|s*\<eta\>>*U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)>*<wide|Q|~>*<around|(|U,h<rsub|<math-up|fr>>|)><rsup|-1>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For the root correction, let <math|\<varrho\><rsub|x>*<around|(|y;\<eta\>|)>>
  be minus the uniform average of the signed sums of <math|\<eta\>> over the
  frame routes from <math|y> to the patch points, as in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>. Define scalar coefficients
  <math|b<rsub|x,e>> by

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|e>b<rsub|x,e>*\<eta\><rsub|e>=\<varrho\><rsub|x>*<around|(|0;\<eta\>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|y>w<rsub|y><rsup|<math-up|av>>*\<varrho\><rsub|x>*<around|(|y;\<eta\>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Let <math|g<rsub|x,e>=h<rsub|<math-up|fr>><around|(|x|)>*U<around|(|\<gamma\><rsub|x,e>|)>>,
  where <math|\<gamma\><rsub|x,e>> is the fixed coordinate route from the
  embedded root <math|x> to <math|e<rsup|->>. The root map and its coarse
  gauge variation are

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|R<rsub|0>*\<eta\>|)><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|e>b<rsub|x,e><math-up|Ad><around|(|g<rsub|x,e>|)>*\<eta\><rsub|e>,<space|2em><around|(|d<rsub|V>*\<phi\>|)><rsub|e>=\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|->|)>-<math-up|Ad><around|(|V<rsub|e>|)>*\<phi\><around|(|e<rsup|+>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  These formulas transport a fine displacement to the predictor and correct
  its root frame. Evaluate them at the stationary pair and set
  <math|\<cal-I\><rsub|\<ast\>>*Y=<around|(|R<rsub|f>+d<rsub|V>*R<rsub|0>|)>*Y<around|(|U<rsub|<math-up|stat>><around|(|V|)>|)>>.
  With <math|\<delta\><rsub|a>=<math-ss|s><rsub|a>-1>, transfer by

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:I-transfer-map>J<rprime|'><rsub|a*b>=\<ell\><rsup|4><math-ss|K><rsub|a>\<cal-I\>*J<rsub|a*b>,<space|1em>Y<rprime|'><rsub|b>=\<ell\><rsup|5><math-ss|K><rsub|0>\<cal-I\><rsub|\<ast\>>*Y<rsub|b>,<space|1em>\<cal-E\><rprime|'><rsub|b>=\<ell\><rsup|5><math-ss|K><rsub|0>\<cal-I\>*\<cal-E\><rsub|b>,<space|1em>q<rprime|'><rsub|a>=\<ell\><rsup|3><math-ss|K><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|edge>>\<cal-I\>*q<rsub|a>+\<ell\><rsup|4><math-ss|K><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tr>>\<cal-I\>*J<rsub|a*a><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  Starting with <math|q<rsub|a>=0>, this preserves
  <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<delta\><rsub|a>*q<rsub|a>>.
  The antisymmetric part, which need not remain zero, is likewise a sum of
  forward differences: its new correction in direction <math|a> is
  <math|\<ell\><rsup|4><math-ss|K><rsub|a><rsup|<math-up|tr>>\<cal-I\>*J<rsub|a*b>>
  and in direction <math|b> is <math|-\<ell\><rsup|4><math-ss|K><rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|tr>>\<cal-I\>*J<rsub|b*a>>,
  in addition to the transferred old differences.

  For an analytic local function <math|F>, its degree-<math|d> Taylor map
  <math|<taylormap><rsub|d><space|0.17em>F>
  (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:local-fields>) is, on a radial jet
  <math|<math-ss|j>>, <math|<big|sum><rsub|v=0><rsup|d><around|[|\<varepsilon\><rsup|v>|]>*F<around|(|U<around|(|<math-up|sc><rsub|\<varepsilon\>><math-ss|j>|)>|)>>,
  in the basis of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D07>. The weighted Taylor
  map is <math|t<rsub|m><rsup|<factorcount>><taylormap><rsub|d><space|0.17em>F>,
  with the factor-count operator of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>.
  Symmetrize <math|J>, average over <math|<hypercubic>> and conjugation,
  apply the degree-four Taylor map and marked transport, and subtract the
  mixture mean. Call the resulting field <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|L>>. The same
  operations on the local contractions defining <math|\<Xi\>,D> give
  <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Xi\>>,B<rsub|i><rsup|D>>.

  <paragraph|Extraction of the scalar coefficient.> We first calculate the
  scalar coefficient before marked transport. For a chart function <math|F>
  define its Hessian Fourier kernel by

  <\equation*>
    <wide|F|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>>*<around|(|p,q|)>=<big|sum><rsub|x,y>e<rsup|i*<around|(|p\<cdot\>x+q\<cdot\>y|)>>*D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|0|)><around|[|e<rsub|x,c,\<alpha\>>,e<rsub|y,c,\<alpha\>>|]><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Fix a colour <math|\<alpha\>>. For a family <math|F<rsub|b>> write

  <\equation*>
    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-ss|l><around|(|F|)>>|<cell|=<math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c,b>i*<around|[|z<rsup|2>*t|]><wide|F<rsub|b>|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>><around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>+t*e<rsub|b>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-ss|q><around|(|F|)>>|<cell|=<around|\||\<Lambda\>|\|><rsup|-1><math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c><around|[|z<rsup|2>|]>*<wide|F|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>>*<around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation*>

  Coefficients use <math|<around|[|z<rsup|r>*t<rsup|s>|]>*F=<around|(|r!s!|)><rsup|-1>*\<partial\><rsub|z><rsup|r>*\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|s>*F<around|(|0|)>>.
  Put <math|<math-ss|h><around|(|F|)>=<math-ss|l><around|(|F|)>-<frac|1|2><math-up|Re><big|sum><rsub|a\<ne\>c><around|[|z<rsup|2>|]><wide|F<rsub|c>|^><rsub|c*\<alpha\>,c*\<alpha\>><around|(|z*e<rsub|a>,-z*e<rsub|a>|)>>.
  The Haar coefficient is <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>=<math-ss|h><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y|)>>;
  the subtraction corrects the lattice derivative. Define

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<tfrac|1|2>log det
    <around|(|H+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)>-log det
    <around|(|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)>,<space|1em>R<rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>=<math-ss|q><around|(|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>,<space|1em>\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>=-\<mu\>*R<rsub|\<Lambda\>><rprime|'><around|(|\<mu\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  where <math|H> is the left Hessian of <math|<math-ss|S>> and <math|d> is
  the infinitesimal gauge insertion. For <math|a\<gtr\>0> and
  <math|r\<in\><N>>, with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>=<around|\||x|\|><rsub|\<infty\>>>
  in the kernel's lattice units, put

  <\equation*>
    \<vartheta\><rsub|a,r><around|(|x|)>=e<rsup|a<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>>*<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>>|)><rsup|r><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  For a chart function anchored at <math|o>, its weighted order-<math|r>
  seminorm is the sum of absolute link coefficients of
  <math|D<rsup|r>*F<around|(|0|)>> against
  <math|<big|prod><rsub|v=1><rsup|r>\<vartheta\><rsub|a,r>*<around|(|e<rsub|v><rsup|->-o|)>>;
  for a displacement use its link components. These weights control the
  spatial moments in the momentum derivatives below. Write
  <math|F\<equiv\><rsub|4>G> if the field derivatives of <math|F> and
  <math|G> at zero agree through order four. The next lemma computes the
  scalar coefficient; its proof follows.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Regulated Ward coefficient><label|lem:I-regulator> Fix a level
    <math|k>, a weight <math|a\<gtr\>0> and <math|C\<less\>\<infty\>>. For
    <math|\<nu\>\<in\><N>> let <math|N<rsub|\<nu\>>\<gtr\>k> and let
    <math|<around|(|J,Y,\<cal-E\>,q,<math-ss|t>|)><rsup|<around|(|\<nu\>|)>>>
    be translation-invariant local data on the level-<math|k> torus of period
    <math|n<rsub|\<nu\>>\<to\>\<infty\>>, with
    <math|<math-ss|S>=<math-ss|S><rsub|N<rsub|\<nu\>>,k>>, such that

    <\enumerate>
      <item*|(a)><math|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>\<equiv\><rsub|4><big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-\<cal-E\><rsub|b>>;

      <item*|(b)><math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<delta\><rsub|a>*q<rsub|a>>
      and <math|J<rsub|a*b>-J<rsub|b*a>=<big|sum><rsub|c>\<delta\><rsub|c><math-ss|t><rsub|a*b*c>>;

      <item*|(c)>the <math|a>-weighted seminorms of orders <math|2>\U<math|4>
      of <math|J,Y,q,<math-ss|t>> are at most <math|C>;

      <item*|(d)>those of <math|\<cal-E\>>, and the deviations of the
      zero-field Hessians of <math|J> and <math|Y> from their free values on
      tests of weight at most <math|4> and <math|5>, are at most
      <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|\<nu\>>\<to\>0>.
    </enumerate>

    Then, for every colour, <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|Y<rsup|<around|(|\<nu\>|)>>|)>\<to\>-12<slope>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The inputs are the normalization of<nbsp><math|<slope>>
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D01>), the kinetic and quadratic
    cancellations and the classical matching of the path action
    (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:C2> and<nbsp><reference|lem:C4>), and the
    one-loop coefficient of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C7>. Write
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|(|H+d*d<rsup|\<ast\>>+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>>,
    <math|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>=<around|(|d<rsup|\<ast\>>*d+\<mu\><rsup|2>*I|)><rsup|-1>>.
    For <math|\<Psi\><rsub|s><around|(|U|)>=e<rsup|s*Y<around|(|U|)>>*U> let
    <math|\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|z|)>=\<partial\><rsub|s>\|<rsub|0>log
    <around|(|\<Psi\><rsub|s>*<around|(|e<rsup|z>*U|)>*\<Psi\><rsub|s><around|(|U|)><rsup|-1>|)>>
    and <math|\<cal-V\>=D*\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|0|)>>. If
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|U>> is the mixed derivative of <math|log
    <around|(|<math-up|gauge><rsub|e<rsup|v>><around|(|e<rsup|z>*U|)>*U<rsup|-1>|)>>
    in <math|v,z> at zero, gauge invariance gives <math|H*d=-\<cal-N\>>,
    <math|\<cal-N\>=D<math-ss|S><around|[|\<cal-J\><rsub|U>|]>>, hence

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*d=d*\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>+\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-N\>*\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The product rule for <math|D<rsup|2><around|(|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y|]>|)>>
    and cyclicity of trace now give

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-contact><tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*D<rsup|2><around|(|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y|]>|)>|)>-<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y=D*\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|Y|]>-\<mu\><rsup|2><math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-V\>|)>+D<math-ss|S><around|[|Z|]>,
    </equation>

    with <math|Z=<frac|1|2><math-up|contr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>,D<rsup|2>*\<cal-Y\><rsub|U><around|(|0|)>|)>-<math-up|contr><around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>*d<rsup|\<ast\>>*<around|(|\<cal-V\>+\<cal-V\><rsup|\<ast\>>|)>*\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>,\<cal-J\><rsub|U>|)>>.
    Contractions sum the indicated matrix indices.

    <em|Cancellation.> The leading quadratic kernel of an invariant scalar
    action at the required momentum order is a colour multiple of
    <math|<around|\||p|\|><rsup|2>*I-p\<otimes\>p>. Write
    <math|X<rsub|a*c*b>> for the coefficient of momentum <math|e<rsub|a>> in
    the diagonal polarization-<math|c> entry of <math|D*Y<rsub|b>>, with its
    colour indices contracted against the action kernel. Gauge covariance
    makes the mixed polarization term <math|-X<rsub|c*a*c>> and gives
    <math|X<rsub|a*a*a>=0>. In the tested variation the three terms, after
    summing the intermediate polarization, are

    <\equation*>
      -2*X<rsub|a*c*a>-X<rsub|c*a*c>+<big|sum><rsub|b>X<rsub|b*c*b>*<space|2em><around|(|a\<ne\>c|)>.
    </equation*>

    Summing over <math|a\<ne\>c> makes the first and second sums
    <math|-3*<big|sum><rsub|b,c>X<rsub|b*c*b>>, cancelling the third sum.
    Higher momentum orders do not contribute. This applies to
    <math|D*\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|Y|]>>,
    <math|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Z|]>> and the further action variation below,
    uniformly on each fixed <math|\<mu\>\<in\><around|[|\<delta\>,1|]>>. For
    the first term on the left of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-contact>, substitute
    <math|D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>\<equiv\><rsub|4><big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-\<cal-E\><rsub|b>>.
    The coefficient linear in the total momentum <math|t*e<rsub|b>> selects
    <math|a=b>; summing <math|b> gives <math|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>J=<big|sum><rsub|a>\<delta\><rsub|a>*q<rsub|a>>.
    Its additional total-momentum factor has zero coefficient linear in
    <math|t>. The defect <math|\<cal-E\><rsub|b>> tends to zero in the
    weighted seminorms, so its contracted coefficient does too. The
    correction in <math|\<frak-h\>> tends to zero by the same identity.

    <em|The regulator term.> For <math|\<chi\><around|(|x|)>=\<b-1\><rsub|x=0>>
    define <math|M<rsub|b>*h<around|(|x|)>=\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*h<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>>,
    <math|<around|(|D<rsub|b>*h|)><rsub|a><around|(|x|)>=\<delta\><rsub|a>*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*h<rsub|b><around|(|x|)>>
    and <math|<around|(|T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|r|)>>*h|)><around|(|x|)>=\<delta\><rsub|b>*\<chi\><around|(|x|)>*h<around|(|x|)>>
    on <math|r>-forms, <math|r=0,1>. Set

    <\equation*>
      W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>=<math-up|sym><around|(|\<cal-V\><rsub|b>-d*M<rsub|b>-D<rsub|b>|)>+<tfrac|1|2>T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>,<space|2em>W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>=<math-up|sym><around|(|M<rsub|b>*d|)>-<tfrac|1|2>T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|<math-up|sym><space|0.17em>A=<around|(|A+A<rsup|\<ast\>>|)>/2>.
    The inverse identity above yields

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<cal-V\><rsub|b>|)>=>|<cell|<math-up|tr><around*|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*<around|(|D<rsub|b>-<tfrac|1|2>T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>|)>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>*T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>|)>+D<math-ss|S><around|[|Z<rsub|b><rprime|'>|]>+<math-up|Rem><rsub|b>,>>|<row|<cell|<math-up|Rem><rsub|b>=>|<cell|<math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>*W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>|)>+<math-up|tr><around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>*W<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|0|)>>|)>,<space|2em>Z<rsub|b><rprime|'>=<math-up|contr><around|(|\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>*M<rsub|b>*\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>,\<cal-J\><rsub|U>|)>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The tested <math|D<rsub|b>> term tends to
    <math|<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>;
    each of the four <math|T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|r|)>>> terms tends to
    <math|<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>
    or <math|<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>
    respectively. This follows by summing <math|\<delta\><rsub|b>*\<chi\>>
    against the two Fourier waves: its symbol is
    <math|e<rsup|-i*t>-1=-i*t+O<around|(|t<rsup|2>|)>>, paired with the
    factor <math|i> in <math|<math-ss|l>>. Translation invariance identifies
    the local anchor sum with the volume-normalized trace in
    <math|<math-ss|q>>. Consequently their sum is
    <math|-<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-R\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>+2<math-ss|q><around|(|<math-up|tr><space|0.17em>\<cal-G\><rsub|\<mu\>>|)>>.
    Differentiation of <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|\<mu\>>> in <math|\<mu\>> gives
    the same expression multiplied by <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>>, namely
    <math|\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>>. We have proved

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-regulated-ward>\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>=\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>+\<mu\><rsup|2><math-ss|l><around|(|<math-up|Rem>|)>+o<around|(|1|)>,
    </equation>

    with error uniform on every fixed <math|<around|[|\<delta\>,1|]>>.

    For the remainder, the inverse-kernel argument in the proof
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand> applies with the scalar regulator
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*I>: <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|r>*R<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|q|)>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around|(|<around|\||q|\|>+\<mu\>|)><rsup|-2-r>>.
    Differentiating <math|R<rsub|\<mu\>>*A<rsub|\<mu\>>=I> once more gives
    the same bound for <math|r=3>. Hypotheses (c)\U(d) give bounded weighted
    derivatives of <math|W<rsup|<around|(|r|)>>>. Their zero-background
    symbols and first momentum derivatives tend to zero, as does the
    zero-momentum first field insertion: the free curvature displacement
    cancels <math|d*M<rsub|b>+D<rsub|b>-<frac|1|2>*T<rsub|b><rsup|<around|(|1|)>>>
    in the displayed formula. Put <math|z=<around|\||q|\|>+\<mu\>>. Taylor
    expansion gives factors <math|z<rsup|2>,z,1> for the zero-, one- and
    two-field insertions of these symbols. In the product rule they pair with
    inverse bounds <math|C*z<rsup|-4>,C*z<rsup|-3>,C*z<rsup|-2>>,
    respectively. Thus the scaled derivative bound through order three is
    <math|C*<around|(|z<rsup|2>*z<rsup|-4>+2*z*z<rsup|-3>+z<rsup|-2>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*z<rsup|-2>>.
    Unscaling the quadratic coefficient gives
    <math|C*z<rsup|-4>\<leqslant\>C<rprime|'>*z<rsup|-5>>. In four dimensions
    the integral of this bound is at most <math|C/\<mu\>>: the ball
    <math|<around|\||q|\|>\<leqslant\>\<mu\>> costs <math|C/\<mu\>>, and the
    annulus <math|2<rsup|j>*\<mu\>\<less\><around|\||q|\|>\<leqslant\>2<rsup|j+1>*\<mu\>>
    costs <math|C*\<mu\><rsup|-1>*2<rsup|-j>>. At fixed <math|\<mu\>\<gtr\>0>
    the finite sums converge; hence <math|<around|\||\<mu\><rsup|2><math-ss|l><around|(|<math-up|Rem>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*\<mu\>+o<around|(|1|)>>
    with <math|C> independent of <math|\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>.

    The proof of<nbsp><eqref|eq:C-integrand>, with <math|\<mu\><rsup|2>*I> in
    place of its regulator, gives <math|<around|\||R<around|(|\<mu\>|)>-12<slope>log
    \<mu\>|\|>\<leqslant\>C> for <math|0\<less\>\<mu\>\<leqslant\>1>. Here
    <math|R/12> is the coefficient <math|\<alpha\><rsub|b>> of this
    scalar-regulated kernel: the Hessian is twice the quadratic-action
    kernel, exactly the factor <math|2> in that coefficient. At fixed
    positive <math|\<mu\>>, Riemann sums give
    <math|R<rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\>|)>\<to\>R<around|(|\<mu\>|)>>.
    Choose <math|u\<less\>0>, then a length <math|L<rsub|\<ast\>>\<gtr\>0>.
    Once the period controls the endpoints, the mean value theorem on
    <math|<around|[|u-L<rsub|\<ast\>>,u|]>> selects
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|\<Lambda\>>\<in\><around|[|e<rsup|u-L<rsub|\<ast\>>>,e<rsup|u>|]>>
    with <math|<around|\||\<cal-D\><rsub|\<Lambda\>><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|\<Lambda\>>|)>+12<slope>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*C+o<around|(|1|)>|)>/L<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    Apply<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-regulated-ward> on this fixed interval, then let
    <math|u\<to\>-\<infty\>> and <math|L<rsub|\<ast\>>\<to\>\<infty\>>. This
    proves the limit without differentiating an asymptotic error.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|A stability lemma.> The maps from raw coefficients to continuum
  vectors only converge to the free quotient map<nbsp><math|s>. The next
  lemma turns a nearly null vector of these maps into functionals that
  read<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> with a rate.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Stability of the trace functional><label|lem:I-stability> Let
    <math|E,H> be finite-dimensional real normed spaces, <math|s:E\<to\>H>
    linear with a right inverse<nbsp><math|t> and <math|ker s=<R>v>,
    <math|v\<ne\>0>, and <math|\<rho\>:E\<to\><R>> linear. Let
    <math|s<rsub|i>:E\<to\>H> and <math|t<rsub|i>:H\<to\>E> be linear with
    <math|s<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i>=I> and <math|t<rsub|i>\<to\>t>, let
    <math|v<rsub|i>\<to\>v>, and let <math|e<rsub|i>\<geqslant\>1> satisfy
    <math|e<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0>
    and <math|e<rsub|i>*\<rho\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\<to\>0>. Then
    <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>s>, the functionals
    <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>=\<rho\>*t<rsub|i>> are uniformly bounded, and
    <math|e<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|\<\|\|\>>\<to\>0>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Put <math|k<rsub|i>=v<rsub|i>-t<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>>. Then
    <math|s<rsub|i>*k<rsub|i>=0>, and <math|k<rsub|i>\<to\>v> since
    <math|t<rsub|i>> is bounded and <math|s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>\<to\>0>. The
    maps <math|\<Phi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|h,c|)>=t<rsub|i>*h+c*k<rsub|i>> from
    <math|H\<times\><R>> to<nbsp><math|E> converge to
    <math|\<Phi\>*<around|(|h,c|)>=t*h+c*v>, which is injective
    (apply<nbsp><math|s>) and hence invertible, as <math|dim E=dim H+1>. So
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>\<to\>\<Phi\><rsup|-1>>; since
    <math|s<rsub|i>*\<Phi\><rsub|i>*<around|(|h,c|)>=h>, also
    <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>s>. For <math|y\<in\>E> write
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*y=<around|(|s<rsub|i>*y,c<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|)>>,
    so that <math|y-t<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>*y=c<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>*k<rsub|i>>
    and <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|y|\<\|\|\>>>
    uniformly in<nbsp><math|i>. Then

    <\equation*>
      \<lambda\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>*y-\<rho\><around|(|y|)>=-c<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>*\<rho\><around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>=-c<rsub|i><around|(|y|)>*<around*|(|\<rho\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>-\<rho\>*<around|(|t<rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Hence <math|e<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>-\<rho\>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*<around*|(|e<rsub|i>\|\<rho\><around|(|v<rsub|i>|)>\|+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\>|\<\|\|\>>*sup<rsub|j><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|j>|\<\|\|\>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsub|i>*<around|\<\|\|\>|s<rsub|i>*v<rsub|i>|\<\|\|\>>|)>\<to\>0>.
    Boundedness of<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>> follows from
    <math|t<rsub|i>\<to\>t>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Transfer to the continuum.> Put
  <math|b<rsub|i>=<refbeta><rsub|k<rsub|i>>>, <math|E<rsub|i>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/5|)>>, <math|R<rsub|i>=exp
  <around|(|<around|(|log log b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/6|)>>, and
  <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4><around|(|<scaleframe|i|4>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  <math|K<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5><around|(|<scaleframe|i|5>|)><rsup|-1><math-up|div>>.
  The frames intertwine signed permutations and conjugation, so
  <math|K<rsub|i>> maps centered real symmetric <math|<hypercubic>>-tensorial
  tensors into<nbsp><math|V<rsub|5>>; on the span of such tensors its kernel
  is the conserved line of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>. Functionals below
  act on <math|V<rsub|5>>, with any fixed norm.

  <big-table|<with|par-mode|center|font-size|0.84|<tabular*|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|5|5|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<cell|Quantity>|<cell|Behaviour
  as the radius tends to zero>|<cell|Proved in>>|<row|<cell|stress insertion
  <math|r<rsup|4>*T<rsub|a*b>>>|<cell|tends to
  <math|<maxwellstress><rsub|a*b>>, which is
  traceless>|<cell|Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>>>|<row|<cell|running
  coupling <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>>>|<cell|<math|\<beta\><rsub|i>/log
  <around|(|1/r<rsub|i>|)>\<to\><slope>>>|<cell|Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>>>|<row|<cell|scalar
  trace coefficient>|<cell|<math|r<rsub|i><rsup|4><around|(|<scaleframe|i|4>|)><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\>=-<slope>\<beta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1><curvaturesquare>+o<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>>|<cell|Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:I-coefficient>>>|<row|<cell|trace
  two-point function>|<cell|<math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)><space|0.17em><tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>\<to\><traceamplitude>>>|<cell|Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>>>>>><label|tab:I-orders>
  >|Orders of the stress comparison: along the sequence of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:SC>, the radii of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>, and all radii. Heuristically the last
  row is the square of the third times the free correlation
  <math|<traceamplitude><around|\||x|\|><rsup|-8>>
  of<nbsp><math|<curvaturesquare>>.>

  <paragraph|Why a rate is needed.> The divergence of the amplified stress
  field <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>> may grow
  like<nbsp><math|R<rsub|i>>. Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:I-coefficient>
  cancels it with multiples of <math|B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Xi\>>,B<rsub|i><rsup|D>>
  of that size and reads the trace coefficient from that
  of<nbsp><math|B<rsub|i><rsup|D>> with functionals<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>>.
  An error of<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>> thus enters multiplied by up
  to<nbsp><math|R<rsub|i>>; an error that merely tends to zero would not
  suffice. It must be <math|o<around|(|E<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>, and
  <math|R<rsub|i>/E<rsub|i>=exp <around|(|-<around|(|log log
  b<rsub|i>|)><rsup|2>/30|)>\<to\>0>. The next lemma transfers the scalar
  coefficient to<nbsp><math|V<rsub|5>> at this accuracy; its proof follows.
  Here <math|\<Upsilon\>> ranges over the indicated tensors.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Fourth-order Ward transfer><label|lem:I-transfer> There are
    uniformly bounded real functionals <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>>
    on<nbsp><math|V<rsub|5>> and <math|C\<less\>\<infty\>>, depending only on
    the chosen construction, such that the divergences satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-divergence>

      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsub|i>,<space|2em>\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|)>\<to\>-<slope>,>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Upsilon\>>\<to\><math-up|div><rsup|<math-up|Max>><space|0.17em>\<Upsilon\>,<space|2em>E<rsub|i>*<around*|[|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Upsilon\>>|)>-\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|raw>><space|0.17em>\<Upsilon\>|)>|]>\<to\>0*<space|1em><around|(|\<Upsilon\>=\<Xi\>,D|)>,>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation>

    and the tensors satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-tensor><math-ss|A><rsub|i>B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Upsilon\>>\<to\>\<Upsilon\><around|(|\<Upsilon\>=L,\<Xi\>,D|)>,<space|2em>b<rsub|i><rsup|p><math-up|tr><around|(|<math-ss|A><rsub|i>B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Upsilon\>>|)>\<to\>0*<around|(|\<Upsilon\>=L,\<Xi\>;p\<in\><N>|)>,<space|2em>\<beta\><rsub|i>/b<rsub|i>\<to\>1<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </lemma>

  Thus <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>> reads <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>>,
  that is <math|-4> times the <math|D>-coefficient, of a divergence with
  error <math|o<around|(|E<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>, and its value on the stress
  divergence, of size <math|O<around|(|R<rsub|i>|)>>, is the coefficient of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator> divided by<nbsp><math|12>.

  <\proof>
    We verify the hypotheses of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator> for
    the transferred current, compute the finite coefficient at each level,
    and read it along the radii of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>. The
    stationary kernels (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>\U<reference|lem:B6>)
    control the transfer, and Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>\U<reference|lem:E2.2a>
    control marked transport.

    <em|Current estimates.> The transfer identities give (a)\U(b) of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator>; we prove (c)\U(d) below.
    Differentiating the stationary equation eliminates its fine variation.
    Fourth-order matching in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C4> preserves
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|a>\<nabla\><rsub|a>*J<rsub|a*b>-\<cal-E\><rsub|b>\<equiv\><rsub|4>D<math-ss|S><around|[|Y<rsub|b>|]>>.
    The kernel identities above preserve the trace and antisymmetric parts as
    differences. Work after stage <math|j\<geqslant\>7> and choose
    <math|0\<less\>a\<less\>c<rsub|j>>, with <math|c<rsub|j>> the kernel
    decay exponent of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3>. Its kernel bound applies
    through polynomial degree three. Divide the order-<math|r> current defect
    by <math|<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|r>>. At orders
    <math|r=3,4>, the linearized stationary fields contribute
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|-2>> each, so transfer costs
    <math|C<rsub|j>*\<ell\><rsup|5-2*r>>. At order two the matched
    low-momentum terms must first be removed. The affine and quadratic parts
    of a stationary test field have weighted sizes
    <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)>/\<ell\><rsup|2>> and
    <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)><rsup|2>/\<ell\><rsup|3>>;
    the remainder is at most <math|C<rsub|j>*<around|(|1+<around|\||x|\|>|)><rsup|3>/\<ell\><rsup|4>>,
    by Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> and<nbsp><reference|lem:B5>.
    Subtracting the affine\Uaffine and affine\Uquadratic products leaves
    quadratic\Uquadratic and affine\Uremainder products, each with factor
    <math|\<ell\><rsup|-6>>. Multiplication by <math|\<ell\><rsup|5>> gives
    <math|C<rsub|j>/\<ell\>>. A bounded matched contribution gives the
    additional <math|C<rsub|j>/<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>>.
    More precisely the order-two factor is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j>*<around*|{|<frac|1|<around|(|log
      <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>>+<frac|<around|(|log
      <refbeta><rsub|m+1>|)><rsup|2>+1|\<ell\><rsub|m><around|(|log
      <refbeta><rsub|m>|)><rsup|2>>|}>.
    </equation*>

    Both terms are small once the ratio and the coupling are large. Nonlinear
    chain-rule partitions involve fewer than <math|r> derivatives and gain
    inverse logarithmic powers. Choose each ratio large, then its entry
    coupling large; the resulting triangular map contracts by <math|1/2>.
    After <math|n<rsub|<math-up|step>>> transfers the normalized defects are
    <math|O<around|(|2<rsup|-n<rsub|<math-up|step>>>|)>>.

    <em|The finite coefficient.> At level <math|k> set
    <math|h<rsub|k>=<around|(|log log <refbeta><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>> and use
    <math|M<rsub|k>=<cutoffsize><rsub|k>>. Define
    <math|<taylormap><rsub|5,k>=t<rsub|k><rsup|-2>*t<rsub|k><rsup|<factorcount>><taylormap><rsub|5>>,
    followed by real <math|<hypercubic>> averaging. Haar integration by parts
    in the density gives the degree-five coefficient in the form

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-five-pieces>\<Sigma\><rsub|k>=<taylormap><rsub|5,k><around|(|<math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|Haar>><space|0.17em>Y-D*\<cal-L\><rsub|k><around|[|Y|]>|)>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|stat>>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tail>>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|cl>>+\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|mark>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|\<cal-L\><rsub|k>> is the quadratic loop action of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D23> at level<nbsp><math|k>. The errors
    are stationary substitution, omitted loop paths, the classical defect and
    the marked remainder. Each has norm at most <math|e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>>>:
    truncate stationary kernels and loop paths at
    <math|C<rsub|j><around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|k>|)><rsup|2>>, using the
    kernel decay in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:B3> and the path
    bound of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C1>. These exponential tails bound
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|stat>>> and
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|tail>>> after the finite projection
    losses. The activity caps<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-activity-caps> and the
    threshold<nbsp><eqref|eq:I12-scales> bound
    <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|cl>>> in the seminorm of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>:

    <\equation*>
      t<rsub|k><rsup|-2>*\<epsilon\><rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|reg>>=<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-1/10>M<rsub|k><rsup|-2><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|<math-up|mark>>>,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a> cancels the radial Taylor coefficients
    through degree five on nonwrapping supports. The Taylor remainder has an
    additional small-field factor, at most one after entry. The fixed
    projection and localization powers of <math|M<rsub|k>> therefore give
    <math|C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-\<eta\><rsub|j>>M<rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>>
    with <math|\<eta\><rsub|j>=1/10>. For the forced term,
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> leaves
    <math|<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|k><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>=e<rsup|-M<rsub|k><rsup|1/5>/2>>,
    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a> retains the original eligibility
    event. Thus the bound is <math|C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-\<eta\><rsub|j>>M<rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>+C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>e<rsup|-M<rsub|k><rsup|1/5>/2>>
    after the finite projection losses. A wrapping support reaches distance
    <math|n<rsub|p,k>/10> from its anchor, where
    <math|n<rsub|p,k>=L<rsub|p>*D<rsub|k>>, so its covering number is at
    least <math|n<rsub|p,k>/<around|[|10*<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>|]>-1>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> at support exponent<nbsp><math|98>, at
    which the scale choices of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.1> retain
    its estimates, bounds its projected contribution by

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|j><refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>M<rsub|k><rsup|C<rsub|j>>*exp
      <space|-0.17em><around*|{|-<frac|n<rsub|p,k>|5*<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>>+2|}><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The wrapping bound vanishes on the ordered period tails at fixed
    <math|k>; the nonwrapping bounds are <math|o<around|(|e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>>|)>>
    after entry.

    The loop term in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-five-pieces> has zero
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> coefficient. If
    <math|H<rsub|<math-up|loop>>> is its quadratic link kernel,
    differentiation of <math|D*\<cal-L\><rsub|k><around|[|Y|]>> pairs
    <math|H<rsub|<math-up|loop>>> with <math|D*Y>. The cubic momentum test is
    a sum of pairings of constant and quadratic polynomial links and of two
    affine polynomial links. Constant links are pure gauge;
    <math|H<rsub|<math-up|loop>>> also kills affine links by both moment
    cancellations of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:C2>. Thus every pairing
    vanishes. The lattice correction in <math|\<frak-h\>> is the
    corresponding correction to this Taylor coefficient. Choose the transfer
    depth and period at this fixed <math|k> so that
    <math|\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>> differs from <math|-12<slope>> by at most
    <math|<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-100>>, using
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-regulator>. Choose the momentum-Taylor
    approximation to its finite local Taylor coefficient within
    <math|<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-30>>. The exact Taylor-coefficient identity
    gives the factor <math|1/12> for the twelve ordered transverse planes.
    Delayed entry bounds the retained loops
    <math|2*M<rsub|k><rsup|\<theta\><rsub|J<rsub|k>>>> and stage losses by
    <math|M<rsub|k><rsup|1/6>>. Therefore

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*M<rsub|k><rsup|1/6>,<space|2em><around|\||\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>|)>-\<frak-h\><around|(|Y|)>/12|\|>\<leqslant\>4<around|\<\|\|\>|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|\<\|\|\>>*e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>>+<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-30>/12<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    At fixed <math|k>, take any joint ordered limit point of the bounded
    coefficient families along the ordered tails<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>,
    and denote its real averaged coefficient by <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>>. All
    closed bounds survive; <math|S> and the maps converging in the proof of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> retain their fixed limits. Thus
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|k>|)>\<to\>-<slope>>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsub|i>>.

    <em|The equation direction.> Let <math|s<rsub|i>:R<rsub|5>\<to\>V<rsub|5>>
    be degree-five marked transport followed by
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|5><around|(|<scaleframe|i|5>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    Let <math|j<rsub|i>> be the raw coefficient of the transferred current's
    divergence after the same threshold normalization. At fixed <math|k>, the
    Taylor coefficient of the local current has zero
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> component by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-rho>:
    simplicity makes the degree-three Taylor coefficient of its trace a unit,
    whose forward difference vanishes. The stationary and marked remainder
    estimates compare the transported coefficient with this Taylor
    coefficient within <math|C*e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>/4>>. Include the current
    components in the joint ordered limit point, denoting their value by
    <math|j<around|(|k|)>>. Closedness preserves
    <math|<around|\||\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|j<around|(|k|)>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-h<rsub|k>/4>>
    for every such value. With <math|j<rsub|i>=j<around|(|k<rsub|i>|)>>,
    Taylor matching consequently gives

    <\equation*>
      j<rsub|i>\<to\>-v<rsub|<math-up|eom>>,<space|1em><around|\||\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|j<rsub|i>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*e<rsup|-h<rsub|k<rsub|i>>/4>,<space|1em>v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>=\<beta\><rsub|i><rsup|-1>*\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>-j<rsub|i>\<to\>v<rsub|<math-up|eom>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|E<rsub|i>*\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>|)>\<to\>0>,
    since <math|E<rsub|i>/\<beta\><rsub|i>\<to\>0> and
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>><around|(|\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|)>>
    is bounded. It remains to show <math|b<rsub|i><rsup|p>*s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>|)>\<to\>0>
    for every<nbsp><math|p>; this takes four steps.

    <em|Step 1: exterior moments vanish.> Localize
    <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>-\<beta\><rsub|i>*j<rsub|i>> before
    transport, with a fixed degree-four spectator. Its exterior moment is
    zero by Haar integration by parts, since the varied link is outside the
    spectator stencil.

    <em|Step 2: the spatial tail is small.> Let <math|v<rsub|X>> be regular
    majorants rooted at the insertion anchor and
    <math|c<rsub|X>=<cover><rsub|B<rsub|k>><around|(|X|)>>. The support
    exponent in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> is
    <math|\<sigma\><rsub|j>=100> (item<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns> of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.1>). Thus, for every
    <math|q\<gtr\>0>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|c<rsub|X>\<geqslant\>q>e<rsup|98*c<rsub|X>>*v<rsub|X>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-2*q>*<big|sum><rsub|X>e<rsup|100*c<rsub|X>>*v<rsub|X>.
    </equation*>

    The same inequality holds for the normalized forced majorants. Since
    every nonzero marked support contains its anchor, a connected term
    reaching a spectator at distance <math|R> in level-<math|k> units has
    <math|c<rsub|X>\<geqslant\>R/<around|(|2*B<rsub|k>+1|)>-1>. Shorter terms
    retain the zero exterior moment.

    <em|Step 3: the matched residual is <math|e<rsup|-H<rsub|n>/100>>.>
    Use<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match-level>, starting at
    <math|k<around|(|n|)>> and ending at <math|n\<geqslant\><reflevel|5>>,
    and replicate by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-residual-root>. Across logarithmic
    length <math|H<rsub|n>=<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|48>> the
    matching gain is <math|e<rsup|-H<rsub|n>/8>>. The fixed degree-five
    projections, threshold factors and transport columns cost
    <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)>>>: the logarithms of the threshold
    costs are <math|O<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|n>|)>>, and the column cost
    is bounded by <math|H<rsub|n>*sup<rsub|m\<geqslant\>n>
    J<rsub|m><rsup|-2>>. Fix the tiling level
    <math|m=m<around|(|n|)>\<less\>n> by the first backward crossing of
    <math|B<rsub|n><rsup|2>>; eventually <math|m<around|(|n|)>\<geqslant\><reflevel|5>>.
    If <math|\<ell\>=max<rsub|m\<leqslant\>v\<less\>n> \<ell\><rsub|v>>, then

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|n><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|n>\<leqslant\>\<ell\>*B<rsub|n><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|n>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|n>+6\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsup|4>*B<rsub|n><rsup|8>*q<rsub|n>+6=o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Indeed <math|\<ell\>\<leqslant\>log <refbeta><rsub|n>> and
    <math|B<rsub|n>=O<around|(|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|2>|)>>. Also
    <math|q<rsub|n>=O<around|(|<around|(|log <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|2>|)>>
    uniformly by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-majorant>, since the stage-cost
    bound in the order of choice (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>)
    gives <math|1+A<rsup|<math-up|path>><rsub|J<rsub|n>>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|n-1>\<leqslant\>log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>>, and <math|c<rsub|<math-up|dir>,j>\<leqslant\>1>.
    Their product is <math|O<around|(|<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>|)><rsup|22>|)>=o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)>>. The allowances
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|e,k>=<refbeta><rsub|k><rsup|-<around|(|q<rsup|<math-up|ht>><rsub|e>+4|)>>>
    fixed in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E>
    before<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-attachment-allowance> give <math|log
    \<zeta\><rsub|e,n><rsup|-1>=O<around|(|log
    <refbeta><rsub|n>|)>=o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)>>, so their factor
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-residual-root> also costs
    <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)>>>. The support tail of Step<nbsp>2
    is smaller, since <math|R> grows exponentially in <math|H<rsub|n>>. At
    radius level <math|n>, multiplication of the physical two-point error by
    <math|a<rsub|n><rsup|5>> cancels the factor <math|a<rsub|n><rsup|-5>> in
    the replicated mark weight. The resulting bound is

    <\equation*>
      exp <around|{|-H<rsub|n>/8+o<around|(|H<rsub|n>|)>|}>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|-H<rsub|n>/100>
    </equation*>

    after entry.

    <em|Step 4: rescaling to the radii.> To pass to the full radius
    normalization multiply by <math|<around|(|r<rsub|i>/a<rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|5>>.
    We may require <math|u<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>log log
    <refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>> in the diagonal extraction. The extra scaling
    factor is then at most <math|exp <around|(|5<around|(|log log
    <refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|3>|)>>, while
    <math|b<rsub|i>/<refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>\<to\>1>. Thus each rescaled
    evaluation after multiplication by <math|b<rsub|i><rsup|p>> is bounded by

    <\equation*>
      C*b<rsub|i><rsup|p>*exp <around|{|5<around|(|log log
      <refbeta><rsub|n<rsub|i>>|)><rsup|3>-H<rsub|n<rsub|i>>/100|}>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Finite separating free two-point tests give
    <math|b<rsub|i><rsup|p>*s<rsub|i><around|(|v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>|)>\<to\>0>.

    <em|Stability.> Construct right inverses
    <math|t<rsub|i>:V<rsub|5>\<to\>R<rsub|5>> of <math|s<rsub|i>>: lift a
    free vector by the common frame, undo its threshold normalization and
    marked transport, and check <math|s<rsub|i>*t<rsub|i>=I> with the frame
    inverse. These operations commute with signed permutations and
    conjugation, so lifts of vectors in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|5>> lie
    in<nbsp><math|R<rsub|5>>. The lifts converge on a finite basis by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>, so <math|t<rsub|i>\<to\>t> with
    <math|s*t=I>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-stability>, with
    <math|v<rsub|i>=v<rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|eq>>>,
    <math|\<rho\>=\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>> and
    <math|e<rsub|i>=E<rsub|i>\<leqslant\>b<rsub|i>>, gives
    <math|s<rsub|i>\<to\>s>, bounded <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>=\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>*t<rsub|i>>
    and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*s<rsub|i>-\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|tr>>|\<\|\|\>>=o<around|(|E<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>.
    The actual divergence satisfies <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>-s<rsub|i>*\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>\<to\>0>
    by the same equation estimate; for <math|\<Upsilon\>=\<Xi\>,D>,
    <math|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Upsilon\>>-s<rsub|i><math-up|div><rsub|<math-up|raw>><space|0.17em>\<Upsilon\>=o<around|(|b<rsub|i><rsup|-p>|)>>.
    Since <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Sigma\><rsub|k<rsub|i>>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsub|i>>
    and <math|R<rsub|i>/E<rsub|i>\<to\>0>, these estimates
    prove<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-divergence>.

    <em|Tensor traces.> Taylor matching gives
    <math|<math-ss|A><rsub|i>B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Upsilon\>>\<to\>\<Upsilon\>>.
    The trace of <math|\<Xi\>> is zero; the transferred trace of <math|L> is
    a forward difference. Its degree-three projection is a unit, since
    simplicity leaves no nonconstant invariant below degree four. For the
    degree-three stencil and its degree-four difference, fix <math|k<rsub|0>>
    so late that its backward crossing <math|m<rsub|0>=m<around|(|k<rsub|0>|)>>
    satisfies <math|<reflevel|4>\<leqslant\>m<rsub|0>\<less\>k<rsub|0>> and
    <math|B<rsub|k<rsub|0>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|m<rsub|0>>/a<rsub|k<rsub|0>>>.
    Match at <math|<reflevel|4>>, stop at <math|k<rsub|0>> and replicate at
    the fixed pair <math|<around|(|m<rsub|0>,k<rsub|0>|)>>.
    Equations<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-backward-match-level>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-residual-root> give

    <\equation*>
      C<rsub|k<rsub|0>>*e<rsup|-<around|(|s<rsub|k>-s<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)>/8>=O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|1/8>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The constant includes the inverse allowance, thresholds and <math|exp
    <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|m<rsub|0>>/a<rsub|k<rsub|0>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<rsub|0>>+6|}>>.
    Coefficient conversions grow as <math|e<rsup|o<around|(|s<rsub|k>|)>>>,
    giving endpoint error <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>>|)>>
    for any fixed <math|0\<less\>\<eta\><rsub|0>\<less\>1/8> after entry.
    Truncating the support sums above at <math|c<rsub|X>=\<eta\><rsub|0>*s<rsub|k>>
    leaves a tail at most <math|a<rsub|k><rsup|2*\<eta\><rsub|0>>>, and
    decreasing <math|\<eta\><rsub|0>> absorbs all finite losses. This
    estimate is uniform for endpoints in a fixed small neighbourhood of the
    separating tests. Average the degree-four difference over
    <math|m=<around|\<lfloor\>|a<rsub|k><rsup|-1+\<eta\><rsub|0>/2>|\<rfloor\>>>
    sites. Telescoping and the position-independent degree-three unit give

    <\equation*>
      <frac|2*C*a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>>|m*a<rsub|k>>=O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>/2>|)>,<space|2em>m*a<rsub|k>=O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>/2>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0.
    </equation*>

    The averaged testing functionals converge to the original separating
    functionals by smoothness, so a finite separating matrix still has a
    bounded left inverse. They therefore bound the trace coefficient by
    <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|k><rsup|\<eta\><rsub|0>/2>|)>>. Subexponential
    inverse transport preserves <math|o<around|(|b<rsub|i><rsup|-p>|)>> for
    every <math|p>, proving the trace assertion in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-tensor>;
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii> gives its coupling assertion.
  </proof>

  Conservation converts these divergence estimates into the scalar
  coefficient.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Scalar trace coefficient><label|prop:I-coefficient> Along the
    radii of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-coefficient>\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4><around|(|<scaleframe|i|4>|)><rsup|-1>*\<Theta\>\<longrightarrow\>-<slope><curvaturesquare><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We cancel the divergence of <math|\<beta\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>>
    with the two other tensors, read the trace coefficient
    with<nbsp><math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>>, and identify the resulting conserved
    tensor with<nbsp><math|T> through the conserved line of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2> and the normalized shell Ward identities
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2>. The free divergences of
    <math|\<Xi\>,D> are independent: a kernel combination would be a multiple
    of <math|L>, impossible in these two tensor coordinates
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>). Thus
    <math|f<rsub|i>:<R><rsup|2>\<to\>V<rsub|5>>,
    <math|f<rsub|i><around|(|c|)>=c<rsub|0>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Xi\>>+c<rsub|1>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|D>>,
    are eventually invertible with bounded inverses
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-divergence>. Set <math|c<rsub|i>=-f<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|)>>.
    Then <math|<around|\||c<rsub|i>|\|>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsub|i>> and

    <\equation*>
      X<rsub|i>=\<beta\><rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>+c<rsub|i,0>*B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Xi\>>+c<rsub|i,1>*B<rsub|i><rsup|D>
    </equation*>

    is conserved. Applying <math|\<lambda\><rsub|i>> to its zero divergence
    gives the exact identity

    <\equation*>
      4*c<rsub|i,1>=\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|i>*K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|L>|)>+c<rsub|i,0>*\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|\<Xi\>>|)>+c<rsub|i,1>*<around*|[|\<lambda\><rsub|i>*<around|(|K<rsub|i>*B<rsub|i><rsup|D>|)>+4|]><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The last two terms are <math|O<around|(|R<rsub|i>|)>\<cdot\>o<around|(|E<rsub|i><rsup|-1>|)>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-divergence> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-rho>; since
    <math|R<rsub|i>/E<rsub|i>\<to\>0> they vanish, and
    <math|c<rsub|i,1>\<to\>-<slope>/4=d<rsub|2>>, where
    <math|d<rsub|2>=-b<rsub|0>/2> is the trace coefficient of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:trace-products>.

    Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-tensor> and
    <math|R<rsub|i>/\<beta\><rsub|i>\<to\>0> give
    <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|4><around|(|<scaleframe|i|4>|)><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|i>\<to\>L>.
    Its weighted trace <math|\<beta\><rsub|i><math-up|tr><around|(|r<rsub|i><rsup|4><around|(|<scaleframe|i|4>|)><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|i>|)>>
    tends to <math|4*d<rsub|2><curvaturesquare>>, since the other traces
    vanish faster than every inverse power. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G2.2>
    now gives <math|X<rsub|i>=\<nu\><rsub|i>*T> with
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>\<in\><R>>: the tensors <math|X<rsub|i>> are centered
    in the mixture by construction, and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2> gives
    the centered, nonzero tensor <math|T>. By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, choose <math|A\<in\><maxwellspace|4>>, a
    separating spectator functional <math|o>, and an axis <math|b> with
    <math|y=o<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|Max>>*A|)>\<ne\>0>.
    For these fixed tests the flux of <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|4><around|(|<scaleframe|i|4>|)><rsup|-1>*X<rsub|i>>
    tends to the flux of <math|L>, namely <math|y>, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>. The flux of <math|T>, multiplied by
    <math|r<rsub|i>>, tends to <math|y> by the normalized Ward identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2> and the jet limit of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>. The first flux is
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>> times the second. Dividing by the latter proves
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|i>\<to\>1>. This gives the weighted trace limit for
    <math|T>.

    Finally <math|\<Theta\>=<big|sum><rsub|a>T<rsub|a*a>-e<rsub|<math-up|tr>><unitfield|4>>
    and <math|<around|(|<scaleframe|i|4>|)><rsup|-1><unitfield|4>=<unitfield|4>>.
    The extra term has size <math|<around|\||e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>|\|>*\<beta\><rsub|i>*r<rsub|i><rsup|4>\<to\>0>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-adjustable>.
  </proof>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-10.svg|291.346311pt|104.731935pt||><label|fig:I-squeeze>
  >|The squeeze. Only <math|<tracetwopoint>> is monotone
  (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2>). For lattice-rounded separations
  <math|t<rsub|i,\<pm\>>\<to\>t<rsub|\<pm\>>>,
  <math|t<rsub|->\<less\>1\<less\>t<rsub|+>>, the outer values, times
  <math|r<rsub|i><rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r<rsub|i>|)>>, tend to
  <math|<traceamplitude>t<rsub|\<pm\>><rsup|-8>> along the radii of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I-radii>; letting <math|t<rsub|\<pm\>>\<to\>1>
  gives the limit <math|<traceamplitude>>. Every null sequence has such a
  subsequence.>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>>
    The phase-table reflection identity E2 of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E1-E3> and the reality of <math|T> in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.2> make <math|e<rsub|<math-up|tr>>> real.
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>, <reference|lem:G3.2>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.4> show that <math|\<Theta\>> is real and
    <math|O<around|(|4|)>> invariant and that its continued label is
    Hermitian and Lorentz scalar. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.1> gives
    vacuum centering. For the asymptotic, take any prescribed positive null
    sequence and the subsequence in<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-adjustable>. By
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:I-coefficient>, the phase comparison,
    and the free normalization in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G1.1>, every
    generic pair <math|x=<around|(|x<rsub|0>,x<rsub|1>|)>> with
    <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<less\>100> and
    <math|<math-up|sep><rsub|\<infty\>><around|(|x|)>\<gtr\>1/100>, with
    <math|x<rsup|<around|(|i|)>>\<in\>r<rsub|i><rsup|-1>*a<rsub|n<rsub|i>><Z><rsup|4>>
    nearest to <math|x>, satisfies

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|i><rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r<rsub|i>|)><tracetwopoint><around|(|r<rsub|i>*<around|\||x<rsub|0><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>-x<rsub|1><rsup|<around|(|i|)>>|\|>|)>\<longrightarrow\><frac|<traceamplitude>|<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|><rsup|8>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Translation and <math|O<around|(|4|)>> invariance
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>) give
    <math|S<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;r*x<rsub|0>,r*x<rsub|1>|)>=<tracetwopoint><around|(|r*<around|\||x<rsub|0>-x<rsub|1>|\|>|)>>.
    Here <math|<slope><rsup|-2><around|(|4*d<rsub|2>|)><rsup|2>=1>, so the
    amplitude does not depend on<nbsp><math|<slope>>.

    Take <math|x<rsub|0>=0> and <math|x<rsub|1>=<around|(|t/2,t/2,t/2,t/2|)>>,
    with <math|1/2\<less\>t\<less\>2>. This pair is generic and has distance
    <math|t>. Monotonicity from Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> bounds the
    lower limit at radius <math|r<rsub|i>> by
    <math|<traceamplitude>/t<rsup|8>> for <math|t\<gtr\>1>, and the upper
    limit by that expression for <math|t\<less\>1>
    (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:I-squeeze>). Let <math|t\<to\>1>. The limit
    along this subsequence is <math|<traceamplitude>>. The subsequence
    criterion gives the full-radius limit. Here
    <math|<traceamplitude>=3<dimG>/\<pi\><rsup|4>\<gtr\>0> since
    <math|<dimG>\<geqslant\>1>; identifying it uses
    <math|d<rsub|2>=-<slope>/4\<ne\>0>. Thus <math|\<Theta\>\<ne\>0>.
  </render-proof>

  The following high-energy law follows from Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>
  and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2>, as proved below.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|High-energy trace limit><label|lem:I1.3> For every
    <math|\<varphi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c>*<around|(|<R><rsup|4>;<C>|)>\<cup\>\<cal-S\>>,

    <\equation*>
      lim<rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>\<to\>\<infty\>>
      R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8><around|(|log
      R<rsub|<math-up|E>>|)><rsup|2>*<big|int>\<varphi\>*<around|(|\<b-up-p\>/R<rsub|<math-up|E>>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<tracespectrum><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>=<frac|<traceamplitude>|1536*\<pi\>>*<big|int><rsub|<forwardcone>><minksquare><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)><rsup|2>*\<varphi\><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|4>*\<b-up-p\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The rescaled spectral measures are controlled through their Laplace
    transforms, which the trace law determines
    (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D16>, Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2>).
    Put <math|\<nu\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>=R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2>
    R<rsub|<math-up|E>><space|0.17em><tracespectrum><around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>><space|0.17em>\<cdot\>|)>>.
    For every future timelike Laplace covector <math|u>, with the Euclidean
    pairing <math|u\<cdot\>\<b-up-p\>> of item<nbsp><reference|def:W0> of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:wightman>, which is the Minkowski pairing
    of <math|\<b-up-p\>> with the future timelike
    <math|<around|(|-u<rsup|1>,-u<rsup|2>,-u<rsup|3>,u<rsup|4>|)>>, Lorentz
    invariance reduces its transform to the time-axis transform at
    <math|s=<sqrt|<minksquare><around|(|u|)>>>. Hence

    <\equation*>
      <big|int>e<rsup|-u\<cdot\>\<b-up-p\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>=R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2>
      R<rsub|<math-up|E>><space|0.17em><tracetwopoint><around|(|s/R<rsub|<math-up|E>>|)>\<longrightarrow\><traceamplitude>s<rsup|-8><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    To identify the constant, write <math|t=\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>> and integrate
    the spatial radius <math|q>:

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|<forwardcone>>e<rsup|-s*t><minksquare><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|4>*\<b-up-p\>=4*\<pi\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>e<rsup|-s*t>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>q<rsup|2>*<around|(|t<rsup|2>-q<rsup|2>|)><rsup|2>*<space|0.17em>d*q*<space|0.17em>d*t=1536*\<pi\>*s<rsup|-8><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The <math|s=1> transform bounds compact masses, so diagonal extraction
    and Riesz representation<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorem<nbsp>2.14>
    give vague subsequential limits. The <math|u/2> transform controls the
    <math|u>-damped tail, so every limit has the displayed transforms.
    Holomorphic uniqueness in each coordinate for timelike real part, then
    Fourier inversion on product Schwartz tests against the finite damped
    measures<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorems<nbsp>10.18 and<nbsp>9.11>,
    identify every limit with the cone density in the statement.

    For Schwartz tests positivity gives

    <\equation*>
      \<nu\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>*<around|{|\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>R|}>\<leqslant\>e*<space|0.17em>R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2>
      R<rsub|<math-up|E>><space|0.17em><tracetwopoint><around|(|<around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*R|)><rsup|-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|8>*<space|2em><around|(|R\<geqslant\>1,R<rsub|<math-up|E>>\<geqslant\>R<rsub|<math-up|E>,0>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Use the trace law at <math|1/<around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*R|)>> and
    <math|log R<rsub|<math-up|E>>\<leqslant\>log
    <around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*R|)>>. A Schwartz bound of order greater
    than eight sums the dyadic energy tails uniformly, since
    <math|<around|\||\<b-up-p\>|\|>\<leqslant\><sqrt|2>*\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>>
    on the cone. The <math|C<rsub|c>> limit then gives the Schwartz limit.
  </proof>

  For <math|\<psi\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>*<around|(|x,y|)>=R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|8>*\<psi\>*<around|(|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*x,R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*y|)>>,
  <math|<wavetransform>\<psi\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>=<wavetransform>\<psi\>*<around|(|\<b-up-p\>/R<rsub|<math-up|E>>|)>>.
  For <math|\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rsub|2>>,
  <math|<wavetransform>\<psi\>\<in\>\<cal-S\>>. Substitution in
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.3> gives the position-space consequence

  <\equation*>
    R<rsub|<math-up|E>><rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2>
    R<rsub|<math-up|E>>*<space|0.17em>W<rsub|2><rsup|\<xi\><rsub|\<ast\>>>*<around|(|\<Theta\>,\<Theta\>;\<psi\><rsub|R<rsub|<math-up|E>>>|)>\<longrightarrow\><frac|<traceamplitude>|1536*\<pi\>>*<big|int><rsub|<forwardcone>><minksquare><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)><rsup|2><wavetransform>\<psi\><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|4>*\<b-up-p\><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  <subsection|Precise non-free comparisons><label|sec:partII-I.2>

  A finite Wick profile of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D17> is, at small
  distance, a finite sum of terms <math|r<rsup|a>*log<rsup|k> r> with
  <math|a\<in\><Z>> and <math|k\<geqslant\>0>, plus a bounded remainder; the
  trace law requires the negative logarithmic power
  <math|log<rsup|-2><around|(|1/r|)>>. This gives the following exclusion;
  its proof follows.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Finite Wick obstruction><label|lem:I2.1> For every finite Wick
    profile <math|W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>>> and every <math|\<delta\>\<gtr\>0>,
    there is <math|r\<in\><around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>\<ne\>W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>><around|(|r|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    We expand each profile at small<nbsp><math|r> and compare with the
    positive limit of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:TR>. For one factor, polar
    coordinates and <math|\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>>=<sqrt|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>><rsup|2>+q<rsup|2>>>
    give

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>,k<rsub|E>,h<rsub|<math-up|mom>>><around|(|r|)>=<frac|1|2><around*|(|<big|int><rsub|S<rsup|2>>h<rsub|<math-up|mom>>|)>*<big|int><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|\<infty\>>\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|k<rsub|E>>*<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|2>-m<rsub|<math-up|sh>><rsup|2>|)><rsup|<around|(|j<rsub|h>+1|)>/2>*e<rsup|-r*\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>=0> this is proportional to
    <math|r<rsup|-j<rsub|h>-k<rsub|E>-2>>. Otherwise split at
    <math|2*m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>>; the bounded interval is analytic. On the
    tail expand <math|<around|(|1-m<rsub|<math-up|sh>><rsup|2>/\<omega\><rsub|m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|2>|)><rsup|<around|(|j<rsub|h>+1|)>/2>>.
    Taking enough terms makes the remainder integrable with any prescribed
    number of energy moments, hence gives a Taylor remainder of the required
    order. The retained terms are <math|I<rsub|a><around|(|r|)>=<big|int><rsub|2*m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>><rsup|\<infty\>>v<rsup|a>*e<rsup|-r*v>*<space|0.17em>d*v>,
    <math|a\<in\><Z>>. Integrate explicitly for <math|a\<geqslant\>0>;
    <math|I<rsub|-1><rprime|'>=-e<rsup|-2*m<rsub|<math-up|sh>>*r>/r> gives
    the logarithm, and <math|I<rsub|a><rprime|'>=-I<rsub|a+1>> gives all
    lower powers. Only nonnegative logarithmic powers occur. For products,
    choose each remainder order above the other factors' pole orders.

    Thus <math|<math-up|Re><space|0.17em>W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>>=<big|sum><rsub|a,k>c<rsub|a,k>*r<rsup|a>*log<rsup|k>
    r+O<around|(|1|)>> with finitely many terms. After multiplying by
    <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>>, the terms with
    <math|a\<gtr\>-8> vanish. Among the others, the least <math|a>, then
    largest <math|k>, gives a nonzero divergent leading term. A finite limit
    is therefore zero. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> makes
    <math|<tracetwopoint>> real, so equality with
    <math|W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>>> implies equality with its real part. The
    positive trace limit contradicts equality on
    <math|<around|(|0,\<delta\>|)>>, proving also
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:NF>.
  </proof>

  To describe polynomial mass-shell Wick contractions, put
  <math|q<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>=<around|(|\<b-up-k\>,<sqrt|m<rsup|2>+<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|><rsup|2>>|)>>,
  <math|m\<geqslant\>0>. Fix a finite set of elementary field indices, with
  elements <math|<math-ss|x>,<math-ss|x><rprime|'>>; words
  <math|u,u<rprime|'>> list its elements. For words of length <math|k>,
  <math|\<pi\>> ranges over permutations of
  <math|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,k|}>>. Comparison data specify finite
  mass\Upolynomial lists <math|\<cal-M\><rsub|<math-up|pair>>*<around|(|<math-ss|x>,<math-ss|x><rprime|'>|)>>,
  with <math|P\<in\><C><around|[|\<b-up-p\><rsup|1>,\<ldots\>,\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>|]>>,
  and complex contraction coefficients <math|c*<around|(|u,u<rprime|'>,\<pi\>|)>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Polynomial mass-shell pairings><label|def:D28> Set

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|m,P><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>=<frac|P<around|(|q<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>|)>|2*\<omega\><rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>>,<space|2em>L<rsub|m,P><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|int>v<rsub|m,P><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>*\<varphi\><around|(|q<rsub|m><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>|)>*<space|0.17em>d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-k\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For words <math|u=<around|(|<math-ss|x><rsub|i>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>>,
    <math|u<rprime|'>=<around|(|<math-ss|x><rprime|'><rsub|i>|)><rsub|i=1><rsup|k>>,
    define the convolutions of the functionals <math|L<rsub|m,P>> by

    <\equation*>
      <math-ss|P><around|(|u,u<rprime|'>;\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<pi\>>c*<around|(|u,u<rprime|'>,\<pi\>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|<around|(|m<rsub|i>,P<rsub|i>|)>\<in\><big|prod><rsub|i>\<cal-M\><rsub|<math-up|pair>>*<around|(|<math-ss|x><rsub|i>,<math-ss|x><rprime|'><rsub|\<pi\><around|(|i|)>>|)>><big|int><big|prod><rsub|i>v<rsub|m<rsub|i>,P<rsub|i>><around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|i>q<rsub|m<rsub|i>><around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|i>|)>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i>d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-k\><rsub|i><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For unequal lengths put <math|<math-ss|P>=0>. A zero denominator at zero
    momentum gives value zero.
  </definition>

  For finite polynomial fields <math|Q=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<alpha\><rsub|i>*u<rsub|i>>,
  including empty words, the bilinear pairing is
  <math|W<rsup|<math-up|pol>>*<around|(|Q,Q<rprime|'>;\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j>\<alpha\><rsub|i>*\<alpha\><rprime|'><rsub|j><math-ss|P><around|(|u<rsub|i>,u<rprime|'><rsub|j>;\<varphi\>|)>>.
  Empty contractions evaluate <math|\<varphi\>> at zero. The shell measure
  has no <math|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|-3>> factor.

  The next lemma separates each comparison by one product test; its proof
  follows.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Polynomial countertest><label|lem:I2.2> For every comparison data
    and every <math|Q,Q<rprime|'>>, there are <math|f,h\<in\>\<cal-S\>> with
    compactly supported <math|<wavetransform><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>\<otimes\>h|)>>
    such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>,\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|h|)>*\<Omega\>|\<rangle\>>\<ne\>W<rsup|<math-up|pol>>*<around|(|Q,Q<rprime|'>;<wavetransform><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>\<otimes\>h|)>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    Equality for all product tests would identify the two spectral
    representations, hence <math|<tracetwopoint>> with a finite Wick profile,
    which Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.1> excludes. In detail, equality for
    all product tests implies equality for every
    <math|\<varphi\>\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>>>: choose
    <math|<wide|h|^><rsub|+>=\<varphi\>> and real <math|<wide|f|^><rsub|+>=1>
    on its support. Approximate <math|e<rsup|-r*\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>>> by
    compact cutoffs. Dominated convergence applies:
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> controls <math|<tracespectrum>>;
    exponential damping controls comparison polynomials at infinity, and
    <math|d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-k\>/<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|>> is integrable at
    zero. Expanding the numerators into monomials factors the integrals into
    the kernels of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D17>, with the empty words
    giving constants. Hence <math|<tracetwopoint><around|(|r|)>=W<rsub|<math-up|Wick>><around|(|r|)>>
    for all <math|r\<gtr\>0>, contrary to Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.1>.
  </proof>

  For the photon comparison use Hilbert tensor products.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Ambient and physical Maxwell Fock spaces><label|def:D29> For
    <math|n\<in\><N>> colours put

    <\gather*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|d*\<sigma\><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>=<frac|d<rsup|3>*\<b-up-k\>|2<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|>>,<space|2em>\<Pi\><rsub|a*b><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>=\<delta\><rsub|a*b>-<frac|\<b-up-k\><rsub|a>*\<b-up-k\><rsub|b>|<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|><rsup|2>>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-K\><rsub|n>=L<rsup|2>*<around|(|d*\<sigma\>;<C><rsup|n>\<otimes\><C><rsup|3>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-F\><rsub|n>=<big|oplus><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0>\<cal-K\><rsub|n><rsup|\<otimes\>k>,<space|2em>\<cal-F\><rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|phys>>=<big|oplus><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0><math-up|Sym><rsup|k><around|(|\<Pi\>*\<cal-K\><rsub|n>|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </gather*>

    The density vanishes at zero momentum, so the value of <math|\<Pi\>>
    there is immaterial, and <math|\<Pi\>> acts pointwise. Thus a physical
    vector is symmetric and transverse in each slot. Translations multiply
    sector <math|k> by <math|exp <around|{|i*z\<cdot\><big|sum><rsub|j><around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>,<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>|)>|}>>.
    The sector-zero unit is <math|\<Omega\><rsub|0>>.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Coherent comparison vectors><label|def:D30> For Schwartz tests
    <math|h=<around|(|h<rsub|\<alpha\>,a>|)>>, put

    <\equation*>
      v<rsub|h>*<around|(|\<alpha\>,a;\<b-up-k\>|)>=<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|>*<big|sum><rsub|b>\<Pi\><rsub|a*b><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>*<wide|h|^><rsub|\<alpha\>,b,+>*<around|(|\<b-up-k\>,<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>=e<rsup|-<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|h>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>/2>*<big|oplus><rsub|k\<geqslant\>0><frac|v<rsub|h><rsup|\<otimes\>k>|<sqrt|k!>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    A comparison <math|U:\<cal-H\>\<to\>\<cal-F\><rsub|n>> is a complex
    linear isometry with image in<nbsp><math|\<cal-F\><rsub|n><rsup|<math-up|phys>>>,
    <math|U*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\><rsub|0>>, intertwining translations. Set
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)>=U*\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*\<Omega\>>.
  </definition>

  Real tests have real components. Causal separation means
  <math|<minksquare><around|(|x-y|)>\<less\>0> for every pair of points in
  their closed supports, where <math|<minksquare><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|x<rsup|4>|)><rsup|2>-<big|sum><rsub|a=1><rsup|3><around|(|x<rsup|a>|)><rsup|2>>.

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.2> compares two-point functions only. The Fock
  test asks more: an embedding of the interacting states into photon Fock
  space under which the trace field is local relative to the free Maxwell
  field. Relative locality would impose the equality excluded below.
  Formally, let <math|W<around|(|h|)>> be Weyl operators of the free Maxwell
  field with <math|W<around|(|h|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>=\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>>
  and <math|W<around|(|h|)><rsup|\<ast\>>=W*<around|(|-h|)>>. If
  <math|U*\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*U<rsup|\<ast\>>> commuted
  with <math|W<around|(|h|)>> for <math|h> causally separated from <math|f>,
  then, since <math|U<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>=\<Omega\>> and
  <math|\<iota\><rsub|4>*\<Theta\>> is Hermitian,

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<langle\>|\<cal-E\>*<around|(|-h|)>,\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)>|\<rangle\>>>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|0>,W<around|(|h|)>*U*\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*U<rsup|\<ast\>>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>|\<rangle\>>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around|\<langle\>|\<Omega\><rsub|0>,U*\<Phi\><rsub|\<Theta\>><around|(|f|)>*U<rsup|\<ast\>>*W<around|(|h|)>*\<Omega\><rsub|0>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>,\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Weyl operators enter only this motivation. The lemma compares the two
  matrix elements directly, and its proof uses spectral growth without
  restricting particle amplitudes.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Fock countertest><label|lem:I2.3> For every <math|n\<geqslant\>0>
    and every comparison <math|U>, for every ball about zero there are
    Schwartz <math|f> compactly supported in that ball and real compactly
    supported Schwartz <math|h<rsub|\<alpha\>,a>>, each causally separated
    from <math|f>, such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|\<cal-E\>*<around|(|-h|)>,\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)>|\<rangle\>>\<ne\><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|<wide|f|\<bar\>>|)>,\<cal-E\><around|(|h|)>|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The proof combines the spectral representation
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2>), the high-energy law
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.3>) and translation covariance
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H2.1>) with the dilation behaviour of local
    photon amplitudes. Fix a ball and suppose equality always holds.

    <em|Sector amplitudes.> Set <math|p<rsub|j>=<around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>,<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>|)>>
    and <math|p<rsub|<math-up|tot>>=<big|sum><rsub|j>p<rsub|j>>. The
    convolution argument of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.2> gives sector
    amplitudes <math|Y<rsub|k,s>>, with <math|s> listing colour and spatial
    indices:

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-sector>\<psi\><rsub|U><around|(|f|)><rsub|k,s>=<wide|f|^><rsub|+><around|(|p<rsub|<math-up|tot>>|)>*Y<rsub|k,s>,<space|2em><tracespectrum>=<around|(|p<rsub|<math-up|tot>>|)><rsub|\<ast\>>*<big|sum><rsub|k,s><around|\||Y<rsub|k,s>|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<sigma\><rsup|\<otimes\>k><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Box masses are finite. Cauchy\USchwarz and polynomial spectral growth
    give integrability against sufficiently high inverse polynomials in each
    momentum.

    The common sector unitary <math|i<rsup|k>> preserves the equality,
    physical vectors and translations. Write
    <math|<math-ss|Y>=i<rsup|k>*Y<rsub|k,s>>.

    <em|Locality and doubling.> Expand the coherent equality in a real
    parameter multiplying <math|h>, justified by Cauchy\USchwarz and the
    exponential series. Polarization and complexification give independent
    complex slot tests. Transversality removes the projector in contractions
    with physical waves. Shrink nonnegative unit-integral <math|f> to zero.
    Then <math|<around|\||<wide|f|^><rsub|+>|\|>\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|<wide|f|^><rsub|+>\<to\>1>; dominated convergence gives, for
    <math|g<rsub|j>> supported spacelike to zero,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-remote><big|int><math-ss|Y><around|(|\<b-k\>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|j><around*|[|<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>*<wide|g|^><rsub|j,-><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|]>*<space|0.17em>d*\<sigma\><rsup|\<otimes\>k>=<big|int><wide|<math-ss|Y><around|(|\<b-k\>|)>|\<bar\>><big|prod><rsub|j><around*|[|<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>*<wide|g|^><rsub|j,+><around|(|p<rsub|j>|)>|]>*<space|0.17em>d*\<sigma\><rsup|\<otimes\>k><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|<wide|g|^><rsub|-><around|(|p|)>=<wide|g|^><rsub|+>*<around|(|-p|)>>;
    the sector factors <math|i<rsup|k>> give exactly these signs.

    Fix other slots and use <math|g*<around|(|x,t|)>=a<around|(|x|)>*\<eta\><rsub|\<epsilon\>><rsup|<around|(|b|)>><around|(|t|)>>,
    with <math|\<eta\><rsub|\<epsilon\>>> approximating unit mass and
    <math|b=0,1>. If <math|0\<nin\><math-up|supp><space|0.17em>a>, these are
    spacelike for small <math|\<epsilon\>>. Thus both Cauchy data
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-remote> are supported at zero. Weighted integrability
    bounds their order by <math|J>, independently of the other tests.

    Each datum depends only on the <math|J>-jet: multiplication by a cutoff
    in <math|B*<around|(|0,\<epsilon\>|)>> leaves the distribution unchanged.
    For a zero-<math|J>-jet test, Taylor's formula bounds derivatives through
    order <math|J> by <math|O<around|(|\<epsilon\>|)>>; let
    <math|\<epsilon\>*\<downarrow\>*0>.

    The next paragraph proves a uniqueness implication: a combination of
    sector amplitudes whose two Cauchy data vanish is zero almost everywhere.
    It is applied below to a polynomial in the doubling operator applied
    to<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Y>>, not to<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Y>> itself. The
    massless wave equation separates the frequency branches from these two
    Cauchy data away from zero momentum. After smearing other slots, each
    branch is integrable, bounded and holomorphic in its timelike tube, with
    continuous real boundary. For such a combination with zero Cauchy data,
    vanishing persists under small translations of the other spacelike tests.
    Schwarz reflection and holomorphic uniqueness<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorems<nbsp>11.14
    and<nbsp>10.18> make the zero set invariant under adding <math|<R>u> for
    every future timelike <math|u>. These vectors span <math|<R><rsup|4>>,
    giving vanishing at every real translation. Successive Fourier
    inversion<nbsp><cite-detail|Rudin1987|Theorem<nbsp>9.11> of the smeared
    integrable amplitudes gives zero almost everywhere. Countably many
    shrinking bumps remove the other tests: their transforms are eventually
    nonzero at each momentum.

    Let <math|D<rsub|j><math-ss|Y><around|(|\<b-k\>|)>=<math-ss|Y><around|(|\<b-up-k\><rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,2*\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>,\<ldots\>,\<b-up-k\><rsub|k>|)>>.
    In the time-order-<math|b> Cauchy datum (<math|b=0,1>), this substitution
    gives <math|2<rsup|-3-b>> times the datum at
    <math|a<around|(|x|)>\<mapsto\>8*a*<around|(|2*x|)>>, from
    <math|d*\<sigma\>*<around|(|2*\<b-up-k\>|)>=4*d*\<sigma\><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>>
    and <math|<around|\||\<b-up-k\>|\|><rsup|1+b>>. Spatial jets of order
    <math|l> scale by <math|2<rsup|3+l>>, giving eigenvalues
    <math|2<rsup|l-b>>. Choose a polynomial that annihilates this
    finite-dimensional dilation action on both Cauchy jets. The uniqueness
    implication then makes the same polynomial in<nbsp><math|D<rsub|j>>
    annihilate<nbsp><math|<math-ss|Y>>: for some <math|J<rprime|'>>,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-doubling><big|prod><rsub|a=0><rsup|J<rprime|'>-1><around|(|D<rsub|j>-2<rsup|a-1>|)><math-ss|Y>=0<space|2em><text|in
      every slot >j<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    <em|Growth.> For <math|R=2<rsup|l>> let
    <math|M<rsub|<math-ss|Y>><around|(|R|)>=<big|int><rsub|<around|\||\<b-up-k\><rsub|j>|\|>\<leqslant\>R><around|\||<math-ss|Y>|\|><rsup|2>*d*\<sigma\><rsup|\<otimes\>k>>.
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-sector> and the trace law,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:I-small-mass>M<rsub|<math-ss|Y>><around|(|2<rsup|l>|)>\<leqslant\><tracespectrum><around|{|\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\><around|(|k+1|)>*2<rsup|l>|}>,<space|2em>2<rsup|-8*l>*M<rsub|<math-ss|Y>><around|(|2<rsup|l>|)>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For the limit, bound the energy mass by
    <math|e<space|0.17em><tracetwopoint><around|(|<around|(|<around|(|k+1|)>*2<rsup|l>|)><rsup|-1>|)>>.

    Lagrange interpolation in the commuting <math|D<rsub|j>> splits
    <math|<math-ss|Y>> into joint eigencomponents
    <math|D<rsub|j><math-ss|Y><rsub|\<b-e\>>=2<rsup|e<rsub|j>-1><math-ss|Y><rsub|\<b-e\>>>.
    Each is a finite combination of doubled copies, so
    retains<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-small-mass>. Moreover
    <math|M<rsub|<math-ss|Y><rsub|\<b-e\>>><around|(|2<rsup|l>|)>=2<rsup|2*l*<big|sum><rsub|j>e<rsub|j>>*M<rsub|<math-ss|Y><rsub|\<b-e\>>><around|(|1|)>>,
    since <math|d*\<sigma\>*<around|(|2*\<b-up-k\>|)>=4*d*\<sigma\><around|(|\<b-up-k\>|)>>.
    If <math|e<rsub|j>=0>, contraction preserves box mass, contradicting
    local square integrability and absence of an atom at zero unless the
    component vanishes. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-small-mass> excludes
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>e<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>4>. Thus
    <math|e<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1>, <math|<big|sum><rsub|j>e<rsub|j>\<leqslant\>3>,
    and <math|k\<leqslant\>3>, with box mass <math|\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|6>>
    for dyadic <math|R\<geqslant\>1>. The vacuum sector vanishes by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.1> and <math|U*\<Omega\>=\<Omega\><rsub|0>>.

    Summing<nbsp><eqref|eq:I-sector> gives
    <math|<tracespectrum><around|{|\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<leqslant\>R|}>\<leqslant\>C*R<rsup|6>>
    at dyadic <math|R\<geqslant\>1>. Multiplication by
    <math|R<rsup|-8>*log<rsup|2> R> gives zero in the limit, contradicting
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I1.3> on a nonnegative compact test in
    <math|<around|{|0\<less\>\<b-up-p\><rsup|4>\<less\>1,<minksquare><around|(|\<b-up-p\>|)>\<gtr\>0|}>>.
  </proof>

  An averaged form of this countertest, in which the coherent vectors are
  moved by maps that preserve physical inner products and fix the image
  of<nbsp><math|U>, is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:I2.4> in
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:correspondence>.

  <section|Products and contact subtractions><label|sec:partII-J>

  This section proves Theorems<nbsp><reference|prop:OP>
  and<nbsp><reference|prop:CT>. The coefficients of the product expansion are
  read off by probes. A finite family of probe correlations distinguishes the
  fields of a fixed degree: two fields of<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>> with the same
  correlations against every probe are equal. Applied to a cluster of
  insertions, the probes return finitely many numbers, and a fixed linear
  reconstruction map, a decoder, returns a field of<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>; on
  the probe values of a single field it returns that field. Lower degrees are
  removed before the next degree is read. The probes are chosen once, before
  the spectators and the phase, and the coefficients are computed from the
  mixed table; hence they depend on the cluster alone. The remainder is
  bounded in the commutative compression algebra<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>>
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2> before a character is evaluated, and a
  norm bound in<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> bounds every character and the
  mixed vacuum. The coefficients do depend on the prescription, that is, on
  the table and the probes chosen here; Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP>
  concerns this one coefficient system.

  For two fields <math|A,B> of degrees <math|d<rsub|A>,d<rsub|B>> at <math|x>
  and<nbsp><math|0>, with center<nbsp><math|0> and
  <math|<around|\||x|\|>\<asymp\>r>, the expansion tested against spectators
  reads

  <\equation*>
    S<rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around*|(|A<around|(|x|)>,B<around|(|0|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|y|)>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D><big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>>c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|A,B;<around|(|x,0|)>,0|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around*|(|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>><around|(|0|)>,\<b-B\><around|(|y|)>|)>+\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<zeta\>>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|<around|\||c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|j-d<rsub|A>-d<rsub|B>-\<varepsilon\>>>
  and <math|<around|\||\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<zeta\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|D+1-d<rsub|A>-d<rsub|B>-\<varepsilon\>>>
  for small<nbsp><math|r>; <math|O<rsub|0,\<lambda\>>> is a nonzero multiple
  of the unit. For <math|D+1\<less\>d<rsub|A>+d<rsub|B>> the remainder bound
  still permits growth as <math|r\<to\>0>; what increases with<nbsp><math|D>
  is its order, and once <math|D\<geqslant\>d<rsub|A>+d<rsub|B>> (with
  <math|\<varepsilon\>\<less\>1>) the remainder tends to zero. Nothing is
  asserted about a series in<nbsp><math|D>. The expansion goes back to
  Wilson<nbsp><cite|Wilson1969>; Fröb and Holland prove its existence for
  Euclidean Yang\UMills theory to all orders in perturbation
  theory<nbsp><cite|FrobHolland2016>. The expansion here is nonperturbative,
  at each finite truncation, with one coefficient system for every phase.

  The lattice estimate starts from the signed local and graded quotient
  bounds of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-local>
  and<nbsp><reference|lem:J-graded-quotient>. Each insertion carries a
  nilpotent external field with a weight. Blocking loses mark weight when
  insertion points approach one another; grouping nearby markers bounds this
  loss by a power of the ratio of insertion and fusion scales
  (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:J-scales>). Taylor subtraction and transport of
  the coefficient back to a coarser level produce one field and a small
  remainder, whose chessboard seminorm reflection positivity converts into a
  norm bound in<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>>. Derivative induction removes
  the restriction to grid points; tree induction allows several clusters to
  shrink at independent rates. The nested and integrated forms
  (Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.4>\U<reference|lem:J1.5> and
  Remark<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.3>) are not used in the proofs of
  Theorems<nbsp><reference|thm:A> and<nbsp><reference|thm:B>. The contact
  extension of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-J.2> is defined from the
  continuum correlations at distinct points, not as a lattice limit at
  coincident points; the lattice bounds of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated> make its shell sums converge.

  Throughout, the lattice insertions and quotient fields are those of
  Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D08> and<nbsp><reference|def:D10>;
  inclusions and derivatives are those of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. The
  rotations<nbsp><math|R<rsub|j><around|(|h|)>> are those of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:G3.1>. Write <math|<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>=<big|sum><rsub|i>deg
  A<rsub|i>> and <math|\<zeta\>\<in\><phases>\<sqcup\><around|{|<math-up|mix>|}>>,
  as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>.

  <paragraph|Inputs from the marked expansion and the quotient.>

  We first establish two estimates needed when the field to be extracted
  varies with the cutoff. Throughout this subsection, a local marked
  expression has the form of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D26> and the
  regularity, zero radial jets and forced supports of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a>. Write

  <\equation*>
    \<mu\><around|(|x|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|\||x<rsub|\<alpha\>>|\|>,<space|2em>x<rsup|\<circ\>>=x-x<rsub|0>*\<b-1\>,<space|2em>\<frak-r\><rsub|i>=\<cal-E\><rsub|i>+<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|i><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|-1/2>*\<cal-K\><rsub|i><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The last quantity is the weighted remainder mass of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>. Fix an output degree<nbsp><math|t>. The
  diagonal rescaling <math|<around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|i>x|)><rsub|\<alpha\>>=\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|t-deg
  \<alpha\>>*x<rsub|\<alpha\>>> accounts for the engineering degrees
  inside<nbsp><math|V<rsub|t>>. Write <math|C<rsub|i>=C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|i>>>
  for the column bound at level<nbsp><math|i>. A normalized column at
  level<nbsp><math|i> is the local expression<nbsp><math|\<frak-c\><rsub|i><around|(|b|)>>
  of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1> with coefficient vector<nbsp><math|b>;
  its coefficient map is<nbsp><math|<math-ss|C><rsub|i>>, and its remainder
  bound is <math|C<rsub|i>*\<mu\><around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|C><rsub|i>b|)><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>>
  by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>.

  Let <math|K<rsub|j>\<geqslant\>1> be the nondecreasing stage bound fixed
  before item<nbsp><reference|it:E-columns> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.1>. The degree rescaling above is the
  map <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|L>> in the proof of
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>. For an input
  coefficient<nbsp><math|v> and remainder mass<nbsp><math|q>, its averaged
  marked estimate gives an output coefficient<nbsp><math|u> and remainder
  mass<nbsp><math|r> with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-marked-recall>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\>*<around|(|u-M<rsub|i><math-ss|d><rsub|i>v|)>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|[|\<mu\><around|(|v<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>+q|]>,>>|<row|<cell|r>|<cell|\<leqslant\>K<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>*\<mu\><around|(|<around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|i>v|)><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>+\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*q,<space|2em>\<epsilon\><rsub|i>=\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-3/8>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  Here <math|M<rsub|i>> is the leading polynomial map, fixes the unit,
  preserves the nonconstant subspace, and satisfies
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|M<rsub|i><rsup|\<pm\>1>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>>.
  These estimates follow from<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-atom-estimates> and the
  symmetry averaging in that proof. In the threshold order of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>, the construction chooses

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-stage-room>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|64*K<rsub|J<rsub|l>+1><rsup|2>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l>,>|<cell|10<rsup|5>*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>+1><rsup|6>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|l+1>|)><rsup|2>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|1/8>,>|<cell|1+2*C<rsub|l>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|1/40>.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  The column choice imposes the first inequality; the ratio choice imposes
  the other two. Stage growth gives <math|J<rsub|l+1>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|l>+1>.

  The next estimate retains the signed difference produced by subtracting a
  column. A transported family on<nbsp><math|<around|[|k<rsub|\<ast\>>,m|]>>
  is a sequence of local marked expressions
  <math|\<frak-c\><rsub|l><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>+R<rsub|l>>,
  <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>m>, in which <math|R<rsub|l>>
  has coefficient zero and each term arises from the next finer one by one
  marked integration step of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>;
  <math|x<rsub|l>> are its coefficients and <math|r<rsub|l>> its remainder
  bounds. Its free affine parameter is a vector added to the column
  coefficient at the finest level<nbsp><math|m>. Write
  <math|q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>> for the combined coefficient and remainder
  bound of the original expression at<nbsp><math|m>, before this affine
  subtraction. In the next lemma, the interval<nbsp><math|<around|[|k<rsub|\<ast\>>,m|]>>
  is sufficiently late.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Signed local transport><label|lem:J-signed-local> (a) Every
    normalized column plus a zero-coefficient remainder at
    level<nbsp><math|m> admits a transported family
    on<nbsp><math|<around|[|k<rsub|\<ast\>>,m|]>>. Its densities satisfy
    <math|H<rsub|l>=\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t><blockmap|l><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,l+1>>H<rsub|l+1>>
    <math|<around|(|k<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>l\<less\>m|)>>, each step
    satisfies<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-recall>,
    <math|r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>+C<rsub|m>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>>,
    and the free affine parameter can be chosen so that
    <math|x<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=0>.

    (b) If <math|x<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=0>, then, for
    <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>m>,

    <\equation*>
      r<rsub|l>+\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|1/8>,<space|2em>r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  Thus a residual that starts with zero coefficient at the coarse end loses
  only the factor <math|4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>> at the fine end, and
  gains <math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|1/8>> at every coarser
  level.

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-signed-start>The termwise remainder estimates and the
    Lipschitz bound in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>
    control one step; the exact column identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1> keeps the densities exact; an induction
    along the interval turns the signed one-step inequalities into the
    discount.

    Let<nbsp><math|R> have coefficient zero and remainder
    bound<nbsp><math|q<rsub|0>>. Its regular terms have zero radial jets
    through<nbsp><math|t>, by the local format of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2a>; its forced terms retain their
    eligibility events. In the marked integration of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step>, the former gain an inverse
    block factor before the connected sums, and the latter keep half the
    forced suppression. The stage constants precede the ratio and its entry
    threshold, so both resulting bounds <math|C<rsup|<math-up|loc>><rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>/\<ell\><rsub|i>+o<rsub|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|i>><around|(|1|)>>
    are at most<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>>. Sum the regular
    masses<nbsp><math|g<rsub|a>> and forced masses
    <math|k<rsub|b>/<around|(|\<epsilon\><rsub|i+1><rsup|<math-up|for>>|)><rsup|1/2>>
    before taking the output norm. Their sum is at
    most<nbsp><math|q<rsub|0>>. The termwise bounds in that proof, or its
    Lipschitz estimate for the difference between a column plus<nbsp><math|R>
    and the same column, therefore bound the output coefficient and remainder
    by<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>*q<rsub|0>>. The rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>, in the marked norm defined below,
    justifies termwise integration and retains the exact density identity.
    The last inequality of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-stage-room> gives
    <math|<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|i>>.
    If<nbsp><math|Z> denotes the resulting local expression and<nbsp><math|z>
    its coefficient, then

    <\equation*>
      H<around|(|Z|)>=\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|t><blockmap|i><rsub|\<beta\><rsub|N,p,i+1>>H<around|(|R|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\><around|(|z|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>*q<rsub|0>,<space|2em>\<frak-r\><rsub|i><around|(|Z|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>*q<rsub|0><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Write the fine affine family as <math|\<frak-c\><rsub|i+1>*<around|(|a+b|)>+R>,
    with<nbsp><math|b> the free parameter. Set
    <math|c=<math-ss|C><rsub|i><rsup|-1>z> and replace it at the coarse level
    by

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-c\><rsub|i>*<around|(|a+c+b|)>+R<rprime|'>,<space|2em>R<rprime|'>=Z-\<frak-c\><rsub|i><around|(|c|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The coefficient of<nbsp><math|R<rprime|'>> is zero, and
    <math|\<frak-r\><rsub|i><around|(|R<rprime|'>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+C<rsub|i>|)>*\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>*q<rsub|0>>.
    Linearity of the column density gives
    <math|H*<around|(|\<frak-c\><rsub|i>*<around|(|a+c+b|)>|)>+H<around|(|R<rprime|'>|)>=H*<around|(|\<frak-c\><rsub|i>*<around|(|a+b|)>|)>+H<around|(|Z|)>>.
    Together with the exact column identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.1>, this proves the asserted density
    identity for every<nbsp><math|b>, including all forced terms. The
    supports remain fixed in<nbsp><math|b> by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>.

    For the column alone use<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-recall>, with fine
    remainder bound<nbsp><math|q<rsub|<math-up|col>>>. Adding<nbsp><math|Z>
    changes the coefficient by at most<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>*q<rsub|0>>.
    For the remainder, recentering costs at
    most<nbsp><math|C<rsub|i>*\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>*q<rsub|0>>, and
    shifting the column parameter costs at most
    another<nbsp><math|C<rsub|i>*\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>*q<rsub|0>>. The
    total extra cost is <math|<around|(|1+2*C<rsub|i>|)>*\<ell\><rsub|i><rsup|-2/5>*q<rsub|0>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*q<rsub|0>>.
    With<nbsp><math|q=q<rsub|<math-up|col>>+q<rsub|0>>, these are the
    asserted bounds. Repeating this construction from the fine end to the
    chosen coarse end produces the transported family on the whole interval.

    For later use put <math|U=\<mu\><around|(|u|)>>,
    <math|Y=\<mu\><around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|i>v|)>>,
    <math|K=K<rsub|J<rsub|i+1>>> and retain<nbsp><math|r,q> for the remainder
    bounds. Applying<nbsp><math|M<rsub|i><rsup|-1>> and using
    <math|\<mu\><around|(|v<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>\<leqslant\>Y> gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-signed-input>Y\<leqslant\>K*<around|[|U+\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*<around|(|Y+q|)>|]>,<space|2em>r\<leqslant\>K*Y+\<epsilon\><rsub|i>*q<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For an interval<nbsp><math|<around|[|k<rsub|\<ast\>>,m|]>>, choose the
    free affine parameter to cancel the coefficient
    at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>; this uses the invertibility
    of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|C><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>. The signed residual
    then starts with coefficient zero. In step<nbsp><math|l> put
    <math|K=K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>> and <math|A<rsub|l>=32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|l>|)><rsup|2>>.
    Induct from<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> toward<nbsp><math|m> on the cone
    condition <math|A<rsub|l>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|l>>,
    which says that the coefficient is small compared with the remainder
    bound; it holds at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> because
    <math|x<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=0>. Equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-stage-room>
    implies

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>4*K<rsup|2>,<space|1em>4*K*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em>4*K*A<rsub|l+1>*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>1,<space|1em><around|(|4*K<rsup|2>+1|)>*\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|1/8>*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>1.
    </equation*>

    In<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-signed-input> at step<nbsp><math|l>,
    <math|U=\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>>, <math|r=r<rsub|l>> and
    <math|q=r<rsub|l+1>>. From <math|A<rsub|l>*U\<leqslant\>r\<leqslant\>K*Y+\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*q>
    and <math|A<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>4*K<rsup|2>> obtain
    <math|4*K*U\<leqslant\>Y+\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*q>. Substitution
    into<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-signed-input> gives
    <math|Y\<leqslant\>4*K*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*q>. Thus
    <math|A<rsub|l+1>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>q>, since
    <math|\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>Y>; this is the cone
    condition at<nbsp><math|l+1>. Also <math|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|1/8>*r\<leqslant\>q>.
    Iteration gives the discount<nbsp><math|<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|1/8>>.
    At the finest level the affine subtraction has
    <math|r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>+C<rsub|m>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>>.
    The cone condition at<nbsp><math|m> and
    <math|A<rsub|m>\<geqslant\>2*C<rsub|m>> give
    <math|2*C<rsub|m>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|m>>,
    hence <math|r<rsub|m>\<leqslant\>2*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>>
    and <math|r<rsub|m>+\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m>|)>\<leqslant\>4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsub|<math-up|fin>>>.
    Applying the iterated discount between any<nbsp><math|l>
    and<nbsp><math|m> proves the interval bound.<label|proof:J-signed-end>
  </proof>

  The next estimate recovers the output-degree power after passing to the
  quotient. Fix any norm on<nbsp><math|F<rsub|t>>. Put
  <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>=\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>|)>>,
  where <math|x<rsub|m><around|(|v|)>=x<rsub|N,p,m;t><around|(|v|)>> is the
  transferred coefficient of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D08>. A
  refinement below is a nontrivial refinement of the ordered cutoff
  limits<nbsp><eqref|eq:E2-tails>; it may depend on the cluster geometry.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Graded quotient bound><label|lem:J-graded-quotient> For
    <math|0\<leqslant\>i\<leqslant\>t> there are
    positive<nbsp><math|C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>>, independent of the
    refinement and label family, with <math|log
    C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>0>. If
    labels<nbsp><math|v<rsub|N,p>\<in\>V<rsub|t>> converge
    in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|t>> to<nbsp><math|v> on such a refinement and
    eventually <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>>>,
    then

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-graded-input><math-up|dist><rsub|F<rsub|t>><around|(|<around|[|v|]>,\<jmath\><rsub|i-1,t>*F<rsub|i-1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>*a<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|t-i|)>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Here <math|<around|[|v|]>\<in\>F<rsub|t>> is the class of<nbsp><math|v>,
    <math|F<rsub|-1>=0>, and <math|m> is sufficiently large.
  </lemma>

  A label that is small at level<nbsp><math|m> is therefore, in the quotient
  and up to subpower factors, within <math|a<rsub|m><rsup|-<around|(|t-i|)>>>
  times its size of the fields of degree below<nbsp><math|i>.

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-graded-start>The triangularity of the leading map proved
    in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> separates high from low
    engineering degrees; the marked bounds of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>
    control the error; the physical quotient of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D10>, with the corrected inclusions of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>, identifies the removed low-degree part
    with a lower field. In the notation of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-recall>,
    the triangularity is

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|M<rsub|l>|)><rsub|\<alpha\>*\<beta\>>=0*<space|1em><around|(|\<alpha\>\<ne\>\<beta\>,deg
      \<alpha\>\<leqslant\>deg \<beta\>|)>.
    </equation*>

    The diagonal entries are the nonzero fine-to-coarse normalization
    factors. The full coefficient map differs from this leading map by the
    marked error. Write<nbsp><math|P> for coordinate projection onto
    engineering degrees at most<nbsp><math|i-1>, first
    assuming<nbsp><math|i\<gtr\>0>. The leading map<nbsp><math|M<rsub|l>> and
    its inverse preserve<nbsp><math|ker P>: triangularity gives preservation
    by<nbsp><math|M<rsub|l>>, and injectivity on this finite-dimensional
    subspace gives preservation by its inverse. Consequently
    <math|\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x|)>\<leqslant\>K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*M<rsub|l>*x|)>>.
    The degree rescaling expands<nbsp><math|P*x> by at
    least<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i+1>>, whereas it
    expands<nbsp><math|x-P*x> by at most<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i>>.
    The marked error is bounded by <math|\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsup|\<circ\>>|)>>.

    The two resulting estimates are

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l+1>|)>>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i+1>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l>|)>,>>>>>>>|<row|<cell|P*x<rsub|l+1>=0>|<cell|\<Longrightarrow\>\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    To obtain the first line, put <math|Y=\<mu\><around|(|P<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x<rsub|l+1>|)>>.
    The low-cone hypothesis <math|\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l+1>|)>>
    and <math|C<rsub|l+1>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l>> give
    <math|\<mu\><around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>2*Y>.
    Applying the inverse leading map to the low coordinates and using
    <math|2*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>*\<epsilon\><rsub|l>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>1>
    gives <math|Y\<leqslant\>2*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>*\<mu\>*<around|(|P*x<rsub|l>|)>>.
    The full coefficient is at most <math|3*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>*Y>, so
    <math|64*K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l>,C<rsub|l+1>>
    gives both conclusions. In the second line use
    <math|\<mu\><around|(|<math-ss|d><rsub|l>x<rsub|l+1>|)>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t-i>*\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l+1>|)>>
    and <math|K<rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>+1\<leqslant\>C<rsub|l+1>>. These numerical
    inequalities follow from<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-stage-room>.

    At the bare level take the plane<nbsp><math|P*V<rsub|t>>. Its transported
    image remains in the low cone by the first line. The map from this plane
    to its low coordinates at any level is injective by its expansion bound,
    hence onto<nbsp><math|P*V<rsub|t>>. Subtracting a transported vector with
    the same low coordinates at level<nbsp><math|m> therefore leaves zero low
    coordinates and increases the full size by at
    most<nbsp><math|1+C<rsub|m>>. Repeat the subtraction at every next
    coarser level, applying the second line between subtractions. This gives
    a vector<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>> in the transported bare low plane with

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|N,p>-y<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>G<rsub|m>*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|<reflevel|t>>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|t-i>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>>,<space|2em>G<rsub|m>=<big|prod><rsub|l\<less\>m><around|(|1+C<rsub|l>|)>*C<rsub|l+1><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Allowing a fixed norm-equivalence factor, put
    <math|\<cal-J\><rsub|m>=<big|prod><rsub|l\<less\>m>C<rsub|l+1>>. The
    first-line expansion, iterated from the bare level, shows that bounded
    vectors<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>> in this plane have bare coefficients
    bounded by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|t-i+1>*e<rsup|o<around|(|s<rsub|N>|)>>>.
    Multiplying the same polynomial by <math|a<rsub|N><rsup|i-1>> instead
    of<nbsp><math|a<rsub|N><rsup|t>> therefore gives a
    degree-<math|<around|(|i-1|)>> local observable with subpower healthy
    derivative and all-configuration supremum bounds. Its stencil is
    unchanged. To match this observable to the selected lower-degree
    insertions, apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-local-comparison>(i): it
    gives tame degree-<math|<around|(|i-1|)>>
    coefficients<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p>> whose insertions differ from these
    observables by a negligible error (Definition<nbsp><reference|def:E2-small>).
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b> removes that error in every separated
    spectator test.

    To pass to a physical lower field, apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F-represent> in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|i-1>> to the
    family<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p>>, with the equation projections
    <math|\<Pi\><rsub|N,p>=\<Pi\><rsub|i-1,N,p>> of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.6>, taken along
    <math|N=<math-ss|n><around|(|j|)>> and <math|p=P<around|(|k|)>>, their
    limit<nbsp><math|\<Pi\><rsub|i-1>> from<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-projections>,
    and the one-slot spectator functionals, whose kernels intersect
    in<nbsp><math|ker \<Pi\><rsub|i-1>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-dimension>. The
    normalized equation remainder <math|<around|(|c<rsub|N,p>-\<Pi\><rsub|N,p>*c<rsub|N,p>|)>/<around|(|1+<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsub|N,p>*c<rsub|N,p>|\<\|\|\>>|)>>
    is tame, being a bounded multiple of<nbsp><math|c<rsub|N,p>> minus a
    family of norm at most one; Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.7>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:F1b> make its separated spectator tests tend to
    zero. If<nbsp><math|y> is a limit of the bounded
    labels<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>>, the lemma gives a label
    in<nbsp><math|V<rsub|i-1>> with exactly the limiting correlations
    of<nbsp><math|y>. The corrected inclusions of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> and the all-spectator definition of the
    null space now give <math|<around|[|y|]>\<in\>\<jmath\><rsub|i-1,t>*F<rsub|i-1>>.
    Boundedness of<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>> follows from the displayed
    distance bound and convergence of<nbsp><math|v<rsub|N,p>>. If these
    bounds hold only on the chosen refinement, set<nbsp><math|y<rsub|N,p>=0>
    off the set where they hold; this extends the bounded plane family to the
    original tails without changing its limit. Passing to this limit proves
    the distance estimate.

    For<nbsp><math|i=0> no plane is removed. Iterating the reverse
    coefficient estimate of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> gives
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|v|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<cal-J\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|<reflevel|t>>/a<rsub|m>|)><rsup|t>*\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>>>
    and hence the same bound in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|t>>. Finally <math|log
    \<cal-J\><rsub|m>=o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>>, since <math|log
    C<rsup|<math-up|col>><rsub|J<rsub|m+1>>\<leqslant\>J<rsub|m+1><rsup|-2>*log
    \<ell\><rsub|m>> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>) and by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>. Since <math|C<rsub|l>\<geqslant\>2>,
    <math|G<rsub|m>*C<rsub|m>\<leqslant\><around|(|\<cal-J\><rsub|m>|)><rsup|3>*C<rsub|0>>.
    Thus <math|log G<rsub|m>=o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>> as well. Fixed
    reference-spacing and quotient-norm factors are absorbed
    in<nbsp><math|C<rsub|t,i><around|(|m|)>>. No estimate depends on the
    refinement used to take the limits.<label|proof:J-graded-end>
  </proof>

  <subsection|Products and nested clusters><label|sec:partII-J.1>

  A probe word is a fixed finite list of fields at distinct nonzero points.
  Correlating a field at the origin against such a word gives a linear
  functional on<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>. The intersection of the kernels of all
  these functionals is zero, by the definition of<nbsp><math|<nullspace|j>>.
  Finite dimensionality therefore permits a finite separating collection.

  Choose the probe functionals recursively. Given biorthogonal
  fields<nbsp><math|u<rsub|\<alpha\>>> and probe
  functionals<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> below degree<nbsp><math|j>,
  continue each<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> to<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>
  by the same correlation formula; inclusions preserve that formula by
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. The retraction

  <\equation*>
    <math-ss|t><rsub|j>A=<big|sum><rsub|deg
    \<alpha\>\<less\>j>\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|A|)>*\<jmath\><rsub|deg
    \<alpha\>,j-1>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|t><rsub|j>\<jmath\><rsub|j-1,j>=1
  </equation*>

  allows a section<nbsp><math|s> of the new quotient to be replaced by
  <math|s-\<jmath\><math-ss|t><rsub|j>s>. Lift a quotient basis with this
  section and realize its dual by finite combinations of probe functionals,
  which span<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j><rsup|\<ast\>>>. The enlarged system is
  biorthogonal. In particular<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>>> vanishes
  on<nbsp><math|\<jmath\>*F<rsub|deg \<alpha\>-1>>. For <math|j=0> take
  <math|u<rsub|0>=\<b-1\>>. Let<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\><rsub|j>> be the union of
  the finite probe sets used through degree<nbsp><math|j>, with
  index<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>>. Write <math|\<ell\><rsub|\<alpha\>><around|(|A|)>=<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>>m<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<gamma\>>*S*<around|(|A<around|(|0|)>,B<rsub|j,\<gamma\>>|)>>
  in this finite collection.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Finite correlation projection><label|def:D31> Define the linear
    reconstruction map <math|D<rsub|j>:<C><rsup|\<cal-P\><rsub|j>>\<to\>F<rsub|j>>
    by

    <\equation*>
      D<rsub|j><around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|deg
      \<alpha\>\<leqslant\>j><around*|(|<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|j>>m<rsub|\<alpha\>*\<gamma\>>*y<rsub|\<gamma\>>|)>*\<jmath\><rsub|deg
      \<alpha\>,j>*u<rsub|\<alpha\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    With <math|<math-up|ev><rsub|j><around|(|A|)>=<around|(|S*<around|(|A<around|(|0|)>,B<rsub|j,\<gamma\>>|)>|)><rsub|\<gamma\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|j>>>,
    it satisfies <math|D<rsub|j><math-up|ev><rsub|j>=<math-up|id><rsub|F<rsub|j>>>:
    it is a fixed left inverse of field evaluation. For a word<nbsp><math|w>
    separated from these probes, set

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|raw>>*w=D<rsub|j><around*|(|<around|(|S*<around|(|w,B<rsub|j,\<gamma\>>|)>|)><rsub|\<gamma\>\<in\>\<cal-P\><rsub|j>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    This extracts a field from finitely many correlations using the fixed
    biorthogonal coordinates.
  </definition>

  Here and below a word is a finite product of labelled insertions; sums of
  words are read linearly. Rotations act on every position and label, and
  conjugation acts on labels and scalar coefficients. Translation of a word
  by<nbsp><math|u> is denoted by<nbsp><math|\<tau\><rsub|u>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Symmetric projection><label|def:D32> When every rotation
    of<nbsp><math|w> is separated from every probe
    in<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\><rsub|j>>, set

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|j>*w=<big|int><rsub|O<around|(|4|)>>R<rsub|j><around|(|h|)>*P<rsub|j><rsup|<math-up|raw>>*<around|(|h<rsup|-1>*w|)>*<space|0.17em>d*h,<space|2em>B<rsub|j>*w=<tfrac|1|2><around*|(|A<rsub|j>*w+<wide|A<rsub|j>*<wide|w|\<bar\>>|\<bar\>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The Haar measure has mass one. Outside this domain set both maps to zero.
    At center<nbsp><math|o>, apply these formulas
    to<nbsp><math|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w>. These averages impose rotation
    covariance and reality.
  </definition>

  Let<nbsp><math|I<rsub|q>*A=A<around|(|0|)>>
  for<nbsp><math|A\<in\>F<rsub|q>>. The inclusions<nbsp><math|\<jmath\><rsub|i,j>>
  identify the same field in different degrees.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|OPE coefficients><label|def:D33> Put
    <math|L<rsub|i,q>=B<rsub|i>*I<rsub|q>> and define

    <\equation*>
      Q<rsub|0>=1,<space|2em>Q<rsub|j>=1-<big|sum><rsub|i\<less\>j>\<jmath\><rsub|i,j>*Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>,<space|2em>E<rsub|j>=<big|cap><rsub|i\<less\>j>ker
      <around|(|Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    For the chosen real basis<nbsp><math|<around|(|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>|)><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>>>
    of<nbsp><math|E<rsub|j>>, let<nbsp><math|\<omega\><rsub|j,\<lambda\>>:E<rsub|j>\<to\><C>>
    be its coordinate functionals and set

    <\equation*>
      c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x,o|)>=\<omega\><rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<space|-0.17em><around*|(|Q<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j><space|-0.17em><around*|[|<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i>*<around|(|x<rsub|i>-o|)>|]>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The recursion removes lower degrees before reading coordinates and is
    independent of arity and truncation.
  </definition>

  In the first two degrees the recursion reads as follows. In degree zero,
  <math|Q<rsub|0>=1>: the degree-zero probes read the multiple of the unit
  line seen by the cluster. In degree one,
  <math|L<rsub|0,1>=B<rsub|0>*I<rsub|1>> reads the degree-zero part of a
  single degree-one field, and

  <\equation*>
    Q<rsub|1>*B<rsub|1>*w=B<rsub|1>*w-\<jmath\><rsub|0,1>*B<rsub|0><around*|[|<around|(|B<rsub|1>*w|)><around|(|0|)>|]>.
  </equation*>

  This is the field read in degree one, minus the multiple of the unit that
  the degree-zero probes see in it. The degree-one coefficients are the
  coordinates of this difference in <math|E<rsub|1>=ker L<rsub|0,1>>.

  The following splitting makes the coefficients a single choice for all
  later estimates.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|One channel prescription><label|lem:J1.1> The real
    fields<nbsp><math|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>> form complex bases of
    conjugation-invariant complements:

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|j>=\<jmath\><rsub|j-1,j>*F<rsub|j-1>\<oplus\>E<rsub|j>,<space|2em>E<rsub|j>=<math-up|span><rsub|<C>><around|{|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>:\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>|}>,<space|2em>F<rsub|-1>=0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The degree-zero vector spans the unit line and need not equal the unit.
  </lemma>

  By Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D33>, all coefficients are fixed before
  spectators, states and truncations.

  <\proof>
    The biorthogonal construction preceding
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D31> gives a reconstruction formula fixing
    every field of<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>; the field calculus of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2> makes the old functionals compatible with
    the inclusions. Continuing an old functional means using the same finite
    correlations, since <math|\<iota\><rsub|j>*\<jmath\><rsub|q,j>=\<iota\><rsub|q>>;
    no extension of a functional is chosen arbitrarily.

    For<nbsp><math|i\<geqslant\>q>, this reconstruction and the rotation and
    reality laws give<nbsp><math|L<rsub|i,q>=\<jmath\><rsub|q,i>>. Also
    <math|L<rsub|i,j>*\<jmath\><rsub|q,j>=L<rsub|i,q>>
    for<nbsp><math|i\<less\>q\<leqslant\>j>: both sides evaluate the same
    single-field word before averaging. For all<nbsp><math|q\<leqslant\>j>,
    induction in<nbsp><math|j> now gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-cuts>Q<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j,q>=0*<around|(|q\<less\>j|)>,<space|2em><big|sum><rsub|i\<leqslant\>j>\<jmath\><rsub|i,j>*Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,q>=\<jmath\><rsub|q,j><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For<nbsp><math|q=j> the second identity is the recursion;
    for<nbsp><math|q\<less\>j>, compose that recursion
    with<nbsp><math|\<jmath\><rsub|q,j>> and use the compatibility just
    proved and the induction hypothesis. In the same induction assume
    <math|Q<rsub|k>*F<rsub|k>\<subset\>E<rsub|k>> and <math|Q<rsub|k>=1>
    on<nbsp><math|E<rsub|k>> for<nbsp><math|k\<less\>j>.
    Applying<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>> to<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>*A>
    then cancels its matching<nbsp><math|i> term; terms
    with<nbsp><math|k\<less\>i> vanish because <math|Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>>
    vanishes on <math|\<jmath\><rsub|i-1,j>*F<rsub|i-1>>, and terms
    with<nbsp><math|i\<less\>k\<less\>j> vanish
    because<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|k>*A\<in\>E<rsub|k>>.
    Thus<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>*F<rsub|j>\<subset\>E<rsub|j>>.
    On<nbsp><math|E<rsub|j>>, the defining sum for<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>> is
    zero, so<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>=1>. Its kernel is
    exactly<nbsp><math|\<jmath\>*F<rsub|j-1>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-cuts>. Haar
    invariance and simultaneous input and output conjugation show that every
    map <math|Q<rsub|i>*L<rsub|i,j>> intertwines rotations and conjugation.
    Thus<nbsp><math|E<rsub|j>> is invariant under both. Its fixed real
    subspace spans it, since <math|v=<around|(|v+<wide|v|\<bar\>>|)>/2+i*<around|(|v-<wide|v|\<bar\>>|)>/<around|(|2*i|)>>;
    a real basis is also a complex basis. This proves the stated splitting
    and the simultaneous choice.
  </proof>

  For <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> write <math|f\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|b*T>> when,
  for every<nbsp><math|\<tau\>\<gtr\>0>, there is<nbsp><math|T<rsub|0>> such
  that for every<nbsp><math|T\<geqslant\>T<rsub|0>> an ordered cutoff tail
  gives <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>e<rsup|<around|(|b+\<tau\>|)>*T>>,
  uniformly in the cutoff and volume on that tail. Products add exponents and
  finite sums take their maximum. Factors of exponent zero are called
  subpower. A level<nbsp><math|k> has height<nbsp><math|b>
  when<nbsp><math|s<rsub|k>/T\<to\>b>; thus
  <math|a<rsub|k>=e<rsup|-b*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>.
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> supplies these levels by taking the first
  with <math|s<rsub|k>\<geqslant\>b*T>, or
  <math|s<rsub|k>\<geqslant\><sqrt|T>> if<nbsp><math|b=0>.

  Insertions are carried through the integration by nilpotent external
  fields. This is the device used for observables by Bauerschmidt, Brydges
  and Slade, who add an external observable field to the interaction and
  truncate the algebra in it<nbsp><cite-detail|BauerschmidtBrydgesSlade2015|Section<nbsp>2.3>.
  A marker is one group of insertions in the reflected cells not yet fused
  with another; it is live while its weight is positive. To each
  marker<nbsp><math|\<iota\>> attach a commuting variable
  <math|z<rsub|\<iota\>>> with <math|z<rsub|\<iota\>><rsup|2>=0> and a mark
  weight<nbsp><math|w<rsub|\<iota\>>\<gtr\>0>. In this finite scalar algebra
  put

  <\equation*>
    c=<big|sum><rsub|A>c<rsub|A>*z<rsub|A>,<space|2em>z<rsub|A>=<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>\<in\>A>z<rsub|\<iota\>>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|mark>>=<big|sum><rsub|A><around|\||c<rsub|A>|\|>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>\<in\>A>w<rsub|\<iota\>>,<space|2em>c<rsub|A>\<in\><C><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Products of monomials with overlapping markers vanish, and the weights of
  disjoint markers multiply, so <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|c*d|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|mark>>\<leqslant\><around|\<\|\|\>|c|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|mark>><around|\<\|\|\>|d|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|mark>>>.
  The coefficient of<nbsp><math|z<rsub|A>> in
  <math|\<rho\>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>><around|(|1+z<rsub|\<iota\>>*O<rsub|\<iota\>>|)>>
  is <math|\<rho\>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>\<in\>A>O<rsub|\<iota\>>>; by
  linearity, taking this coefficient commutes with integration of integrable
  terms.

  If <math|F<rsub|X,A>> is a coefficient with support<nbsp><math|X> and
  marker set<nbsp><math|A>, its weighted majorant is

  <\equation*>
    <big|sum><rsub|X,A>e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|X|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|X,A>|\<\|\|\>>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>\<in\>A>w<rsub|\<iota\>><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|X,A>|\<\|\|\>>> is the derivative or
  forced majorant of Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>;
  at a support ball use its anchored sum. The factors
  <math|e<rsup|\<sigma\><cover><around|(|X|)>><around|\<\|\|\>|F<rsub|X,A>|\<\|\|\>>>
  enter as<nbsp><math|v<rsub|a>> in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected>.
  Only the marked norm is used submultiplicatively. A grouped coefficient
  carries the product of its live mark weights, denoted
  by<nbsp><math|\<omega\>>.

  For the marked expansion put

  <\equation*>
    \<delta\><rsub|l><rsup|<math-up|mark>>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l+1><rsup|-2>,<space|2em>\<phi\><rsub|l>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l+1><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<Xi\><rsub|l>=exp
    <sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l+1>>,<space|2em>y<rsub|t,l>=<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|l><rsup|-\<nu\><rsub|t>>.
  </equation*>

  The fixed integer<nbsp><math|\<nu\><rsub|t>> bounds the local
  degree-<math|t> polynomial losses, and <math|y<rsub|t,l>*C<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>1>
  at entry. The four factors pay respectively for support enlargement,
  regular transport, Taylor extraction and insertion normalization. Their
  logarithms and<nbsp><math|log C<rsub|l>> are
  <math|o<around|(|s<rsub|l>|)>>. For an initial
  weight<nbsp><math|\<varpi\>\<gtr\>0> at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> define
  its later weights by

  <\equation*>
    \<varpi\><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=\<varpi\>,<space|2em>\<varpi\><rsub|l+1>=\<phi\><rsub|l>*\<varpi\><rsub|l>*<space|1em><around|(|l\<geqslant\>k<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The marked entry choices require <math|\<phi\><rsub|l>*\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t+1>*y<rsub|t,l>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|t,l+1>>
  for <math|l\<geqslant\><reflevel|t>>. This follows the zero-jet regular
  remainder through the integration.

  Let <math|B<rsub|l>> be the support radius and <math|q<rsub|l>> the
  per-site logarithmic bound<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-vacuum-majorant> of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants>. Fix a reflected cell at
  level<nbsp><math|k> and choose

  <\equation*>
    k<rsub|\<ast\>>=min <around|{|l\<geqslant\>k:B<rsub|l><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|l>|}>,<space|2em>N<rsub|k>=exp
    <around|{|<around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>+5|}>.
  </equation*>

  These quantities ensure that the marked stencils fit in the cell and bound
  its density normalization.

  For a grouped coefficient at level<nbsp><math|m+1> of
  weight<nbsp><math|\<omega\>\<gtr\>0>, put

  <\equation*>
    q<rsup|<math-up|ext>><rsub|m>=4*\<Xi\><rsub|m>*a<rsub|m+1><rsup|t>/\<omega\>,<space|2em>\<frak-E\><rsub|m>=8*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*q<rsup|<math-up|ext>><rsub|m><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  The terminal weight<nbsp><math|\<varpi\>> must satisfy

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-marked-weights>

    <\aligned>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<varpi\>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|t+1>*y<rsub|t,m>/y<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>\<delta\><rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|mark>>*\<omega\>/<around|(|16*\<Xi\><rsub|m>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|32*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>*\<varpi\>*<space|0.17em>a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|-t>*\<frak-E\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|1/8>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>y<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>/2.>>>>
    </aligned>
  </equation>

  These conditions pay for extraction at<nbsp><math|m> and for the
  transported residual, respectively.

  Informally, after blocking down to level<nbsp><math|m>, a group of
  insertions of total weight<nbsp><math|\<omega\>> is replaced by one
  degree-<math|t> insertion at the origin, the matched field. The next lemma
  bounds its coefficient by<nbsp><math|2*\<frak-E\><rsub|m>>, and the
  chessboard seminorm of the replacement error in a level-<math|k> cell
  by<nbsp><math|2*N<rsub|k>/\<varpi\>>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Marked extraction and insertion costs><label|lem:J-marked-costs> A
    grouped coefficient of weight<nbsp><math|\<omega\>> has a degree-<math|t>
    local extraction with coefficient and remainder each at
    most<nbsp><math|q<rsup|<math-up|ext>><rsub|m>>. With
    <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> as above, sufficiently late, and
    whenever<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-weights> holds, the matched field has
    coefficient size at most<nbsp><math|2*\<frak-E\><rsub|m>>. Its interior
    reflected remainder in the level-<math|k> cell has chessboard seminorm at
    most<nbsp><math|2*N<rsub|k>/\<varpi\>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-costs-start>The extraction is a Taylor expansion in the
    norms of Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D05a>\U<reference|def:D05b>
    and<nbsp><reference|def:D08>; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-local>
    transports its residual to<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>; the majorants of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>, summed by the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> in the marked norm, bound the cell
    integral. Divide a grouped coefficient by its available
    weight<nbsp><math|\<omega\>>. Extract its radial polynomial
    through<nbsp><math|t> and write the regular complement by the integral
    Taylor formula. The finite derivative sums, coefficient conversion and
    remainder constants are at most<nbsp><math|\<Xi\><rsub|m>> after the
    entry levels have been chosen: their logarithms are bounded by stage
    constants and powers of<nbsp><math|log <wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>,
    whereas <math|log \<Xi\><rsub|m>=<sqrt|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m+1>>>.
    Multiplication by<nbsp><math|a<rsub|m+1><rsup|t>>
    gives<nbsp><math|q<rsup|<math-up|ext>><rsub|m>>. The factor four bounds
    the polynomial and complement separately. Forced terms keep their
    original majorants and the forced normalization
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-mass>.

    The combined cost is at most<nbsp><math|2*q<rsup|<math-up|ext>><rsub|m>>.
    Subtract the column matching the transported family
    at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-local>
    gives, for <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>l\<leqslant\>m>,

    <\equation*>
      \<frak-r\><rsub|l>+\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|l>|)>\<leqslant\>\<frak-E\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|1/8>,<space|2em>\<mu\><around|(|x<rsub|m><rsup|<math-up|field>>|)>\<leqslant\>2*\<frak-E\><rsub|m>.
    </equation*>

    The factor <math|4*<around|(|1+C<rsub|m>|)>>
    multiplying<nbsp><math|2*q<rsup|<math-up|ext>><rsub|m>> includes the
    finest residual and coefficient together. The entry requirement
    <math|\<phi\><rsub|l>*\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|t+1>*y<rsub|t,l>\<leqslant\>y<rsub|t,l+1>>
    gives the regular zero-jet gain: iterating
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|l+1>=\<phi\><rsub|l>*\<varpi\><rsub|l>> yields
    <math|\<varpi\><rsub|m>\<leqslant\>\<varpi\>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|t+1>*y<rsub|t,m>/y<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>.
    The first condition of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-weights> permits
    extraction at<nbsp><math|m>. For the second, the level-<math|l> ratio of
    required to permitted residual weight is bounded by its
    level-<math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> value times
    <math|<around|(|a<rsub|l>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|7/8>\<leqslant\>1>:
    use <math|\<varpi\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<varpi\>*<around|(|a<rsub|l>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|t+1>*y<rsub|t,l>/y<rsub|t,k<rsub|\<ast\>>>>,
    the residual bound <math|\<frak-E\><rsub|m>*<around|(|a<rsub|m>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|1/8>>
    and divide by <math|y<rsub|t,l>>. Thus the condition
    at<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> permits the signed residual and
    compensating columns throughout the interval. Their density identities
    are exact by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-local>.

    In the nilpotent variables defined above, a marked polynomial is measured
    by the sum of the absolute coefficients times the product of their
    assigned weights. Consequently the coefficient with one residual per cell
    is at most<nbsp><math|\<varpi\><rsup|-1>> per cell times that sum.
    Summing the unmarked majorants and dividing by the two positive density
    normalizations bounds the integral per cell by<nbsp><math|N<rsub|k>>. The
    singleton, forced-profile, connected regular and eligible forced sums
    contribute at most <math|2,1,1,1> per cell, giving the additive constant
    five. Apply this to the original local difference and the correction
    representing its column, and use the triangle inequality for the
    chessboard seminorm, obtained by expanding the reflected product and
    applying<nbsp><eqref|eq:F-chess> to each term. This
    gives<nbsp><math|2*N<rsub|k>/\<varpi\>>.

    To verify that<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> has the required height, let
    <math|\<frak-n\><rsub|l>=max <around|(|1,q<rsub|l>|)>> bound the
    normalization cost. The entry choices satisfy

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-entrance>B<rsub|l+1><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>2*B<rsub|l><rsup|2>,<space|2em><around|(|\<ell\><rsub|l>*B<rsub|l><rsup|2>|)><rsup|4>*\<frak-n\><rsub|l+1>=o<around|(|s<rsub|l+1>|)>.
    </equation>

    The level<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> exists since
    <math|a<rsub|l>*B<rsub|l><rsup|2>> decreases by at least<nbsp><math|2/3>
    at each step. If <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>=k>, the spacing ratio is one.
    Otherwise <math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>=l+1>, and minimality gives
    <math|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l>*B<rsub|l><rsup|2>>.
    Therefore <math|<around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|4>*q<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>.
    Also <math|0\<leqslant\>s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>-s<rsub|k>=log
    <around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|a<rsub|k>/a<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)><rsup|4>*\<frak-n\><rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>.
    This proves <math|s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>/s<rsub|k>\<to\>1> and <math|log
    N<rsub|k>=o<around|(|s<rsub|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>|)>>. At height zero it still
    gives <math|log N<rsub|k>=o<around|(|T|)>>. Finally<nbsp><math|log
    \<Xi\><rsub|m>=o<around|(|s<rsub|m>|)>> follows from
    <math|<wide|\<beta\>|^><rsub|m>=<wide|\<beta\>|\<bar\>>+\<kappa\><rsub|G>*s<rsub|m>>
    and <math|s<rsub|m+1>/s<rsub|m>\<to\>1>. The other displayed functions
    are fixed powers of<nbsp><math|<wide|\<beta\>|^>> or the subpower column
    cost of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1>.<label|proof:J-costs-end>
  </proof>

  Fix nonempty lattice labels<nbsp><math|v<rsub|\<iota\>>\<in\>V<rsub|d<rsub|\<iota\>>>>,
  let<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>> be their number and
  put<nbsp><math|W=<big|sum><rsub|\<iota\>>d<rsub|\<iota\>>>. Choose
  levels<nbsp><math|k,m,j> of heights <math|0\<leqslant\>\<theta\><rsub|->\<less\>\<theta\>\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|+>>.
  The input points lie on the spacing-<math|a<rsub|j>> grid, in the centered
  level-<math|<around|(|m+1|)>> block <math|<around|[|-a<rsub|m+1>/2,a<rsub|m+1>/2|)><rsup|4>>,
  and their separation is at least<nbsp><math|B<rsub|j><rsup|2>*a<rsub|j>>.
  The configurations belong to<nbsp><math|<Gen|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>>, and
  all stencils are interior to the level-<math|k> cell. These conditions hold
  eventually for clusters of extent <math|e<rsup|-x*T+o<around|(|T|)>>> and
  reciprocal separation at most <math|e<rsup|x*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>, where
  <math|\<theta\>\<less\>x\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|+>>. The
  chosen<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>> has height<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|->>
  by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-entrance>.

  Two contributions make up the exponential cost of bringing the cluster to
  level<nbsp><math|m>. The initial field normalization contributes
  <math|W*\<theta\><rsub|+>>. Transporting at most
  <math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>> groups from
  height<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|+>> to height<nbsp><math|\<theta\>>, at
  four powers of the scale ratio per group, contributes
  <math|4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<theta\>|)>>. We
  therefore set

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Z\>=W*\<theta\><rsub|+>+4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<theta\>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The support enlargements and the finitely many fusions contribute only
  subpower factors. Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:J-scales> shows the three
  scales and these costs.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-11.svg|422.390217pt|174.094538pt||><label|fig:J-scales>>|The
  three scales of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-lattice>. Markers start at the
  insertion resolution<nbsp><math|a<rsub|j>>, are grouped when they cease to
  be <math|B<rsub|l><rsup|2>>-separated, and reach level<nbsp><math|m> as one
  group, where one degree-<math|t> field is extracted; the signed residual is
  matched backward to<nbsp><math|k<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Right: losses and gains up
  to subpower factors, at fixed truncation (no series in<nbsp><math|D>).>

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Lattice cluster replacement><label|lem:J-lattice> For every output
    degree<nbsp><math|t>, the preceding configurations admit
    labels<nbsp><math|v<rsub|N,p><rsup|<math-up|out>>\<in\>V<rsub|t>> on an
    ordered cutoff tail such that

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|\<\|\|\>|v<rsub|N,p><rsup|<math-up|out>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|m>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|\<cal-Z\>-t*\<theta\>|)>*T>,>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>>O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|\<iota\>>,x<rsub|\<iota\>>|)>-O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|N,p><rsup|<math-up|out>>,0|)>|\|><rsub|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|tile>><rsub|k><around|(|0|)>>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|[|\<cal-Z\>-<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>|]>*T>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The difference is interior. The bounds are uniform in cutoff and volume
    on that tail.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-lattice-start>The insertions are expanded in the marked
    format of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D26> and
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>\U<reference|lem:E2.2a>;
    Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-local>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:J-marked-costs> supply the extraction once the
    markers form one group, and the steps below bound the mark weight lost
    before that. In the nilpotent variables defined above, the coefficient of
    their product in <math|<density><space|0.17em>\<rho\><rsub|N>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>><around|(|1+z<rsub|\<iota\>>*O<rsub|N,p><around|(|v<rsub|\<iota\>>,x<rsub|\<iota\>>|)>|)>>
    is the product insertion times the density. Until level<nbsp><math|j> the
    stencils are disjoint, so their marked integrations are separate.

    Moving a marker to its representative through at most<nbsp><math|h> cover
    balls gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-support-move><cover><around|(|X\<cup\><around|{|y<rsub|\<iota\>>:\<iota\>\<in\>A|}>|)>\<leqslant\><cover><around|(|X|)>+h<around|\||A|\|>.
    </equation>

    If at most<nbsp><math|\<frak-N\>> representatives occupy a ball,
    shrinking each affected weight by <math|\<eta\>*e<rsup|-\<sigma\>*h>>
    gives the original term times<nbsp><math|\<eta\><rsup|<around|\||A|\|>>>.
    In the anchored sum, terms incident on the old ball use its original
    majorant; any new incidence can be assigned to an old marker on the
    joining path. There are at most<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>> old
    markers for each new representative and<nbsp><math|\<frak-N\>>
    representatives. Taking <math|\<eta\>\<leqslant\><around|[|<around|(|1+\<frak-N\>|)>*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>|]><rsup|-1>>
    pays for these assignments. Unmarked terms keep their supports. Hence the
    enlarged weighted series is bounded by the original majorant series of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D1>.

    Keep a maximal <math|B<rsub|l><rsup|2>>-separated set of representatives
    at each level, retaining the distinguished one at the origin, and attach
    each discarded point to a retained neighbor. A fusion decreases the
    number of groups, so there are at most<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>-1>
    fusions. The joining-cover bound can be taken as
    <math|h<rsub|l>=16*<around|[|1+\<ell\><rsub|l>*<around|(|B<rsub|l>+B<rsub|l+1>+1|)><rsup|2>|]><rsup|4>>.
    The entry requirements of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:constants> and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E1.1> give <math|<around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|l+1>>+1|)>*h<rsub|l>=o<around|(|s<rsub|l>|)>>
    and subpower occupancy. Choose one<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>\<leqslant\>1>
    below all the resulting enlargement allowances
    for<nbsp><math|m\<leqslant\>l\<less\>j>. It is subpower: the logarithm of
    the running minimum is bounded by the maximum logarithmic cost; split
    this maximum into a fixed initial segment and a tail bounded
    by<nbsp><math|\<tau\>*s<rsub|j>>, then send<nbsp><math|\<tau\>> to zero.
    The factor<nbsp><math|<around|[|<around|(|1+\<frak-N\>|)>*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>|]><rsup|-1>>
    above is included in this allowance<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>/8>.

    Between fusions the one-mark integration costs at
    most<nbsp><math|6*\<ell\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|4>> in
    each weight. To derive this bound, let<nbsp><math|\<frak-m\>> be an
    incoming marked majorant. Apply the rooted clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:D-connected> coefficientwise in the
    submultiplicative marked norm, with the marked factor fixed. Separate the
    transported regular terms into the zero-jet local integration remainder,
    of total weighted mass<nbsp><math|p<rsub|l>>, and the incoming regular
    contribution carried without Taylor subtraction.

    Use the support conversion factor<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>> defined
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-support-conversion>. For a fine support<nbsp><math|X>
    of the unsubtracted incoming contribution, let<nbsp><math|Y> be its
    coarse support, including the incident windows. The support conversion
    estimate there gives

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|l>>+2|)><cover><rsub|B<rsub|l>><around|(|Y|)>\<leqslant\>log
      \<cal-A\><rsub|l>+<tfrac|1|2>\<sigma\><rsub|J<rsub|l+1>><cover><rsub|B<rsub|l+1>><around|(|X|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Exponentiating bounds this incoming mass
    by<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>*\<frak-m\>>; the remaining half of the
    fine exponent is nonnegative. Item<nbsp><reference|it:E-graph> of
    Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-E.1>
    imposes<nbsp><math|\<cal-A\><rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l>>.

    The combined regular mass is thus at most<nbsp><math|p<rsub|l>+\<cal-A\><rsub|l>*\<frak-m\>>.
    Splitting a complex marked term into its real and imaginary parts costs
    at most twice its majorant. For the forced terms,
    let<nbsp><math|f<rsub|l>> denote their total contribution after this
    splitting and the normalization in<nbsp><eqref|eq:E-marked-mass>; their
    eligibility tests remain attached. Use the termwise bounds in the proof
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E-marked-step> for the zero-jet local
    remainder: its regular part retains the Taylor gain, and its forced part
    retains half the forced suppression. The unsubtracted incoming
    contribution is bounded by the support conversion above; the
    <math|\<eta\>*r> graph bound in that proof is not used for this
    contribution. Raising the entry coupling
    gives<nbsp><math|2*p<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<frak-m\>>
    and<nbsp><math|f<rsub|l>\<leqslant\>\<frak-m\>>. Adding the two
    contributions gives

    <\equation*>
      2*<around|(|p<rsub|l>+\<cal-A\><rsub|l>*\<frak-m\>|)>+f<rsub|l>\<leqslant\><around|(|2*\<ell\><rsub|l>+2|)>*\<frak-m\>\<leqslant\>6*\<ell\><rsub|l>*\<frak-m\>.
    </equation*>

    With<nbsp><math|g> groups remaining, their product weight at
    level<nbsp><math|l> is consequently at least

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-marker-weights><around|(|a<rsub|j>/a<rsub|l>|)><rsup|4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>*<around|(|\<alpha\><rsub|j>/8|)><rsup|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>-g|)>>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>>w<rsub|\<iota\>>.
    </equation>

    Indeed a separated step multiplies the weight
    by<nbsp><math|\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|-4*g>\<geqslant\>\<ell\><rsub|l><rsup|-4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>>.
    A fusion to<nbsp><math|g<rprime|'>\<less\>g> uses at
    most<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>
    factors<nbsp><math|\<alpha\><rsub|j>/8>, whereas
    <math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|g-g<rprime|'>|)>\<geqslant\>n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>>.

    At<nbsp><math|m+1> all representatives have the same ancestor. The
    initial normalized-field weights can be chosen with
    <math|<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>>w<rsub|\<iota\>>=e<rsup|-W*\<theta\><rsub|+>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>
    by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2>. Thus<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marker-weights>
    gives <math|\<omega\>=e<rsup|-\<cal-Z\>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>> and
    <math|\<frak-E\><rsub|m>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<cal-Z\>-t*\<theta\>|)>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>.
    Choose

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-terminal-weight>\<varpi\>=exp
      <around|{|<around|[|<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>-\<cal-Z\>|]>*T|}>.
    </equation>

    The second condition of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-marked-weights> has exponent
    <math|-<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>/16\<less\>0>; the first
    has exponent <math|-\<cal-Z\>-15*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>/16>,
    strictly below the exponent<nbsp><math|-\<cal-Z\>> of its right side.
    Both therefore hold, including every subpower factor.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-marked-costs> gives the coefficient
    bound<nbsp><math|2*\<frak-E\><rsub|m>> and chessboard
    bound<nbsp><math|2*N<rsub|k>/\<varpi\>> asserted here. All enlargements
    fit the prescribed cell margin. Reflection and conjugation preserve the
    majorants, so the same calculation applies to all cells and all cutoffs
    on the common tail.<label|proof:J-lattice-end>
  </proof>

  We next state the bounds when the exterior distances also shrink. Write
  <math|w=<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>,
  <math|W=<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>> and
  <math|V=<around|\||\<b-B\>|\|><rsub|deg>>. Consider families of
  configurations indexed by <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>. Suppose the cluster lies
  within <math|e<rsup|-\<alpha\>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>> of<nbsp><math|o>, its
  pairwise distances are at least <math|e<rsup|-\<alpha\>*T-o<around|(|T|)>>>,
  and <math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0>. Exterior points are at distance at least
  <math|e<rsup|-\<beta\>*T-o<around|(|T|)>>> from every cluster point
  and<nbsp><math|o>, and mutually at least
  <math|e<rsup|-\<upsilon\>*T-o<around|(|T|)>>> apart, where
  <math|0\<leqslant\>\<beta\>\<less\>\<alpha\>> and
  <math|\<upsilon\>\<geqslant\>\<beta\>>. The field lists are fixed, or range
  over fixed compact sets of coefficients. Call these conditions<nbsp>(G).
  Let <math|u<rsub|<around|(|i,\<kappa\>|)>>>, <math|\<kappa\>> ranging over
  the degree-<math|i> basis fields, be the biorthogonal fields of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:partII-J.1>, and put
  <math|w<rsub|o>=\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w>. Set

  <\align*>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsub|i,\<kappa\>><rsup|<math-up|raw>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|\<gamma\>>m<rsub|<around|(|i,\<kappa\>|)>*\<gamma\>>*S*<around|(|w<rsub|o>,B<rsub|i,\<gamma\>>|)>,>>|<row|<cell|A<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|h>>|<cell|=P<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|raw>>*w<rsub|o>\<in\>F<rsub|h>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|<math-up|raw>>>|<cell|=M<rsup|0>*<around*|(|w*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l><around|(|y<rsub|l>|)>|)>-M<rsup|0>*<around*|(|A<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|D><around|(|o|)>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l><around|(|y<rsub|l>|)>|)>\<in\>\<frak-A\><rsub|0><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align*>

  Here <math|P<rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|raw>>> is the projection of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D31>, and <math|M<rsup|0>> is the
  compression of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:compression> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>.

  The following estimates retain the exterior distance powers needed for
  nested clusters and contacts.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Power-separated estimates><label|lem:J-power-separated> For every
    <math|i,D\<geqslant\>0> and <math|j\<leqslant\>h>, families of
    configurations obeying<nbsp>(G) satisfy

    <\align>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<lambda\><rsub|i,\<kappa\>><rsup|<math-up|raw>>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|W-i|)>*\<alpha\>*T>,>|<cell|\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|<math-up|raw>>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|[|W*\<alpha\>-<around|(|D+1|)>*<around|(|\<alpha\>-\<beta\>|)>+V*\<upsilon\>|]>*T>,<eq-number><label|eq:J-raw-rates>>>|<row|<cell|Q<rsub|j>*B<rsub|j>*w-Q<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j,h>*A<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|h>>|<cell|\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|W-h-1|)>*\<alpha\>*T>.>|<cell|>|<cell|<eq-number><label|eq:J-average-error>>>>>
    </align>

    The final coefficients and tested remainder of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D33> obey the same respective bounds, in
    norm before state evaluation.
  </lemma>

  A degree-<math|i> coordinate thus costs the cluster degree
  less<nbsp><math|i> at the cluster height<nbsp><math|\<alpha\>>, and the
  remainder gains <math|<around|(|D+1|)>*<around|(|\<alpha\>-\<beta\>|)>>
  from the difference of the cluster and exterior heights.

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-power-start>The proof converts chessboard seminorms into
    algebra norms by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-roots>, applies
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-lattice> on grid configurations, removes the
    grid restriction by derivative descent with the calculus of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>, and then averages the reconstruction map.
    Write<nbsp><math|n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>=<around|\||I<rsub|<math-up|cl>>|\|>>.

    <em|Step 1: chessboard seminorms bound algebra norms.> Suppose a lattice
    difference has centered chessboard seminorm
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.3>) at most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>>,
    and the exterior insertions have chessboard seminorms at
    most<nbsp><math|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>,l>>. Put the difference in a coarse
    cell and the exterior insertions in finer disjoint cells; their stencils
    fit by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F1a>.
    Let<nbsp><math|U\<in\>\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> be the corresponding compressed
    difference. The lattice paragraph after
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-roots>, with the moment identity of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>, gives for every<nbsp><math|n\<in\><N>>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-moments><big|int><rsub|<phases>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|><rsup|2*n>*<space|0.17em>d*\<nu\><around|(|\<xi\>|)>\<leqslant\><around|(|B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>,l>|)><rsup|2*n><space|0.17em>,
    </equation>

    and Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H-roots>
    gives<nbsp><math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|<math-up|rt>,l>>.
    The same argument bounds a word by the chessboard seminorms of its
    insertions, giving the crude exponent<nbsp><math|W*\<alpha\>+V*\<upsilon\>>
    on the stated geometries.

    <em|Step 2: grid configurations.> Consider the generic grid
    configurations defined above in a cluster of
    height<nbsp><math|\<alpha\>\<gtr\>0>. Choose
    <math|0\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|->\<less\>\<theta\>\<less\>\<alpha\>\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|+>>,
    with exterior distance exponent<nbsp><math|\<beta\>\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|->>,
    and <math|\<cal-Z\>=W*\<theta\><rsub|+>+4*n<rsub|<math-up|cl>>*<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<theta\>|)>>.
    Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-lattice> with an auxiliary
    degree<nbsp><math|t\<geqslant\>D>. For each fixed cluster geometry the
    output coefficients are bounded at fixed<nbsp><math|m>; the reverse
    transfer inequality of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:E2.2> bounds them also
    at the reference level. Along a refinement of the ordered cutoff tails
    they converge to a field<nbsp><math|A<rsup|<math-up|out>>\<in\>F<rsub|t>>.
    Step<nbsp>1 bounds the replacement error by exponent

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-Z\>-<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>+V*\<upsilon\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Testing the same difference against the fixed probes bounds the raw
    coordinate errors. Reference-level coefficient convergence and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-graded-quotient> apply on this
    geometry-dependent refinement. The degree-<math|i> raw coordinate
    annihilates<nbsp><math|F<rsub|i-1>> by the biorthogonality before
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D31>, and is a fixed bounded functional on
    the quotient. With<nbsp><math|\<epsilon\><rsub|<math-up|bd>>\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|\<cal-Z\>-t*\<theta\>|)>*T>>,
    <eqref|eq:J-graded-input> therefore bounds that coordinate by
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<cal-Z\>-i*\<theta\>>.

    Choose<nbsp><math|t> large enough that
    <math|<around|(|t+1/16|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<theta\><rsub|->|)>\<geqslant\><around|(|D+1|)>*\<theta\>>
    and also large enough for every coefficient being tested. Exact
    reconstruction of<nbsp><math|A<rsup|<math-up|out>>> by the prescription
    separates the degrees<nbsp><math|i\<leqslant\>D>
    from<nbsp><math|D\<less\>i\<leqslant\>t>. An omitted degree contributes
    at most

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-Z\>-i*\<theta\>+i*\<beta\>+V*\<upsilon\>\<leqslant\>\<cal-Z\>-<around|(|D+1|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<beta\>|)>+V*\<upsilon\><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The low-degree coordinate errors satisfy the same bound, by the chosen
    auxiliary horizon. We have therefore proved grid
    exponents<nbsp><math|\<cal-Z\>-i*\<theta\>> for raw degree-<math|i>
    coordinates and<nbsp><math|\<cal-Z\>-<around|(|D+1|)>*<around|(|\<theta\>-\<beta\>|)>+V*\<upsilon\>>
    for the raw remainder.

    <em|Step 3: derivative descent.> Run the crude, grid and derivative
    bounds uniformly over all characters<nbsp><math|\<xi\>\<in\><phases>>.
    The algebra<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> is commutative, so
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|U|\<\|\|\>>=sup<rsub|\<xi\>><around|\||\<xi\><around|(|U|)>|\|>>.
    To remove the grid restriction, consider these character evaluations for
    every input label list and every positional derivative. A derivative
    replaces its input label by a field of degree one higher, by
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F2>. For suitable
    constants<nbsp><math|c<rsub|0>,d<rsub|0>> independent of the input
    degrees, the crude bound has exponent<nbsp><math|\<alpha\>*W+c<rsub|0>>
    and the grid bound exponent<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|+>*W+d<rsub|0>>. We
    descend in the total input degree<nbsp><math|W>. The crude bound proves
    the desired estimate for all<nbsp><math|W> with
    <math|<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>-\<alpha\>|)>*W\<geqslant\>c<rsub|0>-d<rsub|0>>,
    because <math|\<theta\><rsub|+>\<gtr\>\<alpha\>>. For the induction step,
    move the configuration to a generic level-<math|j> grid configuration a
    distance <math|O<around|(|a<rsub|j>|)>> away; the intervening segment
    remains separated at exponent<nbsp><math|\<alpha\>>. A positional
    derivative has total degree <math|W+1>, so the induction hypothesis and
    the grid spacing give <math|e<rsup|<around|[|\<theta\><rsub|+>*<around|(|W+1|)>+d<rsub|0>|]>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>*a<rsub|j>=e<rsup|<around|(|\<theta\><rsub|+>*W+d<rsub|0>|)>*T+o<around|(|T|)>>>:
    the displacement error has exactly the required degree-<math|W> bound,
    for every slot and axis.

    <em|Step 4: limits.> Now let<nbsp><math|\<theta\>*\<uparrow\>*\<alpha\>>
    and<nbsp><math|\<theta\><rsub|+>*\<downarrow\>*\<alpha\>>,
    keeping<nbsp><math|\<beta\>\<less\>\<theta\><rsub|->\<less\>\<theta\>>.
    Translation moves the origin to<nbsp><math|o>. This
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-raw-rates> for arbitrary configurations. The
    remainder estimate is in<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>>, and empty
    clusters give zero remainder.

    <em|Step 5: the coefficients of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D33>.>
    Let<nbsp><math|A<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|h>\<in\>F<rsub|h>> be the raw
    truncation at an auxiliary degree<nbsp><math|h>. In every rotated probe
    correlation, the difference between the cluster
    and<nbsp><math|A<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|h><around|(|0|)>> has
    exponent<nbsp><math|<around|(|W-h-1|)>*\<alpha\>>. The marked norm bounds
    and multilinearity make<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-raw-rates> uniform on compact
    coefficient sets, hence on the rotation orbit. Haar averaging,
    conjugation and the fixed maps<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>> preserve the
    exponent. This proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-average-error>.
    With<nbsp><math|h=j>, all lower raw modes vanish
    under<nbsp><math|Q<rsub|j>*L<rsub|j,j>> by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-cuts>; the
    remaining modes have exponent<nbsp><math|<around|(|W-j|)>*\<alpha\>>.
    With<nbsp><math|h=D>, use<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-cuts> to
    reconstruct<nbsp><math|A<rsup|<math-up|raw>><rsub|D>>, and telescope the
    raw remainder with the errors<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-average-error>
    for<nbsp><math|j\<leqslant\>D>. Testing an error in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|j>>
    costs<nbsp><math|j*\<beta\>+V*\<upsilon\>>, so their largest exponent
    is<nbsp><math|W*\<alpha\>-<around|(|D+1|)>*<around|(|\<alpha\>-\<beta\>|)>+V*\<upsilon\>>.<label|proof:J-power-end>
  </proof>

  At fixed exterior distances the power-separated estimates become the bounds
  of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP>. The
  parameters<nbsp><math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>> are nonnegative and the
  separation and error parameters are positive, as in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>. For the algebra-valued remainder put

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|D>=M<rsup|0><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l><around|(|y<rsub|l>|)>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D,<space|0.17em>\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>>c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>*<space|0.17em>M<rsup|0>*<around*|(|O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>><around|(|o|)>*<big|prod><rsub|l>B<rsub|l><around|(|y<rsub|l>|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Here<nbsp><math|M<rsup|0>> is the compression of separated words of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:compression> and
  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.2>; its characters and vacuum state give the
  phase and mixed remainders<nbsp><math|\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<zeta\>>> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Compressed remainder bound><label|lem:J1.2> Fix finite lists
    <math|\<b-A\>,\<b-B\>>, <math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>\<geqslant\>0>,
    <math|\<eta\>,\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>,\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0> and
    <math|D\<in\><N>>. There is <math|r<rsub|1>\<gtr\>0> such that, for
    <math|0\<less\>r\<less\>r<rsub|1>>, every
    <math|<around|(|r,M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>,\<eta\>|)>>-cluster
    <math|<around|(|x,o|)>> and every <math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>>-clear
    spectator configuration<nbsp><math|y>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-R\><rsub|D>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>>\<leqslant\>r<rsup|D+1-<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>-\<varepsilon\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J1.2-start>Apply Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated>
    to Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>, with <math|T=-log r>,
    <math|\<alpha\>=1> and <math|\<beta\>=\<upsilon\>=0>. Every admissible
    configuration has these bounds on extent and reciprocal distances. If the
    claimed threshold failed, choose violating configurations at radii
    tending to zero. The estimate just proved along that sequence, with
    loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>/2>, eventually contradicts the violation
    with loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>>: constants and subpower factors are
    absorbed by<nbsp><math|r<rsup|\<varepsilon\>/2>>.
    Taking<nbsp><math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>*r\<less\>\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>/2>
    also ensures separation.<label|proof:J1.2-end>
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:OP>>
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1> fixes the fields and coefficients of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18> before spectators, state indices or
    truncation. The coefficient bound follows from the coefficient estimate
    of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated> by the contradiction
    argument in the proof of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.2>; its threshold
    depends only on the data of the coefficient. Character evaluation and the
    mixed vacuum are contractions on<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>>, so
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.2> gives the remainder bound with its own
    threshold. The argument bounds<nbsp><math|\<cal-R\><rsub|D>>
    in<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> before state evaluation.
  </render-proof>

  A nested configuration retains leaf positions and internal centers, so it
  also describes partially replaced words. Call an internal vertex other than
  the root proper, and fix its truncation<nbsp><math|D<rsub|v>\<in\><N>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Nested geometry and rates><label|def:D34> Give each
    vertex<nbsp><math|v> of a finite rooted tree a
    position<nbsp><math|z<rsub|v>>, each leaf a field<nbsp><math|A<rsub|i>>,
    and each internal vertex a scale<nbsp><math|\<varrho\><rsub|v>\<gtr\>0>.
    Its root<nbsp><math|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>> is internal. Require
    <math|\<varrho\><rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>=r>,
    <math|\<varrho\><rsub|v>\<leqslant\>\<varrho\><rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>>,
    <math|<around|\||z<rsub|u>-z<rsub|v>|\|>\<leqslant\>M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>*\<varrho\><rsub|v>>
    for every descendant<nbsp><math|u>, and
    <math|<around|\||z<rsub|u>-z<rsub|u<rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>\<eta\>*\<varrho\><rsub|v>>
    for positions in different child subtrees. Put

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|W<rsub|v>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|i<text|
      below >v>deg A<rsub|i>,>|<cell|q<rsub|v>>|<cell|=\<varrho\><rsub|v>/\<varrho\><rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>,>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q><around|(|\<varrho\>|)>>|<cell|=r<rsup|q-W<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>-\<varepsilon\>>*<big|prod><rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
      internal>>q<rsub|v><rsup|-W<rsub|v>-\<varepsilon\>>,>|<cell|<math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>><around|(|\<varrho\>|)>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
      internal>>q<rsub|v><rsup|D<rsub|v>+1><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>
  </definition>

  Spectators are<nbsp><math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>>-clear of every tree
  position and each other.

  The recursion retains every leaf, including those above a neighboring
  truncation.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Iterated coefficient><label|def:D35> A leaf contributes its field
    and coefficient one. At a proper internal vertex<nbsp><math|v>, replace
    its children by

    <\equation*>
      <big|sum><rsub|j\<leqslant\>D<rsub|v>><big|sum><rsub|\<lambda\>\<in\><channels|j>>c<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>(<text|child
      fields>;<text|child centers>,z<rsub|v>)O<rsub|j,\<lambda\>>,
    </equation*>

    multiplying their coefficients and summing. At the root retain
    only<nbsp><math|<around|(|q,\<lambda\>|)>>,
    giving<nbsp><math|C<rsup|<math-up|it>,\<b-D\>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>>. The
    direct coefficient is <math|C<rsup|<math-up|dir>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>=c<rsub|q,\<lambda\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;z<rsub|<math-up|leaves>>,z<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>|)>>.
    This compares successive and direct expansion.
  </definition>

  For example, let a cluster<nbsp><math|v> of leaves of total
  degree<nbsp><math|W<rsub|v>> first fuse at
  scale<nbsp><math|\<varrho\><rsub|v>>, and let its output then fuse with the
  remaining leaves at the root scale<nbsp><math|r>. If<nbsp><math|v> is the
  only proper internal vertex, then

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q>=r<rsup|q-W<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>-\<varepsilon\>>*<around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|v>/r|)><rsup|-W<rsub|v>-\<varepsilon\>>,<space|2em><math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>>=<around|(|\<varrho\><rsub|v>/r|)><rsup|D<rsub|v>+1>.
  </equation*>

  The first factor is the size allowed for the direct coefficient; the second
  is the truncation gain at the inner fusion. For a general tree the errors
  from different vertices are added, as in the definition
  of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>>>.

  Fix the tree, leaf list, output<nbsp><math|<around|(|q,\<lambda\>|)>>,
  proper-vertex truncations, <math|M<rsub|<math-up|geo>>\<geqslant\>0>
  and<nbsp><math|\<eta\>,\<varepsilon\>\<gtr\>0>. No lower bound on the
  ratios<nbsp><math|q<rsub|v>> is imposed.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Nested coefficient estimate><label|lem:J1.4> There
    is<nbsp><math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> such that all geometries of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D34> with<nbsp><math|0\<less\>r\<less\>r<rsub|0>>
    satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||C<rsup|<math-up|dir>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q>,<space|2em><around|\||C<rsup|<math-up|dir>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>-C<rsup|<math-up|it>,\<b-D\>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q><math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Both expressions are smooth in neighborhoods of these geometries.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J1.4-start>The estimates of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated> are applied vertex by vertex
    along a tree induction, after reducing to sequences whose scales have
    convergent logarithmic heights. Along any sequence of admissible radii
    and scales tending to the root collision, put
    <math|T=<big|sum><rsub|v<text| internal>>log
    <around|(|1/\<varrho\><rsub|v>|)>>. Eventually every summand is positive.
    After a subsequence, <math|log <around|(|1/\<varrho\><rsub|v>|)>/T\<to\>a<rsub|v>\<geqslant\>0>,
    with<nbsp><math|<big|sum><rsub|v>a<rsub|v>=1>
    and<nbsp><math|a<rsub|v>\<geqslant\>a<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>>.
    Set

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\>=W<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>+<big|sum><rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
      internal>>W<rsub|v>*<around|(|a<rsub|v>-a<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    We prove by induction on the tree that a direct output of
    degree<nbsp><math|q> has exponent<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>-q*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>>,
    and its error against the prescribed finite iteration has that exponent
    minus <math|\<gamma\>=min<rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
    internal>><around|(|D<rsub|v>+1|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|v>-a<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>|)>>.
    With no proper internal vertices the two coefficients coincide exactly.

    First flatten the edges<nbsp><math|u\<to\>v>
    with<nbsp><math|a<rsub|u>=a<rsub|v>>: combine their grouping before
    making any field replacement. The word at<nbsp><math|v> then consists of
    original leaves in this equal-height component and outputs from strictly
    finer children; its distinct positions are separated at
    exponent<nbsp><math|a<rsub|v>> by the tree geometry. For a child
    with<nbsp><math|a<rsub|u>\<gtr\>a<rsub|v>>, expand to an auxiliary
    horizon<nbsp><math|H<rsub|u>>. The remainder exponent has the
    improvement<nbsp><math|<around|(|H<rsub|u>+1|)>*<around|(|a<rsub|u>-a<rsub|v>|)>>
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-raw-rates>. Choose the children in a fixed order,
    choosing<nbsp><math|H<rsub|u>> only after the outputs already used as
    exterior fields are fixed. Each improvement tends to infinity
    with<nbsp><math|H<rsub|u>>, whereas the exterior degree in that step is
    fixed. It can therefore exceed any prescribed error exponent. The
    identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-telescope><big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>X<rsub|i>-<big|prod><rsub|i=1><rsup|n>Y<rsub|i>=<big|sum><rsub|i=1><rsup|n><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|h\<less\>i>Y<rsub|h>|)>*<around|(|X<rsub|i>-Y<rsub|i>|)><around*|(|<big|prod><rsub|h\<gtr\>i>X<rsub|h>|)>
    </equation>

    justifies these successive replacements in every correlation and in every
    probe defining a coefficient. It is an identity of separated expressions,
    with their scalar coefficient factors retained.

    For a fixed collection of child outputs<nbsp><math|j<rsub|u>>, the child
    exponents include<nbsp><math|-j<rsub|u>*a<rsub|u>>, and the parent
    coefficient contributes<nbsp><math|j<rsub|u>*a<rsub|v>>. Their sum
    contains<nbsp><math|-j<rsub|u>*<around|(|a<rsub|u>-a<rsub|v>|)>>; the
    remaining leaf weights telescope to<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>-q*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>>.
    Nonnegative output degrees therefore never increase this exponent. If the
    root height is zero, apply the crude estimate to this root word of child
    outputs; its finitely many field degrees cost exponent zero, while their
    coefficients already carry the child contributions
    to<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>>. This proves the direct bound, by the preceding
    arbitrary-accuracy replacements and the induction hypothesis. Remove
    auxiliary outputs above<nbsp><math|D<rsub|u>>. Each discarded term has at
    least one<nbsp><math|j<rsub|u>\<geqslant\>D<rsub|u>+1> and thus gains at
    least<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>>. Replacing the remaining direct child
    coefficients by their iterated coefficients
    uses<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-telescope> again and gains<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>>
    from one child error. If<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>=0>, the direct and iterated
    bounds themselves give the error exponent.
    If<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0>, every proper internal scale increase is
    positive and the auxiliary horizons can be chosen to make the preceding
    replacement errors smaller than this exponent. This proves the induction
    for the difference, with one improvement rather than a product of
    improvements.

    The logarithmic exponent of<nbsp><math|\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q>>
    is

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-B\>-q*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>+\<varepsilon\>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>,<space|2em>A<rsub|\<ast\>>=a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>+<big|sum><rsub|v\<ne\>o<rsub|<math-up|root>><text|
      internal>><around|(|a<rsub|v>-a<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|v|)>>|)>\<gtr\>0<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Positivity follows because<nbsp><math|<big|sum><rsub|v>a<rsub|v>=1>: if
    every displayed nonnegative term vanished, every<nbsp><math|a<rsub|v>>
    would vanish. The exponent of<nbsp><math|<math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>>>
    is<nbsp><math|-\<gamma\>>. Thus the estimates with
    loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>/2> imply the displayed bounds with
    loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>> and coefficient one along this sequence.
    A sequence of violations would have the subsequence just treated, a
    contradiction. This proves a threshold uniform in the scale vector, even
    when some scales decrease faster than every power of<nbsp><math|r>. All
    positions stay separated and, for small<nbsp><math|r>, clear of every
    finite rotated probe family used in the recursion. The averaging
    integrands are smooth on an open neighborhood with that clearance; their
    derivatives are bounded on compact neighborhoods
    times<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>>. Differentiating the finite sums and
    Haar integrals proves smoothness.<label|proof:J1.4-end>
  </proof>

  For tested products, retain the original leaves at the root; only the
  degree of its output is truncated. Fix additionally the root
  truncation<nbsp><math|m\<in\><N>>, a spectator list<nbsp><math|\<b-B\>>
  and<nbsp><math|\<delta\><rsub|<math-up|sp>>\<gtr\>0>.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Nested tested estimate><label|lem:J1.5> For
    some<nbsp><math|r<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0>, every smaller radius, admissible
    nested geometry, admissible spectator configuration and state
    index<nbsp><math|\<zeta\>> satisfy

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<cal-R\><rsub|m><rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>,z<rsub|<math-up|leaves>>;\<b-B\>,y;z<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,m+1><around|(|\<varrho\>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The same bound holds in the norm of<nbsp><math|\<frak-A\><rsub|0>> before
    evaluation in a character or the mixed vacuum.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The tree induction of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.4> is run with the
    spectators kept in every replacement of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D18>. Their reciprocal distances from
    every tree position and from one another have exponent zero. They
    therefore add no term to<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>>. After the
    arbitrary-accuracy child replacements, the root remainder estimate of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated> has
    exponent<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>-<around|(|m+1|)>*a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>>.
    When<nbsp><math|a<rsub|o<rsub|<math-up|root>>>=0>, apply the crude bound
    to the root word of child outputs after these replacements. The child
    coefficient powers already account for<nbsp><math|\<cal-B\>>; the
    finitely many root output fields and the spectators cost exponent zero.
    The positive quantity<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\>*A<rsub|\<ast\>>> used
    above again absorbs all finite sums and subpower factors. The
    contradiction argument is unchanged. The norm estimate follows
    from<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-moments>; applying a character or the mixed vacuum
    proves the scalar bounds with the same threshold.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Integrated forms><label|lem:J1.3><label|lem:J1.6> The thresholds
    of Lemmas<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.2>, <reference|lem:J1.4>
    and<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.5> precede positions, scale vectors and
    states, so they survive integration. Fix the data of one of these lemmas,
    a measurable space<nbsp><math|Z> and<nbsp><math|B<rsub|0>\<geqslant\>0>.
    Below a threshold preceding the measure, the weight, the scale vector and
    the maps, the following holds. Let <math|\<lambda\><rsub|Z>> be a measure
    on<nbsp><math|Z>, <math|\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>> an integrable complex
    weight with <math|<big|int><around|\||\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|Z>\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>>,
    and the geometry, spectator and phase maps almost-everywhere measurable
    and admissible almost everywhere on<nbsp><math|<around|{|\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>\<ne\>0|}>>;
    in the nested case the scale vector<nbsp><math|\<varrho\>> is fixed
    throughout the integral. Then the weighted full, truncated and compared
    expressions are integrable, and

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|int><rsub|Z>\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>*\<cal-R\><rsub|D><rsup|\<xi\><around|(|z|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|Z>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>*r<rsup|D+1-<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>-\<varepsilon\>>,>|<cell|<around*|\||<big|int><rsub|Z>\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>*C<rsup|<math-up|dir>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|Z>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q>,>>|<row|<cell|<around*|\||<big|int><rsub|Z>\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>*<around*|(|C<rsup|<math-up|dir>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>-C<rsup|<math-up|it>,\<b-D\>><rsub|q,\<lambda\>>|)>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|Z>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,q><math-ss|e><rsub|\<b-D\>>,>|<cell|<around*|\||<big|int><rsub|Z>\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>*\<cal-R\><rsub|m><rsup|\<xi\><around|(|z|)>>*<space|0.17em>d*\<lambda\><rsub|Z>|\|>>|<cell|\<leqslant\>B<rsub|0>*\<cal-Q\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>,m+1>.>>>>
    </align*>

    The first and last bounds also hold for the mixed state. Indeed, for
    fixed<nbsp><math|r> and<nbsp><math|\<varrho\>>, <eqref|eq:J-moments>
    bounds the full correlation uniformly, and the truncated sum is the full
    expression minus the bounded remainder. Smoothness of the coefficients
    where their rotated probes are clear, continuity in the phase
    (Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:H1.3>) and rounding of the geometry to a
    countable dense set give joint measurability. Integrating the pointwise
    bounds against<nbsp><math|<around|\||\<vartheta\><rsub|Z>|\|>> gives the
    estimates.
  </remark>

  <subsection|Contact subtractions><label|sec:partII-J.2>

  Property<nbsp>E7 alone gives some extension through the collision diagonals
  (see the discussion before Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT>). This
  subsection constructs a specific one, by explicit subtraction of normal
  Taylor jets of the test function read through contact projections, and
  proves its local order <math|<around|(|2+<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>|)>*n>
  on compact sets. For each retained field degree we subtract a jet of a
  different order, and partial collisions require the same operation inside
  each smaller cluster; absolute convergence follows by summing normal
  shells.

  We work with finite sums of field words,
  identifying<nbsp><math|A<around|(|x|)>>
  with<nbsp><math|<around|(|\<jmath\><rsub|d,e>*A|)><around|(|x|)>> and
  imposing linearity in<nbsp><math|A>. Their product is concatenation. The
  correlation of a word is read only at distinct positions; elsewhere give it
  value zero. This convention is used inside the algebraic formulas below,
  before any integration. The underlying prescription and its fields are
  those fixed in Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1>; the finite probes chosen
  next are independent of its OPE probes.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Contact projection><label|def:D36> Choose contact probes and
    reconstruction maps as in Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D31>.
    Choose<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>\<gtr\>0> with every
    probe point at distance at least<nbsp><math|2*\<rho\><rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>
    from zero. For a word<nbsp><math|w>, set

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|q>*w=\<b-1\><rsub|<around|{|<text|all positions of >w<text| lie
      in ><wide|B|\<bar\>>*<around|(|0,\<rho\><rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>|)>|}>>*w<space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Apply the averaging and reality formula of
    Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D32> to this clipped word with the contact
    probes; extend linearly to sums, obtaining<nbsp><math|B<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>.
    This extracts contact fields on a fixed region clear of its probes.
  </definition>

  For contact expressions<nbsp><math|I<rsub|q>*A=A<around|(|0|)>> still
  denotes a single field. Unlike the domain restriction in
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D32>, clipping here acts on each monomial
  before summing it.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Coherent contact projections><label|def:D37> Put
    <math|T<rsub|q>=P<rsub|q>*I<rsub|q+1>> and define recursively

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|0>=B<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|ct>>,<space|2em>P<rsub|q+1>=\<jmath\><rsub|q,q+1>*P<rsub|q>+<around|(|1-\<jmath\><rsub|q,q+1>*T<rsub|q>|)>*B<rsub|q+1><rsup|<math-up|ct>>,<space|2em>P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*w=P<rsub|q>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The first term retains the degrees already chosen through<nbsp><math|q>;
    the second takes the new measurement and removes its component
    in<nbsp><math|\<jmath\><rsub|q,q+1>*F<rsub|q>>. In particular,
    <math|P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)><around|[|A<around|(|o|)>|]>=A>
    for<nbsp><math|A\<in\>F<rsub|q>>.
  </definition>

  <\definition>
    <dueto|Contact grades and clearance><label|def:D38> The exact-degree
    components and common clearance are

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|P<rsub|=0><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*w>|<cell|=B<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>,>>|<row|<cell|P<rsub|=q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*w>|<cell|=<around|(|1-\<jmath\><rsub|q-1,q>*T<rsub|q-1>|)>*B<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*<around|(|\<tau\><rsub|-o>*w|)>*<space|1em><around|(|q\<gtr\>0|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<delta\><rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|pres>>>|<cell|=min<rsub|0\<leqslant\>d\<leqslant\>q>
      \<rho\><rsub|d><rsup|<math-up|ct>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
    </align*>

    These give the graded contact subtractions and their common range of
    separated evaluation.
  </definition>

  The projections are defined before any test function or state. Their strata
  are the contact degree subspaces <math|E<rsub|0><rsup|<math-up|ct>>=F<rsub|0>>
  and <math|E<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>=ker T<rsub|q-1>\<subset\>F<rsub|q>>
  <math|<around|(|q\<gtr\>0|)>>. The proof of the next lemma shows that
  <math|F<rsub|q>=\<jmath\><rsub|q-1,q>*F<rsub|q-1>\<oplus\>E<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>;
  these strata, not the complements<nbsp><math|E<rsub|q>> of
  Definition<nbsp><reference|def:D33>, carry the real bases of the contact
  construction.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Real bases><label|lem:J2.1> The contact projections are covariant
    under translations, <math|O<around|(|4|)>> and conjugation. Each contact
    stratum<nbsp><math|E<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>> has a real basis, and
    the degree-zero basis contains the unit.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    The inclusions, rotations and conjugation of Part<nbsp>I act as in
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1>. A ball centered at zero is rotation
    invariant. Haar invariance proves rotation covariance
    of<nbsp><math|B<rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>, and its reality formula
    proves conjugation covariance. These maps
    fix<nbsp><math|A<around|(|0|)>>: before averaging the reconstruction map
    returns<nbsp><math|R<rsub|q><around|(|h<rsup|-1>|)>*A>, and after
    multiplication by<nbsp><math|R<rsub|q><around|(|h|)>> it
    returns<nbsp><math|A>. The recursion gives

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-contact-split>P<rsub|q>*I<rsub|q>=1,<space|2em>T<rsub|q>*\<jmath\><rsub|q,q+1>=1,<space|2em>T<rsub|q>*P<rsub|q+1>=P<rsub|q>,<space|2em>P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>=<big|sum><rsub|d\<leqslant\>q>\<jmath\><rsub|d,q>*P<rsub|=d><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    For the third identity, apply<nbsp><math|T<rsub|q>> to the recursion and
    cancel<nbsp><math|T<rsub|q>*B<rsub|q+1><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>; the fourth
    follows by iteration. By the second identity,
    <math|1-\<jmath\><rsub|q-1,q>*T<rsub|q-1>> projects<nbsp><math|F<rsub|q>>
    onto <math|ker T<rsub|q-1>> along <math|\<jmath\><rsub|q-1,q>*F<rsub|q-1>>,
    which proves the splitting. Each stratum is invariant under conjugation
    and rotations. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1> gives real bases; choose
    the unit in<nbsp><math|F<rsub|0>>. Covariance passes through the
    recursion, with translations following from
    <math|\<tau\><rsub|-<around|(|o+u|)>>*\<tau\><rsub|u>=\<tau\><rsub|-o>>.
  </proof>

  We give the finite subtraction formula before proving its convergence.
  Fix<nbsp><math|\<b-A\>\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>>,
  put<nbsp><math|d<rsub|i>=deg A<rsub|i>>,
  <math|W=<big|sum><rsub|i>d<rsub|i>>, and choose

  <\equation*>
    r<rsub|\<ast\>>=min <around|(|1,\<delta\><rsub|W><rsup|<math-up|pres>>|)>,<space|2em>s<rsub|\<ast\>>=r<rsub|\<ast\>>/4<space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.2> uses only this single
  length<nbsp><math|s<rsub|\<ast\>>>. Expand each input in the contact
  stratum bases using<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-split>, retaining its original
  degree tag<nbsp><math|d<rsub|i>> in every subtraction order.
  For<nbsp><math|h\<subset\><around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>>,
  <math|<around|\||h|\|>\<geqslant\>2>, let<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|h>> be
  its collision diagonal, <math|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>> the orthogonal
  component normal to<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|h>>,
  and<nbsp><math|<wide|x|\<bar\>><rsub|h>=<around|\||h|\|><rsup|-1>*<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>h>x<rsub|i>>.
  Thus<nbsp><math|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>> has
  entries<nbsp><math|x<rsub|i>-<wide|x|\<bar\>><rsub|h>> on<nbsp><math|h> and
  zero elsewhere. Write

  <\equation*>
    d<rsub|h>=<big|sum><rsub|i\<in\>h>d<rsub|i>,<space|2em>n<rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|h>=4*<around|(|<around|\||h|\|>-1|)>,<space|2em>L<rsub|h>=max
    <around|(|0,d<rsub|h>+1-n<rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|h>|)><space|0.17em>.
  </equation*>

  Here <math|n<rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|h>> is the dimension of the normal space
  of<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|h>>. Choose a smooth radial
  cutoff<nbsp><math|\<chi\>> equal to one on the unit ball and zero outside
  the ball of radius two. The normal Taylor operator is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-normal-Taylor><around|(|<math-up|Tay><rsub|h,k><rsup|\<perp\>><space|0.17em>f|)><around|(|x|)>=\<chi\>*<around|(|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>/s<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>*<big|sum><rsub|l=0><rsup|k><frac|1|l!>*D<rsup|l>*f*<around|(|x-x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|)>*<around|[|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|]><space|0.17em>.
  </equation>

  It subtracts only normal variables, leaving the centroid and all exterior
  variables as parameters.

  An expression<nbsp><math|F\<otimes\>U> means the field
  expression<nbsp><math|F<around|(|x|)>> multiplied
  by<nbsp><math|<around|(|U*f|)><around|(|x|)>> when tested
  against<nbsp><math|f>. Products compose the test operators. On a monomial
  write<nbsp><math|F=F<rsub|h>*F<rsub|h<rsup|c>>> for its factors in and
  outside<nbsp><math|h>. Let<nbsp><math|<math-ss|R><rsub|h,q>> replace the
  subword of<nbsp><math|F<around|(|x|)>> on<nbsp><math|h> by the
  field<nbsp><math|P<rsub|=q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|<wide|x|\<bar\>><rsub|h>|)>*F<rsub|h><around|(|x|)>>
  at<nbsp><math|<wide|x|\<bar\>><rsub|h>>, leaving its test operator
  unchanged.

  For two insertions, <math|h=<around|{|1,2|}>>, the centroid
  <math|o=<wide|x|\<bar\>><rsub|h>> and the relative coordinate
  <math|x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|2>> parametrize<nbsp><math|<R><rsup|8>>, and
  <math|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>=<tfrac|1|2><around|(|x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|2>,x<rsub|2>-x<rsub|1>|)>>.
  With <math|F=A<rsub|1><around|(|x<rsub|1>|)>*A<rsub|2><around|(|x<rsub|2>|)>>
  the subtracted expression is

  <\equation*>
    \<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|full>>*f=F*f-<big|sum><rsub|q\<less\>L<rsub|h>><around*|[|P<rsub|=q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*F|]><around|(|o|)><space|0.17em><math-up|Tay><rsub|h,L<rsub|h>-1-q><rsup|\<perp\>><space|0.17em>f,<space|2em>L<rsub|h>=max
    <around|(|0,d<rsub|1>+d<rsub|2>-3|)>.
  </equation*>

  The retained field degree<nbsp><math|q> determines how many normal
  derivatives of the test are subtracted. On a shell
  <math|<around|\||x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|\|>\<asymp\>\<rho\>>, the
  degree-<math|q> component has size<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsup|q-d<rsub|h>>> up
  to a loss<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsup|-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>>, the Taylor
  remainder of<nbsp><math|f> through order <math|L<rsub|h>-1-q>
  is<nbsp><math|O<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|L<rsub|h>-q>|)>>, and the shell has
  normal volume of order<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsup|4>>. The two arms of the
  signed expression, the field minus its projected components
  times<nbsp><math|f> and the projected components times the Taylor
  remainder, therefore contribute <math|O<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|4+L<rsub|h>-d<rsub|h>-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>|)>=O<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|1-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>|)>>
  per shell, summable over dyadic<nbsp><math|\<rho\>>. The individual terms
  need not be integrable: for <math|d<rsub|h>\<geqslant\>4> the
  term<nbsp><math|F*f> alone is only <math|O<around|(|\<rho\><rsup|4-d<rsub|h>>|)>>
  per shell, so each subtraction must stay with the term it subtracts.

  In general, define recursively

  <\align>
    <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<cal-C\><rsub|<around|{|i|}>><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>|<cell|=A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>\<otimes\>1,>|<cell|\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>>>|<cell|=<big|sum><rsub|<stack|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<pi\><text|
    partition of >h>>|<row|<cell|<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>\<geqslant\>2>>>>>><big|prod><rsub|b\<in\>\<pi\>>\<cal-C\><rsub|b><rsup|<math-up|ct>>,<eq-number><label|eq:J-contact-recursion>>>|<row|<cell|\<cal-C\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>|<cell|=-<big|sum><rsub|q\<less\>L<rsub|h>><math-ss|R><rsub|h,q>\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>><space|0.17em><math-up|Tay><rsub|h,L<rsub|h>-1-q><rsup|\<perp\>>,>|<cell|\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|full>>>|<cell|=\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>>+\<cal-C\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*<space|1em><around|(|<around|\||h|\|>\<geqslant\>2|)><space|0.17em>.>>>>
  </align>

  Singleton and empty full expressions are
  respectively<nbsp><math|A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>\<otimes\>1> and the
  unit. In a nested term, the parent Taylor operator acts first on the test
  and the child operators act afterwards: if <math|b\<subsetneq\>h> are a
  child and its parent, with Taylor orders <math|k<rsub|b>,k<rsub|h>>, the
  resulting test factor is

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Tay><rsub|b,k<rsub|b>><rsup|\<perp\>><around*|(|<math-up|Tay><rsub|h,k<rsub|h>><rsup|\<perp\>><space|0.17em>f|)>,
  </equation*>

  so the parent operator is written on the right. Operators on disjoint slot
  sets commute; no commutation is asserted for nested subsets. The recursion
  terminates because every child has fewer slots; expanding it gives forests
  of disjoint or nested collision subsets. This is a position-space forest
  formula. Subtracting a Taylor jet of the test function at a diagonal is, in
  the normal variables, the extension procedure
  of<nbsp><cite-detail|BrunettiFredenhagen2000|Section<nbsp>5.2,
  Theorem<nbsp>5.3>; here the subtracted jets are paired with contact
  projections, and the recursion organizes the partial collisions.

  For three insertions the expression before the total-collision subtraction
  is

  <\equation*>
    \<Pi\><rsub|<around|{|1,2,3|}>><rsup|<math-up|punct>>=A<rsub|1>*A<rsub|2>*A<rsub|3>+\<cal-C\><rsub|<around|{|1,2|}>><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*A<rsub|3>+\<cal-C\><rsub|<around|{|1,3|}>><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*A<rsub|2>+\<cal-C\><rsub|<around|{|2,3|}>><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*A<rsub|1>,
  </equation*>

  with the test operators in each<nbsp><math|\<cal-C\><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>
  retained. The total-collision subtraction is applied to this complete
  expression. Its nested terms have the form
  <math|<math-ss|R><rsub|<around|{|1,2,3|}>,q><math-ss|R><rsub|<around|{|1,2|}>,q<rprime|'>><around|(|A<rsub|1>*A<rsub|2>*A<rsub|3>|)>>
  with test factor <math|<math-up|Tay><rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|<around|{|1,2|}>,k<rprime|'>><around|(|<math-up|Tay><rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|<around|{|1,2,3|}>,k><space|0.17em>f|)>>.
  Where <math|<around|\||x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|2>|\|>> is much smaller than the
  distance to<nbsp><math|x<rsub|3>>, the child arms make the expression
  integrable in the normal variable of<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|<around|{|1,2|}>>>,
  and the parent arms then treat the total collision.
  Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:J-forests> shows both examples.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|figures/fig-12.svg|421.067577pt|109.89861pt||><label|fig:J-forests>>|Left:
  two insertions; the test is Taylor expanded
  in<nbsp><math|x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>> at<nbsp><math|\<Delta\><rsub|h>>
  where <math|<around|\||x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>2*s<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  and each dyadic shell contributes at most<nbsp><math|\<rho\>>, up
  to<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsup|-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>>. Right: a nested
  forest; the parent operator acts on the test first, and only operators on
  disjoint slot sets commute. Both are subtractions of separated
  correlations, not lattice limits at coincident points.>

  Before summing shells we need estimates for complete signed expressions
  after child subtractions. Normalize <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> so that all
  separation heights lie in<nbsp><math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, and fix a finite
  set<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>> of position paths. A finite separated sum of
  cost<nbsp><math|c> at height<nbsp><math|s> has terms

  <\equation*>
    a<around|(|T|)>*<big|prod><rsub|\<iota\>>A<rsub|\<iota\>><around|(|x<rsub|\<iota\>><around|(|T|)>|)>,<space|2em><around|\||a|\|>\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|c-s*<big|sum><rsub|\<iota\>>deg
    A<rsub|\<iota\>>|)>*T>,<space|2em><around|\||x<rsub|\<iota\>>-x<rsub|\<iota\><rprime|'>>|\|>\<geqslant\>e<rsup|-s*T-o<around|(|T|)>>*<space|1em><around|(|\<iota\>\<ne\>\<iota\><rprime|'>|)>,
  </equation*>

  with <math|x<rsub|\<iota\>>\<in\>\<cal-P\>>. This definition permits
  different terms to use overlapping subsets of<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>>.

  The parameter<nbsp><math|s> describes the cluster separation
  scale<nbsp><math|e<rsup|-s*T>>, and<nbsp><math|c> its total exponential
  growth bound. Membership in the class<nbsp><math|\<bbb-A\><rsub|s><around|(|c|)>>
  defined next means that the signed expression can be replaced, to
  arbitrarily high accuracy in exterior correlations, by a finite separated
  sum with this bound. The exterior word may have internal separations as
  small as<nbsp><math|e<rsup|-T>>, but it stays at distance at
  least<nbsp><math|e<rsup|-s*T-o<around|(|T|)>>> from the cluster. The
  approximating sum is chosen before the exterior word and its cost.

  Write <math|\<cal-U\>\<in\>\<bbb-A\><rsub|s><around|(|c|)>> if it has a
  finite height-one representation of some finite cost and satisfies the
  following approximation property. For every <math|L\<gtr\>0>, there is a
  finite separated sum<nbsp><math|g> of cost<nbsp><math|c> at
  height<nbsp><math|s>, using<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>>, such that for every
  <math|c<rprime|'>\<in\><R>> and every exterior finite separated
  sum<nbsp><math|Y> of cost<nbsp><math|c<rprime|'>> at height<nbsp><math|1>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:J-layer-precision><mid|\|>S<rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|<around|(|\<cal-U\>-g|)>*Y|)><mid|\|>\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|-L+c<rprime|'>|)>*T>.
  </equation>

  Every exterior path must be at distance at least
  <math|e<rsup|-s*T-o<around|(|T|)>>> from every path
  in<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>>. The bound permits any varying
  character<nbsp><math|\<zeta\><around|(|T|)>> and the mixed state. Scalar
  factors of rate<nbsp><math|b> change the cost to<nbsp><math|c+b>.

  Suppose the paths in<nbsp><math|\<cal-P\>> lie within
  <math|e<rsup|-s*T+o<around|(|T|)>>> and
  within<nbsp><math|2*s<rsub|\<ast\>>> of a
  center<nbsp><math|o\<in\>\<cal-P\>>. For contact projections assume
  <math|2*s<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>\<rho\><rsub|q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>.
  Write <math|I<rsub|q><rsup|o>*A=A<around|(|o|)>> for insertion at this
  center. These fixed-distance requirements ensure that the contact probes
  are clear; the exponential distances determine the rates.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Signed layer transfer><label|lem:J-signed-layer> For
    <math|0\<leqslant\>u\<leqslant\>s\<leqslant\>1> and
    <math|\<cal-U\>\<in\>\<bbb-A\><rsub|s><around|(|c|)>>, every clear
    contact degree<nbsp><math|q> satisfies

    <\equation*>
      P<rsub|=q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*\<cal-U\>\<preccurlyeq\>e<rsup|<around|(|c-q*s|)>*T>,<space|2em>\<cal-U\>-I<rsub|q><rsup|o>*P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>><around|(|o|)>*\<cal-U\>\<in\>\<bbb-A\><rsub|u>*<around*|(|c-<around|(|q+1|)>*<around|(|s-u|)>|)>.
    </equation*>

    Separated products add their costs; finite signed sums take their
    maximum. Coarsening from<nbsp><math|s> to<nbsp><math|u> without
    subtracting retains cost<nbsp><math|c>.
  </lemma>

  The projected degree-<math|q> component thus costs <math|c> less the Taylor
  gain<nbsp><math|q*s>, and the residual through degree<nbsp><math|q> gains
  <math|<around|(|q+1|)>*<around|(|s-u|)>> when it is viewed from the coarser
  height<nbsp><math|u>.

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J-layer-start>The proof orders the approximation precisions
    before applying the projections. Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-power-separated>,
    including its uniformity on compact label sets, expands each
    approximating term at the center; the
    telescope<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-telescope> handles products; the exact contact
    identities<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-split> give the cancellations.

    First check products, which explains the strength of the approximation
    property. Let<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>,\<cal-V\>> have
    costs<nbsp><math|c<rsub|1>,c<rsub|2>> and a separation at
    height<nbsp><math|s>. Suppose a height-one representation
    of<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\>> has cost<nbsp><math|b>. Choose an
    approximant<nbsp><math|g> to<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>> of
    precision<nbsp><math|L+b>. Its own height-one representation has some
    finite cost<nbsp><math|b<rprime|'>>. Next choose an
    approximant<nbsp><math|g<rprime|'>> to<nbsp><math|\<cal-V\>> of
    precision<nbsp><math|L+b<rprime|'>>. Then

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-U\>*\<cal-V\>-g*g<rprime|'>=<around|(|\<cal-U\>-g|)>*\<cal-V\>+g*<around|(|\<cal-V\>-g<rprime|'>|)>
    </equation*>

    has precision<nbsp><math|L> against every permitted exterior sum. The
    product<nbsp><math|g*g<rprime|'>> has
    cost<nbsp><math|c<rsub|1>+c<rsub|2>>, since its word positions are
    separated at height<nbsp><math|s> and its scalar coefficient rates add.
    Iterate in a fixed order for more factors. Addition and subtraction
    follow by taking the maximum cost and making every approximation as
    accurate as required.

    For<nbsp><math|u\<less\>s>, expand a finite separated approximant
    to<nbsp><math|\<cal-U\>> at<nbsp><math|o> to an auxiliary
    degree<nbsp><math|H>. Applied to each term, <eqref|eq:J-raw-rates> gives
    output fields<nbsp><math|A<rsub|d><around|(|o|)>> whose coefficient rates
    are at most<nbsp><math|c-d*s>, and an error at exterior
    height<nbsp><math|u> of rate at most <math|c-<around|(|H+1|)>*<around|(|s-u|)>>.
    Increasing<nbsp><math|H> gives any specified precision. The original
    approximation error permits these farther exterior tests. This gives an
    arbitrarily accurate single-point sum<nbsp><math|g> at<nbsp><math|o>, of
    cost at most<nbsp><math|c> at height<nbsp><math|u>. If <math|u=s>, retain
    the original approximant. This proves coarsening.

    For <math|s\<gtr\>0>, approximate with exterior height zero. A contact
    probe of total degree<nbsp><math|p> costs at most<nbsp><math|p> at height
    one; raise the precision by<nbsp><math|p>. Compactness
    of<nbsp><math|O<around|(|4|)>> makes this uniform over rotated and
    conjugated probes, so averaging preserves arbitrary precision. On a
    degree-<math|d> field the degree-<math|q> contact row is zero
    for<nbsp><math|d\<less\>q> and is a fixed linear map
    for<nbsp><math|d\<geqslant\>q>. Its rate is at most
    <math|c-d*s\<leqslant\>c-q*s>, proving the row bound. At<nbsp><math|s=0>
    test directly against the fixed clear probes: their positions have height
    zero and add no exponent. The same conclusion follows.

    For the residual, use <math|u\<less\>s> and the single-point
    sum<nbsp><math|g> just constructed. Every field of
    degree<nbsp><math|d\<leqslant\>q> cancels exactly under
    <math|1-I<rsub|q><rsup|o>*P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>>.
    For<nbsp><math|d\<geqslant\>q+1> its coefficient rate at the outgoing
    height is<nbsp><math|c-d*s>. Viewed at height<nbsp><math|u>, its cost is
    at most <math|c-d*<around|(|s-u|)>\<leqslant\>c-<around|(|q+1|)>*<around|(|s-u|)>>.
    The lower fields produced by its contact projection obey the same
    inequality, since their degrees are at most<nbsp><math|d>. Thus
    <math|g-I<rsub|q><rsup|o>*P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*g> has
    the required cost. The exact error is
    <math|<around|(|\<cal-U\>-g|)>-I<rsub|q><rsup|o>*P<rsub|\<leqslant\>q><rsup|<math-up|ct>>*<around|(|\<cal-U\>-g|)>>.
    The first term has arbitrary precision by choice of<nbsp><math|H>; the
    finite probes defining the second cost only finitely many additional
    exponents. Choose the precision to pay for them and for the requested
    exterior test. This proves the residual's approximation property. Its
    finite height-one representation follows from the initial one and the
    finite probe formulas. At<nbsp><math|u=s> the row bound makes the
    projected finite sum have cost<nbsp><math|c> at height<nbsp><math|s>, so
    subtraction retains that cost directly.<label|proof:J-layer-end>
  </proof>

  The extension and its order are proved together.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Contact extension and local order><label|lem:J2.2><label|lem:J2.3>
    Fix a state index<nbsp><math|\<zeta\>>, an arity<nbsp><math|n> and a
    list<nbsp><math|\<b-A\>\<in\>\<bbb-F\><rsup|n>>, and write
    <math|W=<around|\||\<b-A\>|\|><rsub|deg>>. The normal
    subtractions<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-recursion>, with the contact
    projections, bases and length<nbsp><math|s<rsub|\<ast\>>> fixed above,
    define a complex linear functional<nbsp><math|\<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>>>
    on<nbsp><math|C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<R><rsup|4*n>|)>>. For
    every compact <math|K\<subset\><R><rsup|4*n>> there is
    <math|C<rsub|K>\<less\>\<infty\>> such that

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>><around|(|f|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|K>*max<rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|W+2|)>*n><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<space|2em><around|(|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f\<subset\>K|)>.
    </equation*>

    For every<nbsp><math|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)>>,
    <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>><around|(|f|)>=<big|int>f<around|(|x|)>*S<rsub|n><rsup|\<zeta\>>*<around|(|\<b-A\>;x|)>*<space|0.17em>d*x>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    <label|proof:J2.2-start>We prove absolute integrability of the complete
    subtracted expression on every compact set, with a finite test seminorm,
    in four steps. We keep each contact subtraction with the term it
    subtracts; Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> bounds the resulting
    signed expressions on one collision sector; normal Taylor vanishing and
    an induction over partial collisions make the dyadic shell sum converge;
    a partition of unity covers an arbitrary compact set.

    <em|Step 1: keep the signed cancellation.> At a group<nbsp><math|h>,
    reorganize the full expression as

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-contact-arms><around*|(|1-<big|sum><rsub|q\<less\>L<rsub|h>><math-ss|R><rsub|h,q>|)>*\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>>*f+<big|sum><rsub|q\<less\>L<rsub|h>><math-ss|R><rsub|h,q>\<Pi\><rsub|h><rsup|<math-up|punct>>*<around|(|1-<math-up|Tay><rsub|h,L<rsub|h>-1-q><rsup|\<perp\>>|)>*f<space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    These are identities of field expressions with test operators; individual
    unsubtracted summands need not be integrable. Every nonzero Taylor term
    lies within<nbsp><math|2*s<rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>r<rsub|\<ast\>>> of
    its diagonal, so all its field projections have their required contact
    clearance. If<nbsp><math|L<rsub|h>=0>, no projection is subtracted and
    <math|n<rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|h>-d<rsub|h>\<geqslant\>1> already gives
    integrability. For<nbsp><math|L<rsub|h>\<gtr\>0>,
    <eqref|eq:J-contact-split> removes every degree
    below<nbsp><math|L<rsub|h>> from the first arm. We apply
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> to the punctured expression,
    including the child subtractions. Here is the induction invariant: after
    fixing the child arms and their test operators, the field expression
    belongs to<nbsp><math|\<bbb-A\><rsub|s><around|(|c|)>> at its outgoing
    height; a projected arm of degree<nbsp><math|q> has coefficient
    rate<nbsp><math|c-q*s>, and a residual arm through<nbsp><math|q> has cost
    <math|c-<around|(|q+1|)>*<around|(|s-u|)>> at a parent
    height<nbsp><math|u>. Singleton fields start this invariant. Products
    over separated partition blocks preserve it by the product clause of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer>; its projection and residual
    clauses give the next arms, and its coarsening clause handles equal
    heights. Keep every child Taylor operator attached to its arm throughout
    these operations. Each derivative expansion has finitely many scalar test
    factors of finite rate. The scalar-factor and product clauses of
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> include these in the
    approximation cost and retain the invariant for each differentiated arm.

    <em|Step 2: bounds on one collision sector.> Rescale the patch so all
    scales are at most one. Fix its finite collision tree, write
    <math|\<rho\><rsub|b>> for its scales and
    <math|R=\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|h|)>>>, and set

    <\equation*>
      T=log <around|(|1/\<rho\><rsub|min>|)>,<space|1em>s<rsub|b>=log
      <around|(|1/\<rho\><rsub|b>|)>/T,<space|1em>u=log
      <around|(|1/R|)>/T,<space|1em>\<rho\><rsub|min>=min<rsub|b>
      \<rho\><rsub|b><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    After a subsequence these heights converge
    in<nbsp><math|<around|[|0,1|]>>, with <math|s<rsub|h>\<geqslant\>u>.
    Retain the child size factor <math|Z<rsub|h>=<big|prod><rsub|b<text|
    proper below >h><around|(|\<rho\><rsub|b>/\<rho\><rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|b|)>>|)><rsup|-d<rsub|b>>>.
    Before child gains, the incoming cost is
    <math|c=d<rsub|h>*s<rsub|h>+<big|sum><rsub|b<text| proper below
    >h>d<rsub|b>*<around|(|s<rsub|b>-s<rsub|<math-up|par><around|(|b|)>>|)>>.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> gives a degree-<math|q> row of
    rate<nbsp><math|c-q*s<rsub|h>>, and a residual through
    degree<nbsp><math|L<rsub|h>-1> of cost
    <math|c-L<rsub|h>*<around|(|s<rsub|h>-u|)>>. Thus, after
    retaining<nbsp><math|Z<rsub|h>> and the outer field costs, their physical
    size bounds are respectively

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-sector-rates>C<rsub|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>*Z<rsub|h>*\<rho\><rsub|h><rsup|q-d<rsub|h>>*\<rho\><rsub|min><rsup|-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>,<space|2em>C<rsub|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>*Z<rsub|h>*\<rho\><rsub|h><rsup|-d<rsub|h>>*<around|(|\<rho\><rsub|h>/R|)><rsup|L<rsub|h>>*\<rho\><rsub|min><rsup|-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>.
    </equation>

    With one unresolved scale, <math|\<rho\><rsub|min>=\<rho\><rsub|h>=\<rho\>>,
    giving the row bound <math|C<rsub|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>*\<rho\><rsup|q-d<rsub|h>-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>>
    and residual gain <math|<around|(|\<rho\>/R|)><rsup|L<rsub|h>>>. With
    several scales, retain the loss at<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsub|min>> and the
    child gains; the induction below distributes the loss between ancestors
    and descendants. If these estimates failed uniformly in a sector, choose
    a violating sequence and take the convergent height vector just
    described. The signed invariant, including equal heights, gives the rates
    with loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>/2>, contradicting the
    violation with loss<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>. This
    proves<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-sector-rates> uniformly over the sector.

    Taylor's integral remainder gives, on a
    shell<nbsp><math|<around|\||x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|\|>\<asymp\>\<rho\>>
    within the subtraction range,

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|1-<math-up|Tay><rsub|h,L<rsub|h>-1-q><rsup|\<perp\>>|)>*f=O<space|-0.17em><around*|(|\<rho\><rsup|L<rsub|h>-q>*max<rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\>L<rsub|h>-q><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    Including normal volume, every term of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-arms>
    therefore gains<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsup|n<rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|h>+L<rsub|h>-d<rsub|h>-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>>,
    whose exponent is at least<nbsp><math|1-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>.

    <em|Step 3: partial collisions and summable shell bounds.> Partial
    collisions on a normal shell require a further induction. Induct
    on<nbsp><math|<around|\||h|\|>-<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>,
    where<nbsp><math|\<pi\>> is the currently separated partition. The case
    of singleton blocks is a separated correlation against a smooth compactly
    supported test, bounded by Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:F3>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-moments>. For a nonsingleton block, cut its normal
    coordinate into dyadic shells and rescale
    to<nbsp><math|1/2\<leqslant\><around|\||x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|\|>\<leqslant\>2>.
    At any point of this compact annulus its equality classes form a proper
    partition. The centroids of nonempty subgroups contained in different
    classes are distinct there. There are finitely many such subgroups;
    continuity gives a neighborhood where their distances have a common
    positive lower bound. A finite smooth partition of unity on the annulus
    thus gives sectors with all subsequent subgroup centroids separated.
    Within a sector apply the induction hypothesis to this strictly finer
    partition. On overlaps the resulting expressions agree: a group crossing
    two sectors' separated blocks has a Taylor jet on a diagonal disjoint
    from the localized test support, and contributes zero. The subdivision
    therefore preserves the full expression and its ordered test operators.

    For the shell estimate, let<nbsp><math|a\<gtr\>0> be the shell radius
    divided by its parent scale. The rescaled Taylor remainder through
    order<nbsp><math|k> obeys

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-shell-Taylor><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|1-<math-up|Tay><rsub|h,k><rsup|\<perp\>>|)>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|C<rsup|l><text|
      on the rescaled shell>>\<leqslant\>C<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|C<rsup|l+k+1>><choice|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|a<rsup|k+1>,>|<cell|a\<leqslant\>1,>>|<row|<cell|a<rsup|k>,>|<cell|a\<gtr\>1.>>>>>
    </equation>

    For<nbsp><math|a\<leqslant\>1>, differentiate the integral Taylor
    remainder, including the cutoff; for<nbsp><math|a\<gtr\>1>, use the
    bounded test and the degree-<math|k> polynomial. The cutoff derivatives
    have fixed bounds at the parent scale. For a projected
    component<nbsp><math|q>, use Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J-signed-layer> to
    transfer the signed field expression and put<nbsp><math|k=L<rsub|h>-1-q>.
    Its volume and coefficient power<nbsp><math|a<rsup|n<rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|h>+q-d<rsub|h>>>
    times the Taylor factor is at most<nbsp><math|a>
    when<nbsp><math|a\<leqslant\>1>, and at most<nbsp><math|1>
    when<nbsp><math|a\<gtr\>1>. Indeed<nbsp><math|n<rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|h>+q-d<rsub|h>+k=0>
    whenever<nbsp><math|q\<less\>L<rsub|h>>. For the unprojected remainder
    the power is<nbsp><math|n<rsup|\<perp\>><rsub|h>+L<rsub|h>-d<rsub|h>\<geqslant\>1>;
    its support restricts<nbsp><math|a> above by a fixed geometric constant.
    Thus, for every<nbsp><math|0\<less\>\<theta\>\<leqslant\>1>, the combined
    estimate is at most<nbsp><math|C*a<rsup|\<theta\>>>.

    Choose<nbsp><math|\<gamma\>\<gtr\>0> with<nbsp><math|<around|(|3<rsup|<around|\||h|\|>-<around|\||\<pi\>|\|>>+1|)>*\<gamma\>\<leqslant\>1>.
    At a finer partition with<nbsp><math|d<rprime|'>> remaining splits, the
    signed invariant of Step<nbsp>1 remains valid for every projected and
    residual arm. Allocate loss<nbsp><math|3<rsup|d<rprime|'>>*\<gamma\>> to
    its coefficient and test estimates. The induction hypothesis is:

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|<around|(|<math-up|H><rsub|d<rprime|'>>|)><space|1em>>|<cell|<text|each
        projected or residual arm, on a shell>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|<around|\||x<rsub|h><rsup|\<perp\>>|\|>\<asymp\>\<rho\>\<leqslant\>R\<leqslant\>1,<text|
        contributes at most>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|C*<around|(|\<rho\>/R|)><rsup|\<gamma\>>*R<rsup|-3<rsup|d<rprime|'>>*\<gamma\>><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|C<rsup|<around|(|W+2|)>*<around|(|d<rprime|'>+1|)>>><space|0.17em>.>>>>
      </aligned>
    </equation*>

    Apply the preceding shell estimate with<nbsp><math|\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>=3<rsup|d<rprime|'>>*\<gamma\>>
    and<nbsp><math|\<theta\>=\<gamma\>+\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>\<leqslant\>1>.
    One split strictly decreases the remaining number of splits,
    and<nbsp><math|3<rsup|d<rprime|'>+1>*\<gamma\>\<geqslant\>2\<cdot\>3<rsup|d<rprime|'>>*\<gamma\>+\<gamma\>>
    leaves a factor<nbsp><math|a<rsup|\<gamma\>>> after the preceding
    estimate. To see that this is uniform in the parent scale<nbsp><math|R>,
    combine the child loss<nbsp><math|\<rho\><rsup|-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>>
    with its ratio improvement by

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|\<rho\>/R|)><rsup|\<gamma\>+\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>*\<rho\><rsup|-\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>>=\<rho\><rsup|\<gamma\>>*R<rsup|-<around|(|\<gamma\>+\<varepsilon\><rprime|'>|)>><space|0.17em>.
    </equation*>

    The last factor is charged to the parent's allocated loss. Finite sector
    sums change only the constant. For shells<nbsp><math|\<rho\>\<asymp\>2<rsup|-j>>,
    the bound is consequently<nbsp><math|C*2<rsup|-j*\<gamma\>>> times a
    fixed test seminorm. Summing this geometric series completes the
    induction, and gives absolute convergence also for iterated choices of
    disjoint shells.

    <em|Step 4: the distribution on an arbitrary compact set.> Cover a given
    compact set by neighborhoods of the equality classes at each of its
    points. The same separation argument gives finitely many patches,
    including patches outside the total-collision subtraction range. On these
    patches the recursion reduces to the product of the full expressions in
    the separated classes: any crossing Taylor term has zero jet. A smooth
    partition of unity and the Leibniz rule combine their bounds. Each
    descent costs at most<nbsp><math|W+2> derivatives
    by<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-shell-Taylor>; there are at most<nbsp><math|n>
    descents. We obtain

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:J-contact-order><big|int><around*|\||<around*|\<langle\>|\<Pi\><rsub|<around|{|1,\<ldots\>,n|}>><rsup|<math-up|full>>*f|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<zeta\>><around|(|x|)>|\|>*<space|0.17em>d*x\<leqslant\>C<rsub|K>*max<rsub|<around|\||\<alpha\>|\|>\<leqslant\><around|(|W+2|)>*n><around|\<\|\|\>|\<partial\><rsup|\<alpha\>>*f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>*<space|1em><around|(|<math-up|supp><space|0.17em>f\<subset\>K|)><space|0.17em>.
    </equation>

    Define <math|\<frak-D\><rsub|\<b-A\>><rsup|\<zeta\>><around|(|f|)>> by
    this integral. Absolute convergence preserves complex linearity,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-order> with the integral triangle inequality
    gives the asserted local order.

    For<nbsp><math|f\<in\>C<rsub|c><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|<Conf|n>|)>>,
    every normal jet on a collision diagonal vanishes. Each nontrivial term
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:J-contact-recursion> has a largest Taylor operator
    acting first; disjoint operators commute. Only
    <math|f<around|(|x|)>*<big|prod><rsub|i>A<rsub|i><around|(|x<rsub|i>|)>>
    remains, proving the restriction also for empty and singleton
    lists.<label|proof:J2.2-end>
  </proof>

  <\render-proof|Proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT>>
    Definitions<nbsp><reference|def:D36>\U<reference|def:D38> fix the contact
    projections on the prescription of Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J1.1>, and
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.1> gives their bases.
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:J2.2> gives the distribution, its local order,
    and its agreement with the separated correlations.
  </render-proof>

  The conclusions of Theorem<nbsp><reference|prop:CT> assert neither
  uniqueness, positivity of the extension nor contact Ward identities.
  Estimates along general collision subspaces are not stated here and are not
  used in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:B>.

